/* Generado por export_producto.py desde 'IPRAF Producto.xlsx'. No editar a mano. */
window.IPRAF = {"readme":{"mcq":[{"head":null,"rows":[{"label":"IPRAF — READ ME","body":null},{"label":"Start here. This page teaches the method by walking you through one real problem. It takes about fifteen minutes. Do not skip to the end: the point is to get one answer wrong first.","body":null}]},{"head":"STEP 1 — ANSWER THIS BEFORE YOU READ ON","rows":[{"label":"The problem","body":"A farmer owns farmland. He deeds it to a man. The man does NOT record his deed and does NOT take possession. Two years later, the same farmer deeds the SAME land to a woman, who pays full market value and knows nothing about the man. She does not record either. The state's statute protects a later purchaser for value without notice, unless the earlier deed was recorded. WHO OWNS THE LAND — the man or the woman?"},{"label":"Decide now","body":"Answer it in your head, and commit to the answer, before reading the next line. The exercise does not work if you read on first."}]},{"head":"STEP 2 — WHAT YOUR TRAINING PROBABLY ANSWERED","rows":[{"label":"The reflex","body":"Most lawyers trained in a civil-law system answer THE MAN. The reasoning: neither party registered, so the registry cannot decide anything here; with registration out of the picture we fall back on the older title, and the first deed prevails. A second common answer is that NEITHER owns securely, because neither registered and registration is what makes an interest good against third parties."},{"label":"The answer","body":"THE WOMAN OWNS THE LAND. She wins even though she never recorded, and even though the man's deed is older. Under this kind of statute — a NOTICE statute — a later purchaser who pays value and has no notice of the earlier deed prevails, full stop. Recording is not what she needed. Not having notice is."},{"label":"Why the reflex fails","body":"Because it is reasoning from fe pública registral: registration as near-constitutive, the registry as the source of opposability. U.S. recording acts do not create title and do not make registration decisive. They resolve contests between competing claimants, and they protect one thing — a purchaser who paid value without notice. Your instinct was not sloppy. It was a correct application of the wrong system."}]},{"head":"STEP 3 — SEE THAT CASE IN THE WORKBOOK","rows":[{"label":"Open the case","body":"That problem is not invented for this page. It is a real exam case sitting in the workbook. Click the link on the left: it opens the Real Property Comparison Set at that exact row. Look at the four columns — the situation, the trigger, the one-line rule, and the sub-subject. Then come back here.","link":"#/caso/97?subject=property"}]},{"head":"STEP 4 — NOW COMPARE. THIS IS THE ENTIRE METHOD.","rows":[{"label":"How to do it","body":"On that sheet, click the filter arrow in row 5 of the DOCTRINAL FAMILY column and select 'Recording Acts'. Ten cases appear together. Below are three of them. Each changes exactly ONE element of the problem you just answered — and each changes the outcome. Open them in order."},{"label":"Change 1: the statute","body":"Same contest, but the statute now says the later purchaser must ALSO record first. It is called race-notice. Now the later buyer, who did everything right, LOSES if he records second. Nothing about fairness changed. One word in a statute changed.","link":"#/caso/94?subject=property"},{"label":"Change 2: the later taker","body":"Race-notice again, but this time the later taker received the land as a GIFT. He is not a purchaser for value, so the statute does not protect him at all — and the first grantee wins, even though the first grantee is the one who failed to record.","link":"#/caso/102?subject=property"},{"label":"Change 3: no statute","body":"Remove the recording statute entirely. Now it is common law first-in-time, and the earlier interest wins regardless of value or notice.","link":"#/caso/65?subject=property"},{"label":"What just happened","body":"Four cases. Almost the same facts. Four different winners. Nobody was more careful or more deserving than anybody else. The outcome was decided by two formal questions asked in a fixed order: WHICH STATUTE TYPE governs, and DID THE LATER TAKER GIVE VALUE WITHOUT NOTICE. That is what the examination is testing, and it is invisible if you read the cases one at a time."}]},{"head":"STEP 5 — READ THE UNIT THAT EXPLAINS IT","rows":[{"label":"Open Unit 1","body":"Everything you just worked out by comparing is written down in one place: Analytical Unit 1 of Real Property. Open it and read the last two columns. STRUCTURAL SENSITIVITY gives you the method as a sequence — start first-in-time, classify the statute from its wording, then run the later taker through the gates of value and notice. STRUCTURAL MISALIGNMENT names the trap you fell into by name.","link":"#/unidad/1?subject=property"},{"label":"What it will tell you","body":"That the civilian registry schema produces TWO systematic errors, not one: assuming registration always wins, which is false under a notice statute, and assuming non-registration always loses, which is false for donees and under first-in-time defaults. You probably made the second one twenty minutes ago."}]},{"head":"STEP 6 — WHAT YOU JUST MET HAS A NAME, AND THERE ARE FIVE","rows":[{"label":"[Institutional void]","body":"This one. The U.S. institution does not exist in your system, so you substitute the nearest thing you know — the registry — and it fits badly. Also: the civil jury, discovery, summary judgment, class actions, consideration."},{"label":"[Dual-system architecture]","body":"You assume one legal system; the U.S. runs parallel ones and the choice decides the answer. Federal versus state court. UCC versus common law. The first question is always: which system governs?"},{"label":"[Sequence & waiver traps]","body":"The answer depends on WHEN and IN WHAT ORDER, not on merits. A good defence raised one filing too late is gone permanently."},{"label":"[False cognate]","body":"The word translates and the concept does not. Res judicata is not cosa juzgada. Venue is not competencia territorial."},{"label":"[Convergence]","body":"Your instinct is right and the systems agree. Recorded on purpose: knowing where your training already works is as useful as knowing where it fails, and a diagnosis that only finds errors is not a diagnosis."}]},{"head":"NOW DO IT YOURSELF","rows":[{"label":"The loop","body":"Pick the subject you find hardest. Open its Comparison Set. Filter Doctrinal Family to one value. Read the cases one after another, and do not read for the rule — you know the rule. Read for the single element that differs between two cases that come out differently. Then open the linked Analytical Unit and check whether you found the same thing it did."},{"label":"Where things are","body":"Each subject has three sheets and no others. COMPARISON SET — the cases, one per row, for studying: this is where you spend your time. FINAL — the Analytical Units, for reading the analysis and for citing. INDEX — to locate a unit quickly, and to read the Big Picture for the whole subject. That is the entire product; there is nothing else to find and nothing you are missing. IF YOU ARE WORKING ON ESSAYS: the essay layer has its own guided page, MEE — READ ME, near the end of the workbook, because written answers fail in ways multiple-choice answers cannot — issues missed, resolved out of order, or left implicit."},{"label":"One caution","body":"Do not come away distrusting every instinct you have. Most of your training transfers. The five archetypes exist to tell you WHERE it stops transferring, which is a much smaller set than it feels like on a bad day."}]},{"head":"WHAT IS IN HERE, SUBJECT BY SUBJECT — CLICK ANY LINK TO LOOK AROUND","rows":[{"label":"Subject","body":"What the three sheets hold"},{"label":"Contracts","body":"140 exam cases in the Comparison Set, grouped into 71 Analytical Units in the Final. Open the cases to compare, the units to read why your intuition fails, the index to find one fast or read the subject's Big Picture."},{"label":"Civil Procedure","body":"138 exam cases in the Comparison Set, grouped into 50 Analytical Units in the Final. Open the cases to compare, the units to read why your intuition fails, the index to find one fast or read the subject's Big Picture."},{"label":"Real Property","body":"112 exam cases in the Comparison Set, grouped into 40 Analytical Units in the Final. Open the cases to compare, the units to read why your intuition fails, the index to find one fast or read the subject's Big Picture."},{"label":"Torts","body":"126 exam cases in the Comparison Set, grouped into 29 Analytical Units in the Final. Open the cases to compare, the units to read why your intuition fails, the index to find one fast or read the subject's Big Picture."},{"label":"Evidence","body":"137 exam cases in the Comparison Set, grouped into 42 Analytical Units in the Final. Open the cases to compare, the units to read why your intuition fails, the index to find one fast or read the subject's Big Picture."},{"label":"Constitutional Law","body":"122 exam cases in the Comparison Set, grouped into 47 Analytical Units in the Final. Open the cases to compare, the units to read why your intuition fails, the index to find one fast or read the subject's Big Picture."},{"label":"Criminal Procedure","body":"78 exam cases in the Comparison Set, grouped into 35 Analytical Units in the Final. Open the cases to compare, the units to read why your intuition fails, the index to find one fast or read the subject's Big Picture."},{"label":"Criminal Law","body":"95 exam cases in the Comparison Set, grouped into 28 Analytical Units in the Final. Open the cases to compare, the units to read why your intuition fails, the index to find one fast or read the subject's Big Picture."}]}],"essay":[{"head":null,"rows":[{"label":"IPRAF — MEE READ ME (essay layer)","body":null},{"label":"Start here if you are working on essays. The multiple-choice READ ME teaches the framework; this page teaches what changes when the answer has to be WRITTEN. It takes about fifteen minutes and starts with a problem you should get partly wrong.","body":null}]},{"head":"STEP 1 — ANSWER THIS, AND COUNT YOUR POINTS","rows":[{"label":"The problem","body":"Three partners run a service business. Their agreement requires six months' written notice to withdraw. One partner withdraws immediately, without giving the notice, and demands that the business be shut down and its assets sold. The other two want to carry on. WHAT HAPPENS TO THE BUSINESS, AND WHAT DOES HE GET?"},{"label":"Do two things","body":"First answer it. Then — and this is the part that matters here — write down HOW MANY separate points you would make in your answer. Commit to a number before reading on."}]},{"head":"STEP 2 — WHAT YOUR TRAINING ANSWERED","rows":[{"label":"The reflex","body":"Most lawyers trained in a civil-law system answer that the business is dissolved and must be liquidated, and that his only exposure is damages for the missing notice. The reasoning: nobody may be compelled to remain in a partnership, and a partner's withdrawal from a company of indefinite duration dissolves it. Most also count two or three points."},{"label":"The answer","body":"THE BUSINESS CONTINUES. His departure is effective and irrevocable, and it dissolves nothing. The other two may carry on, and they must buy out his interest at a statutory price — reduced by the damages his breach caused, and payable later rather than now. He has no voice in what happens next, and no right to take part in a winding up even if one occurs. He got out. He got nothing he asked for."},{"label":"Why the reflex fails","body":"Because U.S. law separates two things your system fuses. DISSOCIATION is the partner leaving. DISSOLUTION is the firm ending. Wrongful dissociation produces the first without the second. Your instinct was not careless; it was a correct application of a system in which the two are one event, and the correct answer here is therefore not merely unknown to you — it is unthinkable, which is why you will argue confidently for the liquidation the statute exists to prevent."}]},{"head":"STEP 3 — NOW COUNT WHAT WAS ACTUALLY SCORED","rows":[{"label":"Open the Comparison Set","body":"Click the link, then filter SCENARIO ID to MEE-090 — the question you just answered. Ten rows appear, one per issue. COLUMN K is the ISSUE COUNT and COLUMN L is the ISSUE CHAIN, which shows the order the issues must be resolved in and which of them depend on which. Use the ← READ ME link in the corner of that sheet to come back.","link":"#/mee"},{"label":"Ten items, not three","body":"(1) may he leave at all; (2) is leaving in breach wrongful; (3) does it cost him damages; (4) does his departure force a winding up; (5) what may the others do instead; (6) may he take part in a winding up; then the same question asked on the OPPOSITE assumption — (7) with proper notice, is it still wrongful; (8) what does it now trigger; (9) what is his role in it; and finally (10) what is he liable for afterwards."},{"label":"What that means","body":"If you answered with two or three points, you did not misunderstand the law — you answered a different question. This is the central fact of the essay layer: the source data set decomposes 133 essay questions into 1,063 discrete issues, a median of NINE per question. A reader trained to find THE legal problem of a case and expound it will see one problem where nine are being counted. That gap, not any individual rule, is what this layer documents."}]},{"head":"STEP 4 — COMPARE YOUR ANSWER WITH THE ONE THAT SCORES","rows":[{"label":"Open Unit 29","body":"This is the unit that answers items 1 to 5 of the ten you just counted — the first half of the answer, written out. Keep your own answer beside you. Three columns tell you three things you now need to know, and the ← READ ME link in the corner of that sheet brings you back.","link":"#/mee/unidad/29"},{"label":"\"Where does this go in my answer?\"","body":"COLUMN C, ISSUE SEQUENCE. It reads: “Items 1–5 of 10. The wrongful branch. Item 3 is the pivot: everything after it depends on the firm NOT dissolving.” That is the answer's skeleton. If you wrote item 4 before settling item 3, you argued about a buyout before establishing that there was anything to buy out of — and on an essay the order is not style, it is credit."},{"label":"\"What exactly must I write?\"","body":"COLUMN I, REQUIRED EXPLICIT STATEMENT. It lists five propositions: that he had the POWER to dissociate though not the right; that the dissociation was WRONGFUL because it breached the agreement; that it does NOT force a winding up; that the others may continue and must buy him out at the statutory price, less damages; and that he may not take part in a winding up. Read that list against your own answer, sentence by sentence."},{"label":"\"Which question is this?\"","body":"COLUMN B, SCENARIO ID. It reads MEE-090. Some units carry two IDs, because the same doctrine recurs across questions — filter that column to see every unit one question needs, or every question one unit serves."},{"label":"The point of the comparison","body":"You probably knew several of those five propositions and wrote two of them. That gap is not ignorance; it is the difference between knowing a rule and stating it. The Required Explicit Statement column exists to make that difference visible, because it is the one an essay grader can see and the one a multiple-choice question never tests."}]},{"head":"STEP 5 — WHAT ISSUE COUNT IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT","rows":[{"label":"What it is","body":"A COVERAGE TARGET and a difficulty marker. It turns 'write a good answer' into a countable condition: stop asking whether you wrote well and ask whether you accounted for all of them. Comparing a three-issue question with a twelve-issue one also tells you how to spend the time you have."},{"label":"What it is NOT","body":"A count of grading points. The NCBE supplies jurisdictions with analyses enumerating each question's issues, but most jurisdictions grade essays holistically and relatively rather than awarding a point per issue. Any figure presented as a point total would claim more than is known. REQUIRED EXPLICIT STATEMENT is likewise a reasoned inference about what a grader must be able to see, not a published rubric. Both limits are stated wherever these two fields appear."}]},{"head":"STEP 6 — THE FIVE FRICTIONS ARE THE SAME FIVE","rows":[{"label":"They are defined on the main READ ME","body":"One taxonomy governs both layers, which is what lets you compare patterns between the two examination formats. What you met above was [Institutional void]. The essay layer adds one twist to [Sequence & waiver traps]: a point is forfeited not only by acting too late, but by WRITING in the wrong order.","link":null}]},{"head":"NOW DO IT YOURSELF","rows":[{"label":"The loop","body":"Take a question. Before reading any analysis, answer it and COUNT your points. Then open its scenario record and compare your count with the Issue Count. The gap is your diagnosis, and it will be larger than you expect the first few times. Then read the units and check the Required Explicit Statement against what you actually wrote."},{"label":"Open the Comparison Set","body":"START HERE for the loop above. 141 rows, one per issue. Filter DOCTRINAL FAMILY to bring the same doctrine together across all seventeen questions; filter SCENARIO ID instead to walk one question in order, using the ITEM column to follow it. COLUMN K gives the question's ISSUE COUNT and COLUMN L its ISSUE CHAIN, so each row carries the map of the question it came from. Every row links to the unit that answers it.","link":"#/mee"},{"label":"Open the Final","body":"The 46 Analytical Units, for reading the analysis and for citing. The Legal Authorities block sits below the units.","link":"#/mee"},{"label":"Open the Index","body":"One line per unit, to locate one quickly — and the Big Picture for the subject as a whole, in four movements: where you start, what the U.S. system is, what diverges, and what is genuinely the same.","link":"#/mee"},{"label":"Where the 46 units came from","body":"The 46 units were authored from a source database of 1,063 issues drawn from 133 essay questions, of which the 141 Business Associations issues are the ones developed."},{"label":"One caution","body":"Do not treat the Issue Count as a ceiling either. It is the author's decomposition of what the question raises, not a promise about what any particular grader rewarded. Use it to stop under-answering, not to stop thinking."}]},{"head":"WHAT IS IN HERE — CLICK ANY LINK TO LOOK AROUND","rows":[{"label":"Sheet","body":"What it holds"},{"label":"Business Associations","body":"141 exam ISSUES in the Comparison Set — one row per issue, not per question — drawn from 17 essay questions and grouped into 46 Analytical Units in the Final. The Comparison Set also carries each question's ISSUE COUNT and ISSUE CHAIN, so you can check your own count and see the order the issues must be resolved in. Eight units serve more than one question, because the same doctrine recurs. Business Associations is the only essay subject developed: the others were either removed from the examination or are already covered by the eight multiple-choice modules."}]}]},"archetypes":[{"name":"Institutional void","count":134},{"name":"Dual-system architecture","count":36},{"name":"Sequence & waiver traps","count":29},{"name":"False cognate","count":104},{"name":"Convergence","count":85}],"subjects":[{"slug":"contracts","name":"Contracts","bigPicture":"WHERE YOU START. You were trained on a law of obligations that is unified and consensual. A contract is formed by consent on object and causa (C.C. art. 1.141), binds as law between the parties (art. 1.159), and is performed in good faith. Causa validates any serious promise — a gift, a favour, a settlement. Form rarely decides: a writing PROVES the agreement, it does not create it. When someone breaches, the primary remedy is performance in kind (art. 1.264), and damages cover the harm foreseeable at the moment of contracting (arts. 1.274–1.275). One code governs every contract, whether you sell a house, a truckload of steel, or your services.\n\nWHAT THE U.S. SYSTEM IS. There is no causa. The enforceability test is BARGAIN: consideration — something sought by one side and given in exchange by the other. A perfectly serious promise with nothing exchanged is unenforceable unless it fits a named exception (detrimental reliance, a signed writing reviving a time-barred debt, past benefit). Form is sometimes outcome-determinative rather than evidentiary: the Statute of Frauds makes certain agreements unenforceable without a signed writing, and between merchants an unanswered written confirmation binds the silent party. And there are TWO bodies of law, not one — the common law and UCC Article 2 — so the first question on any issue is which governs. The remedial default is money expectation damages, screened by foreseeability, certainty and mitigation; performance in kind is exceptional (land, unique goods).\n\nWHAT DIVERGES. Three trained habits generate the wrong answer. (1) Testing a promise for seriousness and causa instead of for bargained exchange — the whole consideration orbit (revival of prescribed debts, the pre-existing duty rule, past consideration, accord and satisfaction) is where the reflex fails most often, because causa accommodates every promise the bargain test rejects. (2) Treating form as proof rather than as a condition: an unquestionably sincere oral promise fails, while a writing nobody ever answered binds. (3) Demanding performance in kind, or integral reparation, where U.S. law gives money capped by foreseeability, certainty and mitigation — and rewrites your cláusula penal as a policed liquidated-damages clause. Add the governing-law fork: the same facts (a late delivery, a modification without new consideration) come out differently under the UCC than at common law.\n\nWHAT IS THE SAME. Good faith in performance; foreseeability at the time of contracting as the ceiling on damages (arts. 1.274–1.275 is essentially Hadley); the excepción non adimpleti contractus behind concurrent conditions; error and dolo as grounds to AVOID rather than to void; incapacity making the contract voidable at the protected party's option; and the instinct that a party may not profit from preventing the condition he promised to allow (art. 1.208 is excuse by prevention). Trust those, and spend the attention they free on consideration, the Statute of Frauds, and the UCC/common-law fork — which together account for most of the seventy units.","families":[{"name":"Formation","count":18},{"name":"Performance","count":14},{"name":"Conditions","count":12},{"name":"Damages","count":11},{"name":"Remedies","count":11},{"name":"Consideration","count":9},{"name":"Statute of Frauds","count":8},{"name":"Modification","count":7},{"name":"Defenses","count":6},{"name":"Breach","count":5},{"name":"Parol Evidence","count":5},{"name":"Scope","count":4},{"name":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","count":4},{"name":"Accord & Satisfaction","count":3},{"name":"Assignment & Delegation","count":3},{"name":"Interpretation","count":3},{"name":"Public Policy","count":3},{"name":"Capacity","count":2},{"name":"Discharge","count":2},{"name":"Restitution","count":2},{"name":"Risk of Loss","count":2},{"name":"UCC Buyer's Remedies","count":2},{"name":"Warranty","count":2},{"name":"Illegality / Public Policy","count":1},{"name":"Third Parties","count":1}],"units":[{"n":1,"summary":"Written promise to pay a debt after the statute of limitations has run, with no new consideration.","trigger":"Time-barred debt + signed writing acknowledging/promising to pay.","rule":"A written promise to pay a debt otherwise barred by the statute of limitations is enforceable to the extent promised, even absent new consideration (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 82).","sub":"Consideration – Revival of Time-Barred Debt (Written)","sensitivity":"Enforceability is capped “to the extent promised” — a promise to pay $1,000 revives only $1,000, not the original debt. In most jurisdictions a signed writing is required by statute; that writing is the formal condition distinguishing this unit from the oral promise in Unit 2.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The debt is real and acknowledged, so it’s owed” → only enforceable to the extent of the written promise.\n\nCivil-law systems treat prescripción (extinctive prescription) as extinguishing the obligation itself; a later acknowledgment is analyzed as interrupción/renuncia under the Code, not as a consideration substitute. The U.S. analysis reframes the same facts as a consideration-exception problem, so the trained instinct mislocates the issue.","authority":"Revival of time-barred debt (written): Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 82. The requirement of a signed writing is imposed by each jurisdiction’s statute, not by § 82 itself."},{"n":2,"summary":"Oral promise to pay a time-barred debt, with no new consideration or writing.","trigger":"Time-barred debt + merely oral “I promise I’ll pay” + no new consideration.","rule":"An oral promise to pay a debt barred by the statute of limitations is unenforceable absent new consideration or a writing satisfying the jurisdiction’s revival requirement.","sub":"Consideration – No Revival (Oral)","sensitivity":"Outcome flips on a single structural variable — the writing. Identical sympathetic facts produce no liability because the promise is oral; the result does not depend on the quantity of debt or sincerity of the promise.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “He admitted the debt, so he must pay” → unenforceable because the promise is oral, not written.\n\nA civil-law attorney sees an unequivocal acknowledgment of a real debt and expects enforceability on good-faith / pacta sunt servanda grounds, where form is largely irrelevant to validity. The U.S. system makes the writing outcome-determinative, so the misalignment is treating form as a mere formality rather than a dispositive condition.","authority":"No revival (oral): a promise to pay a time-barred debt is unenforceable absent new consideration or a writing satisfying the jurisdiction’s revival statute."},{"n":3,"summary":"A gratuitous promise (e.g., a relative’s promise to fund a gift or annuity) that the promisor should expect to induce reliance; the promisee then reasonably and detrimentally relies.","trigger":"Promise + promisor should reasonably expect it to induce reliance + promisee actually and detrimentally relies + injustice avoidable only by enforcement.","rule":"Under promissory estoppel (Restatement (Second) § 90), a promise the promisor should reasonably expect to induce action or forbearance, which does induce such reliance, is binding if injustice can be avoided only by enforcement; the remedy may be limited as justice requires (it need not always equal the full promised performance).","sub":"Consideration – Promissory Estoppel (Reliance)","sensitivity":"Reliance is the distinct structural condition that makes an otherwise unsupported promise enforceable; absent reliance the same gratuitous promise fails. The scope of recovery is itself flexible — measured by what is needed to avoid injustice, not automatically the full promised sum.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “It’s just a gift promise with no contract” → enforceable via promissory estoppel once the promisee detrimentally relies.\n\nVenezuelan/civil-law doctrine has no consideration requirement, so a gratuitous promise is tested for animus donandi and form (a donation may require notarization), not for reliance. The trained reflex is to ask whether a valid donation was formed, not whether detrimental reliance independently makes the promise enforceable.","authority":"Promissory estoppel / conditional gift: Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 90. A condition attached to a gift promise is not consideration; recovery may be limited as justice requires."},{"n":4,"summary":"Gratuitous “conditional gift” promise (e.g., a purebred puppy “when you graduate”); promisee enrolls, takes loans, graduates; promisor refuses.","trigger":"Promise framed as a gift conditioned on an act the promisee would plausibly undertake regardless; no bargained exchange.","rule":"A condition attached to a gift promise is not consideration; whether the promise is nonetheless enforceable turns on promissory estoppel — i.e., whether the promisee’s reliance was reasonable, detrimental, and substantial enough that injustice can be avoided only by enforcement, as opposed to conduct the promisee would have undertaken regardless.","sub":"Consideration – Conditional Gift vs. Bargained Exchange","sensitivity":"Contrast with Unit 3: similar reliance language, but the outcome turns on whether the act was bargained-for/substantial detrimental reliance or merely a condition on receiving a gift. Where the conduct would occur regardless of the promise, enforcement typically fails; the line is the quality of the reliance, not its mere presence.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “She relied, so the promise binds” → only if the reliance was substantial detrimental reliance, not conduct she’d do regardless of a gift.\n\nThe intuitive (and civil-law) reading — a clear promise plus a graduate who relied — points toward enforcement. The U.S. system distinguishes a conditional gift from a bargained-for exchange; the misalignment is assuming reliance alone binds, when the framework demands the specific structural quality of that reliance.","authority":"Promissory estoppel / conditional gift: Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 90. A condition attached to a gift promise is not consideration; recovery may be limited as justice requires."},{"n":5,"summary":"Common-law contract; one party demands extra pay for the same performance because of conditions that were reasonably foreseeable; other party agrees, then refuses to pay the extra.","trigger":"Modification of a common-law contract with no new consideration; the “new” circumstance was reasonably foreseeable / discoverable.","rule":"At common law a modification is unenforceable unless supported by new consideration; performing (or promising to perform) a pre-existing duty is not consideration. (Foreseeable difficulties do not excuse the pre-existing-duty rule.)","sub":"Modification – Pre-existing Duty Rule (Common Law)","sensitivity":"Enforceability turns on whether the difficulty was unforeseen. Reasonably foreseeable obstacles do NOT supply consideration; the same extra-payment promise is unenforceable here but could be enforceable if the obstacle were truly unanticipated.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The renegotiation was agreed in good faith, so it binds” → unenforceable at common law without NEW consideration, since the difficulty was foreseeable.\n\nCivil-law systems enforce good-faith modifications without a consideration analysis and address changed circumstances through teoría de la imprevisión / hardship. The trained instinct is to ask whether the renegotiation was in good faith; the U.S. system asks the categorically different question of whether new consideration exists.","authority":"Pre-existing duty & modification: Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 73, 89; economic duress, §§ 175–176. (UCC § 2-209 allows good-faith modification of goods contracts without consideration — contrast with common law.)"},{"n":6,"summary":"After completing work, a party refuses to release the other’s property unless an additional sum is promised; the coerced party, having no reasonable alternative, agrees.","trigger":"Modification for the same performance + wrongful threat (withholding property) + no reasonable alternative for the promisor.","rule":"A modification for identical performance is unenforceable for lack of consideration; independently, a promise induced by a wrongful threat leaving no reasonable alternative is voidable for economic duress.","sub":"Modification – Pre-existing Duty + Economic Duress","sensitivity":"Two independent structural defects converge: absence of consideration AND duress. Either alone defeats the extra promise; recognizing duress requires identifying the ‘no reasonable alternative’ condition, not merely that pressure existed.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “They freely agreed to the higher price, so it binds” → unenforceable on TWO grounds — no new consideration AND economic duress (no reasonable alternative).\n\nCivil-law violencia/intimidación as a vice of consent is conceptually close, so duress may be spotted; but the parallel consideration defect is invisible to civil-law training, which would otherwise uphold a freely renegotiated price. The misalignment is missing the second, consideration-based ground.","authority":"Pre-existing duty & modification: Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 73, 89; economic duress, §§ 175–176. (UCC § 2-209 allows good-faith modification of goods contracts without consideration — contrast with common law.)"},{"n":7,"summary":"Work conforms or is only disputed in bad faith; debtor tenders a lesser sum “in full satisfaction” and creditor cashes it, then sues for the balance.","trigger":"Tender of a lesser amount “in full satisfaction” of a debt that is liquidated/undisputed (or where any dispute is not in good faith).","rule":"Accord and satisfaction discharges a claim only where there is a bona fide, good-faith dispute over the amount owed; on a liquidated, undisputed debt, payment of a lesser sum is not satisfaction and the balance remains collectible.","sub":"Accord & Satisfaction – Bona Fide Dispute Requirement","sensitivity":"Outcome hinges on the existence of a genuine good-faith dispute. Without it, cashing the check does NOT bar suit for the remainder — the same act produces opposite results depending on whether the debt was truly disputed.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The creditor cashed the ‘paid in full’ check, so the matter is settled” → NOT settled unless a genuine good-faith dispute supplied consideration for taking less.\n\nCivil-law transacción and pago require a real controversy too, but the U.S. doctrine is sharply gated on consideration: a good-faith dispute is what supplies consideration for accepting less. The misalignment is treating the creditor’s acceptance of the check as a binding settlement regardless of whether a genuine dispute supplied consideration.","authority":"Accord & satisfaction: requires a bona fide, good-faith dispute; cf. UCC § 3-311 (full-satisfaction checks)."},{"n":8,"summary":"Single-delivery sale of goods; seller tenders goods that deviate in some respect (wrong variety, short count); buyer rejects upon tender, before accepting the goods.","trigger":"Non-installment UCC contract + nonconformity in the tender + buyer rejects before acceptance.","rule":"Under UCC § 2-601 (perfect tender), in a single-delivery contract the buyer may, before acceptance, reject goods that fail in any respect to conform — subject to the seller’s right to cure (§ 2-508). Once the buyer accepts, the right to reject is lost and the buyer must instead meet the stricter standards of revocation of acceptance (§ 2-608) or sue for damages (§ 2-714).","sub":"Performance – Perfect Tender Rule","sensitivity":"Two structural gates: (1) the contract must be non-installment for the strict ‘any nonconformity’ standard to apply (contrast Unit 9), and (2) the buyer must not yet have accepted — acceptance converts the analysis from free rejection to the harder ‘substantial impairment’ standard of revocation.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Minor shortfall, just claim damages” → in a single-delivery contract the buyer may reject for ANY nonconformity (before acceptance).\n\nCivil-law sales tolerate minor nonconformity (cumplimiento defectuoso) and lean toward price reduction/damages over rejection. The misalignment runs both ways: under-applying the buyer’s strict pre-acceptance right to reject trivial deviations, while over-applying a right to ‘reject’ after the goods have already been accepted, where U.S. law no longer permits simple rejection.","authority":"Perfect tender / installments: UCC § 2-601 (perfect tender), § 2-607 (effect of acceptance), § 2-608 (revocation of acceptance – substantial impairment), § 2-612 (installment contracts), § 2-714 (buyer’s damages)."},{"n":9,"summary":"Goods to be delivered in separate lots (installments); one installment is nonconforming or short; buyer tries to reject that lot or the whole contract.","trigger":"Installment contract + nonconformity in one (or more) installments.","rule":"Under UCC § 2-612, a buyer may reject an installment only if the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of that installment and cannot be cured, and may cancel the whole contract only if a nonconformity substantially impairs the value of the whole.","sub":"Performance – Installment Contracts (Perfect-Tender Exception)","sensitivity":"The single structural fact of installment delivery displaces perfect tender (Unit 8) and substitutes a substantial-impairment standard. Identical defects yield different rejection rights depending solely on the delivery structure.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Same defect, so same rejection right” → NO — installments use a ‘substantial impairment’ standard, not perfect tender.\n\nBecause civil-law sales already use a materiality/gravity threshold for resolución, an attorney may correctly intuit ‘substantial impairment’ here — but then wrongly carry that same lenient standard back into single-delivery cases (Unit 8). The misalignment is failing to switch standards based on the contract’s structural form.","authority":"Perfect tender / installments: UCC § 2-601 (perfect tender), § 2-607 (effect of acceptance), § 2-608 (revocation of acceptance – substantial impairment), § 2-612 (installment contracts), § 2-714 (buyer’s damages)."},{"n":10,"summary":"Seller makes a nonconforming tender BEFORE the contract deadline (e.g., ships 50-inch mowers on Mar. 30 for an Apr. 1 contract); buyer rejects.","trigger":"Nonconforming tender + time for performance has NOT yet expired.","rule":"Under UCC § 2-508(1), where a buyer rejects a nonconforming tender and the time for performance has not yet expired, the seller may seasonably notify the buyer of intent to cure and make a conforming delivery within the contract time — as a matter of right.","sub":"Performance – Seller’s Right to Cure Before Deadline (§ 2-508(1))","sensitivity":"The dispositive structural fact is whether the performance deadline has passed. Before the deadline, cure is available as of right and does NOT require any ‘reasonable grounds to believe’ the tender was acceptable (contrast Unit 11).","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The seller can always fix it in good faith” → before the deadline, cure is a matter of right, with no ‘reasonable grounds’ showing required.\n\nA civil-law attorney may reflexively import a generalized good-faith cure right and apply the same ‘reasonable grounds’ test in every cure scenario. The misalignment is collapsing § 2-508(1) and (2) into one rule and missing that the pre-deadline right to cure is unconditional.","authority":"Seller’s right to cure: UCC § 2-508(1) (before the contract deadline, as of right) and § 2-508(2) (after the deadline, only with reasonable grounds to believe the tender acceptable)."},{"n":11,"summary":"Seller makes a nonconforming tender AFTER the deadline (e.g., substitutes larger containers with a price allowance), reasonably believing it would be acceptable; buyer rejects.","trigger":"Nonconforming tender + performance time HAS expired + seller had reasonable grounds to believe the tender (with or without allowance) would be acceptable.","rule":"Under UCC § 2-508(2), where the time for performance has expired, the seller may have a further reasonable time to substitute a conforming tender only if the seller had reasonable grounds to believe the nonconforming tender would be acceptable, with or without a money allowance, and seasonably notifies the buyer.","sub":"Performance – Seller’s Right to Cure After Deadline (§ 2-508(2))","sensitivity":"After the deadline, cure is conditional: it exists ONLY if the seller had reasonable grounds to believe the tender acceptable. The same nonconforming shipment that could be freely cured before the deadline (Unit 10) now requires this additional structural showing.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The seller can always fix it in good faith” → AFTER the deadline, cure exists only if the seller had reasonable grounds to believe the tender acceptable.\n\nThe misalignment mirrors Unit 10 from the other direction: a trained reflex toward broad good-faith cure leads to assuming cure is always available, overlooking that post-deadline cure is gated on the seller’s reasonable-grounds belief — a condition with no clean civil-law analogue.","authority":"Seller’s right to cure: UCC § 2-508(1) (before the contract deadline, as of right) and § 2-508(2) (after the deadline, only with reasonable grounds to believe the tender acceptable)."},{"n":12,"summary":"Merchant offeror gives a signed written assurance that an offer to sell goods will be held open for a stated period (e.g., six months); offeror later raises the price within that period.","trigger":"Merchant + signed writing assuring the offer will be held open + stated period exceeding three months.","rule":"Under UCC § 2-205, a merchant’s signed written firm offer is irrevocable without consideration for the time stated, but in no event longer than three months; if the stated period exceeds three months, the offer is irrevocable only for the first three months and is freely revocable thereafter.","sub":"Formation – Firm Offers (§ 2-205 three-month cap)","sensitivity":"Irrevocability is capped at three months regardless of the longer stated period. After three months the offer is freely revocable; it does NOT remain automatically open, so an attempt to accept after the offeror has revoked or raised the price fails.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Six months means six months” → a firm offer is irrevocable for at most three months, then freely revocable.\n\nCivil-law systems (e.g., oferta con plazo) honor the offeror’s stated period largely as written, so the trained expectation is that a six-month promise binds for six months. The misalignment is honoring the stated term over the statutory three-month ceiling, and assuming the offer survives by default rather than lapsing/being revocable.","authority":"Firm offer: UCC § 2-205 (merchant’s signed written offer irrevocable for the time stated, in no event longer than three months)."},{"n":13,"summary":"Buyer orders goods; seller’s acknowledgment/acceptance adds a term (e.g., an arbitration clause) and the goods ship; a dispute arises over whether the added term governs.","trigger":"Sale of goods between merchants + acceptance or written confirmation containing an additional term not in the offer.","rule":"Under UCC § 2-207, a definite acceptance forms a contract even with additional terms; between merchants those terms become part of the contract unless the offer limits acceptance to its terms, the term materially alters the deal, or objection is given within a reasonable time.","sub":"Formation – Battle of the Forms (§ 2-207)","sensitivity":"Whether the added term enters the contract turns on the material-alteration screen and merchant status. An arbitration clause is commonly treated as a material alteration, so it typically does NOT bind — the same exchange would bind for a trivial term.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The reply changed a term, so there’s no contract” → § 2-207 forms a contract anyway; the added term is then screened for material alteration.\n\nCivil-law formation requires mirror-image consent; a reply that varies the offer is a counter-offer, so the trained reflex is ‘no contract’ or ‘last-shot’ governs. The misalignment is failing to recognize that § 2-207 forms a contract DESPITE the variance and then independently screens the added term for material alteration.","authority":"Battle of the forms: UCC § 2-207."},{"n":14,"summary":"Two merchants reach an oral deal for goods ≥ $500; one sends a signed written confirmation; the recipient does not object; later the recipient denies a binding contract under the Statute of Frauds.","trigger":"Both parties merchants + oral agreement + signed confirmatory memo received + no written objection within 10 days.","rule":"Under UCC § 2-201(2), a confirmatory memo signed by the sender and received by a merchant who has reason to know its contents satisfies the Statute of Frauds against the recipient unless the recipient objects in writing within 10 days of receipt.","sub":"Statute of Frauds – Merchant’s Confirmatory Memo","sensitivity":"The recipient is bound by the SOF without ever signing — the dispositive conditions are merchant status, receipt, and the 10-day window. Silence past 10 days satisfies the writing requirement against a party who signed nothing.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “An oral deal is valid and nobody is bound by a letter they ignored” → between merchants, an unanswered signed confirmation satisfies the Statute of Frauds against the recipient after 10 days.\n\nCivil-law sales are largely consensual and an oral sale is valid; there is no SOF and certainly no rule binding a party to another’s unanswered letter. The misalignment is both expecting the oral deal to be enforceable on its own and failing to register that silence can satisfy a writing requirement against a non-signer.","authority":"Merchant’s confirmatory memo: UCC § 2-201(2) (with the general writing requirement in § 2-201(1))."},{"n":15,"summary":"Contract uses an “FOB [seller’s city]” term; seller delivers goods to a carrier and notifies buyer; goods are later destroyed in transit.","trigger":"FOB-place-of-shipment term + goods duly delivered to the carrier + proper notice to buyer.","rule":"Under UCC §§ 2-319 & 2-509, an “FOB seller’s location” term creates a shipment contract; risk of loss passes to the buyer when conforming goods are duly delivered to the carrier and the seller makes any required notice/documents available.","sub":"Risk of Loss – Shipment vs. Destination (FOB)","sensitivity":"Allocation of loss turns entirely on the FOB designation: ‘FOB seller’ (shipment) passes risk at delivery to the carrier, while ‘FOB buyer/destination’ keeps risk on the seller until tender at destination. The single term decides who bears a casualty loss.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Whoever owns/possesses the goods bears the loss” → the FOB shipment term passes risk to the buyer once goods reach the carrier.\n\nCivil-law systems tie risk (riesgo) more to transfer of ownership/possession (res perit domino) and to whether the seller is in mora. The misalignment is reasoning from title/possession or fault instead of from the parties’ shipment-term allocation, which can place loss on a buyer who never received the goods.","authority":"Risk of loss / FOB: UCC §§ 2-319 (FOB term) and 2-509 (risk of loss absent breach)."},{"n":16,"summary":"Consumer buys ordinary goods from a merchant who deals in such goods (e.g., basketball shoes); the goods fail during normal use; seller refuses a remedy.","trigger":"Sale by a merchant who deals in goods of that kind + goods not fit for their ordinary purpose.","rule":"Under UCC § 2-314, a merchant who deals in goods of that kind impliedly warrants that the goods are merchantable — fit for the ordinary purposes for which such goods are used — unless the warranty is effectively disclaimed.","sub":"Warranty – Implied Warranty of Merchantability","sensitivity":"The warranty arises automatically from merchant status, not from any express promise; liability turns on ‘ordinary purpose’ and on whether an effective disclaimer (e.g., ‘as is’/conspicuous) was made. No negligence or fault need be shown.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Hidden-defect / fault claim with a short notice deadline” → the UCC warranty is automatic, status-based, and no-fault.\n\nCivil-law saneamiento por vicios ocultos protects buyers too, but is framed around hidden defects, notice periods, and fault, with shorter actio redhibitoria deadlines. The misalignment is analyzing the defect as a hidden-vice/fault claim and missing the UCC’s automatic, status-based, no-fault warranty and its distinct disclaimer mechanics.","authority":"Implied warranty of merchantability: UCC § 2-314; disclaimers under § 2-316."},{"n":17,"summary":"A minor contracts for non-necessary goods/services, even promising not to disaffirm; after performance the minor (or parent) refuses to pay and the minor disaffirms.","trigger":"Party is a minor + contract is for non-necessaries + minor disaffirms (a promise not to disaffirm notwithstanding).","rule":"An infant’s contract is voidable at the minor’s election; a promise not to disaffirm is ineffective. For non-necessaries the minor owes nothing beyond returning what remains; quasi-contractual liability for reasonable value attaches only to necessaries.","sub":"Capacity – Minors / Disaffirmance & Necessaries","sensitivity":"Liability turns on the necessaries/non-necessaries line and on the minor’s power to disaffirm, which a contractual waiver cannot defeat. The same transaction binds (in quasi-contract) only if reclassified as a necessary.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “He got the benefit and promised not to disaffirm, so he’s liable” → the minor may still disaffirm; liability for non-necessaries is essentially nil.\n\nVenezuelan capacity rules also protect menores, but enforce restitution and may give effect to ratification and to obligaciones naturales differently; a contractual waiver and benefit received may weigh toward liability. The misalignment is honoring the minor’s promise/benefit and missing that disaffirmance is a near-absolute structural right limited only by the necessaries doctrine.","authority":"Capacity / minors: an infant’s contract is voidable; quasi-contractual liability attaches only to necessaries (Restatement (Second) § 14)."},{"n":18,"summary":"A party makes an accurate representation (e.g., a financial statement) that becomes materially false due to a later change before the other party relies; the party stays silent.","trigger":"Prior assertion true when made + material change before the other party’s reliance + failure to disclose the change.","rule":"Under Restatement (Second) § 161, a party has a duty to correct a prior assertion that was true when made but has become materially misleading before reliance; nondisclosure then operates as a misrepresentation rendering the contract voidable.","sub":"Defenses – Misrepresentation by Nondisclosure / Duty to Correct","sensitivity":"Liability turns on the timing of the change relative to reliance and on the materiality of the changed fact. The same silence is innocuous if the change occurs after reliance, but voids the contract if it precedes reliance and is not corrected.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The statement was true when made, so there’s no fraud” → a duty to correct arises once the prior assertion becomes materially false before reliance.\n\nCivil-law buena fe precontractual and dolo/reticencia reach similar ground, so the duty may be intuited; but the U.S. analysis is precise about the structural trigger (true-when-made assertion later rendered false pre-reliance). The misalignment is locating the duty in a general good-faith principle rather than the specific ‘duty to correct’ condition.","authority":"Nondisclosure / duty to correct: Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 161 (when non-disclosure is equivalent to an assertion)."},{"n":19,"summary":"A contractor performs satisfactory work but failed to pay/obtain a local license; the customer refuses payment, citing the contractor’s noncompliance with the licensing scheme.","trigger":"Performance by an unlicensed party + the licensing requirement is primarily revenue-raising rather than for public protection.","rule":"Where a licensing statute is primarily for raising revenue, noncompliance does not bar enforcement and the provider may recover; where the statute is regulatory/protective of the public, the contract is illegal and unenforceable (often with no recovery, even in quantum meruit).","sub":"Illegality / Public Policy – Licensing (Revenue vs. Protective)","sensitivity":"Enforceability turns entirely on the purpose of the licensing scheme. The identical unlicensed performance is recoverable under a revenue statute but void under a protective statute — the classification, not the work quality, decides recovery.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “No license = illegal = void” → a revenue-raising licensing breach does NOT bar enforcement; only a protective one does.\n\nCivil-law illegality (objeto/causa ilícita, normas de orden público) tends to treat statutory violations more categorically and resolves restitution through enriquecimiento sin causa. The misalignment is collapsing all licensing breaches into ‘illegal therefore void,’ missing the U.S. revenue-vs-protective distinction that can preserve enforcement.","authority":"Illegality / licensing: Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 178–181; enforceability turns on whether the licensing statute is regulatory/protective or merely revenue-raising."},{"n":20,"summary":"Payment is expressly conditioned on issuance of a third party's certificate (e.g., an architect's); the certificate is not issued despite the builder's substantial completion.","trigger":"Express condition precedent to payment + condition not satisfied + non-occurrence not caused by the obligor.","rule":"Substantial performance does not excuse the non-occurrence of an express condition; unless the condition is excused, the conditioned duty to pay never becomes due (Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 224–225, 237).","sub":"Conditions – Express Conditions / Strict Compliance","sensitivity":"Express conditions demand strict compliance; substantial performance applies only to constructive conditions (Unit 21). Two safety valves: excuse to avoid disproportionate forfeiture where the condition is not a material part of the exchange (§ 229), and excuse where the third party's certificate is withheld dishonestly or in bad faith.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The work is done, so the owner must pay” → the payment duty never matured because the express condition did not occur.\n\nA civil-law reasoner analyzes performance through good faith and equivalence of prestations, and treats the condición suspensiva (C.C. arts. 1,197 ff.) as a matter of exigibility tempered by equity. The U.S. system enforces the parties' express risk allocation literally; equity enters only through the narrow excuse doctrines — not through a general fairness override.","authority":"Express conditions / strict compliance: Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 224–225, 237; excuse of condition: § 229 (disproportionate forfeiture); third-party satisfaction certificates: honest-judgment (good-faith) standard."},{"n":21,"summary":"A builder or service provider completes performance with minor, good-faith deviations from specifications; the owner refuses to pay anything.","trigger":"Constructive (implied) conditions of exchange + breach that is minor/immaterial + good-faith performance.","rule":"Where performance is substantially complete and the breach immaterial, the other party's duty to pay is triggered; recovery is the contract price minus damages caused by the defects — cost to cure, or diminution in value where cure would be economic waste (Restatement (Second) §§ 237, 241, 348; Jacob & Youngs v. Kent).","sub":"Conditions – Substantial Performance (Constructive Conditions)","sensitivity":"Substantial performance is measured by the § 241 materiality factors and the breacher's good faith, not by percentage alone. It never applies to express conditions (Unit 20) and never applies to UCC single-delivery sales, which use perfect tender (Contracts Unit 8).","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Any deviation is a breach, so nothing is owed” — or, inversely, exporting this lenient standard into sales of goods.\n\nBecause civil-law resolución requires a grave breach, the trained attorney often intuits the common-law result correctly — but then fails to switch standards at the two structural borders: express conditions (strict compliance, Unit 20) and sales of goods (perfect tender, Contracts Unit 8). The misalignment is treating one leniency standard as universal.","authority":"Substantial performance: Restatement (Second) §§ 237, 241 (materiality factors), 348(2) (cost to cure vs. diminution in value); Jacob & Youngs v. Kent, 129 N.E. 889 (N.Y. 1921)."},{"n":22,"summary":"A party performs late. The contract either does or does not state that time is of the essence; the counterparty treats the delay as total breach.","trigger":"Late performance + presence or absence of a “time is of the essence” clause.","rule":"Late performance is always a breach entitling the other party to damages. Absent a time-is-of-the-essence clause, a short delay is not a material breach, so the counterparty must still perform (while recovering delay damages). Where the clause makes timely performance an express condition, lateness discharges the counterparty (Restatement (Second) §§ 235(2), 237, 241–242).","sub":"Performance – Time of the Essence / Materiality of Delay","sensitivity":"The TOE clause converts delay from a compensable, non-material breach into the non-occurrence of an express condition: the same delay produces opposite outcomes depending solely on the clause (cf. Units 20–21). Without TOE, the aggrieved party who refuses to perform becomes the breaching party.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “slight delay and no formal demand — no liability yet” → in U.S. law delay is a breach the moment the date passes, with no demand required.\n\nOn the core point the civilian instinct is closer than it feels: under C.C. art. 1,269 the debtor of an obligation to give or to do is constituted in mora BY THE MERE EXPIRY of the term agreed — dies interpellat pro homine — so no interpelación is needed for a conventional deadline, and delay is already compensable. The reader who insists on a formal demand is importing a rule from other civil-law systems rather than applying his own. Where the divergence really lies is elsewhere: the civilian schema treats the delay as generating damages while the contract survives, whereas a TIME-IS-OF-THE-ESSENCE clause makes timeliness DISPOSITIVE — it converts a delay into a material breach that discharges the other party altogether, which is a consequence the codal apparatus does not attach to a mere term.","authority":"Time of the essence: Restatement (Second) §§ 235(2) (any non-performance is breach), 241–242 (materiality and discharge)."},{"n":23,"summary":"Before performance is due, a party states unequivocally that it will not perform; the counterparty cancels and/or sues immediately.","trigger":"Executory bilateral contract + clear and unequivocal refusal to perform (or definite voluntary disablement) before the due date.","rule":"An anticipatory repudiation is a clear, unequivocal manifestation that a party will not perform; it permits the aggrieved party to treat the contract as breached at once, suspend its own performance, and sue before the performance date. The repudiation may be retracted until the other party materially relies, accepts it, or sues (Restatement (Second) §§ 250, 253, 256; UCC §§ 2-610, 2-611).","sub":"Breach – Anticipatory Repudiation (Unequivocal Refusal)","sensitivity":"The dividing line is the quality of the statement: unequivocal refusal → immediate breach (this unit); equivocation or expressed doubt → no repudiation, only grounds to demand assurances (Unit 24). Retraction remains possible until reliance or acceptance — timing of the aggrieved party's response is itself outcome-determinative.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Nothing is owed until the due date, so nothing can be done now” → the aggrieved party may cancel and sue immediately.\n\nAnticipatory repudiation has no true civil-law counterpart: breach is assessed at exigibility, and early refusals are managed through resolución after default. The misalignment runs in both directions — failing to act on a true repudiation, or (Unit 24) overreacting to mere doubt as if it were one.","authority":"Anticipatory repudiation: Restatement (Second) §§ 250, 253, 256 (retraction); UCC §§ 2-610, 2-611."},{"n":24,"summary":"A party expresses doubt about its ability to perform (“I'll try,” “I might not have the money”); the counterparty wants to cancel or must decide whether to keep performing.","trigger":"Reasonable grounds for insecurity + statement that is equivocal (not an unequivocal refusal).","rule":"Equivocation is not repudiation. The insecure party may demand adequate assurances and suspend any performance for which it has not received the agreed exchange; failure to provide assurances within a reasonable time (under the UCC, a justified written demand and not more than 30 days) operates as a repudiation (Restatement (Second) § 251; UCC § 2-609).","sub":"Breach – Demand for Adequate Assurances (Equivocation)","sensitivity":"Three structural levers: (1) the demand may be oral at common law but must be written under the UCC; (2) the demanding party must allow a reasonable time to respond — covering or canceling immediately makes it the breacher; (3) only the failure to assure converts doubt into repudiation (contrast Unit 23).","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “He said he might not perform — the contract is over” → canceling on mere equivocation makes the canceling party the breaching party.\n\nThe civil codes contain no assurance mechanism, so the trained instinct jumps directly from doubt to resolución. U.S. law interposes a mandatory intermediate step whose formal requirements — writing under the UCC, reasonable time to respond — are themselves outcome-determinative.","authority":"Adequate assurances: Restatement (Second) § 251 (demand may be oral; reasonable time); UCC § 2-609 (justified written demand; assurance within a reasonable time not exceeding 30 days)."},{"n":25,"summary":"After a breach, the aggrieved party claims losses beyond the value of the promised performance: lost profits from collateral deals, penalties owed to third parties, reputational loss.","trigger":"Breach + consequential (special) losses + question of what the breaching party knew or had reason to know at the time of contracting.","rule":"Consequential damages are recoverable only if, at the time of contracting, they were foreseeable as a probable result of breach — either arising in the ordinary course or from special circumstances the breaching party then knew or had reason to know; disclosure at the time of breach is too late (Hadley v. Baxendale; Restatement (Second) § 351; UCC § 2-715(2)).","sub":"Damages – Consequential Damages (Hadley Foreseeability)","sensitivity":"The temporal anchor is formation, not breach: identical losses are recoverable or not depending solely on when the special circumstances were communicated. Satellite rules: goodwill/reputation losses are recoverable if foreseeable and reasonably certain; under the UCC only buyers — never sellers — recover consequential damages (§§ 2-708, 2-710, 2-715); avoidable consequential losses are barred by mitigation.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The breach caused the loss, so it must be compensated” → barred unless foreseeable at formation.\n\nC.C. arts. 1,274–1,275 also anchor foreseeability at contracting, but lift the cap in cases of dolo, where all direct damages become compensable. The instinct that willful breach expands liability has no U.S. analogue — Hadley's cap applies regardless of the breacher's state of mind — and the seller-side bar on consequentials is invisible to a full-reparation schema.","authority":"Consequential damages: Hadley v. Baxendale, 156 Eng. Rep. 145 (Ex. 1854); Restatement (Second) § 351; UCC § 2-715(2) (buyer's consequentials); §§ 2-708, 2-710 (seller limited to incidental damages)."},{"n":26,"summary":"A written contract is signed; one party offers evidence of an earlier oral (or written) term that adds to or contradicts the writing.","trigger":"Integrated writing + prior or contemporaneous extrinsic term + attempt to vary or contradict the writing.","rule":"The parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements offered to contradict any integrated writing. Where the integration is complete (e.g., merger clause), even consistent additional terms are barred; a partial integration admits consistent additional terms but never contradicting ones (Restatement (Second) §§ 209–216; UCC § 2-202).","sub":"Parol Evidence – Integration / Contradicting vs. Additional Terms","sensitivity":"Two sequential gates: first the degree of integration (complete vs. partial), then the character of the term (contradicting — always barred — vs. consistent additional — barred only if the integration is complete). The rule governs only prior/contemporaneous terms; it never blocks evidence of subsequent modifications.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “what was truly agreed can always be proven” → the writing legally displaces the earlier terms.\n\nCivil-law adjudication seeks the parties' real common intent, and the concept of an integrated document that SUPERSEDES — not merely evidences — prior agreed terms has no codal counterpart: the writing is treated as evidence of the deal rather than as the deal itself. Note what the civilian system does instead, because it is a limit on proof rather than a rule about the document: C.C. art. 1,387 excludes witness testimony to prove a convention above a stated value. That is a procedural bar on one MEANS of proof, and it is why the reader has no schema for a writing that shuts out even admitted oral terms proven by other means.","authority":"Parol evidence / integration: Restatement (Second) §§ 209–216; UCC § 2-202."},{"n":27,"summary":"Despite an integrated writing (even with a merger clause), a party offers extrinsic evidence to interpret an ambiguous term, to prove trade usage, or to show fraud or mistake in order to avoid or reform the contract.","trigger":"Integrated writing + extrinsic evidence offered for a purpose other than varying the terms.","rule":"The parol evidence rule does not bar extrinsic evidence offered (1) to interpret an ambiguous existing term — including usage of trade, course of dealing, and course of performance; (2) to prove a formation defense (fraud, duress, mistake) and avoid or rescind the contract; or (3) to establish grounds for reformation (Restatement (Second) §§ 155, 214; UCC §§ 1-303, 2-202).","sub":"Parol Evidence – Exceptions (Interpretation, Avoidance, Reformation)","sensitivity":"The dispositive variable is the PURPOSE of the evidence, not its content: the same conversation is barred if offered to change a term (Unit 26) and admitted if offered to explain an ambiguity or to void the deal. Within interpretation, the hierarchy is express terms > course of performance > course of dealing > usage of trade (UCC § 1-303(e)).","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The merger clause seals the document — nothing outside comes in” → over-applying the bar forfeits fraud, interpretation, and reformation arguments.\n\nHaving internalized the integration bar (Unit 26), the civil-law reasoner tends to absolutize it — the mirror image of the first error. The U.S. structure is purpose-indexed: missing the exception is as costly as missing the rule, and the examiner tests both directions.","authority":"Parol evidence exceptions: Restatement (Second) §§ 214 (interpretation, invalidating causes), 155 (reformation); UCC §§ 1-303 (hierarchy), 2-202."},{"n":28,"summary":"A seller of land (or of unique goods) refuses to perform; the buyer wants the property itself rather than money damages.","trigger":"Breach + unique subject matter (real property; one-of-a-kind or practically unobtainable goods) + inadequacy of the legal remedy.","rule":"Specific performance is an extraordinary equitable remedy available only where damages are inadequate. Real property is conclusively presumed unique; goods qualify only if unique or in other proper circumstances (UCC § 2-716). Personal-services contracts are never specifically enforced, though negative covenants may be enjoined (Restatement (Second) §§ 359–360, 367).","sub":"Remedies – Specific Performance (Uniqueness / Inadequacy of Damages)","sensitivity":"The remedy is exceptional, not the default: analysis starts from damages and reaches specific performance only through inadequacy. Land is per se unique; fungible goods never qualify; services are categorically excluded. The structural gate is the classification of the subject matter.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “He must do what he promised” → denied except for land or unique goods.\n\nThis is inverse friction: under C.C. art. 1,264 performance in kind is the primary remedy and damages are subsidiary. The civilian over-requests specific performance in goods and services cases — and under-appreciates that the money judgment is the U.S. system's default conception of contractual liability.","authority":"Specific performance: Restatement (Second) §§ 359–360 (adequacy of damages), 367 (personal services); UCC § 2-716 (unique goods or other proper circumstances)."},{"n":29,"summary":"An offer requires acceptance by completed performance (reward, task by deadline, requested act); the offeree begins but has not finished when the offeror revokes, dies, or the deadline passes.","trigger":"Unilateral offer (acceptance only by performance) + performance begun but incomplete.","rule":"Beginning the invited performance creates an option contract that makes the offer irrevocable for a reasonable time, but acceptance — and the right to payment — arises only upon completion (Restatement (Second) § 45). The option survives the offeror's death (an ordinary revocable offer does not, § 48), yet it lapses if performance is not completed within the offer's stated deadline.","sub":"Formation – Unilateral Contracts (§ 45 Option; Completion Required)","sensitivity":"Three variables resolve every variant: (1) beginning performance ≠ acceptance — half the task earns nothing; (2) irrevocability protects the offeree who begins: revocation and even the offeror's death are ineffective against the § 45 option; (3) express deadlines are strict — late completion is non-acceptance. Rewards add a knowledge requirement: performance must be rendered with knowledge of the offer.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “He started the work, so there is a contract and he must be paid” → no payment without completion, although the offer became irrevocable.\n\nThe unilateral/bilateral distinction and the § 45 option have no codal analogue. The civilian schema (oferta con plazo irrevocable per se, C.C. art. 1,137) both over-protects — assuming payment for partial work through quasi-contract instincts — and under-protects — assuming that death or revocation kills an offer that beginning performance has already locked open.","authority":"Unilateral contracts: Restatement (Second) §§ 45 (option by part performance), 48 (death of offeror), 62; rewards: knowledge of the offer required."},{"n":30,"summary":"Parties to a sale of goods agree to change a term (price, quantity, time) with no new consideration; one side later insists on the original term.","trigger":"UCC contract + modification agreed in good faith + no new consideration.","rule":"Under UCC § 2-209(1), an agreement modifying a contract for the sale of goods needs no consideration to be binding, provided it is sought and made in good faith; but if the contract as modified falls within the Statute of Frauds (§ 2-201), the modification must satisfy it (§ 2-209(3)).","sub":"Modification – Good-Faith Modification Without Consideration (§ 2-209)","sensitivity":"The governing-law fork is dispositive: the identical renegotiation is unenforceable at common law without consideration (Contracts Unit 5) and binding under the UCC if in good faith. Second gate: a modified contract for goods ≥ $500 must still satisfy the Statute of Frauds. Extorted 'modifications' fail the good-faith screen (cf. Contracts Unit 6).","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “They both agreed, so the new price governs — everywhere” → true under the UCC, false at common law. Civil-law doctrine treats any consensual novación/modificación as binding without a consideration filter (pacta sunt servanda), so the attorney fails to ask the threshold question — goods or services? — on which the entire answer turns.","authority":"UCC modification: UCC § 2-209(1) (no consideration required; good faith per § 1-304), § 2-209(3) (SOF must be satisfied if contract as modified is within § 2-201)."},{"n":31,"summary":"Mid-performance, unforeseen difficulties arise (hidden conditions); the parties agree to a fair price adjustment for the same work; the paying party later refuses the increase.","trigger":"Common-law contract still executory on both sides + circumstances not anticipated when the contract was made + modification fair and equitable in light of them.","rule":"A promise modifying a duty under a contract not fully performed on either side is binding without new consideration if the modification is fair and equitable in view of circumstances not anticipated when the contract was made (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 89).","sub":"Modification – Unforeseen Circumstances Exception (§ 89)","sensitivity":"Three cumulative structural gates: (1) executory on both sides; (2) genuinely unanticipated circumstances — a risk discoverable by reasonable inspection (e.g., asbestos a pre-inspection would have found) does not qualify; (3) fair and equitable adjustment. Absent any one, the pre-existing duty rule (Contracts Unit 5) controls.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Unexpected hardship always justifies renegotiation” → only within § 89’s three gates. The civil-law schema of teória de la imprevisión/onerosidad sobrevenida invites a generalized equity adjustment; U.S. law channels it through a narrow exception whose foreseeability screen (reasonable inspection) does most of the work on exam facts.","authority":"Unforeseen-circumstances modification: Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 89; contrast pre-existing duty rule, Contracts Unit 5."},{"n":32,"summary":"An oral agreement contemplates performance for life (lifetime employment/consulting); one party invokes the Statute of Frauds one-year rule.","trigger":"Oral agreement + duration measured by a lifetime + SOF one-year objection.","rule":"The one-year clause covers only contracts that cannot possibly be fully performed within one year from formation; because a person may die at any time, a lifetime contract can be fully performed within a year and needs no writing (Restatement (Second) § 130).","sub":"Statute of Frauds – One-Year Rule / Lifetime Contracts","sensitivity":"The test is logical possibility at formation, not probability or expectation: a 5-year contract needs a writing though it may end early by breach; a lifetime contract does not, because death completes (not excuses) performance. The distinction between performance and excuse is the hinge.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “A lifelong commitment is the most serious of all — surely it must be in writing” → no writing required. Civil-law formality intuitions scale with the transaction’s importance and duration; the U.S. rule turns on a logical-possibility test that is orthogonal to importance, so the trained instinct inverts the outcome precisely on the most counterintuitive fact pattern.","authority":"One-year rule / lifetime contracts: Restatement (Second) § 130 (possibility of performance within one year; death completes a lifetime contract)."},{"n":33,"summary":"A person orally promises a creditor to 'see to it' that another's debt or liability is paid; the creditor sues the promisor when the debt goes unpaid.","trigger":"Oral promise to answer for the debt/default of another + promise made to the creditor + no writing.","rule":"A suretyship promise — one made to the obligee to answer for the duty of another — is within the Statute of Frauds and unenforceable without a signed writing, unless the promisor's main purpose is to serve his own pecuniary interest (Restatement (Second) §§ 112, 116).","sub":"Statute of Frauds – Suretyship / Main Purpose Exception","sensitivity":"Two structural switches: (1) the promise must run to the creditor — a promise made to the debtor is not a suretyship; (2) the main purpose rule removes the writing requirement where the promisor acts for his own economic benefit. Gratuitous family reassurance ('I will see to it you are reimbursed') sits squarely inside the writing requirement.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “He gave his word to pay — a serious, spontaneous promise binds” → unenforceable without a writing. The civil-law fianza is consensual (writing matters only as proof), so form feels evidentiary rather than constitutive; the U.S. rule makes the writing outcome-determinative, and the main-purpose exception — keyed to self-interest, not solemnity — has no codal analogue.","authority":"Suretyship: Restatement (Second) § 112 (promise to answer for duty of another); § 116 (main purpose exception)."},{"n":34,"summary":"A writing memorializing a deal is signed by only one party; the signer backs out and argues the contract fails the Statute of Frauds because the other party never signed.","trigger":"Transaction within the SOF + writing with essential terms + signed only by the party against whom enforcement is sought.","rule":"The Statute of Frauds requires a writing signed by the party to be charged — the one against whom enforcement is sought; the enforcing party's signature is unnecessary (Restatement (Second) §§ 131, 135; UCC § 2-201(1)).","sub":"Statute of Frauds – Signature of the Party to Be Charged","sensitivity":"Enforceability is asymmetric by design: the same writing may be enforceable against the signer yet unenforceable against the non-signer. Under UCC § 2-201 the writing need only evidence a contract and state a quantity; between merchants, an unanswered confirmatory memo binds even the non-signer (Contracts Unit 14).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “A contract document must be signed by both parties to exist” → one signature suffices against the signer. The civilian schema of the bilaterally executed documento/escritura treats mutual signature as the mark of perfection; U.S. law severs formation (oral consent) from enforceability (signature of the defendant only), producing one-way enforceability the civil-law model does not contemplate.","authority":"Signature of party to be charged: Restatement (Second) §§ 131, 135; UCC § 2-201(1); merchants' confirmatory memo, UCC § 2-201(2) (Contracts Unit 14)."},{"n":35,"summary":"Two parties contract to confer a benefit on a third person; before the third party learns of, relies on, or sues under the promise, the contracting parties modify or reduce it.","trigger":"Contract intended to benefit a third party + attempted modification/rescission + question whether the beneficiary's rights have vested.","rule":"Only an intended beneficiary — identified by the parties' intent and the purpose of conferring a benefit — may enforce the contract; an incidental beneficiary has no rights. The parties may modify or rescind until the beneficiary's rights vest by assent at a party's request, detrimental reliance, or suit (Restatement (Second) §§ 302, 311).","sub":"Third-Party Beneficiaries – Intended vs. Incidental / Vesting","sensitivity":"Two sequential gates: status (intended vs. incidental), then timing (vesting). The beneficiary's objection or ignorance is irrelevant before vesting — the power to modify belongs to the contracting parties; after reliance or assent it is extinguished. The promisor remains liable to the promisee in any event.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The stipulation was made in his favor, so he acquired the right at once” → modifiable until vesting. This is a rare convergence trap: C.C. art. 1,164 likewise lets the stipulator revoke until the third party declares acceptance — but the U.S. vesting triggers (reliance and suit, not only acceptance) are broader, and the intended/incidental screen has no codal counterpart, so the civilian both under- and over-protects the beneficiary depending on the variant.","authority":"Third-party beneficiaries: Restatement (Second) § 302 (intended vs. incidental); § 311 (vesting by assent, reliance, or suit)."},{"n":36,"summary":"A contract says 'this contract may not be assigned' (or 'any assignment is void'); one party nonetheless assigns its right to payment, or hands its performance to a substitute.","trigger":"Anti-assignment clause + transfer of payment rights (assignment) or of performance duties (delegation).","rule":"Unless circumstances indicate the contrary, a prohibition of assignment 'of the contract' bars only the delegation of duties, not the assignment of rights — payment rights remain freely assignable; delegation is barred by such a clause and, independently, wherever the obligee has a substantial interest in the original promisor's performance (UCC § 2-210; Restatement (Second) §§ 317–318, 322). The assignee takes the rights as they exist at assignment, subject to prior valid modifications and defenses.","sub":"Assignment & Delegation – Anti-Assignment Clauses / Non-Delegable Duties","sensitivity":"Three levers: (1) the clause's object — 'the contract' is read against forfeiture as barring delegation only; (2) the nature of the transfer — a mere change of payee never prejudices the obligor; (3) personal-skill or particular-source relationships make duties non-delegable even without a clause. The assignee steps into the assignor's shoes — no better rights.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The clause says no assignment, so the payment assignment is void” → the payment assignment stands; only the delegation fails. The civilian schema reads contract language literally (cesión de créditos vs. cesión de deudas are code-defined and the parties' clause governs); the U.S. construction rule deliberately reads the clause narrowly to favor alienability — an interpretive default that runs against the civilian instinct to honor the written prohibition.","authority":"Assignment & delegation: UCC § 2-210; Restatement (Second) §§ 317–318 (delegability limits), 322 (construction of anti-assignment clauses); assignee subject to prior modifications/defenses, § 336."},{"n":37,"summary":"A contract is losing money for the performing party; the other side wrongfully terminates mid-performance. Expectation damages would be reduced (or erased) by the loss the performer avoided.","trigger":"Owner's total breach mid-performance + contract would have produced a net loss for the performer + restitution claimed instead of expectation.","rule":"The injured party may elect restitution of the benefit conferred, measured by its reasonable value and not limited by the contract price or reduced by the loss the contract would have produced; but restitution is unavailable if the injured party has fully performed and the only duty left is payment of a definite sum (Restatement (Second) § 373).","sub":"Remedies – Restitution as Alternative to Expectation (Losing Contracts)","sensitivity":"Election is the structural move: expectation and reliance both subtract avoided losses; restitution does not, so in losing contracts it yields more. The single cut-off — full performance with only payment due — flips the remedy back to the (losing) contract price. Contrast the breaching party's own recovery, which is capped at benefit conferred minus damages (§ 374).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Damages restore the contract's economics, so the loss must be deducted in every measure” → not under restitution. Civil-law liability doctrine is compensatory and contract-anchored (reparación del daño within the contract's frame); an off-contract, election-based remedy that can exceed the bargain has no codal slot, so the civilian defaults to expectation arithmetic and undervalues the claim.","authority":"Restitution / losing contracts: Restatement (Second) § 373 (incl. § 373(2) full-performance bar); breaching party's restitution, § 374."},{"n":38,"summary":"A contract fixes a sum payable on breach and labels it 'liquidated damages, not a penalty'; the sum is flat regardless of how serious the breach is.","trigger":"Stipulated-damages clause + question whether it is a reasonable forecast graduated to the anticipated or actual harm.","rule":"A stipulated sum is enforceable as liquidated damages only if the amount is reasonable in light of the anticipated or actual loss and actual damages would be difficult to prove; a sum that operates as a penalty — e.g., one flat amount for breaches of trivially different severity — is void regardless of the parties' label (Restatement (Second) § 356; UCC § 2-718(1)).","sub":"Remedies – Liquidated Damages vs. Penalty","sensitivity":"Substance controls over label: the parties cannot contract into enforceability. The tell-tale structural defect is lack of graduation — the same $500 for one missing shingle or a thousand. Reasonableness is judged against anticipated or actual harm; in-terrorem sums designed to compel performance fail.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The parties freely stipulated the sum and even said it is not a penalty — it binds” → void if punitive in operation. The Venezuelan cláusula penal (C.C. arts. 1,257 ff.) is valid precisely as a coercive pre-set penalty, judicially reducible at most; the U.S. system categorically refuses punitive stipulations in contract. The civilian instinct — party autonomy validates the clause — is the exact reflex the doctrine targets.","authority":"Liquidated damages: Restatement (Second) § 356; UCC § 2-718(1)."},{"n":39,"summary":"Goods are damaged or destroyed while a breach is in play: the seller shipped nonconforming goods, or the buyer repudiated while conforming goods awaited delivery.","trigger":"Casualty to goods + an underlying breach (nonconforming tender or buyer's repudiation) + dispute over who bears the loss.","rule":"Breach re-allocates risk of loss: where a tender so fails to conform as to give a right of rejection, the risk remains on the seller until cure or acceptance; where the buyer breaches as to conforming, identified goods, the risk passes to the buyer only to the extent of any deficiency in the seller's insurance coverage, and only for a commercially reasonable time (UCC § 2-510).","sub":"Risk of Loss – Effect of Breach (§ 2-510)","sensitivity":"§ 2-510 overrides the ordinary shipment/destination allocation (Contracts Unit 15): an 'FOB seller's city' term does not protect a seller who shipped nonconforming goods. Asymmetry: seller's breach keeps the whole risk on the seller; buyer's breach shifts risk only as an insurance gap-filler.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Risk follows the shipping term (or possession), full stop” → breach displaces the FOB allocation. Civil-law systems tie risk to ownership or delivery (res perit domino); a rule that re-routes casualty loss through breach status and the content of insurance coverage has no codal analogue, so the schema applies the transport term learned in Contracts Unit 15 one unit too far.","authority":"Risk of loss on breach: UCC § 2-510; ordinary allocations, §§ 2-319, 2-509 (Contracts Unit 15)."},{"n":40,"summary":"An acceptance is sent by mail or another medium; before or after it arrives, the offeror revokes, the offeree also sends a rejection, or an option deadline passes.","trigger":"Acceptance dispatched by an authorized medium + question of the effective moment (dispatch vs. receipt).","rule":"An acceptance is effective upon proper dispatch (mailbox rule), but not where (1) the offer itself requires receipt, (2) an option contract is involved — acceptance is effective only on receipt within the option period, or (3) a rejection was dispatched first, in which case whichever arrives first governs (Restatement (Second) §§ 40, 63).","sub":"Formation – Mailbox Rule and Its Exceptions","sensitivity":"The effective moment flips on discrete structural switches, not on fairness: offer language demanding receipt displaces dispatch; option acceptances must ARRIVE in time (an acceptance mailed on day 29 of a 30-day option but received on day 31 is late); a prior rejection converts the race into first-to-arrive.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The contract exists when the offeror learns of the acceptance” → by default it exists at dispatch. Venezuelan law follows a cognition/reception model (C.C. art. 1,137: the contract forms when the offeror has knowledge of the acceptance), the exact inverse default. The misalignment compounds on options: having learned the U.S. dispatch rule, the civilian then over-applies it to the one context (options) where receipt controls.","authority":"Mailbox rule and exceptions: Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 40, 63 (acceptance effective on dispatch; option acceptances effective on receipt; dispatched rejection → first received governs)."},{"n":41,"summary":"An offeror promises to hold an offer open (orally, or in a signed writing reciting a nominal payment); before the stated period ends, the offeror revokes or sells to someone else.","trigger":"Promise of irrevocability + presence/absence of consideration or a qualifying signed writing.","rule":"An offer is freely revocable before acceptance even if it states it will be held open, unless (1) real consideration was given (classic option), (2) a signed writing recites purported consideration and proposes fair terms within a reasonable time — binding as an option even if the recited consideration was never actually paid (Restatement (Second) § 87(1)(a)), or (3) UCC § 2-205 applies (merchant firm offer, no consideration needed).","sub":"Formation – Options / Irrevocability (§ 87(1)(a))","sensitivity":"Three separate irrevocability tracks with different formal requirements: consideration (any offer), recited-consideration signed writing (§ 87(1)(a)), merchant signed firm offer (goods only, 3-month cap — Contracts Unit 12). An oral promise to hold open, standing alone, fails all three. Options on real property must additionally satisfy the Statute of Frauds.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “He gave his word to hold it open for ten days, so he cannot revoke” → revocable unless one of the three tracks is satisfied. Under C.C. art. 1,137 an offer stating a period is irrevocable for that period by operation of law; U.S. law presumes revocability and demands consideration or a qualifying form. The civilian both over-trusts stated deadlines (missing revocability) and, once corrected, under-uses § 87(1)(a) — not realizing that a mere RECITAL of $1 in a signed writing suffices.","authority":"Options / irrevocability: Restatement (Second) § 87(1)(a) (signed writing reciting purported consideration, fair terms, reasonable time); classic options (consideration); UCC § 2-205 (merchant firm offers — see Contracts Unit 12); real-property options within the Statute of Frauds."},{"n":42,"summary":"A person with diminished mental capacity (illness, medication, advanced age) makes a plainly disadvantageous contract; the other party knows or should know of the condition. No prior judicial declaration of incompetence exists.","trigger":"Mental incapacity at formation + no prior adjudication + counterparty’s knowledge or reason to know.","rule":"A contract made by a person unable to understand the nature and consequences of the transaction (or unable to act reasonably as to it, where the other party has reason to know) is VOIDABLE at that person’s election — not void; only contracts of a person under a prior judicial guardianship adjudication are void (Restatement (Second) § 15). Avoidance requires restoring benefits; liability persists in quasi-contract for necessaries.","sub":"Capacity – Mental Incapacity (Voidable, Not Void)","sensitivity":"The void/voidable line is dispositive: voidable means the contract stands unless and until the incapacitated party (or successor) elects to avoid it — the competent party cannot invoke the incapacity. Adjudication converts the analysis: post-guardianship contracts are void ab initio. The counterparty’s knowledge matters under the volitional prong (§ 15(1)(b)) and affects restitution terms.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The contract is null because she lacked consent” → it is voidable at HER option only. The civil-law nulidad framework (absolute vs. relative nullity, C.C. arts. 1,142–1,143) invites treating incapacity as a defect either party or a court may raise; the U.S. rule assigns the election exclusively to the protected party, keeps the deal alive by default, and resolves the equities through restitution — gross unfairness of price alone does not void a contract.","authority":"Mental incapacity: Restatement (Second) § 15 (cognitive and volitional tests; voidable at the incapacitated party’s election); contracts after adjudication of incompetence are void; quasi-contract liability for necessaries."},{"n":43,"summary":"An expert (trainer, dealer, appraiser) gives a favorable ‘opinion’ to induce a deal, knowing facts that make it unrealistic; the induced party seeks rescission for misrepresentation.","trigger":"Statement framed as opinion + speaker with superior knowledge or a relation of trust + reasonable reliance by the induced party.","rule":"Misrepresentation ordinarily requires an assertion of fact, and pure opinion or puffery is not actionable; but an opinion is actionable — supporting avoidance — where the speaker has superior knowledge or expertise, stands in a relation of trust, or the opinion implies the existence of facts the speaker knows are absent (Restatement (Second) §§ 168–169).","sub":"Defenses – Misrepresentation (Opinion by One with Superior Knowledge)","sensitivity":"The fact/opinion default flips on the speaker’s epistemic position, not on the words used: the identical sentence (‘in my opinion you can reach it’) is inert between equals and voidable when uttered by the expert to the novice. Companion condition: a true statement that becomes misleading before closing triggers a duty to correct (Contracts Unit 18).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “It was just his opinion — no liability” (or the inverse: “any exaggeration is dolo”) → the U.S. line runs through the speaker’s superior knowledge. The civil-law dolo analysis (C.C. art. 1,154: maquinaciones without which the party would not have contracted) is intent-centered and does not carve out opinion/puffery; the civilian schema therefore misses both the safe-harbor for sales talk and the expert-opinion exception that pierces it.","authority":"Opinion as misrepresentation: Restatement (Second) §§ 168–169 (reliance on opinion justified where superior knowledge, relation of trust, or implied facts); cf. § 161 duty to correct (Contracts Unit 18)."},{"n":44,"summary":"A supervening event (illness, accident, catastrophe, market shock) strikes after formation: either performance itself becomes impossible/unsafe, or performance remains possible but its point has evaporated for one party.","trigger":"Supervening event + non-occurrence was a basic assumption at formation + no fault and no assumption of the risk + EITHER performance impracticable OR principal purpose substantially frustrated.","rule":"Impracticability discharges where the performance itself is made impossible or impracticable (death/incapacity of an essential person, destruction of the subject matter, serious health risk) (Restatement (Second) § 261; UCC § 2-615). Frustration of purpose discharges where performance is still possible but the party’s principal purpose — known to both and a basic assumption of the contract — is substantially destroyed (§ 265). Mere added expense, hardship, or reduced desirability discharges nothing.","sub":"Discharge – Impracticability vs. Frustration of Purpose","sensitivity":"The classification question — WHICH duty is impaired — selects the doctrine: ability to perform (impracticability) vs. reason for performing (frustration). Both share the same three gates (basic assumption, no fault, no risk assumption), and both fail on mere hardship: customers ‘in shock’ after a disaster do not frustrate a catering contract, but a chemical in the mine air that endangers this engineer’s health does excuse him.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “This is force majeure — everything is excused” (or nothing is, absent physical impossibility) → U.S. law splits the analysis into two doctrines with distinct targets. The civil-law causa extraña no imputable / fuerza mayor (C.C. arts. 1,271–1,272) maps roughly onto impracticability but has no counterpart for frustration of PURPOSE; the civilian schema either forces purpose-cases into force majeure (where they fail for possibility) or dismisses them — missing the doctrine designed exactly for them.","authority":"Impracticability / frustration: Restatement (Second) §§ 261 (impracticability), 265 (frustration of purpose); UCC § 2-615 (excuse by failure of presupposed conditions); mere hardship or increased cost insufficient."},{"n":45,"summary":"One contract covers several structures/deliveries, each with its own stated price; the performing party completes some units and abandons the rest; the owner refuses to pay anything.","trigger":"Single contract + performances apportionable into corresponding pairs + separate price stated per unit + completion of some units only.","rule":"A contract is divisible where the performances of each party can be apportioned into corresponding pairs of part performances that the parties treat as agreed equivalents; the performing party may recover the contract price for each completed unit, less damages caused by breach of the remainder (Restatement (Second) § 240).","sub":"Performance – Divisible vs. Entire Contracts","sensitivity":"Divisibility is a term-structure fact, not a physical one: separate per-unit prices signal agreed equivalents; a single lump sum for the whole job makes the contract entire, relegating the abandoning party to restitution (and, traditionally, to nothing if the breach was willful). The same 1-of-3-buildings fact pattern produces contract-price recovery or mere restitution depending solely on how the price term was written.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “He built one of the three, so he is owed a proportional third” → only if the contract is structurally divisible. The civilian instinct of proportional counter-performance (and partial-payment equity) assumes apportionment as a default; U.S. law derives it from the parties’ own price architecture, and outside it the breaching performer drops to quasi-contract — a channel (Unit 37, Contracts Unit 7 logic) with its own limits.","authority":"Divisible contracts: Restatement (Second) § 240 (corresponding pairs of part performances as agreed equivalents); entire contracts → restitution only for the breaching performer."},{"n":46,"summary":"A cash sale of goods sets one exchange date (with or without time of the essence); at the appointed moment each side waits for the other to move first, or one side never shows up.","trigger":"Sale of goods + delivery and payment due simultaneously + neither party (or only one) tenders.","rule":"Delivery and payment are concurrent conditions: tender of delivery is a condition of the buyer’s duty to accept and pay (UCC § 2-507), and tender of payment is a condition of the seller’s duty to tender and complete delivery (UCC § 2-511; Restatement (Second) § 234(1)). A party must tender (or show readiness) to put the other in breach; where neither tenders by an essential date, neither breaches and both are discharged.","sub":"Performance – Tender / Concurrent Conditions (§§ 2-507, 2-511)","sensitivity":"Breach cannot be assigned without a tender: the standoff (‘you first’) produces mutual discharge, not mutual liability. The structure contrasts with non-simultaneous exchanges, where the longer performance goes first (§ 234(2)) — e.g., services before payment. A buyer who fails to appear for an essential pickup date never triggers the seller’s delivery duty.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The seller breached by not delivering” (or ‘both breached’) → no one breached; the conditions never matured. The civil-law excepción non adimpleti contractus (C.C. art. 1,168) is a DEFENSE raised against a demanding party, and mora doctrine expects a formal demand; the U.S. structure instead makes each tender a trigger of the other’s duty, so passivity by both simply dissolves the exchange — an outcome with no clean civilian analogue.","authority":"Tender / concurrent conditions: UCC §§ 2-507 (tender of delivery as condition), 2-511 (tender of payment as condition); Restatement (Second) § 234(1) (simultaneous performances due concurrently)."},{"n":47,"summary":"A duty is subject to a condition (inspection, certificate, notice); the protected party proceeds without insisting on it (pays anyway, closes anyway), then later invokes the condition to stop performing.","trigger":"Condition benefiting one party + conduct manifesting intent to perform despite its non-occurrence + later attempt to reassert it.","rule":"A waiver is the intentional relinquishment of a known right: the beneficiary of a condition who manifests willingness to perform despite its non-occurrence cannot later rely on it. Without consideration, only conditions that are NOT a material part of the agreed exchange can be waived; a waiver of a future condition may be retracted before the other party materially relies (Restatement (Second) §§ 84, 246).","sub":"Conditions – Waiver, Materiality Limit, and Retraction","sensitivity":"Three levers: (1) materiality — procedural/technical conditions (an inspection, a certificate deadline) are freely waivable, but a condition constituting a material part of the exchange requires consideration or estoppel; (2) timing — prospective waivers are retractable until reliance; (3) conduct suffices — paying twice without the required inspection waives it (estoppel closes the retraction door once relied upon).","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “A renunciation, once made, is simply valid” → valid only within the materiality limit and until retraction/reliance rules say otherwise. The civil-law renuncia of available rights operates without a consideration screen, so the civilian schema validates ALL waivers — missing that U.S. law polices the line between waiving procedural conditions and effectively re-writing the bargain for free.","authority":"Waiver of conditions: Restatement (Second) §§ 84 (promise to perform despite non-occurrence of condition), 246; materiality limit; retraction of prospective waivers before reliance."},{"n":48,"summary":"A debtor tenders a lesser sum ‘in full satisfaction’ of a debt whose amount and validity are NOT genuinely disputed; the creditor accepts the payment, then sues for the balance.","trigger":"Liquidated, undisputed debt + tender of a lesser amount as payment in full + creditor’s acceptance.","rule":"Absent a bona fide, good-faith dispute over what is owed, payment of a lesser sum is no consideration for discharge of the whole: the pre-existing duty (partial-payment) rule leaves the creditor free to recover the balance even after accepting the reduced payment (Foakes v. Beer; Restatement (Second) § 73).","sub":"Accord & Satisfaction – Undisputed Debt (No Discharge)","sensitivity":"Mirror-image of Contracts Unit 7: the ENTIRE outcome rides on whether a genuine good-faith dispute existed when the lesser payment was tendered. Disputed → accord and satisfaction discharges; undisputed → no consideration, no discharge, regardless of ‘paid in full’ notations or the creditor’s acceptance. Alternative discharge routes (new consideration, different performance, honest compromise) must be checked before concluding.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The creditor took the money as full payment — the debt is settled” → not settled; the promise to accept less is unenforceable. Civil-law remisión/quita permits a creditor to forgive part of a debt by simple agreement or acceptance, with no consideration filter; the U.S. rule makes the creditor’s own acceptance legally inert absent a real dispute — one of the most counterintuitive consideration results for a civilian reasoner.","authority":"Partial payment of undisputed debt: Foakes v. Beer, (1884) 9 App. Cas. 605 (H.L.); Restatement (Second) § 73 (performance of legal duty not consideration); contrast § 74 and Contracts Unit 7 (bona fide dispute)."},{"n":49,"summary":"A buyer of goods needs rights in specific goods before delivery (to insure them, to replevy them, to analyze risk of loss); the seller has shipped, marked, or set aside goods for the contract — or has not yet done so.","trigger":"Contract for sale of goods + act by the seller designating particular goods as the ones to which the contract refers.","rule":"Identification occurs when goods are shipped, marked, or otherwise designated by the seller as the goods to which the contract refers — whichever act comes first (UCC § 2-501); existing goods may be identified at contracting. Identification gives the buyer a special property and insurable interest and is the platform for replevin (§ 2-716(3)) and risk-of-loss analysis (§§ 2-509–2-510) — all independent of title.","sub":"Scope – Identification of Goods (§ 2-501)","sensitivity":"Identification is the operative switch — not ownership, not delivery, not payment: putting a collar with the buyer’s name on one puppy identifies it then and there, before crating or shipping. Sequencing matters (earliest designating act controls), and every downstream doctrine (insurable interest, replevin, risk of loss) keys off this event.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The buyer has no rights until she owns or possesses the goods” → rights attach at identification, long before either. In Venezuelan law property in specific goods passes by consent alone (C.C. art. 1,161), so the civilian schema runs every pre-delivery question through OWNERSHIP; the UCC deliberately decouples these incidents from title (§ 2-401), and the analysis fails unless re-anchored to identification as the operative event.","authority":"Identification of goods: UCC § 2-501 (identification; special property and insurable interest); § 2-716(3) (replevin); §§ 2-509–2-510 (risk of loss); § 2-401 (incidents decoupled from title)."},{"n":50,"summary":"A public reward offer (arrest and conviction; return of property) is claimed by someone who performs years later, performs without knowing of the offer, or merely promises to help.","trigger":"Public unilateral offer + performance that is complete/incomplete, informed/uninformed, or delayed beyond a reasonable time.","rule":"A reward is a unilateral offer accepted only by complete performance rendered with knowledge of the offer; a promise to perform is not acceptance. The offer lapses after a reasonable time or upon materially changed circumstances, and revocation is effective if communicated by means at least as public as the offer (Restatement (Second) §§ 29, 51, 54).","sub":"Formation – Public Rewards (Unilateral Offers)","sensitivity":"Three independent switches, each fatal: (1) knowledge — performance in ignorance of the offer is not acceptance; (2) completion — information that leads to arrest and conviction, not effort or promises; (3) timing — lapse after a reasonable time or changed circumstances, and equally-public revocation cuts off later claims. Cf. Unit 29 (§ 45 option once performance begins).","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The city promised publicly, so anyone who produces the result collects” → no recovery without knowledge, completion, and a still-live offer. Civil-law systems treat the promesa pública de recompensa as a binding unilateral declaration (C.C. art. 1,139) enforceable even by an uninformed performer; the U.S. system runs rewards through offer-and-acceptance, so the contractual gates — knowledge, completion, lapse — do the work that the civilian schema assigns to the declaration itself.","authority":"Public rewards: Restatement (Second) §§ 29, 51, 54 (knowledge of the offer; acceptance by performance); revocation of public offers by equally public means."},{"n":51,"summary":"A party receives services knowing they are offered for pay and says nothing (watches a fence go up; lets a lawyer begin requested work), then refuses to pay for lack of an express agreement.","trigger":"Benefit of offered services taken + reasonable opportunity to reject + reason to know compensation expected (or prior dealings making silence an acceptance).","rule":"Silence is not acceptance except where the offeree takes the benefit of offered services with reasonable opportunity to reject them and reason to know they were offered with expectation of compensation, where prior dealings make it reasonable that silence accepts, or where the offeror has stated silence will suffice and the offeree intends it as such (Restatement (Second) § 69); the resulting contract is implied in fact.","sub":"Formation – Silence as Acceptance / Implied-in-Fact Contracts (§ 69)","sensitivity":"The default — silence never accepts, and an offeror cannot impose “your silence will bind you” — flips only inside the § 69 pockets. The dispositive facts are the opportunity to reject and the reason to know payment was expected; the measure on an implied-in-fact theory is the contract/reasonable price, not mere enrichment.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “There was never any agreement, so at most he owes restitution for enrichment” → a true contract implied in fact exists and is enforced as such. The civilian schema routes these facts to enriquecimiento sin causa (C.C. art. 1,184), a subsidiary quasi-contractual claim; U.S. analysis locates a real contract formed by conduct, which changes both the theory the examiners expect and the measure of recovery.","authority":"Silence as acceptance: Restatement (Second) § 69; implied-in-fact contracts."},{"n":52,"summary":"A dispute arises over a written contract that is silent on one point (guest frequency; completion time) and ambiguous on another; one party drafted the document.","trigger":"Missing term (true gap) vs. ambiguous term (two reasonable readings) + one-sided drafting.","rule":"A missing essential term is supplied by the court as a reasonable default (gap filler) (Restatement (Second) § 204); contra proferentem — construing against the drafter — is an interpretive tiebreaker that applies only to ambiguous language actually in the writing, not to absent terms (§ 206).","sub":"Interpretation – Gap Fillers vs. Contra Proferentem","sensitivity":"Two different defects call for two different tools: absence → § 204 reasonable term; ambiguity → hierarchy of interpretation aids ending in § 206 against the drafter. Misdiagnosing a gap as an ambiguity (or vice versa) selects the wrong tool and the wrong answer; drafter-hostility alone never supplies a term that is not there.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “He drafted it, so every doubt — including what it does not say — cuts against him” → only ambiguities are construed against the drafter; gaps get neutral reasonable defaults. The civilian schema of interpretación conforme a la buena fe y la común intención treats the document as a single equitable whole; U.S. doctrine splits the problem into two regimented tools whose boundary is itself the tested condition.","authority":"Gap fillers / contra proferentem: Restatement (Second) §§ 204, 206; interpretation hierarchy §§ 201–203."},{"n":53,"summary":"A contract is silent on who performs first (renovation with payment terms unstated; services over months payable when?); one side demands payment midway or refuses to start without payment.","trigger":"No express term on order of performance + performances capable/incapable of simultaneous exchange.","rule":"Where performances can be exchanged simultaneously, they are due simultaneously (concurrent conditions); where one performance requires a period of time and the other does not, the longer performance must be rendered first — work precedes payment absent contrary agreement (Restatement (Second) § 234).","sub":"Conditions – Order of Performance (§ 234)","sensitivity":"One structural fact — whether the performances can occur at the same moment — selects the rule: simultaneity (cf. Unit 46 for goods) or work-first. The contractor who demands progress payments not bargained for, and the owner who withholds all payment after completion, are resolved by the same default.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “It is only fair to pay half at the halfway point” → no right to interim payment; the entire wage is due on completion. Civilian proportionality instincts (payment accruing as work progresses) have no purchase: the U.S. default makes completion a constructive condition of the duty to pay, so timing arguments must be won at drafting, not at equity.","authority":"Order of performance: Restatement (Second) § 234 (simultaneity; longer performance first)."},{"n":54,"summary":"A duty is conditioned on an event (financing docs in 7 days; third-party certificate), and the party whose duty is conditioned obstructs or fails to cooperate in bringing the event about, then invokes its non-occurrence.","trigger":"Condition + non-occurrence materially caused by the obligor's own breach of the duty of good faith and fair dealing.","rule":"Non-occurrence of a condition is excused where the party whose duty was conditioned wrongfully prevented or hindered its occurrence; a party cannot rely on the failure of a condition its own lack of cooperation materially contributed to (Restatement (Second) § 245).","sub":"Conditions – Excuse by Prevention (§ 245)","sensitivity":"The excuse operates as a one-way ratchet: prevention does not create liability by itself — it strips the preventing party of the condition as a defense, treating the conditioned duty as due. Pairs with Unit 20: strict compliance protects an innocent obligor; § 245 removes that protection from an obstructing one.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: here the trained instinct is largely CORRECT — C.C. art. 1,208 deems the condition fulfilled when the obligor prevents it. The misalignment is over-transfer: the civilian, comfortable with the principle, tends to apply it to any causal contribution; the U.S. rule requires a breach of good faith that materially contributed, and mere failure of an event the obligor did not control is never excused. Convergence units like this calibrate trust in prior schemas (cf. Contracts Unit 9).","authority":"Prevention / excuse of condition: Restatement (Second) § 245; duty of good faith and fair dealing § 205. (Convergence noted with C.C. art. 1,208 — prior-schema reference only.)"},{"n":55,"summary":"No contract is ever formed (acceptance arrived late; a condition to the deal failed; negotiations collapse after 'no contract until signed'), but one party conferred a benefit or spent in reliance.","trigger":"No enforceable contract + benefit conferred on the other party and/or foreseeable detrimental reliance.","rule":"Absence of a contract does not bar recovery: restitution (quantum meruit) reaches the reasonable value of benefits conferred and retained, and promissory estoppel enforces a promise relied upon to the promisee's detriment, with the remedy limited as justice requires (Restatement (Second) §§ 370–371, 90).","sub":"Remedies – Restitution and Reliance Where No Contract Exists","sensitivity":"The remedial track switches with the theory: restitution is measured by benefit to the defendant (a failed condition returns the deposit — no more); reliance/estoppel by the plaintiff's position (expenses), not expectation. Where the parties expressly negated obligations pre-signing, reliance during negotiations is generally NOT protected (cf.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “No contract, no claim” — or its inverse, claiming full expectation without a contract → both wrong; the claim survives but shrinks to benefit or reliance. The civilian knows enriquecimiento sin causa (C.C. art. 1,184) as a subsidiary action yet on U.S. exams keeps litigating formation; the tested skill is switching remedial tracks and matching each track to its own measure of recovery.","authority":"Restitution / reliance absent contract: Restatement (Second) §§ 370–371 (benefit conferred), § 90 (promissory estoppel; remedy limited as justice requires)."},{"n":56,"summary":"A breach (star cancels an appearance; supplier fails a flagship launch) causes loss of goodwill, reputation, or future business; the aggrieved party seeks those losses as damages.","trigger":"Breach + downstream loss of goodwill/reputation/future business + questions of foreseeability and proof.","rule":"Loss of goodwill and future profits are recoverable as consequential damages if foreseeable at formation and proved with reasonable certainty; doubts about amount are resolved against the breaching party once the fact of loss is established, but speculative losses fail (Restatement (Second) §§ 351–352).","sub":"Damages – Goodwill and Lost Profits (Certainty Requirement)","sensitivity":"Two stacked filters after Hadley (Unit 25): foreseeability at formation AND reasonable certainty of amount. The certainty screen — not foreseeability — is what defeats most goodwill claims (new ventures, projected attendance); established track records survive it.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Reputational harm is real, so it must be compensated — or it is moral damage, so it cannot be” → both instincts miss: goodwill is ordinary economic loss that passes or fails on proof. The civilian daño moral category (autonomous, judicially estimated) has no U.S. contract counterpart; framing goodwill as dignity-based rather than profit-based either inflates the claim or abandons it.","authority":"Goodwill / lost profits: Restatement (Second) §§ 351 (foreseeability), 352 (reasonable certainty)."},{"n":57,"summary":"Buyer repudiates; seller resells the goods (to a cousin at a token price; after proper notice at market; or cannot resell specially made goods except for salvage) and sues for the deficiency.","trigger":"Buyer breach + resale by the seller + questions of procedure (good faith, commercial reasonableness, notice) and of the correct damages formula.","rule":"Under UCC § 2-706 a seller who resells in good faith and in a commercially reasonable manner (with notice) recovers contract price minus resale price plus incidental damages minus expenses saved; a lost-volume seller or one left with specially manufactured goods recovers the lost profit plus costs under § 2-708(2), crediting salvage proceeds. Sellers recover incidental but never consequential damages (§ 2-710).","sub":"Remedies – Seller's Resale and Lost-Profit Damages (§§ 2-706, 2-708(2), 2-710)","sensitivity":"Three structural gates: (1) a resale that is not commercially reasonable (token price to a relative) forfeits the § 2-706 measure and remits the seller to market damages; (2) lost-volume/special-manufacture status unlocks the profit measure of § 2-708(2); (3) the seller-side bar on consequentials is absolute — a missed collateral deal is not compensable even if disclosed. Contrast the buyer, who recovers consequentials under § 2-715 (Unit 25).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The seller lost the deal and the follow-on profit, so full reparation follows” → recovery is formula-bound and asymmetric. The civilian full-reparation schema (all direct, foreseeable loss compensable regardless of the creditor's role) hides both the procedural discipline of resale and the buyer/seller asymmetry — structural features with no codal analogue.","authority":"Seller's remedies: UCC §§ 2-706 (resale in good faith, commercially reasonable, notice), 2-708(2) (lost profit; specially manufactured/lost volume), 2-710 (incidental damages; no seller consequentials)."},{"n":58,"summary":"A creditor assigns a loan or payment stream; the assignee demands performance on the original terms, ignoring a payment schedule or other modification the original parties had validly agreed before the assignment.","trigger":"Valid assignment of contractual rights + pre-assignment modifications or defenses + assignee insisting on stricter original terms.","rule":"An assignee stands in the assignor's shoes: it takes the rights as they exist at assignment, subject to all valid modifications already made and to defenses the obligor could assert against the assignor; until the obligor receives notice of the assignment, performance to the assignor discharges the debt (Restatement (Second) §§ 317, 336, 338).","sub":"Third Parties – Assignment (Assignee Takes Subject to Modifications and Defenses)","sensitivity":"Two clocks control: (1) the content of the right is frozen as of the assignment — earlier modifications bind the assignee, and post-notice modifications generally do not; (2) notice to the obligor switches to whom performance is safely rendered. The assignee's expectations are structurally irrelevant; cf. Unit 36 (what may be assigned) — this unit governs what the assignee gets.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The contract says payment on the 1st, and the bank bought the contract” → the bank bought the claim as modified. The civilian cesión de créditos schema (C.C. arts. 1,549 ff.) makes notification a condition of the assignment's effectiveness against the debtor, so the attorney over-weights notice and under-weights the shoes principle — in U.S. analysis notice only protects payment direction; the modification question is answered by timing, not formality.","authority":"Assignment: Restatement (Second) §§ 317 (effectiveness), 336 (defenses and modifications), 338 (discharge; notice to obligor)."},{"n":59,"summary":"An employer seeks to enforce a covenant not to compete that is excessive in duration or geography (a 5-year bar on practicing law; a nationwide ban on a local trade).","trigger":"Restrictive covenant ancillary to employment or sale of a business + scope broader than needed to protect a legitimate interest.","rule":"Courts enforce the parties' intent unless it violates public policy; a noncompete is enforceable only if reasonable — no broader than necessary to protect a legitimate interest (trade secrets, goodwill), not unduly burdensome on the employee, and not injurious to the public. Facing an unreasonable covenant, jurisdictions split: strict blue-pencil deletion, equitable reformation to reasonable scope (modern trend), or refusal to enforce (Restatement (Second) §§ 187–188).","sub":"Public Policy – Covenants Not to Compete (Reasonableness; Reformation)","sensitivity":"The analysis is interest-indexed, not text-indexed: the same clause survives for a business seller and fails for a junior employee. The remedial fork (blue pencil / reform / void) is jurisdiction-dependent and must be flagged, not assumed; professional-responsibility rules independently bar most lawyer noncompetes.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The clause is what the parties signed — valid or void as written” → U.S. courts may rewrite it. The civilian validity schema is binary (lícito/ilícito under code norms), so the reasonableness balancing and, above all, the judicial power to reform a private covenant to the maximum enforceable scope — saving a partially illegal clause — sit outside the trained model of judicial intervention.","authority":"Noncompetes / public policy: Restatement (Second) §§ 187–188 (reasonableness; legitimate interest); jurisdictional split on blue-pencil vs. reformation vs. voiding."},{"n":60,"summary":"A subcontract states the sub 'will be paid when/if the owner pays the general contractor'; the owner becomes insolvent and never pays; the GC refuses to pay the sub.","trigger":"Payment clause tied to a third party's payment + doubt whether the clause is a condition or a timing promise.","rule":"Doubtful clauses are construed as promises rather than conditions in order to avoid forfeiture; absent unmistakable intent to shift the owner-insolvency risk, a pay-when-paid clause merely fixes a reasonable time for payment, and the contractor must pay within a reasonable time even if never paid (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 227).","sub":"Conditions – Pay-When-Paid / Promise vs. Condition (§ 227)","sensitivity":"The interpretive default is outcome-determinative: unmistakable conditional language → risk on the subcontractor (strict compliance, Unit 20); doubtful language → promise, risk stays on the GC. Who drafted the clause and the forfeiture consequence drive the construction.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The clause says when the owner pays — the owner never paid, so nothing is owed” → construed as a timing promise; the GC must pay within a reasonable time. The civilian reads the clause literally as a condición suspensiva (C.C. arts. 1,197 ff.) whose non-occurrence simply means no debt; U.S. courts apply an anti-forfeiture preference (§ 227) that reverses the literal reading unless the condition is unmistakable.","authority":"Pay-when-paid / promise vs. condition: Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 227 (interpretive preference reducing obligee's risk of forfeiture); cf. § 229 (excuse to avoid disproportionate forfeiture)."},{"n":61,"summary":"After an act or benefit already received (a favor, a completed service, property destroyed by a borrower), the beneficiary promises compensation and later refuses to pay.","trigger":"Promise made after the benefit/act + no bargained-for exchange at the time of the act.","rule":"Past consideration is no consideration: a promise made in recognition of a benefit previously received is generally unenforceable, subject to narrow exceptions — the material-benefit rule (§ 86) and written revival of time-barred debts (§ 82).","sub":"Consideration – Past Consideration (§ 86)","sensitivity":"Temporal sequencing of bargain and act is dispositive: the identical promise is enforceable if exchanged for the act in advance. The exceptions are themselves structural: § 86 requires a material benefit to the promisor (not a gift, not disproportionate); § 82 requires a writing (cf. Contracts Units 1–2).","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “He promised, and there was a real benefit — moral duty makes it binding” → unenforceable for lack of a bargained-for exchange. Civil-law causa accommodates moral consideration and natural obligations (obligaciones naturales) as bases of enforceability; the U.S. bargain test rejects them, so the trained instinct systematically validates promises U.S. law will not enforce.","authority":"Past consideration: Restatement (Second) § 86 (promise for benefit received; material-benefit rule and its gift/disproportion limits); § 82 (written revival of time-barred debts; cf. the Contracts module Units 1–2)."},{"n":62,"summary":"The parties contract under an erroneous belief about a basic factual assumption (both mistaken), or only one party is mistaken (a bid error; a misunderstanding of what the price includes).","trigger":"Mistake existing at formation + basic assumption + material effect + who bears the risk (and, if unilateral, the other party's knowledge).","rule":"A mutual mistake as to a basic assumption with a material effect makes the contract voidable by the adversely affected party unless that party bears the risk (§§ 152, 154); a unilateral mistake does so only if, in addition, enforcement would be unconscionable or the other party knew, had reason to know of, or caused the mistake (§ 153).","sub":"Defenses – Mistake (Mutual vs. Unilateral, §§ 152–154)","sensitivity":"Two switches: (1) the number of mistaken parties — unilateral mistake adds an extra gate (knowledge or unconscionability); (2) risk allocation under § 154 — conscious ignorance or contractual assumption of risk defeats avoidance even for mutual mistake. Mistakes in the written record go to reformation instead (Unit 63).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “There was an error, so consent was vitiated — the contract is void” → voidable at most, and only through the §§ 152–153 gates. The civilian vicios del consentimiento schema (error, C.C. arts. 1,146 ff.) interrogates the quality of consent; U.S. doctrine interrogates the allocation of risk between the parties — the analysis relocates from the mind to the bargain.","authority":"Mistake: Restatement (Second) §§ 152 (mutual), 153 (unilateral), 154 (when a party bears the risk; conscious ignorance)."},{"n":63,"summary":"The parties reach a clear oral agreement, but the writing omits or misstates a term through a drafting error neither party notices; one party later insists on the writing as signed.","trigger":"Antecedent agreement + writing that fails to express it because of a mistake of both parties in reducing it to writing.","rule":"Where a writing fails to express the agreement actually reached because of a mutual mistake in its recording, the court may reform the writing to conform to the true agreement; the parol evidence rule does not bar the proof needed for reformation (Restatement (Second) § 155).","sub":"Defenses – Mistake in Integration / Reformation (§ 155)","sensitivity":"Three mistake tracks must be kept separate: mistake about facts → avoidance (Unit 62); mistake about the writing → reformation (this unit); dispute about meaning of an ambiguous term → interpretation (Units 26–27). Reformation coexists with a merger clause and requires clear and convincing evidence.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The signed text governs — nothing outside it can change it” → the document itself is corrected to match the real deal. Trained on the sanctity of the instrument and on simulación doctrines, the civilian either over-trusts the writing or reaches for nullity; the U.S. remedy is surgical judicial rewriting of the document — a power with no direct codal analogue.","authority":"Reformation: Restatement (Second) § 155 (mutual mistake as to the writing); proof by clear and convincing evidence; the parol evidence rule does not bar evidence offered for reformation."},{"n":64,"summary":"A seller makes a false statement — knowingly or innocently — that induces the contract (e.g., low mileage repeated from a rolled-back odometer the reseller knew nothing about); the buyer seeks rescission.","trigger":"False assertion + inducement + justifiable reliance + assertion either fraudulent OR material.","rule":"A contract is voidable by the induced party if a misrepresentation was either fraudulent (known to be false, intended to induce) or material (likely to induce a reasonable person), even where the speaker honestly believed it; rescission with mutual restitution follows (Restatement (Second) § 164).","sub":"Defenses – Misrepresentation (Fraudulent vs. Material, § 164)","sensitivity":"The disjunctive is the trap: innocent misstatements avoid the contract if material — scienter is unnecessary for rescission (it matters for tort damages, not avoidance). Satellites: expert opinion as actionable assertion (Unit 43); duty to correct assertions that become misleading (Contracts Unit 18).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The reseller believed it himself — no dolo, no remedy” → rescission lies for a material innocent misrepresentation. The civilian schema requires dolo (intentional deception, C.C. art. 1,154) or channels the case into error; the U.S. ‘fraudulent OR material’ disjunctive grants relief precisely where the dolo schema denies it.","authority":"Misrepresentation: Restatement (Second) § 164 (fraudulent OR material; voidable); § 162 (definitions); cf. §§ 168–169 (opinion, Unit 43) and § 161 (nondisclosure, the Contracts module Unit 18)."},{"n":65,"summary":"A breaching party fails to complete promised work (land restoration, repairs); completing it would cost far more than the value it would add; the owner sues for the full cost of repair.","trigger":"Defective or incomplete performance + cost to remedy grossly disproportionate to the resulting difference in value.","rule":"Damages are normally the cost to complete or repair, but where that cost is clearly disproportionate to the probable loss in value, damages are limited to the diminution in value — unless the omitted performance was a central purpose of the contract or the breach was in bad faith (Restatement (Second) § 348(2)).","sub":"Damages – Cost to Repair vs. Diminution in Value (Economic Waste)","sensitivity":"Two escape valves reverse the disproportion cap: contractual centrality of the promised work (restoration bargained for as a key term — e.g., to build rental units on the land) and the breacher's bad faith. Identical numbers produce opposite awards depending on those qualitative findings.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “He promised to restore the land, so he pays what restoring costs” → capped at diminution unless centrality or bad faith is shown. The civilian reparación integral and cumplimiento en especie instincts (cf. Unit 28) resist the efficiency cap; conversely, once the cap is learned, the reasoner tends to miss the two exceptions that restore full cost-of-repair recovery.","authority":"Economic waste: Restatement (Second) § 348(2) (diminution in value where cost of completion is clearly disproportionate); Peevyhouse v. Garland Coal & Mining Co., 382 P.2d 109 (Okla. 1962); Groves v. John Wunder Co., 286 N.W. 235 (Minn. 1939)."},{"n":66,"summary":"After a breach (wrongful discharge, failed delivery), the aggrieved party spends money seeking substitutes, declines an inferior replacement job, and/or earns income from other work.","trigger":"Breach + losses avoidable by reasonable post-breach conduct + substitute transactions or mitigation expenses.","rule":"Damages are not recoverable for loss the injured party could have avoided without undue risk, burden, or humiliation (Restatement (Second) § 350). An employee need not accept different or inferior employment, but earnings actually received from substitute work reduce the award; and reasonable expenses incurred in mitigation efforts are recoverable even if those efforts fail.","sub":"Damages – Avoidability / Mitigation (§ 350)","sensitivity":"Three sub-rules answer three distinct fact patterns: (1) the comparable-employment standard defines what must be accepted; (2) actual earnings offset the award even where the job did not have to be taken; (3) mitigation costs are add-ons, never deductions. Consequential damages are barred to the extent avoidable (cf. Unit 25).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The victim must take whatever work exists” — or its mirror, “money spent after the breach is the victim's own problem” → both wrong, in opposite directions. Civil codes state a general good-faith principle but no articulated mitigation doctrine (Venezuelan law lacks an express carga de mitigar el daño); the three-part U.S. structure — what must be accepted, what offsets, what is reimbursed — must be learned as rules, not intuited as equity.","authority":"Mitigation: Restatement (Second) § 350 (avoidability as a limitation; reasonable effort standard; recovery of mitigation expenses)."},{"n":67,"summary":"A party in breach (a worker who quits mid-term; a defaulting buyer who paid an earnest-money deposit) conferred value before breaching and sues to recover some of it.","trigger":"Plaintiff is the breaching party + benefit conferred on the non-breacher + benefit exceeds the damages caused by the breach.","rule":"A party in breach may recover restitution for benefit conferred in excess of the loss its own breach caused (Restatement (Second) § 374); a defaulting buyer recovers the deposit only to the extent it exceeds the seller's actual damages or a valid liquidated-damages amount.","sub":"Restitution – Recovery by the Party in Breach (§ 374)","sensitivity":"The measure is a netting: benefit conferred minus damages caused. Under the traditional rule a willful breacher recovers nothing (jurisdictions split; the Restatement allows recovery). A valid liquidated-damages clause displaces the netting entirely. Contrast § 373 restitution for the NON-breacher (Unit 37).","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “He breached — he forfeits everything” (or the mirror: “work performed must always be paid”) → both absolutes miss the netting calculation. The civilian arras schema (C.C. art. 1,263: arras forfeited or returned doubled) validates forfeiture as such; § 374 instead runs an unjust-enrichment computation — benefit minus loss — that the trained schema never performs.","authority":"Restitution for the party in breach: Restatement (Second) § 374; traditional willful-breach bar (jurisdictional split); cf. § 373 (Unit 37) and liquidated damages § 356 (Unit 38)."},{"n":68,"summary":"An oral sale of goods ≥ $500 is challenged under the Statute of Frauds, but the goods were specially manufactured, or the defendant admitted the deal in court papers, or payment/delivery was accepted.","trigger":"Oral contract within UCC § 2-201 + specially manufactured goods, judicial admission, or part performance.","rule":"An oral contract otherwise barred is enforceable (a) for goods specially manufactured for the buyer and unsuitable for sale to others, once the seller has made a substantial beginning before notice of repudiation; (b) against a party who admits the contract in pleadings or testimony, up to the quantity admitted; and (c) to the extent payment has been made and accepted or goods have been received and accepted (UCC § 2-201(3)).","sub":"Statute of Frauds – UCC Exceptions (§ 2-201(3))","sensitivity":"Each exception carries its own cap or trigger: substantial beginning must precede notice of repudiation; the admission exception is capped at the quantity admitted; part performance validates only the portion performed. Cf. the merchant's confirmatory memo (Contracts Unit 14) and the party-to-be-charged signature rule (Unit 34).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “No writing, no contract — the custom sign shop loses” → the specially manufactured sign is enforceable once substantially made. Trained to treat form requirements as near-absolute wherever imposed, the civilian misses that the U.S. SOF is a defense riddled with performance-based exceptions — they function as evidence substitutes, not as formality for its own sake.","authority":"UCC Statute of Frauds exceptions: UCC § 2-201(3)(a) (specially manufactured goods; substantial beginning), (3)(b) (judicial admission), (3)(c) (part performance); cf. § 2-201(2) (merchant's memo, the Contracts module Unit 14)."},{"n":69,"summary":"After a seller's breach, the buyer either purchases substitute goods at a higher price (cover) or keeps nonconforming goods after timely notice and sues for the shortfall.","trigger":"Seller breach + buyer covers in good faith, OR buyer accepts nonconforming goods and gives timely notice of breach (§ 2-607(3)(a)).","rule":"A covering buyer recovers the cover price minus the contract price, plus incidental and consequential damages, less expenses saved (UCC § 2-712). A buyer who accepts nonconforming goods and gives timely notice recovers the difference between the value of the goods as accepted and as warranted, plus incidentals and consequentials (§§ 2-714, 2-715). Attorney's fees are not recoverable as damages (American Rule).","sub":"UCC Buyer's Remedies – Cover (§ 2-712) / Warranty Damages (§ 2-714)","sensitivity":"Track selection is structural: rejection and cover operate before acceptance; § 2-714 operates after acceptance and is gated by § 2-607(3)(a) notice — silence within a reasonable time forfeits all remedies. Cover must be reasonable and in good faith, not necessarily the cheapest. Contrast the seller's remedy set and its consequential-damages bar (Unit 57).","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “He accepted the goods, so he waived any claim” — or “add the lawyer's bill to the damages” → acceptance plus timely notice preserves full warranty damages, and attorney's fees are never damages. The civilian saneamiento/vicios ocultos schema (C.C. arts. 1,518 ff.) channels defect claims through redhibitory actions with short prescriptive windows, and fee-shifting is routine in civil procedure — both instincts misfire on U.S. exam facts.","authority":"Buyer's remedies: UCC §§ 2-712 (cover), 2-714 (damages after acceptance), 2-715 (incidental/consequential), 2-607(3)(a) (notice requirement); American Rule on attorney's fees."},{"n":70,"summary":"A single contract mixes goods and services (equipment plus installation; a remodel including fixtures; training plus materials); the parties dispute whether the UCC or the common law governs.","trigger":"Hybrid goods/services contract + need to select the governing body of law.","rule":"The governing law is chosen by the contract's predominant purpose: if the transaction is predominantly a sale of goods with services incidental, the UCC governs the entire contract; if predominantly services, the common law governs. A minority 'gravamen' approach instead asks whether the complaint arises from the goods or the services component.","sub":"Scope – Mixed Contracts / Predominant Purpose Test","sensitivity":"This unit is the master switch for the UCC/common-law fork that drives much of the module: perfect tender vs. substantial performance (Contracts Unit 8 / Unit 21), modification with or without consideration (Contracts Unit 5 / Unit 30), firm offers vs. options (Contracts Unit 12 / Unit 41), and the remedy sets (Units 57, 69). Misclassifying the contract cascades into every downstream rule.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “A contract is a contract — the same rules apply” → the first question on any U.S. contracts issue is WHICH body of law governs. Civil codes regulate obligations unitarily (one Código Civil, with a Código de Comercio keyed to merchant status and actos de comercio); selecting the governing doctrine by the object of the transaction — goods vs. services — with opposite rules downstream is the single most consequential structural habit the framework must install.","authority":"Mixed contracts: predominant purpose test (majority; e.g., Bonebrake v. Cox, 499 F.2d 951 (8th Cir. 1974)); minority gravamen-of-the-complaint approach; UCC § 2-102 (scope)."},{"n":71,"summary":"A deed or contract is delivered complete except for one blank, with oral authority to fill it in one of two specified ways. The recipient instead fills in something else entirely and records it; the drafter sues, and the recipient invokes the parol evidence rule to keep the oral limitation out.","trigger":"Integrated writing + prior or contemporaneous oral understanding + the oral matter is offered NOT to vary an agreed term but to show fraud, alteration, or want of authority to complete the instrument.","rule":"The parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements offered to CONTRADICT or VARY the terms of an integrated writing (Restatement (Second) §§ 213–218; UCC § 2-202). It does not bar evidence offered for a different purpose: to show fraud, duress, mistake, illegality or any other invalidating cause; to show a condition precedent to the writing's effectiveness; to interpret ambiguous language; to prove a separate consistent collateral agreement where the writing is only partially integrated; or to establish grounds for reformation. Nor does the rule validate an instrument never authorized in the form asserted: where a writing is delivered incomplete with authority to complete it in a specified way, completion beyond that authority is an unauthorized alteration, and evidence of the limited authority is admissible because it goes to whether an operative instrument exists at all rather than to varying its terms.","sub":"Parol Evidence – Authority to Complete a Blank; Alteration Beyond Authority","sensitivity":"The boundary is the PURPOSE of the offer, never the content of the oral matter. Offered to change what the writing says → barred. Offered to show the writing is void, was never authorized, or means something other than its literal words → admitted. The blank-completion pattern is the sharpest test of the line: the identical oral statement ('only Lot 40 or Lot 41') is inadmissible to rewrite a validly executed deed and admissible to show that the deed as completed was never authorized. Builds on Units 26–27 (integration and the exceptions map). Parol evidence is a Contracts and Property doctrine, not a rule of evidence.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “there is a signed instrument, so testimony about the earlier oral term is inadmissible — art. 1.387 C.C. forbids proving against a document by witnesses” → the two rules filter on different axes. The Venezuelan rule restricts the KIND of proof (testimony) largely regardless of the purpose; the U.S. rule restricts the PURPOSE (contradicting an integrated term) regardless of the kind of proof. Translating one as the other over-excludes precisely where these facts require admission, because fraud, alteration and want of authority sit outside the rule's scope entirely — as the civilian's own tacha de falsedad and simulación doctrines would recognize.","authority":"Parol evidence limits: Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 213–218 (integration and its exceptions), § 214 (evidence admissible on invalidity, interpretation, and conditions); UCC § 2-202; unauthorized completion of a blank as alteration rather than as a variance of terms."}],"cases":[{"n":1,"summary":"Owner conditioned payment on an architect’s certificate. Architect refused to issue it due to minor defects despite substantial completion. Builder sought payment anyway.","trigger":"Express third-party satisfaction condition not met.","rule":"Substantial performance does not excuse the nonoccurrence of an express condition, and when a third party’s satisfaction is required, the condition must be fulfilled before payment is due.","sub":"Conditions Express","family":"Conditions","unit":20},{"n":2,"summary":"Neighbor installed fence after thumbs-up; man watched installers and said nothing; refused to pay","trigger":"Silence as acceptance when offeree takes benefit of services","rule":"An implied in fact contract is a contract formed by the party’s actions or by silence. Silence can be acceptance where an offeree takes the benefit of offered services with reasonable opportunity to reject them and reason to know that they were offered with the expectation of compensation.","sub":"Acceptance (Implied)","family":"Formation","unit":51},{"n":3,"summary":"Bathroom remodel met minimum tub specs; homeowner disputed and tendered $12k in full; contractor accepted then sued for $3k","trigger":"Accord and satisfaction where amount owed is disputed in good faith","rule":"A lesser amount offered as payment in full can be binding at common law if the offeror has a good faith, reasonable dispute over how much was owed, even if he was wrong.","sub":"Accord & Satisfaction","family":"Accord & Satisfaction","unit":7},{"n":4,"summary":"On March 1, an owner entered into a written contract to sell her car to a buyer for $15,000, with delivery and payment to occur on March 6. The buyer had previously declined to enter into an option contract to hold the owner’s offer open until March 6. On March 3, the buyer emailed the owner to say that he might not have the $15,000 by March 6.","trigger":"Buyer signals inability to pay before due date","rule":"Clear inability or refusal to perform before due date is anticipatory repudiation.","sub":"Anticipatory repudiation","family":"Breach","unit":23},{"n":5,"summary":"Couple reserves a hotel honeymoon suite without paying a deposit. Severe weather threatens to cancel their flight; hotel manager urgently asks whether to keep the reservation and demands immediate payment/assurance so he can re-rent if they won’t arrive. Couple says they’re unsure and will decide by next morning; manager rents the suite to someone else, and the couple arrives to find it unavailable and pays $800 more elsewhere.","trigger":"Demand for adequate assurances is made in a common-law contract context; issue is whether the demand must be in writing.","rule":"Under common law, a demand for assurances may be oral.","sub":"Anticipatory Repudiation – Demand for adequate assurances (common law)","family":"Breach","unit":24},{"n":6,"summary":"Criminal defendant hires lawyer, who promises to call Monday with defense theory. Lawyer works 20 hours but doesn’t call; client leaves a voicemail expressing concern, then fires lawyer and hires another, seeking refund.","trigger":"Failure to give adequate assurance after a reasonable request may amount to anticipatory repudiation.","rule":"Under common law, failure to provide assurance to a reasonable request for assurances turns into an anticipatory repudiation.","sub":"Anticipatory Repudiation / Adequate Assurance","family":"Breach","unit":24},{"n":7,"summary":"Tour guide reports serious ankle sprain and uncertainty; tourist demands commitment within 24 hours, then hires replacement after 2 days. Original guide later assures he’ll be healed and claims tourist breached.","trigger":"Common-law demand for adequate assurances must allow a reasonable time to respond; premature cover can be breach by demanding party.","rule":"Under common law, a person has a reasonable time to give adequate assurances.","sub":"Anticipatory Repudiation / Adequate Assurances","family":"Breach","unit":24},{"n":8,"summary":"Farmer has 5-year requirements K with grocery; later assigns right to receive payments to his son despite clause “any assignment of this contract is prohibited”; only payee changes.","trigger":"Under UCC 2-210, “no assignment of the contract” is read as barring delegation, not assignment of payment rights.","rule":"Under UCC 2-210, unless the circumstances indicate the contrary, a prohibition of assignment “of the contract” is to be construed as barring only the delegation of performance.","sub":"Assignment & Delegation – Anti-assignment Clauses / Payment Rights","family":"Assignment & Delegation","unit":36},{"n":9,"summary":"Baker contracted with a particular farmer for eggs; farmer retired and assigned performance to friend despite anti-assignment clause","trigger":"Attempted assignment/delegation despite contract clause prohibiting assignment and stating contract becomes void","rule":"Under UCC 2-210, an anti-assignment clause of the contract, unless circumstances indicate to the contrary, is to be construed only as barring delegation.","sub":"Assignment & Delegation (UCC)","family":"Assignment & Delegation","unit":36},{"n":10,"summary":"Party to a contract expresses uncertainty about future performance (e.g., 'I’ll try' or 'I’m not sure I can'), and the other party treats it as a breach and attempts to cancel immediately.","trigger":"Anticipatory repudiation requires a clear, unequivocal refusal to perform; equivocal statements trigger a right to demand assurances instead.","rule":"Anticipatory repudiation requires an unequivocal refusal to perform; mere equivocation / “I’ll try” is not AR (→ you’d have to demand assurances rather than cancel immediately).","sub":"Breach – Anticipatory repudiation vs demand for assurances","family":"Breach","unit":23},{"n":11,"summary":"A 14-year-old calls a satellite dish store and hires installation for his father’s dish, promising not to disaffirm. Technician installs. Father refuses to pay, and the minor later disaffirms.","trigger":"Minor’s contract; effect of promise not to disaffirm; liability for non-necessary services.","rule":"A minor may disaffirm a contract despite a promise not to do so; the minor is liable in quasi-contract only for necessaries (reasonable value), not for non-necessary services.","sub":"Capacity – Minors; disaffirmance / necessaries","family":"Capacity","unit":17},{"n":12,"summary":"Contractor agrees in writing to build a basement bedroom for $35,000. Asbestos is discovered, but it would have been found on a reasonable pre‑inspection. Contractor demands an extra $15,000; homeowner agrees but later refuses to pay more than $35,000.","trigger":"Common‑law modification requires new consideration if circumstances were reasonably foreseeable.","rule":"At common law, a valid modification requires additional or different consideration.","sub":"Common Law – Modification / Pre‑existing Duty","family":"Modification","unit":5},{"n":13,"summary":"Couple’s mortgage is conditioned on bank completing loan docs within 7 days and time is of the essence, but delay is caused by the couple’s own failure to timely provide tax documents. Bank is ready on day 8; couple tries to walk away on the timing clause.","trigger":"A party who prevents a condition from occurring cannot rely on its non‑occurrence.","rule":"If a party prevents a condition from being met with their failure to cooperate, the condition will be excused.","sub":"Conditions – Excuse by Prevention of Occurrence of the Condition","family":"Conditions","unit":54},{"n":14,"summary":"Bathroom renovation contract for $22,000 drafted by the owner is silent on when payment is due. Contractor asks for $11,000 halfway through; owner refuses to pay until completion.","trigger":"If performances cannot be simultaneous, the party whose performance takes longer must go first.","rule":"Under the Restatement, if no term is supplied as to timing of performance, if the parties cannot perform at the same time, whichever party’s performance takes longest must perform first.","sub":"Conditions – Order of Performance","family":"Conditions","unit":53},{"n":15,"summary":"Plumber’s $8,000 payment is conditioned on general contractor first being paid by customer; customer later goes bankrupt and never pays; plumber sues GC.","trigger":"Courts prefer to read doubtful clauses as promises rather than conditions to avoid forfeiture.","rule":"Whether a clause is a condition or not depends on the intent of the parties. If intent is not clear, the courts will look at the language used, who drafted the contract, and they will also prefer a promise over a condition since it would result in less forfeiture.","sub":"Conditions – Pay-When-Paid / Conditions vs Promises","family":"Conditions","unit":60},{"n":16,"summary":"A contract calls for performance by a certain date, and one party performs late. The dispute turns on whether the late performance is a breach or is still acceptable under the agreement.","trigger":"Late performance is a breach only if the contract makes time 'of the essence' (express condition); otherwise a small delay may not be a breach.","rule":"Whether late performance is a breach turns on whether contract language makes time “of the essence” (an express condition); if time is of the essence, late = breach even if close/faithful.","sub":"Conditions – Time is of the essence / express condition","family":"Performance","unit":22},{"n":17,"summary":"A contract includes a condition precedent that must occur before the other party’s performance duty arises. The party for whose benefit the condition exists later acts inconsistently with insisting on the condition, and the issue is whether the condition was waived such that performance is still required.","trigger":"Condition precedent not satisfied but potentially waived by beneficiary of the condition.","rule":"A waiver is an intentional relinquishment of a known right. A condition precedent has to be met before the other party’s duty to perform is activated, however, if the condition is waived, the party will have to perform even if the condition was not met.","sub":"Conditions — Waiver of Condition Precedent","family":"Conditions","unit":47},{"n":18,"summary":"Payment conditioned on brother-in-law inspection; man paid twice anyway then stopped payment","trigger":"Condition precedent and waiver by paying without inspection","rule":"A waiver is an intentional relinquishment of a known right. A condition precedent has to be met before the other party’s duty to perform is activated, however, if the condition is waived, the party will have to perform even if the condition was not met.","sub":"Conditions & Waiver","family":"Conditions","unit":47},{"n":19,"summary":"Woman prepays $400 for a female kitten from a specific upcoming litter; contract says birth of a female kitten is a condition precedent and money is refunded if no female is born. Only males are born; breeder lies and sells a male as female.","trigger":"Condition precedent not satisfied means the duty to pay never arises.","rule":"If a condition precedent is not met, the party’s duty under the contract is never activated and there will be no contract.","sub":"Conditions / Fraud","family":"Conditions","unit":20},{"n":20,"summary":"Owner drafted a construction contract conditioning payment on issuance of an architect’s certificate by June 1, with time of the essence. Builder finished early, but the architect failed to issue the certificate through no fault of Owner.","trigger":"Express condition precedent to payment not satisfied.","rule":"When an express condition precedent to payment has not occurred and the failure was not caused by the obligor, the duty to perform does not arise.","sub":"Conditions Precedent","family":"Conditions","unit":20},{"n":21,"summary":"Contractor hired Electrician under a clause stating Electrician would not be paid unless Contractor was paid by Owner. Owner’s insurance failed and Owner filed bankruptcy. Contractor refused to pay Electrician.","trigger":"Contract language suggests a condition but intent shows timing of payment.","rule":"When contract language appears conditional, courts look to the parties’ intent, and absent clear intent to create forfeiture, a pay-when-paid clause is construed as a promise fixing the time of payment, not a condition precedent.","sub":"Conditions vs Promises","family":"Conditions","unit":60},{"n":22,"summary":"Bank wired buyer’s funds to wrong account; seller sold land to someone else; buyer sued bank","trigger":"Consequential losses claimed from bank’s failed wire transfer","rule":"Consequential damages must be either known or foreseeable at the time of contract to be recoverable.","sub":"Consequential Damages","family":"Damages","unit":25},{"n":23,"summary":"While pregnant, a woman breaks up with her boyfriend and moves in with her brother, sister‑in‑law, and their kids. The brother tells her that she and the baby can live with them rent‑free through her maternity leave. While the woman is in the hospital after having the baby, the brother tells her he changed his mind and she and the baby cannot live at his house. The question asks whether the brother is contractually obligated to let her live there through maternity leave.","trigger":"Family promise to provide housing rent-free; analyze whether there is a bargained‑for exchange (consideration) versus a gratuitous promise.","rule":"A gratuitous promise is not enforceable as a contract without consideration; without a bargained‑for exchange, the promise to provide housing is not contractually binding.","sub":"Consideration – Gratuitous promises","family":"Consideration","unit":4},{"n":24,"summary":"Debtor owes bank $10k due June 1; bank orally agrees to accept $1k/month for 10 months (no interest) from buyer; then bank refuses and demands full amount; sues debtor.","trigger":"Common-law modification extending time/accepting installments generally requires consideration; without it, creditor can enforce original obligation.","rule":"Under common law, a promise to accept installments / extend time without new consideration is generally not binding, so the creditor can demand the original due performance.","sub":"Consideration - Modification / Common law (consideration required)","family":"Modification","unit":5},{"n":25,"summary":"A Student borrows $500 from his Roommate with an agreement to repay with interest in one year. The Student never repays. The jurisdiction’s SOL for breach of contract is three years from the agreement date. Ten years later at a reunion, the Student tells the Roommate: “I am so sorry I forgot to pay you back, but I promise I will send you a check to cover the unpaid debt as soon as I get back home on Monday.” He never sends the check, and the Roommate sues for the $500.","trigger":"Debt is time-barred + debtor later makes only an oral “I promise I’ll pay” statement + no new consideration/writing → not enforceable.","rule":"A promise to pay a debt barred by the statute of limitations is unenforceable absent new consideration (or a required writing/recognized revival rule); a mere oral promise is not enough.","sub":"Consideration – No Revival because promise is oral","family":"Consideration","unit":2},{"n":26,"summary":"Owner promised to pay for replacement dog after friend killed it using borrowed mower","trigger":"Promise made after the event without bargained-for exchange","rule":"A promise based solely on past consideration is unenforceable for lack of consideration","sub":"Consideration – Pre-existing Duty","family":"Consideration","unit":61},{"n":27,"summary":"Mechanic has two contracts with neighbor ($500 repair + $3,000 used car); after full performance, he agrees to knock $200 off each; later seeks remaining $400.","trigger":"Pre-existing duty rule bars satisfying full obligation by promising to accept less on an undisputed debt.","rule":"Under the pre-existing duty rule, a person cannot satisfy his entire obligation by agreeing to pay less.","sub":"Consideration – Pre-existing Duty / Partial Payment of Debt","family":"Modification","unit":5},{"n":28,"summary":"An accountant and a bookkeeper agree (as part of dissolving their business) that each will contribute $100,000 to fund an annuity for a longtime clerk who will lose his job due to the dissolution. After being told of the plan, the clerk relies by purchasing a retirement home. The accountant later contributes his $100,000, but the bookkeeper says he can only contribute $50,000 and the accountant agrees; the clerk sues the bookkeeper for the unpaid $50,000.","trigger":"Promise induces reasonable detrimental reliance; promisor cannot later reduce promised performance after reliance (rights vest).","rule":"Promissory estoppel: a promise is enforceable when the promisee reasonably and detrimentally relies; rights vest upon reliance, and the promisor can’t later reduce the promised performance.","sub":"Consideration - Promissory Estoppel – Reliance","family":"Consideration","unit":3},{"n":29,"summary":"An uncle writes to his niece: “I promise to give you a champion purebred puppy when you graduate college.” She is inspired, enrolls, takes loans, graduates, and the uncle refuses.","trigger":"Gratuitous promise + foreseeable reliance; whether promissory estoppel makes it enforceable.","rule":"A promise is enforceable under promissory estoppel when the promisor should reasonably expect reliance, the promisee actually and detrimentally relies, and enforcement is necessary to avoid injustice.","sub":"Consideration – Reliance on a promise","family":"Consideration","unit":3},{"n":30,"summary":"A debtor owed a creditor $4,000 from several years ago for a driveway that the creditor paved for him. The debt had long been barred by the statute of limitations. After the debtor started receiving a steadier income, he sent a letter to the creditor, offering to settle the debt in full for $1,000. The creditor, knowing that the debt had become uncollectible, agreed to the reduced amount. Does the debtor have to pay the creditor $1,000?","trigger":"Written promise to pay debt after SOL expired; enforceability/revival.","rule":"A written promise to pay a debt that is barred by the statute of limitations is sufficient consideration to enter into an agreement for a new binding debt.","sub":"Consideration – Revival of time-barred debt by written promise","family":"Consideration","unit":1},{"n":31,"summary":"A debtor owed money on an old loan, but the statute of limitations has run. The debtor later signs a written promise to pay part (or all) of the debt, and the lender sues to enforce the promise even though no new consideration was given.","trigger":"Debt is time-barred by statute of limitations, but the debtor makes a written acknowledgment/promise to pay; enforceability turns on revival by written promise.","rule":"A written promise to pay all or part of a debt otherwise barred by the statute of limitations is enforceable to the extent promised, even without new consideration.","sub":"Consideration / Revival of time-barred debt by written promise","family":"Consideration","unit":1},{"n":32,"summary":"The parties’ conduct indicates they intended to form an agreement even though they did not use express words of contract formation. The issue is whether an implied-in-fact contract arose based on the parties’ actions and surrounding circumstances.","trigger":"Agreement inferred from conduct (implied-in-fact contract).","rule":"An implied in fact contract is a contract formed by the party’s actions.","sub":"Contract Formation — Implied-in-Fact Contract","family":"Formation","unit":51},{"n":33,"summary":"First-time landlord friend drafts lease heavily favoring himself; lease silent on house guest frequency; parties ask court to decide.","trigger":"Missing term in contract/lease → court uses gap fillers to supply reasonable term.","rule":"Although a court may interpret an ambiguous term against the party who drafted the contract, a missing term will be construed by using gap fillers.","sub":"Contract Interpretation / Gap Fillers","family":"Interpretation","unit":52},{"n":34,"summary":"Two parties dispute the meaning or enforceability of an agreement. The issue is whether a court will enforce the parties’ expressed intent in their contract terms, and when a court will refuse enforcement because the agreement violates public policy.","trigger":"Contract enforcement vs public policy limits.","rule":"The court will honor the intent of the parties unless it violates public policy.","sub":"Contract Law — Intent vs Public Policy","family":"Public Policy","unit":59},{"n":35,"summary":"Buyer repudiated contract for specially manufactured shirts; seller resold for salvage value","trigger":"Seller resold custom goods at low salvage price after breach","rule":"Seller may recover lost profits plus costs, with credit for resale proceeds where appropriate.","sub":"Damages","family":"Remedies","unit":57},{"n":36,"summary":"Parties litigate a breach of contract case. The prevailing party seeks to recover attorney’s fees from the other side even though the contract does not provide for fee shifting and no statute applies. The issue is whether attorney’s fees are recoverable as damages.","trigger":"Prevailing party seeks attorney’s fees in breach of contract without a fee-shifting provision/statute.","rule":"In a breach of contract lawsuit, the general rule is that each party pays for their own attorney fees.","sub":"Damages — Attorney’s Fees (American Rule)","family":"Damages","unit":null},{"n":37,"summary":"A party breaches a contract and the nonbreaching party seeks consequential (special) damages for additional losses beyond the direct benefit-of-the-bargain. The issue is whether the consequential losses were within the parties’ contemplation—known or foreseeable—at the time the contract was formed.","trigger":"Claim for consequential damages after breach; foreseeability/knowledge at contract formation.","rule":"Consequential damages must be either known or foreseeable at the time of contract to be recoverable.","sub":"Damages — Consequential (Foreseeability)","family":"Damages","unit":25},{"n":38,"summary":"Moving company fails to have the reserved truck ready on Jan 31; tenant warns that delay will force him to pay an extra month’s rent and later seeks $1,400 in consequential damages.","trigger":"Consequential damages look to what was foreseeable when the contract was made, not what is disclosed later at breach.","rule":"Consequential damages must be foreseeable at the time of contracting, not at the time of breach.","sub":"Damages – Consequential / Foreseeability","family":"Damages","unit":25},{"n":39,"summary":"Mechanic agrees to repair a knitting machine by March 6. Manufacturer, needing the machine to produce cloth for a designer by March 15, has a liquidated-damages clause with the designer for $5,000/day after March 15. Mechanic is five days late; manufacturer pays $25,000 to designer and sues mechanic for that amount.","trigger":"Consequential damages require foreseeability/notice at the time of contracting; unusual downstream damages (like third-party liquidated damages) are not recoverable without notice.","rule":"Consequential damages are recoverable only if foreseeable at the time of contracting (Hadley), so unexpected downstream liquidated damages aren’t recoverable without notice.","sub":"Damages – Consequential damages (Hadley foreseeability)","family":"Damages","unit":25},{"n":40,"summary":"A promoter paid a famous actor $25,000 to appear at a convention and share revenue from autographs/photos. Tickets sold out nationwide based on that advertising. The day before, the actor backed out for a ‘better opportunity,’ and the promoter used lesser-known replacements; fans were disappointed and future convention attendance dropped for the next three events. Promoter sues for those losses.","trigger":"Consequential damages: whether loss of goodwill/reputation from breach is recoverable when foreseeable and provable with certainty.","rule":"Part of consequential damages can include goodwill, or the diminution of the plaintiff’s reputation.","sub":"Damages – Consequential/Goodwill","family":"Damages","unit":56},{"n":41,"summary":"Oil company contracts for mineral rights and promises to fill holes and restore land so owner can later build rentals. Company drills, then fails to restore the land. Cost to repair is $120,000 but diminution in value is only $10,000.","trigger":"Cost‑to‑repair damages may still be awarded when repair was a central purpose of the contract.","rule":"If the cost to repair is grossly disproportionate to the diminution in value, a court may find that it is a waste to award cost of repair damages unless the breach was in bad faith or repair was one of the main points of the contract.","sub":"Damages – Cost to Repair vs. Diminution in Value","family":"Damages","unit":65},{"n":42,"summary":"A mother contracts with a tutor to teach her adult son a bar-prep course for $5,000 (below the $6,000 going rate), and the mother tells the tutor the son has been promised a $55,000/year job if he passes the bar exam. Before instruction begins, the tutor repudiates the contract; although the mother/son reasonably could have hired an equally qualified replacement tutor for $6,000, they do not. The son fails the bar and loses the job; they sue the tutor for breach and seek damages.","trigger":"Consequential damages are sought but the losses could have been avoided by reasonable mitigation (e.g., hiring a substitute tutor).","rule":"Expectation damages include the benefit of the bargain (price vs. market/replacement), but consequential damages are barred to the extent they could have been avoided by mitigation.","sub":"Damages – Expectation vs consequential; mitigation","family":"Damages","unit":66},{"n":43,"summary":"A Sculptor makes special‑solvent ice sculptures that require constant, expensive upkeep in storage/transport. A Dealer in Japan contracts for ten Greek‑myth sculptures for $300,000, including all shipping and storage to Japan. After the sculptures are created but before shipment, Dealer cancels. Sculptor makes a long good‑faith search and resells to a U.S. buyer for $150,000. Sculptor saves $40,000 by not shipping/storing for Japan, but incurs $5,000 in shipping/storage to deliver to the new buyer. Sculptor sues Dealer for breach and seeks incidental damages.","trigger":"Seller resells after buyer’s breach; compute damages and incidental costs actually incurred (net of savings).","rule":"Incidental damages include commercially reasonable expenses incurred because of the breach (e.g., costs of transportation/storage for resale), and the breaching buyer gets credit for expenses the seller saved because of the breach.","sub":"Damages – Incidental; resale","family":"Remedies","unit":57},{"n":44,"summary":"A chef was hired to travel with a famous actor on a three-month promotional tour of the actor’s upcoming movie at a salary of $36,000. The actor personally hired the chef during a short phone call in which they agreed to all the terms of a valid contract. The chef was to cook three meals a day for the actor based on the actor’s requests. A few days before the tour was to begin, the chef was replaced due to no fault of the chef. The chef spent $500 travelling for an interview for the only similar job available within a reasonable distance but wasn’t hired.","trigger":"Nonbreaching party spends money trying to mitigate damages; whether reasonable mitigation costs are recoverable.","rule":"Money reasonably spent trying to mitigate damages is recoverable, even if the mitigation attempt is unsuccessful.","sub":"Damages – Mitigation costs","family":"Damages","unit":66},{"n":45,"summary":"Builder is halfway through $300,000 home, has spent $170,000, when owner unjustifiably cancels; question asks best damages measure for builder.","trigger":"In losing contracts, restitution lets P avoid subtracting avoided losses, unlike expectation/reliance.","rule":"A party in a losing contract must subtract losses that they avoided when suing under expectation and reliance damages, but not under restitution. I expectation damages the non-breaching party wants to be put in a condition as if the K would have been fully performed. Here, it was a losing contract that if he finished he would have lost $40.000. With expectation you have to subtract losses, under restitution you dont because plaintiffs sues to be restituted the amount of economic value given to the defendant","sub":"Damages – Restitution vs Expectation / Losing Contracts","family":"Remedies","unit":37},{"n":46,"summary":"Output contract for flour; December shortfall; bakery covered at higher price and incurred rush costs","trigger":"Damages after cover; recoverability of specific items (including attorney fees)","rule":"In a breach of contract lawsuit, the general rule is that each party pays for their own attorney fees.","sub":"Damages (Cover/Incidental)","family":"UCC Buyer's Remedies","unit":69},{"n":47,"summary":"Manager had 2-year guaranteed contract; fired after 1 year; refused different-industry manager job; took temporary delivery job","trigger":"Wrongful termination damages and duty to mitigate with available employment","rule":"A person does not have to mitigate by accepting inferior employment, but money they earn from inferior employment taken will be subtracted from their damage award.","sub":"Damages (Mitigation)","family":"Damages","unit":66},{"n":48,"summary":"Woman submits accurate financial statement with loan application; her condition worsens significantly before the bank approves the loan; she fails to disclose change; bank later refuses to lend, claiming contract voidable for misrepresentation.","trigger":"Accurate statement becomes misleading due to material change; duty to correct/disclose before reliance.","rule":"A party who learns that a prior representation has become materially misleading must correct it; failure to disclose a material change can constitute misrepresentation making the contract voidable.","sub":"Defenses – Misrepresentation by Nondisclosure / Duty to Correct","family":"Defenses","unit":18},{"n":49,"summary":"Fraternal organization contracts with a caterer, with a deposit due before deliveries. Four days before the festival, terrorists attack the city, closing businesses for three days. Organization refuses to pay deposit because “no one would want a festival while still in shock.”","trigger":"Contract discharge: frustration of purpose vs impossibility; central purpose vs mere hardship.","rule":"Frustration of purpose discharges duties only when an unforeseen event substantially destroys the contract’s principal purpose for both parties; mere decreased desirability or hardship is not enough.","sub":"Discharge – Frustration of purpose","family":"Discharge","unit":44},{"n":50,"summary":"An engineer signed a two-year employment contract to serve as chief safety engineer for a coal mine for $7,000/month, requiring underground work each workday. After one week, he became very ill; testing revealed a chemical agent in the mine air to which the engineer had a rare allergic reaction. The engineer promptly quit. The mine hired a qualified replacement for the remainder of the contract at $7,500/month. Assume no statute or regulation applies. The mine sued the engineer for breach.","trigger":"Contract discharge defenses: impossibility/impracticability due to serious health risk unique to promisor; not at-will employment.","rule":"A party’s performance may be excused by impossibility/impracticability when an unanticipated condition beyond the party’s control makes performance impossible or would pose serious risk to that party’s health.","sub":"Excuse of Performance – Impossibility/Impracticability (Health Risk)","family":"Discharge","unit":44},{"n":51,"summary":"Store emails buyer offering laptop at stated price; buyer replies “I’ll take it” but adds demand for a free 3-year warranty; store treats laptop as reserved but refuses free warranty.","trigger":"Under 2-207, additional terms in non-merchant contracts are proposals only, not part of the deal.","rule":"Under UCC 2-207, if a contract is found where both parties are not merchants, any additional terms will be treated as mere proposals.","sub":"Formation – Battle of the Forms / Additional Terms (Non-Merchants)","family":"Formation","unit":13},{"n":52,"summary":"General contractor promises to award carpentry subcontract to carpenter if carpenter is lowest bid and GC wins main contract; both conditions occur; GC refuses to award subcontract.","trigger":"Conditional promise + acceptance + consideration; sub’s bid used; question asks carpenter’s best argument GC must award job.","rule":"When a conditional promise is supported by consideration and the condition occurs, a binding contract exists; promissory estoppel is a fallback only if no contract.","sub":"Formation — Conditional Promise / Consideration (vs. Promissory Estoppel)","family":"Consideration","unit":4},{"n":53,"summary":"Butcher sends an offer to sell ground sirloin at $2.50/lb, stating it will be held open for six months. Four months later, buyer tries to accept; butcher says price is now $3.50.","trigger":"UCC firm offer is irrevocable for up to three months but can still remain open afterward unless revoked.","rule":"Although a firm offer will only last for three months, the offer will still be valid after the three months has expired unless it is revoked by the offeror.","sub":"Formation – Offers / Firm Offers","family":"Formation","unit":12},{"n":54,"summary":"Investor initially offers $200 for a 30‑day option to buy land for $10,000; landowner declines. Investor then asks landowner to make a signed, 30‑day written offer “revocable at your pleasure,” promising to pay $200 the next day for doing so. Landowner signs: “For 30 days I offer my land to investor for $10,000, this offer to be revocable at my pleasure at any time before acceptance.” Issue is what duty/contract basis is created by the oral promise and the signed writing.","trigger":"Offer/option situation: promise to keep offer open without consideration; issues of unilateral contract/promissory estoppel/option.","rule":"A unilateral offer is accepted by performance; here, the landowner’s act of drafting the requested signed writing creates a duty (even though it’s not yet an option contract without paid consideration).","sub":"Formation – Option/offer; acceptance","family":"Formation","unit":29},{"n":55,"summary":"Producer and singer agree there will be no enforceable obligations until a final signed contract; negotiations occur; producer spends money; negotiations fail; producer sues.","trigger":"Explicit ‘no contract until signed’ language; reliance expenditures during negotiations.","rule":"When parties agree no contract exists until a signed writing, negotiations are not enforceable; reliance is generally not recoverable absent a reasonable basis (no contract/promissory estoppel).","sub":"Formation — Preliminary Negotiations / ‘No Contract Until Signed’","family":"Remedies","unit":55},{"n":56,"summary":"Collector offers $500 to restore toy; expert begins work; collector dies next day.","trigger":"Unilateral offer accepted by performance before death.","rule":"In unilateral contracts, acceptance by performance before death forms binding contract.","sub":"Formation — Unilateral Acceptance","family":"Formation","unit":29},{"n":57,"summary":"After a series of arsons, the city council adopts a televised reward: the city will pay $10,000 for the arrest and conviction of anyone guilty of the 2010 arsons. The offer is broadcast on the city’s sole TV station once daily for a week; question asks how the reward offer can be effectively accepted.","trigger":"Reward offer is a unilateral contract; acceptance requires performance within the terms (information leading to arrest and conviction).","rule":"A reward is a unilateral offer accepted only by performance, and where the reward is for “arrest and conviction,” acceptance occurs by supplying information that leads to arrest and conviction (not by promising to help, and not necessarily by personally making the arrest).","sub":"Formation – Unilateral contracts (reward)","family":"Formation","unit":50},{"n":58,"summary":"Owner offers $500 to paint bathroom same color by Monday 6 p.m., specifying time is of the essence. Painter starts, but only has one coat and a quarter of the second coat done by 6 p.m. Monday when owner returns and tells him to leave.","trigger":"Unilateral contract requiring completion by a stated deadline is not accepted if performance is late.","rule":"To accept a unilateral contract, performance must be completed by the time requested by the offeror.","sub":"Formation – Unilateral Contracts / Time of the Essence","family":"Formation","unit":29},{"n":59,"summary":"After major surgery and while taking powerful pain medication, an elderly woman offered to sell her car for $450 to her neighbor; the neighbor, who needed a car for his house-cleaning service, accepted. The car was worth $3,000, and the neighbor knew this and also knew the woman had undergone surgery and seemed “out of it” because of the medication. Several days later, the woman’s son demanded $3,450; when the neighbor tendered $450 and demanded the car, the woman refused. The neighbor sued for breach of contract.","trigger":"Contract formation defense: mental incapacity; not adjudicated incompetent → contract voidable (not void) if other party knew/should have known of incapacity.","rule":"A contract made by a person who lacks capacity is voidable (not void) at the option of the incapacitated party when the other party knew or had reason to know of the incapacity.","sub":"Formation Defenses – Mental Incapacity (Voidable Contracts)","family":"Capacity","unit":42},{"n":60,"summary":"A city required home repair contractors who performed work within city limits to pay a licensing fee to a city agency to raise revenue. A contractor unaware of the fee requirement agreed to perform home repairs for a resident. After satisfactory work was completed, the resident discovered the contractor had not paid the licensing fee and refused to pay. The contractor sued for breach of contract.","trigger":"Unlicensed (fee not paid) contractor seeks payment; licensing scheme appears revenue-raising, not protective.","rule":"If licensing is primarily revenue-raising (not to protect the public), failure to obtain/pay the license generally does not bar enforcement; the contract may be enforced (or recovery allowed in quantum meruit). If licensing is protective, the contract is void/unenforceable.","sub":"Illegality / Public Policy / Licensing (Revenue vs Protective)","family":"Illegality / Public Policy","unit":19},{"n":61,"summary":"Buyer entered an installment contract for egg deliveries. First delivery was rejected as substantially impaired. Seller cured properly and delivered eggs with only minor defects. Buyer attempted to cancel the entire contract.","trigger":"Seller’s right to cure in installment contracts.","rule":"In an installment contract, if the seller properly cures a nonconforming installment, the buyer must accept the cure and may not cancel the entire contract unless the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of the whole contract.","sub":"Installment Contracts","family":"Performance","unit":9},{"n":62,"summary":"Inexperienced buyer signs artist’s standard form that heavily favors artist; later dispute whether buyer must pay before portrait is completed, with no express term on order of performance.","trigger":"Ambiguous/missing performance-order terms are filled by default rules and construed against the drafter.","rule":"Ambiguous terms in a contract are construed against the draftsman, but missing terms are not. If the order of performance is not agreed to and the parties cannot act simultaneously, whichever party’s performance takes longer must go first.","sub":"Interpretation – Contra Proferentem / Order of Performance","family":"Interpretation","unit":52},{"n":63,"summary":"Over five years and hundreds of grain deals, seller delivered grain and buyer signed invoices silent on discounts. Although trade custom allows a 2% discount if paid within 10 days, in all prior transactions (without seller objection) the buyer paid in 15 days and deducted 5%. In the current single‑delivery wheat contract for $300,000, buyer signs the invoice; three days later seller sends a note: “Payment in full in accordance with signed invoice is due immediately. No discounts permitted.” Issue is which term controls.","trigger":"Conflict between usage of trade and course of dealing (repeated 5% discount practice) in interpreting contract terms.","rule":"Course of dealing between the parties controls over usage of trade; buyer can take the 5% within 15 days discount consistent with their repeated prior transactions.","sub":"Interpretation – Course of dealing vs usage of trade","family":"Interpretation","unit":52},{"n":64,"summary":"Tutor orally tells bar student that about 20 essays will be graded, but written contract only says \"graded essays.\" Student later wants to introduce the 20‑essay promise in litigation.","trigger":"Parol evidence may be used to explain or interpret an ambiguous term already in the writing.","rule":"Allowing extrinsic evidence to interpret a term that is already in the contract does not violate the parol evidence rule.","sub":"Interpretation – Parol Evidence / Ambiguity","family":"Parol Evidence","unit":27},{"n":65,"summary":"Trainer gives free session, knows client cannot realistically reach 315-lb bench goal, but still tells him (as an expert) “in my opinion” he can with hard work; client pays $8,000, later sues to rescind for misrepresentation.","trigger":"An “opinion” from someone with superior knowledge can count as actionable misrepresentation.","rule":"Although a misrepresentation usually requires a statement of fact, it can be based on an opinion if the opinion comes from one with superior knowledge.","sub":"Misrepresentation – Opinion as Fact / Rescission","family":"Defenses","unit":43},{"n":66,"summary":"Seller earlier rolled back snowmobile odometer; buyer resells to neighbor believing mileage low and touts odometer; mechanic discovers tampering; neighbor seeks rescission; no statute.","trigger":"Material (or fraudulent) misrepresentation induces purchase, even if seller lacked knowledge of falsity.","rule":"A contract can be rescinded if there is a misrepresentation that is either fraudulent or material.","sub":"Misrepresentation / Rescission","family":"Defenses","unit":64},{"n":67,"summary":"A contractor agreed to build a pole barn for a homeowner at an agreed price, with the understanding that the price would increase if lumber costs rose. The homeowner reduced the deal to a written contract but accidentally left out the ‘price-increase-if-lumber-rises’ clause. After the agreement, lumber prices spiked due to shortages, and the homeowner refused to pay more than the written price.","trigger":"Both parties intended the written contract to include a price-adjustment term, but the writing omitted it due to a shared drafting/recording error (mutual mistake about what the writing says).","rule":"When the written contract fails to express the actual agreement because of a mistake of both parties, the court may reform the writing to accurately express the agreement.","sub":"Mistake – Reformation","family":"Defenses","unit":63},{"n":68,"summary":"Landscaper agrees to mow Fridays 10 a.m.–6 p.m. for $40 or $60 with weed trimming. Owner mistakenly thinks weeds are included in $40 and asks for mowing plus trimming \"next week.\" Company mistakenly comes at 8 a.m. and bills $60; owner disputes on price and timing.","trigger":"Unilateral mistake is not a mutual mistake; minor timing deviation doesn’t excuse payment.","rule":"A mutual mistake is where both parties are mistaken about the same fact in a contract.","sub":"Mistake / Conditions","family":"Defenses","unit":62},{"n":69,"summary":"A man signed a contract with a personal trainer for 20 weekly sessions at $100 each and agreed to follow a strict 20‑week diet requiring him to buy specified foods and supplements (he spent $2,300). After 20 weeks, he sues for a partial refund because he disliked his results.","trigger":"Mixed contract with significant services plus required goods; decide whether UCC or common law governs under predominant purpose test.","rule":"Under the predominant purpose test, the court will look to see what the main purpose of the contract was before deciding whether to use common law or the Uniform Commercial Code.","sub":"Mixed Goods & Services","family":"Scope","unit":70},{"n":70,"summary":"Painter does full job; homeowner disputes one wall and tenders $3,500 instead of $4,000 as full payment; painter accepts, then learns work was proper and seeks additional $500.","trigger":"Settlement of a good-faith dispute over performance is a binding modification/accord.","rule":"A modification of a contract based on a good faith dispute will be binding on the parties.","sub":"Modification – Accord & Satisfaction / Disputed Claim","family":"Accord & Satisfaction","unit":7},{"n":71,"summary":"Mechanic agrees in writing to repair truck for $12,000; after repairs, refuses to return truck unless landscaper promises extra $2,000; landscaper needs truck immediately and agrees.","trigger":"Contract modification demanded after performance; no new consideration; coercive leverage (no reasonable alternative).","rule":"Under common law, a modification for the same performance is unenforceable absent new consideration; a promise induced by wrongful threat with no reasonable alternative is unenforceable (economic duress).","sub":"Modification — Preexisting Duty / Economic Duress","family":"Modification","unit":6},{"n":72,"summary":"Buyer and seller have a written contract for 50 gallons of paint at $15/gal. Buyer asks for a price reduction to $13/gal due to financial trouble; seller agrees by email, then ships with an invoice back at $15/gal.","trigger":"Good‑faith UCC modification can be binding without new consideration.","rule":"Under the UCC, a modification in good faith is binding even without additional or different consideration.","sub":"Modification - UCC / Good Faith Consideration","family":"Modification","unit":30},{"n":73,"summary":"Plumber agrees to replace four toilets for $1,000; discovers hidden cracked pipe on fourth and negotiates extra $200; homeowner later refuses to pay the extra.","trigger":"Restatement allows fair, equitable modifications when unforeseen circumstances arise and K is still executory.","rule":"Under the Restatement, a modification will be allowed if the contract is executory, the circumstances are unforeseeable, and the modification is fair and equitable under the circumstances.","sub":"Modification – Unforeseen Circumstances / Fair & Equitable","family":"Modification","unit":31},{"n":74,"summary":"A company agreed to provide regular facilities-maintenance services for a landlord’s apartment buildings for $150 per hour. The parties also orally agreed that if the services required in any given month exceeded 100 hours, the landlord would pay the company a discounted hourly rate of $125 per hour for those extra services. When the parties put their agreement in writing, however, neither party noticed that the contract did not include the discounted-rate provision.","trigger":"Written contract omits agreed discount","rule":"Contracts may be reformed for mutual mistake.","sub":"Mutual mistake/ Parol Evidence","family":"Defenses","unit":63},{"n":75,"summary":"Law firm sought to enforce 5-year noncompete against departing lawyer","trigger":"Restrictive covenant potentially unreasonable; reformation issue","rule":"The court will honor the intent of the parties unless it violates public policy. With an unreasonable covenant not to compete, the court will usually reform the provisions to make them reasonable rather than strike them down.","sub":"Noncompete","family":"Public Policy","unit":59},{"n":76,"summary":"On Oct. 1, a homeowner mails an offer to sell his house for $200,000, open until Oct. 31. The friend mails back a letter with a $100 check asking the offer be held open until Nov. 15; the homeowner cashes the check on Oct. 17 but never responds. On Nov. 14 the friend mails an acceptance to buy; it reaches the homeowner on Nov. 16, and the homeowner refuses to sell.","trigger":"Mailbox rule does not apply to option contracts/firm offers; acceptance is effective only upon receipt within the option period.","rule":"The mailbox rule does not apply when there is an option contract or firm offer.","sub":"Offer & Acceptance – Option contracts/firm offers; mailbox rule inapplicable","family":"Formation","unit":40},{"n":77,"summary":"A man offers on Monday to buy his brother-in-law’s car for $9,000 and says he needs an answer by Friday; the brother-in-law says he needs a couple days to think. On Wednesday, the man calls and revokes, saying he is no longer interested. The brother-in-law insists the man must keep the offer open until Friday.","trigger":"Offer to hold open without consideration; whether the offeror can revoke before acceptance absent an option/firm offer.","rule":"Absent consideration (or a UCC firm offer), a promise to keep an offer open is not binding, and the offeror may revoke any time before acceptance is effective.","sub":"Offer & Acceptance – Revocation; option contracts","family":"Formation","unit":41},{"n":78,"summary":"Client writes attorney asking him to draft a boat-sale contract and assumes standard rate unless she hears otherwise; attorney does the work; client later revokes and refuses to pay.","trigger":"Unilateral offers accepted by performance can’t be revoked once performance has begun within a reasonable time.","rule":"A unilateral offer can be accepted by performing and cannot be revoked for a reasonable time once a party starts performance.","sub":"Offer & Acceptance – Unilateral Contracts / Implied-in-Fact Dealing","family":"Formation","unit":51},{"n":79,"summary":"Buyer paid $5 to keep widget offer open 4 months; wholesaler revoked after 3 months; buyer attempted to accept","trigger":"Option contract consideration can keep offer open beyond firm-offer time limit","rule":"Unlike firm offers, option contracts can be held open beyond three months.","sub":"Offers (Option/Firm Offer)","family":"Formation","unit":41},{"n":80,"summary":"Developer agreed in writing to hold parcel for 10 days for $100, then sold it","trigger":"Attempt to keep land offer open without enforceable option","rule":"An offer is revocable unless supported by a valid option contract","sub":"Option Contracts","family":"Formation","unit":41},{"n":81,"summary":"A car dealership customer is told orally that financing will be at 2.9% interest with about $3,000 total interest. The written 14‑page contract the customer signs lists 12.9% interest and includes an integration clause. Two weeks later, after receiving a higher payment notice, the customer sues under TILA and for fraud, seeks rescission, and offers the oral 2.9% term to show wrongdoing.","trigger":"Parol evidence cannot be used to vary the written terms, but it can be admitted to avoid the contract (e.g., rescission for fraud).","rule":"The parol evidence rule may bar evidence from being introduced to change the terms of the contract but will not bar evidence being used to avoid the contract.","sub":"Parol Evidence – Extrinsic evidence to avoid/rescind for fraud","family":"Parol Evidence","unit":27},{"n":82,"summary":"Contract for '2,000 sets of cups and saucers at $5 each' with a merger clause; studio bills per piece; artist insists per set; studio offers trade usage that 'sets' priced per piece.","trigger":"Merger clause/complete integration; ambiguous term ('set'); evidence offered is trade usage to interpret meaning.","rule":"Even with a merger clause, evidence of usage of trade may be admitted to explain/interpret an ambiguous contract term; it is not barred as parol evidence when used for interpretation.","sub":"Parol Evidence — Interpretation / Usage of Trade","family":"Parol Evidence","unit":27},{"n":83,"summary":"Garage remodel contract silent on completion time; parties orally agreed to 60 days.","trigger":"Partial integration + consistent additional term.","rule":"Parol evidence of consistent additional terms is admissible in partially integrated contracts.","sub":"Parol Evidence — Partial Integration","family":"Parol Evidence","unit":26},{"n":84,"summary":"A buyer and seller orally agree on a $100,000 price for an industrial air-conditioning system while continuing to negotiate other points. When all terms are settled, they sign a written agreement stating the price is $110,000 and delivery will be June 1; the written contract is lengthy and contains no merger clause. The seller delivers the system, but the buyer refuses to pay more than $100,000 and offers evidence of the earlier oral price term. The seller sues for the additional $10,000.","trigger":"Parol evidence offered to prove a prior oral term that contradicts the price term in a later written agreement.","rule":"Parol evidence can’t add a term that contradicts the writing (even if partial integration).","sub":"Parol Evidence Rule – Contradicting term","family":"Parol Evidence","unit":26},{"n":85,"summary":"Buyer contracted for stone crabs for a Valentine’s Day service. Seller delivered the wrong type of crabs. Buyer accepted delivery without inspection but later discovered the error before use.","trigger":"Buyer’s right to reject under perfect tender rule.","rule":"Under the perfect tender rule, a buyer may reject goods that fail in any respect to conform to the contract, unless the buyer has already accepted the goods.","sub":"Perfect Tender Rule","family":"Performance","unit":8},{"n":86,"summary":"Builder contracts to build a $400,000 house with three full baths; he completes the house but one bathroom is a half bath without a tub. Cost to add the tub is $8,000. Owner refuses to pay anything.","trigger":"Substantial performance allows recovery of contract price minus cost to cure minor defects.","rule":"When a contract is substantially performed, meaning the breach was not material, the party will receive the original contract price minus any damages caused by his breach.","sub":"Performance – Substantial Performance / Cost to Cure","family":"Conditions","unit":21},{"n":87,"summary":"Question asks when substantial performance most likely occurred (various partial deliveries/builds; last choice is 85% of home completed, builder quits due to serious illness).","trigger":"Substantial performance focuses on degree of completion plus good faith, not just percentage alone.","rule":"Substantial performance is based not just on the percentage of work performed in a contract, but also, among other factors, on whether the breaching party acted in good faith or not.","sub":"Performance – Substantial Performance / Material Breach","family":"Conditions","unit":21},{"n":88,"summary":"Horse sale: written contract says time is of the essence for Sept 1 delivery/payment; neither party shows up on Sept 1; buyer calls Sept 3 to perform and seller refuses.","trigger":"UCC 2-507 makes proper tender a condition to buyer’s duty to accept and pay.","rule":"According to UCC 2-507, tender of delivery is a condition to the buyer's duty to accept the goods and, unless otherwise agreed, to his duty to pay for them.","sub":"Performance – Tender of Delivery / Buyer’s Duty to Pay","family":"Performance","unit":46},{"n":89,"summary":"Landowner signs one contract with a builder to construct three separate structures on separate parcels, each with its own agreed price. Builder finishes the first structure, then says he will not build the other two and demands payment for the completed structure; landowner refuses to pay anything.","trigger":"A single agreement contains separate performances with separate prices (divisible contract) and one divisible unit is fully completed while remaining units are breached.","rule":"If a contract is divisible (separate performances with separate prices), a party who completes one divisible unit may recover for that unit, subject to offset for damages from the remaining breach.","sub":"Performance & Breach – Divisible contracts / recovery for completed unit","family":"Performance","unit":45},{"n":90,"summary":"Producer hires an actress for a six‑month Broadway role at a weekly salary. She misses one week due to influenza, is replaced, then recovers; the producer refuses to let her return for the rest of the contract term and she sues for breach.","trigger":"Services contract where the performer’s breach is minor; substantial performance means no material breach and allows recovery of contract price minus damages.","rule":"In services contracts, substantial performance means no material breach, so the performer can recover contract price minus damages.","sub":"Performance & Breach – Substantial performance (services)","family":"Conditions","unit":21},{"n":91,"summary":"Buyer orders 100 half-gallon milk containers by Dec 5; seller, short on that packaging, ships 100 one-gallon containers with a price break and note; buyer rejects for nonconformity and space issues.","trigger":"Seller who reasonably thought nonconforming tender would be acceptable gets a further reasonable time to cure.","rule":"Under UCC 2-508, where the buyer rejects a non-conforming tender the seller had reasonable grounds to believe would be acceptable with or without money allowance, the seller may, if he seasonably notifies the buyer, have a further reasonable time to substitute a conforming tender.","sub":"Performance & Cure – Seller’s Right to Cure / Reasonable Time","family":"Performance","unit":11},{"n":92,"summary":"Bathroom remodel completed to satisfaction; homeowner offered $12k in full due to personal finances; contractor accepted then sued for $3k","trigger":"Payment-in-full without good-faith dispute triggers pre-existing duty/debt rule","rule":"Absent a good faith dispute over how much is owed, a lesser amount offered as payment in full will not be binding at common law because of the pre-existing duty/debt rule.","sub":"Pre-existing Duty/Debt Rule","family":"Accord & Satisfaction","unit":48},{"n":93,"summary":"Written house sale for $250,000 with a quitclaim deed agreed. Buyer later emails demanding a general warranty deed at no extra cost; seller rejects. Closing documents reflect a quitclaim deed.","trigger":"Attempted modification is ineffective unless both parties agree; final closing documents control.","rule":"An attempted modification has to be agreed to by both parties.","sub":"Real Property Ks – Deeds / Modification","family":"Modification","unit":5},{"n":94,"summary":"Buyer pays $1,000 earnest money on house contract; before closing buyer breaches (lost job) and seeks return; seller’s actual losses exceed deposit.","trigger":"Buyer default in real estate sale; deposit/earnest money; restitution vs liquidated-damages-style forfeiture.","rule":"In a real estate contract, seller may retain the buyer’s deposit (commonly up to 10% as liquidated damages) when the buyer breaches and seller has damages; breaching buyer recovers only if retention would unjustly enrich seller.","sub":"Remedies — Earnest Money / Liquidated Damages (breach by buyer)","family":"Remedies","unit":38},{"n":95,"summary":"Builder contracts to build greenhouse for $20,000 on completion; after $9,000 labor + $1,000 materials purchased (unused), homeowner wrongfully stops work; builder resells unused materials for $1,000; completion would have cost $5,000 more; partial work increased property value by $3,000.","trigger":"Owner breaches; expectation damages = costs incurred + profit minus salvage/loss avoided.","rule":"Expectation damages for a wrongfully terminated construction contract equal costs incurred plus expected profit minus amounts saved (e.g., salvage proceeds) to put the builder in the position as if fully performed.","sub":"Remedies – Expectation Damages / Construction Contract (Salvage)","family":"Damages","unit":66},{"n":96,"summary":"Roofer is hired for $6,000 with a clause that says roofer must pay homeowner $500 if he fails to replace “each shingle” in the agreed area; clause calls itself “liquidated damages, not a penalty.”","trigger":"Liquidated damages must reasonably track harm; flat amount per breach regardless of severity looks like a penalty.","rule":"A clause that does not measure liquidated damages based on the severity of the breach will likely be a penalty, despite the parties agreeing that it is a valid clause.","sub":"Remedies – Liquidated Damages vs Penalty","family":"Remedies","unit":38},{"n":97,"summary":"A TV network president offers a veteran host a contract to host a new dating show and says he must hear back within 48 hours. The host’s lawyer reviews the contract, the host signs it, and he mails it back. At the 48‑hour mark the president has not heard back and hires a cheaper second choice. The acceptance arrives 70 hours after the offer; the host says he turned down other jobs in the meantime and wants recovery.","trigger":"No valid contract if acceptance is not effective under the offer’s timing; claimant may still seek restitution or promissory estoppel based on reliance.","rule":"When there is no valid contract, a party may still recover for restitution or promissory estoppel.","sub":"Remedies – No contract; restitution / promissory estoppel","family":"Remedies","unit":55},{"n":98,"summary":"Landscaper contracted to maintain yard for 6 months ($300/mo payable at end); quit after 4 months for better job; homeowner refused to pay anything.","trigger":"Service contract; breaching party seeks recovery for work performed; homeowner could mitigate at same rate.","rule":"A breaching party’s recovery is generally limited to restitution to prevent unjust enrichment (FMV of benefit conferred).","sub":"Remedies – Restitution (Breaching party)","family":"Restitution","unit":67},{"n":99,"summary":"Mother pays a $200 deposit to a game re‑seller to use his best efforts to find a rare system, with contract void if he cannot. After using best efforts, he fails to find it; mother sues for more than her deposit but she can only recover her deposit eventhough the contract was void because the condition wasn't met","trigger":"When no valid contract obligation arises, the remedy is restitution of what was conferred.","rule":"One of the times restitution can be used is when there wasn’t a valid contract.","sub":"Remedies – Restitution / Failed Condition","family":"Restitution","unit":67},{"n":100,"summary":"A widow lists her home for sale. Two days later, a musician makes a full-price cash offer, which the widow accepts; closing is set for 30 days. Three weeks later, the widow has second thoughts and tells the musician she will not sell. The musician seeks to force the sale.","trigger":"Seller breaches a land-sale contract; buyer seeks equitable relief rather than money damages.","rule":"Specific performance is an available remedy for breach of a contract to convey real property because real property is considered unique.","sub":"Remedies – Specific performance (real estate)","family":"Remedies","unit":28},{"n":101,"summary":"Buyer and seller signed written land contract without closing date. Ten days later they orally agreed to rescind. Seller sold land to third party. Buyer sued for breach.","trigger":"Oral rescission of written land contract.","rule":"A land sale contract may be orally rescinded; valid rescission extinguishes contractual duties.","sub":"Rescission — Statute of Frauds","family":"Statute of Frauds","unit":null},{"n":102,"summary":"Retailer orders couches “FOB”; manufacturer ships via carrier and gives notice; first tender fails because warehouse is closed; on second attempt, accident destroys 18 couches en route back.","trigger":"In shipment contracts with FOB terms, risk of loss passes when goods are given to the carrier and notice is given.","rule":"When a contract contains an FOB term, it is presumed to be a shipment contract over a destination contract. With a shipment contract, the risk of loss passes from the seller to the buyer once the goods are loaded on to a third-party carrier and notice is given to the buyer.","sub":"Risk of Loss – Shipment vs Destination / FOB","family":"Risk of Loss","unit":15},{"n":103,"summary":"Question asks which item is not a “good” under the UCC among timber, unborn young, minerals severed by buyer, and crops.","trigger":"Goods = movable at identification; minerals are goods only if seller severs them.","rule":"UCC 2-105 defines goods as anything movable at the time of identification to the contract for sale. 2-107 says that a contract for the sale of minerals or the like to be removed from realty is a contract for the sale of goods if they are to be severed by the seller.","sub":"Scope – Definition of Goods (2-105, 2-107)","family":"Scope","unit":70},{"n":104,"summary":"Multiple-choice asks which contract must be in writing under the statute of frauds (options include 1-year, surety, executor promise, lease of goods).","trigger":"Statute of Frauds category: executor’s personal promise to pay estate debts; plus UCC Article 2A writing requirement for certain leases of goods.","rule":"A promise by an executor to pay the bills of the deceased out of his own personal funds must be in writing to be enforceable. A lease contract must be in writing if the total payments to be made, excluding payments for options or to renew or buy, are $1,000 or more.","sub":"Statute of Frauds – Executor’s promise; UCC 2A-201 lease of goods writing requirement","family":"Statute of Frauds","unit":33},{"n":105,"summary":"Retailer and manufacturer (both merchants) talk by phone about TVs; manufacturer sends signed confirmation letter; secretary trashes it; goods later delivered and retailer claims no binding K.","trigger":"Between merchants, a confirmation memo binds recipient if not objected to within 10 days.","rule":"Between merchants, a confirmation letter sent within a reasonable time of a conversation between the parties can meet the statute of frauds if it is not objected to within 10 days of receipt.","sub":"Statute of Frauds – Merchant’s Confirmatory Memo (2-201(2))","family":"Statute of Frauds","unit":14},{"n":106,"summary":"Company president orally offers a retired executive a lifetime consulting arrangement at $200,000/year; executive accepts, spends money in reliance, then the company backs out and refuses to put anything in writing. Executive sues for breach; company argues Statute of Frauds (one-year rule).","trigger":"Agreement is a lifetime contract, which could be fully performed within a year if the person dies, so it falls outside the one-year SOF category.","rule":"Although contracts that cannot be performed within a year from the date of the contract must be in writing under the Statute of Frauds, a lifetime contract is not required to be in writing because the person can die","sub":"Statute of Frauds – One-year provision; lifetime contracts","family":"Statute of Frauds","unit":32},{"n":107,"summary":"Homeowner orally offers neighbor a 10-day option to buy house for $250 non-refundable option fee; neighbor asks lawyer whether option and sale must satisfy Statute of Frauds.","trigger":"Real property interests, including purchase options, must be in a signed writing.","rule":"Any transfer of an interest in real property, including an option to purchase it, must be in writing to be enforceable under the statute of frauds.","sub":"Statute of Frauds – Real Property / Options","family":"Statute of Frauds","unit":null},{"n":108,"summary":"A 25‑year‑old student negligently crashes his father’s uninsured car into a woman’s car; both are injured. The father, mistakenly believing he is liable because he owned the car, tells the woman: “I will see to it that you are reimbursed for any losses you incur as a result of the accident.” The woman sues the father for lost wages; issue is the father’s best defense.","trigger":"Oral promise to pay another’s losses/debt (“see to it”) triggers suretyship Statute of Frauds.","rule":"An oral promise to “see to it” that another’s obligation is paid is a suretyship promise and is unenforceable under the Statute of Frauds absent a writing (unless an exception applies).","sub":"Statute of Frauds – Suretyship","family":"Statute of Frauds","unit":33},{"n":109,"summary":"Homeowner buys an insurance policy that omits a coverage term required by statute designed to protect insured homeowners. After a loss, the insurer argues it cannot be liable because the policy term violates the statute.","trigger":"Statute requires contract terms to protect a class (insureds); regulated party (insurer) cannot avoid liability by pointing to its own noncompliant policy.","rule":"When a statute requires contract terms to protect a class (here, insured homeowners), the regulated party (insurer) generally can’t avoid liability by claiming its own policy violates the statute; enforcement favors the protected class.","sub":"Statutory Contracts – Statute-mandated policy terms; protected-class enforcement","family":"Public Policy","unit":null},{"n":110,"summary":"Buyer agreed to purchase a used television for cash. At the exchange, both parties refused to perform first, each insisting the other go first.","trigger":"Simultaneous duties of payment and delivery in a sale of goods.","rule":"In a sale of goods, unless otherwise agreed, the buyer must tender payment before the seller is required to complete delivery of the goods. Under UCC seller tenders the goods and buyer tenders the payment and seller completes the delivery","sub":"Tender of Performance","family":"Performance","unit":46},{"n":111,"summary":"Creditor loans money with payments due on the first of each month. Creditor agrees (on debtor’s request) to accept payments on the fifth if made by cashier’s check; debtor complies for two months. Creditor assigns the loan to a bank, which demands payment on the first; debtor insists payment is due on the fifth.","trigger":"Assignee takes the assignor’s rights as they exist at assignment, including any valid modifications; assignee cannot enforce stricter original terms.","rule":"An assignee takes only the assignor’s rights as they exist at assignment, including any valid modifications; the assignee cannot impose stricter original terms.","sub":"Third Parties – Assignment (assignee bound by existing modifications)","family":"Third Parties","unit":58},{"n":112,"summary":"A father whose only living relatives were a daughter and a nephew owned land worth $500,000. In return for his daughter’s promise to pay $300,000 to his nephew at the time of the father’s death, the father executed and delivered to his daughter a quitclaim deed to the land. The daughter took possession of the land. Two years later, in a signed writing, the father directed his daughter to pay to a neighbor at the father’s death $50,000 of the $300,000 that was to go to the nephew. The daughter, who had long disliked the neighbor, told her father she did not plan to honor his request. The father died, and his daughter has since refused to pay anything to either the nephew or the neighbor.”","trigger":"Father modifies payment to third party","rule":"Original parties may modify or revoke a contract benefiting a third party until the beneficiary’s rights vest (assent, reliance, or suit); beneficiary consent is not required. Third-party beneficiary rights vest upon reliance or contract terms.","sub":"Third party beneficiary","family":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","unit":35},{"n":113,"summary":"Father hires a limousine driver (sole proprietor) to take his 18-year-old daughter and her date to prom, wait, and bring them home; driver leaves, sends a friend with another limo service to pick them up; friend negligently causes an accident; father claims breach and daughter also wants to sue for breach.","trigger":"Non-party to contract seeks to sue on the contract where contract was made to benefit that person (intended beneficiary).","rule":"Although privity of contract is usually required to sue another for breach of contract, an exception to the required privity is if someone is an intended third-party beneficiary of a contract. A delegation of duties creates a third party beneficiary, unless there is a novation. Here, when the driver (delegator) delegated his duty to friend (delegatee) converted daughter in a third party beneficiary to the delegation and she can sue either the driver or the friend. Delegation also brings up the right to demand assurances. ADT-3 (Assignment of a K also brings up a delegation and delegation creates a third party beneficiary.","sub":"Third-Party Beneficiaries – Intended beneficiary exception to privity (who may sue)","family":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","unit":35},{"n":114,"summary":"Friend promises to lend $10,000 directly to dealer so borrower can buy a car; dealer prepares paperwork but friend then refuses to lend; issue is whether dealer can enforce as third-party beneficiary.","trigger":"Intended beneficiaries must be shown by contract purpose and parties’ intent.","rule":"To see if a third party is intended to be a beneficiary, we look at the intent of the parties to the contract and whether the contract was entered into with the purpose of benefitting the third party.","sub":"Third-Party Beneficiaries – Intended vs Incidental","family":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","unit":35},{"n":115,"summary":"A homeowner hires a landscaper to overhaul a large yard with many trees, and the landscaper calls a tree trimmer to help. One week before the project, the homeowner and landscaper agree that tree trimming is not necessary as part of the project. The tree trimmer sues both the homeowner and the landscaper for breach of contract, claiming rights under their arrangement.","trigger":"Third party seeks to enforce someone else’s contract; determine intended vs incidental beneficiary status.","rule":"Only intended third-party beneficiaries may enforce a contract; an incidental beneficiary has no contractual rights against the contracting parties.","sub":"Third-Party Beneficiaries – Intended vs incidental","family":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","unit":35},{"n":116,"summary":"Collection of a debtor’s $2,000 debt to a creditor was barred by the applicable statute of limitations. The debtor sold and delivered his car to a buyer under a written agreement signed by the buyer in which the buyer promised to pay the $2,000 purchase price to the creditor in satisfaction of the debtor’s debt. The creditor seeks to recover the $2,000 from the buyer.","trigger":"Time-barred debt + later written agreement directing payment to creditor as third-party beneficiary (revival).","rule":"A time-barred debt may be revived by a new promise/commitment (often in writing) to pay; an intended creditor beneficiary may enforce the buyer’s promise even though the original debt was unenforceable due to the statute of limitations.","sub":"Third-Party Beneficiaries / Statute of Limitations / Revival of Time-Barred Debt","family":"Consideration","unit":1},{"n":117,"summary":"Buyer and Seller entered into a valid real estate contract with a June 1 closing date. On May 31, Seller unilaterally attempted to delay the closing by two days. Buyer became upset and refused to close at all.","trigger":"Seller delays performance slightly without making time of the essence.","rule":"When time is not of the essence, a short delay in performance is not a material breach, and the nonbreaching party must still perform but may recover damages caused by the delay.","sub":"Time of Performance","family":"Performance","unit":22},{"n":118,"summary":"Requirements contract for potatoes states it \"cannot be assigned.\" Farmer later directs grocery store to send payments to his neighbor to satisfy a debt; store objects based on anti‑assignment clause.","trigger":"Anti‑assignment of \"the contract\" does not bar assignment of the right to payment under the UCC.","rule":"Under the UCC, an agreement to not assign ta contract is not effective to stop the assignment of rights but can stop the delegation of duties.","sub":"UCC – Assignment vs. Delegation","family":"Assignment & Delegation","unit":36},{"n":119,"summary":"Seller offers to sell 100 disposable cameras at $4 each. Groom accepts price but adds a request that each camera be placed in a decorative bag provided by seller. Seller ships cameras without bags; groom wants out of the deal.","trigger":"Additional term proposed by a non‑merchant buyer is just a proposal and not automatically part of the contract.","rule":"Under UCC 2-207, if the contract is not between merchants, any additional or different term is viewed as a mere proposal that is not binding.","sub":"UCC – Battle of the Forms / Additional Terms","family":"Formation","unit":13},{"n":120,"summary":"Retailer orders twelve 48‑inch riding mowers with an April 1 deadline. Manufacturer, short on that model, ships twelve 50‑inch models on March 30 with a note saying buyer gets an upgrade for same price. Retailer rejects because his customers demand 48‑inch models.","trigger":"Seller who reasonably believed a non‑conforming tender would be acceptable can seasonably notify and get more time to cure.","rule":"Under UCC 2-508, where the buyer rejects a non-conforming tender which the seller had reasonable grounds to believe would be acceptable with or without money allowance the seller may if he seasonably notifies the buyer have a further reasonable time to substitute a conforming tender.","sub":"UCC – Cure / Nonconforming Tender","family":"Performance","unit":11},{"n":121,"summary":"Dad buys a surround-sound system for his son’s TV after relying on the store associate’s compatibility recommendation; the system is nondefective but won’t pair with the TV.","trigger":"Buyer relies on seller’s expertise to select goods for a particular purpose; goods don’t conform (not fit for that purpose).","rule":"An implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose arises when a seller knows or should have known that the buyer was relying on the seller’s expertise in purchasing a good and the buyer actually relies on the seller’s expertise.","sub":"UCC – Cure / Nonconforming Tender","family":"Warranty","unit":16},{"n":122,"summary":"Chef has a year‑long installment contract for 10 dozen eggs each month. After three perfect months, farmer sends only five dozen in month four and says inventory will be normal next month; chef wants to reject and cancel entire contract.","trigger":"Perfect tender rule does not apply to installment contracts; only substantial impairment justifies canceling the whole contract.","rule":"The perfect tender rule doesn’t apply to installment contracts. For installment contracts an installment can be REJECTED if the defect substantially impairs the value of that installment. The contract can be CANCELED if the defects in the installments substantially impair the value of the entire contract.","sub":"UCC – Installment Contracts / Perfect Tender Exception","family":"Performance","unit":9},{"n":123,"summary":"Customer orders $600 blinds to be installed by the home improvement store next week. Question asks why the UCC applies.","trigger":"Mixed goods/services contract; predominant purpose test and incidental services.","rule":"When a contract involves both goods and services, the UCC applies if the predominant purpose is the sale of goods and the service component is incidental to that sale.","sub":"UCC – Predominant purpose (goods vs services)","family":"Scope","unit":70},{"n":124,"summary":"Seller in State A contracts FOB State A to ship 100 iPhone 16s but instead ships 100 iPhone 15s. While in transit with a third‑party carrier, an unavoidable accident destroys many phones; seller wants buyer to pay.","trigger":"A seller who ships non‑conforming goods bears the risk of loss despite shipment terms.","rule":"Regardless of the shipping terms supplied in a contract, if a party breaches the contract, the risk of loss will be on them.","sub":"UCC – Risk of Loss / Breach","family":"Risk of Loss","unit":39},{"n":125,"summary":"Attorney orally orders a custom office sign for $3,000. Before delivery she wants it changed after deciding to take on a partner; sign shop says it’s already made and refuses to modify. She refuses to pay, arguing no signed writing.","trigger":"Specially manufactured goods are an exception to the UCC statute of frauds requirement.","rule":"Although a contract for the sale of goods worth $500 more must be in a signed writing to be enforceable, one of the exceptions is if a good is specially manufactured for the buyer and is not suitable for sale to others.","sub":"UCC – Statute of Frauds / Specially Manufactured Goods","family":"Statute of Frauds","unit":68},{"n":126,"summary":"A buyer contracted to purchase 1,000 pounds of rare coffee beans, with pickup at the seller’s warehouse on a specific date (time of the essence). The buyer failed to appear for pickup. Later, when the buyer demanded delivery, the seller refused. The dispute centers on whether the buyer’s duty to pay/accept was triggered.","trigger":"Sale of goods under the UCC: seller’s tender of delivery is a condition precedent to buyer’s obligation to accept and pay.","rule":"UCC 2-507 says that tender of delivery is a condition to the buyer's duty to accept the goods and, unless otherwise agreed, to his duty to pay for them. Tender entitles the seller to acceptance of the goods and to paymet according to the contract","sub":"UCC – Tender of Delivery","family":"Performance","unit":46},{"n":127,"summary":"Buyer orders 100 boxes of nails at $3/box; seller sends acknowledgment adding arbitration clause and ships; buyer sues for warranty; seller claims arbitration required.","trigger":"UCC 2-207 battle of forms; additional term (arbitration) in acceptance; material alteration issue.","rule":"Under UCC 2-207, an acceptance with additional terms forms a contract; between merchants, added terms become part unless they materially alter or are objected to.","sub":"UCC 2-207 – Additional terms / Material alteration","family":"Formation","unit":13},{"n":128,"summary":"A law professor buys basketball shoes from a sporting goods store for $99 to wear while playing in weekly basketball games. After a few months, the soles completely separate from the shoes. The store refuses a refund or exchange because the shoes were worn. The professor claims breach of the implied warranty of merchantability.","trigger":"Implied warranty of merchantability: whether goods are fit for their ordinary purpose; shoes failing during ordinary use.","rule":"The implied warranty of merchantability requires goods to be fit for their ordinary purpose; basketball shoes that fall apart during normal basketball use breach merchantability.","sub":"UCC Article 2 – Implied warranty of merchantability","family":"Warranty","unit":16},{"n":129,"summary":"Caterer and butcher agree the butcher will deliver 100 pounds of meat on the first day of each month for a year, because the caterer needs several days to trim, tenderize, marinate, and prep for an event on the sixth. After four timely months, the fifth delivery arrives on the fourth day; the caterer rejects it and buys already-prepared meat locally. The butcher claims the caterer should have accepted or allowed cure.","trigger":"Installment contract: perfect tender does not apply; rejection of an installment is allowed only if the nonconformity (late delivery) substantially impairs the value of that installment.","rule":"Installment contracts are not subject to the perfect tender rule. An installment can be rejected if the defect in the installment substantially impairs the value of the installment.","sub":"UCC Article 2 – Installment contracts (substantial impairment; rejection)","family":"Performance","unit":9},{"n":130,"summary":"Buyer orders 50 bales of hay and emphasizes they must be delivered together. Farmer delivers only 48 without notice and suggests buyer can buy 2 elsewhere; buyer rejects all 48. Farmer claims substantial performance and seeks the contract price minus damages.","trigger":"Sale of goods contract (non-installment) triggers UCC perfect tender rule—buyer may reject for any nonconformity unless modified by contract.","rule":"Under the Uniform Commercial Code, the perfect tender rule applies to any contract, other than an installment contract, that does not specify otherwise.","sub":"UCC Article 2 – Perfect tender rule (vs installment contracts)","family":"Performance","unit":8},{"n":131,"summary":"Toy company orders 100,000 sets of red, white, and blue electronic lights for action figures at $10 each for immediate shipment. Seller ships red, white, and green lights instead. Buyer promptly notifies the seller, but due to deadlines uses the lights anyway and later sues for breach after losing sales and dropping price.","trigger":"UCC nonconforming goods; buyer uses goods due to time pressure after giving notice—acceptance and remedies.","rule":"Under the UCC, a buyer who accepts nonconforming goods after timely notice may still recover damages for the seller’s breach (e.g., diminution in value and consequential damages if foreseeable).","sub":"UCC Article 2 – Perfect tender; acceptance & damages","family":"UCC Buyer's Remedies","unit":69},{"n":132,"summary":"Seller contracts in writing to sell $22,000 of electronics for $20,000 (delivery next day, payment on delivery) and tells buyer he needs the money to seize another deal for a quick $3,000 profit; buyer repudiates before delivery; seller resells next day for $19,000 and misses the $3,000 opportunity.","trigger":"Seller seeks to recover consequential-type losses (lost collateral profit opportunity) in addition to resale/market damages under the UCC.","rule":"Under the UCC, although sellers can sue for incidental damages, they cannot sue for consequential damages.","sub":"UCC Article 2 – Seller’s damages (incidental allowed; consequential not recoverable by seller)","family":"Remedies","unit":57},{"n":133,"summary":"Event Planner orders 200 pairs of earrings from a Jeweler at $75/pair. Jeweler makes them; Event Planner repudiates. Jeweler resells all 200 pairs to her cousin for $5/pair; cousin will sell elsewhere. Jeweler sues for $14,000 based on the contract–resale price difference.","trigger":"UCC seller’s resale damages; requirement that resale be commercially reasonable and in good faith.","rule":"To recover contract–resale damages under UCC §2-706, the seller’s resale must be made in good faith and in a commercially reasonable manner (including reasonable price and method).","sub":"UCC Article 2 – Seller’s remedies; resale","family":"Remedies","unit":57},{"n":134,"summary":"A college senior agrees to sell all his furniture to a sophomore for $600 and signs a paper stating that he agrees to sell all of his furniture to the sophomore for $600. The sophomore does not sign. The next week, the senior backs out and says the deal is off. The sophomore seeks to enforce the agreement.","trigger":"Sale of goods over $500; writing signed only by the party to be charged; buyer did not sign.","rule":"A UCC contract for sale of goods $500+ is enforceable if there is a writing indicating a contract and signed by the party to be charged; the other party’s signature is not required.","sub":"UCC Article 2 – Statute of Frauds","family":"Statute of Frauds","unit":68},{"n":135,"summary":"Buyer contracts for “healthiest” puppy from breeder’s future litter; breeder later puts collar with buyer’s name on one puppy; later crates and ships via delivery company.","trigger":"Seller marks/designates specific goods to the contract before delivery.","rule":"According to UCC 2-501, a good is identified when it is shipped, marked, or otherwise designated by the seller as goods to which the contract refers. Whichever occurs first","sub":"UCC Identification of Goods","family":"Scope","unit":49},{"n":136,"summary":"Seller delivered 995 of 1,000 chairs; buyer accepted and paid; buyer sues for breach","trigger":"Non-installment UCC contract invokes perfect tender rule","rule":"Under UCC 2-601, perfect tender is required for any contract that is not an installment contract.","sub":"UCC Performance (Perfect Tender)","family":"Performance","unit":8},{"n":137,"summary":"Buyer contracts in writing to buy 1,000 sets of specially manufactured, non‑standard ball bearings for $10/set (seller’s cost $8/set), delivery in 60 days. After seller completes production, buyer repudiates. Seller resells the bearings to a salvage company for $2/set after notifying buyer and then sues the buyer for damages.","trigger":"Buyer repudiates; seller resells in good faith; UCC damages measured by contract price minus resale price (plus incidentals, minus saved costs).","rule":"For buyer repudiation under the UCC, a seller who resells in good faith can recover contract price − resale price (plus incidentals, minus saved costs).","sub":"UCC Remedies – Seller resale damages","family":"Remedies","unit":57},{"n":138,"summary":"City offered reward; thefts dropped; years later tip led to arrest; reward claimed","trigger":"Passage of time after public reward offer","rule":"A reward is a unilateral offer accepted only by complete performance with knowledge of the offer. A promise to perform is insufficient. The offer is generally revocable until performance is completed, though revocation may be barred once performance has begun if revocation would be unfair. Actual knowledge of the reward is required, whether the offer is public or private. A reward offer may lapse after a reasonable time or due to changed circumstances, and revocation must be communicated in a manner at least as public as the offer.","sub":"Unilateral Contracts","family":"Formation","unit":50},{"n":139,"summary":"An uncle tells his nephew: “If you mow my lawn by the end of the week, I’ll give you $20.” The nephew says he is not sure he will have time. The next day, the nephew goes to the uncle’s house and begins mowing. After mowing about half, the mower runs out of gas, and the nephew does not complete the job. The question asks whether the uncle must pay the nephew $20.","trigger":"Unilateral offer accepted only by completion; beginning performance vs completing performance.","rule":"In a unilateral contract, acceptance requires complete performance; beginning performance may make the offer temporarily irrevocable but does not entitle the offeree to payment unless performance is completed.","sub":"Unilateral Contracts – Acceptance by performance","family":"Formation","unit":29},{"n":140,"summary":"A client emailed an attorney (who had represented him before): ‘Please review these documents and let me know if they are acceptable.’ Without replying, the attorney began reviewing the documents. Before the attorney responded, the client revoked the request. The dispute is whether the attorney’s start of work created a binding contract.","trigger":"Unilateral contract: offer invites performance; beginning performance can make the offer temporarily irrevocable, with acceptance upon completion of performance.","rule":"Unilateral contract: offer invites performance. Beginning performance on a unilateral offer creates an irrevocable offer for a reasonable time and creates an acceptance once the performance is completed.","sub":"Unilateral Contracts – Beginning Performance","family":"Formation","unit":29}]},{"slug":"civpro","name":"Civil Procedure","bigPicture":"WHERE YOU START. One judiciary. The CPC allocates competence by matter, territory and amount, and the TSJ sits above everything. A professional judge takes the evidence, finds the facts, and writes a reasoned sentencia; the appeal is a second full examination of the case, and casación corrects errors of law. Defects of jurisdiction and public order may be raised at any stage — the judge notices them on his own motion. Cosa juzgada requires the triple identity of parties, object and cause (C.C. art. 1.395(3)). Procedure is written, and the expediente IS the case.\n\nWHAT THE U.S. SYSTEM IS. Two court systems run in parallel, federal and state, and WHICH ONE hears the case changes the applicable law (Erie), the devices available, and sometimes the result — removal, venue transfer and supplemental jurisdiction are all machinery for moving between them. Facts are found by a lay JURY, and an entire apparatus exists to protect and police that allocation: summary judgment asks whether a dispute exists without weighing credibility; a motion for judgment must be made BEFORE the verdict to be available after it; instruction errors must be objected to on the spot. And almost every right is TIMED: Rule 12 defences, the jury demand, the removal window, the sanctions safe harbour, compulsory counterclaims — a valid objection raised one filing too late is gone forever.\n\nWHAT DIVERGES. Three trained habits generate the wrong answer. (1) Asking what the law IS instead of which system's law APPLIES — in U.S. litigation the sovereign question comes first. (2) Treating any serious defect as public order, examinable whenever it surfaces: only subject-matter jurisdiction behaves that way; personal jurisdiction, venue and most defences die on a schedule, and the merits of the objection are irrelevant once the deadline passes. (3) Expecting the appeal to re-examine the facts, and preclusion to require triple identity: U.S. claim preclusion bars everything arising from the same transaction whether or not you pleaded it, attaches to many non-trial dispositions, and lets a total stranger borrow a finding made against you.\n\nWHAT IS THE SAME. Amendment of pleadings is even more liberal than you expect, but rests on your instinct; impleader is the llamada en garantía (CPC art. 370) and permissive joinder your acumulación; privity tracks the causahabiente, and res inter alios acta survives; standing is recognizable as cualidad e interés procesal; supplemental jurisdiction over related claims is conexidad. Bank those, then spend everything you have on two things this module tests relentlessly: the CALENDAR, and which sovereign's law governs.","families":[{"name":"Preclusion","count":19},{"name":"Personal Jurisdiction","count":14},{"name":"SMJ","count":10},{"name":"Jury Trial","count":9},{"name":"Appeals","count":8},{"name":"Removal","count":8},{"name":"Supplemental Jurisdiction","count":8},{"name":"Venue","count":8},{"name":"Post-Trial","count":7},{"name":"Discovery","count":5},{"name":"Erie Doctrine","count":5},{"name":"Pleadings","count":5},{"name":"Summary Judgment","count":5},{"name":"Dismissals","count":4},{"name":"Sanctions","count":4},{"name":"Service of Process","count":4},{"name":"Class Actions","count":3},{"name":"Injunctions","count":3},{"name":"Amendments","count":2},{"name":"Interpleader","count":2},{"name":"Joinder","count":2},{"name":"Jurisdiction","count":2},{"name":"Justiciability","count":1}],"units":[{"n":1,"summary":"A federal court sitting in diversity (or hearing supplemental state-law claims) must decide whether a disputed issue is governed by federal law or by state law.","trigger":"Diversity or supplemental state-law claim + apparent conflict between a federal rule or practice and state law.","rule":"A federal court sitting in diversity applies state substantive law and federal procedural law (Rules of Decision Act; Erie R.R. v. Tompkins). If a valid Federal Rule of Civil Procedure or federal statute is on point and directly conflicts with state law, the federal directive governs (Hanna v. Plumer; Rules Enabling Act validity). Only in the absence of a federal directive on point does the court apply the unguided Erie analysis, asking whether ignoring state law would encourage forum shopping or produce inequitable administration of the laws (the twin aims).","sub":"Erie Doctrine – Governing-Law Decision Tree (Master Unit)","sensitivity":"The first question is never “is this issue procedural?” in the abstract; it is “is there a valid federal directive on point?” That gate decides the track: FRCP on point → federal rule applies regardless of outcome effects (Unit 2); no directive → state packages travel with the claim (Unit 3). The same subject (class actions, transfer, pleading) flips depending solely on which track it enters.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “One court, one legal system — the forum’s procedural code and substantive code both apply” → a U.S. federal courtroom runs two legal systems at once, and the law applied depends on a vertical choice-of-law analysis with no civil-law analogue. A civilian lawyer trained under a unitary Código Civil and Código de Procedimiento Civil has no schema for a court applying another sovereign’s substantive law under its own procedure; the instinct is to classify the issue by its label (sustantivo/procesal) — exactly the shortcut Hanna rejects in favor of the is-there-a-federal-directive gate.","authority":"Erie master: Rules of Decision Act, 28 U.S.C. § 1652; Erie R.R. v. Tompkins, 304 U.S. 64 (1938); Hanna v. Plumer, 380 U.S. 460 (1965); Rules Enabling Act, 28 U.S.C. § 2072."},{"n":2,"summary":"State law restricts a procedural device (bans class actions against in-state companies; requires pleading facts the federal rules treat as defenses; makes forum-selection clauses unenforceable), but a Federal Rule or statute addresses the same subject.","trigger":"Diversity case + valid FRCP or federal statute directly on point (Rule 23, Rule 8, 28 U.S.C. § 1404) + conflicting state rule.","rule":"When a valid Federal Rule of Civil Procedure or federal statute is on point, it displaces conflicting state law in federal court: Rule 23 governs class certification despite a state class-action ban (Shady Grove); federal pleading standards govern despite state pleading requirements; § 1404(a) governs transfer despite state hostility to forum-selection clauses (Stewart v. Ricoh).","sub":"Erie Doctrine – Federal Directive On Point (Hanna Track)","sensitivity":"The displacement is categorical, not interest-balanced: once the federal directive is valid and on point, state policy — however strong — is irrelevant in federal court. The trap runs both ways: state “procedural” rules survive when NO federal rule covers the issue, which is Unit 3’s territory.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The state legislature forbade this device, so a court in that state cannot use it” → in federal court the FRCP prevails and the state ban is displaced. The civilian schema treats procedural codes as territorial and mandatory (normas de orden público procesal); the idea that removal to a different courthouse in the same city changes which procedure governs — and can revive a device state law prohibits — has no analogue and is a recurring wrong-answer generator.","authority":"Federal directive on point: Shady Grove Orthopedic Assocs. v. Allstate, 559 U.S. 393 (2010) (Rule 23); Stewart Org. v. Ricoh, 487 U.S. 22 (1988) (§ 1404(a)); Hanna (Rule 4)."},{"n":3,"summary":"No federal rule addresses the disputed issue (choice of law, damages caps, statutes of limitations, elements and burdens); the federal court must decide whose state law applies and whether it must follow it.","trigger":"Diversity case + no valid federal directive on point + issue bound up with state-created rights.","rule":"Absent a federal directive on point, the federal court applies state law to substantive issues and to any rule whose disregard would encourage forum shopping or inequitable administration (twin aims): it must apply the forum state's choice-of-law rules (Klaxon), state statutes of limitations, state damages caps (including punitive-damages limits, absent conflict with federal law), and state substantive elements and burdens.","sub":"Erie Doctrine – No Federal Directive (State Packages Travel)","sensitivity":"The state law that travels is a package selected by the forum state's own conflicts rules — the federal court sits, in effect, as another courtroom of the forum state (including applying the substantive law the state court would choose, even another state's). A state rule labeled 'procedural' (limitations, caps) still binds if outcome-determinative in the twin-aims sense.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “A federal court applies federal law; and limitation periods are procedural, so the forum's own rules govern” → both instincts fail: the federal court borrows the forum STATE's conflicts rules (Klaxon) and treats limitations and caps as binding state law. The civilian lex fori instinct for anything labeled procesal produces systematic errors precisely on the issues (prescripción, quantum) the exam most often tests.","authority":"State packages travel: Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec., 313 U.S. 487 (1941) (forum state's conflicts rules); Guaranty Trust v. York (limitations); Gasperini (damages review); state punitive-damages caps absent federal conflict."},{"n":4,"summary":"An out-of-state defendant objects that the forum court cannot adjudicate claims against it; the claim arises (or not) from the defendant's contacts with the forum (products shipped, intentional acts aimed at the forum, third parties bringing goods there).","trigger":"Nonresident defendant + forum contacts + claim arising out of or relating to those contacts + fairness factors.","rule":"Specific personal jurisdiction requires that (1) the defendant purposefully availed itself of the forum (its own deliberate contacts — unilateral acts of third parties do not count, though intentional conduct expressly aimed at the forum does: the Calder effects test), (2) the claim arises out of or relates to those contacts, and (3) exercising jurisdiction comports with fair play and substantial justice (International Shoe; World-Wide Volkswagen).","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – Specific Jurisdiction (Master Unit)","sensitivity":"Three independent gates, each a separate wrong-answer generator: foreseeability alone fails gate 1 (the buyer who drives the car to the forum creates no contact); a deliberate forum contact unrelated to the claim fails gate 2 (relatedness); and gates 1–2 satisfied can still fail on fairness only in rare cases. The intentional-tort variant (effects test) satisfies gate 1 through aiming, not physical contact.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The court where the harm occurred is competent — competencia territorial” → U.S. personal jurisdiction is not statutory venue allocation but a CONSTITUTIONAL due-process limit personal to the defendant: waivable, defendant-focused, and indifferent to where evidence or victims are. The civilian schema asks ‘which court does the code assign?’; the U.S. question is ‘what did the DEFENDANT do to submit itself to this sovereign?’ — a shift from case-allocation to defendant-protection that reorders every analysis.","authority":"Specific jurisdiction: International Shoe v. Washington, 326 U.S. 310 (1945); World-Wide Volkswagen v. Woodson, 444 U.S. 286 (1980) (unilateral acts/foreseeability); Calder v. Jones, 465 U.S. 783 (1984) (effects test); Ford Motor v. Montana (relatedness)."},{"n":5,"summary":"Plaintiff sues a defendant in a forum unrelated to the claim, relying on the defendant's ongoing presence there (an office, sales, residence); defendant moves to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction.","trigger":"Claim unrelated to forum contacts + defendant's affiliation with the forum asserted as continuous/systematic.","rule":"General (all-purpose) jurisdiction exists only where the defendant is “at home”: for a corporation (and an LLC treated the same way for this purpose), its state of incorporation/formation and its principal place of business (Daimler; Goodyear); for an individual, the state of domicile. A branch office, dispatch center, or regular sales — even substantial ones — do not make a defendant at home; with general jurisdiction the claim need not relate to the forum at all.","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – General Jurisdiction (“At Home”)","sensitivity":"The dispositive variable is the CATEGORY of jurisdiction asserted: unrelated claim → only at-home fora will do; related claim → specific-jurisdiction analysis (Unit 4) rescues fora where the defendant is not at home. An in-forum office answers purposeful availment but NOT at-home status — conflating the two is the standard trap.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The company has an establishment here, so it can be sued here for anything” → only incorporation/PPB (or domicile) support all-purpose suit. The civilian schema of demandar donde el demandado tiene su establecimiento (general venue at any place of business) maps directly onto the WRONG answer: post-Daimler, doing business — however systematic — is not enough.","authority":"General jurisdiction: Goodyear v. Brown, 564 U.S. 915 (2011); Daimler AG v. Bauman, 571 U.S. 117 (2014); Milliken v. Meyer (individual domicile)."},{"n":6,"summary":"A lawsuit involves an LLC and the court must assess both whether it can be sued in the forum (personal jurisdiction) and whether diversity jurisdiction exists (subject-matter jurisdiction).","trigger":"LLC as a party + personal-jurisdiction question and/or diversity-citizenship question.","rule":"The same LLC is measured by two different tests: for PERSONAL jurisdiction it is treated like a corporation — at home in its state of formation and principal place of business; for DIVERSITY (subject-matter) jurisdiction it is a citizen of every state of which any of its members is a citizen — membership controls, and one member sharing the plaintiff's state destroys complete diversity.","sub":"Jurisdiction – The LLC Dual Test (PJ vs. Diversity Citizenship)","sensitivity":"One entity, two structural identities: the exam builds questions where the LLC is “at home” in State B (PJ analysis) yet a citizen of States A and B (diversity analysis) because of its members — the correct answer flips depending on which jurisdictional doctrine the question is really testing. Cross-reference: Unit 5 (at-home test); corporate diversity citizenship uses incorporation + nerve center instead.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “an entity has one legal seat (domicilio social) that answers every jurisdictional question” → U.S. law assigns the SAME entity different identities for different jurisdictional doctrines. The civilian single-seat schema collapses the two tests into one; the framework encodes them as independent switches that must be selected by doctrine, not by entity.","authority":"LLC dual test: PJ — at-home analysis by analogy to corporations; diversity — citizenship of every member (Americold Realty v. Conagra, 577 U.S. 378 (2016)); corporate diversity citizenship: 28 U.S.C. § 1332(c)(1), Hertz v. Friend (nerve center)."},{"n":7,"summary":"An individual defendant is personally handed the summons while briefly present in the forum (a one-day conference, a layover); the claim arose elsewhere. A corporation's officer is served the same way, and the plaintiff argues this binds the corporation.","trigger":"Individual defendant + in-hand service while physically present in the forum + claim unrelated to the forum.","rule":"Transient (“tag”) jurisdiction is valid: personal service on an individual voluntarily present in the forum supports jurisdiction even over unrelated claims (Burnham). It binds only the individual — serving an officer while present does not create jurisdiction over the corporation, whose amenability is measured by its own contacts (Units 4–5). Service mechanics remain Rule 4's: by any nonparty at least 18 years old.","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – Transient (“Tag”) Jurisdiction","sensitivity":"Two switches: (1) WHO was served — tag reaches the individual served, never the entity he works for; (2) presence must be voluntary (not procured by fraud). Tag is a standalone basis: no minimum-contacts analysis is run at all.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “A person passing through cannot be sued there for something unrelated — no court would accept that” → Burnham says otherwise, categorically. Nothing in civilian competencia resembles jurisdiction founded on the physical act of delivering papers to a traveler; the civilian reflex dismisses the correct answer as absurd, which is precisely why the exam tests it.","authority":"Tag jurisdiction: Burnham v. Superior Court, 495 U.S. 604 (1990); service mechanics: Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(c)(2), 4(e)."},{"n":8,"summary":"Unable (or unwilling) to establish jurisdiction over the defendant personally, the plaintiff attaches the defendant's in-forum property (a storage facility, a car) and asks the court to adjudicate a claim unrelated to that property.","trigger":"Attachment of in-forum property + underlying claim unrelated to the property + defendant not otherwise subject to personal jurisdiction.","rule":"Quasi in rem jurisdiction survives in form but, after Shaffer v. Heitner, the exercise must satisfy the same minimum-contacts due-process analysis as in personam jurisdiction; the mere presence of property is not enough where the claim is unrelated to the property. Forum statutes may additionally require reasonable efforts to obtain in personam jurisdiction before resorting to attachment.","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – Quasi In Rem After Shaffer","sensitivity":"The property is only a remedial hook, not a jurisdictional shortcut: if minimum contacts exist, quasi in rem is unnecessary; if they don't, it is unconstitutional — the doctrine matters mainly where statutes channel the sequence (attempt personal service first). Judgment reaches only the value of the attached property.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Where the debtor has assets, there is jurisdiction — embargo the asset and litigate” → post-Shaffer the asset alone no longer confers adjudicatory power. The civilian schema of attachment-based competence (medidas preventivas sobre bienes situados en la jurisdicción) validates exactly the pre-1977 rule the Supreme Court abolished; the misalignment is applying a doctrine that is one constitutional revolution out of date.","authority":"Quasi in rem: Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186 (1977); statutory sequencing requirements for attachment."},{"n":9,"summary":"A federal statute creating the claim authorizes nationwide service of process; the defendant is served outside the forum state and objects that it lacks contacts with that state.","trigger":"Federal-question claim + federal statute authorizing nationwide service (Rule 4(k)(1)(C)) + defendant's contacts with the forum state insufficient under state long-arm analysis.","rule":"Where a federal statute provides for nationwide service of process, the federal court's personal jurisdiction is assessed against the defendant's contacts with the United States as a whole (Fifth Amendment due process), not with the forum state; statutorily authorized service anywhere in the country suffices.","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – Nationwide Service / National Contacts","sensitivity":"The sovereign whose contacts matter switches with the source of the service authorization: ordinary cases borrow the forum state's long-arm and Fourteenth Amendment limits (Rule 4(k)(1)(A)); a nationwide-service statute switches the measuring sovereign to the nation. Same defendant, same conduct — different constitutional yardstick.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “no contacts with this state, no jurisdiction” → with a nationwide-service statute the state-contacts question is simply the wrong question. The civilian unitary schema cannot generate the idea that WHICH sovereign's contacts count is itself a variable set by statute; the misalignment is running the default state-focused analysis in the one context where federal architecture displaces it.","authority":"Nationwide service: Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(k)(1)(C); Fifth Amendment national-contacts analysis (e.g., statutory interpleader, 28 U.S.C. § 2361)."},{"n":10,"summary":"A defendant objects to personal jurisdiction — either too late (after answering on the merits) or by ignoring the suit entirely, defaulting, and resisting enforcement in its home state.","trigger":"PJ defense + timing of its assertion: first Rule 12 response, later amendment, or total non-appearance followed by collateral attack.","rule":"Lack of personal jurisdiction is waived if omitted from the defendant's first Rule 12 response (motion or answer); it can be restored only by an amendment as a matter of course within 21 days of serving the answer (Rule 12(h)(1)(B)(ii)). Alternatively, a defendant who never appears may suffer a default judgment and attack it collaterally for want of personal jurisdiction in the enforcement court — but a defendant who appeared and litigated jurisdiction (or waived it) is bound.","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – Waiver (Rule 12) and Collateral Attack","sensitivity":"Two mutually exclusive strategies with a strict fork: appear-and-object (must be in the FIRST response; preserved thereafter) or never-appear-and-collaterally-attack (all merits defenses are forfeited; the only issue open later is jurisdiction). Choosing both — answering on the merits, then objecting — gets neither.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “jurisdiction is a matter of public order — the court examines it at any stage, even on its own motion” → that is true of U.S. SUBJECT-MATTER jurisdiction, but personal jurisdiction is a waivable personal privilege lost by one mis-sequenced filing. The civilian schema (falta de jurisdicción/competencia declarable de oficio) transfers correctly to SMJ and catastrophically to PJ — the framework encodes the pair as opposite poles of the same instinct.","authority":"Waiver and collateral attack: Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(g)–(h); Rule 15(a)(1) (amendment as of course); Ins. Corp. of Ireland v. Compagnie des Bauxites, 456 U.S. 694 (1982) (PJ waivable); collateral attack limited to defendants who never appeared."},{"n":11,"summary":"A plaintiff pleads only state-law claims in state court; the defendant holds a federal defense, or a federal counterclaim, and seeks removal on federal-question grounds.","trigger":"State-court action + federal issue appearing only in a defense or counterclaim (not on the face of the complaint).","rule":"Federal-question jurisdiction — and removal based on it — requires the federal issue to appear on the face of the plaintiff's well-pleaded complaint; a federal defense or a federal counterclaim (even a compulsory one) cannot support it. The plaintiff is master of the complaint and may defeat removal by pleading only state claims.","sub":"Removal – Well-Pleaded Complaint Rule (Master)","sensitivity":"The screen looks at a single structural location: the complaint's face. The identical federal statute is jurisdiction-creating when it grounds the claim and jurisdictionally irrelevant when it grounds the answer or a counterclaim (Holmes Group / Home Depot). Satellites: forum-defendant rule (Unit 12), mechanics (Unit 13).","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: \"The case turns on federal law, so the federal court can take it\" → not unless the federal issue is on the complaint's face. In a unitary judiciary, which court hears a case never depends on who raises an issue; the civilian schema has no filter comparable to the well-pleaded complaint rule, so the trained instinct counts the defendant's federal defense — exactly what the rule excludes.","authority":"Well-pleaded complaint: Louisville & Nashville R.R. v. Mottley; Holmes Group v. Vornado; Home Depot v. Jackson (counterclaims); 28 U.S.C. § 1331."},{"n":12,"summary":"A defendant sued in the state where it is a citizen removes the case: in one version the claim is state-law (diversity); in another the plaintiff has added a federal claim.","trigger":"Removal by a home-state defendant + basis of federal jurisdiction (diversity vs. federal question).","rule":"Under the forum-defendant rule (28 U.S.C. § 1441(b)(2)), a case removable solely on diversity may not be removed if any properly joined and served defendant is a citizen of the forum state; the rule does not apply to federal-question removal — a home-state defendant may remove a federal claim.","sub":"Removal – Forum-Defendant Rule (§ 1441(b)(2))","sensitivity":"One structural variable — the jurisdictional basis — flips the result for the identical defendant: diversity → removal barred at home; federal question → removal available. The rule's rationale (diversity protects outsiders from local bias; a local defendant needs no protection) is itself the memory anchor.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: \"A defendant can always move the case to the federal system\" (or its inverse, \"a local defendant never can\") → both wrong: the bar is basis-specific. With no analogue to bias-driven diversity jurisdiction, the civilian schema cannot generate the rationale, so the rule reads as arbitrary and is misapplied in both directions; anchoring it to the anti-bias purpose restores predictability.","authority":"Forum-defendant rule: 28 U.S.C. § 1441(b)(2) (diversity only)."},{"n":13,"summary":"Multiple defendants are served at different times; a plaintiff later amends to add a federal claim or a new defendant; a party seeks removal or remand and the timing/consent requirements are disputed. In one version the PLAINTIFF tries to remove.","trigger":"Removal mechanics + who may remove + unanimity + 30-day windows + remand timing.","rule":"Only defendants may remove (never the plaintiff, even against a counterclaim). All properly joined and served defendants must consent (unanimity); removal runs to the district embracing the state court; each defendant has 30 days from its own service, and an amended pleading that first makes the case removable opens a new 30-day window. A motion to remand for procedural defects must come within 30 days of removal; remand for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction may come at any time.","sub":"Removal – Mechanics: Who, Unanimity, Windows, Remand","sensitivity":"Four independent sequence gates, each dispositive alone: (1) party identity (defendants only); (2) unanimity of served defendants; (3) the moving 30-day windows (per-defendant service; revival by amendment); (4) the asymmetric remand clock — procedural defects expire in 30 days, SMJ defects never do (mirror of Unit 10's waiver poles).","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: \"The objection is valid, so it can be raised when the party gets to it\" → procedural removal defects die at day 30 while SMJ defects are immortal. Civilian procedure has forum objections but no two-clock architecture distinguishing waivable mechanics from unwaivable subject-matter power; the schema treats all forum defects alike and misses both deadlines and immortality.","authority":"Removal mechanics: 28 U.S.C. §§ 1441(a), 1446(b) (30-day windows; unanimity), 1447(c) (remand: procedural defects 30 days; SMJ anytime); only defendants remove (Shamrock Oil)."},{"n":14,"summary":"Diversity or amount-in-controversy is challenged based on events occurring before filing (a party relocates or reincorporates), after filing, or on proof at trial that damages are lower than pleaded.","trigger":"Objection to diversity/AIC + the moment the jurisdictional facts are measured.","rule":"Diversity and amount in controversy are determined at the time the complaint is filed — not when the events occurred and not at trial; if the complaint is amended, jurisdiction is assessed as of the amended complaint. The plaintiff's good-faith amount controls unless it appears to a legal certainty that the claim could never exceed $75,000; later proof of smaller damages does not oust jurisdiction, but legal certainty that the amount was never available does.","sub":"SMJ – Timing of Measurement (Filing; Amendment; Legal Certainty)","sensitivity":"Three snapshots resolve every variant: (1) filing date is the default measuring moment; (2) an amended complaint resets the snapshot; (3) the legal-certainty test polices the pleaded amount prospectively, not retrospectively — losing on damages ≠ losing jurisdiction.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: \"Jurisdiction follows the facts as they truly turn out\" → U.S. law freezes jurisdiction at a procedural snapshot. The civilian cuantía governs court hierarchy but is fixed by the demand and rarely litigated; the U.S. system makes the snapshot itself adversarial terrain — post-filing moves are irrelevant, pre-filing manipulation works, and trial results almost never matter.","authority":"SMJ timing: diversity/AIC measured at filing (Grupo Dataflux principle); amended complaint resets; legal-certainty test (St. Paul Mercury)."},{"n":15,"summary":"Several plaintiffs injured in one incident (family members poisoned by the same food) sue together in diversity; each claims less than $75,000 but the total exceeds it.","trigger":"Multiple plaintiffs + separate injuries + no single claim exceeding $75,000.","rule":"Separate plaintiffs cannot aggregate separate claims to satisfy the amount in controversy unless they enforce a single title or right in which they share a common and undivided interest; if at least one plaintiff independently exceeds $75,000, supplemental jurisdiction (§ 1367) may carry the related claims of the others.","sub":"SMJ – Aggregation of Claims (Common & Undivided Interest)","sensitivity":"Common and undivided' means one right owned by the group as a unit (the defendant owes one lump obligation) — not 'same accident.' The anchor-plaintiff route is the modern escape valve: one qualifying claim opens § 1367 for the rest, subject to complete diversity.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: \"The family's claims add up, so the case is big enough\" → separate personal injuries never aggregate. Civilian acumulación de pretensiones has no jurisdictional amount attached to it; the schema sums freely. The U.S. rule indexes aggregation to the structure of the right — unitary vs. several — a distinction the civilian filter does not compute.","authority":"Aggregation: no aggregation of separate claims across plaintiffs; common & undivided interest exception; anchor plaintiff + § 1367 (Exxon Mobil v. Allapattah)."},{"n":16,"summary":"Diversity is disputed and the parties include a corporation, an estate's representative, and/or an LLC; each entity's citizenship must be located.","trigger":"Diversity analysis + non-individual parties (corporation, estate, LLC).","rule":"For diversity: an individual is a citizen of the state of domicile; a corporation of every state of incorporation AND the state of its principal place of business (nerve center / headquarters); the legal representative of a decedent's estate takes the citizenship of the DECEDENT (28 U.S.C. § 1332(c)(2)), not the representative's own; an unincorporated entity (LLC, partnership) is a citizen of every state of every member.","sub":"SMJ – Citizenship Table (Corporation; Estate; LLC)","sensitivity":"Each party type carries its own locator, and mixing them is the tested error: nerve center (corporation) ≠ members (LLC) ≠ decedent (estate). Cross-reference Unit 6: the LLC's diversity citizenship (members) is NOT its PJ home (formation + PPB) — same entity, two tests.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: \"An entity belongs where its seat (domicilio/sede social) is\" → only partly true, and only for corporations. The civilian single-seat schema (domicilio social) collapses all entities into one locator; U.S. law assigns each entity form a different citizenship algorithm, and the estate rule (decedent, not representative) directly contradicts the instinct that the acting party's own status controls.","authority":"Citizenship: 28 U.S.C. § 1332(c)(1) (corporation; nerve center, Hertz v. Friend); § 1332(c)(2) (estate representative takes decedent's citizenship); unincorporated entities take every member's citizenship (Americold). Cf. Unit 6."},{"n":17,"summary":"In a diversity case, additional claims lacking independent jurisdiction are asserted: a compulsory counterclaim under $75,000, a crossclaim between co-defendants of the same state, a third-party impleader claim, or the plaintiff tries to sue the impleaded third party.","trigger":"Anchor claim with SMJ + related claim lacking independent SMJ + identity of the party asserting it.","rule":"Supplemental jurisdiction (28 U.S.C. § 1367(a)) covers claims forming part of the same case or controversy (common nucleus of operative fact) — compulsory counterclaims, crossclaims, and impleader claims qualify regardless of amount or shared citizenship. In diversity cases, § 1367(b) withdraws supplemental jurisdiction ONLY for claims by PLAINTIFFS against persons made parties under Rules 14, 19, 20, or 24 where exercising it would defeat complete diversity.","sub":"Supplemental Jurisdiction – § 1367(a)/(b) (Master)","sensitivity":"The dispositive variable is WHO asserts the claim: defendants and third parties ride § 1367(a) freely; only the plaintiff's claims are policed by § 1367(b) (anti-evasion of complete diversity). Hence: defendant's counterclaim under the amount — in; same-state crossclaim — in; plaintiff's claim against a same-state third-party defendant — out.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: \"Related claims travel with the main case\" → true as instinct (conexidad/acumulación), but the civilian schema misses the party-identity filter: it either blocks legitimate defendant-side claims (over-applying diversity) or admits the plaintiff's evasive claim that § 1367(b) exists to stop. The asymmetry — plaintiffs policed, defendants free — has no codal analogue.","authority":"Supplemental jurisdiction: 28 U.S.C. § 1367(a) (same case or controversy; Gibbs common nucleus); § 1367(b) (restricts only plaintiffs' claims in diversity)."},{"n":18,"summary":"The anchor federal claim is dismissed (or settles) while a related state-law claim remains in federal court; the plaintiff fears the statute of limitations if forced to refile in state court.","trigger":"Federal claims drop out + surviving supplemental state claim + limitations risk on refiling.","rule":"Whether to retain or dismiss the remaining state claim is discretionary under § 1367(c) (courts weigh judicial economy, convenience, fairness, comity; dismissal is common when federal claims drop early, retention when litigation is advanced). If dismissed, § 1367(d) tolls the state limitations period while the claim was pending and for at least 30 days after dismissal.","sub":"Supplemental Jurisdiction – Discretionary Decline & § 1367(d) Tolling","sensitivity":"Two-step architecture: the decline decision is discretionary (not mandatory — SMJ existed at filing and is not destroyed by the anchor's dismissal), and the tolling safety net makes dismissal non-fatal. Stage matters: early dismissal favors decline; a case past discovery favors retention.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: \"The main claim died, so the court lost jurisdiction and the refiled claim is time-barred\" → both halves wrong: retention is discretionary, and § 1367(d) tolling preserves the refiling. The civilian schema ties accessory claims' fate mechanically to the principal (accesorium sequitur principale) and knows interrupción of prescription by filing, but not a statutory cross-system toll designed for the federal-state handoff.","authority":"Decline & tolling: 28 U.S.C. § 1367(c) (discretion; Gibbs factors), § 1367(d) (tolling; Artis v. District of Columbia)."},{"n":19,"summary":"A claim arises under a federal statute (or a self-executing treaty); the plaintiff files in STATE court, and a party argues the state court cannot (or must) hear it.","trigger":"Federal claim + state forum + no express grant of exclusive federal jurisdiction.","rule":"State courts have concurrent jurisdiction over federal claims unless Congress makes federal jurisdiction exclusive; a suit to enforce rights under a self-executing treaty arises under federal law (federal-question jurisdiction) but may still be brought in state court, subject to the defendant's right of removal.","sub":"SMJ – Concurrent Jurisdiction; Treaties as Federal Questions","sensitivity":"The presumption runs toward concurrency — exclusivity is the exception requiring congressional statement. The treaty variant tests the same structure from the other side: 'arising under' includes self-executing treaties (Art. VI), yet federal character alone does not close the state courthouse.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: \"Federal law belongs in federal court\" → the default is the opposite: both systems' courts are presumptively open. A civilian expects materia to dictate an exclusive competent court (competencia por la materia is non-waivable and exclusive); the U.S. baseline of overlapping judicial power — with removal as the sorting valve — inverts that architecture.","authority":"Concurrent jurisdiction: presumption of concurrency (Tafflin v. Levitt; Gulf Offshore); self-executing treaties as federal questions (U.S. Const. art. VI)."},{"n":20,"summary":"A defendant holds a claim against the plaintiff arising from the same incident and does not plead it; or a co-defendant holds a related claim against another co-defendant and asserts (or omits) it.","trigger":"Same transaction or occurrence + claim against an OPPOSING party (counterclaim) vs. against a CO-party (crossclaim).","rule":"A counterclaim arising from the same transaction or occurrence as the opposing party's claim is COMPULSORY — it must be pleaded in the answer or it is waived (Rule 13(a)). A crossclaim against a co-party under Rule 13(g) must arise from the same transaction or occurrence (or relate to the same property) but is always PERMISSIVE — it may be brought later in a separate action; it may include derivative-liability claims and survives the co-party's settlement with the plaintiff.","sub":"Pleadings – Compulsory Counterclaims vs. Permissive Crossclaims","sensitivity":"Same relatedness test, opposite preclusive consequences, keyed solely to the parties' alignment: opposing party → use it or lose it; co-party → optional. Both ride supplemental jurisdiction regardless of amount or citizenship (Unit 17). Affirmative defenses follow the counterclaim logic: plead them in the answer or waive (Rule 8(c)).","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: \"I can always sue later in my own action\" → true for crossclaims, fatal for compulsory counterclaims. Civilian reconvención is voluntary; no code makes silence in the answer extinguish a cause of action. The alignment-indexed asymmetry (opposing vs. co-party) must be learned as structure — the instinct treats both as equally optional.","authority":"Counterclaims/crossclaims: Rule 13(a) (compulsory; waiver), 13(b), 13(g) (crossclaims permissive, same T/O, derivative claims); Rule 8(c) (affirmative defenses in the answer)."},{"n":21,"summary":"A plaintiff who lost (or won) a prior suit files a second action against the same opponent arising from the same underlying event, pleading a different legal theory or a different item of damage.","trigger":"Final judgment on the merits + same parties (or privity) + second claim arising from the same transaction or occurrence.","rule":"Claim preclusion (res judicata) bars a second action between the same parties (or their privies) on any claim arising from the same transaction or occurrence as a prior final judgment on the merits — regardless of the legal theory pleaded; alleged trial errors must be pursued by appeal, not by a new lawsuit.","sub":"Preclusion – Claim Preclusion (Master: Same Transaction or Occurrence)","sensitivity":"The unit of preclusion is the TRANSACTION, not the legal theory: negligence lost → strict liability barred; personal-injury judgment → property-damage claim from the same crash barred. Three gates, each with its own unit: finality/merits (Unit 22), party identity/privity (Unit 23), transactional scope (this unit).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Different cause of action — new suit allowed” → barred. Venezuelan cosa juzgada (C.C. art. 1,395(3)) requires the triple identity — eadem personae, eadem res, eadem causa petendi — so a new legal theory is a new causa and a second suit is natural. The U.S. transactional test erases the causa dimension: one occurrence = one lawsuit, and every theory or damage item not raised is extinguished. The trained instinct systematically under-predicts the bar’s breadth.","authority":"Claim preclusion master: transactional test (same transaction or occurrence / series); Restatement (Second) of Judgments §§ 24–25."},{"n":22,"summary":"A prior case ended without a full trial — by default, voluntary or involuntary dismissal, dismissal for lack of jurisdiction, or dismissal with leave to amend — and a party asserts (or resists) preclusion in a second suit.","trigger":"Second suit + dispute over whether the first disposition counts as a ‘final judgment on the merits’.","rule":"‘On the merits’ does not require a trial: a default judgment, a dismissal with prejudice, a Rule 12(b)(6) dismissal silent as to prejudice, and a second voluntary dismissal (two-dismissal rule) all preclude; a dismissal with leave to amend and a dismissal for lack of jurisdiction (or venue) do not (FRCP 41(b)).","sub":"Preclusion – What Counts as ‘Final on the Merits’","sensitivity":"Tabular switch — same doctrine, opposite outcomes on one variable: default → YES; with prejudice → YES; 12(b)(6) silent → YES (adjudication on the merits unless the order states otherwise); second voluntary dismissal → YES; leave to amend → NO (not final); SMJ/venue dismissal → NO (but the jurisdictional ruling itself binds). The involuntary/voluntary distinction gates the two-dismissal rule.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “No trial, no cosa juzgada” → preclusion attaches to many non-trial dispositions. In Venezuelan procedure the sentencia definitivamente firme that produces cosa juzgada material presupposes adjudication after contradictorio; default (confesión ficta) and procedural dismissals occupy different categories. The U.S. system treats finality as a policy of repose, not a certificate of actual adjudication — the instinct misses that a defendant who never appeared is fully bound.","authority":"Final on the merits: FRCP 41(a) (two-dismissal rule), 41(b) (involuntary dismissal as adjudication on the merits unless stated otherwise; jurisdiction/venue excepted)."},{"n":23,"summary":"After a final judgment involving one plaintiff or property owner, a related person — a successor purchaser of the property, or a different victim of the same tortfeasor — sues (or is sued) on the same claim.","trigger":"Second suit + party who was not named in the first action + question whether that party is bound by (or may invoke) the prior judgment.","rule":"Claim preclusion binds only the parties and those in privity with them — successors in interest to the same property or claim, represented parties, and those who controlled the prior litigation. A judgment against one tortfeasor does not bar a later suit against a different tortfeasor for the same injury; due process forbids binding a stranger to the first case.","sub":"Preclusion – Privity and Its Limits","sensitivity":"Privity runs through legal relationships to the SAME claim or asset (buyer of the nuisance-affected home inherits the paid-out claim); it never runs merely through factual similarity (different tortfeasor, different victim). The due-process floor is absolute: a person with no prior day in court cannot be bound (cf. Unit 24).","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian causahabiente doctrine tracks U.S. privity closely — successors take their author’s position, and res inter alios acta protects strangers. The instinct is broadly RIGHT here; the residual friction is directional: U.S. privity also lets the successor be blocked from re-collecting a claim already satisfied, and the ‘full compensation once’ logic (one satisfaction rule) has no separate codal label. A convergence unit — marked so the reasoner spends effort where systems actually diverge.","authority":"Privity: successors in interest, represented parties, control; due-process limit (Taylor v. Sturgell, 553 U.S. 880 (2008))."},{"n":24,"summary":"A party tries to re-litigate (or to prevent re-litigation of) a specific factual or legal issue — negligence, causation, validity — that a prior case addressed, where the target of preclusion either was or was not a party to that prior case.","trigger":"Prior judgment + same issue + question whether the issue was actually litigated, necessarily decided, and whether the person to be bound had a full and fair opportunity.","rule":"Issue preclusion (collateral estoppel) bars relitigation of an issue that was actually litigated, actually decided, and necessary to a prior final judgment — but only AGAINST a person who was a party (or privy) to the prior action and had a full and fair opportunity to litigate it; it can never be applied against a stranger to the first case (due process).","sub":"Preclusion – Issue Preclusion (Master: Elements & Due-Process Floor)","sensitivity":"Directionality is everything: ask WHO is being bound, not what was decided. The same finding binds the driver who litigated and lost, yet cannot touch the passenger who watched from outside the case. ‘Necessarily decided’ excludes alternative or unnecessary findings; ‘actually litigated’ excludes defaults and stipulations (contrast claim preclusion, Unit 22, where default DOES preclude).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The point was already decided — it stands for everyone” or its inverse, “no identity of parties, no effect at all.” Venezuelan cosa juzgada has no separate issue-preclusion doctrine: findings bind within the triple-identity frame or not at all. The U.S. system severs issues from claims — a finding can travel to a NEW claim (unlike cosa juzgada) but never to a NEW defendant-to-be-bound (due process). The instinct errs in both directions at once.","authority":"Issue preclusion elements: actually litigated, actually decided, necessary to the judgment, full and fair opportunity; Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27."},{"n":25,"summary":"A non-party to the first suit invokes its findings: a new defendant shields itself with the plaintiff’s prior loss (defensive), or a new plaintiff borrows a prior finding against the same defendant (offensive) — including plaintiffs who skipped a low-stakes first case or strategically waited.","trigger":"Issue actually litigated and decided against a party + a NON-party of the first action now seeks to use the finding for its benefit.","rule":"Mutuality is abandoned: non-mutual DEFENSIVE issue preclusion is generally allowed, and non-mutual OFFENSIVE preclusion is discretionary under Parklane — denied where the plaintiff could easily have joined the first action, where the defendant lacked incentive to litigate fully (low stakes), where the plaintiff adopted a wait-and-see strategy, or where procedural opportunities were limited. Federal courts must also give a state judgment only the preclusive effect the RENDERING state would give it (full faith and credit) — if that state retains mutuality, non-mutual use is unavailable.","sub":"Preclusion – Non-Mutual Issue Preclusion (Parklane Fairness; Full Faith & Credit)","sensitivity":"Two stacked switches: (1) direction — defensive (favored) vs offensive (discretionary, Parklane factors); (2) source of the judgment — the preclusion law of the rendering court travels with its judgment, so an identical finding is usable in one courthouse and worthless in another. The $500 verdict that no rational defendant fully litigated precludes nothing in a $40,000 sequel.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “no identity of parties → the finding is res inter alios acta and unusable” → U.S. law lets total strangers borrow findings, subject only to fairness screens. This is the sharpest inversion in the preclusion cluster: the civilian rule (mutuality) is precisely the rule the U.S. system abandoned. Once learned, the over-correction is the new trap — assuming borrowed findings work everywhere, when full faith and credit re-imports the rendering state’s (possibly mutualist) limits.","authority":"Non-mutual preclusion: Parklane Hosiery Co. v. Shore, 439 U.S. 322 (1979) (offensive factors); Blonder-Tongue (defensive); full faith and credit — 28 U.S.C. § 1738; Marrese v. American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons, 470 U.S. 373 (1985)."},{"n":26,"summary":"After a criminal proceeding ends — in acquittal or conviction — a civil suit based on the same conduct follows, and a party argues the criminal outcome settles the civil case.","trigger":"Criminal disposition + subsequent civil action on the same conduct + preclusion asserted from the criminal outcome.","rule":"A criminal ACQUITTAL never precludes a later civil action: failure to prove guilt beyond a reasonable doubt decides nothing about liability by a preponderance (different burdens; often different parties). A criminal CONVICTION, by contrast, may support issue preclusion in a later civil case, because every element was necessarily found under a HIGHER standard after full incentive to litigate.","sub":"Preclusion – Criminal–Civil Asymmetry (Burdens of Proof)","sensitivity":"The asymmetry is pure burden arithmetic: beyond-reasonable-doubt ➤ preponderance runs downhill (conviction precludes) but never uphill (acquittal doesn’t). Party identity adds a second gate — the civil plaintiff was usually not a party to the prosecution, so preclusion runs only against the convicted defendant, never in his favor.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “absuelto en lo penal, cerrado en lo civil” → the acquittal is worthless in the civil suit. Civil-law systems coordinate the two tracks doctrinally (cosa juzgada penal sobre lo civil; the civil action often rides inside the criminal case as acción civil resarcitoria). The U.S. system runs them as strangers connected only by burden logic — O.J. Simpson acquitted criminally, liable civilly — so the instinct that a criminal outcome ‘settles’ the civil question fails in the exact scenario most tested.","authority":"Criminal–civil asymmetry: acquittal (higher burden unmet) vs. conviction (issue preclusion available); different parties and burdens."},{"n":27,"summary":"A case mixes claims for damages and equitable relief (injunction); a party demands a jury on the legal claims and the parties dispute what is tried to whom, and in what order.","trigger":"Federal civil action + legal claim(s) for damages (value over $20) + equitable claim(s) + jury demand + overlapping factual issues.","rule":"The Seventh Amendment preserves the jury right for LEGAL claims (damages) but not for equitable claims (injunctions, specific performance). When both are present with overlapping issues, the jury tries the legal claims FIRST and its findings bind the judge’s later determination of the equitable claims (Beacon Theatres); the equitable claim cannot be advanced to moot the jury right.","sub":"Jury Trial – Seventh Amendment (Legal vs. Equitable; Order of Trial)","sensitivity":"Two switches: (1) the remedy sought classifies the claim — damages = jury, injunction = judge — regardless of subject matter; (2) sequencing is constitutionally loaded: jury first, judge bound by the jury’s findings on shared issues. Trying equity first would extinguish the jury right by preclusion — exactly what Beacon Theatres forbids.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the judge decides the facts — in what order hardly matters” → order determines WHO finds the facts, and the jury’s findings bind the judge. The civil jury simply does not exist in the Venezuelan system; nothing in prior training assigns constitutional weight to the law/equity line (a distinction with no codal meaning) or to trial sequencing. This is the module’s purest institutional void: the entire analytical vocabulary — legal vs. equitable, jury vs. bench — must be built from zero.","authority":"Jury right: U.S. Const. amend. VII ($20); Beacon Theatres, Inc. v. Westover, 359 U.S. 500 (1959) (jury first; findings bind the court); Dairy Queen v. Wood."},{"n":28,"summary":"A party wants (or wants to defeat) a jury: the demand is late or was never filed, the case was removed from a state court where juries are automatic, or the parties stipulate to a smaller jury or a non-unanimous verdict.","trigger":"Jury demand timing (Rule 38) or jury composition/verdict rules (Rule 48) + waiver, removal, or stipulation facts.","rule":"A jury must be demanded within 14 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (motions are not pleadings) or the right is waived; after removal, no new demand is needed if state law made the jury automatic or a proper demand was already made (Rule 81(c)(3)). A federal civil jury has 6–12 members; unless the parties stipulate otherwise the verdict must be unanimous and returned by at least 6 jurors; the parties may stipulate to fewer jurors or a non-unanimous verdict, and a party who stipulated cannot attack the verdict after losing. Either party may have the jury polled.","sub":"Jury Trial – Demand, Waiver, Size & Verdict (Rules 38, 48, 81(c))","sensitivity":"Three traps: (1) the 14-day clock runs from the last PLEADING — a Rule 12 motion does not restart it, but an amended complaint adding new issues does; (2) removal carryover protects the party who relied on automatic state juries; (3) verdict validity is measured against the STIPULATION actually made — reconcile the source questions: with no stipulation, fewer than 6 assenting jurors invalidates; with a stipulation, its terms control and post-verdict regret is waived.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the jury right is constitutional — it cannot be lost by missing a 14-day form” → it is waived precisely that way. For a civilian, fundamental procedural guarantees are self-executing and non-waivable by silence; the U.S. system makes the SACRED right depend on a PROFANE deadline. The double dissonance — constitutional right, bureaucratic forfeiture, plus party autonomy over jury size — is the friction; the demand clock is the single most mechanical trap in the cluster.","authority":"Demand, size & verdict: FRCP 38(b), (d) (14 days; waiver), 81(c)(3) (removal), 48(a)–(c) (6–12 jurors; unanimity unless stipulated; polling)."},{"n":29,"summary":"After an adverse jury verdict, a party asks the judge to enter judgment in its favor for insufficient evidence — but did not move for JMOL before the case went to the jury, or files the renewed motion late.","trigger":"Jury verdict + renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law (JNOV) + preservation and timing facts.","rule":"A renewed JMOL (Rule 50(b)) is available only if the party moved for JMOL BEFORE the case was submitted to the jury; the renewed motion must be filed within 28 days after entry of judgment, and Rule 6(b)(2) forbids any extension — by agreement, good cause, or court order. The standard: judgment as a matter of law only if no legally sufficient evidentiary basis supports the verdict; the judge may not weigh evidence or assess credibility.","sub":"Post-Trial – JMOL / Renewed JMOL (Rule 50: Preservation, 28 Days, Standard)","sensitivity":"Three independent kill-switches, each dispositive alone: (1) no pre-verdict motion → renewed motion and appellate sufficiency review are both forfeited; (2) day 29 → too late forever, even by stipulation (contrast most deadlines, which courts may extend); (3) ANY legally sufficient evidence → motion denied — the judge asks ‘is there evidence,’ never ‘is it credible.’","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the verdict is against the evidence — ask the judge (or appeal) to overturn it” → without the pre-verdict motion there is nothing to renew and nothing to appeal on sufficiency. The civilian schema of apelación — full re-review of facts by a higher court as of right — makes the preservation architecture invisible: relief exists only for those who objected BEFORE the verdict, and the 28-day bar is jurisdictionally rigid. The instinct arrives after the door has closed.","authority":"JMOL/renewed JMOL: FRCP 50(a)–(b) (pre-verdict motion prerequisite; 28 days), 6(b)(2) (no extensions); legally-sufficient-evidence standard, no credibility weighing."},{"n":30,"summary":"A trial goes wrong in front of the jury: the judge gives an erroneous instruction and counsel objects too late (or not at all), or post-trial evidence shows the verdict was tainted (juror bribery, misconduct).","trigger":"Instruction error + objection timing (before vs. after the jury retires) OR verdict tainted by juror misconduct + choice of post-trial remedy.","rule":"Objections to jury instructions must be made on the record before the jury retires to deliberate (Rule 51); an unpreserved error is reviewable only for PLAIN error — an obvious, serious mistake affecting substantial rights. A verdict tainted by juror bribery or misconduct is remedied by a MOTION FOR A NEW TRIAL (Rule 59), not by JMOL — the taint infects the process, not the sufficiency of the evidence.","sub":"Post-Trial – Instructions (Rule 51) & New Trial (Rule 59)","sensitivity":"Two remedy-selection switches: (1) WHEN the objection came — before the jury retires (preserved, ordinary review) vs. after (plain error only); (2) WHAT went wrong — insufficient evidence → JMOL (Unit 29); process contamination (bribery, misconduct, verdict against the great weight) → new trial. Choosing the wrong post-trial vehicle loses the motion even on true facts.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the instruction was wrong / the jury was bribed — the judgment falls” → only through the correct vehicle, on the correct clock. The civilian nulidad-de-actuaciones schema treats grave defects as voiding the proceeding whenever discovered; U.S. law channels each defect through a specific, waivable motion. Convergence note: the new-trial remedy itself resembles civilian invalidación/reposición — the friction is not the remedy’s existence but the forfeiture architecture around it.","authority":"Instructions & new trial: FRCP 51 (objection before the jury retires; plain error), 59 (new trial: misconduct, verdict against the great weight)."},{"n":31,"summary":"A party moves for summary judgment arguing there is no genuine dispute of material fact; the opponent has some (even thin) admissible evidence on the disputed element, or none at all.","trigger":"Rule 56 motion + question of who bears the initial burden + whether any admissible evidence supports the nonmovant's position.","rule":"Summary judgment is granted only when there is no genuine dispute of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law (Rule 56(a)). The movant bears the initial burden of production (by evidence or by pointing to the absence of the nonmovant's evidence, Celotex); the burden then shifts, and the nonmovant with the trial burden must come forward with some admissible evidence on each essential element. The judge asks only whether evidence exists — credibility is never weighed and inferences are drawn for the nonmovant.","sub":"Summary Judgment – Standard, Burden Shifting, No Credibility Weighing (Master Unit)","sensitivity":"Three structural gates: (1) materiality is defined by the substantive law; (2) 'some evidence' on each element defeats the motion — quantity is irrelevant at this stage (a single contradicting affidavit suffices, cf. the dueling dog-bite affidavits); (3) the judge's role is existence-checking, not fact-finding. If the nonmovant cannot yet produce evidence, the escape valve is Rule 56(d) deferral for discovery, not argument (Unit 32).","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The judge has both stories — he should decide which is more credible” → credibility is exclusively for the jury; SJ tests existence, not weight, of proof. The civil-law judge is the fact-finder and weighs documentary records as a matter of course (sana crítica); a pre-trial device that terminates a case precisely because NO weighing is possible — and a judge forbidden from weighing — has no counterpart in the código procesal. The misalignment produces both false positives (expecting SJ because one side's proof looks stronger) and false negatives (missing that zero evidence = mandatory SJ).","authority":"Summary judgment standard: Fed. R. Civ. P. 56(a); Celotex Corp. v. Catrett, 477 U.S. 317 (1986); Anderson v. Liberty Lobby (no weighing)."},{"n":32,"summary":"A party opposes (or supports) summary judgment with material that is inadmissible (hearsay journals, unproduced documents) or asks for more time to gather evidence.","trigger":"SJ record + evidence not admissible or not reducible to admissible form + (alternatively) nonmovant needs discovery to respond.","rule":"Summary-judgment evidence must be admissible or capable of being presented in admissible form at trial (Rule 56(c)); a party cannot rest on pleading allegations or inadmissible hearsay. Affidavits/declarations must be made on personal knowledge by a competent affiant. A nonmovant who cannot yet present essential facts may obtain deferral or denial under Rule 56(d) with a declaration explaining what discovery is needed and why it matters.","sub":"Summary Judgment – Admissible Evidence; Rule 56(d) Deferral","sensitivity":"Two distinct failure modes with different fixes: inadmissible support (fix: convert to admissible form — e.g., the treating physician testifies instead of the hearsay notes) vs. evidence not yet obtained (fix: Rule 56(d) declaration, not a merits response). Pleading allegations never count as evidence — the complaint is not proof.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The journal proves the elements — attach it and the case goes forward” → inadmissible hearsay cannot defeat SJ. In civil-law written procedure the expediente is built of documents freely weighed by the judge (prueba documental, libre valoración); the U.S. filter — only trial-admissible proof counts, tested against evidence rules at the SJ stage — has no analogue, and the civilian instinct to litigate by documents systematically overestimates what the SJ record can carry.","authority":"SJ evidence: Fed. R. Civ. P. 56(c)–(d)."},{"n":33,"summary":"A party attempts discovery immediately after filing/service, or a corporation resists discovery by invoking the privilege against self-incrimination.","trigger":"Discovery attempted before the Rule 26(f) conference + (contrast) corporate party invoking the Fifth Amendment.","rule":"Discovery generally may not begin until the parties have held the Rule 26(f) conference (meet-and-confer), which triggers initial disclosures and the discovery plan; premature discovery is objectionable and a protective order is available. Separately, the Fifth Amendment privilege against self-incrimination belongs to natural persons only — a corporation cannot invoke it to resist discovery.","sub":"Discovery – Rule 26(f) Gate; Corporate Fifth Amendment","sensitivity":"Timing gate: service of the complaint does NOT open discovery — the 26(f) conference does; the presumptive numeric limits (e.g., 25 interrogatories) are decoys when the real defect is timing. Privilege gate: entity status is dispositive for the Fifth Amendment — individual officers retain the privilege personally, but the corporation's documents must be produced.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The case is filed — send the questions” (or “no one must incriminate himself, including companies”) → discovery waits for the 26(f) gate, and corporations have no Fifth Amendment privilege. Party-driven discovery does not exist in Venezuelan procedure — evidence enters through the court in the lapso probatorio; both the existence of compulsory party-to-party discovery and its internal sequencing rules must be learned as a new institution, not mapped from the código.","authority":"Discovery gate: Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(d), 26(f); corporate Fifth Amendment: Braswell v. United States."},{"n":34,"summary":"One party seeks documents the other claims are protected work product (internal reports, investigation files); the court must decide protection, exceptions, and procedure.","trigger":"Discovery request + material prepared in anticipation of litigation (or merely in the ordinary course of business) + claimed substantial need.","rule":"Work product covers materials prepared by a party or its representatives in anticipation of litigation (Rule 26(b)(3)); documents created in the ordinary course of business (no litigation contemplated) are not protected. Ordinary work product must be produced if the requester shows substantial need and inability to obtain the substantial equivalent without undue hardship; mental impressions and legal theories (opinion work product) receive near-absolute protection. The objection must be asserted specifically (privilege log), and the court may inspect the materials in camera.","sub":"Discovery – Work Product Doctrine (Rule 26(b)(3))","sensitivity":"Three sequential screens: (1) anticipation-of-litigation is the entry gate — an architect's report commissioned with no lawsuit in mind fails at the threshold; (2) substantial need + undue hardship unlocks ordinary work product only; (3) opinion work product survives almost everything. Procedure is itself outcome-determinative: a generic objection without a privilege log forfeits the protection.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Internal company documents are private — the other side cannot demand them” → everything relevant and non-privileged is discoverable, and work product is a narrow, waivable island. Because civil procedure without discovery never forces a party to arm its adversary, the civilian schema lacks the category entirely: the trained instinct either over-claims (treating all internal files as untouchable) or under-protects (failing to log the privilege and waiving it).","authority":"Work product: Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(3), (b)(5); Hickman v. Taylor, 329 U.S. 495 (1947)."},{"n":35,"summary":"A party fails to supplement disclosures/discovery responses (e.g., 70 pages of treatment notes surface at trial) and then tries to use the withheld evidence.","trigger":"Rule 26(e) duty to supplement + attempted trial use of evidence not disclosed + no substantial justification / harmlessness.","rule":"A party must timely supplement disclosures and discovery responses when it learns they are incomplete or incorrect (Rule 26(e)); evidence not disclosed or supplemented is excluded at trial unless the failure was substantially justified or harmless (Rule 37(c)(1)), and the court may add sanctions.","sub":"Discovery – Duty to Supplement; Automatic Exclusion (Rules 26(e), 37(c)(1))","sensitivity":"The sanction is self-executing: exclusion follows from the failure itself — no prior motion to compel is required — subject only to the two escape valves (substantial justification; harmlessness). Late discovery of the material by the party itself is argued under those valves, not as a defense to the duty.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The notes are authentic and probative — justice requires admitting them” → undisclosed evidence is presumptively excluded regardless of probative value. In the civilian lapso probatorio the court controls admission and late proof is a judicial-discretion question weighed against truth-finding; the U.S. system makes disclosure discipline itself dispositive — the evidence dies for the procedural failure, not for unreliability.","authority":"Supplementation and exclusion: Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(e), 37(c)(1)."},{"n":36,"summary":"A party seeks Rule 11 sanctions by filing a motion directly with the court; separately, a court raises sanctions on its own after discovering a groundless filing.","trigger":"Sanctionable filing (no reasonable pre-filing inquiry) + party-initiated motion vs. court-initiated (sua sponte) procedure.","rule":"Every filing certifies that, after an inquiry reasonable under the circumstances, the paper is non-frivolous, warranted, and evidentially supported (Rule 11(b)). A party's sanctions motion must first be SERVED on the offender and may be FILED with the court only if the challenged paper is not withdrawn or corrected within 21 days (safe harbor, Rule 11(c)(2)). A court acting sua sponte issues a show-cause order specifying the conduct — no safe harbor applies (Rule 11(c)(3)).","sub":"Sanctions – Rule 11 (Safe Harbor vs. Sua Sponte; Reasonable Inquiry)","sensitivity":"Two procedural tracks with opposite mechanics: party-initiated (serve → 21 days → file; skipping the safe harbor is fatal to the motion) vs. court-initiated (show-cause order, no safe harbor). The substantive standard is objective — reasonable inquiry under the circumstances — so an attorney reasonably deceived by a client (forged contract) may escape sanction while a filer who ignored contradicting facts does not.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The filing is frivolous — ask the judge to sanction now” → a motion filed without serving it and waiting 21 days is denied regardless of merit. Civilian lealtad procesal sanctions (temeridad, multas) are applied directly by the judge; a mandatory private warning period that lets the offender un-ring the bell is a purely American mechanism — the misalignment is procedural, not ethical.","authority":"Rule 11: Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(b), 11(c)(2) (safe harbor), 11(c)(3) (sua sponte)."},{"n":37,"summary":"A plaintiff serves process by leaving it with a household member, mails a waiver request that is ignored, or seeks a default judgment against a defendant who had appeared.","trigger":"Service mechanics under Rule 4 + waiver-of-service request + default-judgment procedure after an appearance.","rule":"Service on an individual may be made by personal delivery, by leaving process at the dwelling with a person of suitable age and discretion who resides there, on an authorized agent, or by any method allowed by the law of the forum state (Rule 4(e)); any non-party 18 or older may serve (Rule 4(c)(2)). If a requested waiver is ignored, formal service is still required — but the defendant who refused without good cause bears the service costs (Rule 4(d)). A defendant who has appeared must receive written notice at least 7 days before a default-judgment hearing (Rule 55(b)(2)).","sub":"Service of Process – Rule 4 Methods; Waiver; Default Notice (Rule 55)","sensitivity":"Three separable mechanics tested independently: (1) abode service requires a resident of suitable age — a visiting relative fails; (2) the ignored waiver does not excuse service, it only shifts costs; (3) 'appearance' (including a losing Rule 12 motion) triggers the 7-day default notice — 3 days' notice voids the judgment. Cross-cutting: valid service ≠ personal jurisdiction (Units 4–7) — the two questions are analyzed separately.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The citación is the court's job — and any defect means nullity” → in the U.S. the PLAINTIFF controls service, private persons serve, and defects are analyzed by rule-compliance and prejudice, not categorical nullity. Venezuelan citación is a court-executed formality with nullity consequences (CPC arts. 215 ff.); the schema misallocates both the responsibility for service and the consequence architecture (cost-shifting, notice windows, voidable defaults).","authority":"Service and default: Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(c)(2), 4(d), 4(e); 55(b)(2)."},{"n":38,"summary":"A defendant must decide which defenses go in a Rule 12 motion and which must be pleaded in the answer; a related claim against the plaintiff from the same incident must also be raised.","trigger":"Responsive-pleading strategy + affirmative defenses + claim against plaintiff arising from the same transaction or occurrence.","rule":"Lack of personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficient process/service, and failure to state a claim may be raised by pre-answer motion (Rule 12(b)); affirmative defenses — duress, statute of limitations, contributory negligence, res judicata — must be pleaded in the answer (Rule 8(c)). A counterclaim arising from the same transaction or occurrence as the opposing claim is compulsory: it must be asserted in the pending action or it is barred (Rule 13(a)).","sub":"Pleadings – Rule 12 Motions vs. Affirmative Defenses; Compulsory Counterclaims","sensitivity":"Three bins with different consequences: waivable threshold defenses (PJ/venue — first response or lost, Unit 10); affirmative defenses (answer or presumptively waived, though amendment may cure); compulsory counterclaims (this action or never — preclusion regardless of merit). Classifying the defense/claim into the right bin IS the exam question.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “A valid defense can be raised whenever it becomes relevant; my claim can be a separate lawsuit” → each bin has a one-shot window. The civilian contestación aggregates defenses without waiver hierarchies, and reconvención is voluntary; the American use-it-or-lose-it architecture — especially the compulsory counterclaim bar — systematically destroys claims the civilian schema would preserve.","authority":"Pleading bins: Fed. R. Civ. P. 8(c), 12(b), 12(h), 13(a)."},{"n":39,"summary":"A party seeks to amend a pleading shortly after serving it, or late in the case when the opponent objects that the amendment strengthens the amender's position.","trigger":"Amendment as of course vs. leave of court + 'prejudice' objection based on facing a better defense.","rule":"A party may amend once as a matter of course within 21 days after serving the pleading (or after a responsive pleading/Rule 12 motion); otherwise amendment requires consent or leave of court, freely given when justice so requires (Rule 15(a)). Leave may be denied for undue delay, bad faith, futility, or prejudice — but having to face a stronger, properly presented defense is not cognizable prejudice.","sub":"Amendments – Rule 15(a) (As of Course; Leave; Prejudice)","sensitivity":"The 21-day as-of-course window interacts with the waiver rules: it can rescue an omitted Rule 12 defense (venue added 6 days after the answer survives; cf. Unit 10). 'Prejudice' means litigation prejudice (lost evidence, unpreparable response), not merits prejudice — the distinction decides most amendment questions.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Pleadings freeze the dispute — late changes are barred” → U.S. amendment policy is MORE liberal than the civilian schema expects. This is inverse friction: Venezuelan preclusión procesal fixes the thema decidendum at the contestación, so the trained instinct under-uses Rule 15's liberality — missing rescues (the 21-day saver) and conceding 'prejudice' arguments U.S. courts reject.","authority":"Amendments: Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(a); prejudice standard."},{"n":40,"summary":"After the limitations period runs, a plaintiff amends to add a new claim against the same defendant — or to substitute/add a new defendant.","trigger":"Amendment after the SOL + same transaction as the original pleading + (if changing parties) notice and mistake requirements.","rule":"An amendment relates back to the original filing date when it asserts a claim arising out of the same conduct, transaction, or occurrence set out in the original pleading (Rule 15(c)(1)(B)). An amendment CHANGING A PARTY relates back only if, additionally, within the Rule 4(m) service period the new party (1) received such notice that it will not be prejudiced and (2) knew or should have known the action would have been brought against it but for a mistake concerning identity (Rule 15(c)(1)(C)).","sub":"Amendments – Relation Back (New Claim vs. New Party)","sensitivity":"Two branches with escalating requirements: same-party/new-claim needs only the transactional test; new-party adds notice + mistake-of-identity within the service window — and 'I didn't know who to sue' is not a qualifying mistake. Conflating the branches (demanding notice for a same-party claim, or skipping mistake for a new party) is the classic exam error — and was the drafting defect this unit corrects in the source row.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The SOL ran — the new claim is dead” (or “the facts are the same, so swapping defendants is fine”) → relation back saves the same-transaction claim, but rescues a new defendant only through the notice-plus-mistake gate. Civilian prescription interruption runs against the demand as filed; a doctrine that retroactively re-dates pleadings — asymmetrically for claims vs. parties — must be learned as structure, not intuited.","authority":"Relation back: Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c)(1)(B)–(C); Krupski v. Costa Crociere, 560 U.S. 538 (2010)."},{"n":41,"summary":"A federal action is filed in a district where no defendant resides and where none of the underlying events occurred; defendants challenge venue.","trigger":"Federal venue selection + defendants' residence (per district) vs. locus of a substantial part of the events.","rule":"Venue is proper under 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) in (1) a district where any defendant resides, if all defendants reside in the same state — an entity 'resides' only in districts where it would be subject to personal jurisdiction treating the district as a state — or (2) a district where a substantial part of the events or omissions occurred. If venue is improper, § 1406 lets the court dismiss or, in the interest of justice, transfer to a proper district.","sub":"Venue – § 1391(b) Proper Districts; § 1406 (Master)","sensitivity":"Venue is measured DISTRICT by district, not state by state: two defendants 'in California' does not make every California district proper — residency requires personal-jurisdiction contacts with the specific district. 'Substantial part' can make several districts proper simultaneously. Improper venue → § 1406 (dismiss/transfer); proper-but-inconvenient → § 1404 (Unit 42).","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “Venue is just territorial competence — sue where the defendant is domiciled in the state” → the operative geography is the federal DISTRICT, an administrative unit with no civil-law counterpart, and an entity's 'residence' is a personal-jurisdiction question in miniature. The competencia territorial schema (domicilio del demandado, lugar del contrato) maps loosely onto § 1391 but misses the district-level granularity and the venue/PJ interlock — source rows 130–131.","authority":"Venue: 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b), (c)(2), (d); § 1406(a)."},{"n":42,"summary":"A case filed in a proper (or improper) federal forum is moved to another district — for convenience, by consent, or because venue was defective — and the parties dispute which state's law travels with it.","trigger":"Transfer between federal districts + basis of the transfer (§ 1404 convenience/consent vs. § 1406 improper venue) + governing state law after transfer.","rule":"Under § 1404(a) a court may transfer, for convenience and in the interest of justice, to any district where the action might have been brought (PJ + venue + SMJ) or to which all parties consent; the transferee court then applies the law the TRANSFEROR forum would have applied (Van Dusen). Under § 1406, transfer cures an improper forum and the TRANSFEREE forum's law applies. Forum non conveniens dismissal is reserved for superior forums outside the federal system (foreign courts, state courts).","sub":"Venue – Transfer (§§ 1404/1406) and Choice-of-Law Consequences","sensitivity":"The choice-of-law consequence is the hidden variable: § 1404 transfer carries the original forum's state law (no law-shopping by transfer); § 1406 transfer does not (the first forum's law never validly attached). The same motion word — 'transfer' — produces opposite governing law depending on whether the original venue was proper. In diversity this interlocks with Klaxon (Unit 3).","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The case moved, so the new court applies its own law” → true only for § 1406 transfers; a § 1404 transfer carries the transferor's choice-of-law rules with it. Horizontal movement of a live case between coordinate courts, each embedded in a different state's law, has no civil-law analogue (declinatoria de competencia restarts the analysis in the new court); the civilian instinct misses that the law can 'travel' with the case — source rows 15, 132–134.","authority":"Transfer: 28 U.S.C. §§ 1404(a), 1406(a); Van Dusen v. Barrack, 376 U.S. 612 (1964); Stewart Org. v. Ricoh (cf. Unit 2); forum non conveniens: Piper Aircraft v. Reyno."},{"n":43,"summary":"A defendant answers on the merits and only later — realizing the district was wrong — tries to object to venue (or amends to add the defense).","trigger":"Improper-venue defense omitted from the first Rule 12 response + later attempt to raise it.","rule":"Improper venue is a waivable Rule 12(b)(3) defense: it is lost unless raised in the first Rule 12 motion or responsive pleading (or added by amendment as a matter of course within 21 days of serving the answer). Unlike subject-matter jurisdiction, a case litigated in an improper venue produces a fully valid judgment.","sub":"Venue – Waiver (Rule 12(b)(3)/12(h))","sensitivity":"Venue sits with personal jurisdiction and process defenses in the waivable-quartet of Rule 12(h)(1); the 21-day matter-of-course amendment is the only saver. Contrast the two poles: SMJ can never be waived (Unit 10); venue is waived by a single mis-sequenced filing — source rows 135, 84.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The court is objectively the wrong one, so the objection survives” → waived at the first response. The civilian schema treats territorial competence as an objective defect assertable through the case (and sometimes sua sponte); U.S. venue is a personal privilege the defendant forfeits by sequence, not substance — the same instinct corrected in Unit 10 recurs here one rung lower.","authority":"Venue waiver: Rules 12(b)(3), 12(h)(1); amendment saver Rule 12(h)(1)(B)(ii)."},{"n":44,"summary":"A plaintiff dismisses his own case (once or twice), or the court dismisses it involuntarily, and the parties dispute whether the claim can be filed again.","trigger":"Voluntary dismissal(s) under Rule 41(a) (timing and count) or involuntary dismissal under Rule 41(b).","rule":"A plaintiff may dismiss without prejudice by mere notice only before the defendant serves an answer or a summary-judgment motion (afterwards: stipulation or court order). A second voluntary dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits (two-dismissal rule) — but only if BOTH dismissals were voluntary; an involuntary first dismissal does not count. Involuntary dismissals operate as adjudications on the merits unless the order states otherwise or rests on jurisdiction, venue, or joinder (Rule 41(b)).","sub":"Dismissals – Voluntary/Involuntary; Two-Dismissal Rule (Rule 41)","sensitivity":"Three switches: (1) the notice-dismissal window closes at answer OR summary-judgment motion — whichever comes first; (2) the two-dismissal rule counts only voluntary dismissals; (3) an involuntary dismissal's preclusive force depends on its stated basis (cross-table in Unit 22). Feeds directly into claim preclusion — source rows 20, 136–138.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “The plaintiff abandoned the case, he can always refile” (or the inverse: “any dismissal kills the claim”) → the answer depends on count, voluntariness, and timing. The civilian desistimiento requires court approval and often the defendant's consent but rarely precludes refiling; the U.S. system lets the plaintiff exit unilaterally — exactly twice — and converts the second exit into a judgment on the merits, a purely numerical preclusion trigger with no codal analogue.","authority":"Dismissals: Rule 41(a)(1) (notice window; two-dismissal rule), 41(a)(2), 41(b) (involuntary = on the merits unless jurisdiction/venue/joinder)."},{"n":45,"summary":"Numerous claimants injured by standardized conduct seek to litigate together through representatives; certification, its requirements, and its immediate appealability are disputed.","trigger":"Putative class action + Rule 23(a) prerequisites + a Rule 23(b) category (typically (b)(3) damages) + appeal of the certification ruling.","rule":"Certification requires numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy (Rule 23(a)) plus, for damages classes, predominance of common questions and superiority (Rule 23(b)(3)) — individualized issues (e.g., many decisionmakers' discretionary intent) defeat predominance. Orders granting or denying certification are appealable only by discretionary permission under Rule 23(f). In diversity, Rule 23 governs over conflicting state class-action bans (Shady Grove).","sub":"Class Actions – Certification (Rule 23(a)/(b)(3)); Appeal (23(f))","sensitivity":"Three stacked screens: (a) prerequisites, (b)(3) predominance/superiority — where most certifications die — and the discretionary 23(f) appeal window (14 days). The Erie hook (Unit 2): a federal court certifies even where state law forbids class treatment. Source rows 22–24.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “each victim sues alone, or the state sues for them” → the representative-litigation architecture (self-appointed class representatives, opt-out absent members bound by the judgment) does not exist in Venezuelan procedure beyond narrow acciones colectivas. The civilian misses both directions: under-using the device (not spotting certification issues) and over-trusting it (missing that predominance kills damages classes with individualized liability facts).","authority":"Class actions: Rule 23(a), 23(b)(3), 23(f); Wal-Mart v. Dukes (commonality); Shady Grove (cf. Unit 2)."},{"n":46,"summary":"A stakeholder holding one fund or item faces multiple incompatible claims and wants to force all claimants into a single proceeding.","trigger":"Single stake + multiple adverse claimants + risk of inconsistent obligations; choose statutory vs. rule interpleader.","rule":"Statutory interpleader (28 U.S.C. §§ 1335, 2361) requires only $500+ in controversy and MINIMAL diversity between any two claimants (stakeholder's citizenship irrelevant), with nationwide service and injunctions against parallel suits. Rule 22 interpleader uses ordinary jurisdiction: complete diversity between stakeholder and claimants and >$75,000 (or a federal question).","sub":"Interpleader – Statutory (§ 1335) vs. Rule 22","sensitivity":"Every variable flips between the two tracks: amount ($500 vs. >$75,000), diversity (minimal among claimants vs. complete against stakeholder), service (nationwide vs. ordinary). Exam questions are solved by simply selecting the track whose numbers fit — source rows 45–46.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the holder waits to be sued and consigns the asset” → U.S. law gives the stakeholder an offensive device to drag all claimants into one forum. The civilian oferta real y depósito (consignación) schema is defensive and bilateral; it neither concentrates rival claimants nor explains why TWO parallel interpleader tracks with different jurisdictional arithmetic coexist.","authority":"Interpleader: 28 U.S.C. §§ 1335, 1397, 2361 (statutory); Rule 22; State Farm v. Tashire."},{"n":47,"summary":"A defendant wants to bring the person it blames into the lawsuit (a misdiagnosing doctor, a subcontractor), or multiple plaintiffs/defendants are joined in one action and a party moves to sever.","trigger":"Third-party claim by a defending party (derivative liability) + permissive joinder of parties on one side.","rule":"Impleader (Rule 14) lets a defending party bring in a nonparty who is or may be liable TO IT for all or part of the claim against it (indemnity/contribution); it is NOT available to say 'he, not I, is liable to the plaintiff.' Permissive joinder (Rule 20) requires claims arising from the same transaction or occurrence (or series) plus at least one common question of law or fact.","sub":"Joinder – Impleader (Rule 14); Permissive Joinder (Rule 20)","sensitivity":"The impleader gate is strictly derivative: liability must flow defendant→third party, contingent on the main claim. An 'it was him' defense is not a Rule 14 claim — the device transmits liability, it does not redirect it. Rule 20's two-part test governs multi-party structure; supplemental jurisdiction usually covers both (Unit 17) — source rows 55–56.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian recognizes this device — impleader maps well onto the llamada en garantía / cita de saneamiento (CPC Art. 370), and Rule 20 onto litisconsorcio facultativo. The instinct is broadly RIGHT; the residual friction is the derivative-only limit: using impleader to deflect (rather than transmit) liability, which the CPC intervention forms tolerate more loosely, fails under Rule 14.","authority":"Impleader/joinder: Rules 14(a), 20(a); cf. supplemental jurisdiction § 1367 (Unit 17)."},{"n":48,"summary":"An organization sues on behalf of its members (or a plaintiff's own stake in the outcome is questioned) and the court asks whether this plaintiff may bring this claim at all.","trigger":"Article III standing + suit brought by an association for injuries to its members.","rule":"Standing requires injury in fact, causation, and redressability. An association has standing to sue for its members when (1) the members would themselves have standing, (2) the interests asserted are germane to the organization's purpose, and (3) neither the claim nor the relief requires individual members' participation (Hunt).","sub":"Justiciability – Standing; Associational Standing (Hunt)","sensitivity":"Standing is a constitutional (Article III case-or-controversy) limit on the court's power — non-waivable, raisable at any time, and independent of the merits. The Hunt test's third prong quietly channels damages claims (which need individual participation) away from associations while permitting injunctive/declaratory suits — source row 109.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the association has interés jurídico actual, so it may sue” → close, but the civil-law cualidad/interés procesal is a merits-adjacent admissibility doctrine, while U.S. standing is a jurisdictional constitutional gate with its own three-element grammar and a distinct associational test. The civilian tends to argue interest in the abstract and misses that damages relief usually destroys associational standing under Hunt's third prong.","authority":"Standing: U.S. Const. art. III; Lujan v. Defenders of Wildlife; Hunt v. Washington State Apple Advertising Comm'n, 432 U.S. 333 (1977)."},{"n":49,"summary":"A party wants to appeal immediately from an interlocutory order — a denial of summary judgment, a privilege/discovery order, a TRO, a preliminary-injunction ruling, or a class-certification denial.","trigger":"Appeal attempted before final judgment + the order's category (injunction, collateral order, certification, discovery).","rule":"Under 28 U.S.C. § 1291 only final decisions are appealable. Exceptions are categorical: § 1292(a)(1) covers orders granting/denying/modifying injunctions (a TRO is NOT appealable unless extended beyond Rule 65 limits, which converts it into a preliminary injunction); the collateral order doctrine requires an order that (1) conclusively determines (2) an important issue separate from the merits that is (3) effectively unreviewable after final judgment — privilege/discovery orders fail the third element; Rule 23(f) gives a discretionary 14-day window for certification orders; § 1292(b) requires dual certification. Denials of summary judgment (including on res judicata grounds) are not appealable.","sub":"Appeals – Final Judgment Rule and Its Exceptions (Master)","sensitivity":"The analysis is categorical, not equitable: identify the order's box — injunction / collateral order / certification / certified question — or the appeal fails, no matter how burdensome interlocutory review would be to forgo. The COD's third element does the killing: harms curable by post-judgment reversal (privilege disclosure, litigation expense) never qualify — source rows 5, 8–10, 23.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “apelación en un solo efecto — interlocutory orders are appealable as of course” → the Venezuelan CPC's generous interlocutory appeal (Art. 289: autos that cause gravamen irreparable) inverts the U.S. default. The civilian instinct appeals everything immediately; § 1291 makes finality the rule and forces most errors to wait — the 'gravamen irreparable' intuition survives only inside the narrow collateral-order box, and even there disclosure-type harms do not count.","authority":"Appealability: 28 U.S.C. §§ 1291, 1292(a)(1), 1292(b); Cohen v. Beneficial Indus. Loan Corp. (collateral order); Mohawk Indus. v. Carpenter (privilege orders); Rule 23(f); Rule 65(b) (TRO limits)."},{"n":50,"summary":"After a bench trial (or a preserved trial ruling), the losing party appeals the judge's factual findings, a legal conclusion, or an issue never raised below.","trigger":"Appellate review + type of question (fact vs. law) + whether the issue was preserved in the trial court.","rule":"A trial judge's findings of fact — required in all bench trials (Rule 52(a)), though they may be stated orally on the record, and required even in documents-only trials — are reviewed for clear error, with particular deference to credibility determinations; legal conclusions are reviewed de novo. Issues not raised in the trial court — including claims of judicial bias — are waived on appeal absent plain error.","sub":"Appeals – Standards of Review; Preservation (Rule 52)","sensitivity":"Two sorting operations decide every question: (1) classify fact (clear error) vs. law (de novo) — the standard, not the merits, usually dictates the outcome; (2) check preservation — an unraised issue is gone regardless of merit. Rule 52 findings may be oral: 'no written opinion' is not error — source rows 6–7, 11–12.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the court of appeals re-examines the facts and decides the case anew” → the civilian apelación is a novum iudicium re-reviewing facts and evidence; the U.S. appeal is error-correction under deference standards. Trained to re-argue facts on appeal, the civilian both overestimates reversal chances (clear error is nearly insurmountable on credibility) and neglects trial-level preservation, which is where appellate rights are actually won or lost.","authority":"Review standards: Rule 52(a)(1), (a)(6); Anderson v. City of Bessemer City (clear error/credibility); plain-error and preservation doctrine."}],"cases":[{"n":1,"summary":"Public nuisance judgment paid; later purchaser sues; preclusion issues.","trigger":"Res judicata vs collateral estoppel and privity.","rule":"Res judicata requires that the lawsuits be between the same parties or parties in privity. Subsequent homeowners will have privity with each other.","sub":"Res Judicata / Privity","family":"Preclusion","unit":23},{"n":2,"summary":"A plaintiff sued for fraudulent misrepresentation, but the court dismissed the complaint under Rule 12(b)(6) for lack of particularity and granted leave to amend. The plaintiff files an amended complaint asserting misrepresentation claims, and the defendant raises res judicata.","trigger":"Dismissal with leave to amend, not a final judgment on the merits.","rule":"A dismissal with leave to amend is not a final judgment on the merits and does not trigger claim preclusion.","sub":"Amended Pleadings – Dismissal with Leave to Amend","family":"Preclusion","unit":22},{"n":3,"summary":"Plaintiff amends complaint to add defamation after SOL; relation back issue.","trigger":"Amendment for purpose of relating back. To relate back it’s a 2 part test: 1) The new claim brought through amendment must be from the STO as the original claim and, 2) the other party against who the claim is brought must have some type of notice of the action (original complaint in some way put them on notice about the amendment)","rule":"For a claim to relate back, under Rule 15(c), the claim must be from the same transaction or occurrence as the claim in the original pleading and the party the claim is brought against received such notice of the action that it will not be prejudiced in defending on the merits.","sub":"Amended Pleadings – Relation Back","family":"Amendments","unit":40},{"n":4,"summary":"In a trespass-to-land case, defendant answered but did not plead license. During deposition two months later, defendant admits plaintiff invited him to stay but revoked the invite upon arrival. Defendant seeks leave to amend answer to add license; plaintiff argues it’s too late and would ruin an easy win.","trigger":"Late amendment adding an affirmative defense; ‘prejudice’ does not include merely facing a stronger defense.","rule":"Although a judge may not allow a late amendment if it prejudices the other party, the fact that the other party may be subject to a better defense is not the type of prejudice that the court will consider.","sub":"Amendments – Rule 15","family":"Amendments","unit":39},{"n":5,"summary":"After losing first contract suit, plaintiff sued again on different provision; court denied defendant’s res judicata SJ motion","trigger":"Immediate appeal sought from denial of summary judgment based on res judicata","rule":"A denial of a summary judgment motion means that there is no final decision in a case yet, so only an interlocutory appeal would be allowed, since most appeals can only take place once there is a final decision.","sub":"Appeals","family":"Appeals","unit":49},{"n":6,"summary":"Bench trial on contract; judge makes credibility findings; appeal challenges fact findings.","trigger":"Appellate review of trial court fact findings. Apellate standard of review","rule":"The standard to reverse on appeal for fact finding (credibility) by a lower court judge is the clearly erroneous standard. De novo is for law","sub":"Appeals – Clearly Erroneous Standard","family":"Appeals","unit":50},{"n":7,"summary":"Bench trial contract dispute; judge makes oral factual findings; contractor appeals.","trigger":"Appeal of judge’s factual findings after bench trial.","rule":"Appellate courts defer to trial court factual findings unless clearly erroneous. When there is a finding of facts by a trial judge in a bench trial you just cant ask to overturn because you don’t agree. To overturn a finding of fact you need clearly erroneous. A JUDGE DOES NOT NEED TO PUT FINDINGAS OF FACTS IN A WRITTEN OPINION","sub":"Appeals — Clearly Erroneous Standard","family":"Appeals","unit":50},{"n":8,"summary":"In a federal SEC civil enforcement action, the SEC sues a stockbroker for misusing client funds and alleges she involved her husband by asking him to open private accounts in his name to hold client funds. The SEC calls the husband; the stockbroker claims spousal communications privilege. The SEC argues crime‑fraud / in‑furtherance‑of‑conspiracy, and the judge orders the husband to answer questions about spousal communications. The stockbroker wants to appeal immediately.","trigger":"Interlocutory order compelling disclosure/testimony; determine if immediately appealable under collateral order doctrine.","rule":"Discovery/privilege orders are generally not immediately appealable; an interlocutory appeal is allowed only if the order fits the collateral order doctrine (narrow category).Collateral Order Doctrine (COD) elements:\n\n1️⃣ Conclusively determines the disputed issue (not tentative or subject to change) 2️⃣ Resolves an important issue completely separate from the merits 3️⃣ Is effectively unreviewable after final judgment If even ONE element fails → NO immediate appeal. Apply here. Judge orders husband to testify about spousal communications. Stockbroker wants to appeal immediately. Element 1 — Conclusive? Yes. Judge already ordered testimony. ✔ satisfied. Element 2 — Separate from merits? Privilege issues are technically separate. ✔ satisfied. Element 3 — Effectively unreviewable later? Here is the killer. Even if testimony happens: 👉 She can appeal AFTER final judgment and argue privilege error. Courts say: Disclosure of privileged info is NOT enough to bypass final judgment rule.","sub":"Appeals – Collateral order doctrine","family":"Appeals","unit":49},{"n":9,"summary":"Federal court issues TRO stopping foreclosure sale for 14 days; because no PI hearing can be scheduled, court extends TRO an additional 30 days; lender appeals; homeowner moves to dismiss appeal.","trigger":"TRO extended beyond 14 days (30-day extension) makes it functionally a preliminary injunction; interlocutory appeal issue.","rule":"While TROs are generally not immediately appealable, an order operating as a preliminary injunction (e.g., extended beyond TRO limits) is interlocutorily appealable. TRO'S ARE NOT APPEALABLE. THIS TRO WAS EXTENDED TO 30 DAYS CONVERTED INTO A PI. OJO SOMETIMES THEY ASK IF THE APPELATE COURT HAS JDX NO HEAR AN APPEAL REMEMBER THE GENERAL RULE IS ONLY FINAL JUDGMENTS ARE APPEALABLE","sub":"Appeals – Interlocutory Appeal / TRO vs Preliminary Injunction","family":"Appeals","unit":49},{"n":10,"summary":"Employee sues employer seeking injunction and damages; injunction denied and immediately appealed.","trigger":"Interlocutory order denying an injunction.","rule":"Although an appeal usually requires a final decision, an interlocutory appeal can be made if the court has issued an order dealing with an injunction.","sub":"Appeals – Interlocutory Injunctions","family":"Appeals","unit":49},{"n":11,"summary":"A lifeguard employed by a coastal town joins a group called the “Yellowmites” and wears a crown of deer antlers at all times. For safety, the city demands he remove the crown while working; he sues in federal court claiming a Free Exercise violation. At trial, he is not permitted to wear the crown (he says it gives access to all knowledge) and he loses. On appeal, he argues (1) he should have been allowed to wear the crown at trial and (2) the city’s lawyer made fun of him to the judge, causing bias—an allegation he did not raise at trial because he feared more scorn.","trigger":"Preservation of issues for appeal: evidentiary/trial‑management rulings vs unpreserved bias claim.","rule":"An issue generally must be raised in the trial court to be reviewable on appeal; failure to raise alleged judicial bias at trial waives it, while preserved trial rulings (like exclusion of an item in court) are reviewable.","sub":"Appeals – Issue preservation","family":"Appeals","unit":50},{"n":12,"summary":"Bench trial based solely on documents; judge failed to issue findings.","trigger":"Bench trial findings requirement.","rule":"If a judge is trying a case based solely on documentary evidence, the judge must provide findings and conclusions.","sub":"Bench Trial – Findings & Conclusions","family":"Appeals","unit":50},{"n":13,"summary":"After a case is filed, one party tries to initiate discovery immediately. The issue is whether discovery is permitted before the required discovery conference (or equivalent procedural step) has occurred.","trigger":"Discovery attempted before discovery conference occurs.","rule":"As a general rule, discovery does not begin until the discovery conference has taken place. Recognize the discovery timing trap: Federal discovery generally does not start immediately upon service of the complaint. Anchor rule: Parties must first hold the Rule 26(f) conference (meet-and-confer) and plan discovery / arrange initial disclosures. Apply: Plaintiff served summons + complaint + 25 interrogatories immediately. That’s too soon (pre–26(f) conference), so defendant can seek a protective order. Ignore decoys: The number 25 is the presumptive limit, so that’s not the issue here.","sub":"Civil Procedure — Discovery Conference Requirement","family":"Discovery","unit":33},{"n":14,"summary":"One party moves for summary judgment. The record shows no genuine dispute over a material fact, and the question tests which party bears the initial burden of production and what must be shown before the burden shifts to the opposing party.","trigger":"Summary judgment standard and burden shifting (no genuine issue of material fact).","rule":"Summary judgment is granted when there is no genuine issue of material fact. The party that has the burden of production to begin with (usually P) has to provide some evidence before the burden shifts to the other party. The judge does not weigh credibility at this stage. The judge only asks: Is there evidence? Start with the summary judgment standard: SJ is granted only if there’s no genuine dispute of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. Key practical rule: To beat SJ, you don’t need a mountain of proof—you need some evidence on the fact you must prove at trial. If you have zero, you lose. If you have some, you usually create a fact issue and avoid SJ. Identify the missing element: The manufacturer’s SJ ground is: worker has no evidence the manufacturer’s insulation was ever used at his work sites (a key causation/exposure link). Best procedural escape when you don’t yet have evidence: Rule 56(d)—ask the court to defer/deny SJ because you need discovery to obtain the evidence, and support it with a declaration/affidavitexplaining: what discovery you need, why it matters, how it will create a genuine dispute. NOTE: You don’t get to “credibility battles” unless you first have some evidence to reach trial.","sub":"Civil Procedure — Summary Judgment Standard/Burdens","family":"Summary Judgment","unit":31},{"n":15,"summary":"A small commercial airplane crashed in State A, killing passengers and the pilot who were all citizens of State B. The airline that owned/operated the plane is a corporation with its principal place of business and maintenance facilities in State C (and is incorporated in State C). One day before the statute of limitations would run, the estates of the pilot and passengers (State B plaintiffs) filed a wrongful death action against the airline in federal court in State A. The airline was served one week later and wants to prevent the State A federal court from hearing the case.\n\nThis question is a classic “venue/transfer hides PJ” trap:","trigger":"Transfer of venue for convenience or by consent to a proper forum.","rule":"Parties can transfer a case for convenience to another place where the lawsuit could have been brought or where the parties agree to transfer to. Tutor’s approach (the “civ pro trap” method): Start from the goal: Airline wants to prevent State A federal court from hearing the action. Test the obvious dismissal routes first: Improper venue? Venue is proper where a substantial part of events occurred → the crash happened in State A, so venue is proper there. No personal jurisdiction? PJ exists because the airline’s plane crashed in State A (suit arises out of that contact) and/or purposeful activity in the forum → PJ likely proper. Forum non conveniens? Typically used when the alternative forum is a different country (or non-federal forum); here the alternative is another federal district, so transfer—not FNC. Use the correct tool: Transfer under 28 U.S.C. § 1404(a) (convenience transfer) to a district where the case could originally have been brought (or all parties consent). Check “could have been brought” in State C: PJ over airline in State C (incorporation/PPB/major facilities) ✔ venue proper in State C (defendant “resides” there for venue) ✔ SMJ would still exist (diversity) ✔","sub":"Civil Procedure — Transfer of Venue","family":"Venue","unit":42},{"n":16,"summary":"After a car accident, a defendant defaulted in a federal negligence action, and the court entered a final judgment awarding damages for personal injuries. The plaintiff later sues the same defendant for property damage to his motorcycle arising from the same crash.","trigger":"Second lawsuit arises from the same transaction after a final default judgment.","rule":"A default judgment is a final judgment on the merits for claim‑preclusion purposes and bars later claims arising from the same transaction or occurrence.","sub":"Claim Preclusion – Default Judgment","family":"Preclusion","unit":22},{"n":17,"summary":"A company’s hot‑sauce odor was declared a public nuisance, and a final judgment required the company to pay $500 to each affected neighbor. One neighbor later sold his property to a buyer, who then sued the company seeking another $500 for the same nuisance.","trigger":"Later plaintiff is in privity with a prior plaintiff and asserts the same nuisance claim after a final judgment and payment.","rule":"Claim preclusion bars subsequent suits by parties in privity asserting the same claim after a final judgment on the merits.","sub":"Claim Preclusion – Privity","family":"Preclusion","unit":23},{"n":18,"summary":"Federal action dismissed as discovery sanction; plaintiff later files state tort claim.","trigger":"Whether second suit is same cause of action. Elements of res judicata (claim preclusion): 1) Full faith and credit, 2) Same parties or privis, 3) Same claim or cause of action (the test is the same transaction or occurrence as the original claim). Always compare the first trial with the second trial, it doesnt matter how you frase the second trial as long as it arises from the same transaction or occurance.","rule":"The federal court’s test to see if it is the same cause of action is whether the second action is from the same transaction or occurrence or same series of transactions or occurrences as the original claim.","sub":"Claim Preclusion – Same Transaction","family":"Preclusion","unit":21},{"n":19,"summary":"A man was injured by a lawnmower he purchased from a store and sued the store for negligence. The jury found the store was not negligent and entered judgment for the store. The man then files a new lawsuit against the same store based on strict products liability arising from the same accident.","trigger":"Second lawsuit asserts a new legal theory arising from the same transaction or occurrence.","rule":"Claim preclusion bars all claims arising from the same transaction or occurrence, even if the plaintiff asserts a different legal theory in the second action.","sub":"Claim Preclusion – Same Transaction or Occurrence","family":"Preclusion","unit":21},{"n":20,"summary":"A plaintiff files a lawsuit and voluntarily dismisses it. The plaintiff later files again and again voluntarily dismisses. The defendant argues the second voluntary dismissal operates as a final judgment on the merits, barring a later suit under claim preclusion (res judicata).","trigger":"Plaintiff voluntarily dismisses the same claim twice (two-dismissal rule).","rule":"If a plaintiff voluntarily dismisses their complaint twice, the second time will be considered a final judgment for the purposes of res judicata (claim preclusion). Remember if its an INVOLUNTARY DISMISSAL for failure to state a claim for example you can ask the court to dismiss without prejudice so you can refile, but voluntarily NO.","sub":"Claim Preclusion — Two-Dismissal Rule","family":"Dismissals","unit":44},{"n":21,"summary":"A man slipped and fell on icy premises and sued a business for negligence. During trial, his attorney objected to multiple alleged judicial errors. The jury returned a verdict for the business. Instead of appealing, the man files a new lawsuit in a different court alleging the judge’s errors affected the outcome.","trigger":"Same parties and same negligence claim after a final jury verdict on the merits.","rule":"Claim preclusion (res Judicata) bars a second lawsuit on the same claim after a final judgment on the merits, even if the plaintiff alleges trial errors that could have been raised on appeal.","sub":"Claim Preclusion (Res Judicata)","family":"Preclusion","unit":21},{"n":22,"summary":"Diversity class action where state law forbids class actions against in-state companies; issue is whether state restriction applies in federal court.","trigger":"Class action certification in diversity → FRCP 23 governs; conflicting state class-action ban is displaced.","rule":"Under the Erie doctrine, federal law will be applied to the exclusion of state law if there is a federal rule of civil procedure that is on point.","sub":"Class Actions – Erie / Rule 23","family":"Class Actions","unit":45},{"n":23,"summary":"Five black or Hispanic homeowners bring a putative federal class action against a bank alleging discriminatory mortgage fees; the proposed class would include thousands of black and Hispanic borrowers. The district court denies class certification for lack of common questions under Rule 23(a)(2) but would allow the five named plaintiffs to proceed individually; because the lawyer expected certification to make the case financially feasible, the plaintiffs seek to immediately appeal the denial of certification.","trigger":"Immediate appeal of denial of class certification; discretionary interlocutory appeal under Rule 23(f).","rule":"An order granting or denying class certification is immediately appealable only if the court of appeals, in its discretion, permits an interlocutory appeal on a timely petition","sub":"Class Actions – Interlocutory appeal (Rule 23(f))","family":"Class Actions","unit":45},{"n":24,"summary":"Plaintiffs try to certify a damages class action under Rule 23(b)(3) based on a defendant’s allegedly discriminatory decisions. The decisions were made by many different managers using discretion, raising individualized questions about intent and causation.","trigger":"Rule 23(b)(3) requires common issues to predominate; individualized issues (many decisionmakers’ intent/discretion) can defeat predominance.","rule":"A 23(b)(3) class action fails when individual issues predominate—e.g., many different decisionmakers’ intent/discretion makes common issues not predominate. Here, the certification failed because 100 different managers made 100 individualized decisions requiring separate intent mini-trials. Rule 23(a) prerequisites. Numerosity: so many members that joinder is impracticable. Commonality: common questions of law/fact. Typicality: representative’s claim is typical of class. Adequacy: rep and counsel will fairly/adequately protect class. Then because plaintiff filed this as Rule 23(b)(3) (money damages), you also need: Rule 23(b)(3) requirements. Predominance: common questions must predominate over individual ones Superiority: class action is the best (superior) method to resolve it. Here, (b)(3) dies when individualized issues dominate.","sub":"Class Actions – Rule 23(b)(3) predominance","family":"Class Actions","unit":45},{"n":25,"summary":"Passenger A sues driver and loses on driver’s negligence; later passenger B sues same driver. Driver seeks collateral estoppel to bar relitigation of driver’s negligence.","trigger":"Non-mutual defensive collateral estoppel cannot be used against a person who was not a party and lacked a full and fair opportunity (due process).","rule":"Due process requires that everyone has their opportunity in court. Using collateral estoppel against someone who hasn’t had a chance to litigate will not be allowed.","sub":"Collateral Estoppel","family":"Preclusion","unit":24},{"n":26,"summary":"After driver is found not negligent, passenger B sues the third-party driver (the one the first jury found negligent) and seeks to preclude him from relitigating negligence.","trigger":"Collateral estoppel requires the party to be bound to have had a full and fair opportunity to litigate; cannot bind a nonparty to the first case.","rule":"A party must have had a full and fair opportunity to litigate before collateral estoppel can be used against them.","sub":"Collateral Estoppel","family":"Preclusion","unit":24},{"n":27,"summary":"An investor has sued a broker in federal court for violation of federal securities law, claiming that the broker misrepresented the investments he made on the investor’s behalf. The investor and the broker are citizens of State A. The broker would like to assert a breach of contract claim as a counterclaim to the investor’s action, alleging that the investor failed to pay the broker for the investments he made on her behalf.","trigger":"Broker asserts contract counterclaim","rule":"Compulsory counterclaims arise from same transaction.","sub":"Compulsory counterclaim","family":"Pleadings","unit":20},{"n":28,"summary":"In a diversity car-accident case, defendant has related $40k claim against plaintiff arising from same accident.","trigger":"Defendant’s related claim against plaintiff in same accident → compulsory counterclaim; supplemental jurisdiction regardless of amount.","rule":"In situations where a compulsory counterclaim is filed, the court will likely be able to obtain supplemental jurisdiction over it if required.","sub":"Counterclaims – Compulsory & Supplemental Jurisdiction","family":"Supplemental Jurisdiction","unit":17},{"n":29,"summary":"Company (State A) sues plumber (State B) in federal court (State A) for negligent pipe installation; plumber filed a Rule 12(b)(2) motion (denied) then failed to respond; default entered; only 3 days’ notice of default-judgment hearing; plumber moves to set aside.","trigger":"Default judgment sought after defendant ‘appeared’ by filing a motion; notice period before default-judgment hearing.","rule":"If a party has appeared, they must be served with written notice of a default-judgment application at least 7 days before the hearing (FRCP 55(b)(2)).","sub":"Default Judgment — Notice after Appearance (FRCP 55)","family":"Service of Process","unit":37},{"n":30,"summary":"Corporation in civil products suit refused discovery citing Fifth Amendment self-incrimination","trigger":"Corporate litigant asserts Fifth Amendment to block discovery","rule":"A corporation, not a natural person, does not enjoy the protections of the Fifth Amendment.","sub":"Discovery","family":"Discovery","unit":33},{"n":31,"summary":"A pedestrian is seriously injured when hit by a car and later sues the driver in a federal diversity action. In automatic disclosures she provides doctor contact information, and during discovery the defendant requests all medical records. Plaintiff produces some records but does not produce 70 pages of handwritten treatment notes made by her treating physician over an 18‑month recovery period; at trial she offers the notes through the physician under a hearsay exception, and the defendant objects because the notes were not produced and detailed analysis is needed for cross-examination. Plaintiff testifies she only learned of the notes the prior week.","trigger":"Failure to supplement discovery/disclosures; court considers exclusion unless the failure was substantially justified or harmless.","rule":"Failure to supplement disclosures can lead to exclusion of evidence unless the failure was substantially justified or harmless.","sub":"Discovery – Rule 26 supplementation; Rule 37(c)(1) sanctions","family":"Discovery","unit":35},{"n":32,"summary":"Plaintiff seeks internal company guidelines in discovery; defendant claims privilege/work product; plaintiff moves to compel.","trigger":"Work-product doctrine → only protects material prepared in anticipation of litigation; ordinary business records are not work product.","rule":"When a claim of privilege is raised, the court can conduct an in-camera review to determine if the materials are privileged. If they are, they will not be disclosed to the other party.","sub":"Discovery – Work Product","family":"Discovery","unit":34},{"n":33,"summary":"Contractor disputes discovery of architect report claimed as work product. No lawsuit was on the mind here so it wasn't work product","trigger":"Work product objection and preservation.","rule":"Work prepared by an attorney or his agents in anticipation of litigation is work product. Work product must be provided to the other side if they can show a substantial need and that they cannot obtain the substantial equivalent of the information without undue hardship. An objection based on work product must be specifically made to preserve the objection (privilege log).","sub":"Discovery – Work Product","family":"Discovery","unit":34},{"n":34,"summary":"A two-car accident occurred in State A. One driver died; his estate’s executor (citizen of State A) later sued the surviving driver (citizen of State A) in federal court. The deceased driver, however, was a citizen of State B at the time of death. The issue is whether diversity exists when an executor sues on behalf of a deceased person.","trigger":"Diversity in cases involving an estate: determine citizenship using the decedent’s citizenship, not the executor’s citizenship.","rule":"For a diversity case involving a deceased party, the courts will look at the citizenship of the deceased party, NOT the executor of the estate.","sub":"Diversity – Decedent Citizenship","family":"SMJ","unit":16},{"n":35,"summary":"A man from State A brought a diversity action for $80,000 against a limited liability company (LLC) in federal court. The man was suing for a breach of contract on the part of the LLC. The man had $40,000 of present actual damages and had $40,000 of consequential damages. The man knew that he might have a tough time in court proving that the consequential damages were foreseeable at the time of contracting with the LLC. The LLC was organized in State B and has its headquarters in State B. However, since the paperwork was made public, before the filing of the man’s complaint, the LLC added members in State A and State B.","trigger":"Diversity analysis involving an LLC; identify citizenship of every member.","rule":"When analyzing citizenship of an LLC for diversity purposes, it will be considered a citizen of every state in which each of its members is a citizen. Here, the LLC had member of state A and B. Therefore, since the plaintiff was from state A, there is no diversity.","sub":"Diversity – LLC citizenship","family":"Jurisdiction","unit":6},{"n":36,"summary":"Multiple plaintiffs (Husband, wife and child) are injured in the same incident (contaminated food) and they sue defendant (company that processed and distributed the food) in federal court. Each plaintiff has separate personal-injury damages, and all are below $75,000 though the combined total exceeds $75,000.","trigger":"In diversity, separate plaintiffs generally cannot aggregate separate claims to meet the amount-in-controversy unless a common/undivided interest exists; supplemental jurisdiction only helps if at least one plaintiff independently exceeds $75,000.","rule":"In diversity, separate plaintiffs cannot aggregate separate personal-injury claims to reach >$75,000 (unless there’s a common/undivided interest); you need at least one plaintiff over $75,000 to use supplemental for others. Here, Husband $60k, Wife $50k, Child $40k → GRANT motion to dismiss. No one plaintiff exceeds $75,000.You cannot add husband + wife + child because these are separate personal injuries (not a common undivided interest). Correct: Grant dismissal for lack of AIC. What does common undived interest mean? “Common/undivided interest” does not mean “same incident.” It means one single right that the group owns together, so the defendant owes one lump obligation to the group, not separate amounts to each person.","sub":"Diversity Jurisdiction – Amount in controversy; aggregation; supplemental","family":"SMJ","unit":15},{"n":37,"summary":"Diversity case filed in federal court in State A; contract has forum-selection clause naming State B; State A law deems such clauses unenforceable; defendant moves to transfer to State B.","trigger":"Erie conflict: state rule vs federal transfer practice; forum-selection clause invoked in diversity.","rule":"In diversity, venue transfer is governed by federal law (28 U.S.C. § 1404); a federal statute on point controls over contrary state law.","sub":"Erie — Transfer of Venue / Forum-Selection (28 U.S.C. § 1404)","family":"Venue","unit":42},{"n":38,"summary":"Plaintiff sues in federal court on diversity in State B, but the dispute involves events and parties tied to multiple states. The court must determine which state’s substantive law applies to a key issue.","trigger":"Diversity case: federal court must apply the forum state’s choice-of-law rules to determine the governing substantive law.","rule":"In diversity, the federal court applies the forum state’s choice-of-law rules—i.e., apply the substantive law the State B court would apply if the case were in State B court.","sub":"Erie / Choice of Law – Forum state choice-of-law rules in diversity","family":"Erie Doctrine","unit":3},{"n":39,"summary":"State law requires plaintiff plead lack of contributory negligence, but under FRCP contributory negligence is an affirmative defense; defendant moves to dismiss for failure to plead it.","trigger":"Erie/Hanna conflict between state pleading requirement and FRCP Rule 8/affirmative defenses → FRCP controls if valid.","rule":"Under the Erie doctrine, a federal court sitting in diversity will use federal law if the issue is procedural in nature.","sub":"Erie Doctrine – Pleading/FRCP vs State","family":"Erie Doctrine","unit":2},{"n":40,"summary":"After a fire burned down a house that a tenant was renting, the tenant brought a federal diversity action against the landlord. The complaint alleged that the fire had been caused by the landlord’s negligent failure to maintain the house’s electrical system in accordance with the applicable housing code. The landlord’s own investigation indicated that the fire had been caused by the tenant’s leaving a soup pot unattended on a hot stove.","trigger":"State pleading rule vs FRCP","rule":"Federal pleading standards govern in federal court.","sub":"Erie pleading","family":"Erie Doctrine","unit":2},{"n":41,"summary":"Beneficiary from State A sues trustee from State B in federal court in State B (diversity) for converting trust assets; State B law provides treble punitive damages in this situation; federal constitutional law limits punitive damages ratios; question asks which law governs punitive damages determination.","trigger":"Potential overlap between state law remedy and federal law; state law can apply unless it conflicts with controlling federal law.","rule":"A state law can still apply, even when there is a federal law on point, if the state law does not conflict with the federal law.","sub":"Federal Courts / Erie – State law vs federal law (apply state law if no conflict)","family":"Erie Doctrine","unit":3},{"n":42,"summary":"Environmental activist sues in federal court under a federal statute that says shoreline owners are 'strongly encouraged' to keep a 30‑foot natural strip by the shore. Plaintiff seeks a preliminary injunction to stop defendant from planting a lawn within 30 feet.","trigger":"Preliminary injunction requires likelihood of success on the merits; an aspirational/encouraging statute creates no enforceable duty, so plaintiff cannot show success.","rule":"A preliminary injunction requires likelihood of success on the merits; an aspirational statute (“strongly encouraged”) creates no enforceable duty, so injunction should be denied.","sub":"Injunctions – Preliminary injunction (likelihood of success)","family":"Injunctions","unit":null},{"n":43,"summary":"Court issues TRO limiting disruptive courthouse protest; group challenges TRO when they learned of it shortly before protesting.","trigger":"TRO without effective notice/service and vague/broad terms → challenge based on Rule 65 notice/specificity requirements.","rule":"A temporary restraining order will be struck down if it stops more conduct than required to prevent harm to the applicant","sub":"Injunctions – TRO (Rule 65)","family":"Injunctions","unit":null},{"n":44,"summary":"A designer seeks an ex parte temporary restraining order (TRO) in federal court to stop a clothing manufacturer from selling allegedly copied neckties, arguing irreparable harm and that notice would cause immediate sales. The court issues the TRO without notice and orders it to expire 21 days after issuance.","trigger":"Ex parte TRO issued; question turns on Rule 65 maximum duration for a TRO without notice.","rule":"A TRO issued without notice may last no more than 14 days (with one possible 14-day extension for good cause); a 21-day TRO exceeds the permissible duration.","sub":"Injunctions – TRO duration (Rule 65)","family":"Injunctions","unit":null},{"n":45,"summary":"Woman faces multiple replevin claims for one remaining phone and wants to force claimants into one suit","trigger":"Statutory interpleader requirements: $500+ stake and minimal diversity among claimants","rule":"Under statutory interpleader, the amount in controversy must be $500 or more and one claimant must be a citizen of a different state than any other claimant. Parties in an interpleader action: Stakeholder and the claimants. Under Rule Interpleader the amount has to exceed $75.000 (needs diversity between stakeholder and claimants). Under Staturory Interpleader for $500 or more (minimal diversity only that 1 claimant is from a different state than the other claimants/stakeholder not taken in consideration)","sub":"Interpleader","family":"Interpleader","unit":46},{"n":46,"summary":"Stakeholder faces competing claims to same bracelet from citizens of different (claimants).","trigger":"Risk of inconsistent obligations among claimants. Statutory Interpleader $500 or more and the stakeholder citizenship doesn't matter, only that amoung the climants there is at least one citizenship diferrent from another. Rule interpleader $75.000 or more and there cannot be a match across the v.","rule":"For statutory interpleader, the amount in controversy must be $500 or more.","sub":"Interpleader – Statutory","family":"Interpleader","unit":46},{"n":47,"summary":"Investor sued company in State A securities action and won finding false offering materials. University later sued same company in federal court and sought to offensively apply State A judgment via collateral estoppel. State A does not allow non‑mutual issue preclusion.","trigger":"Attempted non‑mutual offensive collateral estoppel using prior State A judgment.","rule":"Federal courts must give state judgments the same preclusive effect the rendering state would give them (Full Faith and Credit).","sub":"Issue Preclusion — Full Faith & Credit","family":"Preclusion","unit":25},{"n":48,"summary":"During a crowded dinner rush at a restaurant, a waiter, a waitress, and a customer collided. The customer first sued the waiter for negligence, but the jury found the customer 100% at fault for causing the accident. After losing, the customer files a second lawsuit against the waitress arising from the same collision.","trigger":"Plaintiff already litigated and lost the factual issue of who caused the accident and now sues a different defendant.","rule":"Issue preclusion bars a party from relitigating an issue that was actually litigated and decided against that party, even when asserted by a new defendant, if non‑mutual defensive preclusion is allowed.","sub":"Issue Preclusion – Non‑Mutual Defensive","family":"Preclusion","unit":25},{"n":49,"summary":"A restaurant served spoiled chicken. A man sued for $500 and won a detailed negligence verdict finding the restaurant negligent. A woman who ate the same meal but did not know about the first lawsuit until it was too late later sues the same restaurant for $40,000 and seeks to rely on the earlier negligence finding.","trigger":"Plaintiff seeks to offensively use a prior negligence finding from a low‑stakes case.","rule":"Non‑mutual offensive issue preclusion may be denied where applying it would be unfair, such as when the defendant lacked sufficient incentive to fully litigate the first action.","sub":"Issue Preclusion – Non‑Mutual Offensive (Fairness)","family":"Preclusion","unit":25},{"n":50,"summary":"On April Fool’s Day, a prankster frightened a married couple. The wife sued for intentional infliction of emotional distress and lost, although the judge found the conduct could support negligence. The husband later sues the prankster and attempts to rely on findings from the wife’s lawsuit.","trigger":"Plaintiff sat out the first litigation and attempts to offensively use prior findings.","rule":"Non‑mutual offensive issue preclusion may be denied where the plaintiff strategically waited to sue and fairness considerations weigh against preclusion.","sub":"Issue Preclusion – Sit‑and‑Wait / Fairness","family":"Preclusion","unit":25},{"n":51,"summary":"Post-verdict JMOL motion filed 22 days after judgment.","trigger":"Timeliness of renewed JMOL motion.","rule":"A party must make a motion for a directed verdict to preserve their right to be granted a jnov.","sub":"JMOL – Timeliness","family":"Post-Trial","unit":29},{"n":52,"summary":"Retailer sued wholesaler in federal diversity court for breach of contract and fraud. After trial, jury returned verdict for retailer. Neither party moved for directed verdict (JMOL) before jury deliberated. Wholesaler later moved for JNOV/JMOL and new trial based on insufficient evidence and appealed.","trigger":"Post-verdict JMOL sought without pre-verdict JMOL.","rule":"To obtain JNOV/post-verdict JMOL (and challenge sufficiency on appeal), a party must first move for JMOL/directed verdict before the case goes to the jury.","sub":"JMOL / JNOV — Preservation Requirement","family":"Post-Trial","unit":29},{"n":53,"summary":"After verdict for plaintiff, defendant renews JMOL challenging sufficiency of evidence on key factual dispute.","trigger":"Renewed JMOL after verdict → court applies legally sufficient evidentiary basis standard.","rule":"The judge will not grant a renewed motion for a judgment as a matter of law if there is legally sufficient evidence to support the verdict. However, the judge will not weigh the evidence.","sub":"JMOL/JNOV – Standard (Rule 50)","family":"Post-Trial","unit":29},{"n":54,"summary":"Defendant files renewed JMOL six weeks after verdict; parties agree to extension and counsel shows good cause for delay.","trigger":"Renewed JMOL timing → must be filed within 28 days; court cannot extend time by agreement or good cause.","rule":"According to FRCP 50(b), post-trial motions must be raised within 28 days after entry of judgment. Under FRCP 6(b)(2), no extensions are allowed to those time periods.","sub":"JMOL/JNOV – Timing (Rule 50)","family":"Post-Trial","unit":29},{"n":55,"summary":"Plaintiff slips at a restaurant and injures his leg; doctor misdiagnoses it as a sprain so no cast is applied. Days later, plaintiff falls while carrying groceries because the broken bone can’t support his weight and suffers additional injuries. Plaintiff sues restaurant for all damages; restaurant seeks to implead the doctor.","trigger":"A defendant seeks to bring in a nonparty who may be liable to the defendant for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim (contribution/indemnity).","rule":"Impleader is allowed if the party being sued brings a claim against a nonparty who is or may be liable to him for all or part of the claim against him. NO Impleader when used as a DEFENSE (not liable)","sub":"Joinder – Impleader (Rule 14) / third-party practice","family":"Joinder","unit":47},{"n":56,"summary":"Car accident: plaintiffs sue both driver and vehicle owner under a vicarious-liability statute; owner moves to sever for improper joinder.","trigger":"Rule 20 joinder when multiple parties on a side; must satisfy same transaction/occurrence + common question.","rule":"If there is more than one party on each side to start a lawsuit, the side that has more than one party must meet the 2-part Rule 20 test.","sub":"Joinder – Rule 20","family":"Joinder","unit":47},{"n":57,"summary":"Plaintiff (State A) buys an antique map from Defendant (a State B art‑dealer corporation with PPB in State B) while on holiday in State B; the map is counterfeit. Plaintiff sues Defendant in federal court in State A and learns Defendant advertises that it frequently visits State A and owns a storage facility in State A worth $95,000. Plaintiff does not attempt service under State A’s long‑arm statute (registered mail) and instead attaches the storage facility and asserts quasi in rem jurisdiction under a separate State A statute; Defendant moves to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction.","trigger":"Quasi in rem based on attachment of in-state property; requirement to attempt in personam jurisdiction and constitutional limits (property unrelated to claim).","rule":"If the forum’s statutes require reasonable efforts to obtain in personam jurisdiction first, plaintiff can’t jump straight to quasi in rem by attaching unrelated property without attempting the required service method.","sub":"Jurisdiction – Quasi in rem","family":"Personal Jurisdiction","unit":8},{"n":58,"summary":"In federal diversity jury trial, 6 jurors and 2 alternates seated; two jurors replaced by alternates; a third juror is excused ill; parties stipulate to verdict by 5 jurors; verdict for defendant; plaintiff moves for new trial claiming 5 is too few.","trigger":"Federal civil jury size: must begin with at least 6, but parties may stipulate to fewer for verdict.","rule":"A federal civil jury must begin with 6–12 jurors, but the parties may stipulate to a verdict by fewer than 6; a party cannot later challenge a stipulation after an adverse verdict.","sub":"Jury – Number of Jurors / Stipulation (Rule 48)","family":"Jury Trial","unit":28},{"n":59,"summary":"Civil jury reduced below six without stipulation; verdict entered; new trial sought.","trigger":"Civil jury verdict with fewer than six jurors.","rule":"Under Rule 48, Unless the parties stipulate otherwise, the verdict must be unanimous and must be returned by a jury of at least 6 members.","sub":"Jury – Size and Unanimity (Rule 48)","family":"Jury Trial","unit":28},{"n":60,"summary":"Parties stipulate to 9 jurors and non-unanimous verdict; court polls jury showing a 5–4 split for defendant; plaintiff seeks new trial.","trigger":"Jury verdict requirements → parties may stipulate to non-unanimous verdict; polling permitted; validity depends on Rule 48 and stipulation.","rule":"FRCP 48 states that unless the parties stipulate otherwise, the verdict must be unanimous and must be returned by a jury of at least 6 members. It also allows polling of the jury either on a party’s request or by the court’s own initiative.","sub":"Jury – Verdict/Unanimity (Rule 48)","family":"Jury Trial","unit":28},{"n":61,"summary":"Judge gives erroneous jury instruction; counsel fails to object until after jury has begun deliberations.","trigger":"Jury instruction error → objection must be made before jury retires; otherwise waived absent plain error.","rule":"An objection must be filed in a timely fashion or it will be waived, unless there is plain error involved, meaning that it would affect the party’s substantial rights.","sub":"Jury Instructions – Preserving Error","family":"Post-Trial","unit":30},{"n":62,"summary":"A worker injured by a malfunctioning machine sued in federal diversity against the manufacturer and the maintenance company. At trial, the worker submitted a proposed negligence jury instruction. The court rejected it and instead gave a negligence instruction the worker’s attorney believed was less favorable and legally incorrect. The attorney did not object to the instruction before it was given. The jury returned a verdict for defendants. The question asks what argument has the best chance of persuading the court to grant the motion.","trigger":"Failure to timely object to jury instructions, but seeking relief post-verdict.","rule":"Even without a timely objection, a court may grant relief for plain error affecting substantial rights; plain error is a serious, obvious mistake that likely affected the outcome.","sub":"Jury Instructions / Preservation / Plain Error","family":"Post-Trial","unit":30},{"n":63,"summary":"Plaintiff sought damages and injunction; jury demand made; judge tried injunction without jury","trigger":"Legal vs equitable claims with jury demand. If there is judge and jury the jury goes first because the judge is bound by jury fact finding","rule":"The Seventh Amendment preserves jury trial for legal damages claims, not equitable injunction claims","sub":"Jury Trial","family":"Jury Trial","unit":27},{"n":64,"summary":"A business owner sues an out-of-state investor in State X state court for tortious interference. State X automatically provides a jury trial in such actions unless the plaintiff opts out. The investor removes to federal court. The owner never files a federal jury demand and raises the issue only after 30 days have passed from the defendant’s final amended answer. The investor argues the owner waived a jury.","trigger":"Case removed from state court where jury is automatic; determine whether a federal jury demand is required after removal when state law did not require a demand.","rule":"After removal, a party need not make a new jury demand if, under state law, a jury trial was automatic (or was properly demanded) before removal; the right is preserved in federal court.","sub":"Jury Trial – Removal; jury demand timing","family":"Jury Trial","unit":28},{"n":65,"summary":"Retailer sued architect for fraud. Complaint omitted jury demand. Architect filed Rule 12(b)(6) motion but not answer. Retailer amended complaint adding defamation and demanded jury on both claims.","trigger":"Jury demand filed before defendant answered.","rule":"Jury must be demanded within 14 days after last pleading on the issue; motions are not pleadings.","sub":"Jury Trial — Rule 38 Timing","family":"Jury Trial","unit":28},{"n":66,"summary":"A football team entered a 10-year lease with a city to use the city’s stadium. Five years later, the team threatened to leave and move to another city. The city sued in federal court seeking a permanent injunction to prevent breach. In its answer, the team asserted a counterclaim for $10 million in damages based on the city’s alleged failure to maintain the stadium as required, and demanded a jury trial on the counterclaim. The city moved to try the injunction claim before the counterclaim; the team objected and moved to try its jury counterclaim first.","trigger":"Seventh Amendment / jury trial right: legal claims to jury; equitable claims to judge; when overlapping issues, jury issues tried first and bind judge.","rule":"When a case includes both legal and equitable claims with overlapping issues, the jury must decide the legal claims first, and the judge is bound by the jury’s factual findings when later deciding equitable relief.","sub":"Jury Trial Right – Legal vs Equitable Claims; Order of Trial","family":"Jury Trial","unit":27},{"n":67,"summary":"Question asks when a civil action is entitled to a jury trial.","trigger":"Legal vs equitable claim and amount in controversy.","rule":"The 7th Amendment preserves a right to a jury in legal claims where the value exceeds $20.","sub":"Jury Trial Right – Seventh Amendment","family":"Jury Trial","unit":27},{"n":68,"summary":"A homeowner brought a federal diversity action against a manufacturer of gas grills, asserting products liability claims and seeking damages for injuries she had suffered when her grill exploded as she was lighting it. The manufacturer timely demanded a jury trial, and the court informed the parties that it would seat a seven-person jury. The parties agreed to a non- unanimous jury verdict.","trigger":"7‑person federal jury returns 4–3 verdict","rule":"A federal civil verdict must be supported by at least six jurors.","sub":"Jury verdict","family":"Jury Trial","unit":28},{"n":69,"summary":"A man was criminally tried for attacking and robbing a woman at an ATM but was acquitted because the victim could not identify him. The woman later brings a civil lawsuit against the man for battery based on the same incident.","trigger":"Attempt to use a criminal acquittal to bar a later civil action.","rule":"A criminal acquittal does not preclude a subsequent civil action because the parties and burdens of proof differ.","sub":"No Preclusion – Criminal vs Civil","family":"Preclusion","unit":26},{"n":70,"summary":"A frequent traveler sued a hotel in federal court based on diversity for injuries caused by bed bugs. The jury found for the plaintiff, but the judge later dismissed the case for lack of subject‑matter jurisdiction after discovering diversity was lacking. The plaintiff then files the identical negligence action in state court.","trigger":"Prior dismissal based solely on lack of subject‑matter jurisdiction.","rule":"A dismissal for lack of subject‑matter jurisdiction is not a final judgment on the merits and therefore has no claim‑ or issue‑preclusive effect.","sub":"No Preclusion – Jurisdictional Dismissal","family":"Preclusion","unit":22},{"n":71,"summary":"Construction company (Plaintiff), incorporated in State A, sues Internet company (Defendant), incorporated in State C, in federal court in State A under ICFA. ICFA authorizes suit in plaintiff’s home state, nationwide service, and deems PJ regardless of defendant’s contacts with plaintiff’s home state. Plaintiff serves Defendant by hand-serving Defendant’s CEO while he is temporarily in State D.","trigger":"Federal-question claim + federal statute authorizes nationwide service of process (Rule 4(k)(1)(C)) → PJ is based on “national contacts,” not minimum contacts with the forum state.","rule":"When a federal statute provides nationwide service of process for a federal claim, a federal court may exercise personal jurisdiction so long as service is statutorily authorized and due process is satisfied by the defendant’s contacts with the United States as a whole (not with the forum state).","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction - Federal-question PJ / Nationwide service of process (Rule 4(k)(1)(C)) / “National contacts” (5th Amendment due process)","family":"Personal Jurisdiction","unit":9},{"n":72,"summary":"Defendant ignored suit claiming no PJ; plaintiff obtained default judgment; plaintiff seeks to enforce judgment in another state.","trigger":"Default judgment enforcement in another state → collateral attack allowed if rendering court lacked personal jurisdiction.","rule":"In a collateral attack, a defendant can try to strike down a prior judgment in another state by showing that it is void for personal jurisdiction.","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – Collateral Attack on Default Judgment","family":"Personal Jurisdiction","unit":10},{"n":73,"summary":"An insurer (incorporated/PPB in State A) sold a disability policy to a surgeon while the surgeon lived in State A; the surgeon later moved to State B. Insurer has a small office in State B and sells policies there; after the surgeon sues the insurer in State B for unpaid disability benefits, service is made by handing the summons to the insurer’s CEO in State A. Separately, the CEO is served in State B with a divorce action while attending a one‑day unrelated conference; both the insurer and CEO move to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction.","trigger":"Personal jurisdiction as to a corporation vs an officer: corporation’s contacts vs tag service on the individual while physically present.","rule":"Insurer: no PJ in State B where its forum contacts don’t give rise to or relate to the policy dispute (no general PJ because not incorporated/PPB there). CEO: State B has transient (tag) jurisdiction because he was personally served while physically present in State B, even if the trip was unrelated.","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – Corporation vs individual; tag","family":"Personal Jurisdiction","unit":7},{"n":74,"summary":"Defendant in State A makes false police report causing harm in State B; sued in State B.","trigger":"Intentional conduct aimed at forum causing in-state effects.","rule":"Under the effects test, personal jurisdiction can apply to a party that intentionally aims their conduct at another state, knowing the effects of their actions will be felt in that other state.","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – Effects Test","family":"Personal Jurisdiction","unit":4},{"n":75,"summary":"Taxi company is incorporated in State A, has HQ in State B, and maintains a small office/dispatch center in State C (no customers visit; rides only in A and B). A State C resident is injured in an accident in State B and sues the company in State C; the company moves to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction.","trigger":"Plaintiff asserts general personal jurisdiction based on an in-state office, but the corporation may not be 'at home' there.","rule":"The systematic and continuous contacts test has been sometimes interpreted as only giving personal jurisdiction over a corporation in the states where it has enough contacts to be considered at home, usually where they are incorporated and where their principal place of business is located. Here, there is pruposefull availment because they have an office in state C but the contacts that are in the state are not related to the cause of action. Specific Jdx: 1. D has certain minimum contacts with forum; and purposeful availment so that D can reasonably anticipate being haled into court there (predictable), 2. arises out of or relates to (nexus) ….lawsuit arises out of Ds contacts with the state 3. fairness or reasonableness","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – General jurisdiction ('at home') for corporations","family":"Personal Jurisdiction","unit":5},{"n":76,"summary":"State A corporation sues an individual in State B state court; defendant is served at his home in State B. Suit seeks money damages under State E law for breach of a contract to convey State E real estate; the contract was negotiated face‑to‑face in State C and the final document was signed in State D. No activities connected with the contract occurred in State B; defendant moves to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction.","trigger":"Forum asserts personal jurisdiction over an individual served at home in the forum despite claim arising elsewhere.","rule":"A state may exercise general personal jurisdiction over an individual in the state of domicile/residence (and also via in-state service); with general PJ, the claim need not arise out of forum contacts.","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – General jurisdiction (domicile/service)","family":"Personal Jurisdiction","unit":5},{"n":77,"summary":"A man from State A brought an action for $80,000 against a limited liability company (LLC) in state court in State A. The man was suing for a breach of contract on the part of the LLC. The man had $40,000 of present actual damages and had $40,000 of consequential damages. LLC was organized in State B and has its headquarters in State B. The LLC is comprised of six members, all from state A. The man negotiated a contract with the LLC in state B. The entire performance of the contract, including payment, was to be performed in State B.","trigger":"Personal jurisdiction/general jurisdiction over LLC; determine where LLC is “at home.”","rule":"For personal jurisdiction purposes, an LLC will be considered a resident (at home) in its state of formation and principal place of business. For PJ is similar to corporations. Here, the LLC for purpose of jurisdiction is a resident of state B (considered at home), but the man sued the LLC in state A and no contacts there. Thus, no PJ FOR PURPOSE OF SMJ AN LLC IS CITIZEN OF EACH STATE WHERE THEIR MEMBERS ARE FROM. BUTTT FOR PLJ IS THE SAME AS FOR CORPORATIONS THE STATE OF INCORPORATIONS AND THE PPB","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – LLC “at home”","family":"Personal Jurisdiction","unit":5},{"n":78,"summary":"A woman, a citizen of State A, sued a man, a citizen of State B, in State A for defrauding her in a purchase of an automobile that took place in State B. The woman was driving through State B and saw a for sale sign on a car that was sitting in the driveway of the man’s home. She contacted the man and arranged a test drive of the vehicle. Pleased with the test drive, the woman told the man that she lived in neighboring State A and would go to her bank in State A to get the cash to complete the transaction. She withdrew the cash and returned to State B and purchased the vehicle.","trigger":"Plaintiff’s/third party’s unilateral act creates forum contact; ask whether defendant purposefully availed.","rule":"The unilateral acts of a third party, even if foreseeable, will not be enough for personal jurisdiction over a defendant, unless the defendant purposely availed himself to the benefits of the forum state. Foreseeability is not enough for PJ, we need purposefull availment to the benefits of the forum state. If the man had publicity in state or shipped the car to state A the result would be different.","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – Purposeful availment","family":"Personal Jurisdiction","unit":4},{"n":79,"summary":"A man in State A often buys rare cars out of state and hires drivers to bring them to him. He buys a car from a dealer in State B and hires a driver to drive it 1,500 miles to him in State A. In State C, the car breaks down and is towed to the only mechanic in State C who can fix that car. The mechanic realizes the car belongs to the man (the mechanic had provided services to the man the previous year while in State A, for which the man never paid). The mechanic sues the man in State C state court and asks the court to order the car seized to satisfy any potential judgment for the unpaid State A mechanic work. The man has no systematic and continuous contacts with State C, but the mechanic can establish minimum contacts from the man’s marketing business. State C has no long-arm statute, and the court finds jurisdiction would not offend fair play and substantial justice.","trigger":"Plaintiff seeks to seize/attach defendant’s property located in the forum to secure satisfaction of a personal claim; jurisdiction based on property attachment.","rule":"When a plaintiff seeks to attach a defendant’s property located in the forum to satisfy a judgment on a personal claim, the court is exercising quasi in rem jurisdiction (and due process still requires minimum contacts).","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – Quasi in rem; attachment","family":"Personal Jurisdiction","unit":8},{"n":80,"summary":"Out-of-state manufacturer sued where injury occurred; stream of commerce PJ issue.","trigger":"Specific personal jurisdiction / purposeful availment.","rule":"Before a nonresident defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction, he must have purposely availed himself to the forum state, the lawsuit must arise out of or relate to his contacts with the state, and it must be fair to sue him in that state.","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – Stream of Commerce","family":"Personal Jurisdiction","unit":4},{"n":81,"summary":"Nonresident defendant is served while physically present in forum state; underlying tort occurred in a different state.","trigger":"Personal service while physically present in forum (tag jurisdiction) → PJ typically proper even if claim unrelated.","rule":"Although being served in a forum state would give the state personal jurisdiction over a defendant, they must be served by someone 18 or older and not a party to the lawsuit.","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – Tag Jurisdiction","family":"Personal Jurisdiction","unit":7},{"n":82,"summary":"Defendant answers on the merits, then later amends answer to add lack of personal jurisdiction defense.","trigger":"Rule 12 defenses → personal jurisdiction is waived if not raised in the first Rule 12 response (motion or answer).","rule":"The personal jurisdiction defense must be raised in a defendant’s first response to a complaint or it will be waived. If an answer is amended to add a personal jurisdiction defense, it would be treated just as if the defense had been in the answer originally.","sub":"Personal Jurisdiction – Waiver (Rule 12)","family":"Personal Jurisdiction","unit":10},{"n":83,"summary":"A pedestrian domiciled in State A was crossing a street in State B when hit by a car driven by a citizen of a foreign country. Both suffered injuries. The pedestrian sued the driver in federal district court in State B for $100,000. The driver believes the pedestrian crossed illegally and wants to respond; State B uses contributory negligence.","trigger":"FRCP response: affirmative defense (contributory negligence) + compulsory counterclaim arising from same accident (same transaction/occurrence).","rule":"A defendant must plead affirmative defenses in the answer and must assert any compulsory counterclaim arising out of the same transaction or occurrence or it is waived (use it or lose it).","sub":"Pleading – Affirmative Defenses & Compulsory Counterclaims (Rule 13(a))","family":"Pleadings","unit":20},{"n":84,"summary":"Defendant answered, then sought to add improper venue defense 6 days later","trigger":"Amendment sought within 21 days of serving answer","rule":"A party may amend once as of right within 21 days after serving a responsive pleading","sub":"Pleadings","family":"Personal Jurisdiction","unit":10},{"n":85,"summary":"Which circumstance requires the defendant’s response to be pleaded in an answer (or reply to a counterclaim), rather than raised by a motion to dismiss: (A) lack of personal jurisdiction in Hawaii in a copyright suit, (B) duress as a defense to a contract-performance suit, (C) failure to plead fraud with sufficient facts, or (D) improper venue in SDNY in a false-imprisonment suit.","trigger":"Identify an affirmative defense that must be raised in a responsive pleading rather than via Rule 12 motion.","rule":"Affirmative defenses (such as duress) must be pleaded in the answer, while lack of personal jurisdiction, improper venue, and failure to state a claim can be raised by Rule 12 motion.","sub":"Pleadings – Affirmative defenses vs Rule 12","family":"Pleadings","unit":20},{"n":86,"summary":"In diversity products case, retailer files a crossclaim against manufacturer (both same-state citizens) arising from same incident.","trigger":"Crossclaim between co-defendants from same transaction/occurrence → supplemental jurisdiction allows despite lack of diversity between them.","rule":"Crossclaims are allowed when a party is asserting a claim for relief from a co-party, but not when the party is seeking to assert a defense.","sub":"Pleadings – Crossclaims (Rule 13(g))","family":"Pleadings","unit":20},{"n":87,"summary":"A State A basketball player is tackled by a State B jockey during a charity football game and sues in federal court on diversity alleging an intentional tort. The court grants the jockey’s motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim and enters an order dismissing the case, with no other language in the dismissal order. The player now wants to amend the complaint after dismissal.","trigger":"Case dismissed for failure to state a claim and dismissal order is silent; determine whether dismissal is on the merits/with prejudice and whether amendment is still available.","rule":"An involuntary dismissal— including for failure to state a claim—operates as an adjudication on the merits (with prejudice) unless the order states otherwise, so the plaintiff cannot amend as of right after such a dismissal.","sub":"Pleadings – Rule 12(b)(6); effect of dismissal","family":"Preclusion","unit":22},{"n":88,"summary":"Homeowner injured by improperly installed carpet; sues GC and subcontractor in diversity; GC crossclaims against subcontractor; GC settles and is dismissed; subcontractor moves to dismiss crossclaim.","trigger":"Crossclaim between co-parties survives if from same transaction/occurrence and includes derivative liability.","rule":"A crossclaim is a claim brought by a party against a co-party. It is allowed if it arises out of the same transaction or occurrence as the original claim and includes a claim that the co-party may be liable to the crossclaimant for all or part of a claim asserted in the action against the crossclaimant.","sub":"Pleadings / Crossclaims (Rule 13(g))","family":"Pleadings","unit":20},{"n":89,"summary":"A gymnast paralyzed by malfunctioning athletic equipment sues the manufacturer in federal court. The jury finds the manufacturer negligent but awards only a fraction of the damages sought. After the verdict, the gymnast’s attorneys discover evidence that several jurors were bribed by representatives of the manufacturer to secure a lower damages award.","trigger":"Verdict allegedly tainted by juror bribery/improper influence discovered after trial; select the proper post-trial motion.","rule":"A motion for a new trial is the proper remedy when the verdict is tainted by juror misconduct such as bribery or improper influence.","sub":"Post-Trial Motions – New trial","family":"Post-Trial","unit":30},{"n":90,"summary":"A woman was sued civilly for battery and claimed self-defense, arguing the plaintiff was trespassing on her property. She had previously been acquitted of criminal battery based on the same incident. She now argues the civil case is barred by issue preclusion because the criminal case already resolved the issue.","trigger":"Collateral estoppel (ISSUE PRECLUSION) requires the same issue to have been actually litigated and necessarily decided, but differing burdens matter: failure to meet higher criminal burden doesn’t preclude relitigation under lower civil burden.","rule":"An acquittal in a criminal case does not preclude relitigation of the same issue in a civil case because failure to prove guilt beyond a reasonable doubt does not determine liability by a preponderance of the evidence. A criminal acquittal never precludes a later civil action based on the same conduct. A criminal conviction may preclude a later civil action","sub":"Preclusion – Issue Preclusion & Burdens","family":"Preclusion","unit":26},{"n":91,"summary":"A real estate brokerage firm, which is incorporated and headquartered in State A, has brought a federal diversity action in State A against one of its former employees for theft of trade secrets. The former employee had worked at the firm’s headquarters throughout her employment but is now a citizen of State B. The firm seeks compensatory and punitive damages.","trigger":"Diversity case with punitive damages cap","rule":"State punitive-damage caps apply in diversity unless unconstitutional. Step 1 Ask: Is there a Federal Rule of Civil Procedure on punitive damage limits?👉 No. Only Supreme Court constitutional guidance (common law). That is NOT procedural. Step 2 So we use: 👉 State substantive law BUT not automatically the forum’s law. You must use: 👉 The law the forum state says to apply (choice-of-law). State A’s choice-of-law rules selected State A law. When you see a dicersity case and then a state lw it's an Erie question.","sub":"Punitive damages/Erie","family":"Erie Doctrine","unit":3},{"n":92,"summary":"Plaintiff (State B) sues employer (incorporated and PPB in State A) in State A state court for gender discrimination; employer removes to federal court in State A.","trigger":"Removal based on federal question; forum-defendant rule only limits diversity removal, not federal-question removal.","rule":"A case can be removed even if one of the defendants is a citizen of the state the case was filed in if it is a federal question.","sub":"Removal – Federal Question","family":"Removal","unit":12},{"n":93,"summary":"Employee sues partnership in state court under federal statute; defendants seek removal.","trigger":"Removal based on federal question and unanimity.","rule":"A diversity action in state court cannot be removed if any of the defendants are citizens of the state the action was brought in. However, that rules does not apply for a federal question.","sub":"Removal – Federal Question / Unanimity","family":"Removal","unit":12},{"n":94,"summary":"Wrongfully convicted man files §1983 suit in state court against a prosecutor and a police officer. Prosecutor resides in Northern District of State X; plaintiff and venue are in Southern District of State X; officer resides in State Y. Officer wants removal but prosecutor initially refuses. Two months later plaintiff adds the county as defendant; then all defendants agree to remove.","trigger":"Removal timing and unanimity; later-served defendant and 30-day windows; proper federal district for removal.","rule":"All properly joined and served defendants must consent to removal, and removal must be to the federal district embracing the state court; later-served defendants may trigger a new 30-day removal window.","sub":"Removal – Unanimity; timing; proper district","family":"Removal","unit":13},{"n":95,"summary":"A golf pro fired from a private prep school sues in State X state court under a state age‑discrimination statute; all parties are domiciled in State X. The golf pro seeks $350,000 in damages (claiming he planned to work another 10 years), though associates would testify he planned to stay only two more years before leaving for a graduate degree. The school plans to defend by citing a new federal regulation prohibiting schools from employing anyone convicted of multiple federal drug possession crimes, and the golf pro has such convictions. The school wants to remove to federal court.","trigger":"Plaintiff pleads only a state-law discrimination claim, but defendant relies on a federal regulation as its defense; removal based on federal question.","rule":"Federal‑question jurisdiction (and removal) requires a federal issue on the face of the plaintiff's well‑pleaded complaint; a federal defense does not create federal‑question jurisdiction.","sub":"Removal – Well‑pleaded complaint rule","family":"Removal","unit":11},{"n":96,"summary":"After plaintiff amends to add a federal claim and a new defendant, the new defendant removes; plaintiff later moves to remand.","trigger":"Removal after amended complaint adds federal question; remand motion timing / procedural defects (30-day window).","rule":"A diversity case cannot be removed if any of the defendants are citizens of the state in which the lawsuit was originally filed.","sub":"Removal & Remand","family":"Removal","unit":13},{"n":97,"summary":"Plaintiff (State A) sues defendant (State B) in State B state court for contract >$75k; defendant served; plaintiff amends to add federal discrimination claim; defendant removes 24 days after amended complaint.","trigger":"Removal based on newly added federal question; forum-defendant rule blocks diversity removal but not federal-question removal.","rule":"A defendant cannot remove a diversity case brought against him in his home state but can still remove a federal question claim brought against him in his home state.","sub":"Removal / Forum Defendant Rule","family":"Removal","unit":12},{"n":98,"summary":"Woman sues former employer in state court for age/sex discrimination under state and federal law; plaintiff removes to federal court to avoid state judge; defendant timely moves to remand.","trigger":"Removal attempted by plaintiff (not defendant).","rule":"Only defendants may remove a case from state to federal court; if a plaintiff removes, the federal court must remand.","sub":"Removal/Remand — Only Defendant May Remove","family":"Removal","unit":13},{"n":99,"summary":"Plaintiff (State A) buys a used car from Defendant corporation (State B) in a transaction in State B; car has defective brakes. While driving in State A, plaintiff crashes into a taxi owned/driven by Taxi Driver (State B) who was speeding. Plaintiff sues Defendant in federal court in State B for fraud and wins a $100,000 judgment but cannot collect because Defendant goes bankrupt; plaintiff then sues Taxi Driver in federal court in State A for the same $100,000 injuries, and Taxi Driver argues claim preclusion should bar the second suit.","trigger":"Claim preclusion effect of a prior judgment against one defendant on a later suit against a different defendant for the same injury.","rule":"Claim preclusion requires same parties (or privity); a judgment against one tortfeasor does not bar a later suit against a different tortfeasor who was not a party/privity.","sub":"Res Judicata – Claim preclusion","family":"Preclusion","unit":23},{"n":100,"summary":"Plaintiff's state-court tort case dismissed with prejudice for failure to prosecute; plaintiff refiles same claim against same defendant in federal court under diversity; defendant asserts res judicata.","trigger":"Prior dismissal 'with prejudice' in earlier suit between same parties on same claim.","rule":"A dismissal with prejudice operates as a final judgment on the merits for claim preclusion (res judicata) and must be given full faith and credit.","sub":"Res Judicata — Dismissal With Prejudice","family":"Preclusion","unit":22},{"n":101,"summary":"In a federal diversity car-accident case, the defendant's lawyer filed a general denial shortly after service, after only a brief consultation. The plaintiff's attorney immediately filed a Rule 11 sanctions motion with the court based on the general denial.","trigger":"Rule 11 sanctions sought without serving the motion first and allowing the 21‑day safe harbor to withdraw/correct.","rule":"When a party makes a Rule 11 motion, they must file it with the other party and give them 21 days to amend the act complained of.","sub":"Rule 11 – Safe Harbor","family":"Sanctions","unit":36},{"n":102,"summary":"Attorney files suit despite knowing facts contradict claim; Rule 11 sanctions sought.","trigger":"Rule 11 prefiling investigation and safe harbor.","rule":"Before a party can file a Rule 11 violation motion with the court, they must abide by the 21-day safe harbor provision, filing notice with the party whose conduct complained of.","sub":"Rule 11 – Sanctions","family":"Sanctions","unit":36},{"n":103,"summary":"Court initiates Rule 11 sanctions after trial reveals the contract signature was forged; attorney filed complaint attaching a seemingly signed contract.","trigger":"Court-initiated Rule 11 (no safe harbor) → lawyer avoids sanctions if reasonable pre-filing inquiry under circumstances.","rule":"Under Rule 11, the court can sanction an attorney on its own initiative without allowing the 21-day safe harbor provision, but must specify the conduct that lead to the violation.","sub":"Rule 11 Sanctions","family":"Sanctions","unit":36},{"n":104,"summary":"Attorney sues for specific performance on 'unique tractor' using signed contract client provided; later proven signature forged and client committing fraud; court initiates Rule 11-like sanctions hearing.","trigger":"Rule 11 sanctions based on filing without reasonable pre-filing inquiry into facts.","rule":"An attorney can be sanctioned under Rule 11 if they do not make an inquiry that is reasonable under the circumstances into the facts of the case.","sub":"Rule 11 Sanctions","family":"Sanctions","unit":36},{"n":105,"summary":"Process served on defendant's minor son at home; service challenged.","trigger":"Service of process at dwelling on suitable person.","rule":"Under Rule 4 of the FRCP, one of the ways a defendant can be served is by leaving a copy of the summons and complaint at the individual's dwelling or usual place of abode with someone of suitable age and discretion who resides there","sub":"Service of Process – Abode Service","family":"Service of Process","unit":37},{"n":106,"summary":"A university student (citizen of State A) sues her university (a nonprofit incorporated and located in State B) and the university's athletic director (citizen of State B) in federal court in State B, alleging a federal-law violation related to eliminating funding for a women's varsity team. The question asks the best method of serving the defendants.","trigger":"Service of process in federal court; identify permissible service methods under Rule 4 (including following forum-state service rules).","rule":"Service in federal court follows Rule 4, including methods allowed by forum state law.","sub":"Service of Process – Rule 4 methods","family":"Service of Process","unit":37},{"n":107,"summary":"Plaintiff sends a Rule 4(d) waiver request to a corporate officer but never completes service/filing; seeks default after no response.","trigger":"Rule 4(d) waiver request ignored → plaintiff must formally serve; default requires proper service and entry of default.","rule":"If a party does not agree to waive service of process, they must be formally served before they will be required to respond to the complaint.","sub":"Service of Process – Waiver & Default","family":"Service of Process","unit":37},{"n":108,"summary":"A restaurant in State A burned a customer from State B. After hearing how serious it was, the owner incorporated in State B to shield himself. Months later, the customer sued the corporation in federal court in State B for $290,000. The owner moves to dismiss for lack of SMJ.","trigger":"Diversity citizenship is measured at the time the complaint is filed (not at time of injury).","rule":"Diversity is determined at the time the complaint is filed, not when the actions giving rise to the lawsuit occurred.","sub":"SMJ – Diversity Timing","family":"SMJ","unit":14},{"n":109,"summary":"A county ordinance bans secondary school teachers from earning extra income through private tutoring. A statewide educators organization sues the county in federal court alleging a Fourteenth Amendment violation, based on members' loss of income.","trigger":"Associational standing: organization sues on behalf of members affected by the ordinance.","rule":"An association has standing to sue for its members if members have standing, the claim is germane to the organization's purpose, and neither the claim nor relief requires individual members' participation.","sub":"Standing – Associational standing","family":"Justiciability","unit":48},{"n":110,"summary":"Brother seeks US enforcement of foreign tort judgment under a treaty","trigger":"Treaty-based enforcement obligation invoked in federal court","rule":"Claims arising under a treaty present federal question jurisdiction","sub":"Subject Matter Jurisdiction","family":"SMJ","unit":19},{"n":111,"summary":"Correct Answer: C. A plaintiff sued a defendant in state court where both parties reside, alleging only a cause of action arising under a federal statute. A similar state cause of action exists. The federal statute provides that claims under it can be brought in any court of competent jurisdiction, and the statute has not yet been interpreted by any federal court. The question asks whether the state court should hear the case.","trigger":"Federal-question claim filed in state court; statute allows suit in any court of competent jurisdiction.","rule":"State courts have concurrent jurisdiction over federal claims unless Congress grants exclusive federal jurisdiction; defendants may remove federal-question cases to federal court, but state courts can hear them.","sub":"Subject Matter Jurisdiction / Concurrent Jurisdiction / Removal (Federal Question)","family":"SMJ","unit":19},{"n":112,"summary":"Estate representative files wrongful death and survival claims in diversity.","trigger":"Determining citizenship of estate for diversity in a wrongful death action (the citizenship used is of the deceased) is different than in a survival action (the citizenship used is of the estate, not personal representative).","rule":"For a survival action, the citizenship used for the plaintiff's estate is that of the plaintiff, not the personal representative. Survival vs. Wrongful Death — Full Memory Rule Same filer (personal representative). Different pockets. Different citizenship. Survival = Estate Decedent's pre-death damages Money → Estate Diversity → Estate's citizenship\n\nWrongful Death = Family Survivors' post-death damages Money → Beneficiaries Diversity → Decedent's citizenship","sub":"Subject Matter Jursidiction – Diversity (Estates)","family":"SMJ","unit":16},{"n":113,"summary":"A New York company runs tourist boats around Manhattan. It sells a VIP package with a boat tour and a private fireworks show in the Hudson River. The company uses a non‑tour boat to set off fireworks for the tour boat's guests and buys fireworks that are too powerful. A device misfires and sends flaming debris toward the tour boat, injuring a guest (NY resident). The guest sues the NY company and the firework manufacturer (Pennsylvania citizen) for $300,000 in NY state court on NY tort claims only. The manufacturer seeks removal.","trigger":"Tort injury on navigable waters involving vessels; determine admiralty jurisdiction and removability.","rule":"Federal courts have admiralty jurisdiction over torts occurring on navigable waters with a maritime nexus; admiralty jurisdiction can support federal jurisdiction even when claims arise under state law.","sub":"Subject‑Matter Jurisdiction – Admiralty","family":"SMJ","unit":19},{"n":114,"summary":"A man and his wife, both from State A were involved in a car accident with a woman from State B. The man was stopped at a red light when the woman's car struck his vehicle from behind. The man, who was the sole owner of the car, had $60,000 in medical bills and lost wages, as well as $18,000 of damage to his vehicle. The wife's damages totaled $30,000. The damage to the man's vehicle was completely covered by insurance. The man and wife filed, as co-plaintiffs, a lawsuit against the woman in a United States district court in State A. After filing the complaint, two major developments occurred. First, the woman made a motion to dismiss the complaint for lack of subject matter jurisdiction, arguing that no single claim exceeded $75,000. The man and his wife, who found credible evidence that the woman hit them intentionally because of their ethnicity, also amended their complaint to add a federal discrimination claim under which they sought the same damages as previously stated in their complaints. Will the court have subject matter jurisdiction over the man and wife's claim?","trigger":"Amended complaint changes jurisdictional analysis; determine SMJ at time of amendment.","rule":"If a complaint is amended, subject matter jurisdiction is determined at the time of the amended complaint, not the original one.","sub":"Subject‑Matter Jurisdiction – Amended pleadings","family":"SMJ","unit":14},{"n":115,"summary":"A businessman sues an Ohio corporation in federal court based on diversity, alleging $90,000 in damages. At trial, the corporation elicits testimony showing actual damages are at most $50,000, and the businessman agrees his damages do not exceed $50,000. The corporation then moves to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction based on amount in controversy.","trigger":"Evidence shows to a legal certainty that the plaintiff's claim could never meet the jurisdictional minimum; dismissal for lack of AIC.","rule":"If it appears to a legal certainty that the plaintiff's claim cannot meet the amount-in-controversy requirement, the court lacks diversity jurisdiction and may dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. AIC is judged at filing, but later proof can show \"legal certainty\" it was never met → court must dismiss for no diversity SMJ.","sub":"Subject-Matter Jurisdiction – Amount in controversy","family":"SMJ","unit":14},{"n":116,"summary":"A State A plaintiff sues a corporation for $100,000 in federal court. The corporation is incorporated in State B, does its manufacturing and has its retail outlets in State A, and rents a small suite in State C for executives to meet. The corporation impleads its manufacturing manager (domiciled in State A) for indemnity. After discovery, the manager moves to dismiss for lack of subject‑matter jurisdiction because he and the plaintiff are both State A citizens.","trigger":"Impleader adds a third‑party defendant of the same citizenship as the plaintiff; diversity is not destroyed unless the plaintiff asserts a claim against the third‑party defendant.","rule":"Diversity will not be destroyed if a third-party defendant and the original plaintiff are citizens of the same state as long as the plaintiff is not brining an action against the third-party defendant.","sub":"Subject‑Matter Jurisdiction – Diversity & impleader; supplemental jurisdiction limits","family":"Supplemental Jurisdiction","unit":17},{"n":117,"summary":"Plaintiff sues a corporation in federal court on diversity for strict products liability; issue is corporate citizenship for diversity.","trigger":"Diversity with corporate party → determine corporation’s citizenship (state of incorporation + principal place of business/nerve center).","rule":"For purposes of diversity, a corporation is deemed a citizen of every place it is incorporated as well as its principal place of business, which is determined by the nerve center, or headquarters test.","sub":"Subject-Matter Jurisdiction – Diversity (Corporate Citizenship)","family":"Jurisdiction","unit":6},{"n":118,"summary":"The President enters a self‑executing treaty requiring citizens of two nations to pay tort damages awarded against them by a court of either nation. Two U.S. citizens collide in a foreign country; a foreign court awards the woman a $500,000 judgment. The woman sues the man in federal district court in their home state to enforce the foreign judgment; the man moves to dismiss for lack of jurisdiction.","trigger":"Claim arises under a self-executing treaty; whether treaty-based right creates federal-question jurisdiction.","rule":"A suit to enforce rights created by a self-executing U.S. treaty arises under federal law, creating federal-question jurisdiction even without diversity.","sub":"Subject-Matter Jurisdiction – Federal question","family":"SMJ","unit":19},{"n":119,"summary":"Plaintiff in State A orders a $100 garden hose by phone from a catalog distributed by Defendant, a garden-supply company incorporated in State A with its principal place of business in State A. The hose has a defective nozzle and floods plaintiff’s garden, causing about $5,000 in warranty/consequential damages. Plaintiff also posts disparaging comments about Defendant on an online discussion board; Defendant counterclaims under a federal statute (FTLL) that creates statutory damages for false/disparaging online statements. Defendant removes the whole case to federal court and plaintiff moves to remand.","trigger":"Federal issue appears only as a defense/counterclaim; federal-question jurisdiction/removal requires it to appear on plaintiff’s well-pleaded complaint.","rule":"Federal-question jurisdiction (and removal) requires the federal issue to appear on the plaintiff’s well-pleaded complaint—defenses/counterclaims don’t count.","sub":"Subject-Matter Jurisdiction – Federal question & removal (well-pleaded complaint)","family":"Removal","unit":11},{"n":120,"summary":"Shop owner (A) sues distributor (incorporated B, PPB C) in federal court for $100k; distributor impleads wholesaler (incorporated B, PPB A); wholesaler counterclaims $60k; motion to dismiss for SMJ.","trigger":"Supplemental jurisdiction over counterclaim in diversity case; §1367(b) limits only certain plaintiff claims.","rule":"If a counterclaim is part of the same case or controversy, supplemental jurisdiction exists, and §1367(b) generally does not bar claims by non-plaintiffs in diversity cases.","sub":"Subject-Matter Jurisdiction – Supplemental (§1367)","family":"Supplemental Jurisdiction","unit":17},{"n":121,"summary":"Plaintiff brings federal discrimination claim plus related state-law claim; after federal claim is dismissed by settlement, defendant moves to dismiss remaining state claim for lack of SMJ.","trigger":"Federal-question claim dismissed while related state claim remains → supplemental jurisdiction / discretionary decline under §1367(c).","rule":"For supplemental jurisdiction, the claim must be part of the same case or controversy (or common nucleus of operative facts) as the original claim","sub":"Subject-Matter Jurisdiction – Supplemental Jurisdiction","family":"Supplemental Jurisdiction","unit":18},{"n":122,"summary":"Plaintiff (State A) sues Defendant (State B) in federal court for $200,000 (diversity). Defendant admits breach but files a third-party claim against his own attorney (also State B) for indemnity. Attorney moves to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction.","trigger":"Defendant brings a related claim against another party; question is whether supplemental jurisdiction can cover it without destroying diversity.","rule":"Supplemental jurisdiction claims by a defendant against other parties will not destroy diversity. Supplemental jurisdiction because the anchor claim is from the same case or controversy or CNOP as the plaintiffs claim and wont destroy diversity","sub":"Subject-Matter Jurisdiction – Supplemental jurisdiction (defendant/third-party claims)","family":"Supplemental Jurisdiction","unit":17},{"n":123,"summary":"Plaintiff identified neighbor’s plate despite concussion; defendant offered alibi affidavit; moved for SJ","trigger":"Summary judgment where credibility is disputed","rule":"Summary judgment is granted when there is no genuine issue of material fact. The judge cannot weigh the evidence when deciding a summary judgment motion.","sub":"Summary Judgment","family":"Summary Judgment","unit":31},{"n":124,"summary":"Woman keeps a journal describing toxin exposures and the network’s alleged negligence but dies before suing. Husband brings a survival action. On summary judgment, husband relies only on complaint allegations and the journal contents, which are inadmissible hearsay, though they would establish the elements.","trigger":"Summary judgment evidence must be admissible (or capable of being presented in admissible form).","rule":"A party opposing summary judgment cannot rely solely on inadmissible evidence; the evidence must be admissible or reducible to admissible form at trial.","sub":"Summary Judgment – Admissible evidence","family":"Summary Judgment","unit":32},{"n":125,"summary":"A patient filed a federal medical-malpractice action against a hospital, alleging staff failed to diagnose cancer on an x-ray; six months later another doctor diagnosed the cancer based on the same x-ray, and in the interim the cancer spread. Fact and expert discovery were completed. The hospital moved for summary judgment, submitting a memorandum with claimed undisputed facts and supporting exhibits, including a report from the hospital’s radiologist stating the x-ray showed no signs of cancer. The question asks the best way for the patient to show a genuine dispute of material fact.","trigger":"Summary judgment response: must present admissible evidence creating a genuine dispute; affidavits/declarations based on personal knowledge (not hearsay) are typical.","rule":"To defeat summary judgment, the nonmovant must produce admissible evidence creating a genuine dispute of material fact (e.g., an affidavit/declaration from a qualified expert contradicting the movant’s evidence).","sub":"Summary Judgment – Genuine Dispute & Supporting Evidence (Affidavits)","family":"Summary Judgment","unit":32},{"n":126,"summary":"Plaintiff claims defendant’s two dogs (and a third neighborhood dog) attacked him but he can’t identify which dog bit him; defendant submits affidavit saying his dogs are nonviolent and only the other dog bit; plaintiff submits wife’s affidavit saying she saw defendant’s dog bite plaintiff; defendant moves for summary judgment.","trigger":"Conflicting evidence creates a genuine dispute of material fact; on SJ the judge does not weigh credibility.","rule":"If a party has any evidence on the issues required, summary judgment will not be granted. The judge does not weigh the evidence.","sub":"Summary Judgment – Genuine issue of material fact; no weighing of evidence/credibility","family":"Summary Judgment","unit":31},{"n":127,"summary":"Jogger is mistakenly identified as a burglar, arrested, handcuffed, and taken to the police station; charges are later dismissed as mistaken identity. Jogger sues the officer in federal court with (1) a state‑law false‑arrest claim and (2) a §1983 claim for the stop and excessive force. After discovery and nearly three years, the court grants summary judgment on qualified immunity and dismisses the §1983 claim with prejudice; the officer then moves to dismiss the remaining false‑arrest claim for lack of subject‑matter jurisdiction, and the jogger fears being time‑barred if forced to refile in state court.","trigger":"Federal claims dismissed after extensive proceedings; whether court keeps/dismisses supplemental state claim and the tolling protection for refiling.","rule":"A federal court has supplemental-jurisdiction discretion to dismiss a state claim after federal claims drop out, and if dismissed, § 1367(d) tolls limitations (at least 30 days to refile in state court).","sub":"Supplemental Jurisdiction – §1367; tolling","family":"Supplemental Jurisdiction","unit":18},{"n":128,"summary":"Plaintiff sues fan and beer company; company files crossclaim for indemnity.","trigger":"Crossclaim arising from same case or controversy.","rule":"Supplemental jurisdiction applies if the supplemental claim is from the case or controversy as the original claim, it does not destroy diversity (only plaintiff can destroy diversity for purpose of supplemental jurisdiction), and the judge allows it in his discretion.","sub":"Supplemental Jurisdiction – Crossclaims","family":"Supplemental Jurisdiction","unit":17},{"n":129,"summary":"A borrower sued a mortgage company in federal court asserting a federal Truth in Lending Act claim and a related state-law breach of contract claim based on allegedly misstated interest terms. The federal claim was dismissed, leaving only the state claim in federal court.","trigger":"Court has supplemental jurisdiction at filing, but after the anchor federal claim is dismissed, the judge must decide whether to keep or dismiss the remaining state claim (discretion).","rule":"Supplemental jurisdiction must be met at the time the complaint is filed. If the original claim is dismissed, it is discretionary with the judge whether to dismiss the supplemental claim.","sub":"Supplemental Jurisdiction – Discretion","family":"Supplemental Jurisdiction","unit":18},{"n":130,"summary":"Arizona plaintiff buys a tanning bed in the Central District of California from a vendor; manufacturer resides in the Eastern District of California. Plaintiff’s attorney files a diversity product-liability suit against both in the Northern District of California, where neither defendant has contacts. Court dismisses for improper venue after briefing.","trigger":"Improper venue; whether dismissal (rather than transfer) is permitted when the chosen district is improper and defendants lack ties.","rule":"When venue is improper, a federal court may dismiss (or transfer in the interest of justice) under §1406; dismissal is permissible where the action was filed in an improper district. Under § 1391(b)(1), if all defendants reside in the same state, venue is proper only in a district where any defendant resides.And for venue, a corporate/entity defendant “resides” in a district only if it is subject to personal jurisdiction in that district (treated like the district were its own state). Both defendants are in California, but they do not “reside” in the Northern District because the facts say neither had any contacts/business there (so no PJ there).\n\nThe relevant events happened in the Central (sale/vendor) and Eastern (manufacturer) districts, not Northern.\n\nTherefore, “same state” does not let plaintiff sue in any district in that state; it only lets plaintiff sue in a district where at least one defendant is actually subject to PJ (i.e., “resides”) or where substantial events occurred.","sub":"Venue – Improper venue; dismissal/transfer","family":"Venue","unit":41},{"n":131,"summary":"Multi-state fraud conspiracy; determining proper venue.","trigger":"Venue based on substantial part of events.","rule":"Venue is proper in a district where a substantial part of the events giving rise to the lawsuit occurred.","sub":"Venue – Substantial Events","family":"Venue","unit":41},{"n":132,"summary":"Defendant objects to venue and seeks transfer to his home forum for convenience.","trigger":"§1404(a) transfer → court has discretion based on convenience and interests of justice; must be a proper transferee forum.","rule":"A case can be transferred to a state where the lawsuit could have been brought originally rather than being dismissed if venue is improper in the original court.","sub":"Venue – Transfer","family":"Venue","unit":42},{"n":133,"summary":"Plaintiff sued two robbers in State B federal court; defendants moved to transfer to State A where events, parties, and witnesses are","trigger":"Transfer to more appropriate forum and resulting choice-of-law consequence","rule":"With a transfer for improper forum, the law of the transferee state will apply.","sub":"Venue - Transfer & Choice of Law","family":"Venue","unit":42},{"n":134,"summary":"Pregnant employee is harassed by two partner-brothers at an office in State B; she sues them individually in federal court in State B under a federal harassment statute; both defendants move to transfer to State A and plaintiff agrees to reduce travel.","trigger":"Motion to transfer venue with all parties consenting to transfer to another district.","rule":"Under 28 USC 1404, a district court may transfer any civil action to any other district where it might have been brought or to any district to which all parties have consented.","sub":"Venue – Transfer of venue / consent-based transfer","family":"Venue","unit":42},{"n":135,"summary":"Restaurant owner (State A) sues a freelance restaurant reviewer (citizen of State B) for libel in federal district court in State C (plaintiff’s residence) based on diversity. Defendant answers with a general denial, then later realizes State C is an improper venue and seeks to raise a venue objection after answering.","trigger":"Improper venue objection raised after answering; Rule 12 waiver issue.","rule":"Improper venue is waived if not raised in the first Rule 12 motion or the answer; raising it later is too late.","sub":"Venue – Waiver","family":"Venue","unit":43},{"n":136,"summary":"Fraud complaint dismissed without prejudice; refiled; plaintiff voluntarily dismissed; refiled again","trigger":"Two voluntary dismissals of the same claim","rule":"If a plaintiff voluntarily dismisses their complaint twice, the second time will be considered a final judgment for the purposes of res judicata (claim preclusion).","sub":"Voluntary Dismissal - Two dismissal rule","family":"Dismissals","unit":44},{"n":137,"summary":"Case dismissed without prejudice, then voluntarily dismissed; third filing attempted.","trigger":"Two-dismissal rule under Rule 41. If a case is dismissed involuntarily (motion to dismiss for failure to state a clai, lack of Pj, etc) if considered by the court as without prejudice the plaintiff can file again and no two-dismissal rule","rule":"A second voluntary dismissal will be considered dismissed with prejudice for the purposes of res judicata. Here, the P dismissed 1st time - involuntary dismissal without prejudice and second time voluntary, the 2 dismissal rule doesn't apply","sub":"Voluntary Dismissal – Two Dismissal Rule","family":"Dismissals","unit":44},{"n":138,"summary":"After defendant files a summary judgment motion, plaintiff wants to voluntarily dismiss without prejudice to refile later.","trigger":"Rule 41(a) voluntary dismissal → cannot dismiss by notice after an answer or summary judgment motion; need court order/stipulation.","rule":"A voluntary dismissal is allowed by just giving notice if the other party has not responded to the pleading or filed a motion for summary judgment.","sub":"Voluntary Dismissal (Rule 41)","family":"Dismissals","unit":44}]},{"slug":"property","name":"Real Property","bigPicture":"WHERE YOU START. Property is dominio: one right, full and perpetual (C.C. art. 545), acquired by consent and transferred by contract, with the Registro supplying publicity and, in practice, security of title. Co-ownership is comunidad — ideal shares that pass to the co-owner's heirs. And then a whole familiar terrain, most of it Roman: usucapión with unión de posesiones (art. 781), servidumbres, hipotecas ranked by grado, usufructo with its allocation of charges, arrendamiento with tácita reconducción (art. 1.600).\n\nWHAT THE U.S. SYSTEM IS. On top of that shared ground sit two structures your system never built. FIRST, ownership is split across TIME. There are present estates and future interests — remainders, executory interests, defeasible fees that simply END by themselves when a condition occurs, with nothing for anyone to do — and a Rule Against Perpetuities that voids otherwise lawful private conditions on the ground that they reach too far into the future. SECOND, recording is not a title system. It creates no title and radiates no public faith; it only referees PRIORITY contests among competing claimants, it charges every buyer with INQUIRY notice from the visible state of the land, and it shelters a transferee whose own good faith is legally irrelevant because he inherits his seller's protected position.\n\nWHAT DIVERGES. Three trained habits generate the wrong answer. (1) Reading “whoever registered first owns it” — recording resolves priority between claimants, never title, and an unrecorded interest visible on the ground can defeat a recorded one. (2) Reading comunidad into a joint tenancy: survivorship means the deceased co-owner's share goes to the survivor and NOT to his heirs, and the question usually turns on whether some act severed it. (3) Trusting vocabulary that translates while the gates invert — protocolización is not delivery, saneamiento is not marketable title and does not carry the present/future covenant split, and the enclosed parcel's automatic legal right of way (art. 660) simply does not exist: an easement by necessity must be earned on its own terms.\n\nWHAT IS THE SAME. This is the module with the most convergence — fourteen of forty units. Adverse possession IS usucapión, tacking included; mortgage priority by registration order, and the purge of junior liens on foreclosure; the prohibition of the pacto comisorio behind the anti-clogging rules; the usufructuary's charges mapping onto life-estate waste; tácita reconducción onto holdover tenancies; contribution among co-owners; neighbour liability for excavation; and the cure of a sale of another's thing onto estoppel by deed. Where the Roman roots are shared, trust your training completely — and suspend it entirely for estates-in-time and for the recording acts.","families":[{"name":"Mortgages","count":13},{"name":"Future Interests","count":11},{"name":"Recording Acts","count":11},{"name":"Landlord–Tenant","count":10},{"name":"Co-Ownership","count":9},{"name":"Deeds","count":9},{"name":"Contracts for Sale","count":7},{"name":"Easements","count":7},{"name":"Vendor–Purchaser","count":7},{"name":"Adverse Possession","count":6},{"name":"Covenants","count":5},{"name":"Life Estates","count":3},{"name":"Takings","count":3},{"name":"Wills Crossover","count":3},{"name":"Fair Housing","count":2},{"name":"Vendor Liability","count":2},{"name":"Estates","count":1},{"name":"Fixtures","count":1},{"name":"Incidents of Ownership","count":1},{"name":"Zoning","count":1}],"units":[{"n":1,"summary":"Two buyers (or a buyer and a donee, or a buyer and a lien creditor) claim the same land through successive transfers from the same grantor; a recording statute governs who prevails.","trigger":"Successive conveyances of the same interest + identify the statute type (race / notice / race-notice) + test each claimant against its elements.","rule":"Absent a recording statute, common law first-in-time controls. Under a NOTICE statute, a subsequent bona fide purchaser (BFP) — one who takes for value and without actual, record, or inquiry notice — prevails even if he never records. Under a RACE-NOTICE statute, the subsequent BFP prevails only if he also records first. Donees, heirs, and devisees are not purchasers for value and take subject to prior interests regardless of notice.","sub":"Recording Acts – Statute Types and the BFP (Master)","sensitivity":"Method is sequential: (1) start from first-in-time; (2) classify the statute from its language ('in good faith' = notice; 'first recorded' + 'in good faith' = race-notice); (3) run the subsequent taker through the two gates — VALUE and NO NOTICE — then, if race-notice, the recording race. The same facts flip outcomes across statute types: a BFP who records one day late wins under notice but loses under race-notice.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “whoever registered first owns the land — the registry gives public faith” → U.S. recording acts do not create title; they resolve priority contests and protect only purchasers for value without notice, each statute differently. The civilian fe pública registral schema (registration as near-constitutive, Art. 1,924 C.C. opposability) produces two systematic errors: assuming registration always wins (false under notice statutes) and assuming non-registration always loses (false for donees' grantors and under first-in-time defaults).","authority":"Recording acts: statutory taxonomy (race / notice / race-notice); BFP = purchaser for value without notice; common-law first-in-time as default."},{"n":2,"summary":"A buyer's protection turns on what he knew or should have known: a visible driveway, a deed referenced in the chain, an unrecorded interest mentioned in a recorded document, or possession by a third party.","trigger":"BFP status challenged + classify the notice: actual, record (chain of title), or inquiry.","rule":"Notice takes three forms: ACTUAL (subjective knowledge), RECORD (properly recorded documents within the chain of title — constructive notice regardless of an actual search), and INQUIRY (facts that would lead a reasonable person to investigate — visible use or occupation, references in recorded instruments to unrecorded ones). Any of the three defeats BFP status at the moment of the transaction.","sub":"Recording Acts – Notice Taxonomy (Actual / Record / Inquiry)","sensitivity":"Inquiry notice is the exam's favorite trap: the buyer who never visits the land is still charged with what a visit would have revealed (a driveway used daily, a fence, a tenant in possession). Record notice reaches only the CHAIN of title — a 'wild deed' recorded outside the chain gives no notice. Notice is measured at payment/closing, not later.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “if it is not in the registry, the buyer cannot be blamed” → U.S. law charges the buyer with inquiry notice from the physical state of the land and from references inside recorded documents. The civilian registry-centered schema (what the certificación registral shows is what binds) misses the duty to inspect the property itself — the doctrine that decides most recording questions on the exam.","authority":"Notice: actual, record (chain of title; wild deeds excluded), inquiry (visible use, possession, references in recorded instruments)."},{"n":3,"summary":"A buyer who cannot himself qualify as a bona fide purchaser takes from someone who did qualify; or the original wrongdoer tries to repurchase through the chain.","trigger":"Transfer FROM a protected BFP + transferee's own notice or non-purchaser status + (exception) transferee was the original wrongdoer.","rule":"Under the shelter rule, a transferee from a bona fide purchaser 'shelters' in the BFP's protection and prevails against prior interests even if the transferee had notice or took as a donee; the rule does not protect a transferee who was the ORIGINAL wrongdoer or fraudulent grantor attempting to launder title through a BFP.","sub":"Recording Acts – Shelter Rule and Its Limit","sensitivity":"The protection travels with the TITLE, not the person: once a BFP cuts off the prior claim, every downstream taker inherits that result — knowledge becomes irrelevant one link after the BFP. The single exception reverses the outcome when the taker is the person whose misconduct created the contest.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “this buyer knew of the earlier claim, so he cannot be in good faith” → his own good faith is irrelevant; he inherits the BFP's victory. Civil-law good faith is evaluated per acquirer, transaction by transaction; the idea that BFP status 'cleanses' the title itself and runs downstream has no codal analogue, so the trained instinct re-litigates good faith at every link.","authority":"Shelter rule and original-wrongdoer exception."},{"n":4,"summary":"A deed in the chain was forged, or was obtained from the true owner by fraud; a later buyer without notice pays value and records, then the true owner sues.","trigger":"Defect at the deed's origin + classify it: forgery/non-delivery (void) vs. fraud in the inducement (voidable) + subsequent BFP.","rule":"A FORGED deed (or one never delivered) is VOID and conveys nothing — not even to a subsequent bona fide purchaser who records; recording cannot breathe life into a void instrument. A deed procured by FRAUD in the inducement is merely VOIDABLE: valid until rescinded, so a subsequent BFP who takes before rescission is protected and defeats the defrauded owner.","sub":"Deeds – Void vs. Voidable; Limits of Recording Protection","sensitivity":"The void/voidable classification is the single switch: identical downstream chains (BFP, value, recording) produce opposite winners depending only on whether the original defect was forgery (owner always wins) or induced consent (BFP wins). Delivery defects align with forgery; fraud, duress, and minority align with voidability.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — “registration does not cure an absolutely void title” — is CORRECT here: Venezuelan registration likewise fails to validate a forged instrument (nulidad absoluta), and voidable acts (nulidad relativa) can be consolidated in favor of third parties. The residual friction is terminological: mapping forgery→void→owner wins and inducement-fraud→voidable→BFP wins onto the nulidad absoluta/relativa pair, and remembering that U.S. recording acts — unlike fe pública systems — never protect against void deeds.","authority":"Void vs. voidable deeds: forgery/non-delivery void even against BFPs; fraud in the inducement voidable, BFP protected."},{"n":5,"summary":"A grant creates interests that take effect in the future ('to A for life, then to A's children who reach 21'; 'to my son, but if he has no children, to my daughter'); the parties dispute what each person owns today.","trigger":"Life estate or defeasible fee + follow-on interest + classify: contingent remainder vs. vested remainder (indefeasibly; subject to open; subject to divestment) vs. executory interest.","rule":"A remainder is CONTINGENT if it belongs to an unascertained person or is subject to a condition precedent; it is VESTED if held by an ascertained living person with no condition precedent — even if enjoyment is postponed. A vested remainder followed by divesting language ('but if…') is vested subject to divestment, with an executory interest in the divesting taker. Vested remainders are freely transferable, devisable, and descendible; a child en ventre sa mère counts as ascertained.","sub":"Future Interests – Remainder Classification (Master)","sensitivity":"Classification is driven by PUNCTUATION and word order (the 'comma rule'): a condition woven into the gift ('to B if B reaches 21') creates a contingent remainder; a condition following a completed gift ('to B, but if B dies without children, to C') creates a vested remainder subject to divestment plus an executory interest. Classification, in turn, decides transferability, RAP exposure (Unit 7), and who takes on each contingency.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “either he owns it or he doesn't — a condition just makes the sale conditional” → U.S. law splits ownership across TIME into presently-owned future estates, each classified, valued, and separately transferable. The unitary civil-law dominio (with only usufruct/nuda propiedad and condiciones as obligation-level devices) has no grammar for four kinds of remainder; the civilian collapses them into 'condición suspensiva', which answers almost no exam variant correctly.","authority":"Remainders: Restatement of Property classification; vested subject to divestment vs. contingent ('comma rule'); en ventre sa mère."},{"n":6,"summary":"A grant limits how long ownership lasts ('so long as used as a farm'; 'to the church, but if liquor is served, grantor may re-enter') or springs to a new taker on an event; land use later changes.","trigger":"Durational or conditional language in the granting clause + classify the defeasible fee + identify who holds the follow-on interest and whether termination is automatic.","rule":"So long as / while / during' creates a FEE SIMPLE DETERMINABLE: it ends AUTOMATICALLY on the event, leaving a possibility of reverter in the grantor (and the adverse-possession clock starts at that moment against the holder). 'But if / provided that… right to re-enter' creates a fee simple subject to CONDITION SUBSEQUENT: it continues until the grantor exercises the right of entry. A comparable interest in a THIRD PARTY is an executory interest, which operates automatically and survives the grantor's death.","sub":"Future Interests – Defeasible Fees; Automatic vs. Elective Termination","sensitivity":"Two switches: (1) the wording (durational vs. conditional) decides automatic vs. elective termination — which in turn starts or does not start limitations periods and adverse-possession clocks; (2) the identity of the taker (grantor vs. third party) decides reverter/right-of-entry vs. executory interest — and only the third-party interests face the Rule Against Perpetuities (Unit 7).","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “breach of the condition lets the grantor sue to resolve the transfer” → with a determinable fee NOTHING need be done: ownership ends by itself and possession becomes adverse from that instant. The civilian schema (condición resolutoria enforced through an action, prescription running from demand) misses both the automatic-forfeiture mechanism and the exam's favorite consequence — the silently running adverse-possession clock.","authority":"Defeasible fees: fee simple determinable (automatic; possibility of reverter; adverse-possession clock runs from the event) vs. fee simple subject to condition subsequent (right of entry, elective) vs. executory limitation (third party, automatic; survives the grantor)."},{"n":7,"summary":"A grant creates an interest that may vest far in the future ('so long as used as a farm, then to my sister'); someone challenges it under the Rule Against Perpetuities.","trigger":"Contingent remainder, executory interest, class gift, or option/right of first refusal + test: must vest or fail within a life in being plus 21 years + if violated, strike and re-classify.","rule":"The Rule Against Perpetuities voids an interest that might vest more than 21 years after every life in being at creation. It applies to contingent remainders, executory interests, class gifts, and commercial options/rights of first refusal — not to reversions, possibilities of reverter, rights of entry, or fully vested remainders. The offending interest is STRICKEN and the instrument re-read: 'to A so long as used as a farm, then to B' loses only B's executory interest, leaving A with a fee simple determinable and the grantor with a possibility of reverter.","sub":"Future Interests – Rule Against Perpetuities: Scope and Surgery","sensitivity":"Two-step discipline: (1) SCOPE — grantor-retained interests are immune, so the same forfeiture survives if kept by the grantor but dies if given to a third party; (2) SURGERY — the exam's real question is what REMAINS after the strike, which depends on whether the durational language stays with the granting clause. A vesting event tied to a life in being (e.g., 'if my son has a child') is always safe.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the condition is valid — parties may agree on any lawful condition” → an entire constitutional-style time limit on private dispositions, with no civil-law analogue (prohibiciones de enajenar are policed differently and usufructs are life-capped by nature). The civilian neither spots the RAP issue nor performs the surgery; both steps must be installed from zero — the purest institutional void in the subject.","authority":"Rule Against Perpetuities: lives in being + 21 years; scope (contingent remainders, executory interests, class gifts, options) and immunity of grantor-retained interests; strike-and-reclassify consequence."},{"n":8,"summary":"Property is co-owned — spouses, siblings, or an unmarried couple 'with right of survivorship' — and one co-owner secretly conveys, mortgages, or dies; survivors and buyers dispute who owns what.","trigger":"Identify the cotenancy (joint tenancy / tenancy by the entirety / tenancy in common) + apply its severance and survivorship mechanics to the unilateral act.","rule":"A JOINT TENANCY (four unities + survivorship) is severed by any joint tenant's inter vivos conveyance — even secret — converting the transferred share into a TENANCY IN COMMON; in lien-theory states a mortgage by one (or both) joint tenants does NOT sever. A TENANCY BY THE ENTIRETY (married couples only) is immune to unilateral acts: neither spouse alone can convey or encumber it, and creditors of one spouse cannot reach it; only death, divorce, or joint action ends it. Unmarried grantees cannot hold by the entirety — survivorship language yields a joint tenancy instead.","sub":"Co-Ownership – JT vs. TBE vs. TIC: Severance and Survivorship (Master)","sensitivity":"Three gates in sequence: (1) marital status at acquisition (unmarried → never TBE); (2) form of the unilateral act (deed severs a JT; mortgage severs only in title-theory states; nothing unilateral touches a TBE); (3) timing (severance before death defeats survivorship — the secret-deed cases). The same conveyance is a nullity against a TBE and fully effective against a JT.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “co-ownership is comunidad — each owns an ideal share that passes to heirs” → that describes only the tenancy in common. Survivorship that overrides wills and heirs, severance by secret unilateral deed, and a marital estate that blocks each spouse's creditors have no analogue in the comunidad/comunidad conyugal schema; the civilian defaults every case to inheritance-law reasoning and misses that the classification question decides everything before succession law ever applies.","authority":"Cotenancies: four unities; severance by unilateral conveyance; lien-theory vs. title-theory mortgages; tenancy by the entirety immune to unilateral acts and individual creditors; unmarried grantees take as joint tenants."},{"n":9,"summary":"One cotenant pays the mortgage or taxes alone, occupies the whole property, buys an outstanding lien or tax title for himself, or tries to claim the property outright against the others.","trigger":"Cotenancy in place + one cotenant's payment, sole possession, self-dealing acquisition, or claim of exclusive ownership.","rule":"A cotenant who pays more than his share of carrying charges (mortgage, taxes) may compel CONTRIBUTION from the others. Sole possession alone is neither ouster nor rent liability (absent exclusion or agreement), and adverse possession between cotenants requires OUSTER — clear repudiation communicated to the others. A cotenant who acquires an outstanding title or lien holds it for all (fiduciary duty), though the duty runs only while the cotenancy exists.","sub":"Co-Ownership – Contribution, Ouster, Fiduciary Duties","sensitivity":"Three distinct levers the exam mixes at will: money (contribution — available), possession (exclusive occupation — lawful until ouster), and loyalty (self-dealing — barred during the cotenancy, permitted after it ends). The ouster requirement makes cotenant adverse-possession claims fail on 'hostility' even after decades of sole occupation.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian comunidad schema gets most of this RIGHT — the comunero who pays may recover (Arts. 760 ff. C.C.), co-possession does not ripen into usucapión without interversión del título (≈ ouster), and loyalty duties among co-owners are familiar. The residual friction is the timing limit on the fiduciary duty (it ends with the cotenancy) and the rent question (sole use without exclusion is free) — refinements, not reorganization.","authority":"Cotenant rights: contribution for carrying charges; ouster requirement; fiduciary duty limited to the life of the cotenancy."},{"n":10,"summary":"A non-owner possesses land for the statutory period — sometimes by mistake, sometimes with others, sometimes under a defective deed — and claims title; or possesses government land, or adds a predecessor's years.","trigger":"Statutory period + test the elements (open/notorious, continuous, exclusive, actual, hostile) + special postures: co-possessors, color of title, tacking, government owner.","rule":"Adverse possession requires open and notorious, continuous, exclusive, actual, and hostile possession for the statutory period; mistaken belief of ownership still satisfies hostility (majority). Two or more joint possessors acquire as TENANTS IN COMMON. Color of title extends possession CONSTRUCTIVELY to the whole parcel described — but only within land actually entered and against that owner. Successive possessors may TACK their periods if in privity. No period runs against GOVERNMENT land.","sub":"Adverse Possession – Elements and Special Postures (Master)","sensitivity":"The elements are rarely tested alone; the exam tests the postures: exclusivity shared → co-ownership results (not defeat); color of title → constructive possession of the described tract, but never across separately owned parcels without entry; tacking → requires privity (deed, descent), not sequential trespass; defeasible fees → the clock starts when the fee determinable ends (Unit 6).","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the usucapión schema is a strong base — elements, statutory periods, unión de posesiones (Art. 781 C.C. ≈ tacking), and imprescriptibility of public-domain land all converge. Residual frictions: hostility is OBJECTIVE (mistake qualifies — no buena/mala fe split changing the period), color of title expands possession constructively rather than merely shortening the period, and shared possession produces a cotenancy instead of defeating exclusivity. The civilian should trust the framework but re-tune these three dials.","authority":"Adverse possession: elements; objective hostility (mistake qualifies, majority); joint possessors take as tenants in common; color of title and constructive possession; tacking with privity; no adverse possession against government land."},{"n":11,"summary":"A parcel is landlocked after a common owner splits it (or a neighbor relies on an informal permission to build an access); the user claims a right of way without any written grant.","trigger":"Access claimed without an express grant + one of the non-express creation routes: implication from prior use, necessity, or estoppel.","rule":"Easements are created by express grant/reservation (Statute of Frauds applies), by implication from prior existing use (common ownership + continuous, apparent, reasonably necessary use at severance), by necessity (common ownership + strict necessity arising AT severance), or by estoppel (license + reasonably foreseeable detrimental reliance makes the license irrevocable).","sub":"Easements – Creation (Express, Implied, Necessity, Estoppel)","sensitivity":"Every non-express route has a structural gate: implication needs a PRE-severance use; necessity needs common ownership and strict necessity AT severance (later landlocking does not qualify); estoppel needs investment-backed reliance, not mere permission. No route exists for a parcel that was simply always landlocked between strangers.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “a landlocked parcel always has a legal right of way against the neighbor” → not in U.S. law. Under C.C. art. 660 the enclosed owner may DEMAND passage (with indemnity) as a legal servitude against any neighbor; the U.S. easement by necessity exists only against the parcel's former common owner and dies if the necessity ends. The civilian instinct grants access where U.S. law denies it.","authority":"Easement creation: express (SOF); implied from prior use (quasi-easement); necessity (common ownership + strict necessity at severance); estoppel (irrevocable license). Contrast C.C. art. 660 (legal right of way for enclosed land)."},{"n":12,"summary":"A neighbor uses a path (or maintains an encroachment) over another's land openly and without permission for the statutory period and claims a permanent right to continue.","trigger":"Long, open, continuous, non-permissive USE (not possession) of another's land for the prescriptive period.","rule":"A prescriptive easement requires open and notorious, adverse (non-permissive), and continuous use for the statutory period; unlike adverse possession, EXCLUSIVITY is not required — the owner's simultaneous use of the same path does not defeat the claim. Permission at any point restarts the analysis.","sub":"Easements – Prescription","sensitivity":"The use/possession divide drives everything: prescription earns only the USE proved (a path, a drain), never title; exclusivity drops out of the element list; and permission is the owner's complete antidote. Cf. the adverse-possession master (Unit 10).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “discontinuous servitudes like a right of way can never be acquired by prescription” → in Venezuela only continuous AND apparent servitudes are acquirable by usucapión (C.C. arts. 720 ff.), so the trained instinct rejects precisely the claim U.S. law accepts. The friction runs opposite to most units: the civilian is too RESTRICTIVE here.","authority":"Prescriptive easements: open, adverse, continuous for the statutory period; exclusivity NOT required. Contrast C.C. arts. 720 ff. (only continuous and apparent servitudes acquirable by prescription)."},{"n":13,"summary":"After an easement exists, the parties dispute who may use it, how much use is allowed, who maintains it, and what happens when the servient land is sold or the two parcels come into common hands.","trigger":"Existing easement + transfer of either parcel, expanded use, maintenance dispute, or unity of ownership.","rule":"An appurtenant easement passes automatically with the dominant estate (no separate mention needed) and binds the servient buyer who has notice (actual, record, or inquiry from visible use); overuse is remedied by injunction, not termination; the dominant owner bears maintenance duties absent contrary agreement; and merger of both estates in one owner extinguishes the easement — with no automatic revival on later resale.","sub":"Easements – Transfer, Scope, Maintenance, Merger","sensitivity":"Four switches on one doctrine: automatic transfer (appurtenant status), the notice gate on the burdened side (recording interplay, Unit 2), the overuse remedy ceiling (injunction ≠ forfeiture), and merger's one-way extinguishment. Each is a separately testable outcome flip.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian recognizes most of this — servidumbres are inseparable from the dominant estate (C.C. arts. 709 ff.), extinguished by confusión, and maintained by the dominant owner. The instinct is broadly RIGHT; the residual friction is the notice gate on the burdened buyer (a recording-act overlay, not a property-law one) and the no-revival rule after merger, both of which the registry-based schema answers differently.","authority":"Transfer/scope/merger: appurtenant easements pass automatically; burdened buyer bound with notice; overuse remedied by injunction; dominant owner maintains; merger extinguishes without revival."},{"n":14,"summary":"Two neighbors agree in writing that one will pay for or refrain from something on his land (maintenance fees, building restrictions); a successor owner refuses to honor it and the original promisee (or a neighbor) sues for damages.","trigger":"Written promise respecting land + succession on one or both sides + remedy sought is MONEY damages.","rule":"For a real covenant's BURDEN to run at law (damages), the elements are: writing, intent to bind successors, touch and concern, horizontal privity (mutual interest at creation — e.g., grantor–grantee), vertical privity, and notice; the BENEFIT runs with relaxed requirements (no horizontal privity or notice needed). A promise between neighbors with no shared conveyance typically fails horizontal privity — damages are unavailable even though equity may still enforce.","sub":"Covenants – Real Covenants at Law (Damages)","sensitivity":"Remedy selection selects the doctrine: the SAME promise is analyzed as a real covenant when damages are sought (horizontal privity required) and as an equitable servitude when an injunction is sought (no privity — Unit 15). Burden-side and benefit-side elements are asymmetrical; most exam kills happen at horizontal privity.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “a valid written obligation binds successors who knew of it” → the U.S. system interposes a six-element machinery (with horizontal privity as the classic killer) that has no codal counterpart — obligations bind parties, not land, and obligaciones propter rem form a tiny closed class. The civilian either enforces promises U.S. law voids at law, or misses that equity (Unit 15) still offers a route.","authority":"Real covenants at law: writing, intent, touch & concern, horizontal + vertical privity, notice (burden side); benefit side relaxed."},{"n":15,"summary":"A landowner seeks an INJUNCTION to enforce a recorded (or known) restriction against a successor of the burdened parcel — including a successor who took by gift.","trigger":"Restriction on land + successor on the burdened side + remedy sought is an injunction.","rule":"An equitable servitude requires only writing (or a common scheme, Unit 16), intent, touch and concern, and NOTICE to the burdened successor — no privity of any kind. A recorded restriction gives constructive notice; and a DONEE is bound regardless of notice, because recording acts protect only purchasers for value.","sub":"Covenants – Equitable Servitudes (Injunctions)","sensitivity":"Two independent gates flip outcomes: (1) the notice gate applies only to those who can claim recording-act protection — donees and heirs take subject to the restriction even in total ignorance; (2) privity drops out entirely once the remedy is equitable. Cross-check Unit 14: same facts, opposite result depending on the remedy requested.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “no one can be bound by a private agreement he never signed and never knew of” → a donee is bound by an equitable servitude without signature AND without notice. The equity/law remedial split — one promise, two doctrines, two element lists — has no analogue in a unitary civil action; the civilian applies one test to both remedies and misses half the outcomes.","authority":"Equitable servitudes: writing/scheme, intent, touch & concern, notice; no privity. Donees and heirs are bound regardless of notice (recording acts protect only purchasers for value)."},{"n":16,"summary":"A developer sells lots under a general plan of residential restrictions; some deeds omit the restriction, but the neighborhood's uniform character is obvious; a lot owner builds commercially and neighbors sue.","trigger":"Common development scheme + restriction omitted from some deeds + visible uniformity.","rule":"Under the common-scheme (implied reciprocal servitude) doctrine, a uniform development plan implies the restriction into lots whose deeds omit it, and the buyer's inquiry notice from the neighborhood's visible character satisfies the notice requirement; any lot owner within the scheme may enforce.","sub":"Covenants – Common Scheme / Implied Reciprocal Servitudes","sensitivity":"Two implications operate at once: the restriction itself is implied from the scheme (no writing as to the omitted lot), and notice is implied from what the buyer can SEE. The scheme must predate the sale of the burdened lot; lots sold before the scheme arose escape it.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “if it is not in the title chain or the registry, it cannot burden the lot” → the restriction binds through implication + inquiry notice, with no recorded instrument against the lot at all. Registry-centric training (nothing burdens an immovable without registration — C.C. art. 1,920 ff.) makes this the least intuitive covenant doctrine of all for the civilian.","authority":"Common scheme / implied reciprocal servitudes: general plan implies omitted restrictions; inquiry notice from visible uniformity (Sanborn v. McLean)."},{"n":17,"summary":"A tenant hands the leasehold to someone else — labeled 'sublease' or 'assignment' — and the landlord wants rent or damages from the occupant, the original tenant, or both; the lease may contain an anti-transfer clause the landlord has ignored while cashing rent checks.","trigger":"Transfer of the leasehold + who is sued (occupant vs. original tenant) + anti-transfer clause and the landlord's conduct after learning of the transfer.","rule":"Substance controls over labels: a transfer of the ENTIRE remaining term is an assignment (landlord and assignee are in privity of estate — assignee liable for rent); retention of any part of the term makes it a sublease (no privity between landlord and sublessee — landlord's claims run against the original tenant). The original tenant always remains liable on privity of contract absent a release/novation. Anti-transfer clauses are strictly construed (a no-assignment clause does not bar subleasing), and the landlord waives the clause by knowingly accepting rent from the transferee.","sub":"Landlord–Tenant – Assignment vs. Sublease (Privity Matrix)","sensitivity":"Three switches: (1) full term vs. any retained residue — the label on the document is irrelevant; (2) the two privity tracks (estate vs. contract) determine WHO can be sued — the original tenant is never off the hook without novation; (3) the anti-transfer clause has its own two traps — strict construction and waiver by rent acceptance.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the new occupant pays the rent now — the old tenant is out” (or: “the landlord never consented, so the transfer is void”) → both wrong. The civilian cesión del contrato frame treats consent as constitutive and substitution as complete; the U.S. matrix keeps BOTH parties liable on different privity tracks and treats the clause as waivable conduct-by-conduct. The dual-track liability is the exam's favorite trap.","authority":"Assignment vs. sublease: substance over labels; privity of estate vs. privity of contract; original tenant liable absent novation; anti-transfer clauses strictly construed; waiver by accepting rent."},{"n":18,"summary":"A residential tenant faces uninhabitable conditions (no heat, leaks) under a lease that says the unit is taken 'as is'; a commercial tenant in the same building demands the same protections; after complaining to authorities, the residential tenant receives an eviction notice.","trigger":"Habitability defects + residential vs. commercial lease + lease language purporting to waive protections + eviction following a complaint.","rule":"The implied warranty of habitability applies to RESIDENTIAL leases and cannot be waived ('as is' clauses are void as against public policy); remedies include repair-and-deduct, rent abatement, or termination. Commercial tenants get no habitability warranty — caveat lessee, subject to the covenant of quiet enjoyment/constructive eviction. Retaliatory eviction against a tenant who reports violations is prohibited.","sub":"Landlord–Tenant – Habitability (Residential/Commercial Fork); Retaliation","sensitivity":"The residential/commercial line is outcome-determinative: identical defects yield a full remedial menu for the residential tenant and near-zero protection for the commercial one. The non-waivability rule defeats lease drafting; the retaliation bar adds a timing inference (eviction shortly after a complaint is presumptively retaliatory).","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian expects strong tenant protection and non-waivable habitability — broadly RIGHT for residential leases (Venezuelan housing law is intensely protective). The residual friction is the COMMERCIAL side: the civilian over-extends protection to business leases, where U.S. law preserves caveat lessee and freedom of contract.","authority":"Habitability: implied warranty (residential, non-waivable) vs. caveat lessee (commercial); constructive eviction; retaliatory eviction prohibited."},{"n":19,"summary":"A tenant stays past the lease end and the landlord accepts a rent check; later one side wants out and disputes what tenancy exists and how it ends; separately, a tenant abandons early and the landlord lets the unit sit empty.","trigger":"Holdover + acceptance of rent; OR abandonment + landlord's response; termination notice mechanics.","rule":"Accepting rent from a holdover creates a periodic tenancy measured by the rent payment interval, terminable on notice of one full period. At common law the landlord has NO duty to mitigate after abandonment and may recover accruing rent; the modern/majority residential trend requires reasonable efforts to re-let. A seller who remains after closing is a licensee under the sale contract, not a tenant.","sub":"Landlord–Tenant – Holdover, Periodic Tenancies, Mitigation","sensitivity":"Three levers: (1) the landlord's ELECTION (evict as trespasser vs. accept rent → periodic tenancy); (2) the notice period equals the rent interval — mis-timed notice fails entirely; (3) the mitigation split is jurisdictional — state the common-law default, flag the modern trend.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the tácita reconducción instinct (C.C. art. 1,600: staying on with the lessor's acquiescence renews the lease) maps almost perfectly onto the holdover-to-periodic rule — the civilian gets this RIGHT. The residual frictions are the landlord's election (the civilian misses the evict-as-trespasser option) and the no-mitigation common-law default, which contradicts the good-faith instinct.","authority":"Holdover and periodic tenancies: landlord's election; period = rent interval; notice of one full period; mitigation — common-law no-duty vs. modern trend. Cf. tácita reconducción, C.C. art. 1,600."},{"n":20,"summary":"Between contract and closing, the buyer discovers title problems — a restrictive covenant, an existing mortgage, a zoning violation — or announces early that he will not attend closing; each side claims the other breached.","trigger":"Executory land-sale contract + alleged title defect + timing of the objection relative to closing.","rule":"Every land-sale contract implies a covenant of MARKETABLE title — title free from reasonable doubt: existing encumbrances (covenants, easements, mortgages, options) and PRESENT zoning violations make title unmarketable; zoning restrictions as such, and the buyer's frustrated intended use, do not. The seller need deliver marketable title only AT CLOSING — he may cure with the sale proceeds, and the buyer cannot repudiate early; marketability is the BUYER's shield, not the seller's escape.","sub":"Vendor–Purchaser – Marketable Title (Master)","sensitivity":"Three gates: (1) WHAT counts — encumbrance vs. mere restriction vs. buyer's secret purpose; (2) WHEN it is measured — closing, not contract date (liens payable from proceeds never breach); (3) WHO may invoke it — only the buyer. Quitclaim deeds do not eliminate the implied covenant in the CONTRACT stage.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the seller must guarantee the thing sold — any defect lets the buyer walk” → the saneamiento schema (eviction and hidden defects, post-transfer remedies) mislocates the doctrine: marketable title is a PRE-closing implied contract term with its own catalogue and timing. The civilian objects too early, objects to the wrong defects (zoning as such), and misses that the seller may cure at the table with the buyer's own money.","authority":"Marketable title: implied in every land-sale contract; encumbrances and present zoning violations render unmarketable; measured at closing; curable from proceeds; buyer's shield only."},{"n":21,"summary":"Several mortgages and liens attach to the same land at different times; a lender forecloses and the parties dispute who gets paid and in what order.","trigger":"Multiple encumbrances on one parcel + foreclosure by one lienholder + priority dispute.","rule":"Priority follows first-in-time as modified by the recording act: a recorded mortgage prevails over later interests, and an unrecorded one is vulnerable to a subsequent bona fide purchaser or mortgagee for value under the applicable statute. Foreclosure sale proceeds are distributed by seniority: costs, then the foreclosing lien, then junior liens in order, with any surplus to the mortgagor (juniors first, owner last).","sub":"Mortgages – Priority; Distribution of Foreclosure Proceeds","sensitivity":"Two ranking systems interlock: temporal priority (first in time) and the recording act (Units 1–3) — a later lender who records first and lacks notice can leapfrog. On distribution, the surplus question is a favorite: junior lienholders are paid from surplus BEFORE the mortgagor receives anything.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian ranking instinct is broadly right — hipotecas rank by grado (registration order) and execution proceeds are distributed by rank. The residual friction is the recording-act overlay: in the U.S. an unrecorded senior can LOSE to a junior BFP, an outcome the fe pública registral schema does not naturally generate because unregistered mortgages simply do not exist as real rights in the civil system.","authority":"Mortgage priority and proceeds: first-in-time as modified by the recording acts; distribution by seniority with surplus to juniors before the mortgagor."},{"n":22,"summary":"A recorded mortgage secures a line of credit; the lender advances funds in stages, and an intervening lien attaches between advances.","trigger":"Future-advances (open-end) mortgage + intervening junior lien + later advance by the senior lender.","rule":"Advances made under an OBLIGATORY commitment take the priority of the original mortgage even if made after a junior lien attaches; OPTIONAL advances made WITH NOTICE of an intervening lien are junior to it. The original recorded mortgage gives constructive notice of the credit line's maximum.","sub":"Mortgages – Future Advances (Obligatory vs. Optional)","sensitivity":"The switch is the lender's discretion: contractual obligation to advance → full relation-back priority; discretion plus notice → loss of priority for that advance only. Each advance is ranked separately — one mortgage can hold two priority positions at once.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the mortgage was registered first, so everything it secures is senior” → only obligatory advances relate back. The hipoteca de máximo secures a ceiling with uniform rank; the U.S. advance-by-advance ranking, keyed to the lender's discretion and notice, has no codal counterpart and produces a split-priority mortgage the civilian schema cannot represent.","authority":"Future advances: obligatory advances relate back to the original mortgage's priority; optional advances made with notice of an intervening lien are junior to it."},{"n":23,"summary":"A senior (or intermediate) lender forecloses; junior lienholders, senior lenders, and later buyers dispute which interests survive the sale — including a junior lien the buyer never discovered and a repurchase by the former owner.","trigger":"Foreclosure by a lienholder + interests senior and junior to the foreclosing lien + post-sale repurchase by the mortgagor.","rule":"Foreclosure extinguishes the foreclosing lien and all JUNIOR interests (if their holders were joined/noticed), but the buyer takes SUBJECT TO senior liens. Extinguished juniors do not revive if the former owner later repurchases the land; junior lienholders are relegated to the surplus and to personal actions on the debt.","sub":"Mortgages – Foreclosure: Purge of Juniors; No Revival","sensitivity":"Direction matters: foreclosure wipes DOWN the priority ladder, never up. Two satellite traps: an unrecorded junior with actual notice of the sale is still purged, and the no-revival rule defeats the intuition that liens 'reattach' when the debtor reacquires title.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — execution extinguishes later-ranked charges and the buyer takes subject to prior ones — is essentially correct (remate y purga de gravámenes). The residual frictions are the joinder/notice condition for purging a junior and the no-revival rule on repurchase, both procedural overlays the codal purge does not test.","authority":"Foreclosure: purge of junior interests (with joinder/notice); buyer takes subject to senior liens; no revival upon the mortgagor's repurchase."},{"n":24,"summary":"Land encumbered by a mortgage changes hands (or a relative agrees to help pay); the lender pursues someone other than the original borrower for the debt or a deficiency.","trigger":"Transfer of mortgaged property or side agreement about the debt + lender seeks personal liability beyond the signer.","rule":"Only a person who signed the note — or expressly ASSUMED the mortgage debt — is personally liable; a buyer who takes 'subject to' the mortgage risks only the land. An assuming grantee is liable to the lender as an intended third-party beneficiary, and the original borrower remains secondarily liable as a surety unless the lender grants a NOVATION releasing him.","sub":"Mortgages – Assumption vs. Subject-To; Novation","sensitivity":"Three positions, three exposures: signer (always liable), assuming grantee (liable via third-party-beneficiary doctrine — cross-module link to Contracts Unit 35), subject-to buyer (no personal liability, land at risk). Release of the original debtor never happens by operation of the transfer — only by the lender's novation.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian delegación schema gets the headline right — the delegating debtor is not released without the creditor's express consent (C.C. arts. 1,317 ff.). The frictions are the assumption/subject-to line (a transfer structure with no codal name) and the third-party-beneficiary route by which the lender sues a grantee it never contracted with — the same consideration-free instinct corrected in the Contracts module.","authority":"Assumption vs. subject-to; novation; third-party collateral. Cross-module: Contracts Unit 35 (third-party beneficiaries)."},{"n":25,"summary":"A financially pressed owner conveys a deed that is really security for a loan, buys land on an installment contract with a forfeiture clause, or hands the lender a deed instead of facing foreclosure.","trigger":"Transaction structured to bypass foreclosure + borrower's equity at stake + characterization dispute.","rule":"Equity protects the right to redeem: an absolute deed intended as security is an EQUITABLE MORTGAGE requiring foreclosure (parol evidence admissible); installment land contracts with substantial equity are increasingly treated the same way, barring forfeiture; but a deed in lieu of foreclosure given voluntarily and for fair value AFTER default is valid — the anti-clogging rule bars only advance waivers of redemption.","sub":"Mortgages – Equity of Redemption; Anti-Clogging Family","sensitivity":"The timing of the waiver is the switch: devices that strip redemption AT THE OUTSET (disguised deeds, forfeiture clauses) are recharacterized; a post-default, arm's-length surrender stands. Substance over form governs the whole family.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian nose for this is excellent — the prohibition of the pacto comisorio and simulación doctrine condemn the same disguised-security devices. The residual friction is the temporal gate: U.S. law validates the POST-default deed in lieu that a rigid pacto-comisorio reflex would condemn, because the vice is clogging redemption in advance, not the surrender itself.","authority":"Equity of redemption and anti-clogging: equitable mortgage (absolute deed as security); installment land contracts; deed in lieu of foreclosure valid post-default."},{"n":26,"summary":"A grantor signs a deed but keeps it, hands it over asking that it not be recorded, gives it to a dead person's estate plan, or takes it back after a quarrel.","trigger":"Deed execution + dispute over whether title passed (possession, recording, return of the paper, dead grantee).","rule":"A deed transfers title upon DELIVERY — words or conduct showing the grantor's present intent to be bound — plus acceptance (presumed if beneficial). Recording is unnecessary between the parties; physically returning the deed does not reconvey; and a deed to a grantee who is dead at delivery is VOID, leaving title with the grantor.","sub":"Deeds – Delivery; Void Conveyances","sensitivity":"Delivery is a question of intent, not of paper mechanics: handing over with a request not to record still passes title; getting the paper back changes nothing (reconveyance requires a new deed). The dead-grantee rule is absolute — no grantee in existence, no conveyance.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “without registration (protocolización) nothing happened — and giving the deed back undoes the deal” → both wrong. The civilian transfer schema is document-and-registry-centric (the notarial act constitutes the transfer); U.S. delivery doctrine locates the transfer in the grantor's INTENT at one instant, making recording irrelevant inter partes and physical redelivery legally meaningless.","authority":"Delivery: present intent controls; recording unnecessary inter partes; redelivery ineffective; deed to a deceased grantee void."},{"n":27,"summary":"After closing, the buyer discovers a defect: a lien, a paramount claimant, or a title flaw. Which deed promise (if any) was broken, when, and by whom in the chain?","trigger":"Post-closing title defect + type of deed given (general warranty / special warranty / quitclaim) + timing of the breach.","rule":"Present covenants (seisin, right to convey, against encumbrances) are breached, if at all, AT DELIVERY and traditionally do not run to remote grantees; future covenants (quiet enjoyment, warranty, further assurances) are breached only upon EVICTION or actual disturbance and DO run with the land. Buying off a paramount title asserted against the covenantee counts as constructive eviction. A quitclaim conveys whatever the grantor has, with no covenants at all.","sub":"Deeds – Title Covenants (Present vs. Future)","sensitivity":"Two clocks and two reaches: present covenants — breach at closing, statute of limitations runs immediately, personal to the grantee; future covenants — dormant until disturbance, enforceable by remote grantees down the chain. Deed hierarchy (general warranty → special → quitclaim) sets which promises exist at all.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the seller answers for eviction — saneamiento por evicción covers this” → directionally right, structurally wrong. The codal saneamiento is one statutory guarantee implied in every sale; the U.S. system makes title protection a MENU chosen by deed type, splits it into six covenants with different accrual dates and different runners, and allows the wholly guarantee-free quitclaim — a deed the civilian schema does not expect to exist.","authority":"Title covenants: present (seisin, right to convey, against encumbrances) vs. future (quiet enjoyment, warranty, further assurances); constructive eviction by buying off paramount title; deed hierarchy."},{"n":28,"summary":"A grantor conveys land he does not yet own; he later acquires title (or the deed's boundary/interest description proves premature).","trigger":"Conveyance by warranty deed before the grantor owns + grantor's after-acquired title.","rule":"Estoppel by deed: a grantor who conveys by warranty deed an interest he does not own is estopped to deny the conveyance when he later acquires it — the after-acquired title passes automatically to the earlier grantee.","sub":"Deeds – Estoppel by Deed (After-Acquired Title)","sensitivity":"The doctrine rides on the warranty: it attaches to warranty deeds, not quitclaims (nothing was promised). Recording trap: the earlier grantee's deed may sit OUTSIDE a later searcher's chain of title (a 'wild' link), so a subsequent BFP can still prevail under the recording act — estoppel binds the grantor, not the world.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian recognizes the result — the sale of another's thing is voidable at the buyer's instance where he did not know the thing belonged to someone else (C.C. art. 1,483), and the defect is understood to be cured when the seller afterwards acquires ownership — a result the codal text does not spell out and that doctrine and case law supply. The convergence is genuine; the residual friction is the interaction with recording (the after-acquired title can still be lost to a BFP whose search could not find the early deed).","authority":"Estoppel by deed; after-acquired title; wild-deed recording trap."},{"n":29,"summary":"Between signing the land-sale contract and closing, the property burns, the seller dies, or a will devises 'my real property' — and the parties dispute who bears the loss or what the devisee takes.","trigger":"Executed land-sale contract + casualty, death, or characterization event before closing.","rule":"Equitable conversion: once a specifically enforceable contract is signed, the BUYER owns the land in equity (bearing the risk of loss in the majority rule) and the seller holds bare legal title as security for the price — his interest is PERSONAL property (the right to proceeds). Death, devise, and creditor questions follow that recharacterization; the minority/UVPRA rule keeps risk on the possessing seller.","sub":"Contracts for Sale – Equitable Conversion","sensitivity":"The mechanism, not the result, is what is tested: because conversion happens at SIGNING, a will speaking at death passes the seller's interest as MONEY, not land; the buyer's insurable interest begins immediately; and the doctrine presupposes a contract specific-performance-worthy.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian often gets the risk answer right for the wrong reason — consensual transfer plus res perit domino also puts loss on the buyer. But the U.S. mechanism (equitable/legal title split) drives variants the civil schema cannot reach: the 'my real property' devise that carries only proceeds, the seller-as-trustee characterization, and the dependence on specific enforceability. Right result, wrong engine — until the exam tests the engine.","authority":"Equitable conversion: majority risk-of-loss on buyer; seller's interest as personalty; UVPRA minority."},{"n":30,"summary":"An oral land deal is partly carried out — the buyer pays, moves in, or improves — and the seller invokes the Statute of Frauds; or a tenant already in possession claims an oral purchase.","trigger":"Oral contract for an interest in land + part performance asserted to defeat the writing requirement.","rule":"Land contracts require a signed writing, but equity enforces an oral contract on PART PERFORMANCE where the buyer's acts — traditionally two of three: payment, possession, improvements — are UNEQUIVOCALLY REFERABLE to a purchase. Acts equally consistent with an existing tenancy (a tenant staying and paying) do not qualify.","sub":"Contracts for Sale – Statute of Frauds; Part Performance","sensitivity":"The screen is evidentiary, not moral: the conduct itself must point to a sale and nothing else. The tenant-in-possession pattern is the standard counter-example — continued possession plus rent-like payments explains itself without any contract. Cross-module link: Contracts Units 32–34 (Statute of Frauds family).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “there is principio de prueba por escrito / the payment proves the deal — enforce it” → the civil-law openness to proving contracts by execution maps only loosely: U.S. part performance is a narrow equity gate with a qualitative test (unequivocal referability), not a general evidence substitute. The civilian both over-applies it (any payment) and under-applies it (missing that possession-plus-improvements can carry an oral deal to specific performance).","authority":"Part performance: unequivocally-referable acts (payment, possession, improvements); tenant-in-possession counter-example. Cross-module: Contracts Statute of Frauds units."},{"n":31,"summary":"A conveyance or will uses heirs-language (‘to A, and on A's death to A's heirs’; ‘to B and her heirs’), or a vested future interest holder dies before possession; the parties dispute who owns what.","trigger":"Heirs-language in grants + death of a future-interest holder before the interest becomes possessory.","rule":"Under the Doctrine of Worthier Title, an inter vivos grant of a remainder to the grantor's own heirs creates a reversion in the grantor, not a remainder in the heirs (modern: a rule of construction). 'And his/her heirs' following a named grantee are words of LIMITATION (defining a fee simple), not words of purchase — the 'heirs' take nothing. A vested remainder is fully transferable, devisable, and descendible; the holder's death before possession passes it through his estate.","sub":"Future Interests – Worthier Title; Words of Limitation; Transferability","sensitivity":"Three traps keyed to the same word: 'heirs' after the grantor → reversion (DWT); 'heirs' after a named grantee → mere fee-simple language; heirs of a DEAD remainderman → take through his will/intestacy, not directly. Classification decides who must join a sale and whose consent releases the interest.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the heirs were named, so the heirs own something” → usually they own nothing (yet). The civilian reads 'herederos' through forced-heirship and vocación hereditaria (living persons have no heirs — only expectations), but lacks the DWT/words-of-limitation machinery that makes 'heirs' a term of art doing three different jobs in three positions.","authority":"Worthier Title / words of limitation: Doctrine of Worthier Title (modern rule of construction); vested remainders transferable, devisable, descendible."},{"n":32,"summary":"A life tenant occupies property while another holds the remainder; disputes arise over repairs, mortgage payments, taxes, alterations, or deterioration.","trigger":"Divided ownership in time (life estate + future interest) + allocation of economic burdens and limits on the present possessor's conduct.","rule":"The life tenant must not commit waste: affirmative (active destruction), permissive (neglect of ordinary repairs, to the extent of income or rental value), or ameliorative (unauthorized value-increasing alterations, traditionally actionable). The life tenant bears current charges — mortgage INTEREST, taxes, ordinary repairs; the remainderman bears mortgage PRINCIPAL and extraordinary capital costs.","sub":"Life Estates – Waste; Interest/Principal Allocation","sensitivity":"The interest/principal split is the recurring exam switch: the same mortgage payment divides into a life-tenant share and a remainderman share. Waste comes in three flavors with opposite intuitions — even IMPROVING the property can be actionable (ameliorative waste) because the remainderman is entitled to receive the estate unchanged.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the usufructo schema (C.C. arts. 583 ff.) maps remarkably well — the usufructuario likewise pays annual charges and interest while the nudo propietario bears capital — so the civilian instinct is largely RIGHT. Residual friction: 'waste' as a unified tort-like doctrine (especially ameliorative waste) and its remedies exceed the usufruct duty-of-conservation schema.","authority":"Waste and allocation: affirmative/permissive/ameliorative waste; life tenant pays interest, taxes, ordinary repairs; remainderman pays principal."},{"n":33,"summary":"A grant restricts the grantee's power to sell, or conditions the interest on marriage/divorce behavior (forfeiture if the holder divorces; gift over if a child divorces).","trigger":"Direct restraint on alienation of a fee + conditions keyed to marriage or divorce.","rule":"Absolute restraints on alienation of a fee simple are void; the estate stands free of the restraint. Conditions in partial restraint of MARRIAGE are disfavored (valid if reasonable/support-motivated), but a divestment triggered by DIVORCE is generally upheld where it provides for the changed circumstance rather than induces the divorce.","sub":"Estates – Restraints on Alienation; Marriage/Divorce Conditions","sensitivity":"Two different policy screens: restraints on TRANSFER of a fee die automatically (the deed survives, the clause falls); restraints on personal STATUS are weighed by purpose — provision vs. inducement. The drafting difference between 'shall not sell' (void) and a defeasible fee with a divorce condition (valid, Unit 6 machinery) is outcome-determinative.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “party autonomy — the grantor may condition his gift as he wishes” (or the inverse: “all such clauses offend public policy”). The civilian prohibición de enajenar analysis (limited temporal validity) and buenas costumbres screen exist, but the U.S. system resolves by CLASSIFICATION — restraint-on-alienation vs. defeasible-fee condition — not by a general reasonableness balance, so the same clause lives or dies by how it is drafted.","authority":"Restraints: absolute restraints on alienation of a fee void; marriage/divorce conditions screened by purpose (provision vs. inducement)"},{"n":34,"summary":"The government authorizes a permanent physical installation on private property, or a regulation restricts use (in whole or in part), or condemnation takes part of leased premises; owners and tenants claim compensation.","trigger":"Government action affecting property + track selection: physical occupation vs. regulatory restriction vs. formal condemnation of a leasehold.","rule":"A permanent physical occupation authorized by government is a per se taking regardless of size or public benefit (Loretto) — with no nuisance exception. A regulation is a taking if it denies ALL economically beneficial use (Lucas), unless it merely codifies background principles of nuisance/property law; partial diminutions are weighed under Penn Central. Partial condemnation of leased premises does not terminate the lease — rent continues, and the tenant shares the award for the leasehold value taken; total condemnation extinguishes the lease.","sub":"Takings – Physical vs. Regulatory; Condemnation of Leaseholds","sensitivity":"Track selection is dispositive: the nuisance-prevention escape valve exists ONLY on the regulatory track — it never saves a physical occupation. Lucas requires a TOTAL wipeout; anything less shifts to Penn Central balancing. In leasehold condemnation the partial/total line flips both the rent obligation and the award split.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “expropiación means the state formally takes title and pays” → U.S. takings doctrine also compensates INFORMAL takings — wires on a roof, regulations that zero out value. The expropiación schema (formal decree + justiprecio) has no regulatory-takings track at all, so the civilian misses both the per se physical rule and the Lucas/Penn Central gradations.","authority":"Takings: Loretto v. Teleprompter (per se physical); Lucas v. South Carolina Coastal Council (total regulatory; background principles); Penn Central (partial); leasehold condemnation apportionment."},{"n":35,"summary":"A lawful business predates a new zoning ordinance that now forbids it; the owner continues, expands, or is ordered to phase out; or a buyer discovers the zoning issue at closing.","trigger":"Pre-existing lawful use + subsequent zoning restriction + continuation/expansion/amortization.","rule":"A prior nonconforming use may continue (vested right) but may not be substantially expanded; jurisdictions split on 'amortization' ordinances that phase it out over time (majority uphold reasonable periods). An EXISTING zoning violation makes title unmarketable (cf. Unit 20); the mere fact that current zoning limits future expansion does not.","sub":"Zoning – Nonconforming Use; Amortization","sensitivity":"Three switches: continuation (protected) vs. expansion (not) vs. amortization (split, reasonableness-dependent); destruction or abandonment usually ends the protection. The marketable-title interface runs on present violation, not present restriction.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the new rule applies to everyone immediately” or “acquired rights are absolute” → the U.S. answer is a calibrated middle: the use survives, frozen at its footprint, possibly amortized away. Civilian derecho adquirido/irretroactividad instincts point the right direction but lack the expansion and amortization refinements where the exam lives.","authority":"Zoning: prior nonconforming use; expansion limits; amortization split; marketable-title interface (present violation)"},{"n":36,"summary":"A commercial tenant installs equipment (shelving, machinery, a walk-in cooler) and wants to remove it at the end of the lease; the landlord claims it became part of the realty.","trigger":"Item annexed to leased premises by a tenant for business purposes + removal before (or after) lease end.","rule":"Trade fixtures — chattels annexed by a tenant for business use — remain removable by the tenant if removed before the end of the lease term (or a reasonable time after, for holdovers without fault), provided removal does not substantially damage the premises; fixtures not removed in time become the landlord's.","sub":"Fixtures – Trade Fixtures; Annexation","sensitivity":"Two clocks and one damage screen: WHO annexed (tenant-for-business → removable; owner → fixture analysis by intent/adaptation), WHEN removed (before lease end), and HOW (no substantial injury). Miss the deadline and ownership flips by operation of law.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the accesión/bienhechurías schema (what is attached accedes to the soil; the tenant at most claims reimbursement for improvements) → U.S. law gives the commercial tenant a RIGHT OF REMOVAL, not a reimbursement claim — the exact opposite remedy — but only inside the lease-term window. The civilian both under-claims (assuming the cooler stays) and misses the deadline structure.","authority":"Trade fixtures: tenant business annexations removable before lease end absent substantial damage."},{"n":37,"summary":"A landlord or seller refuses to deal (or imposes conditions) based on a protected characteristic; a small owner-occupied building or single-family seller claims exemption; a disabled tenant requests an accommodation (e.g., a service animal in a no-pets building).","trigger":"Housing transaction + protected class + statutory exemption claim or accommodation request.","rule":"The Fair Housing Act bars discrimination in housing on race, color, religion, sex, familial status, national origin, and disability — including refusal to make reasonable accommodations in rules and policies (a service/assistance animal exception to a no-pets rule). The FHA's exemptions (owner-occupied ≤4 units; single-family sold without a broker) NEVER shield racial discrimination, which 42 U.S.C. § 1982 independently prohibits without exemptions.","sub":"Fair Housing – FHA; § 1982; Reasonable Accommodation","sensitivity":"Two statutes stack: the FHA has exemptions; § 1982 (race) has none — so the 'Mrs. Murphy' landlord who wins under the FHA still loses a race claim. Accommodation analysis asks reasonableness/necessity, not the landlord's generic policy.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the owner chooses his tenant — autonomía de la voluntad” → U.S. housing law overrides private choice through overlapping federal statutes with different exemption maps. No Venezuelan private-housing anti-discrimination machinery of comparable granularity exists, so the civilian misses both the liability and the exemption-stacking trap.","authority":"Fair housing: Fair Housing Act (42 U.S.C. §§ 3601–3619) incl. reasonable accommodation; 42 U.S.C. § 1982 (race, no exemptions; Jones v. Alfred H. Mayer Co.)"},{"n":38,"summary":"A buyer of a NEW home discovers latent construction defects (foundation cracks, bad wiring); the builder-vendor said nothing; a later purchaser (not the first buyer) also sues.","trigger":"Sale of new residential construction by builder-vendor + latent defects + nondisclosure or warranty theory + remote purchaser.","rule":"Builder-vendors of new homes impliedly warrant workmanlike construction/habitability, and must disclose known latent material defects the buyer could not reasonably discover; 'as is' does not defeat fraud-level nondisclosure. Jurisdictions split on whether SUBSEQUENT purchasers may sue the builder on the implied warranty (privity), with a growing number allowing latent-defect claims within a reasonable time.","sub":"Vendor Liability – New-Construction Warranty; Nondisclosure","sensitivity":"Caveat emptor survives for USED homes (disclosure duties vary), but flips for new construction; the second-generation-buyer question turns on the privity split. Distinguish deed covenants (title quality, Unit 27) from these QUALITY-of-structure theories — they answer different questions.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “vicios ocultos — redhibitory action against any seller, short deadlines” → close but mis-scaled: the U.S. warranty targets BUILDER-vendors of new homes specifically, coexists with caveat emptor elsewhere, and the remote-purchaser privity fight has no saneamiento analogue. The civilian over-extends the doctrine to used-home sellers and misses the new/used fork.","authority":"New construction: implied warranty of workmanlike construction/habitability; nondisclosure of known latent defects; subsequent-purchaser privity split."},{"n":39,"summary":"An excavation on one parcel causes the neighbor's land to subside; the damaged land was in its natural state (or was improved with buildings).","trigger":"Excavation + subsidence of adjoining land + natural vs. improved condition of the damaged parcel.","rule":"A landowner has an absolute right to lateral support of land in its NATURAL state: an excavator is strictly liable for subsidence of unimproved land. Where the land subsided because of the WEIGHT of improvements, liability requires negligence (unless the land would have subsided even unimproved).","sub":"Incidents of Ownership – Lateral Support","sensitivity":"The strict/negligence line is drawn by a counterfactual: would the land have collapsed without the buildings? Yes → strict liability even for improved land; no → negligence only. The doctrine is parcel-focused, independent of nuisance or trespass.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian expects neighbor-liability for excavation damage (relaciones de vecindad; responsabilidad por obras, C.C. arts. 785 ff. instincts) — broadly RIGHT. Residual friction: the strict-vs-negligence toggle keyed to the natural-state counterfactual is sharper than the general fault-based vecindad schema and is exactly what the exam tests.","authority":"Lateral support: strict liability for land in natural state; negligence standard where improvements caused the subsidence."},{"n":40,"summary":"A will devises a specific parcel; before death the testator sells it, contracts to sell it, or the property otherwise leaves the estate; the devisee claims the land or its proceeds. After a land contract is signed, one party dies before closing.","trigger":"Specific devise of realty + inter vivos disposition (or pending contract of sale) before the testator's death.","rule":"Ademption: a specific devise fails if the testator no longer owns the property at death — the devisee takes nothing (traditional identity theory), not the sale proceeds. If the testator dies AFTER signing an enforceable contract of sale, equitable conversion (Unit 29) treats the interest as personalty: the realty devisee loses to the taker of personal property/proceeds.","sub":"Wills Crossover – Ademption; Equitable Conversion at Death","sensitivity":"Two doctrines chain: ademption kills the devise when the asset is gone; equitable conversion re-characterizes it when a contract is merely pending. Timing of the contract relative to death — not the closing — controls what the devisee receives (cf. Unit 29).","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian legado de cosa ajena/perecida schema (C.C. legacy rules: the legacy of a thing not in the estate generally fails) points the right way — ademption converges. Residual friction: the equitable-conversion overlay (Unit 29) that silently converts the devise into personalty upon a signed contract has no codal analogue and flips the outcome one step earlier than the civilian expects.","authority":"Ademption and conversion at death: identity theory of ademption; equitable conversion of pending contracts (cf. Unit 29); broker exclusive listings; holdover seller as licensee."}],"cases":[{"n":1,"summary":"A nephew and a niece inherited vacant land. The nephew soon discovered that the land was subject to a mortgage held by a local bank, which required monthly payments for the next 10 years. Without telling the niece or seeking her permission, the nephew paid the monthly mortgage payments for one year.","trigger":"Cotenant pays mortgage alone","rule":"A cotenant paying more than his share may seek contribution. Carring charges (mortgage and taxes) payed by one co-tenant apply contribution (pay now). Repair and improvements payed back after accounting or when property is partitioned.","sub":"Contribution Tenants","family":"Co-Ownership","unit":9},{"n":2,"summary":"A buyer entered into a valid written contract with a seller to purchase vacant land. Unbeknownst to the seller, the buyer planned to construct a gas station on the land. The contract provided that the buyer could rescind the contract if the seller failed to deliver marketable title.","trigger":"Restrictive covenant bars intended use","rule":"Restrictive covenants making intended use illegal render title unmarketable.","sub":"Marketable title","family":"Vendor–Purchaser","unit":20},{"n":3,"summary":"A buyer purchased a commercial building and financed the purchase by executing both a negotiable promissory note and a mortgage to the bank to secure repayment of the loan. The mortgage was promptly recorded. Six months later, the bank assigned only the promissory note to an investor.","trigger":"Note assigned without mortgage recording","rule":"Assignment of the note carries the mortgage.","sub":"Mortgage follows note","family":"Mortgages","unit":null},{"n":4,"summary":"A woman who owned an apartment building decided to give the building to a man whose wife she did not care for. The woman signed and promptly recorded a deed properly describing the property and identifying the man by name. The deed specified that under no circumstances was the property “to ever pass to [the man’s wife].”\n\nUnbeknownst to the woman, the man had died before she executed and recorded the deed.","trigger":"Deed delivered to deceased grantee","rule":"A deed to a deceased grantee is void.","sub":"Void deed","family":"Deeds","unit":26},{"n":5,"summary":"Car subject to bank security interest is repossessed and sold; surplus $12,000 due to debtor; testator’s will specifically devises car to brother, residue to sister; testator dies before receiving surplus check; no statute.","trigger":"Specific devise is not in estate at death (sold/repossessed) so proceeds fall to residuary absent anti-ademption statute.","rule":"If specific property that is no longer in the estate at the testator’s death is devised in a will, the gift adeems by extinction, which means the devisee will not inherit it (unless a statute applies to reverse this).","sub":"Ademption by Extinction","family":"Wills Crossover","unit":40},{"n":6,"summary":"Roommates built home on private land and gardened on state land for 16 years; one dies intestate","trigger":"Adverse possession claim involving multiple possessors and government-owned land","rule":"The exclusivity requirement for adverse possession does not mean that only one person can acquire title to real property. If more than one person acquires title, they will hold title as tenants in common, unless facts indicate otherwise. You cannot adversely possess against the government.","sub":"Adverse Possession","family":"Adverse Possession","unit":10},{"n":7,"summary":"Co-tenant daughter rented then occupied inherited home and later excluded brother; AP statute 5 years","trigger":"Co-tenant adverse possession requires ouster","rule":"Co-tenants can adversely possess property that they share ownership of with others, but they must do so by ousting the other co-tenants.","sub":"Adverse Possession (Co-tenants)","family":"Co-Ownership","unit":9},{"n":8,"summary":"Owner with exclusive 6-month listing refuses broker’s ready buyer, then sells directly to coworker after coworker interest during listing.","trigger":"Exclusive listing plus owner’s own discussions with coworker about the property during the listing period leading to a later private sale.","rule":"If a broker has the rights to an exclusive listing, even a conversation between the homeowner and potential buyer can violate the broker’s rights.","sub":"Brokerage / Exclusive listings","family":"Wills Crossover","unit":40},{"n":9,"summary":"Co-owners had survivorship; woman attempted unilateral conveyance; ownership disputed","trigger":"Unilateral conveyance when survivorship estate may be by entirety","rule":"In a tenancy by the entirety, one spouse cannot unilaterally convey any interest","sub":"Concurrent Estates","family":"Co-Ownership","unit":8},{"n":10,"summary":"Spouses hold land as joint tenants with right of survivorship; wife secretly conveys her interest to a friend, retaining a life estate; friend then transfers half of that interest to her daughter, who later dies leaving a child.","trigger":"Unilateral conveyance by one joint tenant severing the joint tenancy without the other’s consent or knowledge.","rule":"A joint tenancy with rights of survivorship interest can be conveyed even without the knowledge of the other co-tenant.","sub":"Concurrent estates – Joint tenancy severance","family":"Co-Ownership","unit":8},{"n":11,"summary":"Brother and sister co-own mortgaged land; sister silently pays a year of mortgage payments, then demands brother’s half.","trigger":"One co-tenant making necessary payments on a debt that threatens foreclosure of the common property.","rule":"If one co-tenant pays a debt which subjects the property to foreclosure, they are entitled to immediate contribution from the other tenants for their pro rata share.","sub":"Concurrent estates / Contribution","family":"Co-Ownership","unit":9},{"n":12,"summary":"City stops using land as public school and instead runs a transit hub; grantor’s heir sues after grantor’s death to reclaim land.","trigger":"Cessation of the specified use (“so long as” public school) that triggers the possibility of reverter.","rule":"When the event that the possibility of reverter is subject to occurs, the cause of action for ejectment and adverse possession starts to run at that moment.","sub":"Defeasible fees / Possibility of reverter","family":"Future Interests","unit":6},{"n":13,"summary":"Farmer plans a pool that will block his only dirt‑road access to the main road; neighbor orally allows him to cross neighbor’s land; farmer builds pool and uses the shortcut until neighbor revokes permission.","trigger":"Farmer’s substantial reliance (installing the pool and cutting off his prior access) on the neighbor’s oral promise to allow passage.","rule":"An easement by estoppel is when a party detrimentally relies on an otherwise invalid, attempted grant of an easement by another.","sub":"Easements – Estoppel / Reliance","family":"Easements","unit":11},{"n":14,"summary":"Owner sells southern parcel with recorded appurtenant easement over his retained northern land, later buys the southern parcel back, then resells it without mentioning the easement; buyer later discovers the recorded easement and wants to use the path.","trigger":"Dominant and servient parcels coming into common ownership, which would extinguish the easement by merger before the later resale.","rule":"If the dominant and servient estate are subsequently owned by the same person, the easement is extinguished by merger.","sub":"Easements – Merger","family":"Easements","unit":13},{"n":15,"summary":"Owner grants recorded easement to neighbor and heirs; neighbor’s buyer adds 6-unit apartment and wants all tenants to use easement.","trigger":"Attempted expansion of use of an easement beyond the scope reasonably contemplated by the original grant.","rule":"If an easement is overused, the proper remedy is an injunction based on the scope of the original granting of the easement.","sub":"Easements / Scope and overuse","family":"Easements","unit":13},{"n":16,"summary":"Grantor gives southern parcel owner an unrecorded deeded right to use access road over northern parcel; buyer of south parcel seeks to use road. Southern parcel owner had the right to use the easement (benefited parcel) over grantor's northern parcel (dominant estate). Southern parcel owner sells to buyer. Buyer buys the benefited estate and he has the right to use it (easement comes along). But it the burdened estate (grantor) sells to another that burdened estate would be binding only if who buys it has notice.","trigger":"Express easement appurtenant granted to grantee, “her heirs and assigns,” running with the dominant estate. Easement IN Gross (run to the IIIIndividual only the individual can use it). APPurtenant any one that uses the PPProperty rins with the lad can use it (along with heirs and assigns). Easement have to be in writing if not they are a license. If an easement is not recorded and some good faith person buys the property. This was an express easement not implied.","rule":"Appurtenant easements run with the land, meaning that they can still be enforced when a new property owner comes into possession of the land. • Easements need not be recorded to be valid. • But when land changes hands, which side matters: – buying the benefited land = easement comes along – buying the burdened land = easement binding only if buyer had notice","sub":"Easements appurtenant / Running with land","family":"Recording Acts","unit":2},{"n":17,"summary":"Easement appurtenant for southern parcel over a paved path on servient land; pavement deteriorates and damages dominant owner’s car; dominant owner demands repairs and damages.","trigger":"Easement maintenance generally falls on the easement user/dominant estate absent agreement; servient owner not required to maintain.","rule":"The person who uses the easement is responsible for the upkeep and maintenance of the easement.","sub":"Easements appurtenant / Running with land","family":"Easements","unit":13},{"n":18,"summary":"Buyer signs contract for land under a general warranty deed with closing in 30 days; while house‑sitting before closing, an electrical fire destroys the home; parties dispute who bears the loss.","trigger":"Signed, enforceable land‑sale contract triggering equitable conversion, shifting risk of loss to the buyer before legal title passes.","rule":"Under the doctrine of equitable conversion, once a binding contract is signed, the buyer has equitable rights in the home, but also has the risk of loss.","sub":"Equitable conversion – Risk of loss","family":"Contracts for Sale","unit":29},{"n":19,"summary":"Friend buys house in his name and “rents” to man with down payment, long payments, and option to buy; friend later tries to evict.","trigger":"Lease-option structure that in substance functions as seller-financing of a land sale.","rule":"When a court determines equity demands it, a land sale contract, in any form, will create an equitable mortgage in favor of the buyer.","sub":"Equitable mortgage / Installment contracts","family":"Mortgages","unit":25},{"n":20,"summary":"Same subdivision agreement limiting home size and requiring maintenance fee; gifted daughter wants to build a 6,000-square-foot home despite the recorded restriction in the chain of title.","trigger":"Enforcement of a use restriction (home size limit) as an equitable servitude against a successor who has notice but where strict privity is not present.","rule":"Privity of estate is not required to enforce an equitable servitude.","sub":"Equitable servitudes – Privity and notice","family":"Covenants","unit":15},{"n":21,"summary":"Owner‑occupied four‑unit building; landlord wants to rent only to African American tenants based on informal advice that Fair Housing Act exemptions apply to small owner‑occupied buildings.","trigger":"Intentional race‑based discrimination in housing, even in a small, owner‑occupied building where FHA exemptions might otherwise apply.","rule":"Although the Fair Housing Act has 2 major exceptions, the same exceptions do not apply when discriminating against race under the Civil Rights Act.","sub":"Fair Housing / Civil Rights Act – Race discrimination","family":"Fair Housing","unit":37},{"n":22,"summary":"Legally blind tenant in 8-unit building asks no-pets landlord for guide dog as an assistance animal.","trigger":"Disabled tenant requesting reasonable accommodation (service animal) under Fair Housing Act.","rule":"Under the Fair Housing Act, a landlord cannot discriminate against protected classes of people and must make reasonable accommodations for the tenants.","sub":"Fair Housing Act / Reasonable accommodations","family":"Fair Housing","unit":37},{"n":23,"summary":"Grantor deeds “to my mother for life, then to my son, but if my son does not have children, to my daughter,” raising questions about son’s future interest classification.","trigger":"Remainder to an ascertained person that is subject to a condition that could divest the interest in favor of another.","rule":"A remainder is a future interest that follows a life estate. A remainder is contingent if there is either a condition that has to be met before the grantee receives the property or the grantee cannot be ascertained at the time of the grant.","sub":"Future interests – Contingent vs vested remainders","family":"Future Interests","unit":5},{"n":24,"summary":"Grantor conveys property subject to a mortgage to his daughter for life with remainder to his son; mortgagee continues billing grantor; daughter and son dispute responsibility for principal and interest payments.","trigger":"Traditional allocation of mortgage obligations between the life tenant in possession and the remainderman.","rule":"The life tenant has the duty to pay interest and taxes on the property, but the remaindermen must pay the principal of any mortgage on the property.","sub":"Future interests – Life tenant vs remainderman duties","family":"Life Estates","unit":32},{"n":25,"summary":"Grantor conveys land to neighbor for life, then to cousin and her heirs; cousin later wills her estate to a church and dies before the grantor and life tenant; grantor wonders if he can re‑convey the land.","trigger":"Vested remainder that is fully devisable and descends to the devisee even if the remainderman dies before the life tenant. In this question the life tenant - the neighbor still was alive whenn cosin died","rule":"A vested remainder is a devisable interest in an estate that vest when the previous estate terminates. Generally a remainder comes after a life estate.","sub":"Future interests – Vested remainder / Devise","family":"Future Interests","unit":5},{"n":26,"summary":"Mother deeds home to son conditioned on him attending college; she later dies leaving estate to daughter while son still hasn’t attended.","trigger":"Deed language making son’s interest conditional on attending college and earning a degree, surviving the grantor’s death.","rule":"A future conveyance to a grantee which is conditional may survive the life of the grantor and create an executory limitation on the grantor’s estate.","sub":"Future interests / Executory limitations","family":"Future Interests","unit":6},{"n":27,"summary":"Grantor conveys “to my mother for life, then to my son, but if my son does not get married, to my daughter.”","trigger":"Remainder given to an ascertained person, subject to a later condition divesting it in favor of another.","rule":"Whether a remainder is vested or contingent depends on if a condition exists to the remainder obtaining title to the land.","sub":"Future interests / Vested remainders","family":"Future Interests","unit":5},{"n":28,"summary":"Buyer with poor credit enters an installment land contract to pay $1,000 per month for 90 months with deed at the end and forfeiture upon default; after paying 60 installments he misses three payments and seller seeks eviction.","trigger":"Installment land contract where buyer has built up significant equity, prompting courts to treat it like an equitable mortgage.","rule":"A vendee in a land sale contract will have an equitable mortgage in the property when the circumstances dictate that it would be otherwise unfair.","sub":"Land contracts – Equitable mortgage / Forfeiture","family":"Mortgages","unit":25},{"n":29,"summary":"After signing land sale contract and taking early possession, buyer’s house roof is damaged by storm before closing; parties dispute who pays.","trigger":"Signed land contract in a common-law jurisdiction triggering equitable conversion before closing.","rule":"Under equitable conversion, risk of loss passes to the buyer at the time the contract for purchase is executed.","sub":"Land sale contracts / Equitable conversion","family":"Contracts for Sale","unit":29},{"n":30,"summary":"Seller gets buyer to extend closing with a time-is-of-the-essence clause, then misses the new closing date due to hospitalization.","trigger":"Express time-is-of-the-essence clause with a reasonable interval before the specified closing date.","rule":"A time is of the essence clause must be adhered to strictly if there was a reasonable time between the notice of the date and the closing date.","sub":"Land sale contracts / Time is of the essence","family":"Contracts for Sale","unit":null},{"n":31,"summary":"Commercial tenant moves out of state and makes an agreement labeled an “assignment” for his friend to take over the remaining lease term; friend defaults on rent and landlord sues both tenant and friend.","trigger":"Arrangement that is functionally a sublease rather than a true assignment, leaving no privity between landlord and the new occupant.","rule":"In a sublease, the landlord does not have privity of contract or privity of estate with the sublessee, meaning that he cannot sue him for breach of contract.","sub":"Landlord–tenant – Assignments vs subleases","family":"Landlord–Tenant","unit":17},{"n":32,"summary":"Credit‑poor tenant rents a discounted “as is” house with many defects and later discovers that there is no hot water at all; landlord insists the tenant must fix it himself under the as‑is clause.","trigger":"Residential lease of substandard premises where an as‑is clause is being invoked to avoid responsibility for basic habitability like hot water.","rule":"An as is clause will not waive the implied warranty of habitability, especially when the parties do not have equal bargaining power.","sub":"Landlord–tenant – Implied warranty of habitability","family":"Landlord–Tenant","unit":18},{"n":33,"summary":"Month‑to‑month tenant reports nonworking heat; landlord refuses to repair until ordered by court after an implied‑habitability suit; shortly after complying, landlord gives notice to terminate the tenancy.","trigger":"Landlord’s termination of a tenancy soon after the tenant asserted habitability rights and obtained relief, raising a retaliatory eviction issue.","rule":"Under retaliatory eviction, a landlord will not be able to evict a tenant for reporting the landlord to the authorities within a certain time period unless the landlord can prove that there was another valid, non-retaliatory motive for his actions.","sub":"Landlord–tenant – Retaliatory eviction","family":"Landlord–Tenant","unit":18},{"n":34,"summary":"Tenant assigned lease despite anti-assignment clause; landlord accepted assignee’s rent then tried to evict","trigger":"Construction and enforcement of restraints on lease transfer","rule":"Restrictions on transfers of land will be narrowly construed.","sub":"Landlord–Tenant (Assignment)","family":"Landlord–Tenant","unit":null},{"n":35,"summary":"Tenant with 6-month lease sublets to friend while overseas; friend causes fire damage and skips last month’s rent.","trigger":"Sublease rather than assignment, leaving no privity between landlord and sublessee.","rule":"A landlord cannot sue a sublessee of a rental contract because they will neither have privity of contract nor privity of estate.","sub":"Landlord-tenant / Sublease liability","family":"Landlord–Tenant","unit":null},{"n":36,"summary":"Careful excavation caused neighboring land collapse destroying planted trees and shrubs","trigger":"Strict liability for loss of lateral support to land in natural condition","rule":"If someone destroys lateral support for another’s land, it can be strict liability if the land was in its natural condition without artificial structures. Trees and shrubbery will be considered natural condition.","sub":"Lateral Support","family":"Incidents of Ownership","unit":39},{"n":37,"summary":"Seller agrees to convey land by quitclaim deed; days before closing, buyer discovers a substantial lien and announces he will not attend closing, believing seller will be unable to deliver good title.","trigger":"Existence of a significant title defect before closing when marketable title is not yet due.","rule":"The implied warranty of marketability isn’t due until closing time.","sub":"Marketable title – When due","family":"Vendor–Purchaser","unit":20},{"n":38,"summary":"Buyer under land contract discovers existing mortgage two days before closing and refuses to close on a quitclaim deed.","trigger":"Existing mortgage on the property that can be paid off at closing when marketable title is due.","rule":"Marketable title is due at closing time.","sub":"Marketable title / Existing mortgages","family":"Vendor–Purchaser","unit":20},{"n":39,"summary":"Buyer contracts to purchase land for restaurant use, later discovers recorded covenant restricting property to residential use.","trigger":"Restrictive covenant discovered before closing that bars the buyer’s intended use.","rule":"Marketable title would be breached by any covenants that affect the purchaser’s anticipated use of the property.","sub":"Marketable title / Restrictive covenants","family":"Vendor–Purchaser","unit":20},{"n":40,"summary":"Senior mortgage foreclosed; buyer purchased at sale; mortgagor later repurchased","trigger":"Effect of foreclosure on junior mortgage","rule":"A senior foreclosure extinguishes junior mortgages and they are not revived by later repurchase","sub":"Mortgages","family":"Mortgages","unit":23},{"n":41,"summary":"Owner gives three successive properly recorded mortgages to a bank, credit union, and investor; he defaults; credit union initiates foreclosure first; question is lien priority in foreclosure proceeds.","trigger":"Foreclosure by a senior mortgage holder and its effect on junior mortgage interests recorded later in time.","rule":"Whenever a creditor properly forecloses, it wipes out all mortgage interests that all later creditors had. Lien priority in mortgages is determined by the order of recording, not by foreclosure notices or which lender forecloses first.","sub":"Mortgages – Priority and foreclosure","family":"Mortgages","unit":21},{"n":42,"summary":"Construction loan mortgage recorded first; later credit union records second mortgage and notifies bank. Bank then makes additional advances; borrower defaults; both foreclose.","trigger":"Priority of future advances after notice of junior lien; optional advances lose priority unless committed/obligatory.","rule":"A future advance that is made after having knowledge of another creditor will not have priority over the subsequent creditor unless it was given pursuant to commitment.","sub":"Mortgages – Priority and foreclosure","family":"Mortgages","unit":22},{"n":43,"summary":"Investor’s fiancée mortgages her separate property to secure his loan but never signs the note; bank forecloses after default.","trigger":"Non-signing owner granting a mortgage on her land as security for another’s debt.","rule":"A person who does not sign the promissory note will not be liable for the entire debt, but any property that was mortgaged to guarantee the debt can be foreclosed on.","sub":"Mortgages / Third-party security","family":"Mortgages","unit":24},{"n":44,"summary":"Grantor conveys “to my neighbor, so long as the premises are used for residential and farm purposes, then to my sister and her heirs” in a strict common‑law RAP jurisdiction.","trigger":"Executory limitation that could vest in sister’s favor beyond the perpetuities period, potentially violating the Rule Against Perpetuities.","rule":"If the Rule Against Perpetuities applies, it voids any future interest that violates it.","sub":"RAP – Invalid future interests","family":"Future Interests","unit":7},{"n":45,"summary":"Two neighbors agree in writing to 3,000-square-foot home size limits and to pay $600 annually for road maintenance; neighbor later gifts his lot to his daughter, who plans a large home and road maintenance by her son’s business; she never knew of the agreement.","trigger":"Attempt to enforce a real covenant for money payments against a successor where horizontal privity was lacking between the original covenanting parties.","rule":"For a real covenant to be enforceable, there must be horizontal privity between the parties who enter into the original agreement","sub":"Real covenants – Horizontal privity","family":"Covenants","unit":14},{"n":46,"summary":"Owner subdivides lakeside parcel with setback covenant, himself builds too close to lake, then seeks to enjoin buyer building too close.","trigger":"Recorded covenant in chain of title giving constructive notice to subsequent purchasers.","rule":"Covenants can be binding with actual notice or constructive notice, meaning that the buyer has a duty to research the deed in the real estate records.","sub":"Real covenants / Notice & enforcement","family":"Recording Acts","unit":2},{"n":47,"summary":"Business partner forges owner’s signature on deed, sells house to good-faith purchaser who records; owner returns to find stranger in home.","trigger":"Deed obtained by forgery instead of fraud in the inducement.","rule":"If a grantor’s signature is forged on a deed, the deed will be void and unenforceable.","sub":"Recording / Forged deeds","family":"Deeds","unit":4},{"n":48,"summary":"Mother orally promises land to daughter but sells to investor who delays recording; she then mistakenly sells to neighbor who records first and later gifts the land to the daughter; investor later records and asserts title.","trigger":"Donee transferee taking from a prior grantee and stepping into that grantee’s recording‑statute protection under the shelter rule.","rule":"Under the shelter rule, a person who conveys property to another also conveys all the rights that they have in the property.","sub":"Recording acts – Shelter rule","family":"Recording Acts","unit":3},{"n":49,"summary":"Utility has written but unrecorded powerline easement; owner sells to purchaser for value who checked records and saw no encumbrance; after moving in purchaser objects to visible powerlines and sues to remove.","trigger":"Inquiry notice: visible use/encumbrance on inspection gives notice despite lack of record/actual notice.","rule":"A person will be deemed to have inquiry notice of any interest in land that a visual inspection of the land would have revealed.","sub":"Recording Acts / Inquiry Notice","family":"Recording Acts","unit":2},{"n":50,"summary":"Buyer purchases land after checking records; various encumbrances exist including lawsuit with lis pendens filed against seller.","trigger":"Recorded lis pendens giving notice of a pending lawsuit affecting title.","rule":"Lis pendens creates notice to potential buyers that there is a lawsuit which could affect the title to the property.","sub":"Recording acts / Lis pendens","family":"Recording Acts","unit":null},{"n":51,"summary":"Seller discloses recent water leak but not pre-existing mold; buyer discovers mold after closing under general warranty deed.","trigger":"Seller’s failure to disclose known latent physical defects despite giving a general warranty deed. After closing defects in the property can be requested if: 1) If seller knew and did not disclose and 2)New build","rule":"Warranty deeds do not cover physical defects on the property. Failing to disclose known defects can result in a lawsuit by the buyer against the seller.","sub":"Seller disclosure / Deed warranties","family":"Deeds","unit":27},{"n":52,"summary":"Subdivision lots sold with residential-use covenant; later deed omitted restriction","trigger":"Common scheme of uniform residential development","rule":"Common scheme restrictions can bind later purchasers despite omission in deed. For an equitable servitud as different from Covenant you don’t need a writing, Just looking in ES is enough to put it on notice.","sub":"Servitudes","family":"Covenants","unit":16},{"n":53,"summary":"Oral agreement to sell house; tenant paid $20k down and monthly payments; minor improvements","trigger":"Statute of Frauds asserted against oral land contract","rule":"Partial performance removes a land contract from the Statute of Frauds only when acts are unequivocally referable to the sale","sub":"Statute of Frauds","family":"Contracts for Sale","unit":30},{"n":54,"summary":"Same lease-option deal; after 5 years of payments, man seeks to enforce option to purchase despite no written contract.","trigger":"Buyer’s possession, payments, and other acts of part performance under an oral land sale agreement.","rule":"Although contracts for the sale of land have to be in writing to be enforceable, the court may still enforce a contract depending on if the buyer has taken possession, made improvements, and made payments that make the contract look like a sale.","sub":"Statute of frauds / Part performance","family":"Contracts for Sale","unit":30},{"n":55,"summary":"Developer’s hillside subdivision causes subsidence and damage to downhill properties; court permanently enjoins building on 20 of 30 acres to prevent catastrophic landslides; owner seeks compensation for the restriction.","trigger":"Land‑use regulation (or injunction) that stops a dangerous nuisance rather than merely limiting economic use of land.","rule":"Even if a regulation is found to be a taking, it will not be compensable if the regulation is preventing a nuisance.","sub":"Takings – Nuisance prevention","family":"Takings","unit":34},{"n":56,"summary":"Government requires apartment building owners to allow cable companies to place cable boxes on their buildings for tenants’ benefit, physically occupying part of the property.","trigger":"Government regulation that authorizes a permanent physical occupation of private property by a third party.","rule":"A law that requires a property owner to allow a physical occupation by a third party is a per se taking, which is compensable, unless it is to stop a nuisance.","sub":"Takings – Permanent physical occupation","family":"Takings","unit":34},{"n":57,"summary":"Will specifically devised a car to sister; testator sold car and bought TV/stereo; residuary to brother","trigger":"Specific devise no longer in estate at death","rule":"Specific devised property not in the testator’s estate at the testator’s death is said to adeem by extinction, meaning that the devisee will not receive it (unless there is a statute that holds otherwise).","sub":"Wills (Ademption)","family":"Wills Crossover","unit":40},{"n":58,"summary":"A woman conveyed land by general warranty deed to a purchaser who never inspected it. The purchaser then conveyed to an investor by quitclaim deed. Unknown to everyone, an adverse possessor had been occupying the land and later won a quiet title action to 75% of it. The investor sues the original woman for breach of quiet enjoyment.","trigger":"A remote grantee sues on a future covenant after actual eviction/quiet title by a superior title holder; issue is whether privity needed.","rule":"For breach of a future covenant, such as the covenant of quiet enjoyment, privity of estate is required, but privity of contract is not. In warranties for the sales of land regarding who sues whom, be aware of General Warranty deed (warrants 3 present and future covenants since the property exists), Special Warranty (only since the seller bought the property) and quit claim deed no warranty. Here, investor (remote grantee) sues the woman. For quite enjoyment (future warranty) privity of the estate is required and not privity of the contract. Privity of the estate are all the purchasers.","sub":"Deed Covenants – Quiet Enjoyment","family":"Deeds","unit":27},{"n":59,"summary":"Husband and wife own property as tenants by the entirety. Without the wife’s knowledge, the husband grants a mortgage to a friend to cover gambling debt. The state is lien theory; the friend knew the couple lived there but didn’t know whether the wife owned any interest.","trigger":"Unilateral mortgage/transfer attempt of tenancy by the entirety property without both spouses’ consent.","rule":"In a tenancy by the entirety, both parties must agree before an interest can be transferred. This Q asks what interest would the friend have? For friend it doesnt mean he doesn\"t have a mortgage, rather only that he doesn't have a valid enforceable mortgage against both of them. Death and divorce would sever the TBE but not severance. Friend cant enforce it now but yes when wife dies","sub":"Concurrent Estates – TBE","family":"Co-Ownership","unit":8},{"n":60,"summary":"A homeowner sold a house (advertised widely) and the buyer purchased it without visiting the property, relying on the bargain price and a general warranty deed (and the sale being ‘as is’). After closing, the buyer discovered a serious rat infestation. The seller genuinely did not know about the rats at the time of sale, and the buyer seeks to hold the seller liable.","trigger":"Buyer tries to treat a warranty deed / disclosure duties as covering physical defects; key is that general warranty deed protects title, and disclosure liability requires seller’s knowledge of the defect.","rule":"A warranty deed only protects against problems with title to the property, not physical defects. The duty to disclose does not hold a seller liable for facts that they were not aware of at the time of the disclosure.","sub":"Warranty Deeds – Title vs Defects","family":"Deeds","unit":27},{"n":61,"summary":"A father delivered a deed to his unmarried son stating: ‘to my son, but if my son has a child, then to my grandson.’ The son recorded the deed. Years later, before any child was born, the father died. The question is whether the future interest survives and who has what interest when ownership changes over time.","trigger":"Grant creates a present interest in the son with a shifting future interest (executory interest) in a third party triggered by a condition; executory interest persists even as present ownership changes.","rule":"A person who has an executory interest (future interest) can retain it even when a new person becomes the present owner of the property.","sub":"Future Interests – Executory Interest","family":"Future Interests","unit":6},{"n":62,"summary":"A husband and wife owned a vacant lot as tenants by the entirety and planned to build a house. The husband executed a deed conveying the land to his brother, but the brother had already died at the time of the purported conveyance. A later dispute arose over whether anyone received title through that deed.","trigger":"Deed requires a living grantee at delivery; conveyance to a deceased person is void and transfers nothing.","rule":"A deed to a deceased grantee is void.","sub":"Deeds – Deceased Grantee","family":"Deeds","unit":26},{"n":63,"summary":"Tenant in a one-year residential lease pays for six months, then loses job, moves out quietly, leaves key and note admitting he broke a window; landlord fixes the window but does not try to re-rent and instead lets rent damages accrue until lease ends, then rents immediately to a new tenant.","trigger":"Tenant abandons/breaches lease; landlord sues for remaining rent in a common-law (no mitigation duty) jurisdiction.","rule":"At common law, a landlord did not have to mitigate damages when a tenant breached.","sub":"Landlord–Tenant – Abandonment / No duty to mitigate (common law)","family":"Landlord–Tenant","unit":19},{"n":64,"summary":"Man buys home and signs promissory note and mortgage to bank; later sister moves in and agrees by contract with the man to pay half the mortgage and be treated as half owner, but no deed transfer occurs; they default and bank forecloses with deficiency and seeks personal liability against both.","trigger":"Deficiency judgment/personal liability turns on who signed the note or assumed the mortgage obligation to the lender.","rule":"A person who does not sign the promissory note or assume a mortgage with the mortgage company will not be personally liable to the mortgage company.","sub":"Mortgages – Personal liability on the note / assumption vs taking subject to mortgage","family":"Mortgages","unit":24},{"n":65,"summary":"Neighbor is badly injured when a homeowner negligently hits him; the neighbor’s lawyer files a lis pendens while suing. Before the case ends, the homeowner sells the land to a purchaser who pays fair market value and has no notice. After judgment, the neighbor tries to reach the purchaser’s land to satisfy the award.","trigger":"A lis pendens is filed in the county where the land sits; later buyer takes without notice in a jurisdiction with no recording statute.","rule":"A lis pendens filing gives notice that there is a pending lawsuit which can affect the real property. When there is no recording statute, the common law rule of first in time, first in right applies.","sub":"Recording / Notice – Lis pendens; no recording statute (first in time, first in right)","family":"Recording Acts","unit":1},{"n":66,"summary":"Utility company holds an unrecorded easement over a vacant parcel. Owner sells to Buyer (BFP without notice) who later sells to Utility’s employee, who has actual knowledge of the easement. Employee argues he takes free because the easement was never recorded under a notice statute.","trigger":"A transferee with notice takes from a prior bona fide purchaser (BFP), raising the shelter rule (BFP status can be transferred).","rule":"Under the shelter rule, a person who does not qualify as a bona fide purchaser on their own, can still have the rights of a bona fide purchaser transferred to them if the person granting them the property qualifies as a bona fide purchaser.","sub":"Recording Acts – Shelter rule (BFP rights pass to transferee)","family":"Recording Acts","unit":3},{"n":67,"summary":"Landlord and tenant sign a 10-year commercial lease silent on repairs; heat repeatedly fails. Tenant repairs twice and demands reimbursement; landlord refuses and tells tenant to handle it. Tenant moves out quickly and claims constructive eviction; landlord sues for rent.","trigger":"In a commercial lease, absent an express agreement, the landlord may have no duty to make repairs, undermining a constructive eviction claim.","rule":"Absent an agreement to the contrary, a landlord in a commercial lease, especially a longer one, may not have the duty to make certain repairs. In a commercial lease a landlord has to provide the tenant with the covenant of quit enojment but not the implied warranty of habitability. If the commercial lease is silent as to repairs the obligation is of the tenant. A constructive eviction is a remedy that a tenant can use if the landlord for example breaches the covenant of quite enjoyment","sub":"Landlord–Tenant – Commercial lease repairs (no duty absent agreement)","family":"Landlord–Tenant","unit":18},{"n":68,"summary":"Homeowner sells his residence by written contract and is allowed to remain in the home for 30 days after closing before delivering possession. Title passes, but he stays past 30 days; buyer seeks immediate possession without giving tenant eviction notice required for landlords in that jurisdiction.","trigger":"Seller remains briefly after closing under the sales contract—determine status (license vs tenancy) and whether landlord-tenant notice rules apply.","rule":"A seller allowed to remain briefly after closing is typically a licensee, and a license is generally revocable at will (so tenant eviction-notice rules don’t apply).","sub":"Possession – Holdover after closing (licensee vs tenancy)","family":"Landlord–Tenant","unit":19},{"n":69,"summary":"Seller contracts to sell land and tenders a deed at closing, but buyer discovers a mortgage lien and refuses to close because title is not free of encumbrances. Seller offers to pay off the mortgage using the sale proceeds at closing (or via escrow) to cure the defect.","trigger":"Marketable title is required at closing, but seller may cure title defects at closing using the closing proceeds (e.g., pay off liens) so buyer must close if cured.","rule":"Marketable title is required at closing, and the seller may use closing proceeds to cure title defects (e.g., pay off liens) at closing.","sub":"Sales – Marketable title; curing liens at closing","family":"Vendor–Purchaser","unit":20},{"n":70,"summary":"Neighbors have used a path across another’s land openly and continuously for many years without permission. The landowner argues the use cannot create rights because the users did not exclude others, while the users claim a right to continue using the path.","trigger":"Open, notorious, continuous, and hostile use for the statutory period (prescriptive easement); unlike adverse possession, the user need not be exclusive.","rule":"A prescriptive easement arises from open, notorious, continuous, and hostile use for the statutory period; exclusivity is required for adverse possession but not for a prescriptive easement, so the user gets the right to use, not title.","sub":"Easements – Prescriptive easement (elements; no exclusivity)","family":"Easements","unit":12},{"n":71,"summary":"In a land sale, the buyer discovers a potential title defect before closing. The seller points to the defect as a reason to back out or refuse to close, even though the buyer is willing to proceed.","trigger":"Marketable title protects the buyer; only the buyer can terminate for unmarketable title and may waive defects—seller cannot use buyer’s protection to avoid the deal.","rule":"Marketable title is a protection for the buyer; only the buyer can terminate for unmarketable title, and the buyer may waive title defects—seller can’t refuse to close based on them.","sub":"Sales – Marketable title (buyer-only protection; waiver)","family":"Vendor–Purchaser","unit":20},{"n":72,"summary":"Government condemns only part of leased property and the lease is silent about condemnation. Tenant argues rent should be reduced or terminated because the premises were partially taken.","trigger":"Partial condemnation with no condemnation clause/statute generally does not end the tenancy; tenant remains obligated to pay rent.","rule":"If leased property is partially condemned and the lease has no condemnation clause/statute, the tenant generally remains obligated to pay rent (landlord-tenant relationship continues).","sub":"Landlord–Tenant – Condemnation (partial taking; rent obligation)","family":"Takings","unit":34},{"n":73,"summary":"A landowner executes a deed conveying his land to a friend and hands the deed to the friend saying, “This is yours,” but asks the friend not to record it until after the landowner dies to avoid trouble with relatives. Two days later the landowner asks for the deed back because he has decided to leave the land by will instead; the friend says he will destroy it and later falsely tells the landowner that he did. The landowner later dies intestate leaving a daughter as his sole heir, and the friend records the deed the day after the landowner’s death; the daughter sues to quiet title.","trigger":"Deed effectiveness depends on present intent + delivery + acceptance; recording is not required and grantor cannot revoke without reconveyance.","rule":"A deed is effective upon present intent + delivery + acceptance; recording isn’t required, and the grantor can’t revoke without a new reconveyance.","sub":"Deeds – Delivery, acceptance, recording","family":"Deeds","unit":26},{"n":74,"summary":"An uncle is the record title holder of vacant land and repeatedly told friends he would leave it to his nephew in his will. Before the uncle dies, the nephew conveys the land by warranty deed to a woman for $10,000; she does not conduct a title search but promptly and properly records her deed. The uncle later dies leaving the land to the nephew in a duly probated will, and both the nephew and the woman claim ownership (the nephew offers to return the $10,000).","trigger":"Grantor conveys land he does not own by warranty deed and later acquires title; after-acquired title passes to earlier grantee (estoppel by deed).","rule":"Estoppel by deed: If a grantor conveys land he doesn’t own (especially by warranty deed) and later acquires title, the title automatically passes to the prior grantee.","sub":"Deeds – Estoppel by deed / after-acquired title","family":"Deeds","unit":28},{"n":75,"summary":"A developer creates a recorded development plan for 200 lots with streets and public areas, but construction has not begun and the streets cannot be opened as public ways until completed. One planned street is a border road abutting an adjacent 10‑acre parcel owned by a widow who otherwise lacks practical access to a public way; the widow sells to a buyer, and the deed description is changed to reference the border road as laid out on the developer’s recorded plan. The buyer seeks a building permit stating he intends to use the border road for access, and the developer objects because he never expressly granted any right to use that road; the issue is whether the buyer can claim an implied easement/easement by necessity without prior common ownership.","trigger":"Easement by necessity/implied easement requires prior common ownership of dominant and servient parcels.","rule":"Easement by necessity and implied easement require prior common ownership of the dominant and servient parcels. For easement by necessity, you must have: 1. Common ownership of the dominant and servient parcels in the past, and 2. A severance (the owner splits and conveys one parcel), and 3. After severance, one parcel becomes landlocked/without reasonable access, and 4. Necessity existed at the time of severance.\n\nFor an implied easement from prior use, you also need: 1. A single tract of land is divided by the common owner and a piece of that land is conveyed to another, 2. Before the division, the common owner use the entire land as if there was an easement on it, 3. After the division, the common owner's use of the conveyed land must be apparent, continuous, AND 4. Such use must be reasonably necessary for the owners's use and enjoyment","sub":"Easements – Necessity / implied easement; prior common ownership","family":"Easements","unit":11},{"n":76,"summary":"Owner rented a 3‑acre tract with a small residence to a tenant for $200/month. After years in possession, tenant and owner orally agree tenant will buy the tract for $24,000 payable $200/month for 10 years, plus taxes/insurance/maintenance. Owner agrees to deed after 5 years and $12,000 paid; tenant makes $1,000 of improvements. After tenant pays $12,000 and tenders a note/mortgage for the balance, owner refuses to convey and pleads Statute of Frauds in tenant’s specific performance action.","trigger":"Oral land-sale agreement; buyer in possession makes payments and improvements; Statute of Frauds defense; question is whether conduct is unequivocally referable to the sale vs consistent with tenancy.","rule":"A land-sale contract must satisfy the Statute of Frauds (writing + signature + essential terms), and part performance won’t take it out of the SOF if the buyer’s payment/possession/improvements are equally consistent with an existing landlord-tenant relationship (e.g., “rent,” already-existing possession, minor improvements).","sub":"Sales – Statute of Frauds; part performance","family":"Contracts for Sale","unit":30},{"n":77,"summary":"A farm is conveyed to a man and a woman by a deed that created a cotenancy in equal shares with a right of survivorship. The woman later deeds “my undivided one‑half interest” to a purchaser and then dies. In an action between the purchaser and the man, the issue is who has title to the farm.","trigger":"Cotenancy with survivorship + one co-tenant conveys her interest to a third party; effect on survivorship/severance and who takes after death.","rule":"A spouse cannot unilaterally convey to sever a tenancy by the entirety; so the man prevails only if the original co-tenancy was a tenancy by the entirety (if it was only JTWROS, unilateral conveyance severs).","sub":"Concurrent Estates – Joint tenancy; severance","family":"Co-Ownership","unit":8},{"n":78,"summary":"Farmer gives Bank 1 a recorded mortgage on her farm for $100,000; later gives Bank 2 a recorded junior mortgage for $5,000. Farmer defaults; Bank 1 accelerates and conducts a valid nonjudicial foreclosure with notice to Bank 2; a good‑faith buyer wins the foreclosure sale. Months later the farmer repurchases the farm from the buyer by warranty deed and records it; Bank 2 then begins foreclosure and farmer challenges the junior mortgage’s validity after the senior foreclosure.","trigger":"Senior foreclosure with proper notice to junior lienholder; whether junior lien is extinguished and whether mortgagor’s reacquisition revives it.","rule":"A properly conducted senior foreclosure with notice to the junior lienholder extinguishes the junior mortgage lien, and the mortgagor’s later reacquisition doesn’t revive it.","sub":"Mortgages – Foreclosure; junior liens","family":"Mortgages","unit":23},{"n":79,"summary":"Man conveyed 40 of his 45 acres to a developer by warranty deed 25 years ago, recorded, with a covenant running with the land limiting the 40 acres to residential use. Developer created a 40‑lot single‑family subdivision; later deeds to lots did not mention the covenant. A doctor who owns a house in the subdivision (and only recently learned of the covenant) wants to convert her house into medical offices; zoning permits that use. The original grantor, who still owns the retained adjacent five acres, objects; issue is whether the restrictive covenant can be enforced against the doctor.","trigger":"Recorded restrictive covenant intended to run with the land; later purchaser with notice; enforcement by benefited landowner.","rule":"A recorded restrictive covenant with intent to run binds later purchasers with record notice, and the original grantor’s retained land can enforce if it is benefited by the covenant.","sub":"Servitudes – Restrictive covenants; enforceability","family":"Covenants","unit":null},{"n":80,"summary":"A developer subdivides a 20‑acre lot into twenty one‑acre lots. A purchaser buys Lot #7 by deed but mistakenly builds his home on Lot #8 and lives there for twelve years while many lots remain unsold. Later the developer sells Lot #8 to an investor, who discovers the purchaser living there and demands he leave. The purchaser files a quiet title action to Lot #8 (and also Lot #7) in a 10‑year adverse possession jurisdiction.","trigger":"Adverse possession can be hostile even when possession began by mistake, so long as it was without the true owner’s consent for the statutory period.","rule":"A person can adversely possess property by mistake, as long as the possession was without consent from the true owner. Adverse possesion OCEAN CC. Open, continuos, exclusive, adverse, notorious and C (Claim of right / AP owes whatever he uses. Under C - Color of title you not only get what you use but also what is described on the deed.","sub":"Adverse Possession – Mistaken boundary/lot; hostility without consent","family":"Adverse Possession","unit":10},{"n":81,"summary":"Purchaser contracts to buy a house; contract is silent on title. Before closing, purchaser learns a zoning ordinance caps house size at 3,000 sq. ft., and the existing house is already 3,000 sq. ft., preventing any addition. Purchaser tries to rescind, claiming the zoning ordinance makes title unmarketable.","trigger":"Marketable title is affected by zoning only when the property is presently violating the ordinance; limits on future expansion do not create unmarketable title.","rule":"For marketable title to be violated by a zoning ordinance, the property must presently be violating a zoning ordinance.","sub":"Sales – Marketable title; zoning restrictions vs present violations","family":"Vendor–Purchaser","unit":20},{"n":82,"summary":"Husband and wife buy a building to operate a jewelry repair business when the land is zoned commercial. A few months later the city rezones the area residential but lets them continue operating as a lawful nonconforming use to avoid paying compensation. They ask what advice best protects their future right to keep using the building commercially.","trigger":"Nonconforming uses are not protected forever; the city may terminate the use after the owners have recovered a substantial part of their investment (amortization). Zoning changed from commercial to residential → stopping business immediately risks regulatory taking (Penn Central). City instead allows continued operation as a nonconforming use (“grandfathered”). Nonconforming use is limited: Destroyed by fire → typically cannot rebuild the nonconforming use. Sold to a stranger → generally cannot transfer the benefit like that. Transfer by operation of law (inheritance) may preserve it.","rule":"A property will not be allowed to be used as a nonconforming use in perpetuity, only until the owner has regained a substantial part of their investment.","sub":"Zoning – Nonconforming uses; amortization/termination","family":"Zoning","unit":35},{"n":83,"summary":"A man conveyed property to his daughter for life, and then to his daughter’s first-born child. At the time of the conveyance, the daughter did not have any children yet. Several years later, the man died while his daughter was pregnant with her first child. The jurisdiction follows the common law Rule against perpetuities. Which of the following best describes the rights of the parties presently?","trigger":"Remainder after a life estate where taker is unascertainable at conveyance or must satisfy a condition precedent.","rule":"A remainder is contingent rather than vested if either there is a condition on the grantee receiving it OR the grantee is not ascertainable (including alive) at the time of the conveyance. A vested remainder is when the grantee is asertainable","sub":"Future Interests – Contingent vs vested remainders","family":"Future Interests","unit":5},{"n":84,"summary":"A man owned a five-acre tract of undeveloped land. Upon his death, the man’s will properly conveyed the lot to his three sons with the following language: “The two western acres are to go to Son A. The middle one acre is to pass to Son B. The two eastern acres are to pass to Son C.” Each son, all of whom were adults at the time of the man’s passing, recorded their deeds. Son B, who was extremely jealous of his siblings for receiving more land than he did, purported to convey colorable title to the entire five-acre tract to a husband and wife, who had no rights to the land other than the conveyance from Son B.","trigger":"Adverse possessor claims constructive possession under color of title but did not physically enter all parts of described land.","rule":"Although color of title allows constructive adverse possession of land that the possessor never actually entered, to use it, the adverse possessor must have entered some part of the owner’s land described in the title.","sub":"Adverse Possession – Color of title / constructive possession","family":"Adverse Possession","unit":10},{"n":85,"summary":"Twelve years ago, two married couples who were best friends went on a vacation to a distant state. They fell in love with the state so much that they wanted to remain there. They bought a tent and camped out on some unimproved land that they knew didn’t belong to them. One couple stayed on the land in the tent while the other couple drove back to their home state to rent a moving truck to bring all their belongings (and their friend’s belongings) to the new state. They notified their landlord that they were moving and gave him the forwarding address.","trigger":"Adverse possession claim against land owned by the government.","rule":"A person cannot adversely possess property that belongs to the government.","sub":"Adverse Possession – Government land","family":"Adverse Possession","unit":10},{"n":86,"summary":"A man, in his valid will, conveyed land to his daughter for the life of her child, then to the man’s son (who was married), but if the son ever divorced, the land would pass to the local homeless shelter. Recently, the daughter’s child passed away. The man’s daughter and the man’s son are still alive. At this moment in time, what is the present ownership interest of the son?","trigger":"Prior possessory estate ends (e.g., life estate); future interest holder claims present possession.","rule":"A future interest becomes a present possessory estate once the present possessory estate expires. Restriction on divorce does not invalidate a future interest for public policy reasons, a restriction on MARRIAGE YES.","sub":"Future Interests – Becoming possessory","family":"Estates","unit":33},{"n":87,"summary":"Grantor conveys Blackacre “to Actor for life, remainder to Businessman and his heirs.” Two years later Actor deeds the land by general warranty deed to Cook, who later deeds by general warranty deed to Dentist. Dentist discovers the Businessman’s remainder and pays Businessman for that remainder to prevent a superior claim when Actor’s life estate ends. Dentist sues Actor for breach of a title covenant.","trigger":"Remote grantee buys an outstanding future interest to avoid eviction; analyze which covenants run with the land and whether buying superior title counts as breach of a future covenant (quiet enjoyment/warranty).","rule":"A future covenant (quiet enjoyment/warranty) runs with the land and is breached when the grantee is actually or constructively evicted; paying to remove an outstanding superior interest to avoid eviction can establish breach of the covenant of quiet enjoyment.","sub":"Deeds – Warranty deed covenants; quiet enjoyment","family":"Deeds","unit":27},{"n":88,"summary":"A landowner conveys Blackacre “to the farmer for life, remainder to my heirs.” Five years later, the farmer (life tenant) dies. The landowner (grantor) is still alive and has two living children (A and B). A third child (C) is deceased. The landowner has two living grandchildren: D (child of A) and E (child of C). The question asks who owns the land after the farmer’s death.\n\nTrigger","trigger":"Conveyance “to X for life, remainder to my heirs” and grantor is still alive when the life estate ends.","rule":"When a grantor gives a life estate with a remainder to the grantor’s “heirs,” the grantor retains a reversion (Doctrine of Worthier Title), and if the grantor is alive when the life estate ends, the grantor takes the property.","sub":"Future Interests – Reversion / “Remainder to Grantor’s Heirs” (Doctrine of Worthier Title)","family":"Future Interests","unit":31},{"n":89,"summary":"A testator devises his home “to my widow for life, remainder to my son and his heirs.” The home is subject to a bank mortgage. The widow has made the mortgage payments for several years, but then has financial trouble and demands that the son start making the mortgage payments. The question asks what obligation the son has on the mortgage during the widow’s life.","trigger":"Life estate and remainder with an existing mortgage; allocate mortgage interest vs principal responsibility.","rule":"Absent an agreement otherwise, the life tenant pays the interest on the mortgage during the life tenancy, and the remainderman is responsible for the principal.","sub":"Future Interests – Life tenant duties; mortgage","family":"Life Estates","unit":32},{"n":90,"summary":"A father conveys land to his daughter with the words: “to daughter while the land is used for farming.” The question asks what estate the daughter has.","trigger":"Durational language (“while/so long as/during”) tied to continued use of the land.","rule":"Durational language that causes the estate to end automatically upon a stated event creates a fee simple determinable. It would be fee simple subject to an executory interest if the grant to the daughter would have said: \"...while used for farming, then to son\". The son would CUT in. Here, nobody is cutting in, if she doesn't use for farming it goes back to the grantor (REVERSION)","sub":"Present Estates – Fee simple determinable","family":"Future Interests","unit":6},{"n":91,"summary":"Brother and sister own a lake house as joint tenants with right of survivorship. Together they mortgage the property. The sister later dies and devises her interest to her daughter. A dispute arises over who owns the property after the sister’s death.","trigger":"JTWROS + mortgage + death; whether a mortgage severs a joint tenancy (lien vs title theory) and effect of survivorship on a devise.","rule":"In a lien-theory jurisdiction, a mortgage does not sever a joint tenancy; on a joint tenant’s death, the surviving joint tenant takes the entire property by survivorship and the deceased’s devise is ineffective.","sub":"Concurrent Estates – JTWROS; severance; mortgages","family":"Co-Ownership","unit":8},{"n":92,"summary":"A tenant and landlord sign a one‑year condo lease. Near the end of the term, the tenant says she may move out of state and does not want another one‑year lease. They orally agree the tenant may continue living there and must give at least 30 days’ notice before moving out. The tenant remains in possession and continues sending monthly rent checks, which the landlord accepts. The question asks what type of tenancy the tenant has.","trigger":"After fixed term ends, tenant stays with landlord consent and pays rent monthly; classify tenancy created by periodic rent payments.","rule":"When a tenant remains with the landlord’s consent and pays rent periodically (e.g., monthly), a periodic tenancy (month‑to‑month) is created.","sub":"Landlord‑Tenant – Periodic tenancy","family":"Landlord–Tenant","unit":19},{"n":93,"summary":"A bank records a mortgage securing a home purchase. Two years later, the homeowner gives a second mortgage to a finance company, but the finance company does not record. The homeowner defaults on the bank loan; the bank forecloses, notifying both the homeowner and the finance company. The home is sold at foreclosure, and the finance company does not participate. The question asks what interest the finance company has after the foreclosure sale.","trigger":"Senior mortgage foreclosure with junior mortgagee properly notified; determine effect of foreclosure on junior mortgage interest.","rule":"A foreclosure by a senior mortgagee extinguishes junior mortgage interests if the junior mortgagee is joined/notified, even if the junior mortgage was unrecorded.","sub":"Mortgages – Foreclosure; junior interests","family":"Mortgages","unit":23},{"n":94,"summary":"Owner contracts to sell Blackacre to Buyer on June 1 and delivers a deed to Buyer on June 30. On August 1, Owner later deeds Blackacre to Neighbor for value; Neighbor knows Buyer already has a deed but records immediately. On September 1, Neighbor deeds Blackacre to his Son for value; Son has no knowledge of Buyer’s deed. Buyer records on September 30; Son records the next day. The state has a race–notice recording statute.","trigger":"Competing deeds under a race–notice statute; later grantee’s notice and who recorded first.","rule":"Under a race–notice statute, a later purchaser prevails only if they take for value without notice and record first; a grantee with notice is not protected, and a later BFP must still win the “record first” race.","sub":"Recording Acts – Race–notice; BFP","family":"Recording Acts","unit":1},{"n":95,"summary":"Grantor conveys property to a friend for life, then to grantor’s son. The life tenant rents the property. Tenant tells the life tenant about termites; life tenant does nothing. Five years later, severe termite damage is discovered.","trigger":"Life tenant’s duty to prevent permissive waste after notice of needed repairs.","rule":"A life tenant must avoid permissive waste and has a duty to make reasonable repairs and maintain the property; failure to act after notice can make the life tenant liable to the remainderman.","sub":"Future Interests – Waste; life tenant duties","family":"Life Estates","unit":32},{"n":96,"summary":"Home builder knows during construction that the house will leak during heavy rain, tries to remedy it, but does not disclose the issue to the buyer. Buyer purchases in November, has no issues during winter, but heavy rains in April cause leaks and damage.","trigger":"Seller/builder nondisclosure of known latent defect; implied warranty of workmanship.","rule":"A builder–seller has a duty to disclose known latent defects and is liable for breach of the implied warranty of workmanlike construction/habitability when such defects cause damage.","sub":"Sales – Disclosure & implied warranty (new homes)","family":"Vendor Liability","unit":38},{"n":97,"summary":"Farmer deeds farmland to Man; Man neither records nor takes possession. Two years later, Farmer deeds same land to Woman for value; she does not record. Statute is a notice statute protecting subsequent purchasers for value without notice unless prior deed recorded.","trigger":"Notice recording act; later purchaser for value without notice vs earlier unrecorded grantee.","rule":"Under a notice statute, a subsequent purchaser for value without notice prevails over a prior unrecorded interest even if the subsequent purchaser never records.","sub":"Recording Acts – Notice statute","family":"Recording Acts","unit":1},{"n":98,"summary":"Farmer attempts to deed farmland to Businessman, but the deed’s description is defective. Businessman takes possession, builds a home, and lives there. Four years later Businessman transfers to Teacher, who moves in. Two years later Farmer dies leaving estate to Daughter. Statute of limitations to sue for real estate is five years. Daughter sues Teacher.","trigger":"Adverse possession-like statute of limitations; tacking successive possessors in privity to satisfy period.","rule":"Successive possessors in privity may tack their periods of possession to satisfy the statutory period; once the limitations period runs, the original owner’s successor is barred from recovery.","sub":"Adverse Possession / SOL – Tacking; privity","family":"Adverse Possession","unit":10},{"n":99,"summary":"A woman owns land in fee simple and conveys it to a friend “for life,” with the land to go at the friend’s death to the woman’s neighbor “and her heirs.” The neighbor later dies and, by will, devises her entire estate to a local charity (her daughter would be her only heir if intestate). One year after the neighbor dies, the daughter executes a quitclaim deed conveying any interest she might have in the land to the friend. The jurisdiction follows the unmodified common-law Rule Against Perpetuities.","trigger":"Conveyance creates a life estate and a vested remainder; question is whether the remainder is devisable and whether an heir has any interest before the life tenant’s death.","rule":"“To A for life, then to B” gives B a vested remainder; “and heirs” doesn’t create heirs’ interests; future interests are devisable.","sub":"Future Interests – Vested remainder; devisability","family":"Future Interests","unit":5},{"n":100,"summary":"Builder sells new house by warranty deed; first buyer later sells by quitclaim to woman; woman discovers latent foundation defects and successfully sues builder for $25k repair.","trigger":"Latent defect in new construction; subsequent purchaser suing builder; implied warranty vs deed covenants.","rule":"A builder-vendor of new residential construction may be liable for breach of the implied warranty of habitability/workmanlike construction, often enforceable by subsequent buyers.","sub":"Implied Warranty – Habitability/Workmanlike Construction","family":"Vendor Liability","unit":38},{"n":101,"summary":"Two neighbors sign and record agreement that neither will build detached storage sheds; neighbor later gifts home to daughter; daughter plans a shed; man seeks injunction.","trigger":"Recorded restrictive agreement intended to bind successors; enforcement by injunction; successor has constructive notice.","rule":"A recorded restriction intended to bind successors may be enforced by injunction as an equitable servitude against a successor with notice (constructive notice from recording), even without horizontal privity.","sub":"Servitudes – Equitable Servitudes / Notice (Record)","family":"Covenants","unit":15},{"n":102,"summary":"Man gifts land by quitclaim deed to cousin; cousin neither records nor takes possession; six months later man gifts same land by quitclaim deed to friend without notice; friend takes possession; race-notice statute protects purchasers for value without notice who first record; cousin later records first and sues.","trigger":"Recording act is race-notice; later grantee is a donee (not for value) so not protected; revert to common law first in time.","rule":"Under a race-notice statute, only a subsequent purchaser for value without notice who records first prevails; a donee is not protected, so the first grantee wins under common law. When you have a statute for recording start with common law. In CL it's the first in time first in right, thenn go to the recording statute. Here, it's a race notice. The RN needs a BFF with no notice who records first. Look and see if the elements of the statute are met. Here, there are not met because the Friend received the deed by a gift, he did not give value, statute NOT MET apply CL the cousin wins.","sub":"Recording Acts – Race-Notice / Bona Fide Purchaser (Value)","family":"Recording Acts","unit":1},{"n":103,"summary":"Borrower sells mortgaged property; buyer 'assumes' loan; buyer defaults and becomes insolvent; lender forecloses and seeks deficiency judgment against original borrower only.","trigger":"Assumption vs novation; lender did not release original borrower; deficiency after foreclosure.","rule":"Assumption makes the buyer primarily liable, but the original borrower remains liable as a surety unless the lender agrees to a novation/release.","sub":"Mortgages — Assumption vs Novation (original mortgagor as surety)","family":"Mortgages","unit":24},{"n":104,"summary":"Oral 10-year commercial lease ended; tenant held over 4 months paying rent; before leaving, tenant removed appliances and large A/C system installed (no damage); issue: proper removal.","trigger":"Tenant-installed items used for tenant’s business; removal at end of tenancy; landlord vs tenant dispute.","rule":"A tenant may remove trade fixtures (items installed for the tenant’s business) before vacating, absent an agreement otherwise, so long as removal doesn’t materially damage the premises.","sub":"Landlord–Tenant — Trade Fixtures","family":"Fixtures","unit":36},{"n":105,"summary":"Bank forecloses first mortgage; $5k surplus remains; mortgagor and junior lender claim funds.","trigger":"Foreclosure surplus distribution.","rule":"After senior mortgage paid, remaining proceeds go to junior lienholder before mortgagor.","sub":"Mortgages — Priority of Proceeds","family":"Mortgages","unit":21},{"n":106,"summary":"Woman blocks neighbor’s express access easement with solar collector.","trigger":"Servient owner obstructs appurtenant easement.","rule":"Servient owner may not unreasonably interfere with an appurtenant easement.","sub":"Easements — Interference","family":"Easements","unit":13},{"n":107,"summary":"Commercial tenant stayed in building and paid monthly rent for 18 months after five‑year lease expired. Landlord accepted rent, then gave six weeks written notice to vacate after finding higher‑paying tenant.","trigger":"Holdover tenant paying monthly rent; landlord gave more than one month notice.","rule":"Acceptance of rent after lease expiration creates month‑to‑month tenancy terminable by notice equal to one rental period.","sub":"Landlord‑Tenant — Periodic Tenancy","family":"Landlord–Tenant","unit":19},{"n":108,"summary":"Man conveyed land to woman who failed to record or possess. Later conveyed same land to neighbor BFP who recorded. Neighbor then conveyed to friend who had notice. Jurisdiction is notice statute.","trigger":"Subsequent BFP recorded first; later transferee had notice.","rule":"Under shelter rule, anyone who takes from a BFP receives the BFP’s priority rights even if transferee has notice.","sub":"Recording Acts — Shelter Rule","family":"Recording Acts","unit":3},{"n":109,"summary":"An owner contracted to sell land to a buyer for $100,000 (FMV at the time). During the executory period, an unanticipated zoning change increased FMV to $150,000, and the owner refused to close. To avoid litigation, the buyer assigned the contract (the contract did not forbid assignment) to an experienced real-estate investor in an arm’s-length transaction for $25,000. The investor knew of the owner’s refusal to close, demanded performance, and then sued the owner for specific performance.","trigger":"Assignment of a land-sale contract; specific performance for unique land; assignee generally steps into assignor’s shoes.","rule":"An assignee of a valid real-estate purchase contract may enforce the contract and obtain specific performance to the same extent as the original buyer (absent a valid restriction/defense).","sub":"Contracts for Sale – Assignment & Specific Performance","family":"Contracts for Sale","unit":null},{"n":110,"summary":"A builder sold a new house to a buyer for use as the buyer’s residence. The buyer paid 10% down and financed the rest by executing a promissory note and purchase-money mortgage to the builder. A year later, the buyer missed several mortgage payments and became unable to pay. The buyer then conveyed fee simple title back to the builder (a deed in lieu of foreclosure). Property values had declined substantially. The question asks whether the builder now owns fee simple title.","trigger":"Mortgage default; deed in lieu of foreclosure; equity/consumer protections; validity turns on whether transaction is reasonable/fair (no clogging) under circumstances.","rule":"A mortgagor may convey the property to the mortgagee in lieu of foreclosure if the transaction is voluntary and fair/reasonable under the circumstances (i.e., not an impermissible ‘clog’ on the equity of redemption).","sub":"Mortgages – Equity of Redemption; Deed in Lieu of Foreclosure","family":"Mortgages","unit":25},{"n":111,"summary":"Fifteen years ago, two men moved onto vacant rural land owned by a woman they did not know and built a small fishing shack. Twelve years ago, they replaced the shack with a fish processing plant and a commercial fishing boat dock, and they continued operating a commercial fishery there. One man died intestate last year leaving a sole heir; the woman has now learned of the changes and asserts her rights to the land in a jurisdiction with a 10-year adverse possession period.","trigger":"Adverse possession claim with multiple possessors; long-term occupation and improvements over statutory period.","rule":"The exclusivity requirement for adverse possession does not mean that only one person can acquire title to real property. If more than one person acquire title, they will hold title as tenants in common, unless facts indicate otherwise.","sub":"Adverse Possession — Exclusivity; Multiple Possessors","family":"Adverse Possession","unit":10},{"n":112,"summary":"Several people are tenants in common, and one alleges another owed and breached a fiduciary duty related to the property. The issue is when the fiduciary duty among tenants in common arises—specifically, whether it exists before they become co-tenants or only after co-tenancy begins.","trigger":"Tenancy in common fiduciary duty timing (duty arises upon co-tenancy).","rule":"Although co-tenants in a tenancy in common owe a fiduciary duty to each other, the duty begins when they become co-tenants, not before. Under any type of tenancy, co-tenants have fiduciary duties, for example as to not take an interest in land in oposición of other co-tenants like a mortgage. Here, the mortgage was created before in the wil. A fiduciary duty is not retroactive","sub":"Co-Ownership — Tenants in Common Fiduciary Duty","family":"Co-Ownership","unit":9}]},{"slug":"torts","name":"Torts","bigPicture":"WHERE YOU START. One general clause. Article 1.185 of the Civil Code covers intent, negligence and abuse of right alike: damage plus fault plus causation produces liability, and the analysis runs from the norm to the outcome. Presumptions carry much of the load — the guardián of a thing answers for it (art. 1.193), the principal for his dependants (art. 1.191), the keeper for his animals (art. 1.192) — and moral damage is compensated generously to direct and indirect victims alike (art. 1.196).\n\nWHAT THE U.S. SYSTEM IS. There is no general clause. U.S. tort law is a federation of separate NAMED torts — battery, false imprisonment, IIED, trespass, conversion, negligence, nuisance, defamation, the privacy torts, products liability — each with its own elements, its own threshold gates and its own defences. Before anything else you must CLASSIFY: which tort, which status the entrant held on the land, which type of product defect, which constitutional tier the defamation plaintiff occupies, which doorway an emotional or purely economic harm must fit through. Liability then follows or fails on those gates, not on how wrongful the conduct was overall.\n\nWHAT DIVERGES. Three trained habits generate the wrong answer. (1) Reasoning from wrongfulness-plus-damage straight to liability, skipping the classification step on which every question turns — and treating recklessness as “intent” because the general clause never made you distinguish them. (2) OVER-imputing, because the Code's presumptions have no U.S. counterpart: there is no guardián-de-la-cosa liability, so lending your car creates none absent negligent entrustment or a statute; there is no general duty to rescue however easy the rescue; and premises duties are indexed to the entrant's STATUS rather than to ownership. (3) OVER-granting, because U.S. law rations recovery through categorical gates the Code never drew: emotional harm needs a recognized doorway, pure economic loss is barred outright, punitive damages have no codal analogue, and in defamation recovery becomes HARDER as the plaintiff becomes more public.\n\nWHAT IS THE SAME. Ten of the units are pure convergence, and they are worth banking first: comparative reduction for the victim's own fault (art. 1.189), which likewise does not apply to intentional wrongs; solidarity among co-authors with a right of recourse (art. 1.195); necessity that privileges the entry but still pays for the damage (art. 1.188); the inferential instinct behind res ipsa loquitur (culpa virtual); adequate causation behind proximate cause; lex artis behind the professional standard of care, including the defensible-choice safe harbour; and nuisance as relaciones de vecindad, where ordinary annoyances must be tolerated. Trust those — then put every remaining minute into classification.","families":[{"name":"Negligence","count":54},{"name":"Intentional Torts","count":27},{"name":"Strict Liability","count":8},{"name":"Nuisance","count":6},{"name":"Products Liability","count":6},{"name":"Defenses","count":5},{"name":"Damages","count":4},{"name":"Defamation","count":4},{"name":"Privacy","count":4},{"name":"Vicarious Liability","count":4},{"name":"Special Defendants","count":3},{"name":"Misrepresentation","count":1}],"units":[{"n":1,"summary":"Harmful or offensive conduct is analyzed in a U.S. exam: the answer turns on WHICH nominate intentional tort fits and whether its intent element is met — not on a general wrongfulness clause.","trigger":"Intentional-tort question + selection among nominate torts + intent (purpose or substantial certainty) + a voluntary act + transferred intent.","rule":"U.S. law recognizes discrete nominate intentional torts (battery, assault, false imprisonment, IIED, trespass to land, trespass to chattels, conversion), each with its own elements. Intent means purpose to cause the contact/apprehension/confinement OR knowledge to a substantial certainty that it will result; recklessness is not intent. The act must be voluntary (an involuntary spasm or vomit is not an act). Under transferred intent, intent aimed at one person or one of the five trespass-writ torts transfers to the actual victim or tort.","sub":"Intentional Torts – Architecture: Nominate Torts, Intent, Voluntary Act, Transferred Intent (Master)","sensitivity":"Three gates decide most questions: (1) voluntariness of the act; (2) purpose OR substantial certainty — prolonged near-misses (a 20-minute game of catch over a busy street) still fail if neither is shown; (3) transferred intent rescues the 'wrong victim' cases. IIED is outside the transferred-intent list.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the conduct was wrongful and caused harm — Article 1.185-style liability follows” → the U.S. system has NO general clause; liability must be routed through a specific nominate tort whose elements are strictly parsed. The civilian hecho ilícito schema (one unified clause covering intent, negligence and abuse of right) produces two errors: skipping the tort-selection step entirely, and treating recklessness as sufficient 'intent' because the general clause never required the distinction.","authority":"Intentional-tort architecture: Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 2, 8A (intent), 13–14; transferred intent doctrine (five trespass-writ torts; IIED excluded)."},{"n":2,"summary":"A contact or threat reaches the victim indirectly (object intimately connected to the body, thrown object) or the victim's awareness is in issue (unloaded gun, threat from behind).","trigger":"Battery: harmful/offensive contact with person or anything intimately connected + no consent. Assault: reasonable apprehension of imminent contact, perceived by the plaintiff.","rule":"Battery includes contact with objects intimately connected to the person (a phone in the hand, a plate snatched), and the victim need not be aware of the contact. Assault is the mirror image: it requires the plaintiff's contemporaneous awareness/apprehension of the imminent contact — a gun the victim believes is loaded suffices even if unloaded; a threat the victim never perceives is no assault.","sub":"Intentional Torts – Battery & Assault (Contact and Apprehension Gates)","sensitivity":"The two torts allocate the awareness requirement in opposite directions: battery protects bodily integrity (awareness irrelevant), assault protects mental tranquility (awareness essential). What the DEFENDANT knows (gun unloaded) is irrelevant if the victim reasonably apprehends.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “no injury, no liability” (or “the gun was unloaded, so no wrong”) → battery lies for offensive contact without any physical harm, and assault lies for pure apprehension. The civilian schema needs a daño to anchor liability (Art. 1.185 requires damage); dignitary torts that compensate the touching or the fright ITSELF, with damages presumed, have no codal counterpart.","authority":"Battery & assault: Restatement (Second) §§ 13, 18–21; contact with connected objects (Fisher v. Carrousel Motor Hotel); apprehension gate."},{"n":3,"summary":"A person is confined (or believes they cannot leave), or the confinement happens without their knowledge; the defendant acted deliberately or merely negligently (ski-lift operator forgets a skier).","trigger":"False imprisonment: intentional confinement within boundaries + victim aware of it OR harmed by it + no reasonable means of escape.","rule":"False imprisonment requires (1) an INTENTIONAL act of confinement (negligently leaving someone stranded is not FI — the remedy is negligence), and (2) that the plaintiff was aware of the confinement at the time or was harmed by it. Confinement includes physical barriers, force, threats, and invalid assertion of legal authority; a hidden, unnoticed and harmless confinement is not actionable.","sub":"Intentional Torts – False Imprisonment (Awareness and Intent Gates)","sensitivity":"Two independent kill-switches: defendant-side intent (substantial certainty suffices; negligence never does) and plaintiff-side awareness-or-harm. Exam questions isolate one gate at a time — the sleeping/hiding occupant (no awareness), the forgotten skier (no intent).","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “he was deprived of liberty, so there is liability” → without intent it is only negligence, and without awareness-or-harm it is nothing. The civilian privación ilegítima de libertad instinct is criminal-law flavored and fault-generic; the U.S. tort's twin gates (intent quality + victim perception) must be learned as independent switches.","authority":"False imprisonment: Restatement (Second) §§ 35–42 (awareness-or-harm requirement, § 42)."},{"n":4,"summary":"Outrageous conduct causes severe emotional distress — a cruel prank, an abusive debt collector, mistreatment directed at a family member — with no physical injury.","trigger":"IIED: extreme and outrageous conduct + intent or RECKLESSNESS + severe emotional distress; third parties: presence + defendant's knowledge of presence (+ physical harm if a non-family bystander).","rule":"IIED requires conduct beyond all bounds of decency, intent or recklessness, and severe distress — but NO physical injury. Repetition, abuse of power (debt collectors, common carriers) and exploiting known sensitivity aggravate. A third party (family member) may recover only if present at the scene and the defendant knew of the presence; IIED is excluded from transferred intent.","sub":"Intentional Torts – IIED (Outrage, Recklessness, Third-Party Presence)","sensitivity":"IIED is the only intentional tort satisfied by recklessness — and the only one excluded from transferred intent; the third-party presence rule is the substitute. The no-physical-injury feature contrasts with NIED's gates (Unit 12).","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “this is daño moral — compensable” → broadly right in result: Art. 1.196 C.C. compensates moral damage generously, so the civilian instinct to compensate is sound. The residual friction is structural: the outrageousness threshold (mere insults never qualify), the presence-and-knowledge rule for third parties, and the recklessness/transferred-intent asymmetries are technical gates the daño moral schema never required.","authority":"IIED: Restatement (Second) § 46 (incl. § 46(2) third-party presence rule); recklessness suffices; no physical injury required."},{"n":5,"summary":"A defendant invokes a privilege: self-defense or defense of others under a mistaken belief, recapture of chattels, or entry/restraint driven by necessity.","trigger":"Privilege asserted + reasonableness of force + mistake as to the need + type of privilege (defense of self/others, recapture, necessity).","rule":"Self-defense permits reasonable, proportional force and tolerates reasonable mistake. Defense of others: the traditional rule put the intervenor in the shoes of the person defended (reasonable mistake no excuse); the modern/majority rule privileges reasonable belief. Recapture of chattels allows only reasonable, prompt force after demand. Private necessity is an INCOMPLETE privilege: entry is protected (the landowner may not expel or resist), but the entrant pays for actual damage caused.","sub":"Intentional Torts – Privileges: Defense of Self/Others, Recapture, Necessity (Master)","sensitivity":"Three levers: (1) proportionality of force; (2) who bears the risk of mistake — self-defense forgives it, traditional defense-of-others did not (a jurisdictional fork); (3) necessity's split remedy — privileged entry, compensated damage. The landowner who resists a necessity entrant (dogs on the beach camper) commits battery.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: legítima defensa and estado de necesidad map well — and Art. 1.188 C.C. even matches the pay-for-the-damage rule of private necessity. The instinct is broadly RIGHT. The residual friction: the defense-of-others mistake fork (no codal analogue — the civilian assumes reasonable mistake always excuses, which fails under the traditional rule half of the exam split) and the strict sequencing of recapture (fresh pursuit, demand first).","authority":"Privileges: Restatement (Second) §§ 63–76 (self-defense, defense of others — traditional vs. reasonable-belief split), §§ 100–106 (recapture), §§ 197– (private necessity incomplete privilege, Vincent v. Lake Erie; cf. C.C. art. 1.188)."},{"n":6,"summary":"Entries onto land or interferences with goods: a deflected ball, a caused entry by another person, digging under or flying drones over a parcel, taking the wrong umbrella by mistake.","trigger":"Trespass to land: intentional VOLUNTARY act resulting in entry (intent to enter, not to wrong) + no harm required. Conversion/trespass to chattels: dominion over another's property — mistake is no defense.","rule":"Trespass to land needs only intent to do the act that causes entry — not intent to trespass — and lies without any damage; it covers causing a person or thing to enter, and reaches above and below the surface within reasonable use. An involuntary or unintended deflection is not trespass. Conversion requires substantial dominion (mistake of ownership is NO defense; good faith is irrelevant); lesser interferences are trespass to chattels and require damage or dispossession.","sub":"Intentional Torts – Trespass to Land; Conversion vs. Trespass to Chattels","sensitivity":"Three switches: (1) intent attaches to the ACT, not the wrongfulness — the golfer whose ball deflects lacks even act-intent as to the entry; (2) land trespass needs no damage, chattel trespass does; (3) conversion's severity scale (dominion, duration, good faith irrelevant) decides damages = full value.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “no daño, no liability” and “buena fe excuses the mistaken taker” → both wrong: land trespass is actionable without damage, and conversion ignores good faith. The civilian possessory-protection schema (interdictos; reivindicación with buena fe gradations) grades liability by fault and harm; the U.S. system grades it by the OBJECT of the intent and the DEGREE of dominion.","authority":"Trespass & conversion: Restatement (Second) §§ 158 (intent to enter), 217–222A (conversion factors; mistake no defense)."},{"n":7,"summary":"A statute or ordinance is violated (traffic law, safety code) and a party tries to use the violation to establish — or defeat — negligence.","trigger":"Statutory violation + plaintiff within the protected class + harm of the type the statute aimed to prevent + causation + possible excuse.","rule":"Negligence per se substitutes the statutory standard for the reasonable-person standard ONLY when (1) the plaintiff belongs to the class the statute protects AND (2) the harm is of the type the statute was designed to prevent. The violation still must be an actual and proximate cause; excuses (sudden incapacity, greater danger of compliance) are available. A plaintiff's own statutory violation feeds comparative fault the same way.","sub":"Negligence – Negligence Per Se: Two Gates, Causation, Excuses (Master)","sensitivity":"Four sequential screens: protected class → type of harm (a traffic statute does not cover a slip on ice) → causation (a stationary drunk driver did not cause the rear-end crash) → excuse (sudden unforeseeable incapacity). Failing any screen returns the case to ordinary reasonableness analysis — it does not end it.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “he broke a rule, so he was at fault — culpa established” → the U.S. doctrine filters the violation through class and harm-type gates the general clause never had. In the civilian schema any infracción de norma feeds culpa directly; the trained instinct skips both gates and the residual causation check, which is precisely what these questions test.","authority":"Negligence per se: Restatement (Third) §§ 14–15 (class/harm gates, excuses); causation retained."},{"n":8,"summary":"An accident that 'does not ordinarily happen without negligence' occurs (falling object, foreign object in food or in a patient), but direct evidence of what went wrong is missing.","trigger":"Res ipsa loquitur: event of a kind that ordinarily results from negligence + instrumentality within defendant's exclusive control + plaintiff did not contribute + (multiple defendants → generally fails, except Ybarra-type medical teams).","rule":"Res ipsa permits (not compels) an inference of breach where the event ordinarily bespeaks negligence and the defendant controlled the instrumentality; it fails where several independent actors had successive or concurrent control — EXCEPT the medical-team scenario (unconscious patient, all defendants with control may be called to explain, Ybarra). The defendant may rebut with due-care evidence; res ipsa never directs a verdict for the plaintiff.","sub":"Negligence – Res Ipsa Loquitur and Its Multiple-Defendant Limits (Master)","sensitivity":"Three structural facts flip outcomes: (1) number of actors in control (two possible culprits → doctrine fails; unconscious-patient surgery → exception); (2) the chain of custody (canned goods reaching the table unchanged preserve the inference against the packer); (3) procedural effect — inference only, so summary judgment/directed verdict for plaintiff is never the answer.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian recognizes this as prueba indiciaria / culpa virtual — the inferential instinct is RIGHT. The residual friction is procedural and structural: exclusive control as an element (not just persuasion), the multiple-defendant bar with its narrow medical exception, and the doctrine's inference-only effect within the jury architecture (it survives rebuttal but never compels a verdict).","authority":"Res ipsa loquitur: Restatement (Second) § 328D; Ybarra v. Spangard (medical-team exception); Escola-type chain-of-custody preservation; inference-only effect."},{"n":9,"summary":"Someone fails to act: a bystander does not rescue, a business does not verify third-party ads, an amateur rescuer makes things worse, a landowner's peril was not of his making.","trigger":"Nonfeasance + absence (or presence) of a duty-creating relationship: creation of the peril, voluntary undertaking, special relationship, invitation of rescue.","rule":"There is NO general duty to rescue or aid. Duty arises only if the defendant created the peril (even innocently), voluntarily undertook aid (then must act with reasonable care — the negligent rescuer is liable), stands in a special relationship (carrier, innkeeper, custodian), or where a statute imposes it. 'Danger invites rescue': the negligent creator of a peril owes a duty to injured rescuers.","sub":"Negligence – Duty: No General Duty to Aid and Its Exceptions (Master)","sensitivity":"The analysis is binary and sequential: classify the conduct as nonfeasance (default: no duty) vs. misfeasance (default: reasonable care), then scan the exception menu. The coyote-attack pattern (peril not created by defendant → no duty despite easy rescue) and the undertaking pattern (started to help → full duty) sit at the poles.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “failing to help when help was easy is itself fault” → not in U.S. tort law. The civilian arrives from omisión de socorro (a criminal duty to aid) and a general clause that can read callous omission as culpa; the American no-duty baseline — and the exam's insistence that moral outrage does not create legal duty — is among the most counterintuitive rules in the subject.","authority":"No general duty to aid: Restatement (Second) §§ 314–324 (undertakings, special relationships, creation of peril); 'danger invites rescue' (Wagner v. International Ry.); child/adult-activity standard."},{"n":10,"summary":"An entrant on land is injured: a customer in a theater, a social guest near a hidden danger, a hunter crossing a farm, a police officer or firefighter on duty, a customer who wanders into a staff-only area.","trigger":"Entrant status (invitee / licensee / trespasser) + duty tier attached to the status + status changes by area or purpose + public-safety officers.","rule":"Invitees (business/public purpose): duty to inspect, warn and make safe. Licensees (social guests): duty to warn of KNOWN hidden dangers — no duty to inspect, none for open or unknown dangers (knowledge acquired later triggers the duty then). Trespassers: only no willful/wanton injury (discovered/child trespassers aside). Status shifts with the area entered (members-only zone converts a patron into a trespasser). Firefighters and police are licensees, and the firefighter's rule bars recovery ONLY for the inherent risks of the emergency they attend — not for unrelated ordinary negligence.","sub":"Negligence – Premises Liability: The Status Trichotomy (Master)","sensitivity":"Everything hangs on classification at the moment and place of injury: the same wet floor produces three different duties. Two refinements decide hard cases: knowledge-timing for licensees (the host who learns of the danger mid-visit) and the firefighter's rule limit (the parked officer struck by a shortcut-taking driver recovers).","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the owner is responsible for dangers on his property” → the U.S. rule is status-indexed, not ownership-indexed. The civilian guardián-de-la-cosa schema (Art. 1.193: objective liability of the keeper) knows no entrant taxonomy; the trichotomy — and outcomes that flip on which side of a 'staff only' sign the plaintiff stood — must be built from zero.","authority":"Premises liability: status trichotomy (invitee/licensee/trespasser); firefighter's rule and its inherent-risk limit; area-based status shifts."},{"n":11,"summary":"After the defendant's negligent act, something else contributes to the harm — negligent medical treatment, a rescuer, a later accident, or a third party's intentional crime — and the defendant argues the chain of causation was broken.","trigger":"Negligence + intervening force between breach and injury + question whether the intervening force was foreseeable (intervening) or unforeseeable/extraordinary (superseding).","rule":"A defendant is liable for all foreseeable results of the breach, including foreseeable intervening forces: negligent medical treatment and its aggravation, rescuers ('danger invites rescue'), and subsequent injuries flowing from the weakened condition. Unforeseeable, extraordinary forces are superseding and cut off liability: intentional or criminal third-party acts (unless the defendant's negligence created that very risk), truly extraordinary malpractice, and suicide (absent an irresistible-impulse showing). Serving alcohol to an intoxicated patron is a concurring — not superseded — cause of the later crash.","sub":"Negligence – Proximate Cause: Intervening vs. Superseding (Master)","sensitivity":"One variable decides every case: the FORESEEABILITY of the intervening force, judged from the risk the defendant created. The table has two columns — foreseeable (medical negligence, rescue, weakened-condition accidents, criminal acts the negligence invited) and superseding (unprovoked crime, extraordinary misconduct, deliberate suicide).","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian causalidad adecuada and hecho de un tercero doctrines ask essentially the same question, and the instinct usually lands correctly. The residual friction is calibration: U.S. law treats negligent medical aggravation as ALWAYS within the risk (the civilian may cut the chain too early) while treating unprovoked intentional crime as superseding (the civilian general clause may keep the first actor liable too long).","authority":"Proximate cause: Palsgraf/foreseeability framework; intervening vs. superseding forces; dram shop concurring causation."},{"n":12,"summary":"Someone suffers serious emotional distress without physical impact — a near-miss victim, a family member watching an accident, a relative receiving mishandled remains — and sues for negligent infliction of emotional distress (NIED).","trigger":"Emotional harm from negligence + plaintiff outside physical impact + one of three doorways: zone of danger, bystander, or special situation.","rule":"NIED requires the plaintiff to fit a recognized doorway: (1) zone of danger — plaintiff feared for his own safety (minority requires this even for bystanders); (2) bystander recovery — majority (Dillon) allows a plaintiff closely related to the victim, present at the scene, who contemporaneously observes the injury; (3) special situations — negligent mishandling of a corpse or false report of a relative's death allow recovery without either test. Most jurisdictions also require resulting physical manifestations except in the special situations.","sub":"Negligence – NIED: Zone of Danger / Bystander / Special Situations","sensitivity":"The claim lives or dies on doorway selection, not on the reality of the distress: a fiancé fails the close-relationship gate, a parent who arrives late fails contemporaneous observation, and the corpse/telegram cases skip the gates entirely.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the anguish is real and provable, so daño moral is owed” → U.S. law rations recovery through categorical doorways. Article 1.196 of the Civil Code grants moral damages broadly to direct and indirect victims; the civilian over-grants systematically — the learning is not whether emotional harm counts but WHICH structural doorway the plaintiff fits.","authority":"NIED: zone of danger; Dillon v. Legg bystander factors (close relative, presence, contemporaneous observation); special situations (corpse mishandling, false death report)."},{"n":13,"summary":"A negligent act causes only financial loss — lost profits, a devalued product, a missed opportunity — with no personal injury or damage to other property, and the plaintiff sues in negligence.","trigger":"Negligence claim + purely economic loss + no physical injury or property damage to something other than the product/subject itself.","rule":"The economic loss doctrine bars negligence recovery for purely economic loss unaccompanied by physical injury or property damage. In products cases, a product that merely fails or damages only itself gives rise to warranty/contract remedies (UCC), not tort. Where physical harm exists, economic consequences of that harm are recoverable as parasitic damages.","sub":"Negligence – Economic Loss Doctrine (Tort/Contract Boundary)","sensitivity":"The gate is the KIND of harm, not its size: a $1 physical injury opens the tort door to consequential economics, while a $1M pure economic loss stays in contract. In products litigation the doctrine polices the tort/warranty border — cross-reference the Contracts module (buyer's warranty remedies).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “daño patrimonial is fully compensable under the general clause — lost profits included” → in U.S. negligence law, pure economic loss is categorically excluded. Article 1.185's unified reparation of any daño makes this gate invisible; the civilian files a hopeless negligence claim where the real remedy sits in contract/warranty — the tort/contract BORDER is itself the doctrine.","authority":"Economic loss doctrine: no negligence recovery for pure economic loss; East River rationale in products (damage to product itself = warranty). Cross-module: Contracts buyer's remedies."},{"n":14,"summary":"Two or more actors contribute to one injury — simultaneous negligence producing an indivisible harm, a drag race, two hunters and one pellet, or an unidentified wrongdoer among several — and the court must allocate liability.","trigger":"Multiple potential tortfeasors + indivisible injury OR concerted action OR inability to identify which actor caused the harm.","rule":"Concurrent tortfeasors whose negligence combines to produce an indivisible injury are jointly and severally liable, as are parties acting in concert. Alternative liability (Summers v. Tice) shifts the burden to the defendants only when ALL of them were negligent and one certainly caused the harm; if the plaintiff cannot show every defendant was at fault — or cannot bring them all before the court — the claim fails for want of actual causation. A defendant who pays may seek contribution (proportional) from co-tortfeasors, or full indemnity when only vicariously liable or a non-manufacturing seller in the distribution chain.","sub":"Negligence – Multiple Tortfeasors: J&S, Concert, Alternative Liability (Master)","sensitivity":"Three distinct engines that exams deliberately blur: (1) indivisible injury → J&S without identification; (2) concert → all participants liable for the foreseeable acts of each; (3) alternative liability → burden shift ONLY with all-defendants-negligent + all-before-the-court. The baseline never moves: no identification and no qualifying doctrine = plaintiff loses.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: Article 1.195 (solidaridad entre coautores del hecho ilícito) and the acción de regreso map remarkably well onto J&S liability and contribution — the civilian instinct is structurally right. The residual friction is the burden-shifting machinery: Summers-style alternative liability is a common-law construct with precise gates, and the civilian tends to extend solidarity to defendants whose fault was never individually proven.","authority":"Multiple tortfeasors: joint & several liability (indivisible injury); acting in concert; Summers v. Tice alternative liability; contribution vs. indemnity."},{"n":15,"summary":"An employer, principal, or vehicle owner is sued for a tort committed by an employee, contractor, borrower, or family member — and the question is whose conduct the law imputes to whom.","trigger":"Tort by one actor + attempt to hold another liable by relationship (employment, agency, ownership, family) rather than by personal fault.","rule":"An employer is vicariously liable for employee torts within the scope of employment; the ordinary commute is outside it (coming-and-going rule). There is no vicarious liability for independent contractors except non-delegable duties and inherently dangerous activities. Mere ownership imputes nothing: lending a car creates liability only through negligent entrustment, a family-car/permissive-use statute, or the owner's own negligence — and one spouse or friend is not liable for another's torts absent agency. Direct theories (negligent hiring/supervision) remain available for conduct outside the scope.","sub":"Vicarious Liability – Scope, Contractors, Owners (Master)","sensitivity":"Imputation runs through defined channels, not general responsibility for one's 'dependents' or property: scope-of-employment (with the commute carve-out), the contractor exceptions, and statute/entrustment for vehicles. Each channel has its own limit, and direct negligence (hiring, supervision, entrustment) survives even where imputation fails.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the owner/principal answers for the thing and the dependent” (Arts. 1.191–1.193: dueños y principales, guardián de la cosa) → U.S. law has NO guardian-of-the-thing liability: the car owner who merely lends is NOT liable absent entrustment or statute. The civilian systematically over-imputes — this is one of the sharpest wrong-answer generators in the subject.","authority":"Vicarious liability: respondeat superior; coming-and-going rule; independent contractors + inherently dangerous/non-delegable exceptions; negligent entrustment; family-car statutes."},{"n":16,"summary":"A defendant engages in an activity that cannot be made safe — blasting, fumigation with toxic gas, chemical storage, fireworks — and someone is harmed; or the defendant keeps a wild animal that frightens or injures someone.","trigger":"Abnormally dangerous activity (or wild animal) + harm within the characteristic risk + plaintiff seeks liability without proving negligence.","rule":"Strict liability attaches to abnormally dangerous activities — those creating a foreseeable and highly significant risk of harm even with reasonable care, not commonly engaged in — and to wild animals (including fright reactions). The liability covers only harm within the activity's characteristic risk (blasting that frightens mink into killing their young is outside the explosion-and-debris risk — Foster v. Preston Mill); regulatory compliance and reasonable care are NOT defenses, but assumption of the risk is, and comparative-fault regimes reduce recovery. Ordinary activities — fire, driving — remain governed by negligence.","sub":"Strict Liability – Abnormally Dangerous Activities & Wild Animals (Master)","sensitivity":"Three gates: activity qualifies → harm within the characteristic risk → defenses limited to plaintiff-conduct doctrines. The harm-within-risk gate does the exam work (frightened mink vs. blast debris); the compliance-is-no-defense point defeats the best-drafted wrong answers. Boundary map: wild animals and blasting = strict; fire and common carriers = high care but negligence.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the guardián de la cosa (Art. 1.193) and animal-keeper liability (Art. 1.192) give the civilian a working strict-liability instinct — usually right on outcome. The calibration friction: U.S. strict liability is ACTIVITY-specific with a harm-within-risk gate, while Art. 1.193 is thing-based; and the fire rule flips (Art. 1.193 in fine requires fault — as does U.S. law — but the civilian guardian instinct may impose strictness there too).","authority":"Abnormally dangerous activities: Restatement (Third) § 20; wild animals (incl. fright reactions); harm-within-the-risk limit; compliance no defense; fire = negligence."},{"n":17,"summary":"A person injured by a product sues everyone in the distribution chain — manufacturer, wholesaler, retailer — claiming the product was defective when it left the defendant's control.","trigger":"Injury from a product + defendant is a commercial seller in the chain + defect existing when the product left that defendant's hands + defect type identified.","rule":"Strict products liability requires (1) a commercial seller/distributor — casual sellers and pure service providers are out, but every link in the chain is in; (2) a defect when the product left the defendant's control; and (3) causation of physical harm. Defect types: manufacturing (departs from intended design), design (a reasonable alternative design existed — risk/utility), and warning (failure to warn of non-obvious risks of foreseeable use, INCLUDING foreseeable misuse). Foreseeable bystanders and other drivers are protected plaintiffs; privity is not required.","sub":"Products Liability – Elements & Defect Types (Master)","sensitivity":"Answer discipline: identify the defect TYPE first — each has its own test — then check the chain (commercial seller?) and timing (defect when it left THIS defendant?). Warning claims absorb foreseeable-misuse facts; design claims need the reasonable alternative.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “productos defectuosos = responsabilidad objetiva del fabricante” gets the headline right, but the civilian consumer-protection schema (LDPABIS-style, direct against the producer) misses the three-test defect taxonomy and the every-link-in-the-chain rule — and turns warning/design questions into generic 'defect' questions, which is where the distractors live.","authority":"Products liability elements & defect types: Restatement (Third) of Torts: Products Liability §§ 1–2; commercial-seller requirement; manufacturing/design (reasonable alternative)/warning; bystanders protected."},{"n":18,"summary":"The product-liability defendant answers: the user modified the machine, a retailer swapped the component, the buyer used it in a bizarre way, or the plaintiff was careless — and the court sorts real defenses from false ones.","trigger":"Products claim + post-sale conduct by someone else (modification, alteration, misuse) or plaintiff's own conduct.","rule":"Substantial post-sale modification or alteration — by the buyer, a retailer swapping components, or a third party — defeats liability because the defect did not exist when the product left the defendant's control. UNforeseeable misuse is a defense; foreseeable misuse is not (it must be designed/warned against). Ordinary contributory negligence (failing to discover the defect) is NOT a defense to strict liability, but assumption of the risk is, and comparative-fault jurisdictions apportion. A non-negligent retailer held strictly liable has full indemnity up the chain against the manufacturer.","sub":"Products Liability – Defenses, Alteration & Indemnity","sensitivity":"Two sorting rules do all the work: (1) WHOSE conduct — changes by others after leaving the defendant's control break the case; the plaintiff's own conduct only helps if it rises to assumption of risk or comparative fault; (2) FORESEEABILITY — misuse and even carelessness that the seller should anticipate stay inside liability. Indemnity flows up the chain to the culpable link.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “la culpa de la víctima siempre reduce; cualquier alteración exonera” → U.S. law splits finer: contributory carelessness is irrelevant in strict liability while assumption of risk bars, and only SUBSTANTIAL unforeseeable alteration exonerates. The civilian compensación de culpas instinct (Art. 1.189) misgrades both switches.","authority":"Products defenses: substantial alteration; unforeseeable misuse; contributory negligence inapplicable vs. assumption of risk; indemnity up the chain."},{"n":19,"summary":"A plaintiff sues over a false and damaging statement; the analysis depends on who the plaintiff is (public official/figure vs. private person) and whether the matter is of public concern.","trigger":"Defamatory statement + publication + plaintiff classification + matter classification → select the fault standard and damages regime.","rule":"Slander per se (crime, loathsome disease, business/profession incompetence, serious sexual misconduct) dispenses with proof of special damages. Constitutionally: a public official or public figure must prove actual malice (knowledge of falsity or reckless disregard); a private figure on a matter of public concern need show only negligence (Gertz) — but then recovers only actual damages, with presumed/punitive damages requiring actual malice; purely private matters escape the constitutional overlay.","sub":"Defamation – The Constitutional Ladder (Master)","sensitivity":"Two classification steps select everything downstream: plaintiff type × matter type → fault standard → damages regime. The per se categories operate one level below, solving only the special-damages requirement. Misclassify the plaintiff and every later step is wrong.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “el honor se protege — difamación probada, indemnización debida” → the First Amendment inverts the civilian hierarchy: the more public the plaintiff and the matter, the HARDER recovery becomes. A constitutional privilege graded by plaintiff status has no analogue in the civil-law protection of honor (and its criminal-law backbone); the civilian instinct systematically under-protects the speaker.","authority":"Defamation: slander per se categories; New York Times v. Sullivan (actual malice); Gertz v. Robert Welch (private figure/public concern); damages regimes."},{"n":20,"summary":"Someone's image, name, private life, or seclusion is used or exposed — a photo in an ad, publication of private facts, snooping into records — and the plaintiff invokes the privacy torts.","trigger":"Interference with privacy interests + classify which of the four privacy torts fits + check the defenses (newsworthiness, public place, consent).","rule":"Four distinct torts: (1) appropriation / right of publicity — unauthorized commercial use of name, likeness, or other indicia of identity; (2) intrusion upon seclusion — intentional invasion of a private place or affairs, highly offensive to a reasonable person (no publication needed); (3) public disclosure of private facts — barred where the matter is newsworthy or occurred in public; (4) false light. Newsworthiness and public location defeat disclosure claims; identifiability is required for appropriation.","sub":"Privacy – The Four Torts & Their Defenses","sensitivity":"Classification first: each tort has different elements (publication needed? offensiveness? commercial use?) and different defenses. The newsworthy/public-place defense kills disclosure claims that would succeed as intrusion, and vice versa.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the derecho a la propia imagen and constitutional privacy give the civilian strong instincts — appropriation and intrusion usually land correctly (identifiability converges). The calibration friction: newsworthiness and the public-place rule cut far deeper into U.S. privacy than the civilian expects — the celebrity photographed in public loses claims the civil-law schema would grant.","authority":"Privacy: Prosser's four torts (appropriation/right of publicity, intrusion, public disclosure, false light); newsworthiness and public-place defenses; indicia of identity."},{"n":21,"summary":"A neighbor complains of odors, noise, or an ugly structure; a city sues over community-wide harm; the defendant argues the plaintiff moved in next to a pre-existing operation.","trigger":"Interference with use and enjoyment of land + classification (private vs. public) + remedy selection.","rule":"Private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with another's use and enjoyment of land, judged by the ordinary person — a hypersensitive plaintiff's reaction does not qualify, and mere aesthetic displeasure is generally not actionable. Public nuisance is an unreasonable interference with rights common to the community (brought by public authorities, or privately only with special injury). Injunctions issue on a balancing of the equities; 'coming to the nuisance' is a factor, not an absolute defense.","sub":"Nuisance – Private / Public; Remedies (Master)","sensitivity":"Three screens decide most questions: (1) substantial AND unreasonable measured objectively (hypersensitivity and aesthetics fail the substantiality screen); (2) who may sue the public variant; (3) remedy — damages vs. balanced injunction, with priority in time relevant but not dispositive.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian recognizes this terrain — nuisance maps onto relaciones de vecindad / inmisiones and the instinct that neighbors must tolerate ordinary annoyances is broadly RIGHT. The calibration friction: the objective 'ordinary person' substantiality screen (hypersensitive plaintiffs lose), the private/public split with its special-injury standing rule, and treating priority in time as merely one equity factor — three refinements the codal intuition does not supply.","authority":"Nuisance: Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 821B (public), 821D–822 (private), 826–828 (unreasonableness/balancing); 'coming to the nuisance' as factor, § 840D."},{"n":22,"summary":"A plaintiff seeks punitive damages, resists deduction of insurance payments, suffers unexpectedly severe harm from a minor impact, or dies before judgment.","trigger":"Damages doctrines beyond compensation: punitive threshold + collateral source + preexisting vulnerability + survival of claims.","rule":"Punitive damages require willful, wanton, or malicious conduct — ordinary negligence never suffices. Under the traditional collateral source rule, payments from independent sources (insurance) do not reduce the defendant's liability. Under the eggshell-plaintiff rule, once breach and causation are established the defendant takes the victim as found and pays for the full, unforeseeable extent of the harm. Survival statutes preserve the decedent's claim for the estate, subject to the limitations period running from the injury and reduced by the decedent's comparative fault.","sub":"Damages – Punitive; Collateral Source; Eggshell Plaintiff; Survival (Master)","sensitivity":"Sequencing is the trap the exam tests: eggshell operates at the DAMAGES stage only — it never substitutes for breach or causation (a no-negligence defendant owes nothing however fragile the victim). Punitive damages attach to a conduct grade, not to harm size. The collateral source rule tolerates what looks like double recovery.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “no enrichment without cause — deduct the insurance; and severe harm implies serious fault” → both instincts fail. Punitive damages have no civil-law analogue (daño moral compensates, it does not punish); the collateral source rule offends the compensatio lucri instinct; and the eggshell rule's full-extent liability converges with civilian integral reparation — but only AFTER fault is independently established, a sequencing the reflex skips.","authority":"Damages: punitive threshold (willful/wanton); collateral source rule (traditional); eggshell plaintiff (Restatement (Second) § 461); survival statutes and limitations."},{"n":23,"summary":"The plaintiff's own conduct contributed to the injury — by negligence, by knowingly accepting a risk, or both — and the question asks what survives and how much.","trigger":"Plaintiff's contributory fault or assumption of risk + governing regime (traditional vs. comparative) + type of underlying tort.","rule":"At traditional common law, contributory negligence completely barred recovery and assumption of risk (express or implied: known, appreciated, voluntarily encountered SPECIFIC risk) was a separate complete defense. Under pure comparative fault (the UBE default absent contrary instruction), the plaintiff's share — including statutory violations qualifying as negligence per se — merely reduces recovery, fault is allocated among all parties, and implied assumption of risk is generally merged into the comparison. Neither doctrine reduces liability for INTENTIONAL torts; assumption of risk (not contributory negligence) is the conduct defense to strict liability.","sub":"Defenses – Contributory / Comparative Fault; Assumption of Risk (Master)","sensitivity":"Three dials: (1) the regime — read the call of the question for the jurisdiction; UBE default = pure comparative; (2) the underlying tort — intentional torts ignore plaintiff fault; strict liability takes AoR but not contributory negligence; (3) AoR's specificity requirement — the risk appreciated must be the one that materialized.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: compensación de culpas (C.C. art. 1.189) gives the civilian the comparative-reduction instinct — broadly RIGHT for the modern default, and art. 1.189's inapplicability to intentional wrongs converges too. The calibration friction: the traditional complete-bar track still tested on the exam, assumption of risk as a doctrinally separate defense with its own elements, and the strict-liability asymmetry (AoR yes, contributory no) — distinctions the single codal dial does not draw.","authority":"Plaintiff-conduct defenses: contributory negligence (complete bar, traditional); pure comparative fault (UBE default); assumption of risk (express/implied; specific-risk requirement); no reduction for intentional torts; AoR (not contributory negligence) defends strict liability."},{"n":24,"summary":"A defendant with superior knowledge (trainer, expert, professional) states an 'opinion' that induces the plaintiff to pay or act; the plaintiff sues in deceit / fraudulent misrepresentation.","trigger":"False statement + scienter + intent to induce reliance + justifiable reliance + damages; statement framed as opinion.","rule":"Fraudulent misrepresentation (deceit) requires a false representation of material fact made with scienter and intent to induce reliance, justifiable reliance, and pecuniary damages. Statements of opinion are generally not actionable — but an opinion from a speaker with superior knowledge or expertise, or one implying undisclosed facts, is treated as actionable.","sub":"Misrepresentation – Deceit; Opinion by Experts","sensitivity":"The fact/opinion line moves with the speaker's expertise — the same sentence is puffery from a peer and actionable deceit from an expert. Cross-module: the identical friction appears in Contracts Unit 43 (rescission for expert-opinion misrepresentation) — one schema, two remedial doors (tort damages vs. contract avoidance).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “either it is dolo (intentional deception) or mere opinion — and opinions never bind” → the U.S. line is speaker-indexed, not statement-indexed. The civilian dolo schema (C.C. art. 1.154) captures intentional deception but has no developed puffery/expert-opinion gradation; the trained instinct either over-claims (any exaggeration = dolo) or under-claims (missing that expert opinions ground deceit).","authority":"Deceit / fraudulent misrepresentation: Restatement (Second) §§ 525–526, 538A–539 (opinion; superior knowledge). Cross-module: Contracts Unit 43 (rescission track)."},{"n":25,"summary":"The defendant is not an ordinary private actor: the federal government, a common carrier, a bailee, or an online platform running third-party ads.","trigger":"Special-defendant regimes that shift the liability standard up or down from the ordinary negligence baseline.","rule":"Under the FTCA the United States is liable only for NEGLIGENT or wrongful acts — strict liability theories (including abnormally dangerous activities) are excluded. Common carriers owe the highest practicable care to passengers but are not insurers — negligence is still required. A bailee owes care scaled to the bailment's benefit and is liable for negligence, not for mere minor deviations without fault. Platforms/publishers owe no duty to investigate third-party advertisers absent red flags.","sub":"Special Defendants – FTCA; Carriers; Bailees; Platforms","sensitivity":"Each regime is a calibration of the SAME negligence baseline: sovereign defendant → negligence only (never strict); carrier → elevated degree of the same duty; bailee → duty scaled by benefit; platform → no affirmative investigation duty. The exam tests whether the candidate holds the baseline while adjusting the dial.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the carrier guarantees the passenger's safety (obligación de seguridad de resultado) and the State answers like anyone else” → both dials are set differently in U.S. law. The civilian contractual safety obligation over-imposes on carriers (insurer-like liability), while sovereign immunity + FTCA's negligence-only waiver is stricter than the civilian State-liability schema — the reflex misses in opposite directions on the same page.","authority":"Special defendants: FTCA, 28 U.S.C. §§ 1346(b), 2674 (negligence only; Laird v. Nelms); common carriers (highest practicable care, not insurers); bailments (benefit-scaled duty); platforms (no duty to investigate advertisers absent red flags)."},{"n":26,"summary":"The standard of care itself is in issue: an industry followed custom, a professional chose between accepted schools, a minor drove a motorboat, a doctor made a defensible choice that turned out badly.","trigger":"Selection and content of the standard of care: custom evidence + professional standard + minors + adult activities.","rule":"The baseline is the reasonably prudent person under the circumstances. Industry custom is evidence of due care but never conclusive — an entire industry may lag (The T.J. Hooper). Professionals are judged by the knowledge and skill of members of the profession in good standing; choosing one of two accepted approaches is not a breach even if hindsight favors the other. Children are held to a child's standard — EXCEPT when engaged in adult activities (driving, boating), where the full adult standard applies.","sub":"Negligence – Standards of Care (Custom; Professionals; Minors) (Master)","sensitivity":"Three substitution rules on one baseline: custom informs but cannot cap the standard; the professional standard substitutes peer practice for the lay baseline (with the two-schools safe harbor); the child standard is displaced entirely by the adult-activity exception. Misreading WHICH standard governs decides the question before any breach analysis begins.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: lex artis gives the civilian the professional-standard instinct — broadly RIGHT, including the defensible-choice safe harbor. The calibration friction: custom's non-conclusive status (the codal reflex treats compliance with reglamento/práctica as exculpatory — cf. the per se inverse in Unit 7), and the adult-activity exception for minors, which has no analogue in the discernimiento-based capacity rules (C.C. art. 1.186).","authority":"Standards of care: The T.J. Hooper (custom not conclusive); professional standard and two-schools doctrine; child standard and adult-activity exception."},{"n":27,"summary":"A tort victim dies; the estate and the family both seek recovery, and the answer requires sorting which losses travel through which statutory vehicle.","trigger":"Victim's death + claim by estate (survival) vs. statutory beneficiaries (wrongful death) + damages classification + defenses derived from the decedent's own conduct.","rule":"At common law, tort claims died with the person; both modern vehicles are purely statutory and construed by their terms. A survival statute preserves the decedent's own accrued claim for the estate (pre-death pain and suffering, medical expenses, lost earnings to death). A wrongful death statute creates a NEW claim for designated beneficiaries' own pecuniary losses (support, services, consortium), generally free of the estate's creditors. Both are derivative as to liability: defenses good against the decedent (comparative fault, assumption of risk) reduce or bar recovery in either action.","sub":"Death of a Party – Wrongful Death vs. Survival Actions","sensitivity":"Two independent vehicles, one liability analysis. The exam work is damage-sorting — pre-death losses ride the survival claim; the beneficiaries' losses ride wrongful death — plus the derivative-defense point (the decedent's 40% fault follows both claims). Complements Unit 22 (survival timing and comparative-fault mechanics). Supplemental unit — no source-unit antecedent.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the heirs continue the victim's action and add their own daño moral” → Venezuelan practice runs both recoveries through one general-clause analysis: heirs continue the de cujus's claim by succession and claim moral damages iure proprio as indirect victims (Art. 1.196). The U.S. system splits recovery into two statutes with different plaintiffs, different damages menus, and different distribution rules — and the baseline it displaces is NO recovery for death at common law. The translation “heirs' action” obscures the vehicle split, producing misallocated damages on the exam.","authority":"Wrongful death & survival: statutory vehicles (Lord Campbell's Act model); Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 925–926; derivative defenses. Supplemental unit authored at module-completion review (gap identified against MBE coverage; no source-unit antecedent)."},{"n":28,"summary":"A defamatory statement is made in a protected setting (court, legislature, between spouses) or on a protected occasion (job reference, report to police), and the defendant invokes privilege.","trigger":"Defamation elements met + the occasion or setting of the statement + whether the privilege was abused.","rule":"Absolute privileges — statements by participants in judicial proceedings (if pertinent to the matter), legislators in session, high executive officers in the course of duties, between spouses — defeat liability regardless of malice. Qualified privileges attach to occasions serving a legitimate interest (the speaker's, the recipient's, or a common interest: employment references, reports to law enforcement, fair report of official proceedings) and are LOST by abuse — statements outside the occasion's purpose, excessive publication, or actual malice/knowledge of falsity. The defendant bears the burden of establishing the privilege; the plaintiff, of proving abuse.","sub":"Defamation – Common-Law Privileges (Absolute vs. Qualified)","sensitivity":"Classification decides whether malice matters at all: absolute privilege ends the case; qualified privilege converts it into an abuse analysis with a shifted burden. Operates below the constitutional ladder of Unit 19 — check the common-law privilege even when the constitutional standard is satisfied. Supplemental unit — no source-unit antecedent.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “an insult remains injuria — intent to harm creates liability wherever spoken” → immunity attached to the SETTING or OCCASION, independent of the speaker's good faith, has no analogue in the civil-law protection of honor, where animus injuriandi and truth dominate and courtroom statements enjoy at most limited excuses. The civilian under-protects the speaker exactly where U.S. law grants categorical immunity.","authority":"Defamation privileges: Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 583–612 (absolute privileges; qualified privileges; abuse). Supplemental unit authored at module-completion review (no source-unit antecedent)."},{"n":29,"summary":"After winning (or while defending) a prior case, a party sues the person who set the legal machinery in motion — for groundless initiation, or for twisting validly issued process toward an ulterior end.","trigger":"Prior proceeding instituted without probable cause + improper purpose + favorable termination (malicious prosecution) vs. properly issued process used primarily for an ulterior purpose + a willful act in its use (abuse of process).","rule":"Malicious prosecution requires institution of criminal (majority: also civil) proceedings without probable cause, with malice or an improper purpose, termination on the merits in the accused's favor, and damages; public prosecutors are absolutely immune. Abuse of process lies for the use of legally issued process primarily to accomplish an ulterior purpose, plus a willful act in the use of the process — probable cause and favorable termination are NOT required and the underlying case's outcome is irrelevant.","sub":"Wrongful Litigation – Malicious Prosecution & Abuse of Process","sensitivity":"The pair splits on which stage is abused: wrongful INITIATION (malicious prosecution — favorable-termination and no-probable-cause gates) versus wrongful USE of valid process (abuse of process — neither gate). Cross-assigning the elements between the two torts is the standard distractor. Supplemental unit — no source-unit antecedent.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “this is abuso de derecho — Art. 1.185 (single aparte) makes abusive litigation compensable once bad faith is shown” → the civilian abuse-of-right clause gives the right instinct on compensability but has none of the procedural gates: the trained reflex over-claims where the prior proceeding has not terminated favorably, and misses that abuse of process needs no groundlessness at all — only the ulterior-purpose-plus-willful-act pairing.","authority":"Malicious prosecution / abuse of process: Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 653–681B (wrongful criminal and civil proceedings); § 682 (abuse of process). Supplemental unit authored at module-completion review (no source-unit antecedent)."}],"cases":[{"n":1,"summary":"Construction company uses dynamite to blast deep rock while preparing a building site. Despite reasonable care, a flying rock fragment injures a nearby spectator who is lawfully present.","trigger":"Highly dangerous activities like blasting can trigger strict liability even without negligence.","rule":"Blasting with dynamite is likely to be an abnormally dangerous activity, meaning that the defendants can be strictly liable despite using a proper standard of care.","sub":"Abnormally Dangerous Activities","family":"Strict Liability","unit":16},{"n":2,"summary":"Zip lining; hornet nest known; waiver signed; man allergic and injured.","trigger":"Assumption of risk requires plaintiff understand the specific danger.","rule":"Assumption of risk requires plaintiff appreciate the scope of risk when signing waiver.","sub":"Assumption of Risk","family":"Defenses","unit":23},{"n":3,"summary":"Man buys cookie dough with a clear label: “PLEASE DO NOT EAT RAW COOKIE DOUGH.” He reads it but still eats half the package raw, thinking the worst is a stomachache, and gets salmonella. He sues supermarket for warning defect in strict liability.","trigger":"Assumption of risk only applies if the plaintiff actually knew the specific risk and voluntarily took it on.","rule":"Under Defect Warning in a strict product liability case although assumption of the risk bars recovery, a plaintiff must be aware of the specific risks and voluntarily encounter them to assume the risk. Unforeseeable misuse is a defense, MISUSE is not!, Comparative negligence, but NOT contributory!","sub":"Assumption of Risk / Warnings","family":"Products Liability","unit":17},{"n":4,"summary":"Man riding his brother’s motorcycle cuts between cars; angry driver intentionally rams the bike from behind, severely damaging it. Man is unharmed but wants compensatory and punitive damages.","trigger":"Using force against an object intimately connected to the person (like a vehicle being ridden) can be battery.","rule":"A battery can be indirect, such as situations where contact is made with an item that is intimately connected to the plaintiff.","sub":"Battery / Indirect Contact","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":1},{"n":5,"summary":"Best friends meet weekly for lunch. Woman watches from a pizza shop as her male friend is hit by a negligent driver, faints, injures herself, and later has severe emotional distress. They are very close friends, not family.","trigger":"Bystander NIED usually requires a close family relationship, not just friendship.","rule":"For negligent infliction of emotional distress, there must be a close relationship between the person affected by the negligence and the bystander. Most jurisdictions hold that it must be immediate family.","sub":"Bystander Rule / Relationship Requirement","family":"Negligence","unit":12},{"n":6,"summary":"Man ate at 3 restaurants; salmonella; cannot identify which caused harm.","trigger":"Must prove which defendant caused injury.","rule":"Before joint and several liability can apply, plaintiff must show all defendants were partially liable.","sub":"Causation / Multiple Defendants","family":"Negligence","unit":14},{"n":7,"summary":"Jaywalker 60% at fault; driver 40% at fault → pure comparative negligence.","trigger":"Plaintiff may recover even if more negligent than defendant.","rule":"In pure comparative negligence, plaintiff can recover even if plaintiff's negligence exceeds defendant's.","sub":"Comparative Negligence","family":"Defenses","unit":23},{"n":8,"summary":"Medical bills partly paid by insurance; plaintiff sues full amount.","trigger":"Collateral source rule → defendant still liable for full damages.","rule":"The collateral source rule allows plaintiff to recover full damages even if insurance paid part.","sub":"Damages – Collateral Source Rule","family":"Damages","unit":22},{"n":9,"summary":"Man lies about woman cheating; tells friends; friend tells woman; she is upset but no economic loss.","trigger":"Slander per se → no need to prove special damages.","rule":"Although special damages must be proven with slander, they do not have to be proven with slander per se.","sub":"Defamation – Slander Per Se","family":"Defamation","unit":19},{"n":10,"summary":"Drunk friend threatens bar patrons; bouncer uses reasonable force (headlock) to escort him out. Two friends attack the bouncer claiming defense of others. Bouncer sues them; bar owner faces vicarious liability claim.","trigger":"Defense-of-others privilege only applies if the person you are helping actually had a right to use force.","rule":"A person can be liable for incorrectly asserting the defense of others privilege if the person they are aiding would not have the right to act the way that person did.","sub":"Defense of Others / Mistaken Defense","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":5},{"n":11,"summary":"Roller skate manufacturer knows that 1 in 10,000 wheels may fall off due to a latent defect discovered after a million units; they fix future production, but a defect wheel injures a child. Plaintiff sues in strict products liability and also wants to add battery based on that knowledge.","trigger":"Foreseeable injury risk from a product is not “battery” unless the manufacturer knew with substantial certainty that someone would be hurt.","rule":"Although some harm to consumers might be foreseeable during the manufacturing of a product, it will not be considered a battery unless the manufacturer knew with substantial certainty that people would get hurt.","sub":"Design/Manufacturing Defect vs. Battery","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":1},{"n":12,"summary":"Man ignores drowning woman; goes home first; calls 911 too late.","trigger":"No duty to rescue unless defendant created the danger.","rule":"A person has no duty to help another in peril unless they placed them in peril.","sub":"Duty – No Duty to Rescue","family":"Negligence","unit":9},{"n":13,"summary":"Store provides scooters but gives no training; customer injured by negligent scooter user.","trigger":"No duty to train adults on obvious-use items unless circumstances require.","rule":"A store may not have a duty to protect customers by training adults to use motorized scooters.","sub":"Duty of Care","family":"Negligence","unit":9},{"n":14,"summary":"Phone defect deletes business data; only economic loss.","trigger":"Economic loss doctrine → no recovery in tort without personal or property harm.","rule":"To sue in tort, plaintiff must have personal injury or property damage (not pure economic loss).","sub":"Economic Loss Doctrine","family":"Negligence","unit":13},{"n":15,"summary":"Would-be shoplifter locked in dressing room overnight and claims false imprisonment","trigger":"Plaintiff did not respond to manager’s call and remained hidden","rule":"No false imprisonment when confinement results from plaintiff’s own failure to leave or make presence known","sub":"False Imprisonment","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":3},{"n":16,"summary":"Dance instructor exaggerates; gives opinion about talent; elderly woman buys lessons.","trigger":"Opinion by expert can qualify as misrepresentation of fact.","rule":"Although misrepresentation requires a statement of fact, an opinion by someone with superior knowledge can count as fact.","sub":"Fraud / Misrepresentation","family":"Misrepresentation","unit":24},{"n":17,"summary":"Waiter throws phone at man; phone hits shoulder; no dispossession.","trigger":"Battery requires harmful/offensive contact; trespass to chattel requires damage or dispossession.","rule":"For trespass to chattel, property must be damaged or dispossessed.","sub":"Intentional Torts – Battery / Chattel","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":1},{"n":18,"summary":"Man throws bread at friend; hits woman instead; friend unaware.","trigger":"Battery doesn’t require intent toward actual target; assault requires awareness.","rule":"Assault requires victim awareness and apprehension of contact.","sub":"Intentional Torts – Battery/Assault","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":1},{"n":19,"summary":"Man used reasonable force to protect friend from apparent punch; fan fell and sued for battery","trigger":"Defense of others privilege","rule":"Although the elements of a tort may be met, a person will have a privilege and therefore, not be liable if they acted reasonably to defend others.","sub":"Intentional Torts (Defense of Others)","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":5},{"n":20,"summary":"Driver negligently hits a pedestrian (broken arm). Ambulance negligence causes second broken arm; hospital negligence causes broken leg; later a vengeful acquaintance intentionally stabs the pedestrian in the eye at the hospital. Pedestrian sues only the original driver.","trigger":"Original tortfeasor is liable for foreseeable negligent aggravations, but intentional criminal acts usually break the chain.","rule":"Under proximate cause, the original defendant can be liable for all foreseeable acts that aggravate the original injury. Generally, the negligence of another is foreseeable, although the intentional criminal acts of another are not.","sub":"Intervening vs. Superseding Causes","family":"Negligence","unit":11},{"n":21,"summary":"Negligent driver injures 12-year-old; at the hospital, a clown’s juggling ball causes her to fall and break her arm. Parents sue driver for all injuries.","trigger":"Intervening negligent act after D’s negligence; issue is whether it’s a superseding cause breaking proximate cause.","rule":"Negligence of others is foreseeable and will not be a superseding cause.","sub":"Intervening vs. Superseding Causes","family":"Negligence","unit":11},{"n":22,"summary":"Demolition company uses explosives (ultrahazardous) with all precautions; day 1 debris injures pedestrian; day 3 veteran with PTSD commits suicide due to flashbacks from blasts; both sue.","trigger":"Intervening intentional act (suicide) generally unforeseeable superseding cause; direct physical injury from blasting within scope of risk.","rule":"Generally, the intentional acts of another are considered an unforeseeable risk and will be considered an intervening, superseding factor, relieving the defendant of liability.","sub":"Intervening/Superseding Cause","family":"Negligence","unit":11},{"n":23,"summary":"At a birthday party, homeowner sends wife-guest to the garage freezer; she cuts her hand on an unknown hidden rust spot. Homeowner learns of it and apologizes. Minutes later, he sends husband-guest to the same freezer; the husband is cut the same way. Both sue as social guests (licensees).","trigger":"Licensees must be warned of known hidden dangers; duty kicks in once the owner actually knows about the hazard.","rule":"Under premises liability, the duty of the landowner depends on the status of the person entering their property. The duty to a social guest, who will be deemed to be a licensee, is to warn of known, hidden dangers.","sub":"Licensees / Known Hidden Dangers","family":"Negligence","unit":10},{"n":24,"summary":"Medical procedure injured trachea without proof of negligence; plaintiff’s expert testified she would have chosen a different method","trigger":"Medical malpractice claim based only on alternative treatment preference","rule":"Evidence that another person would have acted differently under the circumstances is not enough to show a breach of duty.","sub":"Medical Malpractice","family":"Negligence","unit":26},{"n":25,"summary":"Negligent driver hit pedestrian crossing outside crosswalk","trigger":"Plaintiff violated pedestrian-crossing ordinance","rule":"Plaintiff negligence that contributes to injury reduces recovery under comparative negligence","sub":"Negligence","family":"Negligence","unit":7},{"n":26,"summary":"Restaurant served coffee 10 degrees hotter than custom; warning label; plaintiff suffered worse burns","trigger":"Negligence alleged based on deviation from custom and increased risk","rule":"Custom in the industry is some evidence of negligence, but not conclusive.","sub":"Negligence (Custom)","family":"Negligence","unit":26},{"n":27,"summary":"Car parked during street-cleaning hours; boy crashes bike into car.","trigger":"Negligence per se requires statute protect class + type of harm.","rule":"A statute can be used to find duty and breach under negligence per se if the plaintiff was a member of the class meant to be protected AND the harm was the type the statute was meant to prevent.","sub":"Negligence Per Se","family":"Negligence","unit":7},{"n":28,"summary":"Car made with aluminum instead of required steel; accident would be identical with steel.","trigger":"Even if negligence per se applies → causation must still be proven.","rule":"Even if negligence per se applies, causation must still be proven.","sub":"Negligence Per Se / Causation","family":"Negligence","unit":7},{"n":29,"summary":"Driver negligently runs a red light and hits a pedestrian in the crosswalk, breaking his leg. A witness voluntarily drags the unconscious pedestrian out of the street and negligently tears his rotator cuff. Pedestrian sues both the driver and the rescuer.","trigger":"Once a volunteer actually undertakes a rescue, their own negligent rescue conduct can create liability.","rule":"Although there is generally no duty to rescue, a person who undertakes a rescue negligently can be liable for the harm resulting from the negligent rescue attempt.","sub":"Negligent Rescue / Good Samaritan","family":"Negligence","unit":9},{"n":30,"summary":"Woman witnesses trapeze death from 16 rows back; suffers emotional + physical harm.","trigger":"Bystander NIED requires close relationship + zone of danger.","rule":"Under the bystander rule... the plaintiff must have a close relationship, must observe the injury, and must have physical harm.","sub":"NIED – Bystander","family":"Negligence","unit":12},{"n":31,"summary":"Neighbor’s small dog sneaks into woman’s open garage. Her 14-year-old son scares the dog out by honking a car horn; later the dog is attacked by coyotes overnight. Owners sue the boy’s parents for vet bills even though the boy had no idea coyotes were nearby.","trigger":"There is no duty to rescue or protect unless your conduct actually created the perilous situation.","rule":"A person does not have a duty to get involved unless their actions caused the situation.","sub":"No Duty to Rescue / Created Peril","family":"Negligence","unit":9},{"n":32,"summary":"Woman borrows her cousin’s car and, on the first day, negligently causes an accident injuring four people. One injured man sues the cousin (owner) even though the jurisdiction does not impose strict liability on car owners and the cousin had no control over her driving.","trigger":"Mere ownership or lending of a car, without a control relationship, is not enough for vicarious liability.","rule":"A person will not be vicariously liable for the actions of another unless they control that person, such as an employer/employee or parent/child relationship.","sub":"Owner Liability / Control Requirement","family":"Vicarious Liability","unit":15},{"n":33,"summary":"Library roof known damaged; contractor fails to fix; invitee injured by collapse.","trigger":"Landowner duty to invitees = inspect, warn, repair.","rule":"Landowner must warn of known hidden dangers, inspect, and make reasonable repairs.","sub":"Premises Liability – Invitees","family":"Negligence","unit":10},{"n":34,"summary":"Neighbor routinely stepped over blocks during playdates; distracted one day and fell; sued for failure to warn","trigger":"Premises liability duty to warn a licensee of known hidden dangers","rule":"There is a duty to warn licensees of known, hidden dangers, but not dangers that they are or should be aware of.","sub":"Premises Liability (Licensees)","family":"Negligence","unit":10},{"n":35,"summary":"Store owner circulated robber’s image after release; robber sued for invasion of privacy","trigger":"Newsworthy publication about criminal act in public place","rule":"A claim for invasion of privacy will fail if the subject matter was newsworthy, especially if it occurred in a public place.","sub":"Privacy","family":"Privacy","unit":20},{"n":36,"summary":"Employer disabled two-hand safety feature post-sale; employee injured; sues manufacturer","trigger":"Post-sale modification caused injury","rule":"Manufacturer is not liable when a substantial post-sale modification, not a defect, caused the injury","sub":"Products Liability","family":"Products Liability","unit":18},{"n":37,"summary":"Independent contractor handing out store samples; woman contracts salmonella.","trigger":"Strict liability applies only to commercial sellers/distributors.","rule":"Products liability applies to commercial sellers or distributors of products.","sub":"Products Liability – Commercial Seller Requirement","family":"Products Liability","unit":17},{"n":38,"summary":"Man buys a high-end projector; neighbor borrows it for a business presentation to investors. Projector is defective and doesn’t work; neighbor loses a lucrative opportunity but suffers only economic loss, no personal or property damage.","trigger":"Pure economic loss, without personal or property damage, cannot be recovered in tort under products liability.","rule":"The economic loss doctrine states that before a person can sue in tort, they must have some type of harm to person or property, not just economic loss.","sub":"Products Liability / Pure Economic Loss","family":"Negligence","unit":13},{"n":39,"summary":"Chiropractor injures patient; hospital later commits negligent wrong-site surgery.","trigger":"Negligent medical care is foreseeable; intentional acts usually not.","rule":"The original tortfeasor can be liable for all harm that is foreseeable; negligence of others is foreseeable, intentional acts usually supersede.","sub":"Proximate Cause – Foreseeability","family":"Negligence","unit":11},{"n":40,"summary":"Driver negligently hits pedestrian; at hospital surgeon grabs wrong chart and removes pedestrian’s lung instead of saving legs; delay forces leg amputations; pedestrian sues driver for loss of legs.","trigger":"Original tortfeasor’s negligence is followed by medical malpractice that worsens injuries.","rule":"A defendant will be liable for all foreseeable events that occur after their negligence, including the negligence of others.","sub":"Proximate Cause / Medical Malpractice","family":"Negligence","unit":11},{"n":41,"summary":"Newspaper prints story that a famous basketball player had sex with a minor, based on three now-unreliable sources. Player’s reputation and income suffer; he is later cleared but never regains his prior popularity.","trigger":"Public figure defamation requires proof of “actual malice,” not just negligence.","rule":"When a public figure sues a defendant for defamation, they must prove actual malice, which means the defendant knew the statement was false or acted with reckless disregard of the falsity of the statement.","sub":"Public Figures / Actual Malice","family":"Defamation","unit":19},{"n":42,"summary":"Sriracha factory has operated for years; as population grows, new neighbors move nearby and complain that chili odors cause headaches and sore throats and prevent them from enjoying their property. City sues for public nuisance and seeks to shut the plant.","trigger":"Nuisance focuses on unreasonable interference with others’ use and enjoyment of their land.","rule":"A nuisance is when a person unreasonably interferes with the quiet enjoyment of the land of another.","sub":"Public/Private Nuisance – Odors","family":"Nuisance","unit":21},{"n":43,"summary":"Teacher runs loud vacuum repair business at home in a residential zone, disturbing neighbors and sending debris onto neighbor’s land; neighbor seeks to stop noise.","trigger":"Private nuisance: substantial, unreasonable interference; injunction requires balancing equities/factors.","rule":"When a party substantially interferes with the quiet enjoyment of others, the court will balance factors in determining whether to grant an injunction.","sub":"Public/Private Nuisance – Odors","family":"Nuisance","unit":21},{"n":44,"summary":"Patient has a rare blood infection missed because the lab misreads the test; patient dies. Jury awards $5M compensatory and $20M punitive damages based on negligent misreading.","trigger":"Punitive damages generally require more than ordinary negligence—they require willful or wanton misconduct.","rule":"Punitive damages are usually imposed for willful or wanton conduct, not negligence.","sub":"Punitive vs. Compensatory","family":"Damages","unit":22},{"n":45,"summary":"Four-car pileup: jury allocates fault P 20%, D1 10%, D2 20%, D3 50%. Jurisdiction uses pure comparative negligence. Plaintiff wants to know from whom he can recover.","trigger":"In pure comparative negligence, plaintiff can recover from all negligent defendants, even if plaintiff’s share equals or exceeds some defendants’ share.","rule":"Under pure comparative negligence, the plaintiff can sue all defendants, regardless of if his fault level is equal to or exceeds theirs. The plaintiff’s recovery, however, will be reduced by his own percentage of fault.","sub":"Pure Comparative / Multi-Defendant","family":"Defenses","unit":23},{"n":46,"summary":"Home flooded; cleaner and sitter deny leaving faucet on; plaintiffs sue sitter; sitter moves for SJ","trigger":"Attempt to use res ipsa where cause/actor uncertain","rule":"Res ipsa loquitur can help a plaintiff show negligence when they aren’t really sure what happened. The P must prove that this event doesn’t happen without negligence, P must eliminate other causes, and show that D had exclusive control over the situation.","sub":"Res Ipsa","family":"Negligence","unit":8},{"n":47,"summary":"Scalpel left in patient; only doctor (independent contractor) + hospital nurse touched scalpel.","trigger":"Res ipsa cannot apply where multiple possible negligent actors exist.","rule":"Res ipsa loquitur cannot be used if there is more than one defendant since the plaintiff would not be able to prove that the negligence was within the defendant’s exclusive control.","sub":"Res Ipsa","family":"Negligence","unit":8},{"n":48,"summary":"Farmer hits pig in road near his ranch; sues only neighbor for vehicle damage but cannot prove how pig escaped; neighbor offers evidence of professionally built pen with extremely low escape rating.","trigger":"Res ipsa used to infer negligence where accident ordinarily doesn’t occur without negligence, defendant had exclusive control, and plaintiff eliminates other causes.","rule":"Res ipsa loquitur can be used to help show negligence if the event does not occur without negligence, the party using res ipsa loquitur can eliminate all other causes and can show that the event was within the defendant’s exclusive control.","sub":"Res Ipsa Loquitur","family":"Negligence","unit":8},{"n":49,"summary":"Employee smashes workplace window and climbs onto 18th‑floor ledge to commit suicide; coworker attempts rescue and is badly cut by shattered glass while pulling him back to safety.","trigger":"Defendant’s negligence/intentional act creates peril; rescuer is injured during a reasonable rescue attempt.","rule":"A reasonable rescue attempt is foreseeable, and a person at fault for the peril will be liable for injuries to any rescuer, whether the rescue was successful or not.","sub":"Rescue Doctrine / Proximate Cause","family":"Negligence","unit":9},{"n":50,"summary":"Child drives snowmobile (adult activity), panics, crashes; debris injures bystander.","trigger":"Minor in adult activity → adult standard of care applies.","rule":"A minor will usually be held to the standard of other minors of the same age and experience, but if they are involved in adult activity, they will be held to the standard of reasonably prudent adults under the circumstances.","sub":"Standard of Care – Minors","family":"Negligence","unit":9},{"n":51,"summary":"Driver distracted by texting hits a pedestrian who later dies ten days after surgery. Almost two years after the accident, the estate wants to bring a survival action; the pedestrian’s own negligence may have contributed.","trigger":"For survival actions, you count the statute of limitations from the injury, and decedent’s comparative fault still matters.","rule":"The statute of limitations for a survival action starts running when the injury occurs, NOT on the day of the death. The comparative fault of the decedent can be used to reduce the estate’s recovery in a survival action.","sub":"Survival Actions Statute of Limitations / Comparative Fault","family":"Damages","unit":22},{"n":52,"summary":"Drunk driver is asleep at a red light (DUI), but stationary. Texting driver speeds, doesn’t see the stopped car, and rear-ends it. Texting driver sues the drunk driver for his car damage.","trigger":"Negligence per se only substitutes duty and breach; causation still must link the statutory violation to plaintiff’s harm.","rule":"Although negligence per se may help prove duty and breach, the plaintiff still has to prove causation or his case will fail.","sub":"Statutory Violation / Causation","family":"Negligence","unit":7},{"n":53,"summary":"Baker makes 300 cakes using eggs from dairy farm; eggs contaminated with salmonella; strict products claim against baker; baker seeks indemnity from suppliers.","trigger":"Indemnity in strict liability typically from manufacturer of defective product (commercial supplier).","rule":"Although any commercial supplier can be sued for strict liability, indemnity can be obtained against the manufacturer of the defective product.","sub":"Strict Liability / Indemnity","family":"Negligence","unit":14},{"n":54,"summary":"Caterer causes food poisoning; nurse intentionally poisons victim further (kidney failure).","trigger":"Intentional criminal acts of third parties → superseding cause.","rule":"The intentional, criminal actions of a third party are usually unforeseeable, making them a superseding cause.","sub":"Superseding Causes","family":"Negligence","unit":11},{"n":55,"summary":"Husband jokingly pushes wife; she stumbles and falls onto grouchy neighbor’s lawn. Neighbor threatens trespass suit despite no damage.","trigger":"Trespass requires intent to cause entry (directly or by causing a person/object to enter); liability can attach via the actor who causes entry.","rule":"A trespass is intentionally causing someone or something to enter the land of another. Trespass to land doesn't requiere damages because it protects the right to the land. It only has to be intentional.","sub":"Trespass to Land","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":6},{"n":56,"summary":"Woman’s dog is lost; drone detects heat on farmer’s land. She asks permission and is denied, then organizes a search party, trespasses, finds the dog, and some crops are trampled. Local necessity ordinance lets her enter after denial but still makes her pay for damages.","trigger":"Trespass includes intentionally causing things or people to enter another’s land, including above or below the surface.","rule":"A trespass is intentionally entering or causing someone or something to enter the land of another without consent. The entry may be on the land, below the land, or even above the land.","sub":"Trespass to Land / Necessity","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":5},{"n":57,"summary":"Motorcyclist slides on wet leaves in the road and sustains $14,000 property damage to his bike. He can show neighbors often blow leaves into the road but cannot prove which neighbor’s leaves caused his crash.","trigger":"Without being able to identify which defendant caused the harm, the claim fails.","rule":"If a plaintiff cannot show who the proper defendant is, the case will be dismissed.","sub":"Unknown Tortfeasor / Identification Problem","family":"Negligence","unit":14},{"n":58,"summary":"A homeowner believed an intruder was in her backyard and called police. An officer searched the yard and, while investigating, stepped into an unseen erosion hole and broke his ankle. He sues the homeowner for premises liability.","trigger":"Classify police officer’s entrant status on private property while responding in official capacity.","rule":"For purposes of premises liability, police officers and firefighters are usually classified as licensees.","sub":"Premises Liability – Status","family":"Negligence","unit":10},{"n":59,"summary":"A computer technician guaranteed a quick repair during tax season for $300, but negligently reset the computer, locking an accountant out of client files for hours. The accountant lost $900 in new business and sues for negligence seeking purely economic losses.","trigger":"Negligence claim seeking only lost profits (economic loss) without personal injury or property damage.","rule":"To sue in tort, the plaintiff must have more than just economic damages. Planitiff needs injury or property damage. In contract for example he would suceed (damages)","sub":"Economic Loss Doctrine","family":"Negligence","unit":13},{"n":60,"summary":"A landowner hired a handyman to remove three fence sections on what the landowner believed was his property. In fact, one section belonged to the neighbor, and the handyman removed/destroyed it. The landowner’s belief about ownership was reasonable but mistaken, and the neighbor sues for the loss.","trigger":"Intentional exercise of dominion over another’s chattel causing permanent deprivation; mistake about ownership does not negate intent for conversion.","rule":"Conversion requires the intentional intermeddling with the personal property of another which results in permanent deprivation. A mistaken belief in whose property it is will not be a good defense to conversion.","sub":"Conversion – Mistake Not a Defense","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":6},{"n":61,"summary":"A man posted an online advertisement telling the public to place bumper stickers on a particular car to ‘exercise First Amendment rights,’ but he did not own the car. People did it, and the car owner sued the platform for negligence, arguing it should have screened or fact-checked the advertisement.","trigger":"Negligence duty question: whether an ad platform must verify posts absent red flags suggesting illegality or harm.","rule":"An advertising platform does not have the duty to fact check each post on its site without anything further to indicate that there may be a problem.","sub":"Negligence – Duty of Platforms","family":"Negligence","unit":9},{"n":62,"summary":"In torts class, a student throws a battery toward the professor intending to scare her, but it lands harmlessly on the floor; the professor never saw it and learns about it only later from a video, then sues for assault.","trigger":"Plaintiff did not perceive/experience apprehension at the time (no awareness of imminent contact) despite defendant’s intent to scare.","rule":"In tort law, an assault requires intentionally putting someone in apprehension of an imminent contact, which would require the victim to be aware of the imminent contact.","sub":"Intentional Torts – Assault (apprehension/awareness of imminent contact required)","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":2},{"n":63,"summary":"Man slips on water negligently left by mall custodian and breaks his leg; doctors set his cast improperly allowing movement; next day he trips stepping over a neighbor’s bike left on his property and breaks his arm; he sues the mall for both injuries.","trigger":"Subsequent negligence/medical treatment or other negligent events after initial tort aggravate harm; foreseeability keeps original tortfeasor on the hook.","rule":"Negligence of others is a foreseeable act, meaning that a defendant who breaches their duty can also be held liable for the negligence of others that contribute to the severity of the injury after the original defendant’s negligence.","sub":"Causation – Proximate cause / foreseeable intervening negligence (incl. negligent medical treatment)","family":"Negligence","unit":11},{"n":64,"summary":"Man keeps a pet lion (wild animal) caged; at a party he warns guests; friend asks to see it and stays 5 feet away as instructed; lion is startled, roars and swipes through cage (paw extends 1 foot); friend panics, runs, trips, and breaks her leg; she sues; comparative fault jurisdiction (no implied assumption of risk).","trigger":"Strict liability for wild animals covers harm caused by the animal’s dangerous propensities, including fear reactions causing flight injuries.","rule":"The propensities attributed to a wild animal for strict liability include reactions due to fear of the animal.","sub":"Strict Liability – Wild animals (dangerous propensities include fear/flight reactions)","family":"Strict Liability","unit":16},{"n":65,"summary":"Customer forgets his phone at a closing restaurant; manager refuses entry. Customer forcefully pushes the door open, knocking the manager down, then retrieves the phone. Manager sues for battery and customer claims he was privileged to recover his own property and moved for summary judgment","trigger":"Recapture/recovery of chattel privilege exists, but the degree of force used must be reasonable under the circumstances.","rule":"Although recovery of property can be privileged, the force used to recover the property must be reasonable under the circumstances. This question asked if the man's motion for summary judgment arguing he was privileged. The answer was that because a fact finder can reasonably conclude that the man used an unreasonable amount of force to recover the property. REMEMBER SJ DOES NOT WEIGHT EVIDENCE IF THERE IS \"SOME\" EVIDENCE THE MOTION FOR SJ SHOULD BE DENIED.","sub":"Intentional Torts – Defenses: recapture of chattels / reasonable force","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":5},{"n":66,"summary":"Business hires a reputable fireworks company (independent contractor) to run a fireworks display at its grand opening. Fireworks are not considered abnormally dangerous in the jurisdiction, but contractor fails to take proper precautions and a misfire injures a customer. Customer sues the business.","trigger":"Employer of an independent contractor is usually not vicariously liable, but can be liable where the work involves a special/inherent danger (nondelegable duty).","rule":"A person is generally not vicariously liable for an independent contractor’s torts. However, the hirer may be liable (i) vicariously where the contractor is performing work involving a nondelegable duty, including inherently dangerous activities/peculiar risk requiring special precautions, and (ii) directly for the hirer’s own negligence, such as negligent hiring, instruction, or supervision of the contractor.","sub":"Vicarious Liability – Independent contractor exceptions (special danger)","family":"Vicarious Liability","unit":15},{"n":67,"summary":"Psychology professor describes an experiment about students washing cars at an intersection. Four students try it without her permission; a motorist is injured and sues the university, arguing the students were acting as part of the class experiment.","trigger":"Vicarious liability requires an agency/employment relationship plus authorization/control; no liability where the professor did not authorize the tortious conduct as a class project.","rule":"No vicarious liability without an agency/employment relationship plus authorization/control; a school isn’t liable where the professor did not authorize the tortious conduct as a class project.","sub":"Vicarious Liability – Respondeat superior / agency required","family":"Vicarious Liability","unit":15},{"n":68,"summary":"A newly built law school in a quiet neighborhood installs a large clock tower that chimes every hour. Only one nearby homeowner, who is unusually sensitive to ringing sounds, finds it extremely annoying and sues for private nuisance.","trigger":"Private nuisance requires substantial and unreasonable interference judged by an ordinary person; interference that only affects an abnormally sensitive plaintiff is generally not 'unreasonable.'","rule":"Private nuisance requires a substantial and unreasonable interference; if only an abnormally sensitive plaintiff is disturbed, the interference is generally not “unreasonable.”","sub":"Nuisance – Private nuisance (abnormally sensitive plaintiff)","family":"Nuisance","unit":21},{"n":69,"summary":"Plaintiff’s car engine stalls in city traffic without warm-up and is rear‑ended by a truck. Plaintiff sues car manufacturer for strict products liability design defect and offers evidence of an equally priced alternative design that would prevent stalling; defense argues other driver negligence is superseding and injuries included a heart attack.","trigger":"Design-defect strict liability: plaintiff must show unreasonably dangerous design (often feasible alternative) and proximate cause; other drivers’ negligence is usually foreseeable and not superseding.","rule":"In design-defect strict products liability, plaintiff must show an unreasonably dangerous design (often via feasible alternative design) and proximate cause, and other drivers’ negligence is generally foreseeable (not superseding).","sub":"Products Liability – Design defect (feasible alternative; proximate cause)","family":"Products Liability","unit":17},{"n":70,"summary":"Manufacturing plant stores volatile explosives near a busy highway and uses strict safety measures. During an unusually heavy windstorm, a roof tile blows off the plant and damages a passing car’s windshield; driver sues under strict liability for abnormally dangerous activity.","trigger":"Strict liability for abnormally dangerous activities applies only to injuries caused by the specific risk that makes the activity abnormally dangerous (not unrelated hazards like wind-blown debris).","rule":"Strict liability for abnormally dangerous activities applies only to harms from the specific risk that makes the activity abnormally dangerous.","sub":"Strict Liability – Abnormally dangerous activities (harm within the risk)","family":"Strict Liability","unit":16},{"n":71,"summary":"Al threatens his neighbor. Two days later the neighbor sees Bill (Al’s identical twin) approaching; Bill raises his hand and says hello. Mistakenly thinking Bill is Al and fearing imminent harm, neighbor strikes Bill; Bill sues and neighbor claims self-defense.","trigger":"Self-defense is justified if there is a reasonable objective belief of imminent harm; reasonable mistakes as to the aggressor are permitted.","rule":"Self-defense is justified if there is a reasonable (objective) belief in imminent harm; reasonable mistakes as to the aggressor are allowed.","sub":"Intentional Torts – Defenses: self-defense (reasonable mistake)","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":5},{"n":72,"summary":"Driver violates a traffic safety statute and causes an accident. Plaintiff argues negligence per se, but the defendant claims the violation happened because of a sudden, unforeseeable medical event (like a heart attack) that made compliance impossible.","trigger":"Negligence per se based on statutory violation is defeated if the violation is excused (e.g., unforeseeable sudden incapacity).","rule":"Negligence per se requires an unexcused statutory violation; an unforeseeable sudden incapacity (e.g., heart attack) can excuse the violation, defeating liability.","sub":"Negligence – Negligence per se; excuses (sudden incapacity)","family":"Negligence","unit":7},{"n":73,"summary":"Neighbors complain about a defendant’s activity that substantially interferes with the neighbors’ use and enjoyment of their homes, but the harm is confined to a small group rather than the public at large.","trigger":"Unreasonable, substantial interference with use/enjoyment affecting a limited number of persons indicates private nuisance (not public nuisance).","rule":"Private nuisance is an unreasonable, substantial interference with another’s use and enjoyment of land affecting a limited number of persons (vs. public nuisance affecting the public at large). Reason: stadium noise/light is an interference with use/enjoyment of her land; facts support a localized harm (one adjacent landowner), not widespread public harm.","sub":"Nuisance – Private vs public nuisance","family":"Nuisance","unit":21},{"n":74,"summary":"A newspaper runs a story claiming (in 1980) a labor leader’s first wife, with his knowledge, gave sexual favors to the mayor to get union concessions. The article names the labor leader and identifies the wife by former and current name. Reporter believed it based on two very reliable sources. Wife suffers distress and sues for defamation. A private individual sues for defamation over statements concerning an issue of public concern. The question asks what level of fault the plaintiff must prove under constitutional standards.","trigger":"Private plaintiff + public issue: constitutional minimum for defamation liability is negligence (not actual malice).","rule":"For defamation by a private plaintiff on a public issue, the constitutional minimum is negligence (ordinary care) rather than actual malice. Step 1: Classify plaintiff and issue. Plaintiff (the wife): private figure (not automatically public because spouse of a labor leader; no facts she sought publicity). Issue: public concern (alleged corruption / improper influence on government officials). So: Private figure + public concern → plaintiff must prove at least negligence (not actual malice).","sub":"Defamation – Private plaintiff on public issue (negligence standard)","family":"Defamation","unit":19},{"n":75,"summary":"Defendant takes a low-value item (worth about $25), but does so in an outrageous way that causes severe emotional distress. Plaintiff considers whether to sue for conversion or intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED).","trigger":"Outrageous conduct causing severe distress supports IIED damages that can exceed conversion’s fair market value measure.","rule":"IIED can produce greater damages than conversion when outrageous conduct causes severe distress; conversion damages = fair market value of the chattel (here, ~$25).","sub":"Intentional Torts – IIED damages vs conversion damages","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":4},{"n":76,"summary":"A federally owned and operated nuclear reactor emits radioactive matter that settles on a nearby dairy farm, killing the herd and contaminating the soil. Under the Federal Tort Claims Act (no relevant exceptions), the federal government is liable only for negligence; the factfinder concludes the plant had a sound design and that a valve made by a highly reputable private manufacturer malfunctioned, and there was no way the government could have anticipated or prevented the release.","trigger":"FTCA requires negligence; if no negligence is proven, plaintiff cannot recover via strict liability theory.","rule":"Under the FTCA here, the federal government is liable only for negligence; no negligence proven → defendant wins (do not jump to strict liability for ultrahazardous activity).","sub":"Government Liability – FTCA negligence requirement","family":"Special Defendants","unit":25},{"n":77,"summary":"A cigarette maker publishes an ad showing a model dressed as a race‑car driver standing by a distinctive race car that closely resembles a very unusually marked car driven by a famous driver. The driver is not named and his face is not shown, but the ad was published without his permission. The driver sues for economic loss based on misappropriation/right of publicity; the maker moves to dismiss.","trigger":"Commercial use of a person’s identity/likeness without permission; issue is whether the ad contains enough identifying indicia even without name/face.","rule":"Misappropriation / right of publicity is using another’s identity/likeness (even without name/face) for commercial advantage without permission; it’s enough that there are sufficient indicia that a reasonable person recognizes who it is.","sub":"Privacy Torts – Appropriation / right of publicity","family":"Privacy","unit":20},{"n":78,"summary":"Hardware storekeeper sells rebuilt “reconditioned” power saws and, for a cabinetmaking-shop purchaser, swaps the coarse blade on the chosen saw for a new finer-tooth blade manufactured by a saw‑blade company. The new blade is defective; an employee using the saw is severely cut, and the employee sues the power saw company on strict liability when the blade shaft comes loose and flies off while cutting plywood.","trigger":"Strict products liability involving a rebuilt/reconditioned product and a defective replacement component; question is whether substantial change/alteration breaks liability.","rule":"In strict products liability, a manufacturer is not liable if the product underwent a substantial change after leaving the manufacturer’s control (e.g., rebuilt/reconditioned/major alteration) that caused the harm.","sub":"Products Liability – Strict liability; component/alteration","family":"Products Liability","unit":18},{"n":79,"summary":"Car owner washes her car on a public street during rush hours in violation of a statute enacted only to expedite traffic flow. Waste water forms a puddle that freezes during an unexpected cold snap; a pedestrian slips on the frozen puddle and breaks her leg. At trial, the only negligence evidence is the car owner’s admission of violating the statute; both sides move for directed verdict.","trigger":"Negligence per se based on statutory violation; statute’s purpose (protect class/type of harm) mismatch with injury.","rule":"Negligence per se applies only if the statute was designed to protect this plaintiff class from this type of harm; a traffic-flow statute doesn’t establish negligence for a slip-and-fall.","sub":"Negligence – Negligence per se","family":"Negligence","unit":7},{"n":80,"summary":"Used‑car lot is remote and enclosed by a ten‑foot chain‑link fence. While a customer and a sales representative are inside, a security guard locks the gate at 1:30 p.m. (the lot is supposed to be closed after 1:00 p.m. Saturday until Monday). At 1:45 p.m. they discover they are locked in with no way to summon help; after two hours the customer panics and tries to climb the fence, falls, and is injured, then sues the used‑car company on a false‑imprisonment theory.","trigger":"False imprisonment requires intent/knowledge of confinement; locking gate without awareness of people inside.","rule":"False imprisonment requires intent (purpose or substantial certainty); no liability unless the guard knew (or was substantially certain) people were inside when locking the gate.","sub":"Intentional Torts – False imprisonment","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":3},{"n":81,"summary":"State vehicle code makes it illegal to cross a street in the central business district other than at a designated crosswalk and prohibits parking so as to block any part of a crosswalk. A truck is parked blocking the crosswalk, so a pedestrian steps into the street and walks around the back of the truck; the pedestrian is struck by a negligently driven vehicle. Issue is the effect of the pedestrian’s failure to be in the crosswalk on the pedestrian’s claim against the driver.","trigger":"Plaintiff violates a safety statute; effect is comparative negligence (reduces recovery) rather than an automatic bar.","rule":"Plaintiff’s statutory violation can be used as comparative negligence (negligence per se for the plaintiff), typically reducing recovery rather than barring it.","sub":"Negligence – Comparative negligence","family":"Negligence","unit":7},{"n":82,"summary":"A mother hosts a birthday party and asks parents to RSVP and list allergies; none report allergies. She bakes a chocolate cake that includes a small amount of cherry juice. A child with an undisclosed cherry allergy eats the cake, has a severe reaction requiring hospitalization, and the parents sue the host for negligence.","trigger":"Eggshell‑plaintiff doctrine affects damages only after breach is shown; it does not create a breach where the risk and duty were not proven.","rule":"Someone being an egg-shell plaintiff does not change the outcome of a suit until it has been proven that the defendant breached a duty. Sometimes, the best defense to negligence is not compartive negligence, contributory negligence its that the elements of negligence were not met. There needs to be duty and breach to have an eggshell plaintiff. Think an eggshell plaintiff as related to proximate cause, even if the harm is not foreseeable defandant will still be liable. Here, the child was not an eggshell plaintiff because the mother didn't breach her duty.","sub":"Negligence – Breach required before eggshell plaintiff expands damages","family":"Damages","unit":22},{"n":83,"summary":"A woman leaves her wedding ring at a jewelry store for repair while the owner is on vacation. Two employees are working and both had authority to make the repair. When the woman returns in three days, neither employee can locate the ring. She sues both employees to recover its value, but she cannot show which employee was responsible.","trigger":"Joint liability theories require proof that all defendants were at fault; plaintiff must first establish fault before any joint liability can apply.","rule":"No form of joint liability can be used unless it is shown that all the defendants being sued were at fault.","sub":"Negligence – Multiple defendants; joint liability requires fault proof","family":"Negligence","unit":14},{"n":84,"summary":"A police officer was sitting in his patrol car parked at the police station, just awaiting the end of his shift in a few moments. A driver cut through the police station parking lot to avoid a busy intersection. While driving through the parking lot, the driver negligently crashed into the police officer’s car. The officer was severely injured and brought a negligence lawsuit against the driver. The driver motioned to dismiss the lawsuit, arguing that the common law firefighter’s rule would bar the officer’s recovery.","trigger":"Firefighter/police officer injured responding; claim against person who requested help tied to job risks.","rule":"The firefighter’s rule prevents an emergency public worker like a firefighter or police officer from suing a person who requested help if the injury suffered is related to the specific dangers of the job.","sub":"Firefighter’s Rule","family":"Negligence","unit":10},{"n":85,"summary":"A man invited a friend over to his house for a poker game. After a hand where the friend won most of the man’s money, the man pulled a gun out of his waistband and aimed it at the friend. The man’s intent was to scare the friend, perhaps even persuade him to return the man’s money. The man did not make any statements about whether the gun was loaded. The friend knew that the gun wasn’t loaded, so he wasn’t disturbed in the least bit. He didn’t offer to return any of the man’s money. Does the friend have a proper assault action against the man?","trigger":"Assault claim where plaintiff did not actually apprehend imminent battery.","rule":"For tort assault, the plaintiff must actually feel apprehensive of an imminent battery.","sub":"Assault – Apprehension requirement","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":2},{"n":86,"summary":"A student lends his classmate his car to attend a concert at a theater. Before going to the theater, the classmate drives about two miles to a dealer’s home to buy marijuana, then drives toward the theater and smokes two marijuana cigarettes while driving to be “high” for the show. While the car is parked outside the theater, through no fault of the classmate, another car hits it and damages it; repairs will cost $750. The hit‑and‑run driver cannot be identified. The student sues the classmate for the damage.","trigger":"Bailee makes an unauthorized side trip but the damage occurs without the bailee’s negligence; determine whether the deviation makes the bailee strictly liable (conversion) or whether negligence is required.","rule":"A bailee is liable for damage to bailed property only if the bailee was negligent, unless the bailee’s unauthorized use is a major deviation amounting to conversion; a minor deviation without negligence does not create liability for accidental damage.","sub":"Bailments – Bailee liability","family":"Special Defendants","unit":25},{"n":87,"summary":"A bartender serves six drinks to an accountant who is obviously very intoxicated. The accountant drives away, weaves across the road, and crashes into a pedestrian on the sidewalk, seriously injuring the pedestrian. The question asks how the bartender’s act of serving drinks is viewed in the causation analysis.","trigger":"Negligent service of alcohol precedes a later car crash; classify the role of the service as direct/proximate/intervening/superseding cause.","rule":"An intervening act contributes to the injury but becomes a superseding cause only if it is unforeseeable and breaks the causal chain.","sub":"Negligence – Causation; intervening vs superseding","family":"Negligence","unit":11},{"n":88,"summary":"A city ordinance forbids washing vehicles parked on city streets. A man washes his car on the street, leaving water that forms a puddle which freezes overnight. The next morning a driver’s car slides on the ice and crashes. The driver sues the man for negligence per se. The question asks which additional fact would help the man most.","trigger":"Negligence per se defense: ordinance’s purpose/class of persons vs the harm that occurred; show statute aimed at different risk.","rule":"Negligence per se applies only if the statute was designed to prevent the type of harm that occurred and protect the class of persons injured; otherwise, violation does not establish duty/breach.","sub":"Negligence – Negligence per se (purpose)","family":"Negligence","unit":7},{"n":89,"summary":"A golf ball hit by a 16-year-old deflects off a tree limb and lands on an adjacent homeowner’s property, striking the homeowner’s head (minor injury). Homeowner sues golfer for trespass.","trigger":"Trespass to land requires intent to enter land; accidental entry by object due to deflection.","rule":"Trespass requires intent to enter the land (intent to cause the intrusion); purely accidental, unintentional entry generally is not trespass.","sub":"Property Torts – Trespass to land; intent","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":6},{"n":90,"summary":"A certified airline pilot invites his girlfriend to fly in his single‑engine Cessna. She is scared of flying but reluctantly agrees. Mid‑flight, the pilot plays a “practical joke” by shutting off the engine; the plane goes into a nosedive and he says, “Oh my God, we’re going to crash.” The girlfriend becomes panic‑stricken and screams hysterically. A few seconds later he restarts the engine, says he was joking, and the plane lands safely; no one is physically injured.","trigger":"Extreme scare/prank causing severe emotional distress; whether physical injury is required; intent can be recklessness.","rule":"IIED requires extreme and outrageous conduct done intentionally or recklessly that causes severe emotional distress; physical injury is not required.","sub":"IIED – Extreme & outrageous conduct","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":4},{"n":91,"summary":"A movie theater seat collapses, causing a customer sitting in it to fall and suffer a back injury. The customer sues the theater for personal injuries based on the condition of the premises.","trigger":"Business invitee injured by a dangerous condition; duty to inspect and make safe.","rule":"A possessor of land owes invitees a duty of reasonable care, including reasonable inspection and repair; liability turns on whether the theater failed to reasonably inspect and maintain the seats.","sub":"Premises Liability – Business invitees","family":"Negligence","unit":10},{"n":92,"summary":"At 10:20 p.m. on December 3, a pedestrian urgently needs a restroom and sees a private dinner club. A sign on the front door reads “ADMITTANCE TO MEMBERS ONLY.” The pedestrian is not a member, but he enters the club anyway, finds a door marked “Gentlemen,” and enters the restroom.","trigger":"Person enters private premises despite a clear “members only” restriction; classify entrant status.","rule":"One who enters land without permission (including entering despite an explicit restriction) is a trespasser.","sub":"Premises Liability – Entrant status","family":"Negligence","unit":10},{"n":93,"summary":"A man and woman own adjoining estates in Louisiana. The man inherited his estate in 2000; his family has owned it continuously since 1812. The woman purchased her estate in 2008. The man’s stable (built in 2002) sits 10 feet from the woman’s property line and houses valuable racing horses. Not infrequently, putrid stenches arise from large accumulations of horse manure left unattended, and the woman has often complained about the noises and odors from the stable. The woman brings an action to enjoin the man’s use of the stables.","trigger":"Ongoing odors/noise substantially interfering with neighbor’s use and enjoyment; injunction sought; ‘coming to the nuisance’ fact present.","rule":"A private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with another’s use and enjoyment of land; coming to the nuisance is not a complete defense, and injunctive relief may be granted for substantial interference.","sub":"Nuisance – Private nuisance; injunction","family":"Nuisance","unit":21},{"n":94,"summary":"A law graduate celebrates passing the bar and drinks a potent cocktail she has never heard of. Minutes later she becomes very dizzy while trying to walk to the restroom and vomits on a nearby customer, humiliating the customer. The customer sues the graduate.","trigger":"Tort claim based on physical contact caused by defendant’s intoxication; focus on voluntary act requirement for battery.","rule":"Battery requires a voluntary act resulting in harmful or offensive contact; involuntary conduct is not a voluntary act for battery.","sub":"Intentional Torts – Battery; voluntary act","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":1},{"n":95,"summary":"A commuter enters a subway car and walks toward the rear to find a seat. The subway comes to an abrupt halt, causing the commuter to fall onto another rider and injure him. The injured rider sues the subway company for personal injuries.","trigger":"Common-carrier abrupt stop; liability turns on whether the operator/company was negligent rather than strict liability for sudden movements.","rule":"A common carrier is liable for passenger injuries only if the carrier was negligent; if the sudden stop was not negligent, the carrier is not liable.","sub":"Negligence – Common carriers","family":"Special Defendants","unit":25},{"n":96,"summary":"A furniture store employee driving a delivery truck negligently runs a stop sign and causes an accident. The injured driver sues the furniture store and recovers $25,000. In a jurisdiction allowing contribution and indemnity, the furniture store sues its employee to recover its losses.","trigger":"Employer held vicariously liable for employee’s negligence seeks shifting of the entire loss to the primary wrongdoer.","rule":"A party held vicariously liable may obtain indemnity from the primary wrongdoer whose negligence caused the injury.","sub":"Contribution/Indemnity – Vicarious liability indemnity","family":"Negligence","unit":14},{"n":97,"summary":"A ski resort lift loses power and stops. A customer is stuck suspended in a chair 50 feet above the ground for five hours until power is restored, then returns to the bottom uninjured. The customer sues the resort.","trigger":"Confinement without physical harm; whether there is an intentional tort or negligence when the stoppage is due to an external power failure.","rule":"False imprisonment requires intentional confinement; absent intent and absent negligence by the resort (e.g., unavoidable power outage), the customer has no tort claim.","sub":"False Imprisonment / Negligence – Intent vs fault","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":3},{"n":98,"summary":"A farmer burns leaves in a metal barrel and leaves the fire unattended. A gust of wind blows burning leaves onto a neighbor’s property, starting a small brush fire.","trigger":"Spread of fire after leaving a controlled burn unattended; negligence vs strict liability.","rule":"A person is liable for negligence when they unreasonably leave a fire unattended and it spreads and causes damage; strict liability generally does not apply to ordinary burning.","sub":"Negligence – Duty of care; fire","family":"Strict Liability","unit":16},{"n":99,"summary":"After a breakup, a woman repeatedly calls a man’s home and cell many times daily and frequently tries to visit his home and workplace despite his attempts to stop her. He sues for invasion of privacy.","trigger":"Repeated unwanted calls/visits; intrusion upon seclusion claim.","rule":"Intrusion upon seclusion occurs when a defendant intentionally intrudes (physically or otherwise) on another’s private affairs in a manner highly offensive to a reasonable person; repeated harassing calls/visits can qualify.","sub":"Privacy – Intrusion upon seclusion","family":"Privacy","unit":20},{"n":100,"summary":"Newspaper publishes story alleging a U.S. senator has close ties to organized crime. Senator sues for defamation, claiming the allegations are false.","trigger":"Public official/public figure defamation; actual malice standard.","rule":"A public official/figure must prove falsity and actual malice—knowledge of falsity or reckless disregard for the truth—to recover for defamation.","sub":"Defamation – Public figures; actual malice","family":"Defamation","unit":19},{"n":101,"summary":"Two friends play catch with a baseball across a busy street for 20 minutes, nearly hitting cars/pedestrians. A bicyclist comes along; a thrown ball hits the bicyclist in the neck but causes no injury. The bicyclist is annoyed. Question: is the thrower guilty of battery?","trigger":"Transferred intent / substantial certainty; offensive contact without intent to hit that person.","rule":"Battery requires intent to cause harmful or offensive contact (or knowledge to a substantial certainty); accidental contact without such intent is not battery.","sub":"Intentional Torts – Battery; intent requirement","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":1},{"n":102,"summary":"A farmer lets friends hunt quail on his large farm during hunting season but gives no gun-safety instructions. The neighboring landowner, knowing hunters are on the property, takes a shortcut across the farm without the farmer’s permission to visit an acquaintance. While one friend shoots at quail, he carelessly fires and accidentally hits the neighbor, who sues the farmer for the injuries.","trigger":"Injured entrant was trespassing on land during recreational hunting by landowner’s guests; apply traditional landowner duty to trespassers.","rule":"No duty to trespasser (except avoid willful/wanton harm); no duty to warn of known/obvious dangers.","sub":"Premises Liability – Trespassers","family":"Negligence","unit":10},{"n":103,"summary":"A homeowner is injured when an automatic cutoff switch fails to function on a snowblower he is using. The switch worked properly for about a year after purchase, but later failed after the snowblower had been improperly repaired by a mechanic. The operating manual clearly warned against the exact alteration to the switch mechanism that the mechanic made. The mechanic did not have the manual when he performed the repair. The homeowner sues the manufacturer for damages.","trigger":"Product-liability claim where a third party substantially altered/modified a product after sale and the alteration caused the injury.","rule":"Strict products liability is cut off by a substantial alteration after sale that causes the harm.","sub":"Strict Products Liability – Substantial alteration","family":"Products Liability","unit":18},{"n":104,"summary":"Homeowner downhill sues facility owner and solvent supplier for contaminants migrating to her property; supplier seeks summary judgment arguing damages can’t be allocated between them.","trigger":"Concurrent tortfeasors; indivisible injury; summary judgment standard (any genuine dispute).","rule":"Concurrent tortfeasors causing an indivisible injury are jointly and severally liable, so inability to apportion does not defeat plaintiff’s claim.","sub":"Multiple Tortfeasors – Joint & Several Liability","family":"Negligence","unit":14},{"n":105,"summary":"Fumigation company uses highly toxic chemicals in one building; due to hidden hole in firewall, residents in adjacent building become sick; company not negligent.","trigger":"Abnormally dangerous activity; strict liability despite reasonable care; causation shown.","rule":"One who conducts an abnormally dangerous activity is strictly liable for resulting harm, even with reasonable care, if the activity causes the injury.","sub":"Strict Liability – Abnormally Dangerous Activities","family":"Strict Liability","unit":16},{"n":106,"summary":"Pilot makes emergency landing in homeowner’s yard; homeowner orders dogs to attack and injure pilot despite posted warning signs.","trigger":"Private necessity privileges entry; homeowner intentionally causes contact via dogs.","rule":"A person privileged by private necessity is not a trespasser; a landowner who intentionally causes harmful/offensive contact (including through animals) commits battery.","sub":"Intentional Torts – Battery / Privilege (Necessity)","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":5},{"n":107,"summary":"Man and woman engage in illegal drag race; woman’s tire blows, she crashes and injures pedestrian; pedestrian sues man because woman is judgment-proof.","trigger":"Defendants acted in concert (illegal drag race) leading to injury.","rule":"Participants acting in concert in a tortious activity are jointly and severally liable for resulting injuries, even if only one directly caused the impact.","sub":"Multiple Tortfeasors – Acting in Concert / Joint & Several Liability","family":"Negligence","unit":14},{"n":108,"summary":"Doctor molests child while mother is out of room; two weeks later child tells mother; mother suffers severe distress and physical illness; mother sues doctor for IIED on her own behalf.","trigger":"IIED requires intent (or substantial certainty) to cause distress to the plaintiff; conduct not directed at mother and she was not present.","rule":"IIED requires extreme and outrageous conduct intentionally (or with substantial certainty) directed at the plaintiff; a non-present parent generally cannot recover for IIED absent intent to distress the parent. IIED requires intent toward the plaintiff. Here, the plaintiff is the mother. If she hd sued in the name of the child it would be different. But here, the defendant did not do it in front of her.","sub":"Intentional Torts – IIED (Intent Directed at Plaintiff)","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":4},{"n":109,"summary":"Plumbing company hires worker who drives his own truck (with company rack) and is paid for travel between warehouse and sites; on morning commute to work, worker causes accident; victim sues company for negligent hiring.","trigger":"Injury occurs during commute; plaintiff alleges negligent hiring; scope-of-employment (coming-and-going) limits employer liability.","rule":"As a general rule, commuting to and from work is outside the scope of employment; negligent hiring liability typically requires that the injury arise from conduct within the scope of employment.","sub":"Negligent Hiring / Scope of Employment (Coming-and-going rule)","family":"Vicarious Liability","unit":15},{"n":110,"summary":"Customer injured by glass in canned corn; sues canning company; evidence shows neither customer nor third party did anything after opening that would explain glass; no other evidence.","trigger":"Accident suggesting negligence; product under defendant’s control when defect introduced; other causes negated.","rule":"Res ipsa loquitur permits an inference of negligence when the event ordinarily doesn’t occur absent negligence, was caused by an instrumentality in defendant’s control, and other causes are eliminated.","sub":"Negligence — Res Ipsa Loquitur","family":"Negligence","unit":8},{"n":111,"summary":"Hunter shoots at deer and accidentally hits hiker; hiker sues for battery.","trigger":"Indirect contact but no intent to hit person.","rule":"Battery requires intent to cause harmful/offensive contact (purpose or substantial certainty); accidental injury is not battery.","sub":"Battery — Intent Requirement","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":1},{"n":112,"summary":"Pedestrian hit by chair thrown from hotel window; sues occupants of all possible rooms.","trigger":"Plaintiff cannot identify tortfeasor; defendants not all negligent.","rule":"Alternative liability requires all defendants be tortfeasors; plaintiff must show joint wrongdoing.","sub":"Multiple Tortfeasors — Alternative Liability","family":"Negligence","unit":14},{"n":113,"summary":"Plain‑clothes security guard grabbed arguing customer, knocked him down causing injury. Customer sued store for battery under vicarious liability. Store asserted contributory negligence defense.","trigger":"Intentional tort (battery) with contributory negligence asserted.","rule":"Contributory negligence is not a defense to intentional torts such as battery.","sub":"Battery — Defenses","family":"Defenses","unit":23},{"n":114,"summary":"Chemical company stored toxic chemicals in residential community. Despite compliance with regulations and reasonable care, residents suffered harm.","trigger":"Abnormally dangerous activity in residential area.","rule":"Those engaging in abnormally dangerous activities are strictly liable for foreseeable harm even when reasonable care is exercised.","sub":"Strict Liability — Abnormally Dangerous Activities","family":"Strict Liability","unit":16},{"n":115,"summary":"After her husband died in the hospital, a widow directed the hospital to send her husband’s body to a funeral home for burial. The hospital negligently misidentified the husband’s body and sent it to be cremated. When the widow was informed of the mistake, she suffered serious emotional distress and sued the hospital.","trigger":"Negligent mishandling/misidentification of a corpse causing severe emotional distress to the immediate family member.","rule":"A defendant is liable in negligence for conduct especially likely to cause serious emotional distress to the plaintiff; when the plaintiff is the direct victim of the negligent mishandling of a corpse, bystander/zone-of-danger limits do not bar recovery.","sub":"Negligent Infliction of Emotional Distress / Mishandling of Corpse","family":"Negligence","unit":12},{"n":116,"summary":"A hot air balloon touring company operated near a golf course. A fence separated the properties, and the company posted signs warning people not to enter because of the dangers of balloons landing. A golfer hit an errant shot onto the company’s property, ignored the warning signs, and jumped the fence to retrieve her ball. At about the same time, a balloon had mechanical problems and made an emergency landing to avoid crashing; the out-of-control balloon struck and injured the golfer. The jurisdiction treats hot air ballooning as an abnormally dangerous activity. The question asks whether the company has any affirmative defenses in the golfer’s strict-liability action.","trigger":"Strict liability for abnormally dangerous activity + plaintiff knowingly trespasses despite warning signs.","rule":"Strict liability is not absolute: assumption of risk is a defense to strict liability; a plaintiff who knows of the risk and voluntarily encounters it (e.g., ignores warnings and enters) assumes the risk.","sub":"Strict Liability / Abnormally Dangerous Activities / Assumption of Risk","family":"Strict Liability","unit":16},{"n":117,"summary":"A complaint on behalf of an 86-year-old woman who was unable to speak after a stroke alleged that an ambulance service picked her up from her daughter’s house and transported her to a nursing home. A physician’s report attached to the complaint stated the woman’s leg injury would not have occurred in the absence of negligence. The complaint alleged the woman was under the control successively of the ambulance service and then the nursing home during the time she must have sustained the injury, and that either the ambulance service or the nursing home must have negligently moved or handled her causing the injury. Both defendants argued the complaint’s allegations were inadequate; the question asks the best response.","trigger":"Pleading/cause-in-fact problem with two successive custodians; res ipsa requires exclusive control; alternative liability/concurrent tortfeasors generally requires proof all defendants breached duty.","rule":"Res ipsa loquitur requires that the injury-causing instrumentality was in the defendant’s exclusive control; when control was successive and the plaintiff cannot plausibly allege which defendant caused the injury, the best argument is that the facts are primarily within defendants’ knowledge—though plaintiff generally must still identify a negligent tortfeasor.","sub":"Negligence – Res Ipsa Loquitur / Alternative Liability (Successive Control)","family":"Negligence","unit":8},{"n":118,"summary":"A negligence claim arises in an industry that has established customs or standard practices. One party argues that compliance with (or deviation from) industry custom proves the standard of care. The issue is the evidentiary role of custom in determining negligence.","trigger":"Industry custom offered to prove or disprove breach of duty in negligence.","rule":"Custom in the industry is some evidence of negligence, but not conclusive. Custom could be relevant but not conclusive.","sub":"Negligence — Evidence of Custom","family":"Negligence","unit":26},{"n":119,"summary":"A person sues for invasion of privacy based on publication of facts or images connected to an event that was of public interest. The defendant argues the subject matter was newsworthy and occurred in a public place, defeating the privacy claim.","trigger":"Privacy claim vs newsworthiness/public place defense.","rule":"A claim for invasion of privacy will fail if the subject matter was newsworthy, especially if it occurred in a public place.","sub":"Privacy Torts — Newsworthiness Defense","family":"Privacy","unit":20},{"n":120,"summary":"A plaintiff cannot identify exactly what negligent act caused an accident but claims the accident is the kind that ordinarily does not happen without negligence. The plaintiff seeks to rely on res ipsa loquitur, requiring proof of the traditional elements including elimination of other causes and defendant’s control.","trigger":"Plaintiff uses res ipsa loquitur to infer negligence from the nature of the accident.","rule":"Res ipsa loquitur can help a plaintiff show negligence when they aren’t really sure what happened. The P must prove that this event doesn’t happen without negligence, P must eliminate other causes (P not negligent), and show that D had exclusive control over the situation. You CANNOT use RIL when there is multiple people who are possible defendants and not all of them did something, but you dont know which one. Remember herbeal te, and chicken salad. 3 people buy chicken, they each make chicken salad and combine all the saladas. The chickenn had salmonella someone gets sick. Can you sue all three defendants? No, because before suing you have to show that they are all liable. But, you can go around proving negligence but if its one defendant by using RIL. Here, there is 1 defendant the hospital.","sub":"Negligence — Res Ipsa Loquitur","family":"Negligence","unit":8},{"n":121,"summary":"A defendant uses force to protect another person from harm. Even if the elements of a tort could be met, the defendant argues a privilege applies because the defendant reasonably believed intervention was necessary and used reasonable force to defend others.","trigger":"Defense of others privilege (reasonable belief + reasonable force).","rule":"Although the elements of a tort may be met, a person will have a privilege and therefore, not be liable if they acted reasonably to defend others. Here, would the woman (plaintiff) recover (if she shows she is an eggshell plaintiff) when the defendant broke her arm using reasonable force to defend others. The time to show eggshell plaintiff is after you show there is a duty and it was breached.","sub":"Tort Privileges — Defense of Others","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":5},{"n":122,"summary":"An intoxicated man who was standing on a fifth-floor apartment balcony threatened to jump off the building. A bystander pulled the man back into the building, pushed him into a bedroom, and locked the bedroom door from the outside. When the man became sober, the bystander released him from the bedroom.","trigger":"Bystander restrains intoxicated jumper","rule":"Defense of others is a privilege barring tort liability when reasonable force prevents imminent harm. REMEMBER IF YOU ARE ASKED IN A TORT QUESTION FOR A DEFENSE YOU HAVE TO TALK ABOUT THE UNDERLYING ELEMENTS OF THE TORT.","sub":"Defense of others","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":5},{"n":123,"summary":"A man rented a beach house for a weeklong vacation. On the day he arrived, just after sunset, he took his bag upstairs to a second-floor bedroom and unpacked. As he was about to head back downstairs, he realized that the stairwell had become too dark to navigate without a light.","trigger":"Plaintiff knowingly descends dark stairs","rule":"At common law, assumption of risk completely bars recovery. Under traditional common law, assumption of risk is a complete bar to recovery; under modern UBE default, plaintiff’s fault merely reduces damages unless the question specifies otherwise. Traditional Common Law (when explicitly stated): • Contributory negligence → TOTAL BAR • Assumption of risk → TOTAL BAR UBE Default (unless told otherwise): • Comparative negligence → reduces recovery • Contributory negligence → merged into comparative • Assumption of risk → usually merged","sub":"Assumption of risk","family":"Defenses","unit":23},{"n":124,"summary":"A wealthy elderly woman was repeatedly harassed by a debt collector over a period of two months. The debt collector was trying to collect a large debt owed to his client by the woman’s impoverished adult son. Although the debt collector knew that the woman was not legally responsible for the son’s debt, he called the woman multiple times each day and threatened to destroy her credit.","trigger":"Debt collector repeatedly threatens elderly woman","rule":"Extreme and outrageous conduct causing severe distress supports IIED.","sub":"IIED","family":"Intentional Torts","unit":4},{"n":125,"summary":"While attending an amusement park’s fireworks display, a spectator was struck and injured by a rocket set off as part of the display. The rocket unexpectedly failed to shoot upward but instead followed a trajectory parallel to the ground and struck the spectator. The spectator has sued the amusement park for damages. Question asks under which theory is the spectator most likely able to obtain a summary judgment as to liability?","trigger":"Fireworks rocket injures spectator","rule":"Abnormally dangerous activities impose strict liability (No need to prove breach. Why others fail?\n\n• Battery? ❌ no intent • Products liability? ❌ park isn’t seller/manufacturer","sub":"Abnormally dangerous","family":"Strict Liability","unit":16},{"n":126,"summary":"The owner of a home in a rural area had for many years enjoyed unspoiled views of the surrounding countryside from her back deck. Several months ago, a neighboring farmer placed unsightly items, including an old, rusted tractor and some machine parts, entirely on his own property, but in a location visible from the homeowner’s deck. The homeowner asked the farmer to move the items to a different area of the farm, out of the homeowner’s line of sight.","trigger":"Unsightly farm equipment visible to neighbor","rule":"Unsightly conditions alone usually are not nuisance.","sub":"Private nuisance","family":"Nuisance","unit":21}]},{"slug":"evidence","name":"Evidence","bigPicture":"WHERE YOU START. Libertad probatoria and sana crítica. Anything relevant may be offered (CPC art. 395; COPP art. 182), and a PROFESSIONAL judge weighs it — spontaneity, coherence, corroboration — and writes his reasons. Documents are strong proof: the documento público proves itself (C.C. arts. 1.359–1.360), and the expediente is the case. The perito is the court's neutral auxiliary, appointed to inform the judge. And there is no reason to keep material away from a decision-maker who is trained to discount it — exclusion is the exception, weight is the rule.\n\nWHAT THE U.S. SYSTEM IS. The lay jury turns evidence law into a machine of QUARANTINE. Because the fact-finder is untrained and cannot simply be instructed to weigh carefully, the rules decide IN ADVANCE what may be heard at all — and admissibility is indexed by three things: PURPOSE, FORM and DOOR. The same words are barred for one purpose and admitted for another (character to show propensity, no; the same act to show absence of mistake, yes). Form controls outcomes: an unsworn prior statement may only impeach, while the same statement sworn at a deposition both impeaches and proves the fact. And hearsay is not a question of weight but a WALL, opened only by a finite list of numbered doors, each with its own conditions.\n\nWHAT DIVERGES. Three trained habits generate the wrong answer. (1) Asking “is this reliable?” instead of “for what purpose is it offered, and through which door does it come in?” — reliability is your question; the U.S. question is admissibility by category. (2) Trusting paper: a police report is inadmissible AGAINST the accused, a one-off letter fails the business-records elements however honest it is, and every embedded layer of hearsay needs its own separate door. (3) Expecting the expert to be the court's neutral auxiliary whose file comes into the record — U.S. experts are retained, prepared and paid by a party, tested by cross-examination rather than by institutional neutrality, and the material their opinion RESTS on generally stays out even when the opinion stands.\n\nWHAT IS THE SAME. Eleven units are convergence, and they are the cheapest points in the module: spontaneity as a mark of reliability (excited utterance, present sense impression); bias, interest and sensory defect as grounds to attack a witness, exactly as in the tacha de testigos; privileges for professional secrecy and marital confidence; the judge's power to control the order and mode of proof and to exclude what is disproportionately prejudicial; summaries of voluminous records; and prueba trasladada behind former testimony. Bank those — then learn purpose, form and door, because that is what the other thirty-one units are about.","families":[{"name":"Impeachment","count":22},{"name":"Character","count":14},{"name":"Impeachment/Hearsay","count":13},{"name":"Policy Exclusions","count":9},{"name":"Privileges","count":9},{"name":"Hearsay","count":8},{"name":"404(b)","count":7},{"name":"801(d)(2)","count":5},{"name":"803(6)/(8)","count":5},{"name":"Best Evidence Rule (1002)","count":5},{"name":"Trial Architecture","count":5},{"name":"801(d)(1)","count":4},{"name":"Relevance","count":4},{"name":"702/703/704","count":3},{"name":"803(18)","count":3},{"name":"803(3)","count":3},{"name":"804(b)(3)","count":3},{"name":"Habit vs. Character (FRE 406)","count":3},{"name":"804(b)(1)/806","count":2},{"name":"Foundations","count":2},{"name":"1006","count":1},{"name":"612/803(5)","count":1},{"name":"803(1)/(2)","count":1},{"name":"803(4)","count":1},{"name":"901/902","count":1},{"name":"Constitutional Overlay","count":1},{"name":"Parol Evidence","count":1},{"name":"Rape Shield (FRE 412)","count":1}],"units":[{"n":1,"summary":"A privilege is asserted in a grand jury room; hearsay flows freely in the same proceeding; a judge decides a preliminary admissibility fight without the rules applying to her own inquiry.","trigger":"Forum/stage of the proceeding + which bodies of the FRE apply there + privilege asserted outside the trial itself.","rule":"The Federal Rules of Evidence govern trials, but they relax or disappear in defined zones: grand jury proceedings, preliminary questions under FRE 104(a), sentencing, and bail — where hearsay and lay opinion are usable. PRIVILEGES are the exception to the exception: under FRE 1101(c) the rules on privilege apply at ALL stages of every proceeding, including grand jury and probation revocation.","sub":"Foundations – Scope of the FRE; Privileges Apply Everywhere","sensitivity":"Two independent switches: (1) the STAGE of the proceeding turns most of the FRE on or off (grand jury = relaxed); (2) privilege is stage-proof — the one objection that survives everywhere. Asking 'is this a trial?' before applying any rule is the discipline the exam rewards.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the rules of proof are the rules of proof — they govern the whole expediente” → the civilian procedural codes regulate evidence uniformly across the written file, so a rules-free grand jury (an institution with no Venezuelan analogue) and a stage-indexed FRE feel incoherent. The trained instinct either over-applies the FRE to preliminary stages or, once told the rules are 'relaxed,' wrongly assumes privileges relax with them — precisely the 1101(c) trap.","authority":"Scope of the FRE: FRE 1101 (privileges apply at all stages; rules relaxed in grand jury, preliminary matters)."},{"n":2,"summary":"A robbery defendant on house arrest rents a van under a false name and is caught far away; the defense objects that flight proves nothing conclusively.","trigger":"Evidence offered + any tendency to make a fact of consequence more or less probable (401) + exclusion only if probative value SUBSTANTIALLY outweighed by unfair prejudice or other 403 dangers.","rule":"Relevance is a minimal threshold: evidence is relevant if it has ANY tendency to make a fact of consequence more or less probable (FRE 401), and relevant evidence is admissible unless a specific rule excludes it (402). FRE 403 excludes relevant evidence only when its probative value is SUBSTANTIALLY outweighed by unfair prejudice, confusion, or delay — a rule of inclusion tilted toward admission. Consciousness-of-guilt conduct (flight, false name, evasion) passes 401 easily; its weight is for the jury.","sub":"Relevance – 401/403: The Rule of Inclusion","sensitivity":"The 403 balance is asymmetric by design: the objector must show the prejudice SUBSTANTIALLY outweighs probative value — a tie admits. Answers that exclude because evidence is 'inconclusive' or 'prejudicial' (rather than UNFAIRLY so) misread both halves.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian's libertad probatoria and sana crítica (CPC art. 507) point the same way — indicios like flight are admissible and weighed freely, so the inclusion instinct is broadly RIGHT. The calibration friction: in the U.S. architecture admissibility is a JUDGE's gate distinct from weight (the jury's domain), and 403's 'substantially outweighed' formula is a legal standard to recite, not a global judicial discretion — the civilian tends to argue weight when the question asks admissibility.","authority":"Relevance: FRE 401–403; consciousness of guilt (flight, false name) as circumstantial relevance."},{"n":3,"summary":"The prosecution offers a defendant's prior uncharged act; the defense objects that the act was never proven — no conviction, accusers never testified — so the jury should never hear it.","trigger":"Other-act evidence + preliminary fact question (did the act occur?) + FRE 104(b) conditional-relevance standard + who decides (judge screens, jury finds).","rule":"When relevance depends on a preliminary fact (that the defendant committed the other act), FRE 104(b) — not 104(a) — governs: the judge does NOT find the fact by a preponderance; she asks only whether a reasonable jury COULD find the act occurred by a preponderance (Huddleston v. United States). No conviction is required; the ultimate credibility fight belongs to the jury.","sub":"Relevance – Conditional Relevance: The Huddleston Screen (104(b))","sensitivity":"The switch is WHO decides and by WHAT standard: judge-as-gatekeeper (104(a), preponderance, rules of evidence do not bind her) versus judge-as-screener (104(b), sufficiency-for-a-reasonable-jury). Misassigning the other-act question to 104(a) — 'the judge must first find it happened' — is the recurring wrong answer.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the judge weighs all the evidence and decides what happened — there is no one else to decide” → in a jury system the admissibility/weight frontier must be policed, and 104(b) hands the preliminary fact to the jury on a mere sufficiency showing. The civilian single-decider schema (juez de mérito valuing the whole expediente under sana crítica) has no slot for a two-tier fact-finding split, so the trained reflex systematically inflates the judge's screening role.","authority":"Conditional relevance: FRE 104(b); Huddleston v. United States, 485 U.S. 681 (1988)."},{"n":4,"summary":"A judge orders the plaintiff to testify first, excludes the other witnesses from the courtroom on her own initiative, and counsel's hearsay objection arrives only after the witness has left the stand.","trigger":"Trial-management powers (FRE 611, 615) + preservation of error (FRE 103): timely + specific objection, or the issue is waived (plain error residue).","rule":"The court controls the mode and order of examination (FRE 611) and MUST sequester witnesses on a party's request — or MAY do so sua sponte (FRE 615); a PARTY, however, may never be excluded (615(a) exemption). Under FRE 103, error is preserved only by a TIMELY and SPECIFIC objection (or offer of proof for excluded evidence); an objection raised after the witness leaves the stand is untimely, and unpreserved error survives only as plain error affecting substantial rights.","sub":"Trial Architecture – Judicial Control (611/615) and Preservation (FRE 103) (Master)","sensitivity":"Two independent switches: (1) sequestration is mandatory on request but discretionary sua sponte, and never reaches a party; (2) preservation is a one-shot clock — the RIGHT objection at the WRONG moment is worthless, and appellate review shrinks to plain error. Redirect is confined to significant new matter from cross; cross is confined to the scope of direct plus credibility (the field-test officer cannot be crossed on probable cause).","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the defect is real, the judge examines it whenever raised — nulidad procesal” → in U.S. trial practice a valid objection dies by timing. The civilian schema of written, judge-driven proceedings (impulso procesal, nulidades raisable through the process) has no contemporaneous-objection discipline and no adversarial examination sequence (direct/cross/redirect); the trained reflex treats objection timing as housekeeping when it is outcome-determinative.","authority":"Trial architecture: FRE 611 (mode/order), 615 (sequestration; party exemption), 103 (timely/specific objection; plain error)."},{"n":5,"summary":"A criminal defendant calls a witness to vouch for his honesty or peacefulness; the prosecution answers with a rebuttal witness, with cross-examination about specific acts, or with a trait the defendant never raised.","trigger":"Criminal case + defendant INTRODUCES pertinent-trait character evidence (opinion/reputation only) → door opens → prosecution rebuts by the same methods, crosses the character witness on specific acts (no extrinsic proof), and must stay within the SAME trait.","rule":"In a criminal case the accused may offer character evidence of a pertinent trait as circumstantial evidence of conduct, but only by reputation or opinion — never specific instances on direct (FRE 404(a)(2)(A), 405(a)). Only the defendant opens the door; an opening statement is not evidence and does not open it. Once opened, the prosecution may rebut with reputation/opinion on the SAME trait and may cross-examine the defendant's character witness about specific acts probative of the trait ('Have you heard…?'), taking the answer without extrinsic proof. Rebuttal on an unrelated trait (temper, violence, when honesty was offered) is improper.","sub":"Character – The Criminal Defendant's Door: Methods, Same-Trait Rebuttal (Master)","sensitivity":"Four gates in sequence: WHO opens (defendant only), HOW (reputation/opinion, no specific acts on direct), WHAT rebuttal (same trait, same methods; specific acts only on cross of the character witness, never extrinsic), WHEN (introducing evidence, not previewing it in an opening statement). The stop-sign trilogy's criminal panel sits here.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the court should hear who the accused really is — his record and conduct” → the door-and-methods machinery has no civilian analogue: in a Venezuelan expediente the accused's antecedentes and personality circulate freely for the judge's sana crítica. The civilian misses (1) that character is BLOCKED until the defendant chooses, (2) that the form of proof (reputation/opinion vs. specific acts) is itself outcome-determinative, and (3) that the choice is strategic and irreversible — a waiver-flavored trap inside a substantive rule.","authority":"Criminal defendant's character door: FRE 404(a)(2), 405(a); opening statements are not evidence (door opened only by evidence)."},{"n":6,"summary":"A civil litigant offers proof that a party is a careful driver, an honest man, or an unfit parent — sometimes rejected outright, sometimes suddenly admissible by any method including specific acts.","trigger":"Civil case + character offered as circumstantial evidence of conduct (barred) VS. character as an ESSENTIAL ELEMENT of the claim or defense (defamation truth/damages, negligent hiring/entrustment, child custody) → any party, any method (FRE 405(b)).","rule":"In civil cases character evidence is inadmissible as circumstantial evidence that a party acted in conformity (no 'cautious driver' proof in a negligence suit). Where character IS an essential element of the claim or defense — defamation (plaintiff's reputation on truth and damages), negligent hiring/entrustment (the entrustee's fitness), child custody (parental fitness) — any party may prove it, by reputation, opinion, OR specific instances (FRE 405(b)).","sub":"Character – Civil Cases: The Essential-Element Switch","sensitivity":"One classification flips everything: the same accident-free-driver testimony is inadmissible against the driver-defendant (conduct-in-conformity) yet fully admissible in the negligent-entrustment claim against the owner who lent him the car — same facts, different defendant, opposite ruling. The stop-sign trilogy's civil panels sit here.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “conduct is proven by who the person is — the judge weighs it all” → the categorical civil ban, and its essential-element trapdoor, must be built from zero: sana crítica (CPC art. 507) admits personality evidence freely and grades only its WEIGHT. The civilian both over-admits (propensity proof in ordinary civil cases) and under-uses the switch (missing that negligent entrustment or custody makes character provable by ANY method, including the specific acts the general rule forbids).","authority":"Civil character / essential element: FRE 404(a), 405(b) (defamation, negligent hiring/entrustment, child custody)."},{"n":7,"summary":"The prosecution offers the defendant's other crimes or bad acts — a prior wallet theft, an identical tax-shifting scheme, a robbery with the same red-painted revolver — while the defense cries propensity.","trigger":"Other crimes, wrongs, or acts (FRE 404(b)) + proper non-propensity purpose (motive, intent, plan/preparation, identity/modus operandi, knowledge, absence of mistake) + Huddleston sufficiency + notice in criminal cases.","rule":"Other-acts evidence is never admissible to show character in order to prove conduct in conformity, but is admissible for non-propensity purposes: motive, opportunity, intent, preparation, plan, knowledge, identity (a distinctive modus operandi signature), and absence of mistake or accident (FRE 404(b)(2)). The act need not have produced charges or conviction; the proponent needs only evidence sufficient for a reasonable jury to find the act occurred (Huddleston, FRE 104(b)) plus reasonable pretrial notice in criminal cases. The evidence remains subject to Rule 403 and a limiting instruction on request.","sub":"404(b) – Other Acts: The Proper-Purpose Architecture (Master)","sensitivity":"Three-step discipline: (1) name the SPECIFIC non-propensity purpose the case actually disputes (claimed mistake → absence of mistake; identity contested → signature m.o.); (2) check Huddleston sufficiency — the judge does not find the act by a preponderance, the jury must merely be able to; (3) run 403. A purpose the defense has not put in issue is a wrong answer dressed as a right one.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “prior conduct shows who he is — the judge may weigh it” (or the inverse: “prior acts are simply inadmissible”) → U.S. law splits the atom: the SAME act is categorically barred for propensity yet admissible for a listed purpose, with a jury-sufficiency screen and a limiting instruction managing the leak. The civilian expediente has no purpose-indexed admissibility — antecedentes flow to the judge — so the trained reflex misses that admissibility depends on WHICH inference the evidence is offered to support, not on what it shows.","authority":"404(b) proper purposes: FRE 404(b)(2) (motive, intent, plan, identity/m.o., knowledge, absence of mistake); Huddleston sufficiency; criminal-case notice."},{"n":8,"summary":"Other-acts evidence with a legitimate purpose arrives wrapped in inflammatory packaging — heroin and needles in a theft case — and the fight shifts from purpose to prejudice.","trigger":"Proper 404(b) purpose established + FRE 403: probative value substantially outweighed by unfair prejudice (propensity leakage, inflammatory detail) → exclusion or redaction despite the proper purpose.","rule":"A proper purpose under 404(b) does not immunize the evidence: under FRE 403 the court excludes it if its probative value is substantially outweighed by the danger of unfair prejudice — particularly where inflammatory details (drugs, violence) invite the forbidden propensity inference the proper purpose cannot contain. Courts may admit a sanitized version and must give a limiting instruction on request.","sub":"404(b) – The Rule 403 Screen and Limiting Instructions","sensitivity":"403 is asymmetric by design — a rule of inclusion (exclusion only when prejudice SUBSTANTIALLY outweighs) — yet it is the live battleground of every 404(b) fight: the answer often turns not on whether a purpose exists but on whether the packaging (heroin/needles) overwhelms it. Redaction and limiting instructions are the calibrated middle answers.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct that the judge weighs probative value against prejudice is broadly RIGHT — proporcionalidad and sana crítica do similar work. The calibration friction: 403's threshold is deliberately tilted toward admission ('substantially outweighed'), the weighing protects a lay JURY rather than a professional judge, and the exam expects the middle answers (redact, instruct) rather than all-or-nothing rulings.","authority":"The 403 screen: FRE 403; sanitization and limiting instructions (FRE 105)."},{"n":9,"summary":"A museum guard proves he leaves at 4:30 sharp every day to bet at the track; a business proves its invariable mailing routine — each offered to show the conduct happened again on the disputed day.","trigger":"Habit (specific, regular, semi-automatic response to a particular situation) or organizational routine practice (FRE 406) → admissible to prove conduct in conformity, no corroboration or eyewitness required.","rule":"Evidence of a person's habit — a regular, specific, semi-automatic response to a repeated situation — or of an organization's routine practice is admissible to prove that on a particular occasion the person or organization acted in accordance with it (FRE 406), and it may be admitted without corroboration and without an eyewitness. General character traits ('careful,' 'punctual person') do not qualify; the conduct must be situation-specific and near-reflexive.","sub":"Habit vs. Character (FRE 406) – The Specificity Line","sensitivity":"The admissibility line runs between 'he is a careful driver' (character — barred in the civil conformity posture) and 'he stops at that crossing and looks both ways every workday' (habit — admissible). Specificity, regularity, and automatism are the three markers; organizational routine ('thousands of similar transactions') rides the same rule.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “costumbre — custom — is a source of law, and personal conduct is character evidence” → FRE 406 habit is neither: not normative custom, not character, but an individual's regularized behavior admitted precisely where character is barred. The false translation (hábito/costumbre) hides the doctrinal function: habit is the exam's escape hatch from the character ban, and the civilian either rejects it as propensity or mislabels it as custom.","authority":"Habit: FRE 406 (no corroboration or eyewitness required; organizational routine practice)."},{"n":10,"summary":"A sexual-assault complainant's prior sexual history is offered — in a criminal prosecution to suggest consent, or in a civil suit against a plaintiff who put her virginity in issue.","trigger":"Victim's sexual behavior or predisposition (FRE 412) + criminal exceptions (specific source-of-evidence facts, prior acts with the accused re: consent, constitutional right) vs. civil reverse-403 balancing + reputation only if placed in controversy.","rule":"Rape-shield: evidence of a victim's sexual behavior or predisposition is generally inadmissible. Criminal exceptions: specific instances to prove another source of semen/injury, prior acts between the victim and the accused offered on consent, and evidence whose exclusion would violate the defendant's constitutional rights. In CIVIL cases the court may admit it only if its probative value SUBSTANTIALLY OUTWEIGHS the danger of harm to any victim and unfair prejudice to any party — a reverse-403 that presumes exclusion — and reputation evidence only if the victim placed her reputation in controversy.","sub":"Rape Shield (FRE 412) – Criminal Exceptions vs. Civil Reverse-403","sensitivity":"The rule inverts 403 twice: the default flips to exclusion, and the burden flips to the proponent. The civil track's two switches — reverse balancing + the placed-in-controversy gate for reputation — decide the exam question; a plaintiff who alleges she was 'preserving her virginity' opens the reputation door herself.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “relevant history comes in and the judge weighs it” → a victim-protective categorical exclusion with enumerated exceptions and an inverted balancing test has no analogue in libre valoración; the civilian both over-admits (treating history as ordinary credibility material) and misses the structural inversions — WHO bears the burden and WHICH way the tie breaks.","authority":"Rape shield: FRE 412 (criminal exceptions; civil reverse-403; reputation only if placed in controversy)."},{"n":11,"summary":"After the accident the defendant fixes the walkway, redesigns the machine, or a NEIGHBOR clears the ice — and the plaintiff offers the fix to prove there was a defect all along.","trigger":"Measure taken AFTER the injury that would have made it less likely (FRE 407) + offered to prove negligence/defect → barred; offered to prove ownership/control or FEASIBILITY of precautions WHEN DISPUTED, or impeachment → admissible; measures by non-parties → 407 does not apply.","rule":"Subsequent remedial measures are inadmissible to prove negligence, culpable conduct, a product defect, or a need for warning (FRE 407) — the deterrence rationale protects the repairing DEFENDANT only. They ARE admissible to prove ownership or control, to impeach, or to prove feasibility of precautionary measures when the defendant disputes it (claiming an alternative was impossible opens the door to the post-accident redesign). Repairs made by someone other than the defendant are outside the rule entirely.","sub":"Policy Exclusions – Subsequent Remedial Measures (FRE 407): Ban and Feasibility Door","sensitivity":"Two switches decide these questions: WHO repaired (third-party repairs escape 407's deterrence logic) and WHAT the defense claimed (an impossibility/infeasibility defense converts the barred redesign into admissible rebuttal). The exclusion is purpose-indexed, not evidence-indexed — the same repair is barred for defect and admitted for feasibility.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “he fixed it — that admits the danger; and evidence is evidence, the judge weighs it all” → purpose-indexed categorical exclusions built on extrinsic policy (encouraging repairs) have no counterpart in libre valoración, where subsequent conduct is simply an indicio. The civilian both over-admits (using the repair as confession-by-conduct) and, once taught the ban, over-applies it — missing the third-party and feasibility doors that make the rule a switchboard rather than a wall.","authority":"Subsequent remedial measures: FRE 407 (feasibility/impossibility door; third-party repairs outside the rule)."},{"n":12,"summary":"During settlement talks the defendant admits fault; after a crash a driver apologizes and offers to pay the hospital bill — later each statement is offered at trial.","trigger":"Statement during compromise negotiations of a DISPUTED claim (FRE 408) vs. offer to pay medical expenses (FRE 409) + accompanying admissions → the two rules protect different scopes.","rule":"FRE 408 excludes compromise offers AND conduct or statements made during compromise negotiations of a claim disputed as to validity or amount — including factual admissions — when offered to prove or disprove the claim or to impeach by prior inconsistent statement (permitted other purposes: proving bias, negating undue delay). FRE 409 excludes ONLY the offer or payment of medical expenses itself: admissions accompanying the humanitarian offer remain admissible (and are party-opponent statements). A claim must be disputed for 408; an undisputed-debt 'discount' negotiation is unprotected.","sub":"Policy Exclusions – Compromise (408) vs. Medical Payments (409): The Accompanying-Statement Split","sensitivity":"One variable does all the work: WHICH rule's umbrella the statement stands under. 408 shelters the whole negotiation (statements included); 409 shelters only the payment offer, so 'I wasn't paying attention — I'll cover your bills' splits down the middle: the offer stays out, the admission comes in. The 408 dispute-requirement and the criminal-case wrinkle (408(a)(2)) are the secondary gates.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “an extrajudicial confession is a confession — use it” (or the mirror: “good-faith gestures can never be used”) → U.S. law draws a scope line the civilian schema lacks: negotiation speech is categorically protected to encourage settlement, humanitarian gestures are protected only as to the gesture itself. With no codal analogue to purpose-built exclusionary umbrellas, the trained reflex misgrades exactly the mixed statement the MBE loves.","authority":"Compromise vs. medical payments: FRE 408 (statements protected; disputed claim required), 409 (offer only; accompanying admissions IN), 410."},{"n":13,"summary":"The plaintiff wants the jury to hear that the defendant carries a $1M liability policy — or that the defendant is still listed as the truck's insurer after claiming it sold the truck.","trigger":"Liability insurance (FRE 411) offered to prove negligence/wrongful conduct or ability to pay → barred; offered to prove OWNERSHIP or CONTROL when disputed, agency, or bias of a witness → admissible.","rule":"Evidence that a person was or was not insured against liability is not admissible to prove negligence or other wrongful conduct, or ability to pay (FRE 411). It is admissible for other purposes — proving agency, OWNERSHIP, or CONTROL when disputed (the party still insuring the truck it claims to have sold), or the bias or prejudice of a witness (the insurer's investigator).","sub":"Policy Exclusions – Liability Insurance (FRE 411): Fault Barred, Ownership/Control Door","sensitivity":"Purpose selection is the whole question: the identical certificate of insurance is inadmissible on fault and admissible on disputed ownership. The dispute requirement is the secondary gate — if ownership is conceded, the door closes.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct that insurance says nothing about culpa is RIGHT — and Venezuelan procedure would likewise treat it as irrelevant to fault. The residual friction is architectural: 411 is a categorical rule with enumerated doors rather than a relevance judgment, so the exam tests the DOORS (ownership, control, bias), and the civilian who reasons only 'irrelevant to fault' misses that the same evidence walks in through a listed purpose.","authority":"Liability insurance: FRE 411 (ownership/control, agency, bias doors)."},{"n":14,"summary":"A witness with something at stake — the ex-wife after a bitter divorce, the business-partner alibi, the supervisor the plaintiff had reported for theft, the member of a tax-refusing sect testifying against the government — is challenged for slant.","trigger":"Impeachment for bias, interest, or motive to lie + foundation on cross + extrinsic proof permitted (non-collateral) + FRE 610 line (religion: not for credibility, yes for bias).","rule":"Bias, interest, or motive to falsify is always relevant to credibility and is never collateral: the witness may be crossed on it and, after an opportunity to explain or deny, it may be proved by extrinsic evidence (U.S. v. Abel), subject to 403. Religious beliefs are inadmissible to attack or support credibility as such (FRE 610), but membership or belief may be shown to prove BIAS (e.g., allegiance or hostility to a party). Bias evidence may also be substantively relevant (retaliatory motive in a discharge case).","sub":"Impeachment – Bias, Interest, Motive (Master)","sensitivity":"Two switches: (1) collateral-ness — bias never is, so extrinsic proof survives where 608(b) extrinsic proof dies; (2) the 610 line — the SAME religious-affiliation question is improper as a credibility attack and proper as a bias showing; classification of the purpose decides.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian tacha de testigos (CPC arts. 477 ff.: interest, kinship, enmity) targets exactly this — the instinct converges. The calibration friction: the civilian schema DISQUALIFIES the biased witness ex ante (inhabilidad), while U.S. law ADMITS the testimony and arms the jury with the bias showing — exclusion vs. impeachment is the structural difference; and the 610 purpose-split has no codal analogue.","authority":"Bias: U.S. v. Abel, 469 U.S. 45 (1984) (never collateral; extrinsic OK); FRE 610 (religion: not credibility, yes bias)."},{"n":15,"summary":"The eyewitness wasn't wearing her required glasses forty yards away — or an eyewitness heard the shot BEFORE the lunge the defendant swears came first: attacks on what a witness could perceive, and flat contradictions of what a witness said.","trigger":"Impeachment of perception/capacity (sensory defects) or by contradiction + extrinsic evidence allowed on non-collateral matters.","rule":"A witness's capacity to perceive, remember, or relate is always open to attack, and sensory defects (vision, hearing, intoxication) may be proved by extrinsic evidence — they are never collateral. Impeachment by contradiction — proving through other evidence that a fact the witness asserted is false — is likewise permitted with extrinsic evidence when the contradicted fact is material to the case (a rebuttal eyewitness's contrary timeline is both impeachment and substantive rebuttal); on purely collateral matters, the examiner is stuck with the witness's answer.","sub":"Impeachment – Perception Defects; Contradiction (Extrinsic Allowed)","sensitivity":"The gate is COLLATERALNESS, not the method: perception and material contradiction take extrinsic proof; trivia does not. Note the double character of material contradiction — the same rebuttal witness impeaches AND proves the substantive timeline (self-defense fails if the shot preceded the lunge).","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: attacking what the witness could see is universal advocacy — sana crítica converges. The residual friction is architectural: the collateral/non-collateral line exists to protect JURY time and focus, a triage the civilian written dossier never needed — so the civilian reflex misses why some contradictions may be proved and others must rest on the cross-examiner's question alone.","authority":"Perception & contradiction: capacity attacks and impeachment by contradiction; collateral-matter limit on extrinsic proof."},{"n":16,"summary":"A party wants to parade witnesses vouching that its witness is honest — before anyone said otherwise; after a conviction surfaces on cross, the same vouching becomes proper rehabilitation.","trigger":"Character for truthfulness (FRE 608(a)): support ONLY after attack + methods limited to reputation/opinion + rehabilitation matched to the attack.","rule":"A witness's character for truthfulness may be supported only AFTER it has been attacked (bolstering is barred); both attack and support proceed by reputation or opinion testimony, not specific instances on direct (FRE 608(a)). An attack on character — e.g., impeachment with a crime of dishonesty — opens the door to truthful-character evidence; mere contradiction or bias impeachment generally does not. Support must respond to the kind of attack made.","sub":"Impeachment – 608(a): Attack First, Then Support","sensitivity":"Sequencing rule: the ORDER of events decides admissibility — identical vouching testimony is barred bolstering before the attack and proper rehabilitation after it. Second switch: WHAT kind of attack occurred (character-based vs. mere contradiction/bias) controls whether the door opened at all.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “a party may always shore up its witness's credibility” → not before an attack. The civilian free-proof instinct has no bolstering bar — credibility material flows into the expediente at any time — so the trained reflex misses that timing and attack-type are outcome-determinative, not stylistic.","authority":"Truthfulness character: FRE 608(a) (reputation/opinion; support only after attack; conviction attack qualifies)."},{"n":17,"summary":"The cross-examiner holds a transcript proving the witness lied on his résumé — and a certified copy of the witness's felony conviction: one document is unusable, the other walks in.","trigger":"Specific untruthful acts (FRE 608(b)): cross-examination only, NO extrinsic evidence vs. convictions (FRE 609): provable by the record — the extrinsic-evidence split.","rule":"Specific instances of a witness's conduct probative of untruthfulness may be inquired into on cross-examination, but may NOT be proved by extrinsic evidence — the examiner takes the witness's answer (FRE 608(b)). Convictions qualifying under FRE 609 are the categorical exception: they may be proved by the public record, including after a denial. The same underlying dishonest act is therefore cross-only if unconvicted and provable if convicted.","sub":"Impeachment – The Extrinsic-Evidence Split: 608(b) vs. 609","sensitivity":"One variable — whether the dishonesty ripened into a CONVICTION — flips the availability of extrinsic proof. The trap runs both directions: offering the transcript/newspaper/video (extrinsic) for an unconvicted act fails however probative; and forgetting that the conviction record enters even after denial forfeits the impeachment.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the document proves the lie — admit it” → barred for the unconvicted act. Documentary proof enjoys primacy in the civilian written procedure (documento público, plena fe), so a rule that PREFERS the witness's oral answer over conclusive extrinsic paper — to keep the jury off collateral trails — reads as irrational until the jury-economy rationale is supplied; the conviction/no-conviction switch must be learned as a formal gate.","authority":"The extrinsic split: FRE 608(b) (specific acts: cross only, no extrinsic) vs. FRE 609 (convictions: record admissible)."},{"n":18,"summary":"A prior felony is offered against a testifying witness: against an ordinary witness, against an inmate-plaintiff with a repugnant old conviction, against the accused himself whose prior crime mirrors the charge.","trigger":"Felony conviction (≤10 years) not involving dishonesty (FRE 609(a)(1)) + witness classification → select the balancing track: plain 403 for ordinary witnesses vs. the stricter accused-specific gate.","rule":"A felony conviction within ten years may impeach any witness. For witnesses OTHER than a criminal defendant, it is admissible subject to ordinary Rule 403 (excludable only if prejudice substantially outweighs probative value) — FRE 609(a)(1)(A). For the CRIMINAL ACCUSED who testifies, admission requires that probative value OUTWEIGH the prejudicial effect to that defendant (FRE 609(a)(1)(B)) — a stricter, defendant-protective gate. Similarity between the prior crime and the charged crime heightens prejudice and favors exclusion; low-truthfulness felonies (violence, sex offenses) carry little probative weight.","sub":"Impeachment – 609(a)(1): The Balancing Tracks by Witness Type","sensitivity":"WHO is being impeached selects the test: ordinary witness → admission-tilted 403; testifying accused → exclusion-tilted special balance. The classic exclusion posture: the accused's prior conviction for the SAME crime charged (burglary impeaching a burglary defendant).","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “antecedentes penales are in the file — the judge knows them” → in a jury system the prior record is quarantined behind witness-type-specific balancing gates, and reaches the jury only for CREDIBILITY with a limiting instruction. The civilian dossier architecture (record visible to the professional fact-finder) inverts here; the trained reflex misses both the quarantine and the track split.","authority":"609(a)(1) balancing tracks: FRE 609(a)(1)(A) (plain 403) vs. (B) (accused-protective gate; similarity heightens prejudice)."},{"n":19,"summary":"A conviction for perjury, false statement, or fraud — felony or misdemeanor, however humble — is offered against any witness: no balancing, no discretion.","trigger":"Conviction involving a dishonest act or false statement (crimen falsi, FRE 609(a)(2)) within ten years → automatic admission against ANY witness; misdemeanors qualify ONLY via this route.","rule":"Convictions for crimes involving dishonesty or false statement (perjury, fraud, false pretenses, embezzlement) MUST be admitted to impeach any witness — including the accused — with no Rule 403 or 609 balancing, regardless of whether the crime was a felony or misdemeanor (FRE 609(a)(2)). Misdemeanor convictions have no other door: if the misdemeanor does not involve dishonesty, it is inadmissible under 609 entirely. Crimes of stealth or violence (plain larceny, burglary, assault) are not crimen falsi.","sub":"Impeachment – 609(a)(2): Crimen Falsi, the Automatic Track","sensitivity":"Two switches: (1) the ELEMENT test — deceit must be an element (false pretenses yes, larceny no); (2) the misdemeanor gate — crimen falsi is the only 609 door for misdemeanors. 'Automatic' survives every balancing objection but not the 609(b) ten-year clock.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “all crimes speak equally to character” (or “minor offenses cannot matter”) → U.S. law grades convictions by their RELATION TO TRUTHFULNESS, not gravity: a nine-month false-statement misdemeanor enters automatically while a murder needs balancing. The civilian severity instinct (gravedad del delito) mis-sorts the categories — the axis is deceit, not seriousness.","authority":"Crimen falsi: FRE 609(a)(2) (automatic; element of deceit; only 609 door for misdemeanors)."},{"n":20,"summary":"The conviction is old: released from prison just over — or just under — ten years before trial; the proponent forgot to give notice.","trigger":"More than 10 years since conviction or release (whichever is later) (FRE 609(b)) → reverse balancing (probative substantially outweighs prejudice, on specific facts) + reasonable written notice.","rule":"If more than ten years have passed since the conviction or the release from confinement for it (whichever is LATER), the conviction is admissible only if (1) its probative value, supported by specific facts and circumstances, SUBSTANTIALLY outweighs its prejudicial effect, and (2) the proponent gives reasonable written notice (FRE 609(b)). The stale-conviction gate overrides even crimen falsi's automatic track. Courts divide on the clock's endpoint (commencement of trial vs. date of testimony); the exam typically measures to the start of trial.","sub":"Impeachment – 609(b): The Ten-Year Clock","sensitivity":"Three mechanical switches: (1) the later-of-two start date (release, not conviction, usually controls); (2) the inverted balance (presumption flips to exclusion); (3) written notice as a procedural prerequisite. Engineered edge: released 10 years + 2 days before testifying but trial began inside the window — endpoint selection decides the track.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “an old conviction is simply weaker — the judge discounts it” → U.S. law makes AGE a formal gate with its own burden, notice requirement, and clock arithmetic, not a matter of weight. The civilian weight-based instinct (sana crítica discounts stale facts) misses that two days on a calendar flip the presumption and that missing a NOTICE kills otherwise admissible impeachment.","authority":"Ten-year clock: FRE 609(b) (later of conviction/release; reverse balancing on specific facts; reasonable written notice; endpoint split)."},{"n":21,"summary":"The witness told the police one story and the jury another — or fell silent when speech would have been natural; the statement was sworn at a deposition, or unsworn on a sidewalk; the proponent wants it believed, not merely noted.","trigger":"Prior inconsistent statement (incl. silence/omission where assertion would be natural) + impeachment always available (FRE 613, extrinsic proof after opportunity to explain/deny) + SUBSTANTIVE use only if sworn at a trial, hearing, other proceeding, or deposition with declarant now testifying (FRE 801(d)(1)(A)).","rule":"Any prior inconsistent statement — including silence or omission when a statement would naturally have been made — may be used to IMPEACH; extrinsic proof requires the witness have an opportunity to explain or deny (FRE 613(b)), and the statement must concern a non-collateral matter. The statement is SUBSTANTIVE evidence only if it was given under penalty of perjury at a trial, hearing, other proceeding, or deposition, and the declarant testifies subject to cross-examination (FRE 801(d)(1)(A)); an oath alone (affidavit) is not enough, and an unsworn statement never carries truth-value — unless it independently qualifies as something else (e.g., a party-opponent statement).","sub":"Impeachment/Hearsay – Prior Inconsistent Statements (Master)","sensitivity":"Two independent axes the exam deliberately crosses: PURPOSE (impeachment: always; substance: gated) and FORM (sworn-at-a-proceeding vs. not). Four combinations, two traps: treating the police-station statement as proof of the fact, and forgetting that the deposition statement does double duty. Silence impeaches when speech would have been natural.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the earlier statement is in the record — the judge weighs both versions” → the bifurcation between credibility-use and truth-use does not exist in the civilian expediente, where every recorded declaration is substantive material under sana crítica. The U.S. split — same words, two legal statuses, selected by oath-and-proceeding formality — is among the least intuitive structures in the subject and generates the module's largest wrong-answer family.","authority":"Prior inconsistent statements: FRE 613 (opportunity to explain/deny for extrinsic proof; silence as inconsistency), 801(d)(1)(A)."},{"n":22,"summary":"An out-of-court statement is offered not to prove what it asserts but for what it does: the operative promise, the nickname that identifies, the newspaper that gave notice, the defamatory words themselves.","trigger":"Out-of-court statement + purpose analysis: offered for the truth of the matter asserted (hearsay, FRE 801(c)) vs. a non-truth purpose — verbal act / legally operative words, effect on the listener (notice, fear), circumstantial evidence of identity or state of mind; true questions are not assertions at all.","rule":"Hearsay is an out-of-court statement offered to prove the truth of the matter asserted (FRE 801(c)); if offered for any other relevant purpose it is simply NOT hearsay — no exception needed. Non-truth purposes include: legally operative words (the promise, the offer, the defamatory utterance — the saying IS the fact); effect on the listener (notice of a defect or of a bankruptcy, provocation of fear); and circumstantial identity or association (a kidnapper calling his partner 'Speed'). A genuine question or command is not an assertion and so not a 'statement' under 801(a). The non-truth purpose must itself be relevant, and Rule 403 polices the leak toward the forbidden truth-use.","sub":"Hearsay – Definition and Nonhearsay Purposes (Master)","sensitivity":"The gate question is never 'is this an out-of-court statement' but 'offered FOR WHAT': the same sentence flips from inadmissible to admissible when the theory of relevance changes. The discipline: name the non-truth purpose, confirm it matters in the case, then check 403. Verbal acts anchor a breach-of-promise claim without any exception.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the witness did not perceive the fact himself — testigo referencial, weigh it accordingly” → the civilian system has no exclusionary hearsay rule at all: secondhand accounts reach the judge and are discounted under sana crítica. The U.S. architecture — categorical exclusion, then purpose-indexed escapes, then enumerated exceptions — must be built from zero, and its first load-bearing wall is the truth-purpose distinction the civilian schema never required.","authority":"Hearsay definition & nonhearsay purposes: FRE 801(a)–(c) (verbal acts; effect on listener/notice; circumstantial mental state; questions not assertions)."},{"n":23,"summary":"A pediatrician is called to testify that a child has been drawing pictures of a man hitting a woman, offered to prove the assault the child never testified about.","trigger":"Nonverbal conduct intended as an assertion (FRE 801(a)) + offered for the truth of what it communicates → hearsay; the expert vehicle (FRE 703) does not convert the basis into admissible evidence.","rule":"A 'statement' includes nonverbal conduct intended by the declarant as an assertion (FRE 801(a)); a child's drawings offered to prove that the depicted assault occurred are assertive conduct offered for their truth — hearsay, admissible only through a genuine exception. Routing the drawings through an expert does not cure the problem: an expert may rely on inadmissible material if experts in the field reasonably do (FRE 703), but the basis itself reaches the jury only if its probative value in evaluating the opinion substantially outweighs its prejudicial effect.","sub":"Hearsay – Assertive Conduct; the Expert Vehicle Does Not Cure","sensitivity":"Two switches: (1) INTENT to assert — conduct meant to communicate is a statement (pointing, nodding, drawing the event), while non-assertive conduct is not hearsay at all; (2) the 703 screen — expert reliance ≠ admissibility, and the reverse-403 disclosure gate rarely opens.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the drawings are powerful indicios — the expert explains them and the judge values them” → in the U.S. structure the drawings are assertive hearsay, and the expert pathway is a basis-disclosure regime, not an admissibility bypass. The civilian prueba indiciaria instinct (free inferential proof) collides with a system that classifies even a child's crayon drawing as a 'statement' subject to the exclusionary architecture.","authority":"Assertive conduct: FRE 801(a); expert vehicle does not cure hearsay (FRE 703)."},{"n":24,"summary":"A testifying witness's own earlier words return: the sworn deposition contradiction, the pre-motive consistent statement offered to answer a fabrication charge, the mug-book identification the witness can no longer repeat in court.","trigger":"Declarant testifies and is subject to cross-examination + FRE 801(d)(1): (A) inconsistent + sworn at a proceeding; (B) consistent + offered to rebut fabrication/improper-motive charge + made BEFORE the motive arose (Tome), or to rehabilitate another credibility attack ((B)(ii), 2014); (C) prior identification of a person.","rule":"Three prior statements of a TESTIFYING witness are defined as non-hearsay (FRE 801(d)(1)): (A) a prior inconsistent statement given under penalty of perjury at a trial, hearing, other proceeding, or deposition; (B) a prior consistent statement offered to rebut an express or implied charge of recent fabrication or improper influence — admissible only if made BEFORE the alleged motive to fabricate arose (Tome) — or, since 2014, to rehabilitate credibility attacked on other grounds ((B)(ii)); and (C) a statement identifying a person the declarant perceived earlier. The prior identification survives even total in-court memory failure so long as the declarant is on the stand and subject to cross (Owens); consistent statements that postdate the motive are inadmissible bolstering.","sub":"801(d)(1) – Prior Statements of a Testifying Witness: The Trio","sensitivity":"Each branch has one signature switch: (A) the sworn-at-a-proceeding formality; (B) the Tome timing line — a statement made AFTER arrest cannot rebut a motive that arose AT arrest; (C) the Owens rule — present cross-examinability, not present memory, is the requirement. The trio exists only while the declarant testifies; absent that, the analysis moves to 803/804.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “her earlier statements are all in the expediente — the judge reads them together” → the U.S. system grants truth-value to a testifying witness's prior words only through three narrow, formality- and timing-gated doors, and treats everything else as bolstering or mere impeachment. The civilian instinct of one continuous declarant narrative misses the gates entirely — especially Tome's clock, which turns an 'obviously corroborating' statement into inadmissible self-serving repetition.","authority":"Prior statements of a testifying witness: FRE 801(d)(1)(A)–(C); Tome v. United States, 513 U.S. 150 (1995) (pre-motive); U.S. v. Owens, 484 U.S. 554 (1988) (memory loss); 2014 amendment (B)(ii)."},{"n":25,"summary":"The party's own words come back: the shoplifter's brag to a waiter, the student driver's 'that light was red,' the bribery defendant's offer to an FBI agent — each offered by the opponent.","trigger":"Statement by a party (or its agent/employee on a matter within the scope of employment, or a co-conspirator during and in furtherance) + offered AGAINST that party (FRE 801(d)(2)) → non-hearsay, no oath, formality, against-interest quality, or personal knowledge required.","rule":"A statement made by a party and offered against that party is NOT hearsay (FRE 801(d)(2)(A)) — it needs no oath, no solemnity, no personal knowledge, and it need not have been against the party's interest when made; a boast, a casual remark, or an apology all qualify. The exclusion extends to adopted statements ((B)), statements by authorized spokespersons ((C)), by agents or employees on matters within the scope of and during the employment ((D)), and by co-conspirators DURING and IN FURTHERANCE of the conspiracy ((E)) — a post-arrest confession naming a supplier is not in furtherance and fails (E). The doctrine is one-directional: a party cannot offer his own statement (self-serving hearsay).","sub":"801(d)(2) – Party-Opponent Statements (Master)","sensitivity":"Three switches: (1) direction — offered BY the opponent AGAINST the party, never by the party for himself; (2) no quality filters — the exam's wrong answers add requirements (against interest, personal knowledge, oath) the rule does not contain; (3) the vicarious branches carry their own gates (scope-of-employment; during-and-in-furtherance).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “confesión — it must be a solemn acknowledgment against interest, plena prueba (C.C. art. 1.401)” → the U.S. 'admission' is a much looser and broader creature: any party statement, however casual or self-flattering when made, enters when the opponent offers it. The civilian confesión schema (formal, against-interest, often via posiciones juradas) both under-uses the doctrine (missing casual remarks) and over-translates its name — the classic false cognate of the hearsay family.","authority":"Party-opponent statements: FRE 801(d)(2)(A)–(E) (no oath/personal-knowledge/against-interest filters; scope-of-employment; during-and-in-furtherance)."},{"n":26,"summary":"The declarant announced a plan — 'I'm flying overseas today,' 'I'm going to the cousin's house,' 'I'll return the car once things settle with my wife' — and the statement is offered to prove the plan, or that it was carried out.","trigger":"Statement of then-existing state of mind, emotion, sensation, or physical condition (motive, intent, plan) (FRE 803(3)) + forward-looking intent offered to prove subsequent conduct (Hillmon) vs. backward-looking memory/belief (barred, save the will exception).","rule":"A statement of the declarant's THEN-EXISTING state of mind (motive, intent, plan) or emotional, sensory, or physical condition is excepted from hearsay (FRE 803(3)); under the Hillmon doctrine a declaration of present intent is admissible as circumstantial evidence that the declarant later acted on the plan. The exception NEVER covers a statement of memory or belief offered to prove the fact remembered or believed — the past is reached only through other exceptions — unless the statement relates to the validity or terms of the declarant's will.","sub":"803(3) – State of Mind and the Hillmon Doctrine","sensitivity":"The switch is temporal direction: 'I am going' (forward — admissible, and proves the going) vs. 'I went / he did it to me' (backward — barred as memory/belief). Mental-state words describing PAST events are the trap; the will proviso is the lone backward door.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “what she said about her plans is just another declaration for the judge to weigh” → the exception's forward-only architecture — present intent proves future conduct, but present memory cannot prove past fact — is a formal line the sana crítica schema never draws. The civilian weighs all declarations by credibility; the U.S. rule sorts them by TENSE, and the wrong answers live precisely on the backward side.","authority":"State of mind: FRE 803(3); Mutual Life Ins. Co. v. Hillmon (forward-looking intent); memory/belief bar, will exception."},{"n":27,"summary":"A patient tells the treating doctor how it happened — the fall, the slippery sidewalk — and adds who was at fault and what the neighbor did afterwards; the statement is offered to prove the cause.","trigger":"Statement made for — and reasonably pertinent to — medical diagnosis or treatment, describing history, symptoms, their inception, or GENERAL cause (FRE 803(4)); statements of fault or wrongdoer identity generally excluded.","rule":"Statements made for medical diagnosis or treatment are excepted from hearsay if reasonably pertinent to care: medical history, past or present symptoms or sensations, their inception, and their GENERAL CAUSE (FRE 803(4)). Attribution of FAULT or the wrongdoer's IDENTITY is generally not pertinent to treatment and falls outside the exception ('I slipped on an icy sidewalk' qualifies; 'the neighbor never shoveled and covered it up afterwards' does not) — with a recognized carve-out in child-abuse cases where the abuser's identity is pertinent to treatment. The statement need not be made to a physician (paramedics, nurses) and includes statements to experts consulted only for diagnosis (even litigation-driven).","sub":"803(4) – Medical Statements: The Pertinence and Fault Limits","sensitivity":"One sentence, two halves, two fates: cause-of-injury language enters, fault-and-identity language stays out. Pertinence to TREATMENT — not sincerity — draws the line, and the exam plants both halves in a single quoted statement.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct that statements to treating physicians are trustworthy converges with the exception's rationale — broadly RIGHT on admissibility. The calibration friction is the internal LINE: sana crítica would take the whole narrative and weigh it, while 803(4) surgically splits the same sentence at the point where treatment-pertinence ends and fault-attribution begins.","authority":"Medical diagnosis/treatment: FRE 803(4) (cause pertinent; fault/identity generally not)."},{"n":28,"summary":"Words spill out around a startling event: a narration while watching, a scream when the beating stops — offered through whoever heard them, with the declarant's availability irrelevant.","trigger":"Present sense impression: statement describing an event made WHILE or IMMEDIATELY AFTER perceiving it (FRE 803(1)) vs. excited utterance: statement RELATING TO a startling event made under the stress of the excitement (FRE 803(2)).","rule":"A present sense impression is a statement DESCRIBING an event made while the declarant perceives it or immediately after — minutes of reflective delay defeat it. An excited utterance is a statement RELATING TO a startling event made while the declarant remains under the stress of the excitement — the window is elastic (it tracks the stress, not the clock), and the subject matter may be broader than pure description. Neither requires the declarant to be unavailable, and both apply regardless of who reports the statement.","sub":"803(1)/(2) – Present Sense Impression vs. Excited Utterance","sensitivity":"The pair splits on two dials: TIME (contemporaneity, strict vs. stress-elastic) and SCOPE (describing vs. relating to). A statement 20 minutes later fails 803(1) but may satisfy 803(2) if the stress persists; a calm narration fails 803(2) but may satisfy 803(1) if simultaneous. Classic distractor: forcing the dying-declaration frame onto a victim who survives and testifies — cf. Unit 33.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — spontaneous declarations near the event are reliable — is broadly RIGHT (sana crítica weighs spontaneity). The calibration friction: U.S. law converts the intuition into two SEPARATE exceptions with distinct trigger grammar (describing/while vs. relating/stress), and answer choices exploit the seam between them rather than the shared intuition.","authority":"Present sense impression / excited utterance: FRE 803(1)–(2) (describing/while vs. relating/stress)."},{"n":29,"summary":"Institutional paper is offered for its truth: a hospital chart, an ER doctor's letter written for a school, a police report reciting a bystander's account against a criminal defendant, a sealed certification that no license was ever issued.","trigger":"Business record: made at/near the time, by/from someone with knowledge, kept in the course of a regularly conducted activity, regular practice, custodian foundation, no untrustworthiness (FRE 803(6)) vs. public record (FRE 803(8)) with the criminal-case bars ((A)(ii)-(iii)) + absence-of-record certifications (803(7)/(10)) + hearsay-within-hearsay (FRE 805).","rule":"Business records enter under FRE 803(6) only on the full foundation — timeliness, knowledge source, regularly conducted activity, regular practice, custodian or qualified witness, and no showing of untrustworthiness; one-off documents prepared for outsiders or for litigation (an ER letter to a school) fail the regular-practice element (Palmer v. Hoffman). Public records (803(8)) cover the office's activities and observations pursuant to legal duty — but matters observed by LAW ENFORCEMENT and investigative findings are inadmissible AGAINST a criminal defendant, and the bar is one-way: the defendant may use the report. A certification that a diligent search found no record proves the absence (803(10), with notice-and-demand in criminal cases). Every layer of hearsay within the record needs its own exception (FRE 805) — a bystander's statement inside a police report never rides the public-records exception.","sub":"803(6)/(8) – Business and Public Records; the Criminal-Case Bars (Master)","sensitivity":"Four switches: (1) regular practice vs. one-off (the letter fails where the chart succeeds); (2) the law-enforcement bars, and their ONE-WAY direction; (3) double hearsay — each embedded declarant needs a door; (4) absence certifications as affirmative proof. Workaround the exam rewards: call the officer to testify from present memory instead of offering the report.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the expediente IS the proof — official reports carry plena fe” → the civilian written-procedure schema (documento público, C.C. arts. 1.359–1.360; actas policiales in the file) collides with a system that distrusts precisely the official investigative report when offered against the accused, demands an element-by-element foundation for routine paper, and dismantles documents into hearsay layers. The instinct that paper is the safest evidence is inverted: here live testimony is the default and the record is the suspect.","authority":"Business & public records: FRE 803(6) (Palmer v. Hoffman regular-practice limit), 803(8)(A)(ii)–(iii) (one-way criminal bars), 803(7)/(10) (absence; Melendez-Diaz notice-and-demand), 805 (layers)."},{"n":30,"summary":"The witness kept a contemporaneous diary but cannot remember the facts on the stand; her lawyer shows her the diary — memory returns, or it does not — and the two doctrines part ways.","trigger":"Refreshing recollection (FRE 612: any item, memory revives, witness testifies from present memory, document not evidence) vs. past recollection recorded (FRE 803(5): made/adopted by the witness while fresh, accurately reflects knowledge, witness NOW cannot recall enough even after refreshing → read into evidence).","rule":"Anything may be used to refresh a witness's memory (FRE 612); if memory revives, the witness testifies from present recollection and the refreshing item is not evidence — though the ADVERSE party may inspect it, cross-examine on it, and introduce pertinent portions. If refreshing FAILS, a record qualifies as past recollection recorded (FRE 803(5)) when the witness once had personal knowledge, made or adopted the record while the matter was fresh, and vouches it accurately reflects that knowledge: the record may then be READ into evidence, but is received as an EXHIBIT only if offered by the adverse party.","sub":"612/803(5) – Refreshing Recollection vs. Past Recollection Recorded","sensitivity":"Sequence is the doctrine: attempt to refresh FIRST; only failure opens 803(5). Then the exhibit switch — proponent reads, only the opponent may make it an exhibit — is the most-tested procedural detail. The refreshing item itself has no evidentiary status and may be anything (even inadmissible material).","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the diary is a document — offer it as documentary proof” → the U.S. system routes the document through the WITNESS's memory: first as a mere stimulus (no status), then, only on memory failure, as words read aloud but kept out of the jury room. A written-procedure schema in which private documents are simply filed and weighed has no counterpart to this memory-indexed, two-stage machine, or to its asymmetric exhibit rule.","authority":"Refreshing vs. recorded recollection: FRE 612; FRE 803(5) (read, not received; exhibit only by adverse party)."},{"n":31,"summary":"The declarant is gone — abroad, privileged out, dead — but testified once before: the mistrial transcript, the preliminary-hearing testimony now offered at the later trial, and the credibility fight over an absent voice.","trigger":"Unavailability (FRE 804(a): privilege, refusal, memory failure, death/illness, absence beyond process) + former testimony (FRE 804(b)(1)): prior proceeding + opportunity AND similar motive to develop by the party against whom now offered (civil: or a predecessor in interest) + attacking the absent declarant's credibility (FRE 806).","rule":"Former testimony of an unavailable declarant is admissible if given at a trial, hearing, or lawful deposition and now offered against a party who had — or, in a civil case, whose predecessor in interest had — an OPPORTUNITY and SIMILAR MOTIVE to develop it by direct, cross, or redirect (FRE 804(b)(1)). Unavailability includes privilege invocations sustained by the court, persistent refusal, memory failure, death or illness, and absence beyond the court's process (a declarant who moved abroad). A mistrial does not disqualify the testimony. When any hearsay is admitted, the declarant's credibility may be attacked (and supported) by any evidence that would be admissible had the declarant testified — including bias, convictions, and inconsistent statements without an explain-or-deny opportunity (FRE 806).","sub":"804(b)(1)/806 – Former Testimony; Impeaching the Absent Declarant","sensitivity":"Three switches: (1) unavailability is a defined MENU, not a vibe — a testifying declarant kills every 804 door (cf. Unit 32); (2) the similar-motive comparison across proceedings (criminal cross of a scheme witness ≈ civil fraud posture); (3) FRE 806 — the absent declarant is impeachable as if present, with the 613 foundation excused.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian schema of prueba trasladada — evidence validly formed in one proceeding travels to another where the party could contradict it — lands close to 804(b)(1); the contradiction instinct is RIGHT. The calibration friction: U.S. law formalizes unavailability as a closed menu, tests MOTIVE similarity rather than mere party identity, and adds FRE 806's full impeachment package for the absent voice — refinements the traslado doctrine never drew.","authority":"Former testimony & attacking the declarant: FRE 804(a), 804(b)(1) (opportunity + similar motive; predecessor in interest in civil cases), 806."},{"n":32,"summary":"Someone else confesses: the anonymous caller who says she stole the phone, the fugitive friend who told his mother he was in on the robbery, the arrested dealer naming her supplier from the witness stand.","trigger":"Declarant UNAVAILABLE + statement so contrary to pecuniary/proprietary interest or so exposing to civil/criminal liability that a reasonable person would not have made it unless true (FRE 804(b)(3)) + corroborating circumstances clearly indicating trustworthiness when offered in a criminal case.","rule":"A statement against interest requires (1) an UNAVAILABLE declarant — a declarant who is present and testifying closes this door entirely — and (2) a statement a reasonable person in the declarant's position would have made only if true, judged at the time it was made; (3) in criminal cases, corroborating circumstances must clearly indicate trustworthiness. The exception reaches any declarant (not just parties) but only the genuinely self-inculpatory portions — collateral, blame-shifting passages do not ride along (Williamson). Distinguish the party-opponent exclusion (Unit 25): no unavailability, no against-interest quality, party statements only.","sub":"804(b)(3) – Statement Against Interest","sensitivity":"Three kill-switches the exam cycles: (1) availability — the testifying dealer defeats the exception; (2) corroboration in criminal cases — the anonymous caller usually fails it; (3) granularity — only the self-inculpatory core enters. Non-testimonial confessions to family survive Confrontation (cf. Unit 42).","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “a declaración contra sí mismo is the strongest proof — like a confesión” → the U.S. splits what the civilian fuses: party confessions travel the 801(d)(2) road with no quality filters, while THIRD-PARTY self-inculpation demands unavailability, reasonable-person contrariety, and criminal-case corroboration. Translating both as 'confession' collapses two doors with opposite requirements — the module's cleanest false cognate pair.","authority":"Statement against interest: FRE 804(b)(3) (unavailability; corroboration in criminal cases; Williamson self-inculpatory parsing); distinguish 801(d)(2)."},{"n":33,"summary":"‘I’m dying — don’t let him get away with it’: the classic deathbed accusation — except the victim survives, or the charge is assault, and the doctrine everyone remembers does not apply.","trigger":"Statement made under a settled belief of IMMINENT death + concerning the CAUSE or CIRCUMSTANCES of the impending death + declarant unavailable + offered in a HOMICIDE prosecution or a CIVIL case (FRE 804(b)(2)).","rule":"A dying declaration requires that the declarant, believing death imminent, spoke about its cause or circumstances; the declarant need not actually die, but must be UNAVAILABLE at trial, and the exception operates ONLY in homicide prosecutions and civil cases — never in assault, robbery, or other non-homicide criminal trials. Where the declarant survives and testifies, or the prosecution is for a non-homicide offense, the statement must find another door — typically the excited utterance (Unit 28), which has no death-belief, unavailability, or case-type limits.","sub":"804(b)(2) – Dying Declarations: The Double Gate (and the 803(2) Escape)","sensitivity":"Two structural bars the exam stacks: case-type (homicide or civil ONLY) and unavailability (the surviving, testifying victim fails it) — the dramatic 'I'm dying' scream in an assault case is the distractor; the correct door is the excited utterance. Belief of imminent death is judged subjectively at the moment of the statement.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “words spoken in the face of death carry special credibility — admit them” → the reliability intuition is shared, but U.S. law hard-codes it into a narrow, doubly-gated exception (case-type + unavailability + subject-matter) rather than a weight factor under sana crítica. The civilian admits-and-weighs; the U.S. system asks WHICH door — and the famous one is usually locked.","authority":"Dying declarations: FRE 804(b)(2) (unavailability + homicide-or-civil limit + belief of imminent death; excited utterance as the escape route)."},{"n":34,"summary":"A witness testifies about a deal, a threat, a film, a recorded remark — and someone objects that the writing itself must be produced: sometimes rightly (the case turns on the film's content), usually wrongly (the witness saw the event with her own eyes).","trigger":"Party seeks to prove the CONTENTS of a writing, recording, or photograph (FRE 1002) + originals/duplicates (FRE 1001, 1003) + excuses for nonproduction (FRE 1004) vs. testimony from independent personal knowledge (no BER).","rule":"The best evidence (original document) rule applies ONLY when a party seeks to prove the contents of a writing, recording, or photograph — because the writing is itself the operative fact (obscenity of a film, terms of a contract) or because the witness's knowledge comes solely from the writing. A witness with independent personal knowledge of the event may testify without producing the record that also memorializes it (the plumber who supervised the work; the reporter who heard the statement). Duplicates are admissible to the same extent as originals absent a genuine authenticity question (FRE 1003), and secondary evidence is allowed when the original is lost or destroyed without bad faith, unobtainable, or in the opponent's possession (FRE 1004).","sub":"Best Evidence Rule (1002) – Contents, Not Events (Master)","sensitivity":"One question sorts every scenario: is the WRITING'S CONTENT the thing to be proved, or merely a record of an event the witness independently knows? Contents → original/duplicate or excuse; independent knowledge → no rule at all. The film/recording variants and the sole-knowledge-from-writing posture are the tested edges.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the document always trumps testimony — prueba documental is the queen of proof” → the U.S. rule is narrower and inverted in function: it is not a hierarchy of proof but a CONTENTS-trigger, and testimony from personal knowledge freely coexists with unproduced records. The civilian documentary-primacy schema (C.C. art. 1.387 limits on testimony against writings) over-applies the rule to every scenario where paper exists — precisely the exam's favorite wrong answer.","authority":"Best evidence rule: FRE 1001–1004 (contents trigger; duplicates; excuses; independent personal knowledge outside the rule)."},{"n":35,"summary":"Fifteen years of water-level logs cannot be read to a jury; a chart distills them — and the objection is that the chart is not the records.","trigger":"Voluminous writings that cannot conveniently be examined in court (FRE 1006) + summary/chart/calculation + originals or duplicates made available for examination and copying.","rule":"The contents of voluminous writings, recordings, or photographs that cannot conveniently be examined in court may be proved by a summary, chart, or calculation, provided the underlying originals or duplicates are made available to the other parties for examination and copying at a reasonable time and place, and the underlying materials are themselves admissible (FRE 1006). The court may order production of the originals in court.","sub":"1006 – Summaries of Voluminous Records","sensitivity":"Two gates carry the questions: the underlying records must be ADMISSIBLE (a summary cannot launder inadmissible hearsay) and must be MADE AVAILABLE to the opponent — the summary substitutes for convenience, not for foundation.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian is comfortable with experticia-style synthesis of voluminous material — the instinct is RIGHT. The calibration friction: the U.S. summary is a party-made trial exhibit gated by availability and underlying admissibility, not a court-appointed expert's report; the safeguards are adversarial (opponent inspection) rather than institutional (judicial appointment).","authority":"Summaries: FRE 1006 (availability of admissible originals)."},{"n":36,"summary":"A passage from a treatise is offered — through the opponent's expert who conceded its authority on cross, through no expert at all, or with a request that the jury take the book to deliberate.","trigger":"Learned treatise (FRE 803(18)): established as reliable authority (proponent's expert, opponent's admission, or judicial notice) + called to an expert's attention on cross OR relied on in direct → READ into evidence, never received as an exhibit.","rule":"Statements in a treatise, periodical, or pamphlet are substantive evidence (FRE 803(18)) if the publication is established as a reliable authority — by the proponent's expert, by the OPPONENT's expert's admission (even one who says she did not rely on it), or by judicial notice — and the statement is either relied on by an expert on direct or called to an expert's attention on cross-examination. The passage is READ to the jury; the book itself is never received as an exhibit and does not go to the jury room. Without any expert on the stand, the treatise cannot come in at all.","sub":"803(18) – Learned Treatises: Read, Not Received","sensitivity":"Three switches: (1) reliability foundation can come from the ADVERSE expert — reliance is unnecessary; (2) an expert must be on the stand — the treatise never travels alone; (3) read-not-received — the jury hears the words but never handles the book. Each switch is a separately tested distractor.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “doctrina — file the authoritative text and let the judge read it” → in a jury system the treatise is quarantined: tethered to a live expert, admitted through the ear rather than the hand, and withheld from deliberations. The civilian practice of citing doctrine directly to the court has no counterpart machinery — the trained reflex misses both the expert-tether and the exhibit ban.","authority":"Learned treatises: FRE 803(18) (reliable authority; expert tether; read, not received)."},{"n":37,"summary":"A paid defense expert opines the blade could not fly off; a DEA agent decodes 'apples'; a physician's causation opinion rests partly on an inadmissible letter — and each pillar of expert practice is challenged.","trigger":"Expert testimony (FRE 702): qualification + helpfulness + reliable methods reliably applied (Daubert) + basis may include inadmissible facts if experts in the field reasonably rely on them (FRE 703) + ultimate-issue opinions allowed (FRE 704(a)), except mental-state of a criminal defendant (704(b)).","rule":"A qualified expert may give opinion testimony if specialized knowledge helps the trier of fact and the opinion rests on sufficient facts, reliable principles, and reliable application (FRE 702; Daubert gatekeeping). The expert needs NO personal knowledge: the basis may include facts made known at or before the hearing, including INADMISSIBLE material if experts in the field reasonably rely on such material — but reliance does not make the material admissible, and the proponent may disclose it to the jury only if its probative value in evaluating the opinion substantially outweighs prejudice (FRE 703). Compensation by a party and opinions embracing the ultimate issue do not disqualify (FRE 704(a)); the sole ultimate-issue bar is an opinion that a criminal defendant did or did not have the requisite mental state (FRE 704(b)).","sub":"702/703/704 – Expert Testimony: Qualification, Basis, Ultimate Issue (Master)","sensitivity":"Three tested seams: (1) basis vs. admissibility — the expert may STAND on the letter, but the letter stays out (cf. Unit 29's ER letter); (2) bias goes to weight — the paid expert testifies; (3) 704's near-total permission with its single criminal-mental-state carve-out.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the perito is the court's neutral auxiliary — a party-hired, party-paid expert is inherently disqualified” → U.S. experts are adversarial instruments: retained, prepared, and paid by parties, tested by cross-examination and Daubert gatekeeping rather than by institutional neutrality (CPC arts. 451 ff. experticia). The civilian reflex treats partisanship as inadmissibility when the system treats it as weight — and misses that the expert's file does not enter evidence with the expert.","authority":"Experts: FRE 702 (Daubert), 703 (basis vs. admissibility; reverse-403 disclosure), 704(a)–(b)."},{"n":38,"summary":"A photograph of the scene, a voice on a call, a signature on a letter — the opponent demands an expert or the photographer, and the proponent needs only a witness who knows.","trigger":"Authentication (FRE 901): evidence sufficient to support a finding that the item is what the proponent claims + illustrative methods (personal knowledge, lay familiarity with voice/handwriting, distinctive characteristics, chain of custody for fungibles) + self-authenticating documents (FRE 902).","rule":"Authentication requires only evidence SUFFICIENT TO SUPPORT A FINDING that the item is what the proponent claims (FRE 901(a)) — a conditional-relevance screen for the jury, not a preponderance finding for the judge. A photograph is authenticated by any witness with personal knowledge that it fairly and accurately depicts the scene — no expert and no photographer required; voices by lay familiarity acquired at any time; handwriting by lay familiarity NOT acquired for litigation; fungible or alterable items by chain of custody. Certified public documents, official publications, newspapers, and acknowledged documents are self-authenticating (FRE 902).","sub":"901/902 – Authentication: The Low Bar","sensitivity":"The bar's HEIGHT is the tested fact: 'sufficient to support a finding' — wrong answers raise it (demanding experts, photographers, notarization). The lay-familiarity methods and the handwriting anti-litigation limit supply the variations; 902 removes the foundation witness entirely for listed documents.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian libertad probatoria instinct — any credible witness can identify a photo or a voice — is RIGHT, and 901's low threshold converges with it. The calibration friction: the civilian formal-document reflex (notarization, reconocimiento de documento privado, cotejo by expert) OVER-authenticates precisely where 901 asks almost nothing — and the exam's wrong answers are built from those imported formalities.","authority":"Authentication: FRE 901 (sufficient-to-support-a-finding standard; lay familiarity; chain of custody), 902 (self-authentication)."},{"n":39,"summary":"A frightened caller confesses a crime to a lawyer's secretary; former joint clients turn on each other; the lawyer sues the client for fees — and the privilege holds, or dissolves, by structure.","trigger":"Attorney-client privilege: confidential communication + between privileged persons (incl. the lawyer's agents and prospective clients) + for the purpose of obtaining legal advice + no exception (crime-fraud, joint clients now adverse, attorney-client dispute).","rule":"The privilege protects confidential COMMUNICATIONS between client (or prospective client) and lawyer — including the lawyer's necessary agents, such as a secretary — made to obtain legal advice; it belongs to the client, survives the client's death, and covers neither underlying facts nor pre-existing documents. Structural exceptions: communications in furtherance of future crime or fraud; disputes BETWEEN former joint clients who consulted the same lawyer on a common matter (no privilege between them); and disputes between lawyer and client (fee suits, malpractice), where the lawyer may reveal what is necessary.","sub":"Privileges – Attorney-Client: Scope, Agents, and the Structural Exceptions","sensitivity":"Three tested edges: (1) the privilege attaches BEFORE retention (prospective clients) and THROUGH staff (agents); (2) it protects communications, never facts — the client can be deposed on what happened, not on what he told the lawyer; (3) the exceptions are relational, not discretionary — joint clients and fee disputes dissolve it automatically.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: secreto profesional protects this — broadly RIGHT: the civilian instinct converges on core confidentiality (Ley de Abogados; C.P. sanction for revelation). The calibration friction: the civilian schema is a lawyer's DUTY enforced by professional discipline, while the U.S. privilege is the CLIENT's evidentiary right with structural on/off switches (joint-client adversity, fee disputes, crime-fraud) — the duty-based reflex misses who holds it and when it dissolves.","authority":"Attorney-client privilege: confidential communications; agents; prospective clients; crime-fraud, joint-client, and attorney-client-dispute exceptions."},{"n":40,"summary":"A husband's mid-marriage confession of adultery surfaces in the divorce; a wife is called to testify against her husband in a criminal case — two privileges with different holders, lifespans, and exceptions.","trigger":"Marital communications privilege (confidential communication during marriage; either spouse holds; survives divorce; civil and criminal) vs. spousal testimonial privilege (criminal only; witness-spouse holds; requires current marriage) + the suits-between-spouses exception.","rule":"Two distinct privileges: (1) the marital COMMUNICATIONS privilege protects confidential communications made DURING the marriage, applies in civil and criminal cases, survives divorce, and is held by BOTH spouses; (2) the spousal TESTIMONIAL privilege lets the witness spouse refuse to testify against the defendant spouse in a CRIMINAL case, exists only while the marriage subsists, and (federal rule, Trammel) is held by the WITNESS spouse alone. NEITHER applies in litigation between the spouses (divorce, intra-family torts) or in prosecutions for crimes against the spouse or their children.","sub":"Privileges – The Marital Pair: Communications vs. Testimony","sensitivity":"Four differentiating dials — what is protected (words vs. the act of testifying), when it applies (any case vs. criminal only), how long it lives (survives divorce vs. dies with the marriage), and who holds it (both vs. witness spouse) — plus the shared spousal-litigation exception that kills both. Exam questions select by crossing two dials.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “spouses cannot testify against each other — family solidarity is protected” → the civilian inhabilidad/exención schema (a spouse exempted from testifying) resembles only HALF the pair, and imports none of the four-dial machinery: the U.S. system splits protection into two separately-gated privileges whose holder, lifespan, and case-type diverge. Translating 'marital privilege' as one doctrine is itself the error the pair is built to catch.","authority":"Marital privileges: communications vs. testimonial; Trammel v. United States, 445 U.S. 40 (1980) (witness-spouse holds); spousal-litigation exception."},{"n":41,"summary":"In a diversity case the counselor's records, the reporter's source, the inadvertently produced memo: which sovereign's privilege law governs, and when does a slip waive it all?","trigger":"FRE 501: in civil actions where state law supplies the rule of decision, STATE law governs privilege (the forum's choice-of-law rules select which state, Klaxon); federal common law governs otherwise + FRE 502(b): inadvertent disclosure waives nothing if reasonable protective steps and prompt rectification.","rule":"Under FRE 501, privileges are governed by federal common law EXCEPT that in a civil case, state law governs privilege regarding a claim or defense for which state law supplies the rule of decision — so in diversity, state privilege law controls ALL privileges (there is no FRE-codified attorney-client privilege), and the forum state's choice-of-law rules select WHICH state's law applies (Klaxon). Under FRE 502(b), an inadvertent disclosure of privileged material does not waive the privilege if the holder took reasonable steps to prevent disclosure AND promptly took reasonable steps to rectify the error — months of inaction waive it.","sub":"Privileges – FRE 501 Choice of Law; FRE 502(b) Waiver","sensitivity":"Two structurally independent switches: (1) WHOSE law — rule-of-decision tracking sends diversity privilege questions to state law via the forum's conflicts rules (an Erie satellite inside the FRE); (2) WAIVER mechanics — 502(b)'s prompt-rectification clock turns a paralegal's slip plus two months' silence into a full waiver.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the forum court applies its own procedural law, and privileges are procedural” → the U.S. system classifies privilege as SUBSTANTIVE-tracking in diversity: two sovereigns' laws coexist in one courtroom and the rule-of-decision test — not the courthouse — selects between them, with a second hop through state conflicts rules. The single-sovereign civilian schema has no shelf for a rule of evidence whose CONTENT depends on which system supplies the claim.","authority":"Privilege choice of law & waiver: FRE 501 (rule-of-decision test); Klaxon (forum conflicts rules); FRE 502(b) (inadvertent disclosure; prompt rectification)."},{"n":42,"summary":"An absent declarant's accusation arrives through a police officer — in a civil battery suit where the Constitution is silent, and in a criminal trial where the testimonial line decides everything.","trigger":"Criminal prosecution + testimonial out-of-court statement (primary purpose: creating evidence vs. meeting an ongoing emergency) + declarant unavailable + no prior opportunity for cross-examination (Crawford) → exclusion; civil cases: Confrontation Clause inapplicable.","rule":"The Sixth Amendment Confrontation Clause applies ONLY in criminal prosecutions, and only against the ACCUSED: it bars TESTIMONIAL out-of-court statements of an unavailable declarant absent a prior opportunity for cross-examination (Crawford v. Washington), even if a hearsay exception is satisfied. Statements are testimonial when their primary purpose is to establish past events for prosecution (stationhouse interrogation, affidavits, forensic certificates); statements to meet an ongoing emergency (Davis) and casual remarks to family or friends are non-testimonial. In civil cases the objection simply does not exist — the analysis is pure hearsay law.","sub":"Constitutional Overlay – Confrontation: Criminal Only, Testimonial Line","sensitivity":"Two sequential switches: (1) FORUM — a civil case ends the constitutional inquiry at the threshold; (2) the PRIMARY-PURPOSE line — the same accusation is barred as stationhouse testimony yet admitted as a statement to the declarant's mother (cf. Unit 32). Confrontation runs ON TOP of hearsay: both hurdles must be cleared in a criminal case.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “derecho a la contradicción — due process guarantees confrontation in every proceeding, civil or criminal” → the U.S. clause is a criminal-trial right of the ACCUSED with a doctrine-specific trigger (testimonial statements), not a general principle of contradictory proceedings. The civilian contradicción principle over-extends the objection to civil cases and under-specifies the testimonial line that actually decides criminal ones.","authority":"Confrontation: Crawford v. Washington, 541 U.S. 36 (2004); Davis v. Washington (primary-purpose line); criminal-only scope."}],"cases":[{"n":1,"summary":"Armed robbery prosecution offers evidence that defendant stole a gun a few days earlier as part of its case in chief to show preparation for the charged robbery.","trigger":"Use of prior uncharged bad act to show something other than propensity, such as preparation for the charged crime, under 404(b).","rule":"Prior bad acts cannot be used to show that a person acted in conformity therewith usually, but can be admissible for other purposes such as motive, identity, absence of mistake, intent, common plan or scheme, opportunity, or preparation. The burden is on the proponent to prove by a preponderance that it is being admitted for a proper purpose.","sub":"404(b) – Other purposes (preparation, intent, etc.)","family":"Relevance","unit":3},{"n":2,"summary":"Burglary defendant’s prior robbery conviction from nine years ago offered by the prosecution as substantive evidence to show absence of mistake in entering neighbor’s house.","trigger":"Attempt to use a prior conviction as 404(b) evidence to rebut claim of mistake rather than for impeachment only.","rule":"SEE NUMBER 64.","sub":"404(b) – Prior crimes / absence of mistake","family":"404(b)","unit":7},{"n":3,"summary":"Question asks in which proceeding a timely objection will be sustained, focusing on privilege and relaxed evidence rules in certain hearings.","trigger":"Assertion of attorney–client privilege even where formal rules of evidence otherwise don’t apply.","rule":"Although the rules of evidence do not apply in matters such as probation revocation and grand jury proceedings, an attorney client privilege will still apply.","sub":"Applicability of Rules / Privileges","family":"Foundations","unit":1},{"n":4,"summary":"In negligence suit against hospital, lay witness who took photo of patient on floor will testify to authenticate it.","trigger":"Photograph taken by someone with personal knowledge of the scene offered to show what happened.","rule":"Authenticating a photograph does not require an expert, just a lay witness with personal knowledge.","sub":"Authentication / Photographs","family":"901/902","unit":38},{"n":5,"summary":"Defamation plaintiff forgets allegedly defamatory letter at home and wants to testify to its contents.","trigger":"Party wants to prove contents of a specific writing central to the claim without producing it.","rule":"When a writing is important to a lawsuit, an original is required unless there is a good reason for not having the original and some reliable substitute is used.","sub":"Best Evidence Rule / Writings","family":"Best Evidence Rule (1002)","unit":34},{"n":6,"summary":"Robbery defendant’s character witness testifies he is not violent; prosecution wants to ask about two prior robbery suspicions on cross to rebut that character testimony.","trigger":"Criminal defendant introduces opinion evidence of his own good character for a pertinent trait, opening the door for the prosecution to rebut that same trait.","rule":"In a criminal case, if the defendant opens the door to his character, the prosecution can use testimony to rebut it. Your confusion probably came from mixing two related but different limits: - The prosecution cannot offer specific acts as substantive character evidence on direct → correct - But the prosecution can use specific acts to impeach a character witness on cross → also correct Many students remember #1 and forget #2. That's exactly what this question tests. Also remember the prosecution has to rebut regarding the same trait.","sub":"Character – Accused opens the door","family":"Character","unit":5},{"n":7,"summary":"Patron sues restaurant owner in negligence for food poisoning; owner testifies he is very cautious cooking chicken and always cooks all food himself.","trigger":"Civil negligence action where a party offers character evidence (cautiousness) to prove conduct in conformity rather than an element of the claim.","rule":"Character evidence of the parties is not allowed in civil cases unless it is an essential element of the claim, such as cases of defamation, negligent hiring, wrongful death, or child custody.","sub":"Character – Civil cases / not essential element","family":"Character","unit":6},{"n":8,"summary":"Patron sues owner for negligent hiring of chef; owner offers testimony that chef is known to be very cautious when cooking chicken.","trigger":"Negligent hiring claim where the employee’s character for care is directly in issue as an essential element of liability.","rule":"When character is an essential element of a claim, any party can raise character and can also use any method to prove it.","sub":"Character – Essential element / negligent hiring","family":"Character","unit":6},{"n":9,"summary":"Civil defamation case; reputation and specific instances evidence offered","trigger":"Character directly at issue in defamation","rule":"When character is an essential element, proof may be by reputation, opinion, or specific instances","sub":"Character Evidence","family":"Character","unit":6},{"n":10,"summary":"Defendant’s wife testified he is honest and cited a specific instance; prosecution objected","trigger":"Character evidence offered by defendant using specific acts","rule":"In a criminal case, a witness for the defendant may use character evidence of the defendant as circumstantial evidence, but must use opinion or reputation, not specific instances.","sub":"Character Evidence","family":"Character","unit":5},{"n":11,"summary":"In child custody dispute, neighbor testifies he saw father snorting cocaine while responsible for infant on multiple occasions.","trigger":"Specific instances of conduct offered because parental fitness is directly in issue.","rule":"Although character evidence of the parties is not allowed as circumstantial evidence in a civil case, it is allowed if it offers prof of an essential element such as in a child custody proceeding.","sub":"Character Evidence / Essential Element","family":"Character","unit":6},{"n":12,"summary":"Robbery defendant’s lawyer mentions defendant’s peaceful character in opening statement; prosecution then offers neighbor’s reputation testimony that defendant is violent.","trigger":"Defense reference to character in opening statement only, before any character evidence is actually introduced.","rule":"Although character evidence is allowed as circumstantial evidence in a criminal case, the defendant must open the door. A statement made in an opening statement do not open the door.","sub":"Character Evidence / Opening the Door","family":"Character","unit":5},{"n":13,"summary":"Robbery trial; other robbery with distinctive yellow rose offered","trigger":"Other-acts evidence offered for identity/modus operandi","rule":"Other-acts evidence is admissible for identity/modus operandi when distinctive and offered for a proper purpose","sub":"Character/404(b)","family":"404(b)","unit":7},{"n":14,"summary":"Grand jury robbery investigation; mechanic witness gives opinion that suspect’s car could match tire tracks; no formal training; didn’t personally observe tracks; judge previously refused to qualify him as expert; question is whether grand jury can consider testimony.","trigger":"Grand jury proceedings are not bound by strict evidence rules; evidentiary rules relaxed.","rule":"The rules of evidence are relaxed and less formal in a grand jury proceeding","sub":"Grand Jury / Evidence Rules","family":"Foundations","unit":1},{"n":15,"summary":"Utility customer claims power was shut off without statutory notice; employee describes strict, uniformly enforced shutoff notice and reminder-call procedures with termination for noncompliance.","trigger":"Evidence of an organization’s regular, invariable procedure offered to show it likely followed the same routine in this instance.","rule":"A repeated response to a particular situation, which is habit, or routine practice for a business, can be admitted to show that the party likely acted in accordance with the habit or routine practice, even without corroboration.","sub":"Habit / Routine practice – Business procedures","family":"Habit vs. Character (FRE 406)","unit":9},{"n":16,"summary":"Furnace buyer claims he was promised 7-year warranty without extra fee; company president offers surveys and training practices showing customers are routinely told of extra charge.","trigger":"Evidence of organization’s regular practice in dealing with thousands of similar transactions.","rule":"Under 406, evidence of an organization’s routine practice may be admitted to prove that on a particular occasion the organization acted in accordance with their routine practice, whether or not it is corroborated.","sub":"Habit / Routine Practice (Rule 406)","family":"Habit vs. Character (FRE 406)","unit":9},{"n":17,"summary":"Woman charged with arson told her sister on the phone, right after setting the fire, that she poured gas and lit a match on her ex-boyfriend’s home.","trigger":"Statement by a party, offered by the opposing party against her, and whether it is treated as non-hearsay.","rule":"An admission by a party opponent, meaning a statement made by a party that the other party is seeking to admit, will be excluded from being hearsay.","sub":"Hearsay – Party-opponent admissions","family":"801(d)(2)","unit":25},{"n":18,"summary":"Motorcyclist asks his mechanic if the bike will be safe to ride for a month given brake concerns; later hits a pedestrian; plaintiff wants to use the question to show the rider’s notice of potential danger.","trigger":"Out-of-court question offered not for its truth but to show the declarant’s knowledge or state of mind, and whether a question counts as a “statement.”","rule":"A question does not qualify as hearsay because it is not considered an out of court statement.","sub":"Hearsay – Questions not being statements","family":"Hearsay","unit":22},{"n":19,"summary":"Illegal prostitution business keeps ‘little black book’ of clients/appointments/tips; manager charged; police seize book under warrant; prosecution considers calling officer or woman to admit book as business record.","trigger":"Foundation for FRE 803(6) business records via custodian/qualified witness; maker with knowledge and regular practice.","rule":"To use the business records exception to hearsay under FRE 803(6), the records must be made at or near the time by (or from information transmitted by) someone with knowledge, the record was kept in the course of a regularly conducted business activity, making the record was a regular practice of that activity, and all of these conditions are shown by testimony of the custodian or another qualified witness.","sub":"Hearsay / Business Records (803(6))","family":"803(6)/(8)","unit":29},{"n":20,"summary":"Burglary defendant testifies to innocent purpose and calls friend to repeat same story told six months after arrest.","trigger":"Prior consistent statement offered to bolster witness before or without an attack on credibility.","rule":"A prior consistent statement can only be used to rehabilitate a witness after the witness’s character has been attacked.","sub":"Hearsay / Prior Consistent Statements","family":"801(d)(1)","unit":24},{"n":21,"summary":"Slip-and-fall victim tells doctor he slipped on a slippery, unshoveled sidewalk at neighbor’s house and that neighbor shoveled right after he fell; wants the statement admitted to prove cause.","trigger":"Scope of the medical diagnosis/treatment hearsay exception for statements describing cause and circumstances reasonably pertinent to care.","rule":"A statement that: (A) is made for — and is reasonably pertinent to — medical diagnosis or treatment; and (B) describes medical history; past or present symptoms or sensations; their inception; or their general cause can be a hearsay exception under the statement for medical diagnosis or treatment exception.","sub":"Hearsay exception – Medical diagnosis/treatment","family":"803(4)","unit":27},{"n":22,"summary":"Anonymous caller tells defendant she, not defendant, stole the phone and expresses sorrow he is charged; caller cannot be located despite reasonable efforts; defendant wants the statement admitted.","trigger":"Unavailable declarant’s self-inculpatory statement offered as a statement against penal interest, requiring corroboration in a criminal case.","rule":"When a declarant is unavailable, the statement against interest exception to hearsay can be used if: A statement that: (A) a reasonable person in the declarant’s position would have made only if the person believed it to be true because, when made, it was so contrary to the declarant’s proprietary or pecuniary interest or had so great a tendency to invalidate the declarant’s claim against someone else or to expose the declarant to civil or criminal liability; and (B) if offered in a criminal case as one that tends to expose the declarant to criminal liability, is supported by corroborating circumstances that clearly indicate its trustworthiness after considering the totality of circumstances under which it was made and any evidence that supports or undermines it.","sub":"Hearsay exception – Statement against interest","family":"804(b)(3)","unit":32},{"n":23,"summary":"Woman told friend she was flying overseas for two weeks later that day; crime allegedly occurs during that period; friend testifies to the statement to show her travel plans.","trigger":"Statement of the declarant’s then-existing intent or plan to do something in the future offered under the state-of-mind hearsay exception.","rule":"One of the exception to hearsay is the then existing state of mind exception. A statement of the declarant’s then-existing state of mind (such as motive, intent, or plan) or emotional, sensory, or physical condition (such as mental feeling, pain, or bodily health), but not including a statement of memory or belief to prove the fact remembered or believed unless it relates to the validity or terms of the declarant’s will.","sub":"Hearsay exception – Then-existing state of mind","family":"803(3)","unit":26},{"n":24,"summary":"ER doctor wrote letter to school; expert relied on it to link illness to undercooked eggs","trigger":"Out-of-court letter offered for truth as to medical causation","rule":"Business-record admission requires the record be made in the regular course of business","sub":"Hearsay Exceptions","family":"803(6)/(8)","unit":29},{"n":25,"summary":"Reckless driving prosecution; state seeks to admit police report including witness statement that defendant drove recklessly.","trigger":"Police report offered against criminal defendant and containing third-party statement.","rule":"Under the public records exception, a police report cannot be used against a defendant in a criminal case.","sub":"Hearsay Exceptions / Public Records (Rule 803(8))","family":"803(6)/(8)","unit":29},{"n":26,"summary":"Wealthy shoplifter brags to waiter right after theft that he got a $20,000 bracelet for free; waiter testifies at trial.","trigger":"Party’s own out-of-court statement offered against that party.","rule":"One of the exclusions to hearsay is an admission by a party opponent, which is when a party’s statement is introduced into evidence by the opposing party in the lawsuit.","sub":"Hearsay Exclusions / Admission by Party Opponent","family":"801(d)(2)","unit":25},{"n":27,"summary":"Slander action; witness will testify defendant called plaintiff a thief; hearsay objection","trigger":"Opponent’s statement offered to show statement was made","rule":"A party-opponent’s statement is admissible as nonhearsay, and in defamation it is offered to prove publication (That the words were spoken by the defendant). It's just not hearsay because no offered to prove the truth of the matter asserted. You don’t go through exclusions or exceptions if the statement is not even hearsay.","sub":"Hearsay/Defamation","family":"Hearsay","unit":22},{"n":28,"summary":"Defense cross-examines ex-husband witness in child-endangerment case","trigger":"Witness has custody dispute with defendant","rule":"Bias or motive to lie is always admissible to impeach a witness","sub":"Impeachment","family":"Impeachment","unit":14},{"n":29,"summary":"Armed robbery witness previously convicted of perjury 11 years ago; defendant wants to impeach with that conviction.","trigger":"Impeachment with an old conviction more than 10 years after conviction or release, requiring reverse 403 balancing and advance notice.","rule":"If a conviction is more than ten years old (from the conviction or release from confinement, whichever is more recent), it can only be admitted if its probative value, supported by specific facts and circumstances, substantially outweighs its prejudicial effect and notice is given to the adverse party.","sub":"Impeachment – 10-year time limit (609(b))","family":"Impeachment","unit":20},{"n":30,"summary":"Contract dispute over forged signature; plaintiff testifies defendant signed; defendant offers a character witness who says plaintiff is very dishonest and has a video of plaintiff stealing from him.","trigger":"Use of reputation/opinion versus extrinsic specific-act evidence when attacking a witness’s character for truthfulness under Rule 608.","rule":"When impeaching a witness with character for truthfulness under 608, no extrinsic evidence is allowed.","sub":"Impeachment – Character for truthfulness (608)","family":"Impeachment","unit":17},{"n":31,"summary":"Arson defendant testifies; prosecution seeks to impeach with a three-year-old misdemeanor larceny by false pretenses conviction carrying six months’ sentence.","trigger":"Use of a crimen falsi conviction (dishonesty or false statement) to impeach any witness, regardless of felony/misdemeanor status.","rule":"When seeking to impeach a witness with a conviction for dishonesty or false statement, there is no balancing test used before it is admitted. The only misdemeanor convictions allowed to impeach a witness are those for dishonesty or false statement.","sub":"Impeachment – Crimes of dishonesty (609(a)(2))","family":"Impeachment","unit":19},{"n":32,"summary":"Burglary defendant testifies he mistakenly entered neighbor’s home while drunk; prosecution seeks to impeach with a nine-year-old burglary conviction.","trigger":"Impeachment of a criminal accused with a prior felony (not a crimen falsi) within 10 years, requiring probative value to outweigh prejudice.","rule":"If the witness is the accused in a criminal case, impeachment with a prior crime (other than one involving dishonesty or false statement) can only be admitted if the probative value of the conviction outweighs the prejudice that it will cause to the defendant.","sub":"Impeachment – Criminal accused prior felony","family":"Impeachment","unit":18},{"n":33,"summary":"Same bar fight; friend’s earlier statement blaming bartender was made in a deposition under oath; at trial friend blames defendant; defense wants to introduce the deposition testimony.","trigger":"Prior inconsistent statement given under oath at a formal proceeding, offered to both impeach and prove the truth of the matter asserted.","rule":"If a prior inconsistent statement is made under oath, it can be used to both impeach the witness and as substantive evidence of the matter asserted.","sub":"Impeachment – Prior inconsistent statement (under oath)","family":"Impeachment/Hearsay","unit":21},{"n":34,"summary":"Assault defendant’s friend originally told police bartender instigated fight, but at trial blames defendant; defense wants to introduce friend’s prior inconsistent statement to the police.","trigger":"Prior inconsistent statement not made under oath being offered both to impeach and as substantive evidence.","rule":"If a prior inconsistent statement is not made under oath, it can be used to impeach the witness but will not be admissible as substantive evidence.","sub":"Impeachment – Prior inconsistent statement (unsworn)","family":"Impeachment/Hearsay","unit":21},{"n":35,"summary":"Witness has old murder conviction and lied on housing application; cross-exam seeks both; extrinsic proof issues","trigger":"Impeachment by conviction (609) vs extrinsic limits for truthfulness acts (608)","rule":"Although extrinsic evidence can be used to prove a prior conviction under 609, it is not allowed to prove character for truthfulness under 608.","sub":"Impeachment (608/609)","family":"Impeachment","unit":17},{"n":36,"summary":"Witness denied statement about replacing defendant as subdivision president; friend offered to contradict","trigger":"Extrinsic evidence offered to show bias","rule":"Extrinsic evidence is allowed when impeaching a witness for bias.","sub":"Impeachment (Bias)","family":"Impeachment","unit":14},{"n":37,"summary":"Witness told officer car ran red but testified at trial motorcycle ran red; prior statement offered","trigger":"Prior inconsistent statement (not under oath) offered to impeach","rule":"A prior inconsistent statement can be used to impeach a witness. If it was under oath, it can also be used as substantive evidence in the case.","sub":"Impeachment (Prior Inconsistent)","family":"Impeachment/Hearsay","unit":21},{"n":38,"summary":"Robbery eyewitness previously lied to police about neighborhood fire; defense wants to use newspaper article reporting the lie.","trigger":"Specific instance of dishonest conduct offered to attack witness’s character for truthfulness.","rule":"Under 608, character evidence of a witness’s truthfulness is allowed but extrinsic evidence is not allowed to prove it.","sub":"Impeachment / Character for Truthfulness (Rule 608)","family":"Impeachment","unit":17},{"n":39,"summary":"Drug defendant testifies and prosecution offers 10.5-year-old prior drug possession misdemeanor conviction (max 9 months) to impeach.","trigger":"Old misdemeanor conviction that does not involve dishonesty offered to impeach under Rule 609. Here D convicted to 9 months (misdemeanor). + 1 year felony, less thann 1 year misdemeanor. Here, not dishonesty and not allowed","rule":"Under 609, a witness can be impeached by their prior convictions of misdemeanors only if they involved some element of dishonesty or false statement.","sub":"Impeachment / Prior Convictions (Rule 609)","family":"Impeachment","unit":19},{"n":40,"summary":"Shoplifting witness told manager teen was stealing, but at trial denies making that report under oath.","trigger":"Prior inconsistent out-of-court statement of a witness not given under oath.","rule":"If a prior inconsistent statement is made while not under oath, it can be brought in to impeach the witness but not as substantive evidence in the lawsuit.","sub":"Impeachment / Prior Inconsistent Statements","family":"Impeachment/Hearsay","unit":21},{"n":41,"summary":"Eyewitness to vandalism was 40 yards away without glasses; defense wants optometrist record showing she can’t see clearly beyond 30 yards.","trigger":"Medical record showing witness has impaired vision affecting ability to perceive events.","rule":"One way of impeaching a witness is discrediting their physical abilities, which would allow extrinsic evidence.","sub":"Impeachment / Sensory Defects","family":"Impeachment","unit":15},{"n":42,"summary":"Premises liability action for guest tripping on cracked walkway; plaintiff wants to introduce evidence that homeowner has $1 million liability insurance on the property.","trigger":"Offered use of liability insurance to prove negligence, fault, or ability to pay rather than a permissible purpose.","rule":"Insurance is not admissible to show fault or ability to pay, but may be admissible in other situations, such as to prove ownership or control, if that is an issue in the case.","sub":"Insurance – Inadmissible to show fault","family":"Policy Exclusions","unit":13},{"n":43,"summary":"Pedestrian sues landscaping company over accident involving truck bearing its logo; plaintiff offers proof company insured the vehicle to show it owned/controlled it.","trigger":"Liability insurance offered to prove ownership or control of vehicle, not negligence or ability to pay.","rule":"Although evidence of insurance is not allowed to show fault or ability to pay, it is admissible to show control or ownership if it is in dispute.","sub":"Liability Insurance (Rule 411)","family":"Policy Exclusions","unit":13},{"n":44,"summary":"After rear-end collision, defendant apologizes, admits lack of attention, and promises to pay all doctor bills; plaintiff wants entire statement in to prove negligence.","trigger":"Statement combines offer to pay medical expenses with separate admission of fault.","rule":"Under 409, evidence of offering to pay medical expenses is not admissible to prove liability for the injury, however other statements made in connection with the offer may be admissible.","sub":"Medical Expenses (Rule 409)","family":"Policy Exclusions","unit":12},{"n":45,"summary":"Store display of soda cans falls on customer; owner apologizes, admits he may have stacked them too high, and offers to pay all medical bills; later refuses to pay and is sued.","trigger":"Combined statement including an offer to pay medical expenses plus separate admissions about fault or negligence.","rule":"An offer to pay a hospital bill or similar expense is not admissible because a person may just be offering to pay it for humanitarian purposes. However, other statements made in conjunction with an offer to pay a medical bill can be admissible.","sub":"Offers to pay medical expenses – Accompanying admissions","family":"Policy Exclusions","unit":12},{"n":46,"summary":"At deposition, witness says husband punched wife; at criminal trial, witness recants. Prosecution offers deposition to impeach and for truth; defense objects.","trigger":"Prior inconsistent statement under oath at a deposition: admissible to impeach and substantively as hearsay exclusion.","rule":"If a prior inconsistent statement was made under oath, it can be admissible to impeach a witness and can be brought in for the truth of the matter asserted as a hearsay exclusion.","sub":"Prior Inconsistent Statements","family":"Impeachment/Hearsay","unit":21},{"n":47,"summary":"Client-attorney fee agreement limits scope to settlement; privilege objection in fee dispute","trigger":"Privilege asserted in lawsuit between attorney and client","rule":"When parties are in a relationship that gives rise to an attorney-client privilege, the privilege will not apply in a lawsuit between the parties.","sub":"Privileges","family":"Privileges","unit":39},{"n":48,"summary":"Diversity tort suit in federal court; defendant asserts doctor–patient privilege and court must determine which law of privilege applies.","trigger":"Choice between federal and state privilege law in a civil case where state substantive law provides the rule of decision.","rule":"In a diversity case, state law will be used to determine whether a privilege applies.","sub":"Privileges – Choice of law in diversity","family":"Privileges","unit":41},{"n":49,"summary":"Diversity case filed in State A federal court for accident in State B; State A uses lex loci delicti. Doctor refuses to testify based on doctor–patient privilege.","trigger":"In diversity, privilege is determined by state law selected by forum’s choice-of-law rules (Erie/FRE 501).","rule":"In a diversity case in federal court, the law that the state the court sits in will provide which state’s laws should be used in determining whether a privilege exists","sub":"Privileges (Doctor-Patient)","family":"Privileges","unit":41},{"n":50,"summary":"Defamation in diversity; plaintiff wants drug counselor to testify to defendant’s defamatory statement made in counseling session; state law makes such communications privileged.","trigger":"Civil diversity case seeking to apply a state-created counseling privilege.","rule":"Whether something is privileged in a diversity case is decided by state law, unless it is the attorney client privilege or work product which is covered by the Federal rules of Evidence.","sub":"Privileges / Choice of Law","family":"Privileges","unit":41},{"n":51,"summary":"Federal diversity defamation case; plaintiff seeks reporter’s source; state law where claim arose recognizes reporter-source privilege.","trigger":"Privilege issue in federal court where state law supplies the rule of decision (diversity).","rule":"Under FRE 501, in a civil case, state law governs privilege regarding a claim or defense for which state law supplies the rule of decision.","sub":"Privileges / FRE 501 (Diversity)","family":"Privileges","unit":41},{"n":52,"summary":"In divorce alimony case, wife offers husband’s prior confession of adultery made during the marriage.","trigger":"Confidential marital communication offered in civil case where spouses are opposing parties.","rule":"Although there is a marital communications privilege that either side can raise to disallow evidence of confidential communications between spouses, the privilege does not apply when the lawsuit is between the two spouses.","sub":"Privileges / Marital Communications","family":"Privileges","unit":40},{"n":53,"summary":"Diversity products-liability case; manufacturer inadvertently discloses privileged memo and delays acting for two months.","trigger":"Inadvertent disclosure of privileged document with no prompt steps taken to rectify.","rule":"In a federal proceeding, an inadvertent disclosure does not operate as a waiver if it is inadvertent, the holder of the privilege took reasonable steps to protect the privilege, and the holder of the privilege promptly took reasonable steps to rectify the error.","sub":"Privileges / Waiver","family":"Privileges","unit":41},{"n":54,"summary":"Civil sexual assault suit; defendant offered evidence of plaintiff’s prior sex history via friend statement. Plaintiff alleged that she was preserving her virginity for marriage","trigger":"Civil rape-shield balancing test","rule":"In a civil case, the court may admit evidence offered to prove a victim’s sexual behavior or sexual predisposition if its probative value substantially outweighs the danger of harm to any victim and of unfair prejudice to any party. The court may admit evidence of a victim’s reputation only if the victim has placed it in controversy.","sub":"Rape Shield (Rule 412)","family":"Rape Shield (FRE 412)","unit":10},{"n":55,"summary":"Robbery defendant on house arrest rents van under fake name and is caught far away, suggesting flight.","trigger":"Post-charge conduct showing attempt to flee jurisdiction while on house arrest.","rule":"Evidence is relevant if it has any tendency to help prove or disprove a fact that is of consequence to the action.","sub":"Relevance / Consciousness of Guilt","family":"Relevance","unit":2},{"n":56,"summary":"Man admits intentional can-throwing during settlement talks in prior civil case; prosecution later wants that admission in criminal case.","trigger":"Statement made in course of compromise negotiations over a disputed civil claim.","rule":"Any statement made in an offer to compromise a disputed claim is not admissible to prove liability, although it can be admissible for other reasons such as showing bias of a witness.","sub":"Settlement / Offers to Compromise (Rule 408)","family":"Policy Exclusions","unit":12},{"n":57,"summary":"Homeowner violates ordinance requiring sidewalk clearing within 48 hours; mail carrier slips at 50 hours. Neighbor clears ice right after accident; plaintiff offers this as evidence.","trigger":"Whether subsequent remedial measures evidence is admissible; repairs made by someone other than the defendant.","rule":"Subsequent remedial measures taken by a defendant will not be admissible to prove negligence, but if the repairs are made by someone other than the defendant, they may be admissible.","sub":"Subsequent Remedial Measures","family":"Policy Exclusions","unit":11},{"n":58,"summary":"In negligence trial, judge orders plaintiff to testify first and excludes plaintiff’s other witnesses from courtroom without party request.","trigger":"Judge’s authority to control mode/order of interrogation (Rule 611) and sequester witnesses (Rule 615) sua sponte.","rule":"Rules 611 and 615 allow the judge to control the presentation of evidence and allow the judge, on his own initiative, to exclude witnesses.","sub":"Trial Management / Sequestration","family":"Trial Architecture","unit":4},{"n":59,"summary":"A woman is prosecuted for involuntary manslaughter after a car crash. To prove the road was “normal and dry,” the prosecutor offers the investigating officer’s police report. The defense objects to admitting the report at the criminal trial.","trigger":"Prosecution offers a police report (public record) to prove facts against a criminal defendant.","rule":"The public records exception to hearsay cannot be used against a defendant in a criminal case. If the defendant wants to bring it in a criminal trial, he can, but the prosection cant use it aginst him. How to get around this defense? Bring the police officer to the stand as a witness and make him declare the conditions of the road that day, but not based on the report","sub":"Hearsay – Public Records","family":"803(6)/(8)","unit":29},{"n":60,"summary":"In a civil battery case, a neighbor witnessed the punch and told a police officer what she saw about 20 minutes later. The neighbor refuses to testify at trial, so plaintiff offers the officer to repeat the neighbor’s statement. Defense objects claiming constitutional violation.","trigger":"Whether the Confrontation Clause restricts admission of out‑of‑court testimonial statements in a civil proceeding.","rule":"The 6th Amendment Confrontation Clause only applies to criminal cases. Here, I got confused with present sense exception to the hearsay rule because, PS is a statement made at the moment of perceiving it or immediatly after. Here, witness told police officer 20 minuts later.","sub":"Constitutional Limits – Confrontation","family":"Constitutional Overlay","unit":42},{"n":61,"summary":"A defendant on trial for statutory rape claims he reasonably believed the 15‑year‑old was 18. The prosecution gave notice that it will offer evidence the defendant was accused of statutory rape twice before (no charges filed because accusers refused to testify). Defense objects.","trigger":"Other-acts evidence offered to show lack of mistake; proponent must show proper non‑character purpose by preponderance.","rule":"MIAMICOPS PPPP - TO bring in other crimes, wrongs, or acts, the proponent has the burden by a preponderance of the evidence to prove that the evidence is being brought in for a proper purpose, other than character.","sub":"Rule 404(b) – Other Acts","family":"Relevance","unit":3},{"n":62,"summary":"In a federal negligence case, plaintiff claims a truck with defendant’s logo caused a collision. Defendant argues it sold the truck months earlier. Plaintiff wants to introduce evidence that defendant is still listed as the insurer on the certificate of insurance; defendant objects.","trigger":"Insurance evidence is generally barred to prove fault, but may be admissible to prove ownership/control when disputed.","rule":"Although insurance is not admissible to prove fault or ability to pay, it can be admissible to prove other things, such as control or ownership, if it is in dispute.","sub":"Relevance – Insurance","family":"Policy Exclusions","unit":13},{"n":63,"summary":"At trial, the prosecution offered testimony that included an out-of-court statement. Defense counsel did not object while the witness testified. Only after the witness left the stand did defense counsel object on hearsay grounds, trying to exclude the testimony.","trigger":"To preserve an evidentiary issue, the objection must be made at the time the evidence is offered and must be specific; waiting until after the witness leaves is untimely.","rule":"Under FRE 103, an objection must be timely and specific. An objection raised only after the witness has left the stand is not timely.","sub":"Objections – Timely & Specific","family":"Trial Architecture","unit":4},{"n":64,"summary":"In a civil case, a witness testified at trial inconsistently with what he told the police earlier. The earlier police statement was not under oath. The proponent wants to use the earlier statement not just to impeach, but as substantive proof of what actually happened.","trigger":"Prior inconsistent statements are admissible as substantive evidence only if made under oath in a prior proceeding; otherwise limited to impeachment.","rule":"For a prior inconsistent statement by a witness to be used as substantive evidence, it must have been under oath at the time it was made.","sub":"Impeachment – Prior Inconsistent Statements","family":"Impeachment/Hearsay","unit":21},{"n":65,"summary":"Defendant on trial for larceny of a neighbor’s wheelbarrow claims he mistakenly believed it was his; prosecution wants to introduce a prior incident where he was caught attempting to steal a chainsaw from another neighbor (no charges filed).","trigger":"Proponent offers prior bad act evidence for a non-character purpose (e.g., to negate claimed mistake) under FRE 404(b) and must meet the admissibility showing.","rule":"Under FRE 404(b), other crimes, wrongs, or acts, may be admissible if the proponent can prove by a preponderance of the evidence that they are not being offered to prove character, rather they are offered to prove things like absence of mistake.","sub":"Character Evidence – Other acts (FRE 404(b)) / non-propensity purpose & preponderance standard","family":"Relevance","unit":3},{"n":66,"summary":"Reporter interviews a baseball star who blurts that the GM is committing fraud; reporter writes the statement in her journal; months later, in a civil fraud-related suit, reporter testifies but forgot the journal at home; opposing counsel objects when she recounts what the star said.","trigger":"Best evidence objection where witness has personal knowledge of what was said, not knowledge solely derived from a writing.","rule":"The best evidence rule only applies when a person’s sole knowledge comes from a writing.","sub":"Best Evidence Rule – Applies only when contents of writing are at issue / sole knowledge from writing","family":"Best Evidence Rule (1002)","unit":34},{"n":67,"summary":"At deposition, witness testifies under oath that trucker threw first punch; at trial in biker’s civil suit against trucker, witness changes story and says biker threw first punch; biker wants to introduce the deposition statement.","trigger":"Prior inconsistent statement was made under oath in a prior proceeding (deposition) → admissible substantively and for impeachment.","rule":"If a witness makes a prior inconsistent statement under oath, it can be admitted for both substantive purposes as well as to impeach the witness.","sub":"Hearsay Exemptions – Prior inconsistent statement under oath (FRE 801(d)(1)(A))","family":"Impeachment/Hearsay","unit":21},{"n":68,"summary":"Plaintiff is injured when a knife protrudes through thin packaging and sues manufacturer for strict products liability. Manufacturer claims it was impossible to package the knives differently without major cost; plaintiff wants to introduce evidence that after the accident the manufacturer changed packaging to a safer design with little/no extra cost.","trigger":"Subsequent remedial measures are offered not to prove negligence/defect but to rebut a defense that an alternative was impossible.","rule":"Although subsequent remedial measures are not admissible to prove negligence, they are allowed to refute a claim of impossibility.","sub":"Relevance Limits – Subsequent remedial measures; feasibility/impossibility","family":"Policy Exclusions","unit":11},{"n":69,"summary":"Undercover officer sees a woman sell marijuana and arrests her. After Miranda, she admits selling and says a defendant supplied her. At the defendant’s trial, prosecutor asks what she told the officer about her source; defense objects on hearsay grounds.","trigger":"Attempt to use statement-against-interest hearsay exception when the declarant is available and testifying.","rule":"The statement against interest exception to hearsay requires that the declarant is unavailable. Here, the woman was presente at the trial so defense cannot use statement against interest. Remember this exception requieres declarant unavailable and needs corroboration. I picked party admission by co-conspirator but this was wrong because the woman only admitted that defendant was her supplier and it was not a statment made during a conspiracy or in furher of a conspiracy. Therefore, the answer was inadmissible hearsay","sub":"Hearsay Exceptions – Statement against interest (requires unavailability)","family":"804(b)(3)","unit":32},{"n":70,"summary":"Negligence car accident. At deposition (under oath), Witness said Defendant ran the red light. At trial, Witness flips and says Defendant had the green light (i.e., Plaintiff ran it). Plaintiff offers deposition testimony.","trigger":"Witness gives a prior inconsistent statement made under oath at a deposition and is testifying now (subject to cross).","rule":"A witness’s prior inconsistent statement given under oath at a deposition is admissible both to impeach and as substantive evidence (non-hearsay under FRE 801(d)(1)(A)).","sub":"Impeachment – Prior Inconsistent (Deposition)","family":"Impeachment/Hearsay","unit":21},{"n":71,"summary":"Same accident facts. Witness told the investigating police officer that Defendant ran the red light. At trial, Witness flips and says Defendant had green light. Plaintiff offers the statement to the officer.","trigger":"Prior inconsistent statement was not under oath (statement to police).","rule":"A prior inconsistent statement not made under oath is admissible only to impeach (not for its truth) unless another hearsay exception applies.","sub":"Impeachment – Prior Inconsistent (Not Under Oath)","family":"Impeachment/Hearsay","unit":21},{"n":72,"summary":"Same accident facts. Witness testifies at trial that Defendant ran the red light. Plaintiff wants to introduce Witness’s earlier consistent statement to the police to ‘bolster’ credibility (no impeachment yet).","trigger":"Proponent seeks a prior consistent statement before the witness has been attacked for fabrication/improper motive.","rule":"Prior consistent statements are generally inadmissible to bolster; they become admissible only to rehabilitate after an express/implied charge of recent fabrication or improper influence/motive (and must predate the motive).","sub":"Rehabilitation – Prior Consistent / No Bolstering","family":"801(d)(1)","unit":24},{"n":73,"summary":"Civil rights case: Inmate vs Guard. After Inmate testifies, Guard wants to introduce Inmate’s conviction 14 years ago for sexual assault of his 8-year-old daughter; Inmate served a 10-year sentence.","trigger":"Impeachment by conviction under FRE 609; determine 10-year clock and apply correct balancing for civil case/witness.","rule":"For a non-defendant witness, felony convictions are admissible only if probative value is not substantially outweighed by unfair prejudice (FRE 403/609(a)(1)(A)); even if within 10 years, highly prejudicial non-crimen-falsi felonies may be excluded.","sub":"Impeachment – Conviction (609) / Balancing","family":"Impeachment","unit":18},{"n":74,"summary":"Same civil case. After Inmate testifies, Guard offers Inmate’s conviction 8 years ago for making a false statement on a federal document; sentence was 9 months (misdemeanor).","trigger":"Impeachment by crimen falsi (dishonest act/false statement).","rule":"Crimes involving dishonesty or false statement are admissible to impeach any witness regardless of punishment, subject only to the 10-year limit (FRE 609(a)(2)).","sub":"Impeachment – Conviction (Crimen Falsi)","family":"Impeachment","unit":19},{"n":75,"summary":"After Guard testifies, Inmate wants to prove Guard lied on his resume about having a bachelor’s degree; Inmate has an official transcript contradicting it.","trigger":"Specific instance of conduct probative of truthfulness (lying), but no conviction.","rule":"On cross, a witness may be asked about specific instances of conduct probative of character for truthfulness, but extrinsic evidence is not allowed to prove them (FRE 608(b)).","sub":"Impeachment – 608(b) Specific Acts / No Extrinsic","family":"Impeachment","unit":17},{"n":76,"summary":"After Guard testifies, Inmate wants to impeach Guard with a felony drug conviction from 6 years ago and introduce the conviction record if Guard denies it.","trigger":"Impeachment by felony conviction under FRE 609; witness is a party but not a criminal defendant.","rule":"A witness may be impeached with a felony conviction; if the witness denies it, the conviction record is admissible as extrinsic evidence, subject to balancing (FRE 609(a)(1) and 403 in civil cases).","sub":"Impeachment – Felony Conviction / Extrinsic OK","family":"Impeachment","unit":17},{"n":77,"summary":"Civil conversion case. After Witness testifies for Plaintiff, Defendant wants to introduce evidence about Witness’s moral character.","trigger":"Character for truthfulness can be supported only after attack; and methods are limited.","rule":"A witness’s character for truthfulness may be supported only after it has been attacked; support/attack is by reputation or opinion (not specific acts). Specific instances generally cannot be proved by extrinsic evidence (FRE 608(a), 608(b)).","sub":"Witness Character (608) – Support Only After Attack","family":"Impeachment","unit":16},{"n":78,"summary":"Highly publicized murder trial. Father testifies as alibi witness. Prosecution seeks to impeach Father with a perjury felony: conviction 12 years ago; Father was released from prison exactly 10 years + 2 days before he testified (trial started earlier).","trigger":"Remote conviction: 10-year clock runs from later of conviction or release; if >10 years at time of testimony, 609(b) applies.","rule":"If more than 10 years have passed since the witness’s conviction or release (whichever is later), the conviction is admissible only if its probative value substantially outweighs its prejudicial effect and the proponent gives reasonable written notice (FRE 609(b)).","sub":"Impeachment – Remote Conviction (609(b))","family":"Impeachment","unit":20},{"n":79,"summary":"Robbery trial. Defense impeaches store owner’s perception (not wearing required glasses; wants extrinsic proof from license). Prosecution impeaches defense alibi witness (business partner) for bias (financial interest; wants extrinsic proof).","trigger":"Impeachment by perception and bias; extrinsic evidence generally allowed after foundation.","rule":"A witness may be impeached for lack of perception and for bias; extrinsic evidence is permitted for these non-collateral impeachment grounds (after the witness has a chance to explain/deny).","sub":"Impeachment – Perception & Bias / Extrinsic Allowed","family":"Impeachment","unit":14},{"n":80,"summary":"Defendant is on trial for attempted fraud after switching a price tag on a coat. On cross-examination, prosecutor asks about defendant’s prior misdemeanor fraud convictions for the same price-tag switching misconduct; issue is whether the convictions are admissible.","trigger":"Impeachment by prior conviction for dishonesty/false statement (Rule 609) and potential non-propensity use of similar misconduct with limiting instruction.","rule":"Prior convictions for dishonesty/false statement are admissible to impeach under Rule 609, and similar misconduct may also be admissible for a non-propensity purpose (e.g., intent/common plan) with a limiting instruction.","sub":"Impeachment – Prior convictions (Rule 609); other acts non-propensity use","family":"404(b)","unit":7},{"n":81,"summary":"In a trial, counsel wants to ask questions on re-direct after cross-examination. The issue is what re-direct may cover and when it must be permitted.","trigger":"Re-direct is limited to significant new matters raised on cross-examination (generally within the trial court’s discretion).","rule":"Redirect is limited to significant new matters raised on cross-examination (generally within the court’s discretion).","sub":"Witness Examination – Scope of re-direct","family":"Trial Architecture","unit":4},{"n":82,"summary":"In a criminal trial, the prosecutor wants to introduce other-acts evidence to show a proper non-character purpose (like motive), but the evidence includes inflammatory details (e.g., heroin/needles) that risk the jury using it to infer bad character.","trigger":"Other-acts evidence is offered for a proper Rule 404(b) purpose, but the court must still exclude it if unfair prejudice substantially outweighs probative value under Rule 403.","rule":"Other-acts evidence offered for a “proper purpose” (e.g., motive) is still excluded if, under Rule 403, its probative value is substantially outweighed by unfair prejudice (e.g., heroin/needle suggesting criminal propensity).","sub":"Character Evidence – 404(b) proper purpose; Rule 403 unfair prejudice","family":"404(b)","unit":8},{"n":83,"summary":"Witness gave a prior statement that conflicts with what the witness says at trial. The examiner wants to use the earlier statement to challenge credibility and possibly as proof of what actually happened.","trigger":"Prior inconsistent statement is offered; it is always admissible for impeachment, but is substantive only if made under oath in a prior proceeding.","rule":"A prior inconsistent statement is admissible to impeach; it is admissible as substantive evidence only if it was made under oath in a prior proceeding (e.g., deposition/hearing).","sub":"Hearsay / Impeachment – Prior inconsistent statements (oath requirement for substance)","family":"Impeachment/Hearsay","unit":21},{"n":84,"summary":"Plaintiff sues a defendant for a slip-and-fall on the defendant’s business property. Plaintiff calls the defendant’s property manager only to prove that the defendant owned the property where plaintiff fell. On cross, defense counsel tries to question the manager about the safety precautions the defendant took to protect business invitees.","trigger":"Cross-examination goes beyond the subject matter of direct; majority rule limits cross to matters raised on direct + credibility.","rule":"On cross, the examiner may ask about matters covered on direct and credibility (majority “limited cross” rule; some judges allow broader cross).","sub":"Witness Examination – Scope of cross-examination","family":"Trial Architecture","unit":4},{"n":85,"summary":"Defendant is tried for aggravated assault; the victim testifies she was beaten severely but was not asked about anything she said during the incident. The prosecutor then calls a witness to testify that when the beating stopped the victim screamed, “I’m dying—don’t let the defendant get away with it!” The issue is whether the witness’s testimony about the statement is admissible and under what hearsay exception (excited utterance vs dying declaration).","trigger":"Statement made under stress of a startling event is offered; excited utterance vs dying declaration requirements (unavailability + homicide/civil + impending death).","rule":"A victim’s statement made under stress of a startling event and relating to it is an excited utterance (803); dying declaration (804) requires unavailability and is limited to homicide or civil cases.","sub":"Hearsay Exceptions – Excited utterance vs dying declaration","family":"803(1)/(2)","unit":28},{"n":86,"summary":"A developer sells a lot but the buyer cannot decide between Lot 40 and Lot 41 (each priced at $5,000). The buyer pays $5,000 and the developer delivers a properly executed deed that is complete in every respect except the lot-number blank is left empty; the developer tells the buyer to fill in either Lot 40 or Lot 41 and record the deed. The next day the buyer changes his mind, fills in “Lot 25” (worth $7,500), and records the deed; the developer sues to rescind and challenges the buyer’s use of the deed to take Lot 25.","trigger":"Attempt to use prior oral understanding to contradict the written deed terms (e.g., deed/record controls; cannot contradict by prior oral terms).","rule":"Parol evidence rule: Once the deed/writing is completed, prior oral understandings cannot be used to contradict the written terms (e.g., deed says Lot 25—can’t use oral “40 or 41” to contradict). Once the deed is completed as a writing, the developer can’t bring in the earlier oral “only 40 or 41” term to contradict what the deed now says. In real life, fraud or alteration beyond authority could be argued to unwind it. Your tutor’s point is: the best MC answer is the one that matches the exam-tested doctrine—parol evidence / written deed controls—not “fairness.”","sub":"Parol Evidence Rule – Deeds/writings; no contradiction","family":"Parol Evidence","unit":null},{"n":87,"summary":"In a civil car-accident trial, plaintiff testifies on direct that he came to a full stop at an intersection. On cross, after plaintiff says he was driving carefully and exercising due care, defense counsel seeks to ask about two prior intersection accidents in the last 12 months and traffic citations for failing to stop at stop signs; plaintiff objects to the use of those prior incidents.","trigger":"Civil case attempts to use specific prior traffic incidents to show propensity; traffic incidents do not bear on truthfulness under Rule 608.","rule":"In a civil case, you generally can’t use specific prior bad acts to show propensity (bad driver); and prior traffic incidents don’t bear on truthfulness under 608.","sub":"Character / Impeachment – Specific acts in civil; Rule 608 limits","family":"Character","unit":6},{"n":88,"summary":"At a civil slander trial, plaintiff proves defendant called plaintiff a thief. In defense, defendant offers a neighbor’s testimony that people in the community generally say plaintiff is a thief. Issue is whether testimony about plaintiff’s reputation is admissible.","trigger":"Defamation claim where plaintiff’s character (truth/damages) is in issue; reputation evidence offered.","rule":"In a civil case, character evidence is generally barred unless character is an essential element; in defamation, the plaintiff’s reputation/character (e.g., “is he a thief?”) can be proved by reputation evidence and used both on truth and damages.","sub":"Character Evidence – Defamation; reputation","family":"Character","unit":6},{"n":89,"summary":"In a civil professional‑malpractice trial, plaintiff’s expert bases opinions on experience and says a technical book (“Smith on Milling Systems”) is a reliable treatise consistent with his views. On cross, he admits nobody’s perfect; defense calls a second expert who says the book used to be reliable but is now badly out of date. Plaintiff asks that the jury be allowed to examine the book and judge its reliability.","trigger":"Learned treatise use: whether book can be examined by jury when expert did not rely and reliability is disputed.","rule":"A learned treatise may be read into evidence only if established as reliable authority (and used with an expert), but the book itself is not admitted/examined by the jury, and here the expert did not rely on it.","sub":"Hearsay – Learned treatise (Rule 803(18))","family":"803(18)","unit":36},{"n":90,"summary":"In a gang‑related murder trial, prosecution introduces former testimony from a gang member who testified at a preliminary hearing but now invokes the privilege against self‑incrimination. Defendant seeks to impeach the gang member’s credibility; question asks what impeachment evidence the court is most likely to admit.","trigger":"Former testimony admitted due to unavailability; impeachment of declarant-witness (e.g., bias) and whether extrinsic evidence is allowed.","rule":"When prior testimony is admitted because the witness is unavailable, the opponent may impeach it; bias is always relevant and can be proven with extrinsic evidence.","sub":"Impeachment – Bias/character; extrinsic evidence","family":"804(b)(1)/806","unit":31},{"n":91,"summary":"In a civil action about misrepresentation in a real‑estate sale, whether defendant was licensed as a real‑estate broker is disputed. Defendant testifies she was licensed; plaintiff offers an official certification (sealed and signed by the secretary of the State Board of Realtors) stating that a thorough search found no record that any license was ever issued to defendant. Issue is whether the certification is admissible.","trigger":"Proof that a public record does not exist by an official certification after diligent search (absence of public record hearsay exception).","rule":"A certification that a diligent search found no record is admissible under the hearsay exception for absence of a public record (and can be used to prove nonexistence).","sub":"Hearsay – Absence of public record","family":"803(6)/(8)","unit":29},{"n":92,"summary":"Employee sues employer alleging poor working conditions caused a stomach ulcer. Plaintiff’s medical expert testifies about causation and says the opinion is based in part on information in a letter from plaintiff’s personal physician to the employer explaining why plaintiff missed work. Issue is whether the doctor’s letter is admissible to prove causation.","trigger":"Party tries to admit a doctor’s letter relied on by an expert; expert reliance does not create an independent hearsay exception.","rule":"An expert’s reliance on a document does not make the document admissible; unless a hearsay exception applies, the letter is inadmissible hearsay.","sub":"Hearsay – Expert basis ≠ admissibility","family":"702/703/704","unit":37},{"n":93,"summary":"In an armed‑robbery prosecution, the victim was held up with an unusual revolver with a red‑painted barrel. The prosecutor offers testimony from another witness that one week after the robbery, that witness was robbed by the defendant with a pistol that also had red paint on the barrel. Issue is whether the testimony is admissible.","trigger":"Other-acts evidence offered to prove identity via distinctive “signature” feature; admissibility under 404(b).","rule":"Prior bad acts can be admitted for a non-propensity purpose (e.g., identity) when a distinctive feature (red-painted barrel) links the defendant to the charged crime.","sub":"Other Acts – 404(b) identity","family":"404(b)","unit":7},{"n":94,"summary":"After dinner, a woman tells her boyfriend she is going to her cousin’s house to pick up money the cousin owes her and will come right home. Hours later she is found in her car near the cousin’s house, stabbed to death. The cousin is charged with murder, and the prosecution wants the boyfriend to testify to what she said about going to the cousin’s house.","trigger":"Out‑of‑court statement offered to show the declarant’s then‑existing intent/plan (state of mind) rather than to prove past facts.","rule":"An exception to hearsay can be found in a statement made under the declarant’s then existing state of mind (such as motive, intent, or plan) or emotional, sensory, or physical condition.","sub":"Hearsay Exceptions – Then‑existing state of mind (intent/plan)","family":"803(3)","unit":26},{"n":95,"summary":"A passenger sues a driver for negligence after the driver ran a stop sign and caused a serious injury. In the civil case, the driver wants his mother to testify that she believes he is a cautious driver and that he has been accident‑free for five years, to suggest he acted carefully here.","trigger":"In a civil case, character evidence to prove conduct in conformity is inadmissible unless character is an essential element; cautiousness is not essential to ordinary negligence.","rule":"Character evidence of the parties is not allowed in a civil case unless it is an essential element of the claim.","sub":"Character Evidence – Civil cases (character inadmissible unless essential element)","family":"Character","unit":5},{"n":96,"summary":"A passenger sues a mother for negligently allowing her adult son to use her car after he ran a stop sign and injured the passenger (negligent entrustment theory). The mother wants to testify that her son is a cautious driver and has been accident‑free for five years to show she reasonably allowed him to drive.","trigger":"When character is an essential element of a claim/defense (e.g., competence in negligent entrustment), any method may be used to prove character (opinion, reputation, specific acts).","rule":"If character of the parties is an essential element of a claim, any party can use any method to prove character.","sub":"Character Evidence – Essential element cases (methods of proof)","family":"Character","unit":6},{"n":97,"summary":"After a crash where the son ran a stop sign and a passenger was killed, the state charges the son with involuntary manslaughter. The son wants his mother to testify that he is a cautious driver and has never had an accident in five years.","trigger":"Criminal defendant may offer pertinent character evidence by opinion or reputation, but cannot prove it through specific instances of conduct.","rule":"Character evidence is generally not admissible to prove conduct (i.e., “he’s the kind of person who would/wouldn’t do this”), but a criminal defendant may choose to introduce good-character evidence if it is pertinent to the charged offense.Once the defendant does that, the defendant has opened the door and the prosecution may rebut. In a criminal case, a defendant may raise character as circumstantial evidence of a pertinent trait by using either opinion or reputation evidence, but not specific instances of conduct. Then, the prosecutor can rebute using the same method (opinion/reputation) OR cross-examine the defendant’s character witness about specific acts relevant to the trait the witness testified about (NO EXTRINSIC EVIDENCE ALLOWED PROSECUTION CAN ONLY ASK). So, D cant bring SIC for his good character but prosecution can bring SIC on cross examination of defendants character witness","sub":"Character Evidence – Criminal defendant (opinion/reputation only; no specific acts)","family":"Character","unit":5},{"n":98,"summary":"A woman was being charged for stealing an outdoor lawn ornament from her neighbor. A witness had seen the woman take the ornament and called the police. When the police arrived, the witness spoke to the officer and told him, “I saw the woman take the ornament as I was looking out my front window. I saw her very clearly and could tell it was her.” At trial, the witness took the stand for the prosecution and testified, “I saw somebody take the ornament as I was looking out my front window. I didn’t get a very good look, though, so I’m not sure that it was the woman.” The prosecution would like to admit the statement that the witness made to the police officer.","trigger":"Witness testifies inconsistently with a prior statement not made under oath; impeachment use only.","rule":"A witness’s prior inconsistent statement can only be used to impeach the witness if it was not made under oath.","sub":"Impeachment – Prior inconsistent statements","family":"Impeachment/Hearsay","unit":21},{"n":99,"summary":"A woman was being charged for stealing an outdoor lawn ornament from her neighbor. A witness had seen the woman take the ornament and called the police. When the police arrived, the witness spoke to the officer and told him, “I saw the woman take the ornament as I was looking out my front window. I saw her very clearly and could tell it was her.” While the police were speaking with the witness, the woman returned to the scene and voluntarily told the officer, “I took the lawn ornament because the neighbor owes me money and refuses to pay me.” Do you want to listen to the audio files that accompany these questions?","trigger":"Party witness made inconsistent out‑of‑court statement; qualifies as party admission and is substantive.","rule":"A party witness’s inconsistent statement, even if not under oath, can be used to both impeach the party witness and can be brought in for the truth of the matter asserted if a party admission.","sub":"Hearsay – Party admissions; inconsistent statements","family":"Hearsay","unit":22},{"n":100,"summary":"A man was on trial for robbery. After the man presented evidence that he was a very peaceful person, the prosecution called the man’s ex-wife, who testified that the man frequently beat her and their son. Which of the following pieces of evidence will the court most likely allow to impeach the ex- wife?","trigger":"Impeachment by showing witness bias/motive/interest.","rule":"One of the ways to impeach a witness is through bias.","sub":"Impeachment – Bias","family":"Impeachment","unit":14},{"n":101,"summary":"At a defendant’s trial for intoxication manslaughter, the prosecution offers evidence to show she was intoxicated. Options include: her bar receipt found in her car; her statement to a bystander that she didn’t realize how many drinks she consumed; testimony that a witness told her after the accident that she smelled like alcohol; and testimony that the bartender told her she was too intoxicated to drive. The question asks which is admissible to show she was intoxicated.","trigger":"Evidence offered for intoxication; identify admissible nonhearsay—especially defendant’s own statement as a party admission.","rule":"A defendant’s own statement is admissible as a party‑opponent admission and is not excluded by the hearsay rule.","sub":"Hearsay – Party admissions","family":"801(d)(2)","unit":25},{"n":102,"summary":"A man calls a lawyer’s office; the lawyer was recommended by a friend previously represented by the lawyer. When the lawyer’s secretary answers, the man says: “I need to talk to the lawyer right away. I’ve committed a crime, and now I’m in serious trouble.” The question asks whether the secretary may disclose the fact that the man has committed a crime.","trigger":"Confidential communication to law-office staff for the purpose of obtaining legal advice; privilege scope includes attorney’s agents.","rule":"Attorney–client privilege protects confidential communications made for the purpose of obtaining legal advice, and it extends to the lawyer’s agents (including a secretary), so the secretary may not disclose the communication.","sub":"Privileges – Attorney–client; agents","family":"Privileges","unit":39},{"n":103,"summary":"A florist and her assistant were very close. The florist is terminally ill, and on her deathbed the assistant says: “Don’t you worry, I will take care of your medical bills.” The florist dies. The assistant does not pay; the hospital sends the account to collections, and because of the large sum owed, the florist’s house must be sold to cover expenses. The florist’s heirs sue the assistant for breach of promise and seek to introduce the assistant’s statement at trial.","trigger":"Out-of-court statement offered to prove the making of a promise/undertaking (the words themselves have legal significance).","rule":"A statement is not hearsay if it is offered as a legally operative promise (a verbal act) rather than to prove the truth of what was asserted.","sub":"Hearsay – Nonhearsay (verbal acts)","family":"Hearsay","unit":null},{"n":104,"summary":"A pickpocket is charged with larceny after being caught holding a tourist’s wallet. The prosecution seeks to introduce evidence such as prior bad acts or prior convictions (including a prior larceny conviction involving stealing an out-of-towner’s wallet).","trigger":"Prosecution offers prior misconduct/conviction to show something other than propensity (e.g., intent/knowledge) in a theft case.","rule":"Prior crimes or bad acts may be admitted for a non-propensity purpose such as proving intent, knowledge, or absence of mistake (subject to Rule 403), but not to show character to prove conduct.","sub":"Other Acts – 404(b) intent/knowledge","family":"404(b)","unit":7},{"n":105,"summary":"Question asks in which situation an original document is required under the best evidence rule, with scenarios involving a copy of a will, missing threatening letter, photograph sold to a dealer, and a poem where contents are not closely related to a controlling issue.","trigger":"Best evidence: proving contents of a writing/recording when contents are in dispute and material.","rule":"The best evidence rule requires an original (or acceptable duplicate) when a party seeks to prove the contents of a writing and the contents are material; otherwise, secondary evidence may be used if an exception applies.","sub":"Best Evidence Rule – When original required","family":"Best Evidence Rule (1002)","unit":34},{"n":106,"summary":"In a medical malpractice case, the patient’s expert testifies the doctor’s drugs were experimental and negligent. On cross, the expert admits a treatise (Pharmacology: A Guide for the Practitioner) is a reliable authority in her field but says she did not rely on it in forming her opinion. The doctor’s lawyer seeks to read a passage stating the drugs are widely used; the patient objects.","trigger":"Learned treatise offered after expert acknowledges it as reliable authority; determine admissibility and whether it comes in for truth.","rule":"Statements from a learned treatise may be read into evidence as substantive evidence if the treatise is established as a reliable authority (e.g., by the expert’s admission), even if the expert did not rely on it.","sub":"Hearsay Exceptions – Learned treatise","family":"803(18)","unit":36},{"n":107,"summary":"Which situation most likely triggers the best evidence rule: (A) photo of vandalized mural with signature, (B) obscenity prosecution where prosecutor wants to show the film, (C) recording of comments in harassment case, or (D) reporter’s notes quoting a defendant verbatim when the reporter cannot remember the interview.","trigger":"Best evidence rule applies when a party seeks to prove the contents of a writing/recording/photograph/film; strongest when the recording itself is the object of proof (e.g., obscenity depends on the film’s content).","rule":"The best evidence rule requires producing the original (or a permissible duplicate) when a party seeks to prove the contents of a writing, recording, photograph, or film; it most directly applies when the case turns on the content of the recording/film itself.","sub":"Best Evidence Rule – Proving contents of a recording/film","family":"Best Evidence Rule (1002)","unit":34},{"n":108,"summary":"At a rancher’s first‑degree murder trial, the defense calls a witness who testifies the rancher was with him at the time her husband was killed. On cross‑examination, the prosecutor asks whether the witness told a police investigator the day after the killing that he had not seen the rancher for several days. The question is whether the investigator’s notes of the prior statement are admissible.","trigger":"Witness testifies at trial; opposing party has a prior inconsistent statement; wants to use investigator’s notes (extrinsic evidence).","rule":"A prior inconsistent statement not made under oath may be used only to impeach a witness (not substantively).","sub":"Impeachment – Prior inconsistent statements","family":"Impeachment/Hearsay","unit":21},{"n":109,"summary":"A farmer is on trial for arson for allegedly burning down a neighbor’s barn. The defense wants the farmer’s son to testify that the farmer referred to himself as “Poseidon, ruler of the waters,” and told the son the day after the barn burned that when the farmer saw the barn burning he was terrified because Poseidon’s only true fear is of fire. The question asks for what purpose the son’s testimony may be admitted.","trigger":"Defendant’s bizarre statement offered to show mental condition/capacity to form mens rea, not to prove the literal truth of being Poseidon.","rule":"A defendant’s statements may be admitted for the nonhearsay purpose of showing the defendant’s mental condition and lack of capacity to form the requisite mens rea.","sub":"Relevance/Hearsay – Statements to show mental state","family":"Hearsay","unit":null},{"n":110,"summary":"During a driving test with a private driving school, a student driver with an instructor in the passenger seat is stopped first at a red light and begins to roll forward, striking a pedestrian in a crosswalk. The pedestrian sues the student and the driving school. At trial, the pedestrian tries to prove the light was still red, while the student claims it was green. A jogger on the sidewalk did not see the light but heard the student say to the instructor through an open window: “I shouldn’t have moved…that light was red.” The pedestrian offers the jogger’s testimony.","trigger":"Out-of-court statement by a party to the case offered against that party to prove what happened (light was red).","rule":"A statement offered against an opposing party that was made by that party is admissible as a party-opponent admission and is not excluded by the hearsay rule.","sub":"Hearsay – Party admissions","family":"801(d)(2)","unit":25},{"n":111,"summary":"A plumber sues a contractor for failure to pay. At trial, the plumber testifies from memory that he supervised workers laying tile pipes and installing fixtures, recalling the type and length of pipe, number of workers, and hours worked. The contractor objects because the plumber kept a book recording these details and the book still exists and is in the plumber’s possession.","trigger":"Opponent raises best-evidence objection merely because a writing exists that records the facts; witness is testifying from personal knowledge.","rule":"The best evidence rule applies only when proving the contents of a writing; a witness may testify from personal knowledge even if a written record of the facts exists.","sub":"Best Evidence Rule – When it applies","family":"Best Evidence Rule (1002)","unit":34},{"n":112,"summary":"An artist is charged with illegal possession of cocaine after being arrested in an undercover purchase. At trial, an officer who arrived after the arrest testifies only about how the field test was conducted to verify the substance was cocaine. On cross-examination, the defense wants to ask questions; the issue is which question is improper under these circumstances.","trigger":"Cross-examination limited to scope of direct and witness’s knowledge; witness testified only about field testing, not probable cause or the arrest/search.","rule":"Cross-examination is limited to the scope of direct and the witness’s testimony; questioning a field-test witness about probable cause for the search/arrest is improper when the witness did not testify about those events.","sub":"Witnesses – Scope of cross-examination","family":"Trial Architecture","unit":4},{"n":113,"summary":"Prosecutor seeks to prove a husband assaulted his wife in front of their child by calling the child’s pediatrician as an expert to testify the child has been drawing pictures of a man hitting a woman.","trigger":"Out-of-court drawings/behavior offered to prove the assault occurred; best evidentiary objection.","rule":"Out-of-court statements or conduct offered for their truth are hearsay unless an exception applies; a child’s drawings offered to prove the assault happened are generally inadmissible hearsay.","sub":"Hearsay – Nonverbal conduct / out-of-court statements","family":"Hearsay","unit":23},{"n":114,"summary":"Museum guard on trial for larceny claims he routinely leaves work at 4:30 p.m. daily to place bets at the race track. He offers this to prove he acted in conformity on the day paintings disappeared.","trigger":"Habit evidence vs character evidence; repeated specific conduct offered to show conduct on a particular occasion.","rule":"Evidence of a person’s habit—regular, specific, semi-automatic conduct—is admissible to prove the person acted in accordance with the habit on a particular occasion.","sub":"Character vs Habit – Rule 406","family":"Habit vs. Character (FRE 406)","unit":9},{"n":115,"summary":"Banker on trial for larceny offers co-worker testimony that he has access to cash and has never been accused of dishonesty with bank money. On rebuttal, prosecutor offers testimony that banker has a short temper and was aggressor in fights. Both objections overruled.","trigger":"Defendant opens the door to character evidence for a trait; prosecution’s rebuttal must match the same trait.","rule":"If a defendant introduces character evidence of a pertinent trait, the prosecution may rebut only with evidence about the same trait; rebuttal about unrelated traits is improper.","sub":"Character Evidence – Defendant opens door; rebuttal limits","family":"Character","unit":5},{"n":116,"summary":"A defendant is on trial for theft of a used car that he took for a test drive and did not return; he was arrested in the car two days later. He testifies he had no intent to keep the car and only delayed returning it due to marital problems. He calls a witness to testify that during those two days the defendant said, “I’m going to return this car as soon as I work things out with my wife.”","trigger":"Out-of-court statement offered to show defendant’s then-existing intent to return the property (state-of-mind hearsay exception).","rule":"Then-existing intent/state of mind is an 803 exception to hearsay.","sub":"Hearsay – Then-existing state of mind (803(3))","family":"803(3)","unit":26},{"n":117,"summary":"Wrongful discharge suit; plaintiff calls supervisor as adverse witness; asks if supervisor knew plaintiff reported her theft before firing him.","trigger":"Evidence: impeachment and substantive relevance; showing bias and retaliatory motive.","rule":"A witness may be questioned to show bias and motive, and such evidence is admissible for impeachment and to prove the asserted improper motive when relevant.","sub":"Impeachment – Bias / Motive","family":"Impeachment","unit":14},{"n":118,"summary":"Defendant on trial for bribery; told FBI investigator he’d provide 'hot information' if investigator stopped worrying about 'little vacation'; prosecution offers investigator testimony.","trigger":"Hearsay analysis; statement by defendant offered by prosecution; party-opponent exclusion.","rule":"A party’s own statement offered against that party is not hearsay as an opposing-party statement (party admission).","sub":"Hearsay – Opposing Party Statement","family":"801(d)(2)","unit":25},{"n":119,"summary":"In murder trial of nurse, prosecutor offers to read pages from a medical treatise to prove substance is lethal, without first calling any expert witness.","trigger":"Learned treatise offered without expert foundation.","rule":"Statements from learned treatises are admissible (read into evidence) only through examination of a qualified expert and after establishing the treatise as a reliable authority.","sub":"Hearsay – Learned Treatise (Rule 803(18))","family":"803(18)","unit":36},{"n":120,"summary":"Home-insurance dispute: insurer’s engineer expert says pipe burst due to age; on cross, engineer admits prior tax fraud conviction; insurer calls witness to testify engineer is truthful and neighbors describe him as truthful.","trigger":"Witness impeached for dishonesty; party seeks rehabilitation with opinion/reputation for truthfulness.","rule":"After a witness is attacked for untruthfulness (e.g., crime of dishonesty), the proponent may rehabilitate with opinion or reputation evidence of the witness’s truthful character.","sub":"Witness Credibility – Rehabilitation (Truthfulness)","family":"Impeachment","unit":16},{"n":121,"summary":"Bank-robbery trial: eyewitness previously identified defendant in a mug book at the station but cannot identify him at trial; prosecutor offers officer testimony about the earlier ID.","trigger":"Witness recants/claims inability to ID at trial; prior out-of-court identification offered through another witness; declarant testifies and is subject to cross.","rule":"A prior identification is NOT hearsay if the declarant testifies and is subject to cross-examination about the statement. Under 801(d) certain prior statements of a tetifying witness are NOT HEARSAY (EXCLUDED). One of them is Prior Identification: Witness previously identified someone (lineup, photo array, etc.). Witness now testifies and can be cross-examined","sub":"Hearsay — Prior Identification (FRE 801(d)(1)(C))","family":"801(d)(1)","unit":24},{"n":122,"summary":"At drug-dealing trial, expert agent testifies that defendant’s statement about selling 'apples' was drug code; issue is whether expert opinion is admissible.","trigger":"Expert interprets coded language based on experience; no personal knowledge; helpfulness to jury.","rule":"Expert opinion is admissible if the witness is qualified and the specialized knowledge will assist the trier of fact; experts need not have personal knowledge of the case facts (Rule 702).","sub":"Expert Testimony — Rule 702 (specialized knowledge; no personal knowledge required)","family":"702/703/704","unit":37},{"n":123,"summary":"Defendant tried for bank robbery; co-robber/friend fled country (unavailable); friend told mother ‘I was involved in the robbery and don’t want them to catch me’; prosecution offers statement.","trigger":"Out-of-court confession by unavailable declarant; statement exposes declarant to criminal liability; corroborating circumstances; not testimonial.","rule":"An unavailable declarant’s statement against penal interest is admissible if supported by corroborating circumstances (FRE 804(b)(3)); non-testimonial statements to family don’t violate Confrontation.","sub":"Hearsay Exception — Statement Against Penal Interest (FRE 804(b)(3))","family":"804(b)(3)","unit":32},{"n":124,"summary":"Plaintiff sues government for car forced off road by military convoy; on cross, eyewitness asked if he belongs to religious group refusing to file federal tax returns because taxes fund weapons; issue: proper question.","trigger":"Religious beliefs offered to show bias against government/military rather than general credibility.","rule":"Religious beliefs generally aren’t admissible to attack/support credibility (FRE 610), but may be inquired into to show bias.","sub":"Impeachment — Bias / Religion (FRE 610)","family":"Impeachment","unit":14},{"n":125,"summary":"Manufacturer redesigns machine after injury; plaintiff offers redesign to rebut infeasibility defense.","trigger":"Subsequent remedial measure offered to show feasibility.","rule":"Subsequent remedial measures admissible to show feasibility when disputed (FRE 407).","sub":"Subsequent Remedial Measures — Feasibility","family":"Policy Exclusions","unit":11},{"n":126,"summary":"15 years of fisherman water‑level logs summarized into charts for trial.","trigger":"Voluminous business records summarized.","rule":"Summaries of voluminous business records are admissible if originals available (FRE 1006).","sub":"Voluminous Records — Summaries","family":"1006","unit":35},{"n":127,"summary":"Defense witness testified defendant said he had an alibi. Prosecutor sought to impeach by asking why witness failed to mention alibi earlier when questioned by police.","trigger":"Failure to mention material fact when earlier questioned.","rule":"Silence or omission when a statement would naturally be made qualifies as prior inconsistent statement for impeachment (FRE 613).","sub":"Impeachment — Prior Inconsistent Statements","family":"Impeachment/Hearsay","unit":21},{"n":128,"summary":"A defendant accountant was charged with fraud for allegedly helping a client file false income tax returns by shifting substantial medical expenses from one year to another. The defendant pleaded not guilty, claiming he made an honest mistake about the dates expenses were paid. At trial, the prosecutor offered evidence of the defendant’s involvement in an earlier scheme to help a different client falsify tax returns in the same way. The question asks whether that evidence is admissible.","trigger":"Prior bad act offered after defendant claims mistake.","rule":"Rule 404(b) bars propensity evidence but allows other-acts evidence for a proper purpose such as absence of mistake; when the defendant claims mistake, similar prior acts may be admitted to show absence of mistake (subject to Rule 403 and limiting instruction).","sub":"Character Evidence / 404(b) Other Acts / Absence of Mistake","family":"404(b)","unit":7},{"n":129,"summary":"A plaintiff sued a lawnmower manufacturer for strict products liability, alleging a design defect injured him when the mower blade flew off after striking a sprinkler head. At trial, the manufacturer called an expert witness it had retained for a fee to test identical mowers and, if his opinion was helpful, to testify. The expert testified he tested the mowers and, as designed and installed, the blade could not fly off as claimed. Assume the expert used a reliable method.","trigger":"Expert testimony admissibility (FRE 702/Daubert); bias goes to weight/impeachment, not admissibility; experts may testify to ultimate issues (FRE 704).","rule":"If qualified and using reliable methods, an expert’s opinion that is relevant and helpful is admissible even if paid by a party and even if it embraces an ultimate issue.","sub":"Expert Testimony – FRE 702/704 (Daubert; Ultimate Issue; Bias)","family":"702/703/704","unit":37},{"n":130,"summary":"A defendant was indicted for a fraudulent investment scheme. At the criminal trial, the prosecutor called a witness who participated in the scheme; the witness testified about how the scheme operated and was cross-examined by the defendant’s attorney, but the case ended in a mistrial. An allegedly defrauded investor then brought a civil action against the defendant and sought to introduce the witness’s testimony from the criminal trial. The witness had moved to a foreign country. The question asks whether the prior testimony is admissible in the civil action.","trigger":"Hearsay; declarant unavailable (abroad); former testimony exception (FRE 804(b)(1)) requires prior opportunity and similar motive to cross-examine.","rule":"Former testimony of an unavailable declarant is admissible if given at a prior proceeding and offered against a party who had an opportunity and similar motive to develop the testimony by cross-examination (FRE 804(b)(1)).","sub":"Hearsay Exceptions – Former Testimony (FRE 804(b)(1))","family":"804(b)(1)/806","unit":31},{"n":131,"summary":"Two or more parties jointly consult the same attorney about a common matter. Later, the parties become adverse in litigation against each other, and one seeks to assert attorney-client privilege to block disclosure of the joint communications.","trigger":"Joint-client communications with one attorney; later dispute between joint clients.","rule":"If two or more parties consult with an attorney jointly, the attorney-client privilege will not apply in a lawsuit between the parties.","sub":"Privileges — Joint-Client Attorney-Client Privilege","family":"Privileges","unit":39},{"n":132,"summary":"In a criminal trial, the defendant wants to introduce character evidence to show conduct in conformity with a good character trait. The question tests the proper form of character proof on direct examination (opinion or reputation, not specific instances) and that it is circumstantial evidence.","trigger":"Criminal defendant offers character evidence; method of proof (opinion/reputation only).","rule":"In a criminal case, a witness for the defendant may use character evidence of the defendant as circumstantial evidence, but must use opinion or reputation, not specific instances.","sub":"Character Evidence — Defendant’s Character (Opinion/Reputation)","family":"Character","unit":5},{"n":133,"summary":"A statement was made out of court and is offered at trial. The issue is whether the statement is hearsay, focusing on whether it is offered for the truth of the matter asserted or instead for another relevant, non-hearsay purpose.","trigger":"Out-of-court statement offered for a non-truth purpose (not hearsay).","rule":"An out of court statement will not be hearsay if it is not offered for the truth of the matter asserted. Remember always when you have an out of court statement do the hearsay prayer. Here, victim testified that one kidnapper refer to the other as “SPEED”. Is this an out of court statement? Yes, is it offered for its truth? No, it’s offered to prove that he was called in a specific way. Doesn’t have any relation to kidnapping. It could be a prior ID. Under 403 we could use the balancing test and this evidence comes in unless the probative value substantially outweighs by the prejudice. This is a rule of inclusion.Let’s suppose we say it is offered for to prove the truth of the matter asserted. Then we would have to look for an exclusion or exception. Under exclusion maybe prior of ID. But, Jhon prefers not hearsay","sub":"Hearsay — Not Offered for Truth","family":"Hearsay","unit":null},{"n":134,"summary":"The plaintiff sued an industrial facility for health injuries caused by air pollution. At trial, she was asked about the days she observed heavy dust and how long it lasted. She testified that she could not remember the specific dates, but explained that she had kept a diary where she accurately recorded this information every day.\n\nHer attorney first attempted to refresh her recollection using the diary, but refreshing failed—she still could not remember.\n\nThis triggers Past Recollection Recorded, not refreshed recollection.","trigger":"Past recollection recorded after refreshing recollection fails; read to jury.","rule":"When refreshing recollection doesn’t help, past recollection is allowed (if elements are met), however the evidence will be read to the jury. Upon motion by the opposing party, the exhibit can be brought in rather than just read. Step-by-step: - She once had personal knowledge (she personally observed the dust). - She made the diary herself, contemporaneously (daily entries while events were fresh). - The diary accurately reflects her knowledge. - She now cannot recall well enough to testify, even after refresh. All FRE 803(5) requirements are satisfied. Important procedural detail: The diary itself does NOT go to the jury as an exhibit (unless the defense requests it). Instead, the plaintiff may READ the diary into evidence. AN ELEMENT TO THE HEARSAY EXCEPTION PAST RECOLECTION RECORDED IS THAT THE WITNESS CANT REFRESH HER RECOLECTION WHEN THE ATTORNEY PRIVATELY SHOWS THE DOCUMENT If memory IS refreshed. Then this becomes REFRESHED recollection, not past recollection recorded. Witness testifies from present memory. Document does NOT come in. Done.","sub":"Evidence — Past Recollection Recorded","family":"612/803(5)","unit":30},{"n":135,"summary":"A defendant is charged with murder after allegedly shooting a victim during an argument in a crowded nightclub. At trial, the defendant claims self-defense, testifying that he shot the victim only after the victim lunged at him with a knife. In rebuttal, the prosecutor calls an eyewitness to testify that she observed the argument from a short distance away and that she heard a gunshot before the victim lunged at the defendant.","trigger":"Eyewitness perception challenged (alcohol/noise)","rule":"Extrinsic evidence may impeach a witness’s ability to perceive events. REMEMBER YOU CAN BRING EXTRINSIC EVIDENCE POR IMPEACHMENT","sub":"Impeachment perception","family":"Impeachment","unit":15},{"n":136,"summary":"A defendant is on trial as one of the armed robbers of a liquor store. The defendant testifies that he robbed the store involuntarily because his fellow gang members threatened to kill him right then if he backed out, as he wanted to do. Thereafter, the defense seeks to call a witness who would testify that six months after the defendant was arrested in connection with the robbery, the defendant told him that he had been threatened when he wanted to pull out of the robbery.","trigger":"Defendant offers own prior statement","rule":"Prior consistent statements are admissible only to rebut fabrication. GABY PORFAVOR RECUERDA APPLICAR A CADA MBE Q CUANDO TE PREGUNTEN HEARSAY HACER EL HEARSAY PRAYER DE JHON: iS THIS AN OUT OF COURT STATEMENT? IS IT OFFERED TO PROVE THE TRUTY OF THE MATTER ASSERTE? IF ANSWER TO BOTH IS YES IT'S HEARSAY. THEN LOOK FOR AN EXCLUSION OR EXCEPTION. Here, the out of court statement is a witness that will take the stand and say that \"the defendant told him he was threatened\". This is offered for its truth, but doesn't fit and exception or exclusion. Hearsay because: - Not party admission (wrong side) - Prior consistent? only if attacked for fabrication + timing works. A prior consistent statement is admissible only if: - the declarant testifies and is subject to cross (yes), and - it’s offered to rebut a charge that the declarant recently fabricated or acted from an improper motive/influence, and - (modern approach) it was made before the alleged motive to fabricate arose. Otherwise = bolstering → excluded. Exclusion then existing state of mind: NO, has to be a present event \"I'm going to the beach\" not a past event like in this case","sub":"Prior consistent stmt","family":"801(d)(1)","unit":24},{"n":137,"summary":"A plaintiff has sued a defendant in federal court, alleging that the defendant knowingly pursued the plaintiff for payment of debts in violation of an automatic bankruptcy stay. The defendant has moved for summary judgment, arguing that he had no notice of the plaintiff’s bankruptcy. In opposing the defendant’s motion for summary judgment, the plaintiff offers a copy of the local newspaper that contains a front-page story about the plaintiff’s petition for bankruptcy, along with evidence that the defendant is a daily subscriber to the newspaper.","trigger":"Newspaper offered to show notice","rule":"Statements offered to show notice are not hearsay.","sub":"Nonhearsay notice","family":"Hearsay","unit":null}]},{"slug":"conlaw","name":"Constitutional Law","bigPicture":"WHERE YOU START. You were trained on a constitution that is a catalogue of ends and guarantees: art. 2 declares the values of the State, art. 21 commands equality, arts. 83 and 102 oblige the State to deliver health and education. Rights bind everyone — the State, an employer, a neighbour — and any of them is enforced through one action, the amparo. Competence is allocated by the text and is rarely the fight; when a measure burdens a right, the court weighs the burden against the public purpose. One constitution, one catalogue of rights, one method: proportionality.\n\nWHAT THE U.S. SYSTEM IS. The U.S. Constitution is not a catalogue of ends. It is an instrument that distributes power and then withholds it. The federal government holds only the powers listed in Article I, so the first problem for any federal statute is authority, not merit. Its rights provisions restrain government and no one else, and they restrain interference without commanding assistance. And in place of one weighing test, courts SORT each case into a track — by the class affected, the right at stake, which sovereign acted, which forum the speech occurred in, and which clause is invoked — and the track fixes who carries the burden of proof, which usually fixes the result.\n\nWHAT DIVERGES. Three trained habits generate the wrong answer here. (1) Asking whether the law is beneficial before asking which enumerated power supports it. (2) Suing a private party, or demanding that the State fund a protected right, on a constitutional theory that has no defendant and no basis — there is no amparo against the neighbour, and no right to be paid for. (3) Reaching for proportionality where the question is really one of sorting: rational basis upholds nearly everything, strict scrutiny defeats nearly everything, and the case is effectively decided the moment the tier is chosen. Add one federal feature your system has no reason to expect: supremacy operates on PEOPLE, so Congress may regulate an entire national market and still be forbidden to order a State to legislate.\n\nWHAT IS THE SAME. More than you would guess, and this is what to fix first because it costs you nothing: criminal law may not be applied retroactively; expropriation requires compensation, and the compensation reaches every affected interest; legislators are absolutely immune for what they say in session; a person must be heard before the State withdraws a benefit already granted; and minors may not receive perpetual sentences. Where those instincts fire, trust them — and spend the attention they free on the twenty-three units that describe institutions your training never had to build.","families":[{"name":"First Amendment","count":22},{"name":"Federalism","count":14},{"name":"Equal Protection","count":10},{"name":"Separation of Powers","count":9},{"name":"Congressional Power","count":8},{"name":"Takings","count":8},{"name":"Dormant Commerce Clause","count":5},{"name":"Equal Protection / Substantive Due Process","count":5},{"name":"Eleventh Amendment","count":4},{"name":"Establishment Clause","count":4},{"name":"Justiciability","count":4},{"name":"Right to Travel","count":4},{"name":"Article I § 10","count":3},{"name":"Judicial Power","count":3},{"name":"Procedural Due Process","count":3},{"name":"State Action Doctrine","count":3},{"name":"Federal Supremacy","count":2},{"name":"Routing the Claim","count":2},{"name":"Sixth Amendment","count":2},{"name":"Substantive Due Process","count":2},{"name":"Eighth Amendment","count":1},{"name":"Fair Housing","count":1},{"name":"Fourteenth Amendment","count":1},{"name":"Fourth Amendment","count":1},{"name":"Supreme Court Review","count":1}],"units":[{"n":1,"summary":"Congress acts for the public good — grants to small businesses, a research-funding commission, an excise tax on computers funding free software, a discriminatory soda tax — and the challenge is that no enumerated power authorizes it.","trigger":"Federal statute + identify the enumerated power + distinguish REGULATING for the general welfare (no such power) from TAXING and SPENDING for it (Art. I § 8 cl. 1) + conditional-spending limits.","rule":"The federal government has no general police power: every federal statute must rest on an enumerated power, and the General Welfare Clause is a limit on the SPENDING power, not an independent regulatory authority. Congress may tax and spend for the general welfare, and courts uphold an appropriation if Congress could rationally conclude it serves the general welfare (Helvering v. Davis). A federal tax is valid so long as it raises revenue, even if its purpose or effect is to discourage conduct (Sonzinsky), though an exaction functioning as a penalty is not saved by the label (NFIB v. Sebelius). Conditions on federal funds must be unambiguous, related to the federal interest, not coercive, and not require unconstitutional action (South Dakota v. Dole; NFIB). Over the District of Columbia, by contrast, Congress holds exclusive legislative authority — including the general police power it lacks in the States (Art. I § 8 cl. 17).","sub":"Congressional Power – Enumerated Powers; Taxing and Spending (Master)","sensitivity":"The decisive move is naming the power BEFORE testing the means: 'promotes the general welfare' answers a spending question and fails a regulatory one. Three switches follow: revenue-production validates a tax whatever its motive; Dole gates conditional spending; and the same Congress wields a general police power inside D.C. and none inside the States.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the national legislature may legislate for the common good — that is what a legislature is” → in the U.S. system the enumerated-power question comes first and can defeat a law no one disputes is beneficial. The Venezuelan Asamblea Nacional exercises plenary national competence (Const. arts. 187, 156) with no counterpart inquiry, so the trained reflex skips the threshold step entirely — and then mis-sorts the spending/regulating boundary, because a code-based system never had to separate the power to fund from the power to command.","authority":"Enumerated powers; taxing and spending: Art. I § 8 cls. 1, 17; Helvering v. Davis; Sonzinsky v. United States; South Dakota v. Dole; NFIB v. Sebelius (2012)."},{"n":2,"summary":"Congress criminalizes online gaming fraud; Congress caps gasoline prices nationwide — each defended as a regulation of interstate commerce.","trigger":"Federal statute regulating economic activity + one of the three Lopez categories: channels of interstate commerce, instrumentalities/persons or things in it, or activities substantially affecting it (aggregation for economic activity) + no compelled activity (NFIB).","rule":"Under the Commerce Clause Congress may regulate (1) the channels of interstate commerce, (2) the instrumentalities of interstate commerce and persons or things moving in it, and (3) activities that substantially affect interstate commerce — and may attach criminal penalties to any of them. For ECONOMIC activity, the effects of many actors are aggregated (Wickard; Raich), so an intrastate transaction inside a national market qualifies. Non-economic activity requires a closer nexus (Lopez; Morrison), and Congress may not compel individuals to ENTER commerce (NFIB v. Sebelius). Nationwide price regulation of a good moving in interstate commerce, and criminalization of internet commercial fraud, sit inside categories one and three.","sub":"Congressional Power – Commerce Clause (Master)","sensitivity":"Category selection does the work, and the economic/non-economic line controls whether aggregation is available. A validly enacted commerce regulation then binds the States through the Supremacy Clause — but only if it regulates the market rather than commanding the State to legislate (Unit 3).","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “economic regulation is inherently national — the State legislates, and states of the federation follow” → U.S. commerce power is broad but categorical, and its breadth is precisely what the anti-commandeering and enumerated-power limits fence in. In Venezuela's federal-in-name system the national government holds direct competence over economy, commerce, and criminal law (Const. art. 156), so the civilian never learns to ask WHICH commerce category a statute occupies — nor that the answer can defeat the statute.","authority":"Commerce Clause: Art. I § 8 cl. 3; United States v. Lopez; Gonzales v. Raich; Wickard v. Filburn; United States v. Morrison; NFIB v. Sebelius."},{"n":3,"summary":"Congress orders States to repeal mechanic licensing, to enact citizenship regulations, to ban self-service gas pumps, to administer a water-pollution program — versus Congress imposing drug-testing duties on private AND state employers alike.","trigger":"Federal statute directed at the State AS SOVEREIGN (compelling it to enact, administer, or refrain from enacting law) → unconstitutional commandeering; federal statute imposing the same duties on private and public actors in a market → valid generally applicable regulation.","rule":"Congress may not compel a State to enact, administer, or enforce a federal regulatory program (New York v. United States; Printz v. United States), and the bar equally forbids commanding a State NOT to legislate (Murphy v. NCAA); the duration of the command is irrelevant. But a federal law that regulates States and private parties alike — imposing the same substantive duties on both — is a generally applicable regulation, not commandeering (Garcia; Reno v. Condon), because the private actors' political recourse protects the process. Congress's lawful alternatives are direct federal regulation that PREEMPTS state law, and conditional spending under Dole.","sub":"Federalism – Anti-Commandeering vs. Generally Applicable Laws (Master)","sensitivity":"Two questions in fixed order: (1) does Congress have the power at all; (2) is it commanding the STATE'S lawmaking or enforcement machinery? Only the second is commandeering — so 'regulates the State along with everybody else' saves the statute, while 'requires the State to pass a law' destroys it, however valid the underlying subject.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the national law is superior — states must implement it; that is what supremacy means” → U.S. supremacy operates on PEOPLE, not on state legislatures: a federal command to the States is not supreme, it is void. Venezuelan constitutional structure expressly assigns states the execution of national policy (Const. arts. 4, 164) and treats coordination as hierarchy, so the civilian reads the anti-commandeering rule as a denial of federal supremacy rather than as its structural precondition — the single deepest misalignment in the federalism material.","authority":"Anti-commandeering: New York v. United States; Printz v. United States; Murphy v. NCAA (2018); Garcia v. San Antonio MTA; Reno v. Condon."},{"n":4,"summary":"A State sets a stricter trans-fat cap than Congress; a State imposes a minimum fuel-efficiency standard where federal law requires only labeling; a State caps gas prices below the federal ceiling — and each side invokes the Supremacy Clause.","trigger":"Valid federal statute + state law in the same field + no express preemption clause → test conflict preemption (impossibility or obstacle to federal objectives) and field preemption (congressional intent to occupy) — and note that the Supremacy Clause confers no state power.","rule":"A valid federal law preempts conflicting state law (Art. VI). Absent an express preemption clause, preemption is either CONFLICT preemption — compliance with both is impossible, or the state law obstructs the federal purpose — or FIELD preemption, where the scheme's comprehensiveness shows congressional intent to occupy the area. A stricter state standard coexists with a federal FLOOR (a labeling requirement does not displace a substantive state standard) but is preempted where the federal rule sets a uniform CEILING or the field is occupied. Critically, the Supremacy Clause is not itself a source of state regulatory authority: a State defends its law through its police powers plus the absence of preemption, never by claiming the law is 'valid pursuant to the Supremacy Clause.'","sub":"Federalism – Preemption; the Supremacy Clause Is Not a Grant of State Power","sensitivity":"Sequence: (1) is the federal law valid (Units 2–3 — an invalid law preempts nothing); (2) express, conflict, or field; (3) floor or ceiling. The tested trap is the mislabeled defense — a State invoking the Supremacy Clause as its own authority argues against itself.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the higher norm displaces the lower — jerarquía normativa settles it” → U.S. preemption is not a hierarchy of norms but an inquiry into congressional INTENT and the possibility of coexistence, in which a stricter state rule often survives a valid federal one. The civilian pyramid (Const. art. 7; supremacía constitucional) has no category for two valid rules of different sovereigns operating simultaneously on the same conduct — so the reflex resolves by rank where U.S. law resolves by purpose.","authority":"Preemption: Art. VI cl. 2; conflict (impossibility/obstacle) and field preemption; the Supremacy Clause is not a source of state power."},{"n":5,"summary":"A State taxes IRS property; a State imposes a gross-receipts tax on a federal agency's sales; a State applies its ordinary sales tax to a private antiques dealer who resells to the National Park Service.","trigger":"State tax + federal entity or federal contractor as the economic burden-bearer → immunity if the legal incidence falls ON the United States or its instrumentalities, or if the tax discriminates against the federal government or those who deal with it; otherwise valid.","rule":"A State may not tax the federal government or its instrumentalities (McCulloch v. Maryland), nor may it impose a tax that discriminates against the federal government or those with whom it deals. But a nondiscriminatory state tax whose legal incidence falls on a PRIVATE party — including a contractor who sells to or purchases for the United States — is valid, even though the economic burden is ultimately passed to the federal government (James v. Dravo Contracting; United States v. New Mexico). The mirror principle limits federal taxation of the States, which may not target their integral governmental functions.","sub":"Federalism – Intergovernmental Tax Immunity","sensitivity":"The switch is LEGAL INCIDENCE, not economic burden: the same dollar is immune when the statute taxes the United States and taxable when it taxes the dealer. Discrimination against federal dealings is an independent, separate ground of invalidity.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the State cannot tax anything that ends up costing the national treasury” → U.S. doctrine draws the line at who bears the LEGAL incidence, tolerating full economic pass-through. Venezuelan public finance resolves such conflicts by express constitutional allocation of tax competences and prohibitions (Const. arts. 156(12), 183), not by a judge-made immunity keyed to statutory incidence — so the civilian instinct over-immunizes and misses the discrimination prong entirely.","authority":"Intergovernmental tax immunity: McCulloch v. Maryland; James v. Dravo Contracting; United States v. New Mexico."},{"n":6,"summary":"A town bars hiring citizens of an 'aggressor' foreign country into police, fire, and EMT positions, in response to an international conflict.","trigger":"State or local measure keyed to foreign nationality, foreign policy, or an international dispute → foreign-affairs preemption, plus independent equal protection review of the alienage classification.","rule":"State and local laws that intrude into foreign affairs — or that conflict with federal policy or an exercise of the exclusive federal power over foreign relations — are preempted even absent a directly conflicting federal statute (Zschernig v. Miller; Crosby v. National Foreign Trade Council; American Insurance Ass'n v. Garamendi). Such measures are independently vulnerable on equal protection grounds: STATE alienage classifications are ordinarily suspect and subject to strict scrutiny, and the political-function exception (Unit 23) reaches core self-government offices but not ordinary public employment such as emergency medical service.","sub":"Federalism – Foreign-Affairs Preemption","sensitivity":"Two independent grounds converge, and the exam rewards naming both: structural (foreign affairs is exclusively federal, so the local rule is preempted without any conflicting statute) and individual (suspect alienage classification failing strict scrutiny).","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: foreign relations belong exclusively to the national government — broadly RIGHT; Const. art. 236(4) vests foreign policy in the President and the civilian instinct correctly denies subnational competence. The calibration friction: in the U.S. the preemption operates through judicial review of a locality's ordinance in an actual case, and it coexists with a SEPARATE individual-rights claim — the civilian frames the defect purely as a competence violation and never reaches the equal protection analysis the same facts also generate.","authority":"Foreign-affairs preemption: Zschernig v. Miller; Crosby v. National Foreign Trade Council; American Insurance Ass'n v. Garamendi."},{"n":7,"summary":"Congress makes it a federal crime for state officials to conspire with each other or with private persons to deny equal access to state facilities, and authorizes damages for the victims.","trigger":"Federal statute enforcing the Fourteenth Amendment + state officials as essential participants → valid § 5 legislation; purely private conduct or a remedy exceeding the constitutional violation → outside § 5.","rule":"Section 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment empowers Congress to enact appropriate legislation enforcing the Amendment's guarantees, including statutes reaching conspiracies in which STATE officials participate to deprive persons of equal protection. The power has two limits: it does not authorize regulation of purely private conduct (United States v. Morrison; the Civil Rights Cases), and remedial legislation must be congruent and proportional to the constitutional violation Congress documented — Congress may not redefine the right itself (City of Boerne v. Flores). Section 5 legislation may also abrogate state sovereign immunity when Congress says so unmistakably (Unit 10).","sub":"Fourteenth Amendment – Section 5 Enforcement Power","sensitivity":"One structural fact decides validity: whether STATE action is an element of the prohibited conduct. Present → § 5 supports the statute; absent → Congress must find another power (commerce, spending) or fail. Congruence-and-proportionality then screens the remedy's breadth.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the constitution guarantees equality, so the legislature may protect it against anyone who violates it” → U.S. constitutional guarantees run against GOVERNMENT, and the enforcement power inherits that limit: Congress cannot use § 5 to reach a private discriminator. Venezuelan constitutional rights bind private parties directly and the legislature enforces them generally (Const. art. 21; amparo against private conduct), so the state-action-shaped enforcement power has no analogue and the civilian systematically over-reads congressional authority.","authority":"Section 5 enforcement power: U.S. Const. amend. XIV § 5; City of Boerne v. Flores; United States v. Morrison; the Civil Rights Cases."},{"n":8,"summary":"Congress forbids federal courts to order busing remedies; Congress tries to expand the Supreme Court's original jurisdiction; Congress removes its jurisdiction over cases affecting ambassadors.","trigger":"Statute altering federal judicial power → distinguish lower federal courts (Congress creates and may limit), Supreme Court APPELLATE jurisdiction (Exceptions Clause), and Supreme Court ORIGINAL jurisdiction (fixed by Art. III, unalterable).","rule":"Congress creates the lower federal courts and may define and restrict their jurisdiction, including remedial jurisdiction (Art. III § 1), and may make exceptions to the Supreme Court's APPELLATE jurisdiction (Art. III § 2, Exceptions Clause; Ex parte McCardle). It may NOT enlarge or contract the Supreme Court's ORIGINAL jurisdiction, which the Constitution fixes for cases affecting ambassadors, other public ministers and consuls, and those in which a State is a party (Marbury v. Madison). Two external limits persist: Congress may not direct the outcome of pending cases or prescribe a rule of decision for them (United States v. Klein), and jurisdictional limits may not themselves violate other constitutional provisions.","sub":"Judicial Power – Congressional Control of Jurisdiction; Original vs. Appellate","sensitivity":"Three tiers with three different answers, and the exam picks by which court and which kind of jurisdiction is at issue. The original/appellate line is absolute and textual; the appellate power is broad but bounded by Klein; the lower courts are almost entirely at Congress's disposal.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “jurisdiction and procedure are for the legislature to organize — the code of civil procedure allocates competence” → in the U.S. some jurisdiction is CONSTITUTIONALLY fixed and immune to legislative adjustment, and the very existence of the lower federal courts is a legislative choice. The civilian schema (judicial organization by organic law; competencia as a statutory matter, CPC arts. 28 ff.) supplies neither the constitutional floor nor the Klein-style prohibition on legislatively dictating outcomes.","authority":"Judicial power and jurisdiction: Art. III §§ 1–2; Marbury v. Madison; Ex parte McCardle; United States v. Klein."},{"n":9,"summary":"A state supreme court upholds a statute by construing it to avoid conflict with federal law, saying nothing about independent state grounds; another rests on state constitutional grounds but reasons entirely from a federal appellate decision. Both losers seek certiorari.","trigger":"State-court judgment + both state and federal grounds available → review barred if the state ground is ADEQUATE and INDEPENDENT; where the opinion fairly appears to rest on federal law or is unclear and no plain statement appears, review is available (Michigan v. Long).","rule":"The Supreme Court will not review a state-court judgment resting on an adequate and independent state-law ground, because reversal of the federal ruling could not change the outcome. But under Michigan v. Long, where a state decision fairly appears to rest primarily on federal law, or is interwoven with it, and the state court has not made a PLAIN STATEMENT that its holding rests on independent state grounds, the Supreme Court presumes federal law controlled and takes jurisdiction. The burden of clarity is on the state court; construing a state statute merely to harmonize it with federal law is not such a statement.","sub":"Supreme Court Review – Adequate and Independent State Grounds; Michigan v. Long","sensitivity":"The doctrine turns on a drafting act by the state court, not on the substance of the rights: an explicit 'we rely solely on our state constitution' ends the case, while silence or federal-law reasoning opens it. State courts may grant MORE protection under state law — that is the structural point the presumption protects.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “a supreme court reviews any final judgment of a lower court — casación corrects errors of law wherever they appear” → the U.S. Supreme Court is not a general court of last resort for state litigation: it reviews only the FEDERAL question, and a state court can insulate its judgment by saying so. A unified single-hierarchy system (TSJ atop all courts) has no space for a coordinate sovereign's high court whose own law can foreclose national review.","authority":"Adequate and independent state grounds: Michigan v. Long; Herb v. Pitcairn."},{"n":10,"summary":"A business sues a city for damages over a labeling ordinance; the United States sues a State over federally funded programs; a homeowner sues a city over a sign ban; a student sues a public school district — and each defendant claims sovereign immunity.","trigger":"Private damages suit in federal court against a State or an arm of the State → barred; defendant is a municipality, county, or school district → not barred; plaintiff is the United States or another State → not barred; suit seeks prospective relief against a state officer → not barred (Ex parte Young).","rule":"The Eleventh Amendment (and the broader principle of state sovereign immunity) bars private suits against a STATE or an arm of the State in federal court, whether for damages or equitable relief. It does NOT cover political subdivisions — cities, counties, and local school districts are municipal corporations, freely suable in federal court (Mt. Healthy City School District v. Doyle) and liable under § 1983 for their own policy or custom (Monell), unlike a State, which is not a 'person' under § 1983 (Will). The immunity also does not bar suits by the United States or by another State, suits for prospective injunctive relief against a state OFFICER (Ex parte Young), suits where the State consented, or suits where Congress validly abrogated immunity under § 5.","sub":"Eleventh Amendment – Scope and Exceptions (Master)","sensitivity":"Defendant classification decides everything before any merits analysis: State or arm of the State → immune; local government → not; officer sued prospectively → not. Note the deliberate trap that state-actor status and immunity are DIFFERENT questions — a school district is a state actor for Fourteenth Amendment purposes and simultaneously amenable to suit.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the State answers for its own acts — responsabilidad patrimonial del Estado is a constitutional guarantee (Const. art. 140)” → U.S. law starts from the opposite premise: the sovereign is immune from private suit in federal court unless an exception applies, and the plaintiff must engineer around the immunity (sue the officer, sue the city, find abrogation). The civilian schema of direct, integral state liability supplies no reason to distinguish a State from its municipality, or a damages claim from a prospective injunction — exactly the distinctions that decide these questions.","authority":"Eleventh Amendment: Hans v. Louisiana; Ex parte Young; Mt. Healthy City Sch. Dist. v. Doyle; Monell v. Dep't of Social Services; Will v. Michigan Dep't of State Police."},{"n":11,"summary":"A losing bidder seeks only an injunction after the contract work is finished; a city repeals the challenged ordinance mid-litigation; a community sues before any landfill site is chosen; a nightclub sues in case lobbying against its permit succeeds.","trigger":"Article III case-or-controversy screen: injury already redressed or ended (mootness, with its exceptions), injury contingent or not yet inflicted (ripeness), or request for a legal opinion without a live dispute (advisory-opinion bar).","rule":"Federal courts decide only live cases and controversies. A case is MOOT if the controversy is resolved before decision — but the exceptions matter: voluntary cessation by the defendant does not moot unless it is absolutely clear the conduct cannot reasonably be expected to recur (Friends of the Earth v. Laidlaw), with legislative repeal by a government defendant given greater weight; disputes capable of repetition yet evading review survive (Roe v. Wade); and a certified class survives the named plaintiff's expired claim. A claim is UNRIPE where the threatened harm is speculative or contingent on future events, and federal courts may never issue advisory opinions.","sub":"Justiciability – Mootness, Ripeness, Advisory Opinions (Master)","sensitivity":"The two doctrines are timing mirrors — too late and too early — and both are measured at the moment of DECISION, not filing. The tested subtlety is that mootness has escape hatches (and the party asserting it bears the burden) while ripeness has none: a contingent injury simply is not yet a case.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the court should settle the legal question — legal certainty serves everyone” → U.S. federal courts are structurally forbidden to answer questions unattached to a live injury, and will dismiss a fully briefed constitutional challenge because the harm ended or has not begun. Civilian systems accommodate abstract constitutional review (acción de nulidad por inconstitucionalidad before the Sala Constitucional; recurso de interpretación), so the trained reflex treats justiciability as a technicality rather than as the jurisdictional precondition it is.","authority":"Justiciability: Art. III case-or-controversy; Friends of the Earth v. Laidlaw; Roe v. Wade (capable of repetition); DeFunis v. Odegaard; advisory-opinion bar."},{"n":12,"summary":"A state judge issues a writ of habeas corpus ordering the release of a state prosecutor held in federal custody on a federal warrant.","trigger":"State court process directed at federal custody, federal officers, or the enforcement of federal law → barred; state courts adjudicating federal claims within their ordinary jurisdiction → required.","rule":"A state court has no power to issue a writ of habeas corpus to release a prisoner held in federal custody, nor otherwise to enjoin or interfere with federal officers executing federal law (Tarble's Case; Ableman v. Booth); such interference violates the Supremacy Clause. The structural relationship is asymmetric: state courts may not control federal custody, yet they MUST hear and enforce federal claims falling within their ordinary jurisdiction and may not discriminate against federal causes of action (Testa v. Katt).","sub":"Federal Supremacy – Limits on State Courts (Habeas and Tarble's Case)","sensitivity":"The asymmetry IS the doctrine: obligation running one way (enforce federal law), prohibition running the other (no control over federal custody or officers). A question that frames the state court as merely 'protecting liberty' tests whether the candidate sees the structural bar.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “any competent judge may grant hábeas corpus — it is a universal guarantee against unlawful detention (Const. art. 27)” → in the U.S. the writ is channeled by SOVEREIGN: federal detention answers only to federal courts. A unified judiciary in which every judge exercises the same constitutional protective jurisdiction has no concept of a detention that is legally beyond the reach of an otherwise competent court — making this among the most counterintuitive federalism rules for a civilian.","authority":"Limits on state courts: Tarble's Case; Ableman v. Booth; Testa v. Katt."},{"n":13,"summary":"Congress creates a commission with rulemaking and prosecution powers whose members are chosen by the Speaker and the Senate President pro tempore; elsewhere a congressionally appointed chairman is given power to appoint eight sub-chairs.","trigger":"Federal officer exercising significant authority + classify as principal or inferior + identify the appointing authority → principal: President with Senate advice and consent; inferior: Congress may vest in the President alone, courts of law, or heads of departments — never in Congress or its own officers.","rule":"All principal officers of the United States must be appointed by the President with the advice and consent of the Senate (Art. II § 2 cl. 2). For INFERIOR officers, Congress may by law vest appointment in the President alone, in the courts of law, or in the heads of departments — and in no one else: Congress may not appoint executive officers itself or through its own leadership (Buckley v. Valeo), and a defective appointment structure can invalidate the agency's exercise of authority. Congress may, however, delegate rulemaking authority to an agency so long as it supplies an intelligible principle to guide the exercise.","sub":"Separation of Powers – Appointments Clause","sensitivity":"Two sequential switches: principal-vs-inferior (does the officer report to and is removable by a superior other than the President?), then the closed list of permissible appointors. 'Congress appointed them' is dispositive — no further analysis needed. Nondelegation is a separate and far weaker constraint.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the legislature creates the body, so it names its members — that is ordinary institutional design” → in the U.S. the power to CREATE an office is sharply separated from the power to FILL it, and legislative appointment of executive officers is per se unconstitutional. The Venezuelan Asamblea Nacional designates the holders of major state organs (Const. arts. 187(14), 279 — magistrates, Contralor, Defensor), so the civilian reflex treats legislative appointment as normal rather than as the paradigm separation-of-powers violation.","authority":"Appointments Clause: Art. II § 2 cl. 2; Buckley v. Valeo; Edmond v. United States (principal vs. inferior); nondelegation/intelligible principle."},{"n":14,"summary":"The President orders $2 million paid to a State contrary to Congress's allocation scheme; the President reduces by regulation the agency operating hours Congress fixed by statute.","trigger":"Executive order or unilateral presidential action → locate it in the Youngstown categories: express or implied congressional authorization (maximum), congressional silence (twilight zone), or action contrary to Congress's expressed will (lowest ebb).","rule":"Presidential authority to act unilaterally is measured by its relation to congressional will (Youngstown Sheet & Tube v. Sawyer, Jackson, J., concurring): (1) acting pursuant to express or implied congressional authorization, the President's power is at its maximum and carries the strongest presumption of validity; (2) acting in the absence of congressional action, he operates in a zone of twilight where authority is uncertain and congressional acquiescence may matter; (3) acting against Congress's expressed will, his power is at its lowest ebb and can be sustained only by an exclusive constitutional power of his own. An executive order altering a statutory allocation or a statutory schedule falls in category three and fails.","sub":"Separation of Powers – Executive Power; the Youngstown Framework","sensitivity":"The analysis never asks whether the action was wise or urgent — only where it sits relative to Congress. Reading the statute first is therefore the whole technique: a statute that speaks moves the case to category one or three, and only genuine silence produces the hard middle.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the President holds inherent executive authority and issues decrees — the reglamento is a normal instrument of government” → U.S. executive orders carry no independent legislative force: their validity is parasitic on congressional authorization. The Venezuelan schema of decretos and even habilitating laws (Const. arts. 236(10), 203) makes autonomous executive norm-creation ordinary, so the civilian imports a presumption of validity precisely where U.S. law imposes its harshest scrutiny.","authority":"Executive power: Youngstown Sheet & Tube v. Sawyer (Jackson, J., concurring); Dames & Moore v. Regan; Train v. City of New York."},{"n":15,"summary":"Congress delegates spending authority but reserves a veto over its exercise; a statute funds a monument but forbids spending until both Houses approve the plans by concurrent resolution never presented to the President.","trigger":"Congress retains control over the execution of delegated authority through a device that alters legal rights and duties without satisfying bicameralism AND presentment → unconstitutional legislative veto.","rule":"Once Congress delegates authority, it may not reserve a veto over its exercise. Any congressional action with the purpose and effect of altering the legal rights, duties, and relations of persons outside the legislative branch is legislative in character and must satisfy BOTH bicameralism (passage by both Houses) and presentment to the President (INS v. Chadha). A one-House veto, a two-House concurrent resolution not presented, or a committee approval requirement therefore all fail; Congress's legitimate levers are new legislation, appropriations, and oversight. Congress likewise may not reserve for itself the removal of an officer performing executive functions (Bowsher v. Synar).","sub":"Separation of Powers – Legislative Veto; Bicameralism and Presentment","sensitivity":"One mechanical test resolves every variant: did the congressional action go through both Houses AND to the President? If it altered outside legal rights without doing so, it is void regardless of how sensible the oversight or how explicit the reservation in the original delegation.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the legislature that granted the power may condition and supervise its use — control político is a legislative function” → U.S. law converts ongoing legislative supervision of execution into a formal defect: oversight is permitted, but binding approval is legislation and must be enacted as such. Venezuelan constitutional practice makes parliamentary control over the executive a standing power (Const. arts. 187(3), 222), so the civilian sees Chadha as a denial of legislative authority rather than as an enforcement of the manner in which that authority must be exercised.","authority":"Legislative veto: INS v. Chadha; Bowsher v. Synar; Clinton v. City of New York."},{"n":16,"summary":"A senator, speaking on the floor of Congress, accuses a federal purchasing officer of corruption; the officer sues for defamation alleging falsity and negligence.","trigger":"Civil or criminal liability sought against a member of Congress for conduct that is an integral part of the legislative process → absolute immunity; conduct outside legislative acts (press releases, newsletters, republication) → no immunity.","rule":"The Speech or Debate Clause (Art. I § 6) confers ABSOLUTE immunity on members of Congress for statements and acts that are an integral part of the legislative process — including floor speeches, committee work, and voting — regardless of falsity, negligence, or even malice. The immunity is functional rather than personal: it does not extend to republication outside the legislative sphere, such as press releases and constituent newsletters (Hutchinson v. Proxmire), nor to non-legislative activity like political errand-running.","sub":"Separation of Powers – Speech or Debate Clause","sensitivity":"The switch is WHERE the statement was made, not whether it was true or careful: the identical accusation is absolutely privileged on the floor and fully actionable in a press release. Note the doctrinal cousin — this is a structural immunity, distinct from the First Amendment defamation standards of the press cases.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: parliamentary inviolability protects the legislator — broadly RIGHT: Const. art. 200 grants Venezuelan deputies immunity for their votes and opinions, and the civilian instinct correctly identifies an absolute, function-based privilege. The calibration friction: the U.S. clause is narrower and purely functional — it confers no personal immunity from unrelated prosecution and evaporates the moment the legislator repeats the statement outside the legislative sphere, a line the broader civilian inmunidad parlamentaria does not draw.","authority":"Speech or Debate Clause: Art. I § 6; Hutchinson v. Proxmire; Gravel v. United States."},{"n":17,"summary":"A State bans out-of-state grapes and soybeans to keep out a pest it has never had; another requires that most guitars sold in-state be made largely of rosewood grown in that State.","trigger":"State law that discriminates on its face (or in purpose or effect) against interstate commerce, favoring in-state economic interests → virtually per se invalid unless the State proves a legitimate non-protectionist purpose and no reasonable nondiscriminatory alternative.","rule":"A state law that discriminates against interstate commerce is presumptively unconstitutional under the dormant Commerce Clause and survives only on a showing that it is necessary to achieve a legitimate LOCAL purpose with no reasonable nondiscriminatory alternative available (Philadelphia v. New Jersey; Hunt v. Washington State Apple Advertising Comm'n; Maine v. Taylor is the rare survivor). Economic protectionism is never a legitimate purpose, and local-processing or in-state-sourcing requirements are classic violations (Dean Milk; C & A Carbone). Where less restrictive tools exist — inspection, quarantine, certification, treatment — a total embargo fails. Congress may, however, authorize state discrimination it could itself enact.","sub":"Dormant Commerce Clause – Facial Discrimination (Master)","sensitivity":"Classification is destiny: label the law discriminatory and the State almost always loses; label it evenhanded and Pike balancing usually saves it (Unit 18). The tested move is spotting the less-discriminatory alternative the State ignored — that single fact converts a sympathetic health or safety rationale into a violation.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the State may protect its economy and its agriculture within its own territory — that is what state competence means” → U.S. law derives from a grant of power TO CONGRESS an unwritten prohibition on the States, enforced by courts against ordinary local regulation. The comparison that matters is not one ordinal but the ARCHITECTURE: the Constitution enumerates the competences of the national power in art. 156 and those of the states in art. 164, and the economic and agricultural matters at issue here sit with the nation while the states are left a narrow, largely administrative list. Venezuelan states therefore hold no such economic competence to begin with, so the reader has no experience of a subnational law struck down for protectionism — and no schema at all for a constitutional limit inferred from congressional silence rather than written down.","authority":"DCC facial discrimination: Philadelphia v. New Jersey; Hunt v. Washington State Apple Advertising Comm'n; Dean Milk v. Madison; C & A Carbone v. Clarkstown; Maine v. Taylor."},{"n":18,"summary":"A State requires the national standard red/black battery terminals, forcing an out-of-state seller with red/green terminals to spend thousands; a city routes trucks over 50 feet onto a two-hour detour, saving road-repair costs.","trigger":"State or local law that is evenhanded in its treatment of in-state and out-of-state commerce but imposes incidental burdens on interstate commerce → Pike balancing: invalid only if the burden is clearly excessive relative to the local benefit.","rule":"A nondiscriminatory state or local regulation that incidentally burdens interstate commerce is valid unless the burden imposed on that commerce is CLEARLY EXCESSIVE in relation to the putative local benefits (Pike v. Bruce Church). Uniform safety and standardization requirements applying equally to all sellers normally survive, even when out-of-state firms must adapt their products or packaging. The rare invalidations involve substantial disruption of interstate transportation with weak safety justification (Kassel v. Consolidated Freightways; Raymond Motor Transportation), or extraterritorial regulation of out-of-state transactions.","sub":"Dormant Commerce Clause – Pike Balancing (Incidental Burden)","sensitivity":"Pike is deliberately deferential — 'clearly excessive' is the operative phrase — so the default outcome is validity, and answer choices that invalidate on mere compliance cost are wrong. Note the drafting trap: an excessive burden makes a neutral law INVALID; it does not reclassify the law as discriminatory.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian proportionality instinct — weigh the burden against the public benefit — is RIGHT, and Pike is essentially a proportionality test. The calibration friction: the U.S. thumb is heavily on the scale for validity, and the interest being weighed is a STRUCTURAL one (the national market) rather than an individual right — so a civilian applying ordinary proporcionalidad tends to invalidate regulations that Pike comfortably upholds.","authority":"DCC Pike balancing: Pike v. Bruce Church; Kassel v. Consolidated Freightways; Raymond Motor Transportation v. Rice."},{"n":19,"summary":"A State that owns and operates a nuclear power system refuses to sell electricity to purchasers in States that will not accept its spent fuel for disposal.","trigger":"State acting as a BUYER or SELLER in a market with its own funds (rather than as a regulator) → dormant Commerce Clause inapplicable; downstream restrictions on parties it does not deal with, or Article IV privileges-and-immunities claims → exception unavailable.","rule":"When a State participates in a market as a buyer or seller rather than regulating it, the dormant Commerce Clause does not apply, and the State may prefer its own residents (Hughes v. Alexandria Scrap; Reeves v. Stake; White v. Massachusetts Council of Construction Employers). Two limits: the State may not impose conditions reaching DOWNSTREAM transactions beyond the market in which it participates (South-Central Timber v. Wunnicke), and the exception provides no defense to the Article IV Privileges and Immunities Clause, which protects out-of-state CITIZENS in fundamental economic activities such as pursuing a livelihood (Camden).","sub":"Dormant Commerce Clause – Market-Participant Exception","sensitivity":"Characterization of the State's role decides the entire dormant-Commerce analysis before any balancing: participant → immune, regulator → tested. But the exception is clause-specific — candidates who stop at 'market participant' miss the surviving Article IV claim, which is the exam's favorite second layer.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the State is bound by constitutional non-discrimination whenever it acts — form of action is irrelevant” → U.S. law switches OFF a structural constraint based on the government's commercial versus regulatory posture. The civilian distinction between actos de imperio and actos de gestión is the nearest cousin but does the opposite work: it allocates jurisdiction and applicable law, never suspends a constitutional prohibition — so the reflex refuses to accept that the same discrimination is lawful when the State sells and unlawful when it commands.","authority":"Market-participant exception: Hughes v. Alexandria Scrap; Reeves v. Stake; White v. Massachusetts Council of Constr. Employers; South-Central Timber v. Wunnicke; United Building & Constr. Trades v. Camden (Art. IV)."},{"n":20,"summary":"A private bulletproof-vest maker selling mostly to the federal government imposes mandatory retirement at 60; a privately owned mall with a public library branch expels peaceful protesters; a court enforces a racially restrictive covenant between private parties.","trigger":"Constitutional claim against a nominally private actor → test for state action: traditional public function, significant state entanglement or encouragement, or judicial/official enforcement of the private arrangement.","rule":"The Fourteenth Amendment and (through it) the Bill of Rights constrain only STATE ACTION; a private party's conduct is not subject to constitutional limits merely because it is regulated, licensed, publicly funded, or a government contractor (Rendell-Baker v. Kohn; Blum v. Yaretsky; Jackson v. Metropolitan Edison). Two doctrines convert private conduct into state action: the PUBLIC FUNCTION exception, for private entities performing traditionally exclusive government functions (Marsh v. Alabama — the company town; cabined to near-total municipal substitution by Lloyd Corp. and Hudgens, so an ordinary shopping mall is not a state actor); and ENTANGLEMENT, where the government authorizes, encourages, or facilitates the conduct — including JUDICIAL ENFORCEMENT of private discrimination, which is itself state action (Shelley v. Kraemer). Separately, the Thirteenth Amendment reaches purely private conduct, and § 1982 legislation under it bars private racial discrimination in property transactions.","sub":"State Action Doctrine (Master)","sensitivity":"The threshold question is not how governmental the actor FEELS but whether one of the two doctrines fits. Two tested edges: the mall (public-function claim fails despite public access and civic amenities) and the covenant (state action found in the courtroom, not in the contract). The Thirteenth Amendment is the escape route when no state actor exists at all.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “fundamental rights bind everyone — a private employer or landowner cannot violate equality” → U.S. constitutional rights run only against government, and the entire analysis begins with a hunt for a state actor. Venezuelan constitutional rights have direct horizontal effect: Const. art. 21 binds private parties and amparo lies against private conduct (LOA art. 2), so the civilian never developed the state-action reflex and will litigate a constitutional claim that has no constitutional defendant — among the most consequential structural gaps in the subject.","authority":"State action: Marsh v. Alabama; Lloyd Corp. v. Tanner; Hudgens v. NLRB; Shelley v. Kraemer; Rendell-Baker v. Kohn; Blum v. Yaretsky; Jones v. Alfred H. Mayer Co. (13th Am. / § 1982)."},{"n":21,"summary":"A contractor is shut out by an evenhanded licensing law; judges must retire at 65; landlords must upgrade rental housing; a State bans direct-to-consumer car sales; diagnostic centers must affiliate with hospitals.","trigger":"Government classification or liberty restriction + no suspect/quasi-suspect class and no fundamental right → rational basis: upheld if rationally related to any conceivable legitimate interest, with the burden on the challenger.","rule":"Equal protection and substantive due process share one architecture of three tiers. STRICT scrutiny (suspect classifications — race, national origin, state alienage — and fundamental rights): the government must show the law is necessary to a compelling interest. INTERMEDIATE scrutiny (gender, illegitimacy): the government must show a substantial relationship to an important interest. RATIONAL BASIS (everything else — age, wealth, disability, economic and social regulation): the law is upheld if rationally related to a legitimate interest, the government need not have articulated it, over- and under-inclusiveness are tolerated, and the CHALLENGER must negate every conceivable justification (Williamson v. Lee Optical; FCC v. Beach Communications; Murgia). Age classifications, including mandatory retirement, therefore survive despite individually capable claimants (Gregory v. Ashcroft).","sub":"Equal Protection / Substantive Due Process – Tiers of Scrutiny (Master)","sensitivity":"Tier selection decides the case, and the burden travels with the tier — which is why identifying the class or right is the whole exercise. Rational basis is not proportionality: fit is irrelevant, and 'the legislature could have thought so' is enough.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the measure must be proportionate — idoneidad, necesidad, proporcionalidad in every case” → U.S. law does not run one graduated proportionality test; it sorts cases into three tiers with fixed burdens, and in the largest tier it declines to weigh means against ends at all. The civilian who applies proporcionalidad universally invalidates ordinary economic legislation the U.S. system upholds automatically, and simultaneously under-protects in the strict-scrutiny tier by treating it as a balancing exercise rather than a near-categorical bar.","authority":"Tiers of scrutiny: Williamson v. Lee Optical; FCC v. Beach Communications; Massachusetts Bd. of Retirement v. Murgia; Gregory v. Ashcroft."},{"n":22,"summary":"A state police academy admits only those 'born male'; a state nursing school admits only women to remedy past discrimination; a city skin-care program is chartered for women; a state insurance plan excludes childbirth benefits.","trigger":"Government classification by gender, on its face or by intentional design → intermediate scrutiny with the burden on the government and an exceedingly persuasive justification; benign or remedial motive does not change the tier.","rule":"Gender classifications by government are quasi-suspect: the government bears the burden of showing an EXCEEDINGLY PERSUASIVE JUSTIFICATION — that the classification serves important governmental objectives and is substantially related to them (United States v. Virginia). Overbroad generalizations about the capacities or preferences of either sex cannot satisfy it, and the justification must be the government's actual purpose, not one invented in litigation. Remedial or 'benign' gender classifications receive the same scrutiny and fail where the asserted remedial purpose is pretextual or the exclusion perpetuates stereotype (Mississippi University for Women v. Hogan). Note the doctrinal fault line on pregnancy: Geduldig v. Aiello held that a pregnancy classification is not per se a gender classification for constitutional purposes, but Congress overrode the statutory analogue through the Pregnancy Discrimination Act, and the Court has since treated pregnancy-based stereotyping as gender-based (Nevada Dep't of Human Resources v. Hibbs).","sub":"Equal Protection – Gender Classifications (Master)","sensitivity":"Three features do the work: the burden sits on the government; the justification must be its real one; and 'we are helping women' does not lower the tier. The pregnancy line is the module's sharpest constitutional/statutory divergence — the same facts lose under the Constitution and win under the statute.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “equality between men and women is constitutionally guaranteed and affirmative measures for women are expressly favored (Const. arts. 21(2), 88)” → U.S. law subjects PROTECTIVE and remedial gender classifications to the same heightened scrutiny as hostile ones, and strikes them down when they rest on the very stereotypes they claim to remedy. The civilian schema of promotional equality (medidas positivas as a constitutional mandate) predicts validation where U.S. doctrine predicts invalidation — a directional error, not a gap in knowledge.","authority":"Gender classifications: United States v. Virginia; Mississippi Univ. for Women v. Hogan; Craig v. Boren; Geduldig v. Aiello and the Pregnancy Discrimination Act; Nevada Dep't of Human Resources v. Hibbs."},{"n":23,"summary":"A State denies a non-citizen a game-warden position; a town bars nationals of an 'aggressor' country from EMT work; a federal statute bars non-U.S.-born applicants from federal office.","trigger":"Alienage classification → identify the sovereign: STATE classification is suspect (strict scrutiny) unless it concerns a political function central to self-government; FEDERAL classification receives rational-basis deference under the immigration and foreign-affairs powers.","rule":"STATE and local discrimination against aliens is generally suspect and reviewed under strict scrutiny (Graham v. Richardson), with a narrow POLITICAL-FUNCTION exception applying rational basis to positions central to democratic self-government — police officers, probation officers, public school teachers, and the like (Foley v. Connelie; Ambach v. Norwick; Cabell) — which does not extend to ordinary public employment. FEDERAL alienage classifications, by contrast, receive great deference and rational-basis review because of Congress's plenary immigration and foreign-affairs powers (Mathews v. Diaz). A federal classification by NATIONAL ORIGIN aimed at citizens or lawful residents is different again: national origin is suspect and receives strict scrutiny.","sub":"Equal Protection – Alienage: State Suspect, Political Function, Federal Deference","sensitivity":"The same discrimination gets three different tiers depending on WHO discriminates and for WHAT post: State → strict; State for a self-government office → rational; federal → rational. The trap is applying the federal deference to a state law, or the political-function exception to a job (EMT, notary, civil engineer) that merely serves the public.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the rule on foreigners is the rule on foreigners — one national standard governs” → U.S. law makes the LEVEL OF GOVERNMENT the decisive variable, granting the nation latitude it denies the States over the identical classification. A single-sovereign system in which the treatment of extranjeros is fixed nationally (Const. arts. 21, 40 — with citizenship reserved for defined offices) provides no schema for scrutiny that varies by which government acted, so the civilian collapses the two tracks and answers half the questions wrong.","authority":"Alienage: Graham v. Richardson; Foley v. Connelie; Ambach v. Norwick; Cabell v. Chavez-Salido; Mathews v. Diaz."},{"n":24,"summary":"An intestacy statute bars an unacknowledged nonmarital child from proving parentage and inheriting, preferring a spouse, other blood relatives, and even the State.","trigger":"Government classification burdening nonmarital (illegitimate) children → intermediate scrutiny; a total bar fails, while narrowly drawn proof-of-paternity or timeliness requirements survive.","rule":"Classifications disadvantaging nonmarital children are quasi-suspect and receive intermediate scrutiny: they must be substantially related to an important governmental interest (Trimble v. Gordon; Clark v. Jeter). A statute that categorically bars an unacknowledged nonmarital child from inheriting fails, because the State's legitimate interests in orderly estate administration and accurate proof of paternity can be served by less absolute means; a requirement of timely, reliable proof of paternity is therefore permissible (Lalli v. Lalli). The rationale is that penalizing a child for the circumstances of birth bears no relation to individual responsibility.","sub":"Equal Protection – Illegitimacy (Intermediate Scrutiny)","sensitivity":"The tier is fixed (intermediate) and the outcome turns on absoluteness: total exclusion fails, procedural proof requirements survive. The State's asserted interest is almost always evidentiary — so the question is whether the statute pursues accuracy or simply punishes status.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — no discrimination among children by filiation — is RIGHT and indeed stronger: Const. art. 76 and C.C. reforms equalized filiación, and forced-heirship rules protect all children alike. The calibration friction is remedial architecture rather than principle: U.S. law reaches the result through a scrutiny TIER that still permits proof-of-paternity conditions, whereas the civilian expects flat statutory equality — so the civilian over-predicts invalidation of legitimate evidentiary requirements.","authority":"Illegitimacy: Trimble v. Gordon; Lalli v. Lalli; Clark v. Jeter."},{"n":25,"summary":"A neutral federal driving test fails 39 of 120 male applicants and only 5 of 80 women; the rejected man sues for gender discrimination on the strength of the disparity alone.","trigger":"Facially neutral government action + statistically disproportionate impact on a protected class + no evidence of discriminatory purpose → no equal protection violation; rational basis applies.","rule":"An equal protection violation requires discriminatory PURPOSE: a facially neutral law or practice with a racially or sexually disproportionate impact triggers heightened scrutiny only if the plaintiff shows the government acted at least in part BECAUSE of that impact (Washington v. Davis; Personnel Administrator v. Feeney). Impact is evidence of purpose, and courts consider the pattern's starkness, the historical background, departures from normal procedure, and legislative history (Arlington Heights). Absent purpose, the action is reviewed under rational basis and normally upheld. Statutory disparate-impact liability (Title VII, the FHA) is a legislative creation and is not constitutional doctrine.","sub":"Equal Protection – The Discriminatory-Purpose Requirement","sensitivity":"One question governs: was the classification INTENDED? A dramatic statistical disparity produced by a neutral, job-related test is constitutionally irrelevant standing alone — which is why the exam supplies vivid numbers and no evidence of motive. The constitutional/statutory divergence is the second layer.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “discriminación indirecta is discrimination — the effect proves the violation” → U.S. constitutional law demands proof of purpose and leaves effects-based liability to statutes. Venezuelan constitutional and labor doctrine treats disparate effect as itself actionable inequality (Const. art. 21(1)–(2); LOTTT anti-discrimination provisions), so the civilian reads the numbers as dispositive and never looks for intent — a systematic over-claim precisely where U.S. doctrine is at its most restrictive.","authority":"Discriminatory purpose: Washington v. Davis; Personnel Administrator v. Feeney; Village of Arlington Heights v. Metropolitan Housing Dev. Corp."},{"n":26,"summary":"A State denies a marriage license to anyone divorced more than three times; a State requires every homeowner to post a sign declaring which party they will vote for.","trigger":"Government burdens a fundamental right → route the claim: intentional burden falling on an identifiable CLASS → Equal Protection; general restriction on everyone's liberty → Substantive Due Process; burden on expression → the First Amendment governs directly.","rule":"Equal protection and substantive due process are overlapping, not mutually exclusive, routes to the same fundamental rights. Where the government singles out a CLASS for the burden, the Court analyzes the claim under Equal Protection, and a direct and substantial interference with a fundamental right such as marriage triggers heightened review (Zablocki v. Redhail; Loving v. Virginia). Where the restriction applies to everyone, the claim proceeds under substantive due process (Unit 29). But when the burden falls on expression, the First Amendment supplies the governing analysis on its own terms — a compelled declaration of political affiliation is compelled speech and an invasion of associational privacy (West Virginia v. Barnette; McIntyre; NAACP v. Alabama), and framing it as a classification of 'homeowners' misses the constitutional defect.","sub":"Routing the Claim – Equal Protection vs. Substantive Due Process vs. the First Amendment","sensitivity":"Routing precedes scrutiny, and the exam tests the routing itself. Ask: is a CLASS singled out (EP), is EVERYONE restricted (SDP), or is SPEECH or association burdened (First Amendment, which controls whenever it applies)? Choosing the wrong doorway makes every later step wrong even when the outcome is right.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the right was violated — name the right and grant the amparo” → U.S. adjudication requires selecting a doctrinal vehicle whose choice changes the burden, the tier, and often the result. The civilian amparo tradition is remedy-centred and clause-agnostic (Const. art. 27 protects all constitutional rights through one action), so the trained reflex never learned that the same facts must be filed through a particular clause — producing answers that identify the right violation under the wrong doctrine.","authority":"Claim routing: Zablocki v. Redhail; Loving v. Virginia; West Virginia Bd. of Educ. v. Barnette; McIntyre v. Ohio Elections Comm'n; NAACP v. Alabama."},{"n":27,"summary":"Congress requires all Greek nationals to register handguns; a federal statute bars non-U.S.-born applicants from federal office; a federal program discriminates in a way that would plainly violate equal protection if a State did it.","trigger":"Discrimination by the FEDERAL government → the Fourteenth Amendment does not apply; the claim proceeds through the equal protection COMPONENT of the Fifth Amendment Due Process Clause, applying the same tiers.","rule":"The Equal Protection Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment applies only to the States. Federal discrimination is reached through the Due Process Clause of the FIFTH Amendment, which the Court has held contains an equal protection component of essentially identical scope (Bolling v. Sharpe — 'reverse incorporation'; Adarand Constructors v. Pena). There is therefore no such thing as a 'Fifth Amendment Equal Protection Clause': the correct formulation is the equal protection component of Fifth Amendment due process. The same tiers apply, with the notable exception that federal ALIENAGE classifications receive rational-basis deference (Unit 23) while national-origin classifications remain suspect.","sub":"Reverse Incorporation – Federal Equal Protection via the Fifth Amendment","sensitivity":"The doctrine is a routing rule with a naming trap: identify the defendant's sovereign, then cite the correct clause. Answer choices invoking the Fourteenth Amendment against the federal government, or a nonexistent Fifth Amendment equal protection clause, are wrong on the label even when right on the tier.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “equality is one constitutional guarantee binding all public authorities” → in the U.S. the guarantee reaches the two sovereigns through two DIFFERENT textual routes, one of them judicially inferred. A unitary constitution whose art. 21 binds every organ of the State (Const. arts. 7, 137) offers no analogue to a rights provision that omits the national government and must be read back into another clause — so the civilian never learns the clause-selection step this doctrine exists to teach.","authority":"Reverse incorporation: Bolling v. Sharpe; Adarand Constructors v. Pena."},{"n":28,"summary":"Welfare benefits are cut off with no prior hearing; a doctor's license is revoked without a hearing after a fraud conviction; a parent commits a minor to psychiatric care.","trigger":"Government deprivation of a protected LIBERTY or PROPERTY interest → identify the interest, then determine WHAT process is due under Mathews balancing; where the operative facts were already adjudicated, no further hearing is required.","rule":"Procedural due process requires (1) a protected liberty or property interest — a professional license or an existing statutory entitlement to benefits qualifies once granted — and (2) constitutionally adequate procedures, determined by balancing the private interest affected, the risk of erroneous deprivation and the probable value of added safeguards, and the government's interest including administrative burden (Mathews v. Eldridge). Termination of welfare benefits already being received requires a PRE-termination evidentiary hearing (Goldberg v. Kelly), while a post-deprivation hearing suffices for disability benefits (Mathews). Where the facts justifying the deprivation were fully adjudicated in a criminal proceeding, no second trial-type hearing is owed (Dixon v. Love). Parental commitment of a minor requires review by a neutral medical decisionmaker, not an adversarial hearing (Parham v. J.R.).","sub":"Procedural Due Process – Protected Interests and Mathews Balancing (Master)","sensitivity":"Two questions, in order, and the second is a balance rather than a rule: IS there an interest, then HOW MUCH process. The Goldberg/Mathews pair is the tested seam — both involve benefit terminations and they come out differently on timing, because the private interest and the error risk differ.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — no deprivation without prior hearing and defense (Const. art. 49; debido proceso) — is RIGHT in principle and often in result. The calibration friction is that U.S. law makes the QUANTUM of process a case-by-case balance rather than a fixed guarantee: sometimes a post-deprivation remedy suffices, sometimes a neutral medical review replaces a hearing, and a prior adjudication can exhaust the entitlement — gradations the codal guarantee of audiencia previa does not draw.","authority":"Procedural due process: Mathews v. Eldridge; Goldberg v. Kelly; Board of Regents v. Roth; Dixon v. Love; Parham v. J.R."},{"n":29,"summary":"A city requires public-school attendance until 16, defeating a family's homeschooling plan; a city refuses to fund abortions or allow them in its hospital, leaving a distant private clinic as the only option.","trigger":"Government restriction burdening a recognized fundamental liberty (family, childrearing, marriage, procreation, bodily integrity) → strict scrutiny; government REFUSAL TO SUBSIDIZE the exercise of a right → no violation.","rule":"Substantive due process protects certain fundamental liberties from government interference regardless of the process afforded, including the right of parents to direct the upbringing and education of their children (Meyer v. Nebraska; Pierce v. Society of Sisters; Troxel v. Granville). The right is not absolute: the State may compel education and set academic standards — Pierce forbids only requiring EXCLUSIVELY public schooling — and religious objections proceed through a Free Exercise hybrid analysis (Wisconsin v. Yoder). Distinctly, these are NEGATIVE rights: the Constitution bars government interference but imposes no affirmative duty to fund or facilitate the exercise of a right, so a refusal to pay for a procedure or to provide it in public facilities is not an infringement (Maher v. Roe; Harris v. McRae; DeShaney).","sub":"Substantive Due Process – Fundamental Liberties; the No-Funding Principle","sensitivity":"Two independent moves: recognize the liberty (and its internal limits — the State may regulate education without monopolizing it), then apply the negative-rights principle, which defeats every claim framed as entitlement to government support. Currency note: Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health (2022) withdrew the federal abortion right, but the no-funding principle it once illustrated is independent and intact.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “a right the State cannot deliver is an empty right — the State must guarantee its effective exercise” → U.S. constitutional rights are overwhelmingly negative: they restrain government without obliging it to act. The Venezuelan constitution is programmatic and expressly imposes affirmative duties (Const. arts. 83 health, 102 education, 76 maternity protection), so the civilian reflexively converts a liberty into a state obligation — and answers the funding questions exactly backwards.","authority":"Substantive due process / no-funding principle: Meyer v. Nebraska; Pierce v. Society of Sisters; Wisconsin v. Yoder; Troxel v. Granville; Maher v. Roe; Harris v. McRae; DeShaney v. Winnebago County. Currency note: Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Org. (2022)."},{"n":30,"summary":"A State conditions public employment on one year's residency; a city requires a year's residency for free medical care; another requires two years before free healthcare and denies a resident injured at four months.","trigger":"DURATIONAL residency requirement conditioning an important benefit or right on length of residence → strict scrutiny under the right to travel and the Fourteenth Amendment's Privileges or Immunities Clause; bona fide residence requirements and lower tuition rates are treated differently.","rule":"The right to interstate travel is fundamental, and its third component — the right of a newly arrived citizen to be treated equally with longer-term residents — is protected by the Privileges OR Immunities Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment (Saenz v. Roe). Durational residency requirements conditioning important benefits on length of residence therefore receive strict scrutiny and generally fail: one year for welfare (Shapiro v. Thompson), for nonemergency medical care (Memorial Hospital v. Maricopa County), and for voting (Dunn v. Blumstein). Two distinctions matter: a BONA FIDE residence requirement (actually living in the jurisdiction) is permissible, and durational requirements for reduced state college tuition or for divorce jurisdiction have been upheld. This clause is not the Article IV Privileges and Immunities Clause, which protects out-of-state CITIZENS against discrimination in fundamental economic activities such as pursuing a livelihood.","sub":"Right to Travel – Durational Residency; the Two Privileges Clauses","sensitivity":"The dispositive distinction is durational versus bona fide: 'do you live here' is valid, 'how long have you lived here' is not. Equally tested is clause selection — new residents invoke the Fourteenth Amendment's Privileges or Immunities Clause (the rare clause with real force after Slaughter-House), while out-of-staters invoke Article IV.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “a residency period is a reasonable administrative condition — domicilio requirements are routine” → U.S. law treats waiting periods for important benefits as penalties on migration itself, subject to strict scrutiny. In a unitary state, internal movement raises no constitutional question and domicile conditions are ordinary administration (Const. art. 50 protects free transit without any equal-treatment-of-newcomers corollary), so the civilian sees bureaucracy where U.S. law sees a burden on a fundamental right — and then compounds the error by citing the wrong privileges clause.","authority":"Right to travel: Saenz v. Roe; Shapiro v. Thompson; Memorial Hospital v. Maricopa County; Dunn v. Blumstein; contrast Art. IV § 2 (Camden)."},{"n":31,"summary":"A State diverts employer 401(k) matching funds under existing contracts; a State raises drug-sale maximums and applies them to a defendant arrested earlier; Congress names three park rangers and bars them from federal employment.","trigger":"Retroactive legislation → sort by target: substantial impairment of an EXISTING contract (Contracts Clause), retroactive criminalization or increased punishment (Ex Post Facto), or legislative punishment of named individuals without trial (Bill of Attainder).","rule":"Article I § 10 imposes three retroactivity limits on the States (the latter two also bind Congress under Art. I § 9). CONTRACTS CLAUSE: a state law substantially impairing existing contractual obligations is invalid unless reasonable and necessary to serve a significant and legitimate public purpose; review is stricter when the State impairs its OWN contracts, and the clause does not reach prospective regulation (federal impairments are tested under Fifth Amendment due process). EX POST FACTO: a law is invalid if it retroactively criminalizes conduct, increases the punishment for a completed crime, or reduces the evidence required to convict — it applies only to criminal or punitive laws. BILL OF ATTAINDER: a legislative act naming specific persons and inflicting punishment without judicial trial, including permanent disqualification from employment, is void (United States v. Lovett).","sub":"Article I § 10 – Contracts Clause, Ex Post Facto, Bill of Attainder","sensitivity":"Three clauses, one trigger (retroactivity), sorted by what the law does: touches a contract, punishes past conduct, or punishes a named person. Two frequent traps: the Contracts Clause binds only the States, and Ex Post Facto reaches only criminal or punitive measures — a retroactive civil or tax rule is tested under due process instead.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — irretroactividad de la ley (Const. art. 24; C.C. art. 3) and pacta sunt servanda — is RIGHT, and the criminal non-retroactivity rule converges almost exactly. The calibration friction lies in scope and structure: the U.S. Contracts Clause binds only the STATES and yields to legitimate public purpose, the Ex Post Facto bar is criminal-only, and the bill-of-attainder concept (a LEGISLATURE punishing named individuals) has no codal category at all, because the civilian assumes punishment is inherently judicial.","authority":"Article I § 10: Contracts Clause (Allied Structural Steel v. Spannaus; United States Trust Co. v. New Jersey); Ex Post Facto (Calder v. Bull; Collins v. Youngblood); Bill of Attainder (United States v. Lovett; Nixon v. Administrator of General Services)."},{"n":32,"summary":"A city denies a permit to one group because its videos are disturbing while allowing others the same day; only protesters criticizing the President's immigration stance must wait until 5 p.m.; a statute bans flag burning 'as part of a protest'; an ordinance restricts only labor-strike picketers near schools.","trigger":"Speech regulation in a public forum → determine whether it draws lines by SUBJECT MATTER or VIEWPOINT (content-based → strict scrutiny, burden on the government) or applies regardless of message (content-neutral → intermediate scrutiny under Unit 34).","rule":"A law is content-based if it restricts speech because of the topic discussed or the idea expressed — including a rule singling out one subject (immigration protests) or one category of speaker-message (labor picketing) — and it is then presumptively invalid, surviving only strict scrutiny: necessary to a compelling interest and the least restrictive means (Reed v. Town of Gilbert; Police Dept. of Chicago v. Mosley; Carey v. Brown). Viewpoint discrimination is the most serious form and is forbidden in every forum. Selective permit denial by message is content discrimination even where the stated concern is neutral (crowd control, children present), because content-neutral alternatives — uniform crowd, time, and permit limits — would serve the interest without reference to message.","sub":"First Amendment – Content-Based vs. Content-Neutral (Master)","sensitivity":"Classification is the whole case, and it is made on the FACE of the rule: 'all labor-strike picketers' and 'burning as part of a protest' are content-based however neutral the motive. Once content-based, the availability of a neutral alternative is what defeats the government.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “speech may be limited for public order, and the authority may weigh the disturbance each message causes” → U.S. law forbids exactly that weighing: government may regulate the circumstances of speech but almost never its subject. Venezuelan constitutional speech protection is expressly qualified by responsibility and public order (Const. art. 57, with prohibitions on anonymity and war propaganda), so the civilian reflex treats message-sensitive regulation as ordinary administration rather than as the presumptively fatal defect it is.","authority":"Content-based regulation: Reed v. Town of Gilbert; Police Dept. of Chicago v. Mosley; Carey v. Brown; R.A.V. v. City of St. Paul."},{"n":33,"summary":"A veteran burns a small U.S. flag while chanting at a rally and is cited under a neutral no-fires-without-permit rule; a protester burns dollar bills in a park and is ticketed under the same kind of ordinance.","trigger":"Conduct with communicative intent + regulation directed at the CONDUCT for reasons unrelated to suppressing the message → O'Brien intermediate scrutiny; regulation aimed at the message the conduct conveys → strict scrutiny.","rule":"Where speech and nonspeech elements combine in the same course of conduct, a government regulation is sufficiently justified if it is within the government's constitutional power, furthers an important or substantial interest UNRELATED to the suppression of expression, and the incidental restriction on expression is no greater than essential to that interest (United States v. O'Brien). A neutral fire-permit or park ordinance applied to symbolic burning therefore survives. But a law targeting the act BECAUSE of what it communicates is content-based and fails strict scrutiny: flag burning as political expression is protected (Texas v. Johnson; United States v. Eichman).","sub":"First Amendment – Expressive Conduct (O'Brien vs. Johnson)","sensitivity":"The identical physical act — setting a flag alight — produces opposite outcomes depending on what the statute targets: fire (valid, O'Brien) or desecration/protest (invalid, Johnson). Reading the STATUTE rather than the conduct is the entire technique.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “burning the national flag is an offence against national symbols — plainly punishable” → U.S. doctrine protects precisely the symbolic destruction of state symbols as core political speech. Venezuelan law criminalizes contempt for national symbols (Const. art. 8; C.P. provisions on ultraje), so the civilian instinct is not merely different but inverted, and the exam's neutral-ordinance variant then compounds the error by looking like the same case.","authority":"Expressive conduct: United States v. O'Brien; Texas v. Johnson; United States v. Eichman; Clark v. CCNV."},{"n":34,"summary":"A city regulates billboard size and spacing near highway exits after complaints about clutter and sightlines; a locality restricts picketing and leafleting by location and hour for traffic safety.","trigger":"Content-neutral regulation of the time, place, or manner of expression in a traditional or designated public forum → intermediate scrutiny: narrowly tailored to a significant government interest, leaving ample alternative channels of communication.","rule":"In a public forum, a content-neutral time, place, and manner restriction is valid if it is justified without reference to the content of the speech, is narrowly tailored to serve a significant governmental interest, and leaves open ample alternative channels for communication (Ward v. Rock Against Racism; Clark v. Community for Creative Non-Violence). 'Narrowly tailored' here does NOT require the least restrictive means — it requires only that the regulation not burden substantially more speech than necessary. Permit schemes are permissible if they apply neutral, definite criteria and leave no unbridled discretion in the licensor (Forsyth County; Shuttlesworth); size, spacing, noise, and hour limits routinely survive, while total prohibitions on a medium do not (Unit 35).","sub":"First Amendment – Time, Place, and Manner Restrictions","sensitivity":"Three elements, and the tailoring element is deliberately softer than strict scrutiny — answer choices demanding the least restrictive alternative misstate the test. The ample-alternatives element is where sweeping rules fail: a limit on HOW one speaks survives, a rule that eliminates the channel does not.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — the authority may regulate the manner of public assembly and expression for order and traffic, subject to proportionality — is broadly RIGHT (Const. art. 68 permits regulation of public demonstrations). The calibration friction: U.S. law demands content-neutrality as a threshold before any balancing, forbids discretionary licensing, and treats 'ample alternative channels' as an independent requirement — three structural filters the civilian proportionality analysis folds into a single reasonableness judgment.","authority":"Time, place, manner: Ward v. Rock Against Racism; Clark v. CCNV; Forsyth County v. Nationalist Movement; Shuttlesworth v. Birmingham."},{"n":35,"summary":"A city bans 'all First Amendment activity' in a park before an election, and elsewhere after 5 p.m.; a State bans all leafleting within 500 feet of casinos to fight litter; a school bans possession of any Star of David after a gang adopts the symbol; a statute punishes any false or misleading public statement.","trigger":"Speech regulation that reaches substantially more protected expression than necessary to serve the asserted interest → overbreadth (facial invalidation available even to a defendant whose own speech could be punished); vagueness where the standard is undefined.","rule":"A law regulating speech is invalid on its face as OVERBROAD if it prohibits substantially more protected expression than necessary to achieve the government's interest, and a litigant may challenge it facially even if his own conduct could constitutionally be reached (Broadrich; Houston v. Hill). A total ban on a mode of expression in a public forum fails even for a legitimate interest such as litter control (Schneider v. State), and a prohibition on 'all First Amendment activity' is overbroad by definition. A related defect is the absence of a fault requirement: falsity alone is not unprotected (United States v. Alvarez), and liability for false statements requires at least fault as to falsity (Gertz). In the public-school setting, however, the governing framework is Tinker — student expression may be restricted on a showing of material and substantial disruption — so a symbol ban responding to documented gang violence is analyzed under that standard rather than pure overbreadth.","sub":"First Amendment – Overbreadth, Narrow Tailoring, and the Fault Requirement","sensitivity":"Overbreadth is the doctrine that lets a bad law fall entirely rather than being applied case by case — the remedy, not just the standard, is what makes it distinctive. Two calibrations: the school context imports Tinker, and false-speech statutes need scienter.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “apply the rule to this case — if this speaker's conduct was unlawful, he is liable” → U.S. law permits a speaker whose own conduct is punishable to strike the statute down for OTHERS, treating the chilling effect on absent third parties as the constitutional injury. Concentrated constitutional review in Venezuela does allow abstract nullity actions, but not as a defense raised by a properly charged defendant in his own case — so the civilian misses that overbreadth is simultaneously a standard and a standing exception.","authority":"Overbreadth and fault: Broadrick v. Oklahoma; City of Houston v. Hill; Schneider v. State; City of Ladue v. Gilleo; United States v. Alvarez; Gertz v. Robert Welch; school context: Tinker v. Des Moines."},{"n":36,"summary":"A city sells bus advertising space but refuses an animal-rights placard to protect a circus that buys many ads; supporters of a defendant hold 'Death to Jurors Who Convict' signs on courthouse grounds during deliberations.","trigger":"Government property used for speech → classify the forum: traditional public, designated public, limited public, or nonpublic → apply the matching standard; viewpoint discrimination is barred in all four.","rule":"Forum classification sets the standard. In TRADITIONAL and DESIGNATED public forums, content-based restrictions require strict scrutiny and content-neutral ones must satisfy time-place-manner review. In LIMITED public and NONPUBLIC forums — including government advertising space, military bases, jails, and courthouse grounds — restrictions need only be reasonable in light of the forum's purpose and VIEWPOINT NEUTRAL (Perry Education Ass'n; Lehman v. City of Shaker Heights, treating transit advertising as a nonpublic forum; Cornelius). Reasonable, viewpoint-neutral rules protecting the administration of justice on courthouse grounds are therefore valid (Cox v. Louisiana); and a threat of violence against jurors is a true threat, unprotected in any forum. Excluding an advertisement to protect a paying advertiser's sensibilities is viewpoint-based and fails regardless of classification.","sub":"First Amendment – Forum Doctrine","sensitivity":"Two steps that candidates reverse: classify the forum FIRST, then select the standard — and remember that the viewpoint-neutrality floor survives every downgrade in classification. The nonpublic-forum label lowers the bar but never licenses discrimination against a point of view.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “public property is public — either the authority may regulate it or citizens may use it freely” → U.S. law grades government property into four speech categories with different constitutional standards, a taxonomy built entirely by case law. The civilian schema of bienes de dominio público allocates use through administrative authorization and never varies the intensity of rights review by the property's speech history — so the trained reflex has no way to predict that a bus placard and a public sidewalk are governed by different tests.","authority":"Forum doctrine: Perry Educ. Ass'n v. Perry Local Educators' Ass'n; Lehman v. City of Shaker Heights; Cornelius v. NAACP Legal Defense Fund; Cox v. Louisiana; true threats (Virginia v. Black)."},{"n":37,"summary":"An unlicensed man advertises that he can 'help like an attorney' for less; Congress bans all advertising of red meat while leaving it lawful to sell; a State bans all in-person lawyer solicitation.","trigger":"Government restriction on speech proposing a commercial transaction → Central Hudson: is the speech lawful and non-misleading; is the asserted interest substantial; does the restriction directly advance it; is it no more extensive than necessary (reasonable fit).","rule":"Commercial speech that concerns unlawful activity or is false or misleading receives NO First Amendment protection — so advertising the unlicensed practice of law may simply be prohibited. Otherwise the government must show a substantial interest, that the regulation directly advances it, and that it is not more extensive than necessary (Central Hudson Gas & Electric v. Public Service Comm'n); a TOTAL ban on truthful advertising of a lawful product ordinarily fails the fit requirement (44 Liquormart; Virginia Pharmacy). Attorney advertising follows the same framework with one calibration: because of the risk of overreaching, a State may categorically prohibit IN-PERSON, live solicitation for pecuniary gain (Ohralik v. Ohio State Bar Ass'n), while truthful targeted mailings are protected (Shapero) and non-pecuniary public-interest solicitation is protected (In re Primus).","sub":"First Amendment – Commercial Speech (Central Hudson)","sensitivity":"Gate one disposes of many questions on its own: unlawful or misleading commercial speech is unprotected, full stop. Past that gate, the fit prong is where total bans die — with the in-person solicitation exception as the deliberate counterexample the exam uses to test whether the candidate knows the calibration rather than the slogan.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “commercial advertising is economic activity subject to consumer-protection regulation, not a constitutional freedom” → U.S. law treats truthful commercial speech as constitutionally protected expression that the State must justify restricting. The Venezuelan schema places publicidad under administrative consumer regulation (Const. art. 117; LOPCYMAT-style protective statutes) with no expressive dimension, so the civilian concedes regulatory power the First Amendment denies — and then misses that the protection disappears entirely for unlawful or misleading proposals.","authority":"Commercial speech: Central Hudson Gas & Elec. v. Public Serv. Comm'n; Virginia Pharmacy Bd. v. Virginia Citizens Consumer Council; 44 Liquormart v. Rhode Island; Ohralik v. Ohio State Bar Ass'n; Shapero v. Kentucky Bar Ass'n; In re Primus."},{"n":38,"summary":"A prosecutor threatens an artist with prosecution over one sexually graphic painting in a public gallery and charges him with displaying obscene material.","trigger":"Criminal obscenity prosecution → the government must prove every element of the Miller test beyond a reasonable doubt; sexual explicitness alone is not obscenity.","rule":"Obscenity is unprotected speech, but only if the government proves all three Miller elements beyond a reasonable doubt in a criminal prosecution: (1) the average person applying contemporary COMMUNITY standards would find the work, taken as a whole, appeals to the prurient interest; (2) it depicts sexual conduct in a patently offensive way, again by community standards; and (3) taken as a whole it LACKS serious literary, artistic, political, or scientific value (Miller v. California). The third element is measured by a national reasonable-person standard rather than community standards (Pope v. Illinois), and the work must be judged as a whole rather than by isolated passages or images.","sub":"First Amendment – Obscenity (Miller)","sensitivity":"Three elements, two different measuring rods: community standards for prurience and offensiveness, a national reasonable-person standard for serious value — and the whole-work requirement defeats single-image prosecutions. Because the burden is criminal, an unproved element ends the case.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “obscene material is contrary to morals and public order — the authority may suppress it” → U.S. law makes sexual expression presumptively PROTECTED and forces the prosecution to prove a three-element definition beyond a reasonable doubt, with artistic value functioning as a complete defense. Civilian public-morals regulation (administrative classification and seizure powers) supplies no such definitional burden, so the civilian assumes the government's characterization controls where U.S. law makes it the contested issue.","authority":"Obscenity: Miller v. California; Pope v. Illinois; Jenkins v. Georgia."},{"n":39,"summary":"A statute lets reporters observe executions but permits publishing only the warden's official statement; after a botched execution a newspaper publishes a detailed eyewitness account and is prosecuted.","trigger":"Criminal or civil sanction for PUBLISHING truthful information that was lawfully obtained on a matter of public significance → near-absolute protection; distinguish the absence of any special right of ACCESS.","rule":"Punishing the publication of lawfully obtained truthful information about a matter of public significance requires a state interest of the highest order and virtually never survives (Smith v. Daily Mail; Florida Star v. B.J.F.; Landmark Communications v. Virginia). Prior restraints are subject to an even heavier presumption of invalidity (Near v. Minnesota; New York Times v. United States). The press has NO special constitutional right of access to information or government proceedings beyond that of the public, and it enjoys no privilege to disobey generally applicable laws in gathering news — but once a reporter is lawfully present and lawfully in possession of the information, the government may not criminalize its publication.","sub":"First Amendment – Publication of Lawfully Obtained Truthful Information","sensitivity":"The doctrine splits ACCESS from PUBLICATION: the State may close the execution to reporters entirely, but having admitted them it cannot dictate what they publish. Answer choices that treat the observation privilege as a bargaining chip for editorial control invert the rule.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the State may set conditions on access to official acts, and may sanction breaches of official confidentiality” → U.S. law severs the conditions from the consequence: a truthful publication about public business is protected even when it defies the terms under which access was granted. Venezuelan media regulation is built around responsibility for content and administrative sanction (Const. art. 58; Ley RESORTE), so the civilian reflex reads the reporter's breach as the operative fact rather than the publication's truthfulness and public significance.","authority":"Publication of truthful information: Smith v. Daily Mail Publishing Co.; Florida Star v. B.J.F.; Landmark Communications v. Virginia; Near v. Minnesota; New York Times Co. v. United States; Branzburg v. Hayes (no special access/privilege)."},{"n":40,"summary":"A businessman prints thousands of anti-incumbent T-shirts and banners; after re-election the mayor moves city events out of his banquet hall to another city at 25% higher cost, damaging his business.","trigger":"Government imposes an adverse action on an employee or CONTRACTOR because of protected political speech or association → unconstitutional retaliation unless the affiliation is relevant to job performance or the government meets the Pickering/Mt. Healthy defense.","rule":"Government may not penalize employees, and may not terminate or refuse to deal with independent CONTRACTORS, because of their protected political beliefs, speech, or association; the employee framework applies to contractors (Board of County Commissioners v. Umbehr; O'Hare Truck Service v. City of Northlake). Party affiliation may be a permissible criterion only for policymaking or confidential positions (Elrod v. Burns; Branti v. Finkel). The plaintiff must show the protected activity was a substantial or motivating factor in the adverse decision, after which the government may avoid liability by proving it would have taken the same action regardless (Mt. Healthy v. Doyle); for employee speech, the speech must address a matter of public concern and survive Pickering balancing against workplace disruption.","sub":"First Amendment – Retaliation Against Employees and Contractors","sensitivity":"The right does not depend on entitlement to the benefit: no one has a right to the city's catering contract, yet the government may not withdraw it BECAUSE of protected speech — the unconstitutional-conditions insight. Causation then carries the case, with the Mt. Healthy same-decision defense as the government's escape.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the administration chooses its contractors freely — discretionary award decisions are unreviewable, and no one is owed a contract” → U.S. doctrine polices the REASON for a discretionary decision even where the benefit itself may be denied for any other reason. Venezuelan administrative-contracting review focuses on procedural regularity and desviación de poder within administrative law, not on a constitutional speech-retaliation theory, so the civilian sees lawful discretion where U.S. law sees a First Amendment violation.","authority":"Retaliation: Board of County Comm'rs v. Umbehr; O'Hare Truck Service v. City of Northlake; Elrod v. Burns; Branti v. Finkel; Mt. Healthy v. Doyle; Pickering v. Board of Educ."},{"n":41,"summary":"Citing political unrest, a State bans all political expenditures; a corporation that funds a campaign-fraud research group challenges the ban.","trigger":"Government limit on political spending → distinguish independent EXPENDITURES (core political speech, strict scrutiny, limits fail) from CONTRIBUTIONS to candidates (associational interest, closely drawn scrutiny, limits often upheld); corporate speaker status does not remove protection.","rule":"Political spending is protected political speech. Limits on independent EXPENDITURES — including expenditures by corporations and unions — fail strict scrutiny (Buckley v. Valeo; Citizens United v. FEC), because independent spending does not create the quid pro quo corruption that is the only interest sufficient to justify restriction. Limits on CONTRIBUTIONS to candidates receive less rigorous 'closely drawn' scrutiny and are frequently upheld as anti-corruption measures, though aggregate caps have been invalidated (McCutcheon). Disclosure and disclaimer requirements are generally valid. Corporations hold First Amendment speech rights, though not every individual right — they have no privilege against self-incrimination and no Article IV privileges-and-immunities protection.","sub":"First Amendment – Political Expenditures and Contributions","sensitivity":"The expenditure/contribution split governs the tier and usually the outcome; the anti-corruption interest is narrow (quid pro quo only), so anti-distortion or 'level the playing field' rationales fail. A blanket ban on all political expenditures is invalid on its face.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “campaign finance is regulated to protect electoral equality — and corporations certainly have no political rights” → U.S. law treats spending as speech and extends that protection to corporate speakers, rejecting equalization as a justification. Venezuelan electoral law prohibits corporate and foreign political financing outright and centralizes campaign propaganda under CNE control (Const. art. 67, which expressly bars financing of associations with political ends from State funds and regulates private financing), so the civilian instinct is not a gap but an inversion — the presumption of regulability collides with a presumption of protection.","authority":"Political spending: Buckley v. Valeo; Citizens United v. FEC; McCutcheon v. FEC; corporations' limited rights (no Fifth Amendment self-incrimination privilege; no Art. IV privileges and immunities)."},{"n":42,"summary":"A city funds education checks usable at religious schools; a public school donates leftover lunches to a church; a program buys secular textbooks for secular schools and bibles for religious ones; a school board proposes a below-market no-bid sale of a building to a church.","trigger":"Government action alleged to advance religion → apply the history-and-tradition test (Kennedy v. Bremerton, replacing Lemon); distinguish neutral aid reaching religion through independent private choice from direct financial preference for religion.","rule":"The Establishment Clause is now applied by reference to historical practices and understandings rather than the three-part Lemon test, which the Court abandoned in Kennedy v. Bremerton School District (2022). Under current doctrine, aid programs that are religiously NEUTRAL and reach religious institutions only through the independent private choices of beneficiaries are permissible (Zelman v. Simmons-Harris; Trinity Lutheran and Carson v. Makin hold that excluding religious institutions from a neutral benefit because they are religious violates the FREE EXERCISE Clause). By contrast, direct government financial preferences for religion — purchasing bibles for religious schools, or transferring public property to a church below market value without competitive bidding — fail under any framework. Lemon's secular-purpose, primary-effect, and entanglement inquiries should now be cited only as the superseded framework.","sub":"Establishment Clause – The Post-Lemon History-and-Tradition Approach","sensitivity":"Two things must be current: the TEST (history and tradition, not Lemon) and the direction of the modern pressure (neutral programs must often INCLUDE religious institutions, or they violate Free Exercise). The dividing line is neutrality plus private choice versus direct preference.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the State is secular and independent of religion, so any public benefit touching a church is suspect — or conversely, cooperation with religion is normal” → U.S. doctrine occupies neither pole: it forbids preference while increasingly REQUIRING equal access for religious institutions in neutral programs. Venezuelan constitutional structure guarantees religious freedom alongside a concordat-shaped tradition of state cooperation with the Church (Const. art. 59), so the civilian lacks the non-establishment axis entirely and cannot predict a rule that simultaneously bars aid-because-religious and bars exclusion-because-religious.","authority":"Establishment Clause: Kennedy v. Bremerton Sch. Dist. (2022) (replacing Lemon v. Kurtzman); Zelman v. Simmons-Harris; Trinity Lutheran v. Comer; Carson v. Makin."},{"n":43,"summary":"A State imposes a public hiking easement across private land without paying; a highway widening encroaches a few feet onto a vineyard; a federal statute authorizes surveillance facilities on a tiny unused corner of a large tract with no compensation; a State voids long-term leases to permit extraction.","trigger":"Government permanently occupies, appropriates, or grants public access to private property → per se taking regardless of size or economic impact; just compensation is owed to every affected property interest.","rule":"A permanent physical occupation or appropriation of private property is a per se taking requiring just compensation, however small the intrusion and even if it does not diminish the property's value or interfere with its use (Loretto v. Teleprompter Manhattan CATV). A government-imposed right of public access is likewise a physical taking (Nollan v. California Coastal Comm'n; Cedar Point Nursery v. Hassid). Just compensation is measured by fair market value — the owner's loss, not the government's gain — and must be paid to EVERY property interest affected, including leaseholds and easements held by third parties. Exactions conditioning permits on dedications require an essential nexus and rough proportionality (Nollan; Dolan).","sub":"Takings – Per Se Physical Occupation and Just Compensation (Master)","sensitivity":"Physical invasion short-circuits all balancing: no Penn Central weighing, no de minimis exception, no defense that value was unaffected. The second half is enumerative — identify every interest holder (owner, lessee, easement holder) because each must be compensated.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — expropriation requires prior judicial declaration of public utility and payment of just compensation (Const. art. 115) — is RIGHT and in some respects stronger. The calibration friction: U.S. law reaches INFORMAL and partial occupations that never pass through an expropriation procedure at all — a cable box, a few feet of roadway, a public path — and remedies them through an inverse-condemnation suit brought by the owner, a procedural posture the civilian schema (which assumes the State initiates) does not supply.","authority":"Physical takings: Loretto v. Teleprompter Manhattan CATV; Nollan v. California Coastal Comm'n; Dolan v. City of Tigard; Cedar Point Nursery v. Hassid (2021); compensation to all affected interests."},{"n":44,"summary":"A coastal regulation cuts land value but leaves residual worth; a zoning change destroys a mall's development value; a bird-protection law forbids any permanent structure on a lot bought to build a home.","trigger":"Government REGULATION (no physical invasion) restricting use → Lucas categorical rule if all economically beneficial use is destroyed; otherwise Penn Central balancing of economic impact, investment-backed expectations, and the character of the action.","rule":"A regulation that deprives land of ALL economically beneficial or productive use is a categorical taking requiring compensation (Lucas v. South Carolina Coastal Council), subject to the background-principles exception — no compensation is owed where the prohibited use was already barred by nuisance or property law. Short of total deprivation, the question is resolved by balancing (Penn Central Transportation Co. v. New York City): the economic impact of the regulation on the claimant, the extent to which it interferes with distinct investment-backed expectations, and the character of the governmental action. Diminution in value alone, even substantial, is not a taking; denial of the most profitable use is not a taking; and the parcel is evaluated as a whole rather than by the segment burdened.","sub":"Takings – Regulatory: Lucas and Penn Central","sensitivity":"Total-wipeout is the switch that selects the track: 100% loss → categorical, anything less → three-factor balancing that the government usually wins. Two structural refinements decide close cases: background principles defeat even a total wipeout, and the whole-parcel rule denies claimants the chance to define the deprivation narrowly.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “limitaciones legales a la propiedad are ordinary — property is subject to the restrictions the law imposes for social utility (Const. art. 115), so no compensation is due” → U.S. law recognizes a compensable REGULATORY taking, converting sufficiently severe use restrictions into constructive expropriations. The civilian schema knows expropriation (transfer of title) and legal limitation (uncompensated) as a binary with nothing in between, so the trained reflex has no category for the middle and systematically denies compensation where U.S. doctrine may require it.","authority":"Regulatory takings: Penn Central Transp. Co. v. New York City; Lucas v. South Carolina Coastal Council; Palazzolo v. Rhode Island; Tahoe-Sierra (whole-parcel rule)."},{"n":45,"summary":"A city condemns beachfront houses to convey the land to a developer for a luxury hotel expected to bring jobs, taxes, and tourism; the owners contest the public-use requirement.","trigger":"Exercise of eminent domain transferring property to a PRIVATE recipient → the public-use requirement is satisfied by a public PURPOSE, including economic development, provided just compensation is paid; note state-law limits.","rule":"The Fifth Amendment's public-use requirement is satisfied by a public PURPOSE, which is construed broadly and deferentially: a taking that transfers property to private parties as part of a comprehensive economic-development plan expected to produce jobs, tax revenue, and revitalization serves a public purpose (Kelo v. City of New London; Hawaii Housing Authority v. Midkiff; Berman v. Parker). A taking for the sole purpose of conferring a private benefit on a particular party would fail. Just compensation remains required. Following Kelo, a large majority of States restricted economic-development takings by statute or constitutional amendment — so the federal rule and the practical law of most jurisdictions diverge.","sub":"Takings – Public Use After Kelo","sensitivity":"The tested point is the breadth of 'public use': deference to the legislature's judgment, no requirement that the public physically use the property, and no requirement that the transferee be public. The state-law counter-reform is the honest footnote — the MBE tests the federal rule.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “utilidad pública means the public must actually receive the work — a hotel for a private developer cannot be expropriated for” → U.S. doctrine collapses public use into public purpose and accepts a private transferee as the vehicle. The Venezuelan requirement of a declared utilidad pública o interés social with judicial control (Const. art. 115; Ley de Expropiación) reads as a substantive limit on the beneficiary, so the civilian predicts invalidity in the paradigm case U.S. law upholds — while the post-Kelo state statutes would vindicate that instinct, which is why the divergence must be stated explicitly.","authority":"Public use: Kelo v. City of New London; Hawaii Housing Authority v. Midkiff; Berman v. Parker; post-Kelo state statutory limits."},{"n":46,"summary":"A misdemeanor defendant is denied counsel and a jury and receives a six-month SUSPENDED sentence; another is denied both and sentenced to 60 days' actual jail on a charge carrying a maximum of six months.","trigger":"Petty offense + denial of appointed counsel or jury → two independent thresholds: counsel is required whenever any incarceration is actually IMPOSED (including a suspended sentence); the jury right attaches only to offenses punishable by MORE than six months.","rule":"The Sixth Amendment rights turn on different measures. Appointed COUNSEL is required in any prosecution that actually results in imprisonment, regardless of the offense's classification (Argersinger v. Hamlin; Scott v. Illinois — no actual imprisonment, no right), and a SUSPENDED sentence counts: a court may not impose even a suspended term of incarceration on an uncounseled defendant, because the suspended sentence may later be activated (Alabama v. Shelton). The JURY right attaches only to 'serious' offenses, meaning those punishable by more than six months' imprisonment (Baldwin v. New York; Blanton), measured by the authorized maximum rather than the sentence imposed; aggregated petty offenses do not combine to create the right (Lewis v. United States).","sub":"Sixth Amendment – Counsel and Jury Thresholds","sensitivity":"Two thresholds measured differently: counsel by the sentence ACTUALLY imposed (including suspended), jury by the maximum AUTHORIZED. A defendant can therefore be validly tried without a jury and yet be entitled to counsel — the asymmetry is the point of every question in this family.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “defense counsel is mandatory in every criminal case, and the gravity of the charge determines the tribunal by statute” → U.S. law rations both rights by numerical thresholds, tolerating an uncounseled conviction where no incarceration follows and a judge-alone trial for offenses up to six months. The Venezuelan guarantee of assistance by counsel is unconditional (Const. art. 49(1)) and lay participation via escabinos was a statutory design later abandoned, so the civilian has no schema for a constitutional right that switches on at a sentencing threshold.","authority":"Sixth Amendment thresholds: Argersinger v. Hamlin; Scott v. Illinois; Alabama v. Shelton; Baldwin v. New York; Blanton v. North Las Vegas; Lewis v. United States."},{"n":47,"summary":"A 14-year-old tried as an adult for armed robbery, with no homicide involved, is sentenced to life without the possibility of parole.","trigger":"Juvenile offender + sentence of life without parole → categorically barred for NON-homicide offenses; for homicide, a MANDATORY LWOP scheme is barred and individualized sentencing is required.","rule":"The Eighth Amendment's proportionality principle imposes categorical limits keyed to the offender's youth. Life without parole may not be imposed on a juvenile for a NON-homicide offense (Graham v. Florida). For homicide, a MANDATORY life-without-parole scheme is unconstitutional: the sentencer must have discretion to consider the offender's youth and its attendant characteristics (Miller v. Alabama), and the rule applies retroactively (Montgomery v. Louisiana). The death penalty may not be imposed on offenders who were under 18 at the time of the crime (Roper v. Simmons) or on the intellectually disabled (Atkins v. Virginia).","sub":"Eighth Amendment – Juvenile Sentencing and Categorical Proportionality","sensitivity":"The doctrine works by categories rather than case-by-case proportionality: age of the offender crossed with the class of offense produces a per se rule. Trying the juvenile as an adult does not change the analysis — the constitutional line follows the offender's age at the time of the crime.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — juveniles cannot receive adult perpetual sentences, and perpetual penalties are themselves prohibited — is RIGHT and in fact broader: Const. art. 44(3) bars penalties exceeding thirty years and the LOPNNA caps adolescent deprivation of liberty at far shorter terms. The calibration friction is structural: the U.S. protection is built case by case from a cruel-and-unusual-punishment clause through evolving-standards reasoning, and it operates as a set of discrete categorical exceptions rather than a codified maximum — so the civilian gets the outcome right while missing that the exam tests which category (non-homicide, mandatory, capital) the facts occupy.","authority":"Eighth Amendment: Graham v. Florida; Miller v. Alabama; Montgomery v. Louisiana; Roper v. Simmons; Atkins v. Virginia."}],"cases":[{"n":1,"summary":"City ordinance requires locally made products to say on the label they were manufactured in the city; repeated violations lead to fines and a 7-day shutdown. A local business refuses and is shut down, then sues the city in federal court seeking $15,000 damages for alleged due process violations.","trigger":"Damages claim is brought against a city/municipality; question is whether sovereign immunity bars the suit.","rule":"Cities are not protected from 11th Amendment immunity like states are.","sub":"11th Amendment – Sovereign immunity (municipalities not covered)","family":"Eleventh Amendment","unit":10},{"n":2,"summary":"Court enforces racially restrictive covenant.","trigger":"Judicial enforcement of private discrimination.","rule":"For a 14th Amendment violation, there must be government action. The 13th Amendment prohibits involuntary servitude and can be used even when there is no government action.","sub":"13th vs 14th Amendment","family":"State Action Doctrine","unit":20},{"n":3,"summary":"President appoints all members of advisory agency.","trigger":"Appointment of inferior vs principal officers.","rule":"All principal officers of the United States must be appointed by the President, with the advice and consent of the Senate. If Congress wishes, they may vest the power of appointment of inferior officers to the President alone, Courts of Law, or heads of departments.","sub":"Appointments Clause","family":"Separation of Powers","unit":13},{"n":4,"summary":"Congress recently passed a law that created an agency called “NICE.” The purpose of the agency was to study the effects of deportation on families and to make recommendations to Congress on new regulations concerning deportations. Congress appointed a chairman for the agency and then gave the chairman the power to appoint 8 sub-chair members of the agency. The President has complained that the agency was created under an improper delegation and that the members of the agency were not appointed constitutionally. Which of the following would the court likely find?","trigger":"Officer appointment question; classify as principal vs inferior and identify proper appointing authority.","rule":"Principal officers must be appointed by the President with the advice and consent of the Senate. For inferior officers, Congress may vest the power in the President alone, heads of departments, or courts of law.","sub":"Appointments Clause – Principal vs inferior officers","family":"Separation of Powers","unit":13},{"n":5,"summary":"Congress enacts a law stating that “no federal court shall order” implementation of a school desegregation plan requiring transportation of any student to a school other than the closest or next closest to the student’s residence. Issue is the strongest argument supporting the act’s constitutionality.","trigger":"Congress restricts federal court jurisdiction by statute; Article III power to define/limit jurisdiction.","rule":"Under Article III, Congress may restrict the jurisdiction of lower federal courts (and Supreme Court appellate jurisdiction), so limiting remedial jurisdiction is constitutionally supported.","sub":"Article III – Congressional control of jurisdiction","family":"Judicial Power","unit":8},{"n":6,"summary":"A Senate investigating committee report names three U.S. citizens as helping organize support for terrorist activities, and all three are employed by the federal government as park rangers. Congress enacts a statute naming the three individuals and providing they cannot hold any federal employment position. The individuals challenge the statute’s constitutionality.","trigger":"Legislature names specific individuals and imposes punishment (e.g., disqualification from government employment) without a judicial trial.","rule":"Bill of attainder = legislature names specific individuals and imposes punishment (including disqualifying from government employment) without a judicial trial.","sub":"Bill of Attainder","family":"Article I § 10","unit":31},{"n":7,"summary":"State A requires licensed mechanics for safety-related vehicle repairs. Congress passed a law requiring states to repeal licensing requirements. State A refused. A non-licensed man repaired brakes and was fined under the state law; he argues the state statute should be invalid.","trigger":"Whether Supremacy Clause preemption applies depends on whether the federal law is valid/constitutional.","rule":"The Supremacy Clause will only apply if the federal law is valid. Here, Congress acted within the power of the commerce clause, but comandeered the state, thus not valid and preemption doesn't apply","sub":"Comandeering -Supremacy/Preemption","family":"Federalism","unit":3},{"n":8,"summary":"With rapid growth of internet gambling sites and related fraud claims, Congress enacts the Online Gaming Safety Act creating a federal crime of online gaming fraud. A defendant convicted under the criminal provision argues the conviction is unconstitutional.","trigger":"Congress creates a federal criminal prohibition tied to internet commercial activity; analyze Commerce Clause power.","rule":"Congress may regulate—and criminalize—economic activity and channels/instrumentalities of interstate commerce, including internet‑based commercial conduct, under the Commerce Clause.","sub":"Commerce Clause – Congressional power","family":"Congressional Power","unit":2},{"n":9,"summary":"Congress passed law justified by general welfare concerns","trigger":"Congressional action must be tied to enumerated power","rule":"Congress may legislate only pursuant to an enumerated power; general welfare alone is not a regulatory power","sub":"Congressional Power","family":"Congressional Power","unit":1},{"n":10,"summary":"Federal statute imposes a $100 excise tax on each new computer sold in the U.S. and appropriates the entire proceeds to a special fund to purchase computer‑software licenses that will be made available free of charge to any U.S. resident. Issue is whether the statute is constitutional.","trigger":"Federal excise tax used to fund a nationwide benefit program; Taxing & Spending Clause (general welfare).","rule":"Congress may tax and spend for the general welfare; a uniform excise tax and spending the proceeds for nationwide benefit is constitutional absent another constitutional violation.","sub":"Congressional Power – Taxing & spending","family":"Congressional Power","unit":1},{"n":11,"summary":"Congress regulates future rental contracts; landlord rents single unit in DC.","trigger":"Congress’s plenary power over District of Columbia.","rule":"Congress can pass laws for the general welfare of its citizens in the United States capitol of D.C.","sub":"Congressional Powers – D.C.","family":"Congressional Power","unit":1},{"n":12,"summary":"State diverts employer 401K matching funds","trigger":"Substantial impairment of existing contract","rule":"A state cannot pass a law that substantially impairs an existing contract unless their law is substantially related to a legitimate government interest.","sub":"Contracts Clause","family":"Article I § 10","unit":31},{"n":13,"summary":"A defendant charged with misdemeanor theft (max 6 months) demanded counsel and a jury. The judge denied both and, after a bench trial, imposed a 6‑month suspended sentence (no jail if he stays out of trouble).","trigger":"Right to counsel when a sentence includes incarceration, including a suspended sentence; jury right generally for >6 months.","rule":"The right to a jury usually applies when the D is charged with a crime punishable by more than 6 months of incarceration. If the judge denies a defendant his right to counsel, the judge cannot sentence the defendant to actual jail time, including a suspended sentence.","sub":"Criminal Procedure – Counsel/Jury","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":46},{"n":14,"summary":"A clown is charged with a misdemeanor (max 6 months or $2,000 fine) for writing an insufficient funds check with intent to defraud. At pretrial, he requests counsel and a jury trial; both are denied. He is convicted and sentenced to 60 days in jail.","trigger":"Sixth Amendment rights in a petty offense; actual imprisonment and jury-trial threshold.","rule":"The right to appointed counsel applies when a defendant is actually sentenced to imprisonment; the jury-trial right generally attaches only to “serious” offenses (typically >6 months).","sub":"Criminal Procedure – Right to counsel / jury","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":46},{"n":15,"summary":"State A requires all car batteries sold in-state to have positive terminals colored red and negative terminals colored black (the national standard). An out-of-state battery seller already uses red and green terminals and would spend thousands to change batteries shipped into State A. The seller sues under the Dormant Commerce Clause (DCC).State mandates battery terminal colors.","trigger":"Neutral safety regulation affecting interstate commerce.Non-discriminatory safety regulation that forces an out-of-state seller to change labeling/packaging → DCC Pike balancing (incidental burden vs local benefit).","rule":"A state law that does not discriminate under the dormant commerce clause, will be valid as long as the burden put on commerce does not outweigh the benefit the government is receiving from the law. “Same rule for everyone + safety colors = Pike balancing → usually upheld.”","sub":"Dormant Commerce Clause","family":"Dormant Commerce Clause","unit":18},{"n":16,"summary":"A state with no reported cases of the spotted lanternfly bans all out-of-state shipments of grapes and soybeans because the insect can hitchhike in those products and spread across regions. The products are grown locally, so the ban won’t create a shortage. An out-of-state farmer who has shipped grapes there for decades sues under the Dormant Commerce Clause (DCC).State bans out-of-state produce to prevent pest invasion","trigger":"Facial discrimination under dormant commerce clause","rule":"If a state law discriminates under the dormant commerce clause, it will be invalid unless it is narrowly tailored to a legitimate non-economic state interest. “DCC death” = the law is presumed unconstitutional under the Dormant Commerce Clause because it is facially discriminatory (it blocks out-of-state goods). So the state starts in a “losing position” and can save the law only if it proves both: a legitimate, non-protectionist purpose (e.g., stopping invasive species), and no reasonable, less discriminatory alternative exists (e.g., inspection, quarantine, certification, treatment). If the state cannot prove (2), the law is “dead” under DCC.","sub":"Dormant Commerce Clause","family":"Dormant Commerce Clause","unit":17},{"n":17,"summary":"State X requires that 75% of new guitars sold by large retail stores be made from materials with at least 50% rosewood that is only grown/harvested in State X. Out-of-state manufacturer challenges the law.","trigger":"Dormant Commerce Clause; facial discrimination favoring in-state economic interests.","rule":"A state law that facially discriminates against interstate commerce is presumptively invalid and survives only if the state shows it is necessary to achieve a legitimate local purpose with no reasonable nondiscriminatory alternatives.","sub":"Dormant Commerce Clause – Facial discrimination","family":"Dormant Commerce Clause","unit":17},{"n":18,"summary":"City ordinance forces trucks longer than 50 feet to take an alternate route adding about two hours, saving the city major road-repair costs but increasing costs for a trucking company that only uses 55-foot trucks; competitor using smaller trucks benefits. Company challenges the ordinance under the Dormant Commerce Clause.","trigger":"Nondiscriminatory local regulation that burdens interstate commerce triggers Pike balancing (benefits vs burden).","rule":"A state law that does not discriminate against out of state commerce will be valid if the benefit the state receives from the law is not outweighed by a clearly excessive burden on commerce. Compare the benefit from the law with burden on commerce if burden on commerce is higher its discriminatory","sub":"Dormant Commerce Clause – Pike balancing (incidental burden)","family":"Dormant Commerce Clause","unit":18},{"n":19,"summary":"A state owns and operates an electric power system that includes a nuclear power plant. To ensure disposal sites for spent fuel, the state refuses to supply electric power to out-of-state purchasers residing in states that will not accept spent fuel from the plant for storage or disposal. Assume there is no federal statute.","trigger":"State acting as seller of electricity (state-owned utility) and discriminating against out-of-staters.","rule":"Market-participant exception: when a state acts as a market participant (buyer/seller), its discriminatory choices are not subject to the Dormant Commerce Clause.","sub":"Dormant Commerce Clause / Market Participant Exception","family":"Dormant Commerce Clause","unit":19},{"n":20,"summary":"Minor committed to psychiatric care by parent","trigger":"Parental admission triggers procedural due process","rule":"Procedural due process is satisfied with a post-commitment hearing when a parent of a child places them in psychiatric care since it is presumed that the parent will be acting in the best interest of their child.","sub":"Due Process","family":"Procedural Due Process","unit":28},{"n":21,"summary":"14‑year‑old tried as adult for armed robbery (no homicide) sentenced to life without parole.","trigger":"Juvenile LWOP for non‑homicide offense.","rule":"Juveniles cannot receive life without parole for non‑homicide crimes under the Eighth Amendment.","sub":"Eighth Amendment — Juvenile Sentencing","family":"Eighth Amendment","unit":47},{"n":22,"summary":"A federal statute provides states with funds for child welfare programs conditioned on administering those programs in accordance with federal standards. The United States sues a state in federal court for injunctive relief, alleging the state’s federally funded child welfare programs fail to comply with federal standards. The state moves to dismiss.","trigger":"State argues sovereign immunity/Eleventh Amendment against a federal suit by the United States in federal court.","rule":"11th Amendment doesn’t bar suits by the United States against a state in federal court.","sub":"Eleventh Amendment – Suits by U.S.","family":"Eleventh Amendment","unit":10},{"n":23,"summary":"Out-of-state contractor denied bid due to new licensing law","trigger":"Evenhanded licensing requirement; no fundamental right","rule":"A law that regulates evenhandedly and does not deprive a person of a fundamental right will receive rational basis review.","sub":"Equal Protection","family":"Equal Protection / Substantive Due Process","unit":21},{"n":24,"summary":"A state statute denies a marriage license to anyone who has been divorced more than three times, supposedly to protect the “sanctity of marriage.” Even though statistics show the law impacts mostly white males (80%), the classification is not race/sex—it is marital history (# of divorces)—so the core constitutional problem is that the state is blocking access to marriage, which is a fundamental right.","trigger":"Marriage is a fundamental right intentionally restricted","rule":"When a class is being intentionally discriminated against, it will be an equal protection violation rather than a substantive due process violation.","sub":"Equal Protection","family":"Routing the Claim","unit":26},{"n":25,"summary":"A state passes a law requiring all judges to retire at age 65. The state admits one purpose is cost savings (older judges earn more), and also cites statistics showing many people have major cognitive/physical decline around 65. A judge about to turn 65—who is fit and cognitively strong—is told to retire and sues under the 14th Amendment Equal Protection Clause, arguing the law unfairly forces her out despite her individual ability.","trigger":"Age classification reviewed under rational basis. Mandatory retirement at age 65” → age classification + Equal Protection challenge","rule":"With rational basis, the law may be overinclusive and still be valid since it doesn’t have to be narrowly tailored to the government interest. Age is not a suspect (or quasi-suspect) classification, so an age-based retirement rule gets rational-basis review and will be upheld if it is rationally related to a legitimate government interest (like ensuring competent judges), even if it’s overinclusive.","sub":"Equal Protection","family":"Equal Protection / Substantive Due Process","unit":21},{"n":26,"summary":"Non-citizen denied game warden position","trigger":"Politically sensitive position","rule":"A state can discriminate against aliens under the rational basis standard if it involves a politically sensitive position.","sub":"Equal Protection","family":"Equal Protection","unit":23},{"n":27,"summary":"Intestacy law barred unacknowledged illegitimate child from proving parentage and inheriting","trigger":"Classification based on illegitimacy","rule":"Illegitimacy classifications trigger intermediate scrutiny under equal protection","sub":"Equal Protection","family":"Equal Protection","unit":24},{"n":28,"summary":"A state law requires all homeowners to post a sign on their property one month before an election stating which political party they will vote for. The state’s stated goal is preventing election fraud by comparing sign counts to election results. A married couple wants to vote for different parties and challenges the law as violating multiple constitutional rights. The question asks: what is the most likely reason to strike it down?","trigger":"Law targets a specific class of people. Class of people discriminated: \"Homeowners\" - NON SUSPECT CLASSIFICATION - BUT it's bumped up to STRICT SCRUTINY, because a fundamental right is involved - Speech","rule":"When a law only affects a class of people, the law will be challenged under the Equal Protection Clause, even if it is abridging a fundamental right.","sub":"Equal Protection","family":"Routing the Claim","unit":26},{"n":29,"summary":"The federal government is hiring drivers for a new post office in State A. Applicants for driver jobs must submit certified DMV records and, if selected as finalists, take a rigorous driving test administered by the federal government. Applicants for driver: 200 (120 men, 80 women). DMV records show moving-violation points: ~90% of men vs 20% of women.Finalists: 80 total (40 men, 40 women). Failures on driving test: 44 failed, including 39 men and 5 women. A man who failed sues claiming gender discrimination.Male applicant challenges gender disparity from neutral driving test.","trigger":"Disparate impact without discriminatory intent.","rule":"Before an equal protection violation can be shown, there must be intent to discriminate on the part of the government. A disparate impact on a certain class, without intent to discriminate, will not be a violation. Disparate impact (men fail at much higher rate) without evidence of discriminatory intent → Equal Protection claim fails.","sub":"Equal Protection – Disparate Impact","family":"Equal Protection","unit":25},{"n":30,"summary":"Federal law bars non–US-born applicants from federal office.","trigger":"Federal alienage discrimination.","rule":"Because there is no Equal Protection Clause that applies to the federal government, the Supreme Court has held that the Due Process Clause of the 5th Amendment applies to the federal government when they are discriminating. When the federal government discriminates based on alienage, the law receives rational basis review.","sub":"Equal Protection – Federal Alienage","family":"Equal Protection","unit":23},{"n":31,"summary":"A state-run police academy has an admissions policy that categorically excludes women. In the first year, 27 women and 352 men applied. The academy accepted 140 men and 0 women. Each woman received a denial letter stating: “all first-year academy students must be born male.” The academy justifies the policy by saying women are less likely to withstand the job’s physical requirements. A denied woman sues in state court under the Constitution (Equal Protection). She is in peak physical condition and likely could outperform many accepted men.Police academy excludes all women from admission.","trigger":"Gender-based classification by state actor. NO STEROTYPES Facial gender classification (“must be born male”) by a state actor → Intermediate scrutiny applies → government bears burden.","rule":"With gender discrimination, the burden is on the government to meet intermediate scrutiny, which means they traditionally had the burden to show that the law is substantially related to an important government interest. However, the Supreme Court held that gender discrimination must pass the enhanced scrutiny test where the government must show that they have a clearly persuasive justification for the discrimination.","sub":"Equal Protection – Gender","family":"Equal Protection","unit":22},{"n":32,"summary":"State-run nursing school opens and limits admission to women only to remedy past discrimination; a male applicant is denied solely because he is male and sues under the Equal Protection Clause.","trigger":"Government (state) creates a gender-based classification in admissions (women-only public program).","rule":"When a state discriminates based on gender, a quasi-suspect class, the state must prove that they have a clearly persuasive justification for their restriction.","sub":"Equal Protection – Gender classification (intermediate scrutiny / exceedingly persuasive justification)","family":"Equal Protection","unit":22},{"n":33,"summary":"A city creates an esthetics program within its community college with a charter stating the purpose is to support women’s achievements in professional skin care. A male student is denied admission based on the charter and sues, alleging a Fourteenth Amendment violation.","trigger":"Government gender-based classification; apply intermediate scrutiny and require an exceedingly persuasive justification.","rule":"Gender classifications must be supported by an exceedingly persuasive justification and must be substantially related to an important governmental interest.","sub":"Equal Protection – Gender discrimination","family":"Equal Protection","unit":22},{"n":34,"summary":"A man is the illegitimate, unacknowledged child of his father, who died intestate leaving no spouse and no other children. State intestacy law bars an unacknowledged illegitimate child from inheriting and instead prefers spouse/other blood relatives (and even the state) over him. The man challenges the statute and seeks to be declared the lawful heir.","trigger":"State inheritance rule discriminates against nonmarital (illegitimate) children; Equal Protection challenge.","rule":"Discrimination against illegitimate children triggers heightened scrutiny (typically intermediate) under Equal Protection; on a multiple-choice, pick the option that frames the challenge as lack of adequate governmental justification for illegitimacy discrimination.","sub":"Equal Protection – Illegitimacy (intermediate scrutiny)","family":"Equal Protection","unit":24},{"n":35,"summary":"City enacted ordinance requiring upgraded rental housing features. Low‑income landlords sued alleging economic harm.","trigger":"Economic regulation affecting housing profits.","rule":"Economic and social welfare regulations receive rational basis review; plaintiff bears burden to negate any legitimate state interest.","sub":"Equal Protection — Rational Basis","family":"Equal Protection / Substantive Due Process","unit":21},{"n":36,"summary":"A lawful U.S. resident who is Greek lives in Freedonia. As U.S.–Greece tensions rise, Congress passes a federal law requiring all Greek people to register any handguns with their local states. The worker challenges the law.Federal law targeted Greeks for handgun registration","trigger":"Federal classification challenged as equal protection","rule":"Equal protection limits on federal action are enforced through the Fifth Amendment Due Process Clause. REMEMBER GABY THERE IS NO SUCH THING AS A VIOLATION OF THE 5TH AMENDMENT EQUAL PROTECTION CLAUSE.","sub":"Equal Protection (Federal)","family":"Equal Protection","unit":23},{"n":37,"summary":"State employer excluded childbirth benefits from employee insurance; pregnant employee challenged as discriminatory","trigger":"Equal protection challenge to pregnancy-related benefit exclusion","rule":"A law that discriminates against pregnant people does not specifically discriminate against women.","sub":"Equal Protection (Pregnancy)","family":"Equal Protection","unit":22},{"n":38,"summary":"City gives education checks usable at religious schools.","trigger":"Neutral aid program reaching religious schools indirectly.","rule":"The test for determining an Establishment Clause violation has changed to whether the government action would violate the history and tradition of what the founding fathers intended.","sub":"Establishment Clause","family":"Establishment Clause","unit":42},{"n":39,"summary":"Public school donates leftover lunches to church","trigger":"Establishment Clause Lemon test analysis","rule":"To see if the Establishment Clause is violated, we look to see if there is a secular purpose for the government action, if the primary effect of the action is to promote or inhibit religion, and whether there would be excessive government entanglement with religion.","sub":"Establishment Clause","family":"Establishment Clause","unit":42},{"n":40,"summary":"State spending program buys textbooks for secular schools and bibles for religious schools. A taxpayer with no other connection sues claiming an Establishment Clause violation. Question asks the key factor the court uses to evaluate the claim.","trigger":"Establishment Clause analysis uses the modern 'history and tradition' approach rather than the Lemon test.","rule":"The court has changed the traditional Lemon test to determining if the Establishment Clause is violated by looking at historical practices and understandings. Under the lwmon test the courts look in the facts if there is a non secular purpose and the primary effect is to advance religion.","sub":"Establishment Clause – History and tradition / post-Lemon approach","family":"Establishment Clause","unit":42},{"n":41,"summary":"State increases max sentences by 2 years for drug-sale crimes; defendant was arrested before change but sentenced under new law; appeals as unconstitutional.","trigger":"Ex post facto: retroactively increases punishment/sentencing after crime committed.","rule":"An ex post facto law (unconstitutional) works retroactively to make something a defendant already did a crime or changes sentencing after a crime has been committed.","sub":"Ex Post Facto","family":"Article I § 10","unit":31},{"n":42,"summary":"State judge issues habeas writ for federal prisoner","trigger":"State court interference with federal custody","rule":"A state court cannot interfere with individuals in federal custody by issuing a habeas corpus writ.","sub":"Federalism","family":"Federal Supremacy","unit":12},{"n":43,"summary":"Congress, seeking to ensure access to public transportation, passed a law that sought to regulate the maximum prices that buses could charge customers. Congress passed a law that stated, “All transportation charges on any bus, public or private, are subject to the following maximum charges…” The law then went on to give formulas on how to calculate the maximum charges. State A, who had both state run and privately run bus companies in their state, challenged the law as being excessively intrusive on the state’s sovereignty.","trigger":"Federal statute regulates states only vs regulates both states and private actors (commandeering).","rule":"A federal law is only commandeering if it regulates just the state, not if it is regulating the state and private citizens as well.","sub":"Federalism – Anti‑commandeering","family":"Federalism","unit":3},{"n":44,"summary":"Congress enacts a federal law that attempts to require a state legislature or state officials to enact or administer a federal regulatory program of limited duration to reduce water pollution. The state challenges the law as unconstitutional because it forces the state to implement federal policy rather than regulating individuals directly.","trigger":"Federal law compels state to enact/administer federal program (commandeering).","rule":"If the federal government tries to force the state to enact a law, the federal law will be struck down as unconstitutional commandeering. It doesn’t matter if the federal law comands even for a limited duration of time it would be comandeering. The 10th amendment does not give states inmunity from all direct federal regulations. If the feds pass a law making the state pass a law thats comandeering. But, if the federal goverment passes a law that everybody has to follow and it does not make the state pass a law this would not be comandeering. If congress wants states to follow law make a federal law that preempts state law or (preemption) or use the spending power","sub":"Federalism — Anti-Commandeering Doctrine","family":"Federalism","unit":3},{"n":45,"summary":"Town ordinance bars hiring citizens of an “aggressor” foreign country into public health/safety jobs (police, fire, EMT). A resident citizen of that country who fled to avoid military service wants to work as an EMT and challenges the ordinance.","trigger":"State/local regulation targeting foreign nationals tied to foreign conflict; federal foreign-affairs preemption.","rule":"State and local laws that intrude into foreign affairs or conflict with the federal government’s exclusive authority over foreign relations can be unconstitutional under foreign-affairs preemption.","sub":"Federalism – Foreign affairs preemption","family":"Federalism","unit":6},{"n":46,"summary":"National Park Service contracts with an independent antiques collector to buy items owned by city residents during a former U.S. president’s lifetime and resell them to the Park Service at cost plus 10%. State law ordinarily imposes a sales tax on purchases by antiques collectors; the collector sues in state court to enjoin collection of the sales tax as unconstitutional as applied to these purchases.","trigger":"State sales tax applied to private contractor selling to/for the federal government; intergovernmental tax immunity.","rule":"A nondiscriminatory state tax on a private party’s sales to the federal government is generally valid (intergovernmental tax immunity bars taxes on the U.S. itself or discriminatory taxes).","sub":"Federalism – Intergovernmental tax immunity","family":"Federalism","unit":5},{"n":47,"summary":"State requires lawnmowers sold in-state to meet minimum fuel efficiency; later federal statute requires energy-efficiency labeling for power equipment; no express preemption.","trigger":"Federal preemption analysis: conflict vs field; federal labeling law vs state minimum standard.","rule":"A state law survives if it neither conflicts with federal law nor is preempted by Congress’s intent to occupy the field.","sub":"Federalism – Preemption","family":"Federalism","unit":4},{"n":48,"summary":"Congress compels states to give tax incentives","trigger":"Federal law commandeers state legislature","rule":"If Congress passes a law that requires a state to pass a law, the federal law will be considered unconstitutional as commandeering.","sub":"Federalism (Commandeering)","family":"Federalism","unit":3},{"n":49,"summary":"Congress passed a federal law that requires each state to pass new regulations to determine the citizenship of residents. Congress says it’s to address illegal entry, protect jobs, and reduce crime. State A sues challenging the law.Congress forces states to enact citizenship regulations.","trigger":"Federal government commandeering state regulatory process. If congress regulates States and people YES","rule":"Even if Congress has the power to regulate, if they force the states to regulate, the federal law will be struck down as commandeering.","sub":"Federalism (Commandeering)","family":"Federalism","unit":3},{"n":50,"summary":"Congress passed a federal law requiring states to pass legislation banning customers from pumping their own gas and requiring attendants to pump gas, justified by safety data (fires/explosions; $20M property damage). State A challenges the law (it has many private gas stations and also runs a few state-operated stations as a market participant).","trigger":"Federal law compels states to enact legislation","rule":"If the federal government tries to force the state to enact a law, the federal law will be struck down as unconstitutional commandeering. Even if Congress can regulate the underlying activity under an enumerated power, Congress cannot compel states to enact or enforce federal regulatory policy (anti-commandeering). Therefore the law is unconstitutional. Commerce Cause + N&PC: channels, instrumentalities and acts that substantially affect commerce. If a law regulate private individuals and the state it's not comandeering, because private citizens have political recourse.","sub":"Federalism (Commandeering)","family":"Federalism","unit":3},{"n":51,"summary":"Congress passed the Safety in the Workplace Act requiring drug tests for employees operating dangerous machinery: at hiring and randomly every three months. State A runs multiple affected businesses—some as a market participant and others as the state government—and sues arguing unconstitutional commandeering.","trigger":"Federal law imposes uniform drug-testing duties on both private employers and state-run employers.","rule":"A federal law will not be commandeering if it regulates both private and public actors. Test to use when Congress passes a law regulating an area: 1) Does Congress have the power?, 2) Is Congress comandeering the state?","sub":"Federalism (Commandeering)","family":"Federalism","unit":3},{"n":52,"summary":"Two competitors flood highway exits with billboards. Residents complain the clutter distracts drivers and blocks sightlines. City responds with an ordinance regulating billboard size and requiring minimum spacing. The company sues, claiming a First Amendment violation.","trigger":"Content-neutral time, place, manner regulation of Commercial Speech","rule":"A content neutral time, place, manner restriction will receive intermediate scrutiny, meaning the government has the burden to show that their law is narrowly tailored to a substantial government interest and leaves reasonable alternative methods of communication.","sub":"First Amendment - Commercial Speech","family":"First Amendment","unit":34},{"n":53,"summary":"A man hands out flyers on a sidewalk in front of a business district advertising that, although he has not graduated law school and is not licensed, he can help people like an attorney for less. Practicing law without a license is illegal in the jurisdiction. After attorneys complain, the prosecutor tells him to stop or face prosecution; the man argues he is engaging in protected commercial speech.","trigger":"Commercial speech is regulated where the speech proposes/relates to illegal activity (unlicensed practice of law).","rule":"For commercial speech to be protected, it must relate to lawful activity. Under the Central Hudson test elements: LSD - Overdose: L (LAWFUL) the speech has to be lawful, S (SUBSTANTIAL) the law that regulates commercial speech must relate to a subsantial goverment interest, D (DIRECTLY) the law that regulates commercial speech must directly advance that interest, and OVERDOSE, the law cannot be overbroad.","sub":"First Amendment – Commercial speech (lawful activity requirement)","family":"First Amendment","unit":37},{"n":54,"summary":"Scientific studies link red meat consumption to cancer. Congress passes a federal statute prohibiting all commercial advertising of red meat products, while still allowing the products to be sold. Red meat producers challenge the statute as violating the First Amendment protection for commercial speech.","trigger":"Federal statute bans commercial advertising of a lawful product (commercial speech restriction).","rule":"A law is not narrowly tailored if it is more extensive than necessary to serve the government’s interests. The law is banning commercial speech on its face","sub":"First Amendment — Commercial Speech / Narrow Tailoring","family":"First Amendment","unit":37},{"n":55,"summary":"An anti-antisemitism group sought a city permit to demonstrate in a public park and show graphic videos of killings tied to current events in Israel. The city denied the permit, while allowing other groups (anti-abortion, anti-Biden, labor strike, etc.) to demonstrate the same day. The city justified the denial by saying the violent videos would scare children, and there was a large youth soccer tournament in the park that day.","trigger":"Content-based restriction on speech","rule":"A content based law will receive strict scrutiny, meaning that the burden is on the government to show that the law is narrowly tailored to a compelling government interest.","sub":"First Amendment - Content Based","family":"First Amendment","unit":32},{"n":56,"summary":"The city’s Capitol building area is a common spot for rallies. Crowds have created traffic/safety problems. The city passes an ordinance that only protesters criticizing the President’s immigration stance at the Capitol may protest only after 5:00 PM (to reduce traffic/safety issues). A man protests at 3:00 PM and is ticketed. He challenges the ordinance.","trigger":"Content-based restriction on speech in public forum.","rule":"If the government singles out speech based on its topic in a public forum, the regulation survives only if it passes strict scrutiny (compelling interest + narrowly tailored). Under strict scrutiny, the city must prove compelling safety need and that limiting only immigration protests to after 5 PM is the least restrictive way—hard because the city could achieve safety with content-neutral limits (crowd size/permits/time limits for all rallies).","sub":"First Amendment - Content based","family":"First Amendment","unit":32},{"n":57,"summary":"A veteran at a rally in a public park accepts a small U.S. flag from a reporter and burns it while chanting a political message. Police cite him under a law banning starting a fire in a public place without a permit. He claims the law violates free speech.","trigger":"Expressive conduct (speech/chanting politcal message + conduct/burning flag) regulated by neutral fire ordinance","rule":"Expressive conduct, which is speech mixed with conduct, can be regulated under an intermediate scrutiny standard if the law is not content based. A content-neutral regulation that incidentally burdens expressive conduct is constitutional if it satisfies intermediate scrutiny (O’Brien): it furthers an important/substantial government interest unrelated to suppressing expression and is narrowly tailored (no greater than necessary). → Likely constitutional here because fire-safety/permit requirements are unrelated to suppressing speech and can be applied even to expressive flag burning.","sub":"First Amendment - Content Neutral Regulation that incidentally burdens expressive conduct","family":"First Amendment","unit":33},{"n":58,"summary":"Thousands of protesters gathered at a state Capitol. One protester burned a U.S. flag and was arrested/prosecuted under a state statute that makes it illegal to burn a flag “as part of a protest” when more than 100 people are gathered. The protester claims others were burning other items and were not arrested, and argues he was singled out either because of his ethnicity or because he burned a flag.","trigger":"Law targets speech based on message.","rule":"A law is content based and will receive strict scrutiny review if it prohibits speech based on what the message being communicated is.","sub":"First Amendment – Content-based","family":"First Amendment","unit":32},{"n":59,"summary":"During a teacher labor strike, teachers picket outside schools; city passes an ordinance requiring all labor-strike picketers to stay 250 feet away from schools while school is in session to avoid disturbing children; teachers sue under the First Amendment.","trigger":"Law restricts speech based on the message/topic (targets labor-strike picketing) → content-based regulation. The ordinance specifically targets \"all labor-strike picketers\" and NOT ALL PICKETERS so for this reason is CONTENT BASED. This is called singling out who can and who cannot. they try to trick that it's content neutral","rule":"Stopping speech based on the message being shared makes a law content based, which will mean the law will receive strict scrutiny. In an essay 1st determine the constitutional provision, here its a city so the it's the 1st amendment through the14th amendment due process clause. When you see picketing or leafliting look at the specific message being stopped. Is it fighting word, or any unprotected speech. Here, the mesage was \"Equal pay for equal work\". Then look at the place where the speech is taking place. Here, sidewalk is a traditional public forum. Is it content based or content neutral. Here, its content based","sub":"First Amendment – Content-based regulation (strict scrutiny)","family":"First Amendment","unit":32},{"n":60,"summary":"State law requires auction of vacant public school buildings; school board proposes special law to sell building below fair market value to a church for its school.","trigger":"Government transfer of property at below-market value specifically to a church (no competitive bidding).","rule":"A government action that provides special financial benefit to a religious institution may violate the Establishment Clause (favoring religion).","sub":"First Amendment — Establishment Clause","family":"Establishment Clause","unit":42},{"n":61,"summary":"A well‑known controversial artist holds a public gallery event with sexually graphic paintings. After complaints, a police officer and the prosecutor believe one painting may be obscene and demand it be removed or the artist will be prosecuted. The artist refuses and is charged with displaying obscene material; the question asks the burden of proof for obscenity.","trigger":"In a criminal obscenity prosecution, the government must prove every element of obscenity beyond a reasonable doubt.","rule":"The burden is on the prosecution to prove all elements of obscenity beyond a reasonable doubt. Obscenity is unprotected speech only if it meets Miller (3-part test): (1) Appeals to prurient interest (community standards), (2) Patently offensive depiction of sex (community standards), (3) Lacks serious SLAP value SERIOUS, LITERARY, ARTISTIC, POLITICAL OR SCIENTIFIC VALUE.","sub":"First Amendment – Obscenity (burden of proof)","family":"First Amendment","unit":38},{"n":62,"summary":"State A bans in-person or live lawyer solicitation. A lawyer visits a jailed DUI arrestee (friend-of-a-friend) and offers free representation. Client wins at trial. Coworkers later gift lawyer $2,000. State charges lawyer under the anti-solicitation law; lawyer claims the law is unconstitutional.","trigger":"Law sweeps more speech than necessary.Lawyer solicits in person / live contact” (jail visit, real-time pitch) → commercial speech + attorney solicitation rule.","rule":"A law is overbroad if it prohibits more speech than necessary to achieve the government interest. “Total ban” on lawyer solicitation by in-person/live communication (sweeps in too much, including non-coercive / beneficial contact) → overbreadth.","sub":"First Amendment - Overbreadth","family":"First Amendment","unit":37},{"n":63,"summary":"State bans all leafletting on public streets/sidewalks within 500 feet of any casino to reduce litter. Adult entertainment company (70% of leafletting) challenges the law; flyers show non-nude lingerie images advertising strip clubs/escort services.","trigger":"Government restricts leafletting in public forum near casinos to reduce litter","rule":"A law is not narrowly tailored if it is more extensive than necessary to serve the government’s interests. Even with a significant interest like preventing litter, the government cannot impose a sweeping ban on leafletting in a public forum; the restriction must be narrowly tailored (reasonable fit) and leave ample alternatives—so an overinclusive ban gets enjoined.","sub":"First Amendment - Overbreadth","family":"First Amendment","unit":35},{"n":64,"summary":"Before upcoming elections, protests in a public park became frequent and disruptive. In response, the city enacted a law banning all First Amendment activity in the park for a period of time, aimed at reducing disruption but sweeping in peaceful speech as well.","trigger":"Law restricts substantially more protected speech than necessary to achieve the government interest → overbreadth problem.","rule":"A law is overbroad if it stops more speech than necessary to accomplish a government interest.","sub":"First Amendment – Overbreadth","family":"First Amendment","unit":35},{"n":65,"summary":"A city ordinance bans “all First Amendment activities” after 5:00 p.m. at one public park due to noise complaints, while other parks nearby remain open. Teens talking and joking loudly in the park after 5:00 are ticketed.","trigger":"Overbroad restriction on protected speech","rule":"A law that stops more speech than necessary to accomplish its objective is overbroad since it is not narrowly tailored to the government interest.","sub":"First Amendment - Overbreadth","family":"First Amendment","unit":35},{"n":66,"summary":"Student wears a Star of David necklace for style; gang adopts the symbol leading to escalating violence including deaths and daily fights at school; school bans possession of any Star of David on school property; parents challenge as unconstitutional.","trigger":"Regulation prohibits a broad category of expressive symbols and may sweep more speech than necessary for safety goals (overbreadth doctrine).","rule":"A law is overbroad if it stops more speech than necessary to achieve the government interest.","sub":"First Amendment – Overbreadth (restriction sweeps too broadly)","family":"First Amendment","unit":35},{"n":67,"summary":"City owns and operates a municipal bus system and sells advertising space on bus placards. Administrator refuses to sell space to an animal-rights group for a paid placard with photos criticizing circuses because it would offend a circus that pays for many ads; the group sues under the First Amendment.","trigger":"Government creates a designated public forum and then restricts speech based on content; assess scrutiny and whether time/place/manner limits are properly tailored.","rule":"In a designated public forum, content-based limits require strict scrutiny, and content-neutral time/place/manner limits must be narrowly tailored and leave ample alternatives.","sub":"First Amendment – Public forums (designated forum; content-based strict scrutiny)","family":"First Amendment","unit":36},{"n":68,"summary":"Airline charged under state law penalizing any false/misleading public statement; airline repeated erroneous safety-record info from a nonprofit website.","trigger":"Statute punishes any false/misleading statement with no fault requirement; First Amendment challenge.","rule":"A speech regulation that sweeps in protected speech and lacks narrow tailoring (e.g., no fault element) is vulnerable as overbroad.","sub":"First Amendment – Speech (Overbreadth)","family":"First Amendment","unit":35},{"n":69,"summary":"Supporters of an indicted crime boss stand on courthouse grounds during jury deliberations holding signs reading “Death to Jurors Who Convict.” A statute criminalizes expressive conduct intended to influence court proceedings during courtroom hours on or adjacent to courthouse grounds. A supporter is prosecuted under the statute.","trigger":"First Amendment challenge to speech restrictions on courthouse grounds aimed at influencing proceedings.","rule":"The government may impose reasonable, content-neutral time, place, and manner restrictions in a nonpublic/limited public forum like courthouse grounds to protect judicial proceedings, so long as the restriction is reasonable and viewpoint neutral.","sub":"First Amendment – Speech near courthouses; forum","family":"First Amendment","unit":36},{"n":70,"summary":"A protester opposed a cashless society and, in a public park, briefly lit a pile of one‑dollar bills on fire while holding a sign and chanting political messages. He was ticketed for violating a content-neutral permit rule requiring permits for fires in public parks. He challenges as a First Amendment violation.","trigger":"Content‑neutral time, place, and manner restriction in a traditional public forum; permit requirement can be upheld if properly tailored.","rule":"Content neutral time, place, manner restrictions in a public forum can be upheld.","sub":"First Amendment – Time Place and Manner","family":"First Amendment","unit":33},{"n":71,"summary":"Local government restricts expressive activity (like picketing or leafleting) based on neutral criteria such as location/time, claiming public safety or traffic control. Challenger argues the restriction burdens speech too much.","trigger":"Content-neutral time/place/manner restrictions must be narrowly tailored to an important interest and leave adequate alternative channels.","rule":"A content-neutral time/place/manner restriction must be narrowly tailored to an important interest and leave adequate alternative channels; if not, it’s unconstitutional.","sub":"First Amendment – Time, place, and manner (content-neutral)","family":"First Amendment","unit":34},{"n":72,"summary":"A state criminal law prohibits publishing any description of the details of an execution of any prisoner sentenced to death. Although a specified number of reporters may observe an execution, only the prison warden’s official statement that the prisoner was executed as provided by law at a certain time and date may be published. After an execution was mishandled and the prisoner suffered, a newspaper published a detailed story written by a reporter who was permitted to observe the execution and did not promise officials he would report only the warden’s statement. A prosecutor filed charges against the newspaper for publishing the details in violation of the state law.","trigger":"Criminal sanctions for publishing lawfully obtained truthful information about an execution (content-based restriction on protected speech).","rule":"Punishing publication of lawfully obtained truthful information on a matter of public significance violates the First Amendment absent adequate justification (law must satisfy strict scrutiny; overbroad/content-based restrictions are unconstitutional).","sub":"First Amendment / Freedom of Speech (content-based restriction, overbreadth)","family":"First Amendment","unit":39},{"n":73,"summary":"Influential businessman in a small city prints and distributes thousands of political T-shirts and banners: “It’s time for a change. Vote for a new mayor.” Mayor threatens him: “Good luck filling that banquet hall… take down the banners and stop with the t-shirts.” Businessman refuses; mayor wins re-election. Mayor then changes city traditions: moves holiday events to another park and switches city events to a new banquet hall in a neighboring city (costs city 25% more). These changes economically hurt the businessman’s restaurant and banquet hall business.","trigger":"Government retaliation against contractor/business for political speech/association.","rule":"First Amendment rights apply to government contractors in the same manner as they do for government employees. Government officials cannot penalize employees and contractors because of their political beliefs unless they are relevant to job performance.","sub":"First Amendment / Retaliation","family":"First Amendment","unit":40},{"n":74,"summary":"Congress enacted a statute making it a federal crime for any state official to conspire with other state officials or private individuals to deny the right to equal access to state facilities (including schools, courts, sports or convention facilities, hospitals, or agencies). The statute also authorizes damages against conspirators in favor of individuals whose equal protection rights are infringed. The question asks whether this conspiracy statute is constitutional.","trigger":"Federal statute targets a conspiracy involving state officials to deny equal access to state facilities (equal protection deprivation); analyze Congress’s §5 enforcement power.","rule":"Under §5 of the Fourteenth Amendment, Congress may enact appropriate legislation to enforce the Equal Protection Clause, including prohibiting conspiracies involving state actors to deprive individuals of equal protection.","sub":"Fourteenth Amendment – §5 Enforcement Power","family":"Fourteenth Amendment","unit":7},{"n":75,"summary":"Without a warrant, a police officer searched garbage cans in the public alley behind a man’s house and found meth-making chemicals, utensils, and containers with the man’s fingerprints. The alley was a public thoroughfare maintained by the city. The garbage was picked up weekly by a private sanitation company. Items were inside garbage cans and tied plastic bags further secured with tape. The man was charged in federal court with manufacturing meth.","trigger":"Fourth Amendment “search” requires a reasonable expectation of privacy; garbage left in a public place (alley/curb) is exposed to third parties.","rule":"There is no reasonable expectation of privacy in garbage left for collection in a public area (e.g., an alley/curb), so police inspection is not a Fourth Amendment search.","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Reasonable Expectation of Privacy (Garbage)","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":null},{"n":76,"summary":"State, citing political unrest, bans all political expenditures. A corporation that donates to a campaign-fraud research group challenges the ban as unconstitutional.","trigger":"State restricts political spending/expenditures (political speech) even when the speaker is a corporation.","rule":"Political expenditures, even for corporations, are rights protected under the First Amendment freedom of speech clause of the United States Constitution. Corporations don’t have: 1) No 5th amendment right against self incrimination, 2) No -P& Inmunity clause.","sub":"Freedom of Speech – Corporations Political Speech","family":"First Amendment","unit":41},{"n":77,"summary":"State attempts to tax IRS property","trigger":"State taxation of federal agency","rule":"The federal government, including its agencies, is immune from taxation by the states.","sub":"Intergovernmental Immunity","family":"Federalism","unit":5},{"n":78,"summary":"Federal childcare agency selling goods in state; state imposes gross receipts tax on merchandise sales","trigger":"State tax imposed on a federal agency’s sales","rule":"A state cannot tax the federal government or any of its agencies.","sub":"Intergovernmental Immunity","family":"Federalism","unit":5},{"n":79,"summary":"Congress expands Supreme Court original jurisdiction","trigger":"Alteration of Supreme Court jurisdiction","rule":"Congress can make changes to the Supreme Court’s appellate jurisdiction, but not to its original jurisdiction.","sub":"Judicial Power - Jurisdiction","family":"Judicial Power","unit":8},{"n":80,"summary":"Congress removes Supreme Court jurisdiction over ambassador defamation.","trigger":"Congress altering Supreme Court original jurisdiction.","rule":"Although Congress can make exceptions to the Supreme Court’s appellate jurisdiction, they cannot make any changes to the Supreme Court’s original jurisdiction, which includes cases affecting ambassadors.","sub":"Judicial Power – Jurisdiction","family":"Judicial Power","unit":8},{"n":81,"summary":"Whether the case is moot when the plaintiff seeks only an injunction and the challenged government action (the contract work) has already been completed. IRS (federal agency) hires an outside firm one time to process 100,000 2022 tax returns due to COVID staffing. Two finalists; IRS chooses the other firm because it can process faster. Losing firm sues IRS for gender discrimination (head is a woman). Only remedy requested: injunction. By the time the case goes to trial, the chosen firm has already completed the 100,000 returns.","trigger":"Only injunctive relief sought; issue resolved","rule":"A case is moot if all its issues have been resolved by the time it is set to be heard.","sub":"Justiciability","family":"Justiciability","unit":11},{"n":82,"summary":"City bans all yard signs; homeowner fined.","trigger":"Municipal ordinance violating federal speech rights.","rule":"The 11th Amendment immunity applies to states, but not cities or local municipalities.","sub":"Justiciability – 11th Amendment","family":"Eleventh Amendment","unit":10},{"n":83,"summary":"Nightclub owner seeks injunction to stop city council from considering residents’ lobbying against a permit, alleging First/14th violations if lobbying succeeds.","trigger":"Suit based on conditional future harm (“if” something happens); no present denial or imminent enforcement.","rule":"A claim is not ripe unless there is present harm or an imminent threat of harm; speculative, contingent injuries are not justiciable.","sub":"Justiciability — Ripeness","family":"Justiciability","unit":11},{"n":84,"summary":"State legislature authorizes a state agency to establish five new state‑owned toxic waste landfills and decide sites later after investigation. A community scheduled for inspection sues in federal court for declaratory and injunctive relief to stop selection of their community before any site is chosen.","trigger":"Federal courts require an actual or imminent injury; declaratory/injunctive relief is dismissed as unripe when harm is speculative and would be advisory.","rule":"Federal courts cannot issue advisory opinions; claims for declaratory/injunctive relief are dismissed as unripe absent actual or imminent injury.","sub":"Justiciability – Ripeness / advisory opinions","family":"Justiciability","unit":11},{"n":85,"summary":"City passes law: all vehicles must have a locking gas cap to stop gas siphoning. Many cars don’t have one; adding a lock costs about $75. Plaintiff sues in federal court under equal protection, arguing disproportionate burden on poor people. City moves to dismiss. Before the court rules, city repeals the law and adopts an emergency measure: free gas-cap locks for people who can’t afford them.","trigger":"Voluntary cessation makes case moot if no live controversy remains at time of decision.","rule":"A case is moot and will be dismissed if there is no longer a case or controversy at the time the lawsuit is ready to be heard.","sub":"Justiciability / Mootness","family":"Justiciability","unit":11},{"n":86,"summary":"Congress caps trans fat content in margarine at 5% or less. New Jersey sets a stricter 2% cap and prosecutes a manufacturer whose product is 4% (after reformulation). Manufacturer sues in federal court challenging enforcement; question asks state’s weakest argument.","trigger":"Preemption/supremacy analysis where state law is stricter than federal regulation.","rule":"The Supremacy Clause is not itself a source of state regulatory power; a state’s defense must rely on police powers and absence of preemption, not “valid pursuant to the Supremacy Clause.”","sub":"Preemption – Supremacy Clause / state regulation","family":"Federalism","unit":4},{"n":87,"summary":"Spending bill gave Comptroller General discretion; President ordered $2M paid to State A","trigger":"Executive order directs spending contrary to Congress’s allocation scheme","rule":"The President can issue executive orders, but whether he has the power depends on whether Congress has expressly delegated the power to him, impliedly authorized the power (acquiesced), or whether the Presidential order conflicts with Congress’s wishes.","sub":"Presidential Power","family":"Separation of Powers","unit":14},{"n":88,"summary":"Doctor convicted of prescription fraud; statute mandates license revocation; board revokes license without a separate hearing; doctor sues for lack of due process.","trigger":"Property interest (professional license) + procedural due process; but underlying facts already adjudicated in criminal conviction.","rule":"When the facts justifying license revocation have been fully adjudicated in a criminal proceeding, due process does not require an additional trial-type hearing before revocation. The doctor's license is a privilege, but once you have it it's a property interest and required due process. Dr. was convicted and already had his day in Court.","sub":"Procedural Due Process — No Additional Hearing After Conviction","family":"Procedural Due Process","unit":28},{"n":89,"summary":"State immediately cuts welfare benefits without prior hearing.","trigger":"Termination of welfare benefits without pre-deprivation hearing. PDP 3 part test: 1) Nature right involved, 2) Risk erroneous deprivation, 3) Nature government action","rule":"Because welfare is such an important right to people and deprivation of it would affect people greatly, a hearing is required before receipt of welfare can be terminated from someone who had already been receiving it.","sub":"Procedural Due Process – Welfare","family":"Procedural Due Process","unit":28},{"n":90,"summary":"State requires one-year residency for employment.","trigger":"Durational residency requirement affecting new residents. P&I clause - Art. 4 section 2 / Out of state cant work in other state, Different from P&I clause 14th amendment","rule":"A durational requirement to receive an important right will trigger a 14th Amendment Privileges & Immunities Clauses violation, which will also violate the fundamental right to travel.","sub":"Right to Travel","family":"Right to Travel","unit":30},{"n":91,"summary":"City offered free medical care but required 1-year residency; new resident denied","trigger":"Durational residency requirement to receive government benefit","rule":"A durational requirement that requires someone to be a resident of a state for a certain period of time before being able to receive important rights can violate the 14th Amendment Privileges & Immunities Clause, as well as the fundamental right to travel.","sub":"Right to Travel","family":"Right to Travel","unit":30},{"n":92,"summary":"A state adopts a durational residency requirement that a person must live in the state for a specified period before receiving an important benefit/right. A new resident is denied solely because she has not lived in the state long enough and challenges the requirement under constitutional protections for the right to travel and related privileges and immunities principles.","trigger":"Durational residency requirement conditions important benefits/rights on length of state residency.","rule":"A durational requirement that requires someone to be a resident of a state for a certain period of time before being able to receive important rights can violate the 14th Amendment Privileges & Immunities Clause, as well as the fundamental right to travel.","sub":"Right to Travel — Durational Residency Requirements","family":"Right to Travel","unit":30},{"n":93,"summary":"State offers free healthcare but only to residents who have lived there for two years; new resident injured after 4 months challenges requirement.","trigger":"Durational residency requirement burdens the fundamental right to travel → strict scrutiny.","rule":"A durational requirement imposed on citizens before they can receive an important right will violate the right to travel, which is a fundamental right.","sub":"Right to Travel / Strict Scrutiny","family":"Right to Travel","unit":30},{"n":94,"summary":"A federal statute creates an “Automobile Commission” to investigate safety, recommend new laws, make rules establishing standards, and prosecute violations. The Chairman is appointed by the President, but other members are selected by the President pro tempore of the Senate and the Speaker of the House. A manufacturer seeks to enjoin enforcement of the Commission’s rules and argues the Commission lacks authority because of how its members were appointed.","trigger":"Congress (or its leaders) attempts to appoint executive officers; Appointments Clause defect can invalidate agency authority.","rule":"Congress cannot appoint (or have its leaders appoint) executive officers; improper Appointments Clause structure can invalidate an agency’s authority. Congress can delegate regulatory authority to an agency as long as there are adequate/intelligible standards guiding the agency.","sub":"Separation of Powers – Appointments Clause","family":"Separation of Powers","unit":13},{"n":95,"summary":"Congress set agency hours; President reduced them by regulation.","trigger":"President acting without delegated authority from Congress.Youngstown -President overstepping- 1)High zone C delegates expressly to P, 2) Lowest: P goes against congress, 3) Grey Zone / Twilight:","rule":"The burden is on the party exercising the powers to prove that there was a delegation to them that allowed them to exercise such powers.","sub":"Separation of Powers – Delegation","family":"Separation of Powers","unit":14},{"n":96,"summary":"The President issues an executive order directing government action. The dispute is whether the President had constitutional or statutory authority to act, requiring analysis of whether Congress expressly authorized the action, impliedly authorized it through acquiescence, or instead prohibited or conflicted with the presidential action.","trigger":"Executive order authority depends on congressional authorization or conflict (Youngstown framework).","rule":"The President can issue executive orders, but whether he has the power depends on whether Congress has expressly delegated the power to him, impliedly authorized the power (acquiesced), or whether the Presidential order conflicts with Congress’s wishes. Congress is not going against what Congress wanted","sub":"Separation of Powers — Executive Orders (Youngstown)","family":"Separation of Powers","unit":14},{"n":97,"summary":"Congress delegates spending authority but retains veto.","trigger":"Legislative veto retained after delegation.","rule":"When Congress delegates power, they cannot keep veto power.","sub":"Separation of Powers – Legislative Veto","family":"Separation of Powers","unit":15},{"n":98,"summary":"Statute authorizes agency to site and build monument and appropriates funds, but prohibits spending until both Houses pass a concurrent resolution approving plans that is not presented to the President.","trigger":"Congress attempts to control execution through non-presented concurrent resolution (legislative veto).","rule":"Congress cannot use a concurrent resolution not presented to the President to approve/disapprove executive action; such a legislative veto violates bicameralism and presentment.","sub":"Separation of Powers – Legislative Veto (Presentment)","family":"Separation of Powers","unit":15},{"n":99,"summary":"A U.S. senator, speaking on the floor of Congress, accused a federal purchasing officer of corruption. The purchasing officer sued the senator for defamation, alleging only that the accusation was false and the senator was negligent.","trigger":"Defamation claim based on a statement made on the floor of Congress; Speech or Debate Clause / legislative privilege.","rule":"The Speech or Debate Clause (Art. I, § 6) absolutely bars civil tort liability for statements made by members of Congress in legislative proceedings (e.g., on the floor of Congress).","sub":"Separation of Powers – Speech or Debate Clause","family":"Separation of Powers","unit":16},{"n":100,"summary":"Student sues public school in federal court","trigger":"Suit against state entity by private party. A school counts as a state actor and cant be sued in federal court the 11th amendment makes the school inmune","rule":"Under the 11th Amendment, a state is immune from being sued in federal court by an individual.","sub":"Sovereign Immunity","family":"Eleventh Amendment","unit":10},{"n":101,"summary":"Congress gives grants to small businesses","trigger":"Spending for general welfare","rule":"Although Congress can spend money for the general welfare under its spending power, they cannot regulate for the general welfare.","sub":"Spending Power","family":"Congressional Power","unit":1},{"n":102,"summary":"Congress, encouraged by breakthroughs in genetic research (multiple sclerosis, cystic fibrosis, cancers), passes legislation establishing a commission to review projects and award grant money to research facilities and individual scientists. Applications are intensely reviewed by a panel of industry and university scientists; panel members and their affiliated facilities cannot receive grants. Congress appropriates $90 million for the program. The appropriation is challenged as unconstitutional.","trigger":"Congress appropriates funds for a research‑grant program; evaluate Spending Clause/general welfare power and judicial deference.","rule":"Under the Spending Clause, Congress may spend for the general welfare, and courts uphold the appropriation if there is a rational basis to believe the spending promotes the general welfare.","sub":"Spending Power – General welfare","family":"Congressional Power","unit":1},{"n":103,"summary":"Bulletproof vest manufacturer sells mostly to the federal government and mandates employees retire at age 60. A 62-year-old sues the company for violating Equal Protection under the Fourteenth Amendment.","trigger":"Equal Protection claim against a private employer; state action requirement.","rule":"The Fourteenth Amendment’s Equal Protection Clause applies only to state action; a private company’s policy is not subject to EP unless fairly attributable to the state.","sub":"State Action – Private conduct","family":"State Action Doctrine","unit":20},{"n":104,"summary":"A large, privately owned and operated shopping mall open to the public contained shops, department stores, restaurants, movie theaters, an ice skating rink, a small amusement park, and a branch of the local public library. Ten peaceful protesters gathered in the pedestrian area near a department store entrance to denounce the sale of animal-fur products; they did not block traffic. Mall management broke up the protest and required the protesters to leave. The protesters sued, claiming violation of First and Fourteenth Amendment free-speech rights.","trigger":"Constitutional rights require state action; privately owned mall generally not a state actor merely because open to public / has many amenities.","rule":"Absent state action, the First and Fourteenth Amendments do not apply; a privately owned shopping mall’s decision to exclude speakers generally does not violate constitutional free-speech guarantees.","sub":"State Action – Private Property & Free Speech","family":"State Action Doctrine","unit":20},{"n":105,"summary":"Arizona enacts a regulation effectively banning direct-to-consumer car sales in response to franchised dealers’ complaints of unfair competition. A luxury electric-car company that sells directly challenges the law on substantive due process grounds.","trigger":"Economic regulation of business practices; apply rational basis review under substantive due process.","rule":"Economic regulations are upheld under substantive due process if they are rationally related to a legitimate governmental interest, and the challenger must show the law lacks any rational connection to such an interest.","sub":"Substantive Due Process – Economic regulation (rational basis)","family":"Equal Protection / Substantive Due Process","unit":21},{"n":106,"summary":"A religious group homeschools children to avoid public‑school influences. City research shows many homeschooled children are not completing curricula or earning diplomas/GEDs and are instead leaving school to work. The city passes a law requiring all students to attend public school until age 16. A family challenges the law as applied to their 12‑year‑old son they planned to homeschool until 16.","trigger":"Law interferes with parents’ fundamental right to direct upbringing/education; strict scrutiny applies and the burden is on the government. Law applies to everyone → not targeting religion (secular justification).\n\nSo not Equal Protection (no discriminatory intent/class).\n\nCore right implicated: parents’ fundamental right to direct upbringing/education of children (substantive due process).","rule":"There is a fundamental right to raise a child the way a parent sees fit.","sub":"Substantive Due Process – Parental rights/childrearing (fundamental right)","family":"Substantive Due Process","unit":29},{"n":107,"summary":"In this state, advanced diagnostic tech exists only in hospitals (and is a major revenue source). In other states, the same tech is offered at independent diagnostic centers. A group of physicians announces plans to immediately open an independent diagnostic center in the state. The state hospital association lobbies the legislature, claiming only hospitals can reliably handle the technology. Legislature passes law: prohibits diagnostic centers unless affiliated with a hospital. Physicians sue challenging constitutionality.State enacts a law affecting a group (e.g., a health/technology regulation) that neither burdens a fundamental right nor targets a suspect class. Challengers claim it violates equal protection, but the state argues it serves legitimate public goals.","trigger":"No fundamental right and no suspect/quasi-suspect classification is involved, so the law is reviewed under rational basis.","rule":"If a law doesn’t burden a fundamental right or target a suspect class, it’s reviewed under rational basis and will be upheld if the legislature could rationally believe it serves a legitimate goal. Decide whether this is Equal Protection or Substantive Due Process. EP = government is treating a class differently. SDP = government is restricting everyone’s conduct (a liberty right), not targeting a protected class. Here: law bans all non-hospital-affiliated diagnostic centers. Not a suspect class (not race, gender, alienage, etc.). Not out-of-staters vs in-staters (so not P&I / DCC).So this is best framed as Substantive Due Process (economic regulation). Next step: Pick the level of scrutin is there a fundamental right to run this kind of business / open a diagnostic center? No fundamental right. So standard = rational basis.","sub":"Substantive Due Process – Rational basis review","family":"Equal Protection / Substantive Due Process","unit":21},{"n":108,"summary":"City refused to fund abortions and barred abortions in state hospital; private clinic far and costly","trigger":"Challenge to government refusal to subsidize abortion services","rule":"There is no fundamental right for the state to pay for an abortion for indigent women.","sub":"Substantive Due Process (Abortion)","family":"Substantive Due Process","unit":29},{"n":109,"summary":"Federal officers arrested a state prosecutor on a federal judge’s warrant for alleged federal-law violations and held him in federal custody. A state court judge then issued a writ of habeas corpus ordering the prosecutor’s release from federal custody, attempting to override the federal detention.","trigger":"State court attempts to use state process (habeas) to interfere with federal custody and enforcement of federal law.","rule":"The Supremacy Clause prohibits states from interfering with federal detention by issuing habeas writs.","sub":"Supremacy Clause – Federal Detention","family":"Federal Supremacy","unit":12},{"n":110,"summary":"Whether Congress can set a nationwide price cap on gasoline (a product moving in interstate commerce), and whether that is a valid Commerce Clause regulation (not commandeering, not “general welfare” limits). Summary of facts (memory version) State A passes a law: gas cap = $2.75/gallon. Congress responds with a federal law: gas cap = $3.00/gallon (nationwide). Question: is the federal law constitutional?State capped gas prices; Congress capped gas nationwide at a different price","trigger":"Valid federal commerce regulation and Supremacy Clause preemption if not commandeering","rule":"If Congress has the power to pass a law, the sates will be bound to follow it under the Supremacy Clause if the law does not commandeer the states. Congress may regulate interstate commerce—including nationwide regulation of gasoline pricing—so a federal gas price cap is constitutional under the Commerce Clause (and it’s not commandeering).","sub":"Supremacy/Commerce","family":"Congressional Power","unit":2},{"n":111,"summary":"A legislator challenges a state ethics law in state court, arguing it violates the state constitution and is inconsistent with codified federal ethics law. The State Supreme Court narrowly construes the state law to avoid inconsistency with federal ethics law and upholds it, but is silent on the other grounds. The legislator petitions the U.S. Supreme Court for certiorari.","trigger":"State court judgment rests on state-law construction/grounds sufficient to support the result; assess U.S. Supreme Court jurisdiction.","rule":"The U.S. Supreme Court cannot review a state-court judgment that rests on adequate and independent state-law grounds, even if federal issues were raised or discussed. If a case is decided only on state law the US Supreme Court would not get involved. The state has to make clear they are only using state law. The lower court has the burden to show they only used state law. But in this question the State Supreme Court did not do this job well because they construe the statute not inconsistent with the federal law but they did not say they were only using state law. Therefore, answer B is a little bit better","sub":"Supreme Court Review – Adequate & independent state grounds","family":"Supreme Court Review","unit":9},{"n":112,"summary":"A nursing mother challenges a state statute banning public breastfeeding of a child over 18 months in state buildings, claiming it violates both the state constitution’s Due Process Clause and the Fifth Amendment. The state supreme court upholds the statute on state-constitutional grounds, but its opinion states its reasoning is based on a federal appellate decision upholding a similar federal statute under the Fifth Amendment. The mother seeks U.S. Supreme Court review.","trigger":"State-court opinion fairly appears to rest on federal law (or is unclear), even though state grounds are discussed; apply Michigan v. Long.","rule":"If it is unclear whether a state-court judgment rests on federal or state grounds and the opinion fairly appears to rest on federal law, the U.S. Supreme Court may review (Michigan v. Long).","sub":"Supreme Court Review – Michigan v. Long","family":"Federalism","unit":4},{"n":113,"summary":"Coastal regulation reduces land value","trigger":"Regulation leaves residual economic value","rule":"A per se taking will occur if the regulation deprives the property of all its economic value.","sub":"Takings","family":"Takings","unit":44},{"n":114,"summary":"A state known for hiking trails sought to boost tourism by creating new public hiking access. The state imposed an easement across private land to connect trail systems, allowing public entry/use without paying compensation to the landowner or other interest holders, and the affected parties challenged the action as a taking.","trigger":"Government creates/appropriates a property interest (easement/public access) → per se taking; compensation must be paid to all affected property interests.","rule":"When there is a taking, the government must pay fair market value as compensation to every interest affected by the taking.","sub":"Takings – Compensation","family":"Takings","unit":43},{"n":115,"summary":"City condemns beach houses to sell land to developer for luxury hotel to boost tourism and economy; owners challenge public-use requirement.","trigger":"Eminent domain transferring to private developer; public use vs public benefit/economic development.","rule":"Public use for takings includes public benefit; economic development (jobs/taxes/tourism) can satisfy the public-use requirement if just compensation is paid.","sub":"Takings — Eminent Domain / Public Use (economic development)","family":"Takings","unit":45},{"n":116,"summary":"A city expands a two-lane highway to four lanes, and the expansion encroaches a few feet onto a vineyard owner’s land.","trigger":"Physical occupation/appropriation of private land for a public project.","rule":"A permanent physical occupation or appropriation of private property by the government is a per se taking requiring just compensation, even if the intrusion is small.","sub":"Takings – Per se physical taking","family":"Takings","unit":43},{"n":117,"summary":"Congress authorizes the President to build U.S.-owned surveillance facilities on privately owned land if the President deems the facility necessary for national security. A surveillance facility is constructed on a very small, unused portion of an owner’s large tract, it does not affect any current use of the rest of the land, and the statute provides no compensation. The owner sues to challenge the constitutionality of building the facility without compensation.","trigger":"Permanent physical occupation of private land is authorized (even on a tiny portion and even if use/value is unaffected).","rule":"Any permanent physical occupation of land (even tiny and even if it doesn’t affect use/value) is a per se taking requiring just compensation.","sub":"Takings – Permanent physical occupation (per se)","family":"Takings","unit":43},{"n":118,"summary":"Zoning change destroys mall development value.","trigger":"Regulation interferes with investment-backed expectations.","rule":"To determine if the government action amounts to a taking, we look at: 1) the economic impact of the regulation, 2) whether the regulation interferes with distinct investment-backed expectations of the owner, and 3) the character of the government action.","sub":"Takings – Regulatory","family":"Takings","unit":44},{"n":119,"summary":"An architect buys a 5-acre undeveloped residential lot intending to build a permanent home. Four months later, the state enacts a law to protect an indigenous bird species that prohibits erection of permanent structures on many parcels, including the architect’s. The architect sues claiming the ban violates the Fifth and Fourteenth Amendments. The question asks his strongest argument.","trigger":"Regulation bars all permanent structures, eliminating all economically beneficial use of the property for its intended purpose.","rule":"A regulation that deprives an owner of all economically beneficial use of the property is a per se (total) taking requiring just compensation (Lucas).","sub":"Takings – Total regulatory taking (Lucas)","family":"Takings","unit":44},{"n":120,"summary":"State needs oil/gas during shortage; changes law to void long-term tenant leases if tenants refuse extraction; state pays tenants fair rental value for remaining term.","trigger":"Government action eliminates/terminates leasehold interests (property interests) to facilitate resource extraction.","rule":"When a taking of property occurs, the government must compensate ALL property interests that have been affected.","sub":"Takings / Just Compensation","family":"Takings","unit":43},{"n":121,"summary":"After the FDA published a study linking artificial food coloring to increased cancer risk, Congress enacted a tax: 10¢ per gallon on artificially colored soda and 2¢ per gallon on non‑colored or naturally colored soda. A convenience store bought 100 gallons of artificially colored soda for resale (taxed $2 total) and was later sued by the United States for a statutory penalty. The store argues the discriminatory tax is unconstitutional because it effectively regulates/suppresses artificially colored soda rather than raising revenue.","trigger":"Federal excise tax challenged as unconstitutional regulation because it discourages certain products; determine validity if it raises revenue.","rule":"A federal tax is valid so long as it raises revenue; it is not unconstitutional merely because it has a regulatory or suppressive effect.","sub":"Taxing Power – Regulatory effect","family":"Congressional Power","unit":1},{"n":122,"summary":"Owner-occupied four-unit building advertises 'White male preferred' for vacant unit; owner motivated by desire for tenant mix.","trigger":"Fair Housing Act: Mrs. Murphy exception (owner-occupied ≤4 units) vs prohibition on discriminatory advertising.","rule":"Even when an owner-occupied small building is exempt from certain FHA rental restrictions, the FHA still prohibits discriminatory advertising.","sub":"Fair Housing Act – Advertising","family":"Fair Housing","unit":null}]},{"slug":"crimpro","name":"Criminal Procedure","bigPicture":"WHERE YOU START. Illegality voids. Article 49(1) of the Constitution and article 181 of the COPP make illicitly obtained proof void — a defect of the EVIDENCE ITSELF — and articles 174–175 annul acts performed in violation of form together with everything that flows from them. Rights attach to a STATUS: from the moment a person is investigated, the imputado holds the right to counsel, to remain silent, and to be informed of the charge (COPP art. 127). And there is one judiciary, one constitution, one standard of protection.\n\nWHAT THE U.S. SYSTEM IS. Lawfulness and usability are two SEPARATE questions. A Fourth Amendment violation may carry no remedy at all (a knock-and-announce breach suppresses nothing); the remedy may belong only to the person whose own privacy was invaded; it may switch off according to the forum (grand jury, deportation, parole revocation, or impeaching the defendant's own testimony); and it may be defeated by an officer's honest reliance on a defective warrant or by attenuation of the causal chain. Protections are triggered by factual CONDITIONS rather than by status: warnings require custody PLUS interrogation, and the system runs TWO counsel rights — a Sixth Amendment right that attaches at formal charge and runs offence by offence, and a Fifth Amendment right that runs to the person once unambiguously invoked. The lay jury explains the trial rules, and the federal Constitution is only a FLOOR: fifty-one charters of rights operate at once, so identical police conduct can be lawful in one State and suppressible in the next.\n\nWHAT DIVERGES. Three trained habits generate the wrong answer. (1) Concluding “unlawful, therefore excluded” — usually wrong: ask first whose right was violated, in which proceeding the evidence is offered, and whether good faith, inevitable discovery or attenuation rescues it. (2) Attaching rights to the status of investigated person: extensive UNWARNED questioning at the roadside or in the suspect's own kitchen is perfectly lawful because there is no custody, and a defendant already represented on one charge may be questioned about a different, uncharged offence. (3) Expecting an unlawful arrest to void the process — it does not: the court keeps the case and only the FRUITS are at risk, so a motion to dismiss is the wrong instrument however flagrant the conduct.\n\nWHAT IS THE SAME. Nine units are convergence: warrantless entry to aid a victim or pursue a fleeing suspect (COPP art. 196 is essentially exigent circumstances); seizure of contraband seen in plain sight, which is your flagrancia; securing the immediate scene of an arrest; the prosecutor's duty to gather exculpatory as well as incriminating evidence (COPP art. 111(4) is Brady); the presumption of innocence and in dubio pro reo governing the elements; and the right to be informed and assisted by counsel. Bank those — then spend everything on the remedy question, because that is where this module actually lives.","families":[{"name":"Fourth Amendment","count":30},{"name":"Fifth Amendment","count":16},{"name":"Sixth Amendment","count":13},{"name":"Exclusionary Rule","count":6},{"name":"Due Process","count":3},{"name":"Identifications","count":3},{"name":"Trial Rights","count":3},{"name":"Sixth Amendment / Due Process","count":2},{"name":"Federalism","count":1},{"name":"Remedies","count":1}],"units":[{"n":1,"summary":"Officers cross a barbed-wire fence past no-trespassing signs and spot marijuana in a distant field; elsewhere they let a drug dog sniff a business patio the owner treats as a break area, and inspect garbage left for collection.","trigger":"Government intrusion on a place or thing → is there a reasonable expectation of privacy at all? Open fields, garbage left for collection, and areas outside the curtilage are unprotected; the home's curtilage and its immediate surroundings are protected.","rule":"There is no Fourth Amendment 'search' where the person has no reasonable expectation of privacy — an expectation society recognizes as reasonable (Katz v. United States). Open fields carry no such expectation even when fenced, gated, or posted, and officers may enter and observe though committing a civil trespass (Oliver v. United States); garbage left for collection in a place accessible to the public is likewise unprotected (California v. Greenwood); and commercial premises carry a REDUCED but real expectation, with areas outside the curtilage treated as open fields (Dow Chemical v. United States). A dog sniff conducted from a lawful public vantage or of a lawfully stopped vehicle's exterior is not a search (Illinois v. Caballes), but a sniff conducted from within constitutionally protected curtilage IS (Florida v. Jardines).","sub":"Fourth Amendment – What Counts as a Search: REP, Open Fields, Curtilage, Dog Sniffs (Master)","sensitivity":"The threshold question precedes every warrant and exception analysis: if there was no search, the Fourth Amendment simply does not engage and no justification is needed. Two switches decide the cases: PLACE (curtilage versus open field or public-facing area) and VANTAGE (from where the officer or the dog stood).","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the owner fenced and posted the land, so entry without a warrant is unlawful — propiedad privada is inviolable” → U.S. doctrine protects PRIVACY EXPECTATIONS rather than property lines, so a trespass onto posted farmland is not even a search. Venezuelan law fuses the two, protecting the home and its dependencies as a matter of property and domicile inviolability (Const. art. 47; C.P. trespass offenses), so the civilian reflex treats ownership and signage as dispositive — and then cannot explain why a porch sniff is a search but a distant field is not.","authority":"What counts as a search: Katz v. United States; Oliver v. United States (open fields); California v. Greenwood (garbage); Dow Chemical v. United States; Florida v. Jardines; Illinois v. Caballes."},{"n":2,"summary":"Police install a one-way mirror into a hotel guest's bathroom with the owner's permission; an undercover officer talks his way into a home posing as a buyer, and later lifts papers on the desk.","trigger":"Privacy claim in temporary or shared premises → a hotel guest has a protected expectation and the owner or clerk cannot consent for him; a person who voluntarily admits an undercover officer assumes the risk as to what is knowingly exposed, but not as to concealed items.","rule":"A guest has a reasonable expectation of privacy in a rented hotel or motel room for the rental period, and a hotel owner, clerk, or manager has no authority to consent to a search of an occupied room (Stoner v. California); covert visual surveillance of such a space is a search requiring a warrant. By contrast, one who voluntarily admits an undercover officer or informant assumes the risk that what he knowingly exposes will be reported: entry by deception with the occupant's permission is not itself a Fourth Amendment violation (Lewis v. United States; Hoffa v. United States; On Lee). That assumption of risk is limited to what is exposed — the officer may not move or manipulate concealed items (Unit 8), and consent may be given by anyone with actual or APPARENT authority (Illinois v. Rodriguez).","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Privacy in Temporary Premises; Undercover Entry and Assumed Risk","sensitivity":"Two independent axes: WHOSE consent can bind (the guest's, never the innkeeper's) and WHAT the occupant exposed (visible conduct, not hidden papers). The invited undercover officer is lawfully inside — which is exactly why the later manipulation, not the entry, is the violation.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the hotel is the owner's property, so the owner may authorize entry — and an officer who entered by deceit obtained consent through error, vitiating it” → U.S. law inverts both intuitions: the transient occupant holds the privacy right and the owner cannot waive it, while deception that induces admission does not vitiate the entry at all. The civilian schema of consent vitiated by error or dolo (C.C. arts. 1.146, 1.154) and of the owner's dominion over his premises predicts the opposite result on both facts.","authority":"Temporary premises and undercover entry: Stoner v. California; Hoffa v. United States; Lewis v. United States; On Lee v. United States; Illinois v. Rodriguez (apparent authority)."},{"n":3,"summary":"Officers illegally search a woman's home and her phone; the text messages identify a man as a customer, and he moves to suppress them at his own trial.","trigger":"Defendant seeks suppression of evidence obtained through a violation of SOMEONE ELSE'S privacy → no standing; Fourth Amendment rights are personal and cannot be asserted vicariously.","rule":"Fourth Amendment rights are personal: a defendant may seek suppression only where the search or seizure violated HIS OWN reasonable expectation of privacy, and may not assert the violation of a third party's rights however egregious (Rakas v. Illinois; Alderman v. United States). Applications: a car passenger has no privacy interest in the glove compartment or trunk but does have standing to challenge the STOP that seized him (Brendlin v. California); an overnight guest has standing in the host's home (Minnesota v. Olson) while a short-term commercial visitor does not (Minnesota v. Carter); and an owner of seized property has standing as to the seizure though not necessarily as to the place searched.","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Standing: Rights Are Personal","sensitivity":"The doctrine defeats otherwise winning suppression motions on a purely relational ground: the police conduct was flagrantly unlawful, and the evidence is still admissible against this defendant. Identifying WHOSE expectation was invaded — before analyzing whether the search was reasonable — is the tested sequence.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “prueba ilícita is void and cannot be used by anyone against anyone — nulidad absoluta (Const. art. 49(1); the COPP's licitud-de-la-prueba rule)” → U.S. exclusion is a personal REMEDY, not a property of the evidence, so unlawfully obtained proof remains fully usable against everyone whose rights were not violated. The civilian schema treats illegality as an objective defect of the act, producing systematic over-suppression — the single most consequential structural difference in the exclusionary material.","authority":"Standing: Rakas v. Illinois; Alderman v. United States; Brendlin v. California; Minnesota v. Olson; Minnesota v. Carter."},{"n":4,"summary":"An officer tells a suspicious neighbor he cannot enter but adds 'nothing stops you'; the neighbor searches the house and finds a laptop while the officer waits outside and spots a monitor on the porch.","trigger":"Search conducted by a private person → unregulated by the Fourth Amendment UNLESS the government instigated, encouraged, directed, or participated in it, converting the private actor into a government agent.","rule":"The Fourth Amendment restrains only governmental action. A search by a purely private party — acting on his own initiative and for his own purposes — is not subject to it, and the fruits may be handed to police and used (Burdeau v. McDowell). But a private person becomes a government agent where officials instigate, encourage, direct, or participate in the search, or where the private actor acts with the intent to assist law enforcement and the government knows of and acquiesces in the conduct (Skinner v. Railway Labor Executives; Coolidge v. New Hampshire). Police may not accomplish through a proxy what they could not do themselves; and once a private search has occurred, officers may replicate its scope but may not exceed it (Walter v. United States; United States v. Jacobsen).","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Government Action and Private Searches","sensitivity":"The switch is ENCOURAGEMENT, not formal agency: 'nothing stops you' is instigation, and the officer's own observation from the porch is separately analyzed under plain view. Note the second-tier rule — police may re-examine what a genuine private search already revealed, but not go further.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “evidence obtained by unlawful means is inadmissible whoever obtained it — the private person also violated the law” → U.S. doctrine makes the identity of the searcher dispositive: the neighbor's burglary is a crime but not a Fourth Amendment violation, and the laptop is admissible unless the officer's words made him an agent. Venezuelan proof theory tests the licitud of the evidence itself (the COPP's licitud-de-la-prueba rule) without a state-action filter, so the civilian never asks WHO searched — the very question that decides these cases.","authority":"Government action and private searches: Burdeau v. McDowell; Skinner v. Railway Labor Executives; Coolidge v. New Hampshire; United States v. Jacobsen; Walter v. United States."},{"n":5,"summary":"A judge issues a warrant two days before a party for drugs expected to arrive then; elsewhere a warrant naming 123 Jones Street is executed on the wrong unit of a duplex, and a warrant for a basement is used to search the attic.","trigger":"Warrant validity and execution → anticipatory warrants require probable cause that the triggering condition will occur and that evidence will then be present; execution must stay within the place PARTICULARLY DESCRIBED, with reasonable mistakes tolerated.","rule":"A warrant must be issued by a neutral and detached magistrate on probable cause and must particularly describe the place to be searched and the items to be seized. An ANTICIPATORY warrant is valid where there is probable cause to believe that, once the triggering condition occurs, contraband will be at the described place, and probable cause that the triggering condition will occur (United States v. Grubbs). Execution may extend to any area of the described premises where the item sought could reasonably be found, and contraband discovered there may be seized — but officers may not search beyond the place described: a warrant for a basement does not authorize the attic. A reasonable mistake about the premises' layout does not invalidate the search until the error becomes apparent (Maryland v. Garrison).","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Warrants: Anticipatory Warrants, Particularity, and Scope of Execution","sensitivity":"Three dials: TIMING (a warrant may lawfully anticipate future facts), PLACE (the described premises bound the search absolutely), and MISTAKE (objective reasonableness excuses a wrong-unit entry only until the officers should have known). The exam's favorite move is a warrant describing one part of a building and a search that wanders.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the judge authorized a search of the house, so the officers may search the house — and no order can be issued for evidence that does not yet exist” → U.S. warrants are drafted and policed by their own text: the description is the limit, and probable cause may rest on predicted future facts. The civilian orden de allanamiento, judicially authorized and executed under prosecutorial direction (the COPP's allanamiento provisions), is understood as authorizing entry to the dwelling rather than as a text whose wording defines the lawful perimeter.","authority":"Warrants: United States v. Grubbs (anticipatory); Maryland v. Garrison (particularity and reasonable mistake); Art. IV particularity requirement."},{"n":6,"summary":"Officers with a valid warrant skip the knock-and-announce and detain the homeowner during the search; elsewhere, executing a warrant for a bar, they detain and search a patron against whom there is no individualized suspicion.","trigger":"Execution of a valid search warrant → knock-and-announce violations do not trigger suppression; occupants of a RESIDENCE may be detained for the duration; persons present who are not occupants require individualized suspicion.","rule":"Officers executing a search warrant must ordinarily knock and announce, but a violation of that requirement does NOT result in suppression, because the interests protected by the rule are unrelated to the seizure of evidence (Hudson v. Michigan). Officers may detain the occupants of a residence for the duration of a search executed under a valid warrant, as an incident of the authority to search (Michigan v. Summers; Muehler v. Mena), though the detention must occur in the immediate vicinity of the premises (Bailey v. United States). A warrant to search PREMISES does not, however, authorize the search or detention of persons found there who are not occupants and against whom there is no individualized suspicion (Ybarra v. Illinois), and evidence obtained through such an unlawful detention is suppressible.","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Warrant Execution: Knock-and-Announce; Detention of Occupants and Others","sensitivity":"The unit separates VIOLATION from REMEDY: a knock-and-announce breach is unconstitutional yet produces no exclusion, which is the cleanest illustration in the module that the two questions are distinct. Then the occupant/non-occupant line decides who may be held: a homeowner yes, a bar patron no.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “a defect in executing the judicial order taints the act and voids what was seized — nulidad by procedural irregularity” → U.S. law asks separately whether the rule was broken and whether exclusion serves its purpose, and answers yes-then-no here. The civilian nullity architecture (the COPP's regime of nullities: acts performed in violation of form are void) ties remedy automatically to defect, so the trained reflex suppresses on a formality where U.S. doctrine does not, and simultaneously misses that detaining a mere bystander IS a substantive violation.","authority":"Warrant execution: Hudson v. Michigan (knock-and-announce); Michigan v. Summers; Muehler v. Mena; Bailey v. United States; Ybarra v. Illinois."},{"n":7,"summary":"A driver stopped for speeding reeks of marijuana and admits possessing some; after the arrest the officer searches the trunk and pries open a briefcase, finding a machine gun. Elsewhere an arrestee is secured in the patrol car before the trunk is searched.","trigger":"Vehicle + probable cause that contraband is somewhere inside → automobile exception permits a warrantless search of the entire vehicle and every container that could hold the item; search INCIDENT TO ARREST of a vehicle is separately and much more narrowly limited.","rule":"Under the automobile exception, probable cause to believe contraband or evidence is somewhere in a vehicle authorizes a warrantless search of every part of it and every container capable of concealing the object of the search, including a locked trunk and a closed briefcase (Carroll v. United States; United States v. Ross; California v. Acevedo). The odor of marijuana supplies probable cause ('plain smell'), and the search may extend to passengers' belongings (Wyoming v. Houghton). SEARCH INCIDENT TO ARREST is a distinct and narrower doctrine: after Arizona v. Gant, a vehicle may be searched incident to arrest only while the arrestee is unsecured and within reaching distance of the passenger compartment, or where it is reasonable to believe the vehicle contains evidence of the offense of arrest. Once the arrestee is secured in the patrol car, the search must rest on probable cause under the automobile exception, on consent, or on inventory.","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Automobile Exception vs. Search Incident to Arrest after Gant (Master)","sensitivity":"Two exceptions the exam deliberately blurs, distinguished by what they require and how far they reach: probable cause plus a vehicle unlocks the WHOLE car; an arrest alone unlocks almost nothing once the arrestee is secured. Naming which doctrine the facts support — and noticing when the answer choices swap them — is the entire technique.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “a vehicle inspection requires judicial authorization like any other search, or is a routine administrative control” → U.S. law creates a categorical, mobility-and-reduced-expectation exception that dispenses with the warrant entirely once probable cause exists, while simultaneously restricting the arrest-based search that seems intuitively broader. Venezuelan practice authorizes vehicle inspections on suspicion as a police measure under the COPP's rules on the inspection of vehicles without this two-doctrine architecture, so the civilian neither appreciates how far the automobile exception reaches nor how narrow Gant has made the arrest rationale.","authority":"Automobile exception and SILA: Carroll v. United States; United States v. Ross; California v. Acevedo; Wyoming v. Houghton; Arizona v. Gant."},{"n":8,"summary":"An officer leaving an apartment sees a tin fall open revealing marijuana; another seizes a gun spotted through a doorway and then runs its serial number; a third lifts papers on a desk and finds credit cards.","trigger":"Officer lawfully positioned observes an item → plain view permits seizure only if the officer had a lawful vantage point AND lawful right of access, and the item's incriminating character was IMMEDIATELY APPARENT without moving or manipulating it.","rule":"Plain view is a seizure doctrine, not an entry doctrine: it requires that the officer be lawfully present at the vantage point, have a lawful right of access to the object, and that the item's incriminating nature be immediately apparent (Coolidge v. New Hampshire; Horton v. California). Manipulation defeats the doctrine: moving equipment to read a serial number, or lifting papers to see what lies beneath, is itself a search requiring independent justification (Arizona v. Hicks). Cognate doctrines follow the same logic — 'plain smell' supports probable cause, and plain FEEL permits seizure during a lawful frisk only where the object's identity is immediately apparent from the pat-down (Minnesota v. Dickerson).","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Plain View: Immediately Apparent; Manipulation Defeats It (Master)","sensitivity":"Three elements, and the tested one is almost always immediacy: an item that requires an extra investigative step (serial number, lifting, opening) was never in plain view. Equally important, plain view never justifies the ENTRY — a gun visible through a doorway does not authorize walking in.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — an officer lawfully present may seize contraband he sees, and evidence in plain sight needs no separate order — is broadly RIGHT and matches the flagrancia logic of the COPP's definition of flagrancia. The calibration friction is the immediacy element: U.S. law forbids the small confirmatory step (turning the object, reading a number, lifting a cover) that the civilian treats as part of the same lawful observation, so the reflex over-seizes precisely at the doctrine's boundary.","authority":"Plain view: Coolidge v. New Hampshire; Horton v. California; Arizona v. Hicks; Minnesota v. Dickerson (plain feel)."},{"n":9,"summary":"An officer with a reliable kidnapping tip sees blood on the doorknob and hears scuffling, enters, and finds cocaine; elsewhere officers with probable cause hear yelling and a toilet flushing and enter a motel room with a passkey.","trigger":"Warrantless entry into a home or dwelling → exigent circumstances: emergency aid or risk to life, hot pursuit, or imminent destruction of evidence, with the police-created-exigency limit.","rule":"Exigent circumstances excuse the warrant requirement for entry where officers reasonably believe someone inside needs emergency aid or faces danger (Brigham City v. Stuart), where they are in hot pursuit of a fleeing felon, or where evidence is about to be destroyed (Mincey v. Arizona). Police may create the exigency and still rely on it, PROVIDED they do not themselves violate or threaten to violate the Fourth Amendment in doing so — knocking and announcing their presence is lawful even if it prompts the destruction of evidence (Kentucky v. King, 2011). The gravity of the offense matters: the Court has rejected a categorical exigency for minor offenses and requires a case-by-case assessment even for suspected drunk driving (Welsh v. Wisconsin; Missouri v. McNeely).","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Exigent Circumstances (Master)","sensitivity":"Three recognized exigencies plus one limit, and the limit is where answers most often go wrong: after Kentucky v. King, an exigency is disqualified only if POLICE MISCONDUCT produced it, not merely because police action did. Scope also matters — an emergency entry justifies addressing the emergency, not a general search.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT — the COPP's allanamiento provision expressly permits warrantless entry to prevent a crime, aid a victim, or pursue a fleeing suspect, closely tracking the U.S. exigency list. The calibration friction lies in the limits: U.S. law asks whether the police themselves manufactured the emergency through unlawful conduct, and refuses to let offense gravity be presumed — refinements the codal enumeration supplies as a list of permitted situations rather than as a tested standard.","authority":"Exigent circumstances: Brigham City v. Stuart; Mincey v. Arizona; Kentucky v. King (2011); Welsh v. Wisconsin; Missouri v. McNeely."},{"n":10,"summary":"Officers arrest a man in his bedroom on a valid arrest warrant after hearing noises suggesting others are present, and open a closet along the exit path where they see a machine gun.","trigger":"Lawful in-home arrest → officers may look in spaces immediately adjoining the place of arrest as a precaution, and may sweep further only on articulable facts supporting a reasonable belief that a dangerous person is present.","rule":"Incident to a lawful in-home arrest, officers may as a precautionary matter look in closets and other spaces IMMEDIATELY ADJOINING the place of arrest from which an attack could be launched, without any level of suspicion; a broader protective sweep of the premises requires articulable facts supporting a reasonable belief that a dangerous individual is present (Maryland v. Buie). The sweep is limited to a cursory inspection of places where a person could hide and may last no longer than necessary to dispel the suspicion; contraband observed in plain view during a lawful sweep may be seized. An arrest warrant authorizes entry into the ARRESTEE'S OWN home (Payton v. New York) but not into a third party's home to arrest him, which requires a search warrant (Steagald v. United States).","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Protective Sweep Incident to Arrest","sensitivity":"Two tiers with different thresholds: immediately adjoining spaces need NO suspicion; anything beyond needs articulable facts. The sweep is for PERSONS, not evidence — which is why plain view does the seizing and why opening a drawer would exceed it.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — officers making an arrest may secure the immediate area for their own safety — is RIGHT and codified in similar terms. The calibration friction is the tiered suspicion structure and the persons-not-evidence limit: U.S. law grants a suspicionless look only at adjoining spaces and demands articulable facts for anything more, whereas the civilian aseguramiento del sitio (the COPP's rules on securing the scene of the act) is framed as a single practical power over the scene.","authority":"Protective sweep: Maryland v. Buie; Payton v. New York; Steagald v. United States."},{"n":11,"summary":"A neighbor consents to a look inside, then revokes and demands the officer leave, who lifts a blanket anyway; a man 'prefers' no search but acquiesces; a wife consents while the handcuffed husband, present, expressly objects.","trigger":"Consent as the justification → test voluntariness (not mere submission to authority), SCOPE (objectively reasonable understanding), REVOCATION (search must stop), and AUTHORITY (actual or apparent; present co-occupant's objection defeats it).","rule":"A warrantless search is valid where voluntary consent is given by a person with actual or apparent authority (Schneckloth v. Bustamonte; Illinois v. Rodriguez); the suspect need not be told he may refuse, but mere acquiescence to a claim of lawful authority is not consent (Bumper v. North Carolina). The SCOPE of consent is measured by what a reasonable person would have understood it to cover (Florida v. Jimeno), and consent may be limited or REVOKED at any time, after which the officer must stop unless an independent exception applies. As to shared premises, any co-tenant may consent (United States v. Matlock) — but where a physically present co-occupant expressly OBJECTS, the search is unreasonable as to him (Georgia v. Randolph), unless the objector has been lawfully removed from the scene before consent is obtained (Fernandez v. California).","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Consent: Voluntariness, Scope, Revocation, and Co-Occupants (Master)","sensitivity":"Four independent kill-switches, each separately tested: involuntary submission, exceeded scope, post-revocation continuation, and a present objector. The Randolph/Fernandez seam is the sharpest — physical presence of the objecting co-tenant, not his mere existence, is what defeats the other tenant's consent.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the occupant let them in, so the search is lawful — consent given is consent given” → U.S. consent is a continuing, bounded, and personally held authorization that can be exceeded, withdrawn, or vetoed by a co-tenant standing in the doorway. The civilian schema treats autorización del morador (the COPP's allanamiento provision) as a one-time gateway to lawful entry, so the reflex misses that the search can become unlawful midway and that two occupants can disagree with legal consequences.","authority":"Consent: Schneckloth v. Bustamonte; Bumper v. North Carolina; Florida v. Jimeno; United States v. Matlock; Georgia v. Randolph; Fernandez v. California; Illinois v. Rodriguez."},{"n":12,"summary":"After a bar fight, an officer orders a suspect and his two companions to stop and arrests one who keeps walking; elsewhere officers frisk a third friend of two hat-wearing suspects and find cocaine.","trigger":"Brief investigative detention → requires individualized REASONABLE SUSPICION of criminal activity based on articulable facts; a frisk requires separate reasonable suspicion that THIS person is armed and dangerous.","rule":"An officer may briefly detain a person on reasonable suspicion, supported by articulable facts, that criminal activity is afoot (Terry v. Ohio), and may conduct a limited pat-down for weapons only on separate reasonable suspicion that the person is armed and dangerous. The suspicion must be INDIVIDUALIZED: mere presence in the company of a suspect, or in a high-crime area, is not enough (Ybarra v. Illinois; Brown v. Texas), though unprovoked flight in a high-crime area can suffice (Illinois v. Wardlow), and refusing to cooperate with or walking away from a consensual encounter cannot itself supply suspicion (Florida v. Bostick). During a lawful frisk, an object whose identity as contraband is immediately apparent from the pat-down may be seized (plain feel, Minnesota v. Dickerson) — but where the frisk itself was unlawful, the seizure fails with it.","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Terry Stops and Frisks: Individualized Suspicion (Master)","sensitivity":"Two separate suspicions for two separate intrusions — stop (crime) and frisk (weapons) — and 'he was with the suspect' satisfies neither. The plain-feel doctrine is entirely parasitic on the frisk's lawfulness, which is why the exam supplies contraband found on the companion.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “officers investigating a disturbance may stop and search those involved — detención preventiva and cacheo are ordinary police powers” → U.S. law requires person-specific articulable facts for each intrusion and forbids group-based detention. Venezuelan practice permits inspection of persons on reasonable suspicion of concealed objects (the COPP's rules on the inspection of persons) without the individualization discipline or the two-tier structure, so the civilian reflex sweeps in the companion the Fourth Amendment protects.","authority":"Terry stops and frisks: Terry v. Ohio; Ybarra v. Illinois; Brown v. Texas; Illinois v. Wardlow; Florida v. Bostick; Minnesota v. Dickerson."},{"n":13,"summary":"An officer stops a driver going two miles over the limit — contrary to department practice and admittedly hoping to find drugs — and sees a bag of marijuana while standing at the window.","trigger":"Traffic stop supported by an objectively valid basis → the officer's subjective motive and departure from departmental policy are irrelevant; contraband seen from the lawful vantage point may be seized.","rule":"A traffic stop is reasonable if the officer has probable cause or reasonable suspicion of a traffic violation, and the officer's actual motivation is IRRELEVANT: pretext does not invalidate an objectively justified stop, and departmental practice does not create a constitutional standard (Whren v. United States; Arkansas v. Sullivan). During a lawful stop officers may order the driver and passengers out (Pennsylvania v. Mimms; Maryland v. Wilson), and may seize contraband seen in plain view. The stop's DURATION is limited to the time reasonably needed to complete the mission: extending it to conduct a dog sniff without independent reasonable suspicion is unlawful (Rodriguez v. United States). Selective enforcement on racial grounds remains challengeable, but under the Equal Protection Clause rather than the Fourth Amendment.","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Traffic Stops: Pretext Is Irrelevant; Duration Is Not","sensitivity":"The Fourth Amendment inquiry is entirely objective — which is why the officer's candid admission of a drug-hunting motive changes nothing — while the constraint that DOES bite is temporal. Answer choices built on the officer's bad motive test whether the candidate knows the objective standard and where the real limit lies.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the stop was a pretext — desviación de poder vitiates the act and everything flowing from it” → U.S. Fourth Amendment analysis is deliberately motive-blind, relegating improper purpose to an equal protection claim with a far heavier burden. The civilian schema treats the authority's purpose as an element of the act's validity (a core administrative-law principle), so the trained reflex invalidates the stop and suppresses — exactly the wrong answer under Whren.","authority":"Traffic stops: Whren v. United States; Arkansas v. Sullivan; Pennsylvania v. Mimms; Maryland v. Wilson; Rodriguez v. United States (duration)."},{"n":14,"summary":"State law requires a cheek swab from every person arrested for a felony; the sample is stored in a registry and later matches blood on a knife from an unrelated crime.","trigger":"Booking-stage DNA collection from a person arrested for a serious offense and taken into custody → a search, but reasonable without a warrant as an identification measure.","rule":"Taking a buccal swab is a search, but DNA collection and storage from persons ARRESTED FOR A SERIOUS OFFENSE and brought into custody is reasonable under the Fourth Amendment as a legitimate booking and identification procedure, given the minimal intrusion and the government's interest in identifying arrestees (Maryland v. King, 2013). The rationale is identification rather than investigation of unrelated crimes, though a database match may of course be used. Contrast the warrant requirement for investigative bodily intrusions on less than that footing — a blood draw from a DUI suspect ordinarily requires a warrant absent exigency (Missouri v. McNeely; Birchfield v. North Dakota distinguishes breath from blood).","sub":"Fourth Amendment – DNA Collection from Arrestees","sensitivity":"Two conditions carry the holding: a SERIOUS offense and custodial booking — King is not a license for suspicionless DNA collection generally. The contrast with McNeely and Birchfield shows the doctrine's boundary: routine booking identification is reasonable, investigative bodily intrusion generally is not.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — identification and registry procedures for detainees are routine and lawful — is RIGHT, and Venezuelan practice similarly treats reseña and identification measures as incidents of detention. The calibration friction is that in the U.S. the same act is formally a SEARCH requiring constitutional justification, and its validity depends on the arrest's gravity and the custodial posture — conditions the civilian administrative framing does not impose.","authority":"DNA collection: Maryland v. King (2013); contrast Missouri v. McNeely; Birchfield v. North Dakota."},{"n":15,"summary":"Evidence from a warrantless home entry is offered in a civil deportation case and in a criminal prosecution; elsewhere a suppression motion is made at a grand jury proceeding; and a defendant's own inconsistent testimony is met with his unwarned statement.","trigger":"Illegally obtained evidence → map where the exclusionary rule applies: the criminal case-in-chief yes; civil, quasi-criminal, and grand jury proceedings and impeachment of the defendant generally no.","rule":"The exclusionary rule is a judicially created deterrent remedy, not a personal constitutional right, so it applies only where its deterrent benefit outweighs its costs. It bars unlawfully obtained evidence from the prosecution's CASE-IN-CHIEF, but does not apply in grand jury proceedings (United States v. Calandra), civil deportation proceedings (INS v. Lopez-Mendoza), parole revocation hearings (Pennsylvania Bd. of Probation v. Scott), civil tax and forfeiture proceedings, or to impeach the defendant's own inconsistent trial testimony (Harris v. New York; but an actually COERCED statement may not be used for any purpose, Mincey v. Arizona, and post-warning silence may never impeach, Doyle v. Ohio). Its costs are also cabined by the good-faith exception and the derivative-evidence limits (Units 16–17).","sub":"Exclusionary Rule – Scope and Where It Does Not Apply (Master)","sensitivity":"The rule's rationale governs its reach: because it is deterrence-based rather than rights-based, the FORUM decides admissibility as much as the illegality does. The most-tested items are the grand jury (no exclusion) and impeachment (allowed for statements, never for silence).","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “prueba obtenida ilegalmente es nula y no surte efecto en ningún proceso — Const. art. 49(1) is categorical” → U.S. exclusion is a cost-benefit remedy that switches off in whole classes of proceedings and even within the criminal trial for impeachment purposes. The civilian constitutional nullity of illicit proof admits no such forum-by-forum gradation, so the reflex predicts suppression across the board and cannot account for a system that tolerates the evidence almost everywhere except the case-in-chief.","authority":"Exclusionary rule scope: United States v. Calandra; INS v. Lopez-Mendoza; Pennsylvania Bd. of Probation v. Scott; Harris v. New York; Mincey v. Arizona; Doyle v. Ohio."},{"n":16,"summary":"A magistrate who feared for his children pressures an informant to fabricate the affidavit supporting a child-pornography warrant; elsewhere officers reasonably but mistakenly search the wrong unit of a duplex.","trigger":"Officers act under a warrant later shown to be defective → evidence is admissible in good faith UNLESS one of the four Leon disqualifiers applies; separately, an objectively reasonable factual mistake in execution does not violate the Amendment at all.","rule":"Evidence obtained by officers acting in objectively reasonable reliance on a warrant later held invalid is admissible (United States v. Leon), and the exception extends to reliance on a statute later held unconstitutional (Illinois v. Krull) and on erroneous database records absent systemic negligence (Herring v. United States; Arizona v. Evans). Good faith does NOT save the search where (1) the magistrate abandoned his neutral and detached role, (2) the affidavit contained information the officer knew or should have known was false, (3) the affidavit was so lacking in probable cause that reliance was unreasonable, or (4) the warrant was facially deficient in particularity. Distinct from Leon is the reasonable-MISTAKE principle: an objectively reasonable factual error in executing a valid warrant is not a violation in the first place (Maryland v. Garrison).","sub":"Exclusionary Rule – Good Faith and Its Four Limits","sensitivity":"Leon reverses the intuitive relationship between defect and remedy: the warrant fails and the evidence still comes in — unless the defect implicates POLICE or MAGISTRATE integrity rather than mere legal error. Keeping Leon (invalid warrant, honest reliance) separate from Garrison (valid warrant, reasonable factual mistake) is the tested distinction.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “if the judicial authorization was defective the act is void and its fruits inadmissible — nullity flows from the defect” → U.S. law asks instead whether suppression would deter POLICE misconduct, and answers no where the error was the magistrate's. The civilian nullity architecture makes the defect itself dispositive and has no place for an officer's honest reliance as a curative, so the reflex suppresses in exactly the cases Leon admits.","authority":"Good faith: United States v. Leon; Illinois v. Krull; Herring v. United States; Arizona v. Evans; Maryland v. Garrison."},{"n":17,"summary":"A college president and police wire dorm suites; the recordings support a warrant that yields marijuana. Elsewhere an officer exceeds the scope of consent to use a phone, notes caller-ID numbers, and builds a warrant from them.","trigger":"Evidence derived from a prior Fourth Amendment violation → suppressible as fruit of the poisonous tree unless the connection is broken by an independent source, inevitable discovery, or attenuation.","rule":"Evidence derived from an unconstitutional search or seizure is inadmissible as fruit of the poisonous tree, and a warrant procured with illegally obtained information is invalid (Wong Sun v. United States; Silverthorne Lumber). Three limits break the causal chain: INDEPENDENT SOURCE (the evidence was also obtained lawfully — Segura v. United States; Murray v. United States); INEVITABLE DISCOVERY (it would have been found through lawful means anyway — Nix v. Williams); and ATTENUATION, judged by the time elapsed, intervening circumstances, and the flagrancy of the misconduct (Brown v. Illinois; Utah v. Strieff). A defendant's voluntary act — a later Mirandized statement, a new crime committed in response — may attenuate; live-witness testimony is also treated more leniently (United States v. Ceccolini).","sub":"Exclusionary Rule – Fruit of the Poisonous Tree and Its Three Limits (Master)","sensitivity":"The question is never simply 'was there an earlier violation' but whether the challenged evidence is CAUSALLY tied to it and, if so, whether one of the three breakers applies. Note the structural consequence: excising the tainted material from an affidavit can leave enough probable cause to save the warrant.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — derivative proof obtained from an unlawful act is also tainted — is RIGHT: the COPP's licitud-de-la-prueba rule and the doctrine of prueba derivada capture the same idea. The calibration friction lies in the exceptions: U.S. law systematically rescues derivative evidence through inevitable discovery and attenuation, doctrines the civilian nullity framework does not develop — so the reflex suppresses further down the chain than U.S. courts will.","authority":"Fruit of the poisonous tree: Wong Sun v. United States; Silverthorne Lumber; Nix v. Williams; Murray v. United States; Brown v. Illinois; Utah v. Strieff; United States v. Ceccolini."},{"n":18,"summary":"Federal agents abduct a suspect from Mexico without a warrant or foreign consent and break into an accomplice's home to arrest her; both move to DISMISS the indictment.","trigger":"Unlawful arrest, abduction, or entry → the remedy is suppression of the fruits, not dismissal of the charges; personal jurisdiction over the defendant is unaffected.","rule":"An illegal arrest, including a forcible international abduction, does not deprive the court of jurisdiction over the person and does not require dismissal of the indictment (Ker v. Illinois; Frisbie v. Collins; United States v. Alvarez-Machain). The exclusionary consequence is limited to evidence that is the fruit of the violation, and an unlawful arrest without more does not taint a later valid prosecution. The separate violations remain actionable in their own terms: a warrantless nonconsensual entry into a home to make a routine arrest violates the Fourth Amendment (Payton v. New York), and a confession obtained close in time to an unlawful arrest may be suppressed unless attenuated (Brown v. Illinois).","sub":"Remedies – An Illegal Arrest Does Not Void the Prosecution","sensitivity":"The unit separates three questions the answer choices deliberately merge: was there a violation (yes), does the court lose jurisdiction (no), and what is excluded (only the fruits). A motion to dismiss is the wrong vehicle no matter how flagrant the police conduct.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “an unlawful detention voids the proceeding — the process is born tainted and must be annulled” → U.S. law treats the illegality as generating a remedy against the evidence, never against the court's power to try the accused. The civilian schema, in which an aprehensión ilícita triggers immediate release and nullity of subsequent acts (the COPP's regime of nullities), predicts dismissal in precisely the case where U.S. courts proceed to trial.","authority":"Illegal arrest and remedies: Ker v. Illinois; Frisbie v. Collins; United States v. Alvarez-Machain; Payton v. New York; Brown v. Illinois."},{"n":19,"summary":"A routine traffic stop ends with an open-ended question and an admission about drugs; a parolee answers questions at a scheduled parole-office meeting; a man makes an incriminating remark to an officer who responded to his 911 call at home.","trigger":"Statement obtained without warnings → Miranda applies only to CUSTODIAL interrogation: custody means a formal arrest or restraint on freedom of movement of the degree associated with formal arrest, judged objectively.","rule":"Miranda warnings are required only before CUSTODIAL INTERROGATION. Custody exists where the person is formally arrested or his freedom of movement is restrained to a degree associated with formal arrest, judged from the perspective of a reasonable person in the suspect's position; the officer's subjective investigative focus is irrelevant (Stansbury v. California; Howes v. Fields). A routine traffic stop is not custody (Berkemer v. McCarty), nor is a scheduled meeting with a probation or parole officer (Minnesota v. Murphy), nor questioning in the suspect's own home during a consensual encounter (Beckwith v. United States). INTERROGATION means express questioning or its functional equivalent — words or actions police should know are reasonably likely to elicit an incriminating response (Rhode Island v. Innis) — so a volunteered statement is admissible without warnings; and the public-safety exception permits unwarned questions to neutralize immediate danger (New York v. Quarles).","sub":"Fifth Amendment – Miranda Custody and Interrogation (Master)","sensitivity":"Two elements, both required, and the exam usually removes one: an unrestrained motorist or a parolee who walked in is not in custody, and a spontaneous blurt is not interrogation. The objective standard defeats answer choices built on what the officer privately suspected.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “any person questioned by police about a crime must first be advised of his rights — the imputado's guarantees attach on suspicion (the COPP's catalogue of the imputado's rights)” → U.S. warnings are triggered by CUSTODY plus INTERROGATION, not by suspicion, so extensive unwarned questioning on the roadside or at home is perfectly lawful. The civilian schema attaches rights to the status of investigated person rather than to the coerciveness of the setting, producing a systematic over-prediction of suppression.","authority":"Miranda custody and interrogation: Miranda v. Arizona; Berkemer v. McCarty; Minnesota v. Murphy; Beckwith v. United States; Howes v. Fields; Rhode Island v. Innis; New York v. Quarles."},{"n":20,"summary":"An officer gives warnings but omits that counsel can be appointed free of charge; the suspect stays silent and later confesses, then challenges the warnings as deficient.","trigger":"Adequacy of warnings → exact wording is not required so long as the substance is reasonably conveyed, but all four content elements must be present; waiver must be knowing, intelligent, and voluntary, proved by a preponderance.","rule":"Miranda requires that a suspect in custody be advised, before interrogation, of the right to remain silent, that anything said may be used against him, the right to the presence of an attorney, and the right to APPOINTED counsel if he cannot afford one. No precise formula is required and warnings need only reasonably convey these rights (California v. Prysock; Duckworth v. Eagan), but omission of one of the four content elements — notably the indigency component — renders the warnings deficient. A waiver need not be express and may be inferred from conduct, but must be knowing, intelligent, and voluntary; the prosecution bears the burden by a PREPONDERANCE of the evidence (Colorado v. Connelly; North Carolina v. Butler). A statement taken in violation of Miranda is inadmissible in the case-in-chief but usable to impeach (Unit 15), and physical fruits of an unwarned voluntary statement are generally admissible (United States v. Patane).","sub":"Fifth Amendment – Content of Warnings and Validity of Waiver","sensitivity":"Substance over form has a floor: paraphrase is fine, omission is not. The second dial is the standard of proof — waiver by a preponderance, deliberately lower than the beyond-a-reasonable-doubt standard governing elements and sentencing facts (Unit 33).","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — the detainee must be informed of his rights, including the right to a public defender, and cannot validly waive without understanding — is RIGHT and constitutionally entrenched (Const. art. 49(1); the COPP's catalogue of the imputado's rights). The calibration friction: U.S. law tolerates informal paraphrase, accepts implied waiver from conduct, and sets the government's burden at a preponderance — whereas the civilian expects a formal, documented act of information and an express waiver, so the reflex over-invalidates loose warnings and under-appreciates how easily waiver is found.","authority":"Warnings and waiver: California v. Prysock; Duckworth v. Eagan; North Carolina v. Butler; Colorado v. Connelly; United States v. Patane."},{"n":21,"summary":"A suspect who invoked counsel on a burglary is later questioned about an unrelated robbery and confesses; another says he 'might need an attorney' and questioning continues.","trigger":"Invocation of the Miranda right to counsel → must be clear and unambiguous; once validly invoked, ALL custodial interrogation must cease as to ANY offense until counsel is present, subject to the break-in-custody rule.","rule":"The Miranda right to counsel must be invoked clearly and unambiguously; an equivocal reference such as 'maybe I should talk to a lawyer' is insufficient, and police need not seek clarification or stop questioning (Davis v. United States). Once unambiguously invoked, all interrogation must cease and may not be reinitiated by police as to ANY offense — the Fifth Amendment right is NOT offense-specific (Edwards v. Arizona; Arizona v. Roberson) — unless the suspect himself reinitiates the dialogue (Oregon v. Bradshaw) or there has been a break in custody of at least fourteen days (Maryland v. Shatzer). This is the deliberate mirror image of the Sixth Amendment right, which IS offense-specific (Unit 24).","sub":"Fifth Amendment – Invoking the Miranda Right to Counsel; Not Offense-Specific","sensitivity":"Two switches in sequence: was the invocation unambiguous (a hedge fails), and if so the protection is global across offenses. The exam's favorite pairing puts this unit against Unit 24 — same facts, opposite answers, depending on which right the question names.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the right to counsel is one right — once asserted, it governs the whole case” → the U.S. system runs TWO counsel rights with different triggers, different scopes, and different waiver rules, and the answer depends on which one the facts engage. A unitary derecho a la defensa attaching from the first act of the investigation (the COPP's catalogue of the imputado's rights) offers no schema for two coexisting rights whose protections diverge on the very question of whether uncharged offenses are covered.","authority":"Invoking counsel: Edwards v. Arizona; Arizona v. Roberson; Davis v. United States; Oregon v. Bradshaw; Maryland v. Shatzer."},{"n":22,"summary":"A Mirandized suspect sits silently through questioning and then, after an officer's remark, blurts out a partial confession.","trigger":"Invocation of the right to REMAIN SILENT → must also be unambiguous; mere silence is not invocation, and a later voluntary statement after valid warnings is admissible.","rule":"The right to remain silent, like the right to counsel, must be invoked unambiguously: a suspect who simply remains silent for a period has not invoked it, and a subsequent voluntary statement following valid warnings is admissible (Berghuis v. Thompkins). Once silence IS invoked, interrogation must cease — but the protection is weaker than the Edwards counsel rule: police may reapproach after a significant lapse of time, with fresh warnings, about a different offense (Michigan v. Mosley). Post-warning silence may never be used against the defendant at trial (Doyle v. Ohio).","sub":"Fifth Amendment – Invoking the Right to Remain Silent","sensitivity":"The paradox the exam exploits: exercising the right by being silent does not INVOKE the right — the suspect must say so. And the two invocations carry unequal protection: counsel bars all reapproach, silence permits a fresh attempt after a lapse.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the accused's silence IS the exercise of his right — no one may be compelled to testify against himself, and refusal needs no words” → U.S. doctrine requires an affirmative, unambiguous assertion before the protection engages, so prolonged silence followed by one sentence yields an admissible confession. The civilian derecho a no declarar (Const. art. 49(5); the COPP's rules on the imputado's declaration) treats silence as self-executing and never penalizes the failure to articulate it — making this among the least intuitive rules in the Miranda material.","authority":"Invoking silence: Berghuis v. Thompkins; Michigan v. Mosley; Doyle v. Ohio."},{"n":23,"summary":"A post-arrest lineup requires the suspect to repeat the robber's phrase; the victim identifies him by voice. He was never Mirandized and moves to suppress the identification and the voice sample.","trigger":"Compelled participation in identification or bodily-evidence procedures → not testimonial, so the Fifth Amendment and Miranda do not apply; the applicable protections are the Sixth Amendment right to counsel and due process.","rule":"The Fifth Amendment privilege and Miranda protect only TESTIMONIAL or communicative evidence. Compelled participation in a lineup, giving a voice or handwriting exemplar, providing blood, fingerprints, or a DNA sample, and being required to try on clothing are not testimonial, and suppression on Miranda grounds is unavailable (Schmerber v. California; United States v. Wade; Gilbert v. California). The protections that DO apply come from elsewhere: a post-charge live lineup is a critical stage triggering the SIXTH Amendment right to counsel (Wade), and any identification procedure may be challenged as unnecessarily suggestive under due process (Unit 27).","sub":"Fifth Amendment – Testimonial Scope: Lineups and Exemplars Are Not Covered","sensitivity":"The unit works by redirection: the correct answer is rarely 'suppressed' or 'admissible' simpliciter but 'the wrong right was invoked.' Identifying the applicable clause — Fifth (no), Sixth (yes, if post-charge), due process (if suggestive) — is what the question tests.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “no one may be compelled to contribute to his own incrimination — forcing him to speak the robber's words is exactly that” → U.S. law draws the line at COMMUNICATION of assertions, so compelled speech used as a physical voice sample falls outside the privilege. The civilian schema of no autoincriminación treats any coerced cooperation as a violation and lacks the testimonial/physical distinction, so the reflex claims a privilege that does not exist — and misses the Sixth Amendment claim that does.","authority":"Testimonial scope: Schmerber v. California; United States v. Wade; Gilbert v. California."},{"n":24,"summary":"A defendant charged and represented on a robbery is baited by police about a new rape allegation; he reinitiates, is Mirandized, and confesses to the uncharged offense.","trigger":"Sixth Amendment right to counsel → attaches only when adversary judicial proceedings have begun, and is OFFENSE-SPECIFIC: it does not bar questioning about uncharged offenses; it prohibits deliberate elicitation once it attaches.","rule":"The Sixth Amendment right to counsel attaches at the initiation of adversary judicial proceedings — formal charge, preliminary hearing, indictment, information, or arraignment (Rothgery v. Gillespie County) — and is OFFENSE-SPECIFIC: representation on a charged robbery does not bar uncounselled interrogation about an uncharged rape (Texas v. Cobb; McNeil v. Wisconsin). Once attached, the government may not DELIBERATELY ELICIT statements about the charged offense without counsel or a valid waiver, including through informants (Massiah v. United States; United States v. Henry; but Kuhlmann v. Wilson permits passive listening). The right applies at all critical stages: post-charge lineups, arraignment, plea negotiations, trial, sentencing, and a first appeal as of right — but not at photographic arrays (Unit 27) or grand jury proceedings.","sub":"Sixth Amendment – Attachment, Offense Specificity, and Deliberate Elicitation (Master)","sensitivity":"Two gates in order: has the right ATTACHED (formal proceedings commenced), and is the questioning about THAT offense. A defendant with counsel on one charge is fully exposed on another — the mirror image of the Fifth Amendment rule in Unit 21, which is precisely why the exam pairs them.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the defendant has a lawyer — police may not interrogate him at all, about anything” → the U.S. Sixth Amendment protects charge by charge, leaving uncharged conduct open to questioning even from a represented, detained defendant. The Venezuelan derecho a la defensa runs to the PERSON under investigation from the first imputation and admits no offense-by-offense partition, so the reflex produces a categorical prohibition U.S. law does not recognize.","authority":"Sixth Amendment attachment and offense specificity: Rothgery v. Gillespie County; Texas v. Cobb; McNeil v. Wisconsin; Massiah v. United States; United States v. Henry; Kuhlmann v. Wilson."},{"n":25,"summary":"A misdemeanor defendant facing up to six months is denied counsel and a jury, then given 48 hours in jail; another receives a six-month suspended sentence on the same footing.","trigger":"Petty offense prosecution → two independent thresholds: counsel whenever any incarceration is actually imposed (including a suspended term); jury only where the authorized maximum exceeds six months.","rule":"Counsel must be appointed for any indigent defendant in a FELONY prosecution (Gideon v. Wainwright) and in any misdemeanor prosecution in which a term of incarceration is ACTUALLY imposed (Argersinger v. Hamlin; Scott v. Illinois — no imprisonment, no right); a SUSPENDED sentence counts, because it may later be activated (Alabama v. Shelton). The JURY right attaches only to 'serious' offenses, meaning those for which the AUTHORIZED maximum exceeds six months (Baldwin v. New York; Blanton v. North Las Vegas), measured by the authorized penalty rather than the sentence imposed, and multiple petty offenses do not aggregate to create the right (Lewis v. United States). Cross-module: the same doctrine appears as Constitutional Law Unit 46.","sub":"Sixth Amendment – Counsel and Jury Thresholds in Petty Offenses","sensitivity":"Two thresholds measured by different yardsticks: counsel by the sentence ACTUALLY imposed, jury by the maximum AUTHORIZED. A defendant may therefore be validly tried without a jury yet be entitled to appointed counsel — the asymmetry every question in this family turns on.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “defense counsel is mandatory in every criminal proceeding, and the tribunal's composition is fixed by statute according to the offense” → U.S. law rations both rights by numerical thresholds, tolerating an uncounselled conviction where no incarceration follows. The Venezuelan guarantee of assistance by counsel is unconditional (Const. art. 49(1)) and lay participation was a statutory design later abandoned, so the civilian has no schema for a constitutional right that switches on at a sentencing line.","authority":"Counsel and jury thresholds: Gideon v. Wainwright; Argersinger v. Hamlin; Scott v. Illinois; Alabama v. Shelton; Baldwin v. New York; Blanton v. North Las Vegas; Lewis v. United States. Cross-module: Constitutional Law Unit 46."},{"n":26,"summary":"Capital counsel declines to investigate a dysfunctional childhood; another lawyer never interviews an alibi witness; a third wrongly assures a client that a plea will not affect his firearms license.","trigger":"Claim of ineffective assistance → Strickland's two prongs: performance below an objective standard of reasonableness, AND prejudice — a reasonable probability that the result would have been different.","rule":"Ineffective assistance requires (1) DEFICIENT PERFORMANCE — representation falling below an objective standard of reasonableness, reviewed with a strong presumption that counsel's choices were sound strategy — and (2) PREJUDICE, a REASONABLE PROBABILITY that but for counsel's errors the result would have been different: a probability sufficient to undermine confidence in the outcome, not a showing that the result certainly would have changed (Strickland v. Washington). A decision not to investigate is not strategic where counsel never learned what the investigation would reveal, and failure to investigate mitigation in a capital case is deficient (Wiggins v. Smith; Williams v. Taylor). In the plea context, prejudice means a reasonable probability the defendant would not have pleaded guilty (Hill v. Lockhart), and counsel must give correct advice about DEPORTATION consequences (Padilla v. Kentucky) — an exception to the general rule that purely collateral consequences fall outside the duty, though affirmative misadvice presents a stronger claim than silence.","sub":"Sixth Amendment – Ineffective Assistance of Counsel; Plea Advice (Master)","sensitivity":"Both prongs are required and the prejudice standard is the tested one — answer choices that demand certainty of a different outcome overstate it, while those that stop at 'counsel erred' omit it. Padilla's direct/collateral line is the plea refinement: deportation yes, licensing generally no.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “defective defense violates the right to defense — the proceeding is void” → U.S. law demands a showing of OUTCOME-RELATED prejudice, so plainly poor lawyering yields no remedy where the conviction was inevitable. The civilian schema treats indefensión as a structural defect producing nullity without proof of a different result, so the reflex claims relief in exactly the cases Strickland's second prong defeats.","authority":"Ineffective assistance: Strickland v. Washington; Wiggins v. Smith; Williams v. Taylor; Hill v. Lockhart; Padilla v. Kentucky."},{"n":27,"summary":"Officers steer home-invasion witnesses toward suspect number three with incriminating details before the lineup; elsewhere a witness views a photo array and the defendant claims he was entitled to counsel.","trigger":"Challenge to an identification → two separate questions: was there a right to counsel at the procedure (post-charge LIVE lineup yes, photo array no), and was the procedure unnecessarily suggestive so as to violate due process.","rule":"Identification procedures raise two distinct claims. COUNSEL: a post-charge live lineup or showup is a critical stage at which the Sixth Amendment right applies (United States v. Wade; Gilbert v. California), but a PHOTOGRAPHIC array is not, because the accused is not present (United States v. Ash), and neither is a pre-charge lineup. DUE PROCESS: an identification is excluded only if the procedure was unnecessarily suggestive AND the resulting identification was unreliable under the totality — opportunity to view, degree of attention, accuracy of prior description, level of certainty, and time elapsed (Manson v. Brathwaite; Neil v. Biggers). Suggestiveness alone does not exclude, and there is no due process claim at all unless the police ARRANGED the suggestive circumstances (Perry v. New Hampshire).","sub":"Identifications – Counsel at Lineups; Due Process Suggestiveness (Master)","sensitivity":"Two independent tracks, and each has a trap: counsel turns on live-versus-photo and pre-versus-post-charge; due process turns on the second step — reliability can rescue a suggestive identification.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “a suggestive identification is unreliable and therefore void — the reconocimiento must follow the statutory form or it fails” → U.S. law admits a suggestively obtained identification whenever it is nonetheless reliable, converting a procedural defect into a weight question. The Venezuelan reconocimiento de imputados is a formalized judicial act with prescribed conditions (the COPP's rules on the reconocimiento del imputado) whose breach produces nullity, so the reflex expects exclusion where U.S. doctrine performs a reliability rescue.","authority":"Identifications: United States v. Wade; Gilbert v. California; United States v. Ash; Manson v. Brathwaite; Neil v. Biggers; Perry v. New Hampshire."},{"n":28,"summary":"A defendant with an eighth-grade education complains about his public defender; the judge refuses substitution and says his only alternative is self-representation, which he then undertakes at trial.","trigger":"Defendant proceeds without counsel → waiver must be knowing, intelligent, and voluntary and established on the record; dissatisfaction with appointed counsel does not compel substitution, but a coerced choice is not a valid waiver.","rule":"A defendant may waive counsel and represent himself, but only by a knowing, intelligent, and voluntary waiver made on the record after being warned of the dangers and disadvantages of self-representation (Faretta v. California; Johnson v. Zerbst). An indigent defendant has no right to counsel of his choice and mere dissatisfaction does not require substitution — yet presenting self-representation as the only alternative to unwanted counsel, without inquiry into the complaint or any warnings, does not produce a valid waiver. The court may require competence to WAIVE counsel to be assessed separately from competence to stand trial (Indiana v. Edwards), may appoint standby counsel, and may terminate self-representation for disruptive conduct.","sub":"Sixth Amendment – Waiver of Counsel and Self-Representation","sensitivity":"The unit turns on the RECORD: the question is not whether the defendant said the words but whether the court established that he understood what he was giving up. A Hobson's choice framed by the judge is the classic invalid waiver.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “no one may be tried without a defender — self-representation in a serious criminal case is simply not permitted” → U.S. law recognizes an affirmative constitutional RIGHT to refuse counsel and conduct one's own defense. Venezuelan procedure requires technical defense as a condition of validity (the COPP's requirement of technical defence: the imputado must be assisted by a defensor), so the civilian reflex treats self-representation as a structural defect rather than as a right whose exercise requires a documented waiver.","authority":"Waiver of counsel: Faretta v. California; Johnson v. Zerbst; Indiana v. Edwards; McKaskle v. Wiggins (standby counsel)."},{"n":29,"summary":"A disruptive defendant is removed from the courtroom during a witness's testimony after warnings; elsewhere voir dire is conducted in chambers with one defense lawyer and without the defendant.","trigger":"Defendant absent from a portion of the proceedings → he has a right to be present at all critical stages, including jury selection, but forfeits it by persistent disruption after warning.","rule":"A defendant has a right, grounded in the Sixth Amendment's Confrontation Clause and in due process, to be present at every critical stage of the proceedings, including JURY SELECTION (Gomez v. United States; Snyder v. Massachusetts; Illinois v. Allen), so conducting voir dire in chambers without him violates it. The right is forfeitable: a defendant who persists in disruptive conduct after warning may be removed, and the trial may continue in his absence until he agrees to behave (Illinois v. Allen); binding, gagging, or contempt are alternatives. A defendant may also waive presence by voluntarily absconding after trial begins (Crosby v. United States distinguishes pre-trial flight). Removal during CROSS-examination raises an additional Confrontation concern.","sub":"Sixth Amendment / Due Process – Right to Presence and Its Forfeiture","sensitivity":"One right, two directions: the baseline reaches further than candidates expect (chambers voir dire counts) while forfeiture is easier than they expect (disruption after warning suffices). The pairing of the two fact patterns is how the exam tests whether both halves are known.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — the accused must be present at the acts of the process, and disorder may be met with removal by the presiding judge — is broadly RIGHT and matches the oralidad and inmediación principles plus the judge's police power over the hearing. The calibration friction is which acts count as critical: U.S. law treats jury selection as one of them, an act with no counterpart in a system without lay juries.","authority":"Right to presence: Illinois v. Allen; Gomez v. United States; Snyder v. Massachusetts; Crosby v. United States."},{"n":30,"summary":"After conviction the defense learns the prosecution never disclosed that its key witness had a prior conviction for false pretenses, and elsewhere that exculpatory evidence was withheld.","trigger":"Prosecution suppressed evidence favorable to the accused → due process violation only if the evidence was MATERIAL: a reasonable probability that disclosure would have produced a different result.","rule":"The prosecution must disclose evidence favorable to the accused that is material to guilt or punishment, and suppression violates due process irrespective of the prosecutor's good or bad faith (Brady v. Maryland). The duty covers IMPEACHMENT evidence as well as exculpatory evidence (Giglio v. United States — including a key witness's prior conviction), extends to information known to the police even if not to the prosecutor (Kyles v. Whitley), and requires no defense request. MATERIALITY is the operative limit: relief follows only where there is a reasonable probability that disclosure would have changed the result, assessed CUMULATIVELY across all suppressed items (United States v. Bagley; Kyles). There is no general constitutional right to pretrial discovery, and the duty does not extend to evidence the defense could obtain itself.","sub":"Due Process – Brady Disclosure and Materiality","sensitivity":"Three elements: favorable, suppressed, material — and the third does the filtering. Good faith is irrelevant, which surprises candidates; and materiality is cumulative, so several individually trivial omissions can together warrant relief.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT — the prosecutor is bound by objectivity and must act in good faith, seeking the truth rather than a conviction (the COPP's statement of the Ministerio Público's duties: the Ministerio Público must gather evidence that exculpates as well as incriminates). The calibration friction is remedial: U.S. law converts that duty into a post-conviction MATERIALITY inquiry with the burden on the defendant, whereas the civilian expects the file to be open to the defense from the outset, so the reflex under-anticipates how much depends on proving outcome-changing significance.","authority":"Brady: Brady v. Maryland; Giglio v. United States; United States v. Bagley; Kyles v. Whitley."},{"n":31,"summary":"A defendant claiming narcotics intoxication is instructed that he must prove the defense by a preponderance; the jury is also told a Miranda waiver need only be shown by a preponderance.","trigger":"Allocation of the burden of proof → the State must prove every ELEMENT beyond a reasonable doubt, but may place the burden of an AFFIRMATIVE DEFENSE on the defendant; collateral admissibility facts are proved by a preponderance.","rule":"Due process requires the prosecution to prove every element of the offense BEYOND A REASONABLE DOUBT (In re Winship), and a State may not evade that requirement by relabeling an element as a defense (Mullaney v. Wilbur) or by instructing a mandatory presumption that shifts persuasion to the defendant (Sandstrom v. Montana). Within that limit, a State MAY constitutionally place on the defendant the burden of proving a genuine affirmative defense — insanity, duress, intoxication, self-defense in some States — by a preponderance (Patterson v. New York; Leland v. Oregon; Martin v. Ohio). Preliminary and collateral facts governing admissibility, including the validity of a Miranda waiver and the voluntariness of a confession, are decided by the judge on a preponderance (Colorado v. Connelly; Lego v. Twomey).","sub":"Due Process – Burdens of Proof: Elements vs. Affirmative Defenses","sensitivity":"The line is ELEMENT versus DEFENSE, and the legislature's label does not control — Mullaney polices the recharacterization. A third tier sits underneath: admissibility facts never require reasonable doubt, which is why waiver and voluntariness are preponderance questions.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct — in dubio pro reo and the presumption of innocence place the whole burden on the prosecution — is RIGHT as to elements (Const. art. 49(2)). The calibration friction is that U.S. law permits a genuine affirmative defense to be shifted onto the accused, a division the civilian presumption of innocence resists; and it operates a third, lower standard for admissibility questions that the codal framework never articulates.","authority":"Burdens of proof: In re Winship; Mullaney v. Wilbur; Sandstrom v. Montana; Patterson v. New York; Leland v. Oregon; Martin v. Ohio; Lego v. Twomey; Colorado v. Connelly."},{"n":32,"summary":"After overwhelming proof, the judge directs the jury to return a guilty verdict and counsel objects only afterward; elsewhere a biased juror is not struck for cause but is removed by peremptory; and a prosecutor answers a defense missing-evidence argument.","trigger":"Trial error → classify it: a directed guilty verdict is STRUCTURAL and reversible despite the absence of timely objection; an erroneous for-cause ruling cured by peremptory is harmless; a fair response to a defense argument is not a comment on silence.","rule":"A court may direct a verdict of acquittal but NEVER a verdict of guilty; doing so denies the jury-trial right and is a STRUCTURAL error, reviewable as plain error notwithstanding the absence of timely objection and not susceptible to harmless-error analysis (Sullivan v. Louisiana; Rose v. Clark). By contrast, most trial errors are subject to harmless-error review: an erroneous denial of a for-cause challenge does not require reversal where the juror was removed by peremptory and the seated jury was impartial, because the loss of a peremptory is not of constitutional dimension (Rivera v. Illinois; Ross v. Oklahoma) — though a race- or sex-based peremptory is itself a violation (Batson v. Kentucky; J.E.B. v. Alabama). A prosecutor may not comment on the defendant's failure to testify (Griffin v. California), but may fairly respond to a defense argument about missing evidence by noting the defense could have produced it (United States v. Robinson).","sub":"Trial Rights – Directed Verdicts, Harmless vs. Structural Error, Comment on Silence","sensitivity":"Error CLASSIFICATION decides the remedy: structural errors (directed guilty verdict, total denial of counsel, biased judge, denial of public trial) escape harmless-error review; everything else must be shown to have mattered. The Griffin/Robinson seam turns on whether the remark answers the defense or targets the silence.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the judge is the fact-finder and may declare guilt on overwhelming proof; and procedural errors either annul the judgment or they do not” → U.S. law forbids the judge to convict at all and then sorts errors into two remedial classes, one of which tolerates acknowledged constitutional violations. Both features presuppose a lay jury as the constitutionally exclusive convicting body — an institution the civilian system lacks, which is why neither the absolute bar nor the harmless-error apparatus has a codal analogue.","authority":"Trial error classification: Sullivan v. Louisiana; Rose v. Clark; Rivera v. Illinois; Ross v. Oklahoma; Batson v. Kentucky; J.E.B. v. Alabama; Griffin v. California; United States v. Robinson."},{"n":33,"summary":"A statute raises the maximum sentence by two years where the victim was selected by race; elsewhere use of a weapon triggers a mandatory minimum, and the defendant demands a jury finding.","trigger":"Fact that increases the statutory MAXIMUM or triggers a mandatory MINIMUM → it functions as an element: it must be charged, submitted to the jury, and proved beyond a reasonable doubt, except the fact of a prior conviction.","rule":"Other than the fact of a prior conviction, any fact that increases the penalty for a crime beyond the prescribed statutory MAXIMUM must be submitted to a jury and proved beyond a reasonable doubt (Apprendi v. New Jersey), and the same rule applies to facts that trigger a mandatory MINIMUM (Alleyne v. United States). Apprendi governs state sentencing guidelines (Blakely v. Washington), rendered the federal guidelines advisory (United States v. Booker), and requires jury findings of capital aggravating factors (Ring v. Arizona; Hurst v. Florida). The prior-conviction exception survives (Almendarez-Torres v. United States), and judicial fact-finding within an unenhanced statutory range remains permissible.","sub":"Sixth Amendment – Sentencing Facts Are Jury Questions (Apprendi / Alleyne)","sensitivity":"The test is not whether the legislature called the fact an 'element' or a 'sentencing factor' but what it DOES to the authorized range: pushing the ceiling up or the floor up makes it a jury question. Only the prior conviction is exempt.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “aggravating circumstances are matters for the sentencing judge — the code lists them and the court applies them” → U.S. law reassigns any range-altering fact to the jury under the reasonable-doubt standard, dissolving the civilian distinction between the elements of the type and the circumstances of individualization (C.P. arts. 74–77, applied by the judge). The reflex leaves the enhancement with the court, which is precisely the error Apprendi and Alleyne exist to correct.","authority":"Sentencing facts: Apprendi v. New Jersey; Alleyne v. United States; Blakely v. Washington; United States v. Booker; Ring v. Arizona; Hurst v. Florida; Almendarez-Torres v. United States."},{"n":34,"summary":"A grand jury refuses to indict and the prosecutor re-presents months later with more evidence; a kidnapping crossing state lines is prosecuted by both States; a judge abruptly declares a mistrial four hours into trial after a family death, without consulting the parties.","trigger":"Double jeopardy claim → three separate questions: has jeopardy ATTACHED (jury sworn, or first witness sworn in a bench trial); are the prosecuting authorities SEPARATE SOVEREIGNS; and did the mistrial rest on MANIFEST NECESSITY or the defendant's consent.","rule":"Jeopardy attaches when the jury is SWORN, or when the first witness is sworn in a bench trial (Crist v. Bretz; Serfass v. United States) — so a grand jury's refusal to indict is not an acquittal and the prosecutor may re-present to a new grand jury. Under the DUAL SOVEREIGNTY doctrine, successive prosecutions by separate sovereigns for the same conduct are permitted: two States (Heath v. Alabama), or a State and the federal government (Gamble v. United States, 2019) — but municipalities are not separate from their State (Waller v. Florida). After attachment, retrial following a mistrial declared WITHOUT the defendant's consent is barred unless there was manifest necessity, which requires the judge to consider alternatives on the record (Arizona v. Washington; Illinois v. Somerville); retrial is permitted after a defense-requested mistrial, a hung jury, or a reversal on appeal for trial error, but not after an acquittal or a dismissal for insufficient evidence (Burks v. United States).","sub":"Fifth Amendment – Double Jeopardy: Attachment, Dual Sovereignty, Manifest Necessity (Master)","sensitivity":"Three gates, and a question can fail at any one: too early (no attachment), wrong sovereign (no bar), or a mistrial the record justifies. The same-offense inquiry adds a fourth dial — Blockburger asks whether each offense requires proof of an element the other does not.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “non bis in idem is absolute — no one may be tried twice for the same facts (Const. art. 49(7))” → U.S. law permits two sovereigns to prosecute the same conduct sequentially and fixes attachment at a procedural moment rather than at the filing of charges. A unitary state with a single prosecuting authority has no reason to develop a dual-sovereignty exception, so the civilian reflex predicts an absolute bar in precisely the fact pattern the exam builds around federalism.","authority":"Double jeopardy: Crist v. Bretz; Serfass v. United States; Heath v. Alabama; Gamble v. United States (2019); Waller v. Florida; Arizona v. Washington; Burks v. United States; Blockburger v. United States."},{"n":35,"summary":"Police trick a neighbor into wearing a recorder to obtain a confession, believing federal law permits it; clear state supreme court precedent holds the conduct violates the STATE constitution and that exclusion is the remedy.","trigger":"Police conduct valid under the federal Fourth Amendment but unlawful under the state constitution → the state court may suppress: the federal Constitution is a floor, not a ceiling.","rule":"The federal Constitution establishes a MINIMUM level of protection, not a maximum: a State may, through its own constitution or statutes, afford greater protection than the Fourth, Fifth, and Sixth Amendments require, and its courts may order suppression for a state-law violation that federal doctrine would tolerate (PruneYard Shopping Center v. Robins; Oregon v. Hass). The federal Constitution constrains only the STATE's ability to give LESS. A state court that rests its decision on independent state grounds insulates the judgment from Supreme Court review, provided it says so plainly (Michigan v. Long; cf. Constitutional Law Unit 9); silence or federal-law reasoning invites review.","sub":"Federalism – The Federal Constitution Is a Floor, Not a Ceiling","sensitivity":"The unit reframes every other answer in the module: a correct statement of federal doctrine does not settle a state prosecution, because the state charter may be more protective. The companion move is the plain-statement rule — the state court must claim its own ground to keep the case.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “there is one constitution and one standard of rights — the national one, uniformly applied” → in the United States fifty-one charters of rights operate simultaneously, the federal one setting only the minimum, so identical police conduct can be lawful in one State and suppressible in the next. A unitary constitutional order in which the national constitution is the exhaustive source of fundamental rights (Const. arts. 7, 19) supplies no schema for cumulative, competing rights floors — making this the module's clearest structural lesson.","authority":"Federal floor, not ceiling: PruneYard Shopping Center v. Robins; Oregon v. Hass; Michigan v. Long. Cross-module: Constitutional Law Unit 9."}],"cases":[{"n":1,"summary":"A defendant was arrested and charged with burglary. Under state law, police are required to obtain DNA samples from all persons arrested for felonies and to store those samples in a state registry. The police informed the defendant of the state law and said that they would be using a cheek swab to obtain a DNA sample from him.","trigger":"DNA taken from felony arrestee","rule":"DNA collection from felony arrestees does not violate the Fourth Amendment.","sub":"DNA / 4A","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":14},{"n":2,"summary":"Assigned counsel for a capital defendant had heard that his client had an unsavory background and a highly dysfunctional childhood. He was reluctant to delve into the details for fear that they might reflect poorly on his client, and he decided not to investigate. After his client was convicted, counsel presented a penalty phase defense that repeated the denials of the liability phase.","trigger":"Capital counsel fails to investigate mitigation","rule":"Failure to investigate mitigation in capital cases is ineffective assistance.","sub":"Innefective Assistance of Counsel","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":26},{"n":3,"summary":"Traffic stop; strong marijuana odor; driver admits small amount; arrested; officer pressures consent to search trunk; finds machine gun in pried-open briefcase.","trigger":"Probable cause to search vehicle for contraband under automobile exception (scope includes containers). Plain smell is the same as plain view (marijuana). Brief detention: Reasonable suspicion, for arrest probable cause. Note: In a car stop if there is PC of drugs, the police can search any container in search of, and is he finds other contraband it will be a legal search and seizure (plain view)","rule":"Under the auto exception, an officer can search anywhere in the vehicle (without a warrant) where he has probable cause to believe contraband can be found.","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Automobile Exception (Scope)","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":7},{"n":4,"summary":"Neighbor theft suspicion; homeowner searches neighbor’s porch/deck box and home; officer stays outside and later finds monitor under tarp on porch.","trigger":"Private citizen search + police encouragement/participation to turn private search into government action.","rule":"A 4th Amendment violation requires government action, which is when a government actor acts himself or encourages a non-government actor to act.","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Government Action / Private Search","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":4},{"n":5,"summary":"Misdemeanor theft max 6 months/$500; defendant requests counsel and jury; denied; sentenced to 48 hours jail.","trigger":"Right to counsel attaches when actual jail imposed; jury right depends on >6 months authorized.","rule":"A defendant has a right to counsel for any felony OR a misdemeanor where actual jail time is imposed. The right to jury usually applies when the defendant is charged with a crime with a possible punishment of more than six months.","sub":"6A – Counsel & Jury (Petty Offenses)","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":25},{"n":6,"summary":"Convicted attempted murder; claims ineffective assistance; lawyer failed to interview alibi witness.","trigger":"Strickland: deficient performance + prejudice (different outcome but-for error).","rule":"Ineffective assistance of counsel requires that the attorney’s conduct falls below the standard of care AND that but for the attorney’s conduct the results would have been different.","sub":"6A – Ineffective Assistance (Strickland)","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":26},{"n":7,"summary":"Charged/represented for robbery; later police taunt leads to discussion about new rape allegation; Mirandized; confession; claims 6A counsel violation.","trigger":"6th Amendment right to counsel is offense/charge specific. Aplies once Adverserial Judicial Proceedings (AJP)commence.Once invoked the Police: Not deliberalty elicit any statement","rule":"The 6th Amendment right to counsel is charge specific, meaning that it only applies to the charged crime for which the defendant has retained counsel.","sub":"6A Right to Counsel – Charge Specific","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":24},{"n":8,"summary":"Prosecution fails to disclose witness’s prior conviction for false pretenses; defense seeks new trial.","trigger":"Brady violation requires reasonable probability of different outcome.","rule":"To be granted a new trial, a defendant usually has to show that but for the violation, there is a reasonable probability that the outcome would have been different.","sub":"Due Process / Brady Materiality","family":"Due Process","unit":30},{"n":9,"summary":"Attempted burglary defendant claimed narcotics intoxication; jury instructed defendant must prove defense by preponderance","trigger":"Due process challenge to burden on defendant for an affirmative defense","rule":"Due process allows the burden to be on a criminal defendant to prove a defense by a preponderance of the evidence.","sub":"Due Process/Burdens","family":"Due Process","unit":31},{"n":10,"summary":"Attempted-murder defendant claims ineffective assistance; strongest showing is that counsel failed to interview an alibi witness.","trigger":"Ineffective assistance requires deficient performance plus prejudice—failing to investigate an alibi witness shows both.","rule":"Ineffective assistance of counsel requires that the attorney’s conduct falls below the standard of care AND that but for the attorney’s conduct the results would have been different.","sub":"Effective Assistance of Counsel","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":26},{"n":11,"summary":"Warrantless home entry; evidence used for deportation and criminal harboring charge","trigger":"Exclusionary rule requested in civil vs criminal use","rule":"The exclusionary rule does not apply in civil deportation proceedings but may apply to criminal charges","sub":"Exclusionary Rule","family":"Exclusionary Rule","unit":15},{"n":12,"summary":"Anonymous tip drugs in house; officer gets no response and kicks in door; drugs/cash/scales in plain view; suppression motion at grand jury stage.","trigger":"Evidence suppression sought in grand jury proceeding (exclusionary rule applicability). Current: Informant tip leads to PC depends on the totality of the circumstances.","rule":"The exclusionary rule does not apply to grand jury proceedings, where the rules of evidence are relaxed.","sub":"Exclusionary Rule – Grand Jury","family":"Exclusionary Rule","unit":15},{"n":13,"summary":"Anonymous tip about drug dealing; officer forcibly kicks in door to home without warrant and finds drugs; defendant moves to suppress at grand jury proceeding.","trigger":"Use of illegally obtained evidence in a grand jury proceeding (relaxed rules of evidence).","rule":"The exclusionary rule does not apply to grand jury proceedings, where the rules of evidence are relaxed.","sub":"Exclusionary Rule – Grand Jury Proceedings","family":"Exclusionary Rule","unit":15},{"n":14,"summary":"Driver stopped for speeding; strong marijuana odor, admission to possessing some; after arrest, officer searches trunk and pries open briefcase, finding machine gun.","trigger":"Probable cause plus vehicle → automobile exception allows search of entire car and containers.","rule":"Under the auto exception, an officer can search anywhere in the vehicle (without a warrant) where he has probable cause to believe contraband can be found.","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Automobile Exception","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":7},{"n":15,"summary":"Neighbor urges police to search; after officer says he can't enter but 'nothing stops you,' victim enters neighbor's home and finds laptop while officer finds monitor on porch; phone already discovered on deck box.","trigger":"Officer encouraging private person to search another's property (government action vs private search).","rule":"A 4th Amendment violation requires government action, which is when a government actor acts himself or encourages a non-government actor to act.","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Government Action / Private Search","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":4},{"n":16,"summary":"Police, with hotel owner’s ‘consent,’ install a one-way mirror into suspect’s hotel bathroom and watch him use cocaine; suspect moves to suppress.","trigger":"Hotel room carries reasonable expectation of privacy; warrant generally required for such surveillance/search.","rule":"A person will have an expectation of privacy in a hotel room that they rent.","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Government Action / Private Search","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":2},{"n":17,"summary":"State takes DNA cheek swab from felony arrestee and later matches it to blood on knife from another crime.","trigger":"SCOTUS: DNA collection from felony arrestees is a reasonable search.","rule":"The Supreme Court has found that obtaining and storing DNA sample of suspected felons is not an unreasonable search.","sub":"Fourth Amendment / DNA Collection","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":14},{"n":18,"summary":"Officer enters apartment under apparent emergency; on leaving, sees Altoid tin fall open revealing marijuana.","trigger":"Plain view: lawful entry + immediately apparent contraband.","rule":"Plain view, a search warrant exception, requires that the officer, from a lawful vantage point, immediately recognizes an item as illegal, without any type of manipulation of the item.","sub":"Fourth Amendment / Emergency Aid & Plain View","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":8},{"n":19,"summary":"Officer with reliable kidnapping tip goes to suspect home without warrant; sees blood on doorknob and door ajar; after knocking hears scuffling; enters and sees cocaine; arrests for drugs; moves to suppress.","trigger":"Warrantless home entry justified by exigent circumstances not created by police.","rule":"One of the exceptions to requiring a search warrant is exigent circumstances. Exigent circumstances require an emergency situation that was not caused by the government.","sub":"Fourth Amendment / Exigent Circumstances","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":9},{"n":20,"summary":"Officers with valid warrant fail knock‑and‑announce and detain homeowner during search, finding illegal weapons.","trigger":"Knock‑and‑announce violation and detention during search do not trigger exclusion.","rule":"Although a police officer failing to knock and announce his presence when executing a valid search warrant is a 4th Amendment violation, the courts do not usually suppress evidence found when there is a failure to follow knock and announce rules. An officer may detain any occupants of a private residence when executing a valid search warrant of that residence.","sub":"Fourth Amendment / Knock-and-Announce & Detention","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":6},{"n":21,"summary":"Home invasion; victims unsure; officer steers them to suspect #3 with incriminating facts/arrest history; later certainty; move to suppress ID.","trigger":"Unduly suggestive identification procedure violating due process.","rule":"If a lineup is unduly suggestive, it will violate the suspect’s Due Process rights.","sub":"Identification – Due Process (Suggestive Lineup)","family":"Identifications","unit":27},{"n":22,"summary":"Home-invasion victims see masked intruder; in lineup of four similar men, officer tells them suspect #3 was found near house with duffle bag and has prior home invasions; they then identify #3.","trigger":"Unduly suggestive lineup procedure that steers witnesses to pick a particular suspect.","rule":"If a lineup is unduly suggestive, it will violate the suspect’s Due Process rights.","sub":"Identifications – Unduly Suggestive Lineups / Due Process","family":"Identifications","unit":27},{"n":23,"summary":"Theft defendant pleads to misdemeanor to avoid felony; repeatedly asks counsel about concealed-carry license impact; counsel wrongly says no impact; after plea, license revoked due to third offense; moves to vacate for ineffective assistance.","trigger":"Guilty plea challenged for ineffective assistance based on incorrect legal advice causing harmful consequence.","rule":"Ineffective assistance of counsel requires that an attorney’s deficient performance causes prejudice to the client.","sub":"Ineffective Assistance / Plea","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":26},{"n":24,"summary":"Biased former coworker not struck for cause, but defense uses peremptory; defendant later convicted.","trigger":"Erroneous failure to strike for cause is harmless if juror removed with peremptory and jury impartial.","rule":"Even if the judge makes a mistake, if it is a harmless error, a conviction will not be set aside.","sub":"Jury Selection / Harmless Error","family":"Trial Rights","unit":32},{"n":25,"summary":"Routine traffic stop ending; after returning documents officer asks open-ended question; driver admits drugs in glove compartment; no Miranda warnings.","trigger":"Whether roadside questioning during routine traffic stop is “custody” for Miranda.","rule":"Custody, for Miranda purposes, requires that the suspect be deprived of his freedom in a significant way.","sub":"Miranda – Custody (Traffic Stop)","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":19},{"n":26,"summary":"Officer about to let speeder go asks, 'Is there anything else I need to know?'; driver, not Mirandized, admits drugs in glove compartment during routine stop.","trigger":"Routine traffic stop is generally noncustodial for Miranda purposes.","rule":"Custody, for Miranda purposes, requires that the suspect be deprived of his freedom in a significant way.","sub":"Miranda – Custody (Traffic Stops)","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":19},{"n":27,"summary":"Post-arrest lineup; suspect required to speak perpetrator’s phrase; victim IDs by voice; no Miranda warnings.","trigger":"Whether lineup/voice exemplar is testimonial for Miranda suppression.","rule":"Miranda only suppresses evidence that is testimonial in nature. Lineups and voice samples are not considered testimonial.","sub":"Miranda – Testimonial Evidence","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":23},{"n":28,"summary":"Robbery suspect in lineup repeats robber’s phrase; victim identifies him; suspect not Mirandized and seeks to suppress lineup and voice sample.","trigger":"Miranda suppresses only testimonial evidence, not physical or identification evidence like lineups and voice samples.","rule":"Miranda only suppresses evidence that is testimonial in nature. Lineups and voice samples are not considered testimonial.","sub":"Miranda – Testimonial vs Physical Evidence","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":23},{"n":29,"summary":"Suspect invoked Miranda counsel on burglary, later questioned without counsel about unrelated robbery and confessed.","trigger":"Miranda right to counsel is not offense-specific; bars all further custodial interrogation.","rule":"The Miranda right to counsel (5th amendment) is not crime specific, meaning it applies to any crime that the defendant is interrogated about, not just one he has already been charged with but must de unambiguosuly invoked. Sixth Amendment = offense-specific, charge-based Miranda = not offense-specific, but must be unambiguously invoked","sub":"Miranda - Counsel Not Crime-Specific","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":21},{"n":30,"summary":"Parole officer schedules meeting with parolee at parole office about ‘a matter’; questions about burglaries; parolee admits within 5 minutes; no Miranda warnings; moves to suppress.","trigger":"Miranda required only for custodial interrogation; custody = significant restraint on freedom.","rule":"For Miranda purposes, custody is defined as being deprived of freedom in a significant way.","sub":"Miranda - Custody","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":19},{"n":31,"summary":"Suspect says he ‘might need an attorney’; officer doesn’t clarify and keeps questioning; suspect confesses.","trigger":"Right to counsel must be clearly and unambiguously invoked.","rule":"The Miranda right to counsel must be asserted clearly and unambiguously. The police do not have to clarify whether a suspect is attempting to invoke their right to counsel.","sub":"Miranda / Right to Counsel","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":21},{"n":32,"summary":"Officer gives Miranda warnings but omits that counsel can be appointed for free; suspect stays silent, later confesses during custodial questioning, claiming warnings were deficient.","trigger":"Miranda warnings must reasonably convey rights; exact wording not required (including appointed counsel concept).","rule":"Miranda warnings do not have to be exact; they just have to be reasonably conveyed.","sub":"Miranda - Right to Counsel","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":20},{"n":33,"summary":"Suspect Mirandized, sits silently; later blurts out partial confession after officer’s comment.","trigger":"Silence alone is not invocation of right to remain silent.","rule":"A right to remain silent must be asserted or it may be waived. A suspect does not invoke their right to remain silent by merely remaining silent for a period of time.","sub":"Miranda - Right to Remain Silent","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":22},{"n":34,"summary":"Defendant charged with misdemeanor theft carrying up to six months; judge denies counsel and jury, then imposes 48 hours in jail.","trigger":"Actual jail time on a misdemeanor triggers right to counsel, but jury right generally requires authorized punishment over six months.","rule":"A defendant has a right to counsel for any felony OR a misdemeanor where actual jail time is imposed. The right to jury usually applies when the defendant is charged with a crime with a possible punishment of more than six months.","sub":"Right to Counsel & Jury – Petty Offenses","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":25},{"n":35,"summary":"Question asks when denial of requested counsel violates right to counsel (felony vs misdemeanor, etc.).","trigger":"Automatic right to counsel in all felony prosecutions.","rule":"All defendants being tried for felonies have a right to counsel. Usually, defendants being tried for misdemeanors will have a right to counsel if actual jail time is imposed.","sub":"Right to Counsel / Felony vs Misdemeanor","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":25},{"n":36,"summary":"Police obtained a warrant for 123 Jones Street to search for illegal drugs, but the building was a duplex. Officers searched the front unit (occupied by a woman not named) and found cocaine in her purse. The named defendant lived in the rear unit. Woman moves to suppress.","trigger":"Good-faith execution of warrant: reasonable mistake in searching wrong unit can still allow admissibility.","rule":"When police execute a search warrant reasonably and in good faith, even evidence found by mistake will be admissible.","sub":"Search & Seizure – Good Faith","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":5},{"n":37,"summary":"Assault sentence enhanced if victim selected by race; defendant requested jury trial","trigger":"Fact increases statutory maximum sentence","rule":"In a criminal case with a jury, the jury must find each fact that changes the potential sentencing beyond a reasonable doubt.","sub":"Sentencing (Apprendi)","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":33},{"n":38,"summary":"Robbery arrestee invokes counsel and is told not to be questioned; days later officer baits him about rape, defendant re-initiates, is Mirandized, and admits sex in rape case.","trigger":"Sixth Amendment right to counsel attaches and is charge specific to the offense for which defendant has counsel.","rule":"The 6th Amendment right to counsel is charge specific, meaning that it only applies to the charged crime for which the defendant has retained counsel.","sub":"Sixth Amendment – Charge-Specific Right to Counsel","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":24},{"n":39,"summary":"Same facts as 77, but issue is Sixth Amendment right to counsel on uncharged robbery.","trigger":"Sixth Amendment right attaches only to charged offenses (crime-specific).","rule":"The Sixth Amendment right to counsel is crime specific, meaning it only applies to crimes that adversarial judicial proceedings have begun on.","sub":"Miranda - Counsel Not Crime-Specific","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":24},{"n":40,"summary":"Bar fight involves men in white and blue shirts; blue-shirt leaves with two friends in red shirts; officer orders all three to stop and arrests one red-shirt friend who keeps walking away.","trigger":"Mere association with a suspect in a public place, without individualized suspicion, is not enough for a Terry stop.","rule":"Being in the company of a suspect in a public place, without more, does not give an officer the reasonable suspicion that is required for a Terry stop.","sub":"Stops & Frisks – Reasonable Suspicion / Association","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":12},{"n":41,"summary":"Bar fight disturbance; officer stops blue shirt plus two red-shirt friends; one red shirt walks away; arrested for noncompliance.","trigger":"Terry stop requires individualized reasonable suspicion; mere association insufficient. In a public place but if in a private place (house) YES.","rule":"Being in the company of a suspect in a public place, without more, does not give an officer the reasonable suspicion that is required for a Terry stop.","sub":"Terry Stops – Reasonable Suspicion","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":12},{"n":42,"summary":"Police received a reliable tip that a drug dealer would bring drugs to a house party on a specific date. Two days before the party, a judge issued a search warrant to search the house when the party occurred. When officers executed it, they searched the house pursuant to the warrant for drugs expected to be there at that future time.","trigger":"Anticipatory warrant: validity depends on probable cause that evidence will be at the location when the warrant is executed (future triggering condition).","rule":"An anticipatory warrant is valid if it based on probable cause that the evidence will be in the place to be searched at the time it is to be searched.","sub":"Search Warrants – Anticipatory","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":5},{"n":43,"summary":"A defendant was convicted of battery after a jury trial. After trial, he learned the prosecution failed to disclose exculpatory evidence. He argues a constitutional violation occurred and seeks to overturn the verdict based on the nondisclosure.","trigger":"Brady-type nondisclosure: conviction only overturned if the withheld evidence was material—i.e., reasonable probability the result would have been different.","rule":"Even if there is an alleged constitutional violation, a verdict based on non-disclosure of exculpatory evidence by the prosecution will not be overturned if it cannot be shown that that there is a reasonable probability of a different outcome if the violation had not occurred.","sub":"Brady – Materiality","family":"Due Process","unit":30},{"n":44,"summary":"At a sexual assault trial, defendant repeatedly interrupts and threatens the witness despite warnings; the judge removes him from the courtroom until the witness finishes (including cross), then allows him back. Defendant appeals claiming his constitutional rights were violated by the exclusion.","trigger":"A criminal defendant’s right to be present can be forfeited when the defendant is disruptive in court after warnings.","rule":"Although a party cannot be generally excluded from a proceeding, they forfeit the right to be present if their courtroom behavior is disruptive.","sub":"Trial Rights – Right to be present; forfeiture by disruptive conduct","family":"Sixth Amendment / Due Process","unit":29},{"n":45,"summary":"Officer stops a driver, arrests him (expired license), and—without Miranda—asks why he lacks required paperwork; driver admits he forcefully took the car. At trial for theft, defendant testifies the owner gave permission. Prosecution wants to use the unwarned statement.","trigger":"Un-Mirandized statements are generally excluded from the prosecution’s case-in-chief but may be used to impeach inconsistent trial testimony.","rule":"Statements obtained in violation of Miranda can be used to impeach a witness at trial.","sub":"Miranda – Impeachment use of unwarned statements","family":"Exclusionary Rule","unit":15},{"n":46,"summary":"Defendant is out on bail awaiting a burglary trial and calls 911 when his son has a seizure. An officer responds to help, then remarks that defendant should hide drugs and points to marijuana on the counter; defendant makes an incriminating statement. Issue is whether the statement is admissible without Miranda warnings.","trigger":"Miranda applies only to custodial interrogation; question is whether defendant was in 'custody' in his home during the exchange.","rule":"Before Miranda warnings are required, there must be a custodial interrogation. Custody means that the suspect was deprived of their freedom in a significant way.","sub":"Miranda - Custody requirement / custodial interrogation","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":19},{"n":47,"summary":"Defendant is tried for forcible rape; the victim identifies him, and defendant does not testify. Defense argues the state’s failure to introduce defendant’s clothing suggests it would be unfavorable; prosecutor responds that the defense could have produced the clothing if it helped them.","trigger":"Prosecutor rebuts a defense 'missing evidence' inference; allowed as long as it is not a comment on the defendant’s silence.","rule":"A prosecutor may rebut defense claims about missing evidence by noting the defense could have produced it, so long as it is not a comment on the defendant’s silence.","sub":"Trial – Closing argument / comment on evidence vs silence","family":"Trial Rights","unit":32},{"n":48,"summary":"College president arranges for police to place concealed microphones in student dorm suites to record conversations about drugs. Tape is used to obtain a warrant; police search and find marijuana. Student moves to suppress the marijuana as fruit of an illegal search.","trigger":"Evidence from a warrant is suppressed if the warrant was based on illegally obtained evidence; recordings made where there is a reasonable expectation of privacy are searches and trigger fruit-of-the-poisonous-tree.","rule":"A warrant based on illegally obtained evidence is invalid, and evidence seized is suppressible as fruit of the poisonous tree (government eavesdropping where there’s a reasonable expectation of privacy is a search).","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Fruit of the poisonous tree / warrant based on illegal search","family":"Exclusionary Rule","unit":17},{"n":49,"summary":"Police show a witness a photo array to identify a suspect. Defendant later argues he was entitled to counsel at the lineup and that the identification should be excluded because the procedure was unfair.","trigger":"No right to counsel at photo lineup; identification excluded only if unnecessarily suggestive and violates due process.","rule":"A photo lineup does not trigger a right to counsel (not a critical stage) and is excluded only if it is unnecessarily suggestive so as to violate due process; otherwise the identification is admissible.","sub":"Identification – Photo lineup (no counsel; due process suggestiveness)","family":"Identifications","unit":27},{"n":50,"summary":"After a liquor store robbery, police get an anonymous call naming a store employee. Believing federal law permits their conduct, police trick a neighbor into going to the employee’s home with a hidden tape recorder to get the employee to talk; the employee admits committing the robbery. The employee is charged in state court and moves to suppress the recording, and there is clear state supreme court precedent that the police conduct violated the state constitution and that exclusion is the proper remedy.","trigger":"State constitutional rule provides broader protection than federal Fourth Amendment; state court may suppress under state constitution even if federal law would not.","rule":"The federal Constitution is a floor, not a ceiling—states may give greater protections, and state courts may suppress evidence for state-constitutional violations even if federal law wouldn’t.","sub":"Search & Seizure – State constitution can exceed federal floor","family":"Federalism","unit":35},{"n":51,"summary":"FBI agents, without a warrant and without permission of Mexican officials, enter Mexico, kidnap a U.S. citizen wanted for drug smuggling, and forcibly bring him to Texas. Later, without a warrant, agents break into the Texas home of the accomplice and arrest her. Both defendants move to dismiss their narcotics indictment on the ground that their arrests violated the Fourth Amendment.","trigger":"Illegal arrest/abduction without warrant; remedy is not dismissal of indictment (suppression vs dismissal).","rule":"An illegal arrest/abduction does not require dismissal of the indictment; exclusionary rule applies only to suppress evidence that is the fruit of the Fourth Amendment violation.","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Illegal arrest; remedies","family":"Remedies","unit":18},{"n":52,"summary":"A robbery suspect is arrested and placed in a lineup with similar‑looking fillers; the victim immediately identifies him. The suspect requests counsel and argues police never Mirandized him. Defense moves to suppress the victim’s lineup identification at trial based on the lack of Miranda warnings (and counsel).","trigger":"Miranda is a testimonial‑evidence protection; identification procedures like lineups are non‑testimonial and are not suppressed as Miranda violations.","rule":"Miranda violations are only used to suppress testimonial evidence, not things such as lineups or voice samples.","sub":"Miranda – Scope (testimonial evidence only; lineups/voice samples not suppressed)","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":23},{"n":53,"summary":"Police receive a tip that a man has a stolen gun and plans a robbery. Without a warrant, officers go to his home; when he partially opens the door, an officer sees a gun on a table inside and shouts “Gun!” They enter, seize the gun, and later check the serial number to confirm it is stolen. The defendant moves to suppress the gun in theft and felon‑in‑possession prosecutions.","trigger":"Plain‑view seizure requires the incriminating nature to be immediately apparent; needing to manipulate/inspect (e.g., run a serial number) defeats plain view.","rule":"If an item has to be moved or manipulated in any way to discover its evidentiary value, it will not be considered plain view.","sub":"Search & Seizure – Plain view (immediately apparent incriminating nature)","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":8},{"n":54,"summary":"The local prosecutor brought charges against a defendant for murder. A grand jury was impaneled and found that there wasn’t enough evidence that the defendant was involved. A few months later, the same prosecutor, armed with more evidence, impaneled a grand jury for the same issue of whether the defendant was guilty of the same murder the previous grand jury decided. The defendant’s attorney made a motion to dismiss based on Double Jeopardy grounds. How should the court rule on the motion to dismiss?","trigger":"Double jeopardy timing: jury sworn or first witness sworn in bench trial.","rule":"Double jeopardy does not attach until a jury is empaneled or, in a bench trial, until the first witness is sworn in.","sub":"Double Jeopardy – Attachment","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":34},{"n":55,"summary":"A police officer obtains a search warrant from a magistrate judge based on a sworn affidavit from the suspect’s associate claiming the suspect has child pornography at home. The officer executes the warrant and finds illegal pornography. Later it is discovered the associate fabricated the affidavit at the magistrate’s insistence because the magistrate feared for his children. The suspect challenges admissibility of the pornography.","trigger":"Search warrant process corrupted by a magistrate who is not neutral/detached; evaluate good-faith exception to exclusionary rule.","rule":"The good-faith exception does not apply when the issuing magistrate abandons the neutral and detached role, so evidence obtained under such a warrant is suppressed.","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Exclusionary rule; good-faith limits","family":"Exclusionary Rule","unit":16},{"n":56,"summary":"A woman is suspected of growing large amounts of marijuana in her basement. Police, from outside the house, see powerful lights running at all hours of the night in the basement, smell marijuana coming from basement windows, and obtain a neighbor affidavit stating the woman admitted she grew marijuana in the basement. Based on this evidence, police obtain a search warrant for the basement of the woman’s house. Police enter the house and detain the woman while they search. They find marijuana in the basement and also enter the woman’s attic, where they find a large cache of illegally modified weapons. The woman moves to suppress the weapons.","trigger":"Search warrant tied to drug activity at a residence + officers search additional area of the home and seize different contraband; suppression turns on scope/places drugs could reasonably be found.","rule":"A warrant to search a residence for contraband authorizes officers to search the areas of the home where the item sought could reasonably be found, and contraband discovered during that authorized search may be seized.","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Warrant scope; suppression","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":5},{"n":57,"summary":"Officer goes to neighbor’s home to look for a stolen lawn chair. Neighbor consents to the officer looking inside. Officer sees a chair under a blanket; neighbor then revokes consent and demands the officer leave. Officer lifts the blanket, confirms the tag, and seizes the chair.","trigger":"Consent search with consent revoked mid-search; officer continues and seizes evidence.","rule":"Consent searches are valid only while consent remains; once consent is revoked, officers must stop searching unless another exception applies (e.g., a warrant, exigency, or true plain view).","sub":"Fourth Amendment – Consent; revocation","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":11},{"n":58,"summary":"At a trial for armed bank robbery, the prosecution presents overwhelming evidence (multiple teller identifications, fingerprint on the counter, and bank money found in the defendant’s car). After the prosecution rests, the judge directs the jury to return a guilty verdict. Defense counsel does not object until after the jury returns the verdict. The defendant is sentenced to 20 years and appeals.","trigger":"Trial judge directs a guilty verdict in a criminal case; appellate review despite lack of timely objection.","rule":"Directed guilty verdict in criminal case violates the jury-trial right (plain error).","sub":"Jury Trial Right – Directed verdict","family":"Trial Rights","unit":32},{"n":59,"summary":"Kidnapping begins in State A and continues into State B; convicted in State A; State B prosecutes under identical law; defendant moves to dismiss on double jeopardy.","trigger":"Double jeopardy; dual sovereignty doctrine (different states).","rule":"Double jeopardy does not bar successive prosecutions by separate sovereigns (e.g., different states) for the same conduct.","sub":"Double Jeopardy – Dual Sovereignty","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":34},{"n":60,"summary":"Undercover officer gains entry after lying about being a buyer; while homeowner is in another room, officer lifts papers on a desk and finds credit cards; seizes cards; defendant moves to suppress.","trigger":"Officer manipulates/moves items to discover evidence; government claims plain view; entry by deception vs later search.","rule":"Plain view requires lawful access and immediately apparent illegality WITHOUT moving/manipulating items; moving papers is a search requiring a warrant/exception. A person like in this case that lies to get in does not violate an expectation of privacy because the person let him in. Do you have an expectation of privacy if you let anyone come into your house?","sub":"Fourth Amendment — Plain View / Search (manipulation defeats plain view)","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":2},{"n":61,"summary":"State defendant seeks post-conviction relief alleging ineffective assistance; court finds counsel deficient for failing to object to evidence; asks what more must be found.","trigger":"IAC claim after conviction; deficiency established; missing element is prejudice standard.","rule":"To prove ineffective assistance, defendant must show a reasonable probability that, but for counsel’s errors, the result would have been different (Strickland prejudice).","sub":"Sixth Amendment — Ineffective Assistance (Strickland: reasonable probability)","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":26},{"n":62,"summary":"Capital murder defendant’s jury selection conducted in judge’s chambers with only one defense lawyer present; defendant remained in courtroom during voir dire; question: constitutional violation.","trigger":"Defendant excluded from voir dire/jury selection after charges filed (AJP begun).","rule":"Jury selection is a critical stage; the defendant has a right to be present (Sixth Amendment / Due Process).","sub":"Sixth Amendment — Right to Presence at Critical Stages (Voir Dire)","family":"Sixth Amendment / Due Process","unit":29},{"n":63,"summary":"Police with probable cause knock motel door; hear yelling and toilet flushing; enter with passkey and see drugs destroyed.","trigger":"Probable cause + imminent destruction of evidence.","rule":"Exigent circumstances (destruction of evidence) justify warrantless entry.","sub":"Fourth Amendment — Exigent Circumstances","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":9},{"n":64,"summary":"Police enter suspect’s home with arrest warrant; conduct protective sweep; find cocaine in closet.","trigger":"Arrest warrant + reasonable suspicion accomplice present.","rule":"During lawful protective sweep, contraband in plain view is admissible.","sub":"Fourth Amendment — Protective Sweep / Plain View","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":10},{"n":65,"summary":"Four hours into jury assault trial, judge abruptly declared mistrial due to death in wife’s family and discharged jury without consulting parties. Defendant objected to retrial on double jeopardy grounds.","trigger":"Mistrial declared without defendant consent and without manifest necessity.","rule":"After jeopardy attaches, retrial following mistrial is barred unless there was manifest necessity or defendant consented.","sub":"Double Jeopardy — Manifest Necessity","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":34},{"n":66,"summary":"A defendant charged with armed robbery had only an eighth-grade education and no legal knowledge. At a pretrial hearing, he told the judge he was unhappy with his appointed public defender and wanted another lawyer. The judge refused and, with no further explanation, told the defendant his only other choice was to represent himself. The defendant said he would represent himself because anything was better than having that lawyer. He did not raise the subject again and represented himself at trial. After overwhelming evidence of guilt, he appealed, claiming denial of counsel.","trigger":"Self-representation after judge refuses substitute counsel; question is whether there was a valid waiver of the Sixth Amendment right to counsel.","rule":"A waiver of the right to counsel must be knowing, intelligent, and voluntary and must be established on the record; absent a clear, valid waiver, forcing self-representation violates the Sixth Amendment.","sub":"Right to Counsel / Waiver (Faretta) / Knowing-Intelligent-Voluntary","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":28},{"n":67,"summary":"In a criminal jury trial, a fact finding will increase the defendant’s potential sentence beyond what would otherwise apply. The question tests the rule that any fact (other than a prior conviction) that increases the statutory maximum or mandatory minimum must be found by the jury beyond a reasonable doubt.","trigger":"Sentencing-enhancing fact in jury case must be proved beyond a reasonable doubt.","rule":"In a criminal case with a jury, the jury must find each fact that changes the potential sentencing beyond a reasonable doubt. If using a weapon going to get you a highet charge it counts as an element of the crime. Elements of a crime and sentencing factores BOP: Beyond a reasonable doubt. That a defendant waived his miranda warnings BOP by a preponderance of the evidence.","sub":"Sixth Amendment — Jury Finding of Sentencing Facts","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":20},{"n":68,"summary":"During police questioning, the defendant made statements without Miranda warnings. The issue is whether the defendant was 'in custody' for Miranda purposes—i.e., whether the defendant’s freedom was restrained to a degree associated with formal arrest.","trigger":"Miranda custody determination (significant restraint on freedom).","rule":"A person is not in custody for Miranda purposes unless they are deprived of their freedom in a significant way.","sub":"Fifth Amendment — Miranda Custody","family":"Fifth Amendment","unit":19},{"n":69,"summary":"Officer gained entry into a home by falsely claiming car trouble and requesting permission to use the phone. Although consent was given to use the phone, Officer exceeded the scope of that consent by reviewing the caller ID and writing down phone numbers. Officer later used that information to obtain witness statements and a search warrant, which led to discovery of drugs in the home.","trigger":"Evidence obtained after exploiting a prior Fourth Amendment violation. The questions asks: Which of the following would be the most relevant in whether the drugs get suppressed? Consent, apparent authority, good faith, attenuation. The answer is consent.","rule":"When evidence is derived from illegal police conduct, admissibility depends on whether the taint is sufficiently attenuated.","sub":"Consent – Scope; Attenuation","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":11},{"n":70,"summary":"Police conducted a prostitution sting using an undercover officer posing as a prostitute. Man solicited the officer for sex in exchange for money and was arrested after a traffic stop. When asked to search the car, Man stated he preferred the officers not search but acquiesced if he had no choice. Officers searched the glove compartment and later, after Man was secured in the police car, searched the trunk and found additional contraband.","trigger":"Valid arrest combined with consent language and probable cause vehicle contains contraband.","rule":"Consent is judged by objective reasonableness; SILA ends once the arrestee cannot access the vehicle; under the automobile exception, police may search the entire vehicle with probable cause. Here, first it was SILA they found drugs and handgun, then it turned into the auto exception and the officers could search all the car, because ilegal contraband was found","sub":"Consent; Search Incident to Arrest; Automobile Exception","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":7},{"n":71,"summary":"Police executed a search warrant for a bar suspected of drug activity. During the search, officers detained the bartender, a known suspect, and a patron sitting with him who was not named in the warrant and had no individualized suspicion against him. While detained, drugs fell from the patron’s pocket.","trigger":"Detention of an individual not lawfully subject to detention during execution of a warrant.","rule":"Plain view requires lawful police presence; evidence discovered during unlawful detention is subject to suppression.","sub":"Detention During Warrant Execution","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":6},{"n":72,"summary":"Man operated a tutoring business and treated the office patio as a personal break area, including eating meals there. He stored marijuana under a picnic table on the patio. While walking past with a drug-sniffing dog, an officer allowed the dog to sniff the patio area, the dog alerted, and the officer searched under the table without a warrant.","trigger":"Dog sniff and warrantless search of a business property.","rule":"Businesses lack curtilage like homes; suppression must be based on an unreasonable warrantless search, not the curtilage doctrine.","sub":"Dog Sniff – Business Property","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":1},{"n":73,"summary":"Police arrived at Man’s home with a valid arrest warrant and heard noises suggesting destruction of evidence. Officers entered, arrested Man in a bedroom, and handcuffed him. While escorting Man out of the room, an officer opened a closet along the exit path to ensure no one was hiding and observed an illegal machine gun inside.","trigger":"Lawful in-home arrest combined with reasonable concern for officer safety.","rule":"During a lawful protective sweep incident to arrest, contraband observed in plain view may be seized.","sub":"Protective Sweep – Plain View","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":10},{"n":74,"summary":"Police entered Man’s large fenced farm without a warrant, crawling under a barbed-wire fence marked with no-trespassing signs. Officers stayed away from the house and walked the property, where they observed plants that, based on their training and experience, they immediately recognized as marijuana.","trigger":"Observation of activity in an open field outside the curtilage.","rule":"There is no reasonable expectation of privacy in open fields, even if fenced or posted with no-trespassing signs.","sub":"Search Definition – Open Fields","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":1},{"n":75,"summary":"Officer forcibly entered Woman’s home without a warrant or consent and searched her cell phone, reviewing text messages that identified Man as a customer of illegal prostitution. Man was later prosecuted based on this information, even though the Fourth Amendment violation occurred in Woman’s home and involved her phone.","trigger":"Defendant seeks suppression although his own privacy rights were not violated.","rule":"A defendant may suppress evidence only if his own Fourth Amendment rights were violated.","sub":"Standing","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":3},{"n":76,"summary":"After responding to a restaurant fight, police were told one of two men wearing baseball hats had pulled a knife, though the witness could not identify which one. Police detained both hat-wearing men and a third friend sitting with them who was not wearing a hat. Officers frisked all three individuals and seized cocaine from the third friend during the pat-down.","trigger":"Terry frisk conducted without reasonable suspicion that the individual was armed and dangerous.","rule":"The plain-feel doctrine applies only when a Terry frisk is lawful; without reasonable suspicion, seizure is unconstitutional.","sub":"Terry Frisk – Plain Feel","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":12},{"n":77,"summary":"Police responded to a domestic violence call at a one-bedroom apartment shared by Husband and Wife. After observing injuries on Wife, police arrested Husband. Wife consented to a search of the bedroom, but Husband, who was present and handcuffed nearby, expressly objected. Officers searched the bedroom anyway and found marijuana belonging to Husband.","trigger":"Co-occupants present with conflicting consent.","rule":"When one co-occupant consents and another is physically present and objects, police may not rely on consent to search shared premise. If 1 person says yes and the others said no, there is no consent, but if the person was being arrested says no then there would be consent","sub":"Third-Party Consent","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":11},{"n":78,"summary":"Officer stopped Man for driving only two miles over the speed limit, even though department practice was not to stop drivers unless speeding more than five miles over. Officer had never made such a stop before and admitted he was motivated by a desire to find drugs. While lawfully standing at the driver’s window requesting license and registration, Officer observed a clear bag containing a leafy green substance he immediately recognized as marijuana.","trigger":"Traffic stop based on observed speeding; officer positioned lawfully at vehicle window.","rule":"An officer’s ulterior motive does not invalidate an objectively lawful traffic stop, and contraband seen in plain view from a lawful vantage point may be seized.","sub":"Traffic Stops – Plain View","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":13}]},{"slug":"crimlaw","name":"Criminal Law","bigPicture":"WHERE YOU START. Of all the MBE subjects, this is the one your training prepared you for best. The general part of the criminal law is shared Roman inheritance: you already reason about dolo and culpa, about the position of guarantor and liability for omission (comisión por omisión), about adequate causation and concausas, about error in persona and aberratio ictus, about legítima defensa with its unlawful aggression and proportionate means (C.P. art. 65), about inimputabilidad for mental disturbance (C.P. art. 62), and about the difference between hurto, robo, apropiación indebida and estafa. The Venezuelan Penal Code even states the mens rea principle you will meet again: no one is punished for a crime without having intended the act that constitutes it, culpa being punishable only where the law so provides (art. 61).\n\nWHAT THE U.S. SYSTEM IS. The same terrain, reorganised around four devices your code does not contain. First, mens rea is sorted into FOUR categories rather than two — specific intent, general intent, malice and strict liability — and the category, not the gravity of the offense, decides which defenses are even available. Second, murder is defined by MALICE, a term of art satisfied by four alternative states of mind including reckless indifference to human life, so a killing nobody intended is murder. Third, inchoate and accessorial liability reach much further: the attempt demands a HIGHER mental state than the completed crime, conspiracy never merges into the offense committed, and under Pinkerton every conspirator answers for the foreseeable crimes of the others. Fourth, felony murder converts any death during a dangerous felony into murder for every participant, subject to four limits that carry their own jurisdictional splits.\n\nWHAT DIVERGES. Three trained habits generate the wrong answer. (1) Reasoning from the gravity of the harm to the mental state, when the analysis runs the other way: petty larceny requires specific intent while murder requires only malice, and 'malice' means neither malicia nor animosity. (2) Under-charging the unintended death — routing it to culposo or preterintencional when U.S. law calls conscious disregard of a grave risk murder, and calls an accidental shooting by a co-felon murder for the lookout waiting outside. (3) Under-convicting the peripheral participant, because the codal instinct allocates responsibility by each person's own contribution, while this system convicts through the group: the conspiracy survives the completed crime, and the agreement itself carries liability for what the others foreseeably did.\n\nWHAT IS THE SAME. More than in any other module — ten of the twenty-eight units are convergence, and this is where the risk actually lies. Omission liability and the duty to act; causation, including taking the victim as you find him; error in persona; the four provocation requirements; the refusal to allow deadly force in defense of chattels; the initial aggressor who withdraws; apropiación indebida mapping almost exactly onto embezzlement; incendio reachable by recklessness; premeditation as an aggravating feature. Trust those. But notice the trap the run of successes sets: the danger in this subject is not ignorance, it is CONFIDENCE. You will be right about causation and self-defense and then apply the same instinct to mens rea categories, to Pinkerton, to felony murder and to attempt — and there the system is running on machinery your code never had.","families":[{"name":"Homicide","count":24},{"name":"Theft","count":15},{"name":"Inchoate Offenses","count":14},{"name":"Defenses","count":9},{"name":"Mens Rea","count":9},{"name":"Parties to Crime","count":8},{"name":"Conspiracy","count":4},{"name":"Property Offenses","count":3},{"name":"Arson and Kidnapping","count":2},{"name":"Causation","count":2},{"name":"Crimes Against the Person","count":2},{"name":"Actus Reus","count":1},{"name":"Fourth Amendment","count":1},{"name":"Sixth Amendment","count":1}],"units":[{"n":1,"summary":"The same conduct is charged under offenses with different mental states: an attempt, a general-intent battery or rape, a malice crime such as murder or arson, and a regulatory possession offense. Whether a defense works at all depends on which mental state the charged offense requires.","trigger":"Any criminal charge → classify the required mental state before analysing anything else: specific intent, general intent, malice, or strict liability. The classification controls which defenses are even available.","rule":"Common-law offenses fall into four mens rea categories, and the category determines the analysis. SPECIFIC INTENT crimes require a particular purpose beyond the act itself — all attempts, solicitation, conspiracy, larceny, embezzlement, false pretenses, robbery, burglary, forgery and premeditated murder; only these are vulnerable to voluntary intoxication and to an unreasonable mistake of fact. GENERAL INTENT crimes require only awareness of the act — battery, rape, false imprisonment, kidnapping — and are defeated only by a reasonable mistake. MALICE crimes (murder and arson) require intent or reckless disregard of a high risk of the prohibited result, and malice is a term of art requiring no ill will, hatred or spite. STRICT LIABILITY offenses — regulatory, administrative and public-welfare crimes, including statutory rape as to age — require no mental state as to the element made strict, and motive is irrelevant to them.","sub":"Mens Rea – The Four-Category Architecture (Master)","sensitivity":"Classification precedes every defense argument, and the answer choices are built on it: voluntary intoxication and unreasonable mistake reach ONLY specific-intent crimes; reasonable mistake reaches general-intent crimes; nothing about the defendant's motive reaches a strict-liability element. The trap is assuming that a graver crime carries a stricter mental state — murder is a malice crime, while petty larceny requires specific intent.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “there was dolo, or at most culpa — that is the whole question” → U.S. criminal law runs FOUR categories, not two, and the availability of every mens rea defense depends on which one the offense occupies. The Venezuelan schema (C.P. art. 61: no punishment without the intention to commit the act, with culpa punishable only where the law so provides) is a two-tier dolo/culpa system in which gravity and mental state move together. Two errors follow: treating 'malice' as malicia or animosity when it is a technical term satisfied by recklessness, and expecting intoxication or mistake to operate across the board when each reaches only its own category.","authority":"Mens rea architecture: the four common-law categories (specific intent, general intent, malice, strict liability); Morissette v. United States (presumption of a mens rea requirement); United States v. Balint; Staples v. United States."},{"n":2,"summary":"A father does not take his critically ill child to hospital for fear of the cost, treating him with a wet towel; the child dies overnight. No act caused the death — the charge rests on what the defendant failed to do.","trigger":"Death or harm caused by a FAILURE to act → liability only if a legal duty to act existed, the defendant knew the facts giving rise to it, and compliance was possible.","rule":"An omission is criminal only where a LEGAL duty to act existed, the defendant had knowledge of the facts giving rise to the duty, and it was reasonably possible to perform. There is no general duty to rescue. Duties arise from five sources: statute; a status relationship (parent to child, spouse to spouse); a contract; the voluntary assumption of care where the defendant has secluded the victim from other help; and the defendant's own creation of the peril. Where the duty exists, breach carries the mens rea of the offense charged — so a parent's grossly negligent failure to obtain medical care for a child supports involuntary manslaughter, and on sufficiently egregious facts depraved-heart murder becomes available.","sub":"Actus Reus – Omission Liability and the Duty to Act","sensitivity":"Two gates, in order: is there a duty from one of the five sources, and was performance possible. Only then does mens rea grade the offense. The exam supplies morally damning inaction with no duty (the stranger) or a duty with impossibility (no means of transport) to test whether the candidate runs the gates rather than the sympathy.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT — comisión por omisión is a developed doctrine, the position of garante is familiar, and parental duty is beyond argument. The calibration friction is narrower than in most units: U.S. law fixes the duty sources as a CLOSED list of five and denies any general duty to rescue however easy the rescue, whereas civilian doctrine reasons from the position of guarantor and can extend it more readily on grounds of solidarity. Trust the instinct as to the parent; distrust it as to the stranger.","authority":"Omission liability: the five duty sources (statute, status relationship, contract, voluntary assumption with seclusion, creation of peril); Jones v. United States; Commonwealth v. Pestinikas."},{"n":3,"summary":"A nephew poisons a gravely ill patient awaiting transfer, delaying the flight that later crashes; the patient dies of a heart attack the next day. Elsewhere a referee's shove kills a haemophiliac who bleeds to death from a minor fall.","trigger":"Death follows the defendant's act but another factor intervened or the victim was unusually frail → test but-for causation, then whether the intervening force was foreseeable, then apply the take-the-victim-as-found rule.","rule":"Criminal homicide requires that the defendant's act be a but-for cause and a substantial factor in the death, and that the death be a proximate result. A pre-existing condition never breaks the chain: the defendant takes the victim as he finds him, so an unforeseeable frailty such as haemophilia or a weakened heart does not excuse. A FORESEEABLE intervening force — negligent medical treatment, a rescuer's intervention, the victim's own natural reaction — leaves liability intact; an unforeseeable, independent superseding force cuts it off. Where the defendant's act merely accelerates an inevitable death, that is still causation. At common law the year-and-a-day rule barred prosecution where death occurred later, a limit abolished in most jurisdictions.","sub":"Causation – But-For, Substantial Factor, Intervening Forces, Frail Victims","sensitivity":"Three questions in fixed order, and the exam usually loads only one: but-for, then foreseeability of what intervened, then the eggshell rule at the end. Note the deliberate red herring — a catastrophe the victim was spared (the plane he never boarded) is not an intervening cause at all, because it never touched the causal chain.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian analysis lands correctly — causalidad adecuada asks essentially the same question, and the treatment of concausas preexistentes likewise refuses to excuse the offender because the victim was frail. The calibration friction is one of sequence and vocabulary rather than result: U.S. law separates but-for causation from proximate causation into two distinct steps with different tests, and resolves intervening forces by FORESEEABILITY judged against the risk created, where civilian doctrine tends to resolve the whole question inside a single adequacy judgment.","authority":"Causation: but-for and proximate causation; the take-the-victim-as-found rule; foreseeable versus superseding intervening forces; acceleration of an inevitable death; the abrogated year-and-a-day rule."},{"n":4,"summary":"A gun discharges accidentally during a foiled robbery and attempted murder is charged; a man has sex with a fifteen-year-old holding a perfect fake identification; a driver relies on a breath device reading below the limit; a defendant repeatedly enters wrong PINs with another's card.","trigger":"Attempt charged → the prosecution must prove SPECIFIC INTENT to commit the target offense plus a substantial step, whatever mental state the completed offense requires.","rule":"Attempt requires (1) the specific intent to commit the target offense and (2) an overt act constituting a substantial step towards its commission — mere preparation is not enough. The specific-intent requirement holds EVEN WHERE the completed offense requires no intent at all: there is no attempted statutory rape without intent as to age, no attempted driving-under-the-influence by a defendant who believed he was under the limit, and no attempted murder from an accidental discharge, however grave the result. It follows that offenses defined by recklessness or negligence cannot be attempted, and that attempted murder requires the intent to KILL — intent to inflict great bodily harm, which suffices for completed murder, does not suffice for the attempt. Attempt merges into the completed offense.","sub":"Inchoate Offenses – Attempt: Specific Intent and Substantial Step (Master)","sensitivity":"One rule generates most of the wrong answers in this block: the mens rea for the attempt is NARROWER than for the completed crime. Two corollaries follow, and each is separately tested — strict-liability and reckless offenses cannot be attempted, and attempted murder demands intent to kill rather than malice.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the tentativa follows the mental element of the completed offense — whoever begins executing the crime attempts it” → U.S. law imposes a HIGHER mental state on the attempt than on the consummated crime, so conduct that would convict if the result occurred acquits when it does not. The civilian tentativa is defined by commencement of execution with the intent proper to the offense (C.P. art. 80), which supplies no reason to demand more for the attempt than for the crime — producing systematic over-conviction in exactly the fact patterns the exam builds.","authority":"Attempt: specific intent plus a substantial step; Model Penal Code § 5.01; no attempt liability for reckless or strict-liability offenses; attempted murder requires intent to kill; merger into the completed offense."},{"n":5,"summary":"A defendant pries at a window that is in fact impossible to force; a man buys what he believes is stolen property from an undercover officer; another buys codeine believing the prohibiting statute is still in force when it has been repealed.","trigger":"Attempt where the target offense could not have been completed → distinguish FACTUAL impossibility (no defense) from LEGAL impossibility (a defense), and note the modern trend collapsing the distinction.","rule":"FACTUAL impossibility — the defendant intended a crime and would have committed it but for a circumstance unknown to him — is NOT a defense: the theft-proof window, the empty pocket, the unloaded gun leave the attempt intact. LEGAL impossibility — the conduct the defendant intended is not a crime at all, because no such offense exists or the statute has been repealed — IS a defense, and the defendant's belief that he was breaking the law cannot create liability. Property supplied by law enforcement is not stolen, so the completed offense of receiving stolen property is legally impossible while the ATTEMPT remains chargeable. The Model Penal Code and many modern codes largely abolish the distinction by judging the actor on the facts as he believed them to be.","sub":"Inchoate Offenses – Factual vs. Legal Impossibility","sensitivity":"The switch is whether the OBSTACLE was factual or definitional: a fact the defendant got wrong leaves him guilty of attempt; a legal proposition he got wrong does not make him guilty of anything. The undercover-property pattern is the hybrid the exam favours — the completed offense is impossible, the attempt is not.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “no hubo delito posible — the object of the crime never existed, so there is nothing to punish” → U.S. law punishes the attempt in precisely that situation whenever the defect was factual, and refuses to punish where the defect was legal, which inverts the civilian intuition twice. Civilian doctrine treats the delito imposible or the falta de objeto as negating the attempt itself; there is no counterpart to a system that convicts on a mistaken belief about the world while acquitting on a mistaken belief about the law.","authority":"Impossibility: factual impossibility no defense; legal impossibility a defense; People v. Jaffe; the Model Penal Code approach judging the actor on the facts as he believed them (§ 5.01(1)(a))."},{"n":6,"summary":"A caterer asks a butler for the safe combination; the butler feigns agreement and the caterer then goes to the room but is stopped before touching the safe. Elsewhere a partner hires a professional killer, who agrees, and the killing follows.","trigger":"Inchoate offenses stacked on one course of conduct → apply the merger rules: solicitation merges into conspiracy or the completed offense; attempt merges into the completed offense; conspiracy merges into NOTHING.","rule":"SOLICITATION is complete the moment the request or encouragement is made, and is unaffected by the solicitee's refusal or feigned agreement; it MERGES into a conspiracy if the solicitee agrees, and into the target offense if it is committed. ATTEMPT merges into the completed offense. CONSPIRACY does NOT merge at common law: a defendant may be convicted of both conspiracy and the completed crime. Feigned agreement also defeats a bilateral conspiracy, because there was never a genuine meeting of two minds — which is why a solicitation followed by a pretended acceptance and then a substantial step leaves attempt as the most serious available conviction. Where the question asks what the defendant may be CONVICTED of, lesser included and merged offenses must be eliminated.","sub":"Inchoate Offenses – Merger: Solicitation, Attempt, Conspiracy","sensitivity":"Three offenses with three different merger rules, and the question format ('of which crimes may he be convicted') is itself the test. The asymmetry is the point: conspiracy survives the completed crime while solicitation and attempt are absorbed by it. Feigned agreement is the switch that decides whether the chain stops at solicitation-plus-attempt or advances to conspiracy.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the acts leading up to the crime are absorbed by the crime — only the consummated offense is punished” → U.S. law preserves conspiracy as a separate conviction alongside the completed offense, so the same course of conduct yields two convictions. The civilian principle of absorption or progression (actos preparatorios and tentativa absorbed by the consummated delito, with asociación para delinquir a distinct and narrower offense) has no analogue to a general agreement crime that never merges — and this is the single most common under-conviction error in the block.","authority":"Merger of inchoate offenses: solicitation merges into conspiracy or the target offense; attempt merges into the completed offense; conspiracy does not merge at common law; feigned agreement defeats bilateral conspiracy."},{"n":7,"summary":"Three men plot to kill a governor and then two of them plot against the third; a lookout drives away as the others enter the bank; a man steals a getaway car and leaves a note saying he wants no further part, which his co-conspirator never reads.","trigger":"Agreement between two or more to commit a crime + (in most jurisdictions) an overt act → each conspirator is liable for the reasonably foreseeable crimes of the others committed in furtherance; withdrawal requires affirmative communication.","rule":"Conspiracy requires an agreement between two or more persons to commit an unlawful act, the specific intent that it be committed, and in most jurisdictions an overt act in furtherance — mere preparation suffices. The traditional bilateral rule demands two genuinely agreeing minds, so feigned agreement by an undercover officer defeats it; the Model Penal Code's unilateral approach does not. Under PINKERTON, each conspirator is liable for the substantive crimes of the others that were committed in furtherance of the conspiracy and were reasonably foreseeable, including killings, even if he neither agreed to nor knew of them. WITHDRAWAL requires an affirmative act communicating the withdrawal to the co-conspirators before the substantive crime; it limits liability for later crimes but never erases liability for the conspiracy itself. Simply leaving, or a note that is never received, is not withdrawal.","sub":"Conspiracy – Agreement, Pinkerton Liability, Withdrawal (Master)","sensitivity":"Three switches the exam crosses deliberately: (1) genuine versus feigned agreement, which decides whether a conspiracy exists at all; (2) foreseeability and furtherance, which extends liability to crimes never agreed upon — including a homicide by a co-felon; (3) the mechanics of withdrawal, where the tested failure is always non-communication. A conspirator who agreed to an unarmed robbery is still liable for a shooting that was foreseeable in it.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “each participant answers for what he agreed to and for his own contribution — nothing more” → Pinkerton extends criminal liability to crimes the defendant never agreed to, never foresaw individually and was not present for, on the sole basis that they were foreseeable in the venture. Venezuelan law punishes agreement narrowly (asociación para delinquir as a distinct offense) and allocates responsibility by each participant's own contribution under the rules of coautoría and complicidad, so vicarious criminal liability of this breadth has no codal counterpart and is systematically under-predicted.","authority":"Conspiracy: agreement, specific intent and (in most jurisdictions) an overt act; the bilateral rule and the Model Penal Code unilateral approach (§ 5.03); Pinkerton v. United States; withdrawal by communicated notice."},{"n":8,"summary":"A friend unknowingly drives a man to a house and only learns of the robbery as they flee; a woman gives her companion a thumbs-up that prompts an assault; eight gang members watch a killing and do nothing; a friend lends a car with no knowledge of the planned robbery.","trigger":"Liability sought for another's crime → accomplice liability requires BOTH the intent that the crime be committed and an act of aid, encouragement or facilitation; presence, knowledge or after-the-fact assistance is not enough.","rule":"An accomplice is one who, with the intent that the offense be committed, aids, encourages, counsels or otherwise facilitates its commission, and he is liable for the crime itself and for other foreseeable crimes committed in its course. Both elements are indispensable: mere presence, silence, approval or a failure to intervene is not aid, and knowledge acquired only AFTER the crime cannot supply the intent — one who then helps the perpetrator escape is an accessory after the fact, a separate and lesser offense. A word, a signal or a gesture that prompts the principal satisfies the act requirement, while lending property without knowledge of the criminal purpose does not. A supplier becomes an accomplice where he knows the criminal use and has a stake in the venture, as an inflated price may show. Accomplice liability does not require presence at the scene, and one who procures a crime is liable for a resulting death under felony murder.","sub":"Parties to Crime – Accomplice Liability (Master)","sensitivity":"The two elements are tested against each other in paired fact patterns: the gang members had intent and no act; the unwitting driver and the lender had an act and no intent. The supplier cases turn on the stake in the venture, which is what separates a merchant from an accomplice. Timing is the third dial — intent must exist before or during, never only after.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “helping after the fact, or standing by while it happens, makes you a participant” → U.S. law requires intent AND an affirmative act of assistance concurrent with the offense, so passive witnesses are innocent of the crime and later helpers are guilty only of a distinct lesser offense. The civilian schema grades participation as coautoría, complicidad necesaria and complicidad simple, with encubrimiento as a separate figure, but it reasons from the degree of contribution rather than from a two-element test — so the reflex convicts the bystander the U.S. system acquits.","authority":"Accomplice liability: intent plus aid or encouragement; mere presence insufficient (State v. Gladstone); the stake-in-the-venture test for suppliers; accessory after the fact as a distinct lesser offense."},{"n":9,"summary":"A defendant intends to kill his neighbour, mistakes the neighbour's brother for him and shoots at the brother, missing and wounding an unseen bystander. Elsewhere a shot aimed at a husband ricochets and kills his wife.","trigger":"The victim harmed is not the victim intended → transferred intent supplies the mens rea for the COMPLETED crime against the actual victim, but does not apply to ATTEMPT; mistake of identity does not change who the intended victim was.","rule":"Under TRANSFERRED INTENT, where the defendant intends harm to one person and by mistake or accident causes the same harm to another, the intent transfers and he is guilty of the completed offense against the actual victim; he also remains liable for the attempt against the intended victim. Transferred intent does NOT apply to attempt liability, so the defendant is guilty of attempting to kill only the person he actually aimed at. MISTAKE OF IDENTITY is different again and changes nothing: one who shoots at a person believing him to be someone else intends to kill the person he shot at, and the mistake is irrelevant. Where the crime charged is graded by a characteristic of the victim, whether transferred intent satisfies that element is a separate question.","sub":"Mens Rea – Transferred Intent and Mistake of Identity","sensitivity":"Two doctrines the answer choices deliberately blend, distinguished by what went wrong: the defendant hit the WRONG PERSON by accident (transferred intent, completed crime follows) or he hit the RIGHT TARGET under a false belief about who it was (mistake of identity, irrelevant). The limit that decides the hardest questions is that transferred intent never reaches an attempt.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT — error in persona does not excuse, and aberratio ictus is a familiar figure resolved in favour of liability for the result caused. The calibration friction lies at the edges rather than the core: U.S. law refuses to transfer intent to an ATTEMPT, and simultaneously preserves the attempt against the intended victim, so one act yields two distinct convictions with two different victims — a splitting the civilian treatment of aberratio ictus does not perform.","authority":"Transferred intent and mistake of identity: intent transfers for the completed offense but not to attempt; the attempt survives against the intended victim; error in persona is immaterial."},{"n":10,"summary":"A diner shoots her husband at close range; a waiter poisons food meaning to cause severe suffering but not death; a firebomb set to destroy a house kills someone inside.","trigger":"Unlawful killing charged as murder → identify which of the four routes to MALICE the facts supply: intent to kill, intent to inflict great bodily harm, depraved-heart recklessness, or felony murder.","rule":"Common-law murder is an unlawful killing committed with MALICE AFORETHOUGHT, and malice is established by any one of four states of mind: (1) the intent to kill; (2) the intent to inflict great bodily harm; (3) reckless indifference to an unjustifiably high risk to human life, known as depraved-heart or abandoned-and-malignant-heart malice; or (4) the intent to commit an inherently dangerous felony, which supplies malice through the felony-murder rule. Malice is a term of art: it requires neither ill will, hatred, spite nor premeditation, and a killing may be malicious though the defendant acted from sympathy. A deadly weapon used on a vital part of the body permits an inference of intent to kill. Absence of all four routes reduces the offense to manslaughter or to no homicide at all.","sub":"Homicide – Murder and the Four Routes to Malice (Master)","sensitivity":"The analysis is a menu, not a test: identify ANY one route and murder is established, so the exam supplies facts that satisfy an unexpected route — intent to make someone suffer, not to kill, still yields murder through great-bodily-harm malice. Naming the route also controls the later steps, because only intent-to-kill malice can be aggravated by premeditation into first degree.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “there was no intention to kill, so this is homicidio preterintencional or culposo, not murder” → U.S. law calls all four states of mind MALICE and all four produce murder, so a defendant who wanted only to cause suffering is a murderer. The Venezuelan schema grades homicide by the intention actually directed at the death (doloso, preterintencional, culposo), which makes the absence of intent to kill decisive; and the word malicia invites a further error, because it suggests animosity where the common law requires none.","authority":"Murder and malice: the four routes (intent to kill; intent to inflict great bodily harm; depraved-heart recklessness; felony murder); malice as a term of art requiring no ill will; the deadly-weapon inference."},{"n":11,"summary":"A man broods after being bumped in a bar, deliberately breaks a bottle, keeps the jagged edge, waits for the victim to leave and cuts his throat. Elsewhere a woman draws a revolver from her purse after an exchange of insults and shoots the speaker between the eyes.","trigger":"Intent-to-kill murder + evidence of reflection and preparation → statutory first-degree murder; the question is whether premeditation and deliberation are present, not how long they took.","rule":"At common law murder was not divided by degree; modern statutes make an intentional killing committed with PREMEDITATION and DELIBERATION first-degree murder, and all other murder second degree. Premeditation requires that the defendant actually reflect on the decision to kill; deliberation requires that he do so in a cool state of mind. No particular length of time is needed and the reflection may occupy only an instant, but there must be evidence of actual reflection rather than a mere intent formed simultaneously with the act. Preparation — arming oneself, lying in wait, procuring a weapon, waiting for the victim — is the classic proof. Statutes also commonly designate killings by poison, torture, lying in wait, or during enumerated felonies as first degree regardless of proof of reflection.","sub":"Homicide – First-Degree Murder: Premeditation and Deliberation","sensitivity":"The tested proposition is that premeditation can be formed in an instant — so answer choices that reject first degree because 'there was no time to plan' are wrong. The counterpart is that the earlier provocation is legally spent once the defendant cooled off, which is precisely why the same facts defeat voluntary manslaughter (Unit 12).","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT — premeditación and alevosía are familiar aggravating circumstances, and lying in wait aggravates in both systems. The calibration friction is structural rather than substantive: in the U.S. these are ELEMENTS of a distinct degree of the offense that a jury must find beyond a reasonable doubt, not circumstances the judge weighs at sentencing under a codal list — and 'an instant of reflection suffices' is a threshold far lower than the civilian notion of premeditación implies.","authority":"First-degree murder: premeditation and deliberation as elements to be found by the jury; reflection may occupy an instant; statutory first-degree categories (poison, torture, lying in wait, enumerated felonies)."},{"n":12,"summary":"A husband comes home early, finds his wife in the act with a neighbour, takes a gun from the dresser and kills the neighbour. Elsewhere an employee is taunted about her work and stabs the taunter; and a woman is called a slut in a bar and shoots the speaker.","trigger":"Intentional killing in response to provocation → murder is mitigated to voluntary manslaughter only if all four provocation requirements are met; words alone never suffice.","rule":"An intentional killing that would otherwise be murder is reduced to VOLUNTARY MANSLAUGHTER where four requirements are satisfied: (1) provocation that a reasonable person would find adequate; (2) the defendant was in fact in a heat of passion; (3) there was no reasonable opportunity to cool off; and (4) the defendant did not in fact cool off. Adequate provocation is confined to recognized categories — a serious battery, mutual combat, an unlawful arrest, and the discovery of a spouse in the act of adultery. WORDS ALONE, however insulting or humiliating, are never adequate provocation. Mitigation is a question for the jury, so where the categories are met the judge must instruct on both murder and voluntary manslaughter; where they are not, only murder goes to the jury. An unreasonable belief in the need for self-defense supports imperfect self-defense, which mitigates on the same footing in many jurisdictions.","sub":"Homicide – Voluntary Manslaughter and Adequate Provocation","sensitivity":"The four requirements are cumulative, and the exam removes one at a time: adequate category but a cooling-off period; genuine passion but only words; adultery discovered by report rather than in the act. Note what the doctrine does procedurally — satisfying the categories entitles the defendant to an INSTRUCTION, not to a verdict.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “this is arrebato o intenso dolor — an emotional state that mitigates, and grave insult surely qualifies” → U.S. law confines mitigation to a closed list of provoking events and excludes words categorically, however grave the insult. The civilian mitigating circumstance is framed around the defendant's emotional state and the provocation's gravity assessed openly (C.P. art. 67 and the atenuantes of art. 74), so the trained reflex mitigates on facts where the common law refuses even to instruct the jury.","authority":"Voluntary manslaughter: the four provocation requirements; the closed categories of adequate provocation; words alone never adequate (Girouard v. State); imperfect self-defense."},{"n":13,"summary":"A speeding driver loses control and kills a parked truck's driver; an impaired driver at 65 in a 35 zone kills a jogger; a father does not take his sick child to hospital; a roommate miscalculates a sedative dose; a referee's shove kills a haemophiliac.","trigger":"Unintentional killing without malice → involuntary manslaughter on either of two branches: criminal (gross) negligence, or death caused during an unlawful act.","rule":"INVOLUNTARY MANSLAUGHTER is an unintentional killing without malice, established on either of two branches. (1) CRIMINAL NEGLIGENCE: conduct creating an unreasonably high risk of death or serious bodily harm, a standard substantially more culpable than civil negligence but short of the reckless indifference that constitutes depraved-heart malice; it includes a grossly negligent failure to perform a legal duty, as where a parent withholds medical care. (2) The UNLAWFUL-ACT branch, historically misdemeanour-manslaughter: a death caused during the commission of a misdemeanour or of a felony not qualifying for felony murder. That branch is limited — the misdemeanour must be malum in se, or the violation must be the proximate cause of the death, and many jurisdictions require criminal negligence independently, so a mere regulatory or speeding violation does not automatically suffice. Involuntary manslaughter is a lesser included offense of murder, and the defendant takes the victim as found, so an unforeseeable frailty does not excuse.","sub":"Homicide – Involuntary Manslaughter: Negligence and Unlawful-Act Branches","sensitivity":"Two branches with different triggers, and the boundary ABOVE them decides the hardest questions: gross negligence is manslaughter, while reckless indifference to human life crosses into depraved-heart murder (Unit 14). The unlawful-act branch is the one the source materials over-state — a traffic infraction plus a death is not automatically manslaughter.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT — homicidio culposo covers exactly this territory, the duty-based omission is familiar, and the frail-victim outcome matches the treatment of concausas. The calibration friction is the DOUBLE boundary the civilian does not draw: below, ordinary civil negligence is not enough; above, recklessness converts the same death into murder. A single culpa category cannot locate either line, so the reflex under-charges the reckless driver and over-charges the merely careless one.","authority":"Involuntary manslaughter: the criminal-negligence branch and the unlawful-act (misdemeanour-manslaughter) branch, with its malum in se and proximate-cause limits; lesser included offense of murder; frail-victim rule."},{"n":14,"summary":"A driver flees police at extreme speed through a school zone and kills his passenger; a man points a loaded handgun with his finger on the trigger to demand a debit card and it discharges, killing his friend.","trigger":"Unintentional killing produced by conduct showing conscious disregard of a very high risk to human life → depraved-heart MURDER, not manslaughter.","rule":"DEPRAVED-HEART MURDER is an unintentional killing resulting from conduct that creates an unjustifiably high risk to human life, undertaken with the defendant's CONSCIOUS AWARENESS of that risk. It is one of the four routes to malice, so the offense is murder — not manslaughter — even though the defendant neither intended to kill nor to injure. Classic instances include firing into an occupied building or vehicle, pointing a loaded firearm at another with a finger on the trigger, driving at extreme speed through a populated area, and abandoning a helpless person in conditions of extreme danger. The distinction from involuntary manslaughter is one of degree and awareness: gross negligence is manslaughter, conscious disregard of a very high risk of death is malice. Voluntary intoxication is never a defense, because the offense rests on recklessness rather than on specific intent.","sub":"Homicide – Depraved-Heart Murder: the Recklessness Boundary","sensitivity":"This is the single most misclassified doctrine in the area, and the reason is that the label sounds like manslaughter language while the consequence is murder. Two markers separate them: the MAGNITUDE of the risk and the defendant's AWARENESS of it. An intervening act that merely triggers the very risk created — a bystander jostling the gun hand — does not break causation.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “no intention to kill, therefore not murder — at most a homicidio culposo aggravated by the recklessness” → U.S. law treats conscious disregard of a grave risk as MALICE and convicts of murder. The civilian architecture places dolo eventual close to this idea, but the Venezuelan grading scheme routes unintended deaths into culposo or preterintencional categories with markedly lower penalties, so there is no familiar shelf for an unintended killing punished as murder — and the reflex under-charges precisely where the exam expects the graver verdict.","authority":"Depraved-heart murder: conscious disregard of an unjustifiably high risk to human life as malice; Commonwealth v. Malone; distinguished from gross negligence; voluntary intoxication no defense."},{"n":15,"summary":"A terminally ill wife asks her husband to disconnect her respirator; he complies and she dies peacefully. He is prosecuted for homicide.","trigger":"Killing at the victim's request, or by withdrawal of life-sustaining treatment → consent is no defense to homicide; the operative distinction is between an affirmative act by a private person and the lawful withholding of treatment.","rule":"CONSENT is not a defense to homicide. A crime is an offense against the state as well as the victim, so the victim's request, however informed and however dire his condition, does not privilege the killing, and a mercy killing is murder if the elements of malice are satisfied — indeed the deliberation involved will ordinarily make it first degree. Consent is a defense only to a narrow class of offenses where lack of consent is an element or the conduct is a recognized risk, such as minor batteries in sport. The line the facts test is between an affirmative act causing death and the lawful WITHHOLDING or WITHDRAWAL of treatment: a physician acting on a competent patient's valid refusal permits death rather than causing it, whereas an unauthorized act by a private person is a homicide, and no U.S. jurisdiction recognizes euthanasia as a common-law defense.","sub":"Homicide – Consent, Mercy Killing, and the Act/Omission Line","sensitivity":"One categorical rule plus one fine distinction: consent never justifies a killing, but who withdraws the treatment and on whose authority determines whether there was a killing at all. The sympathetic facts are the test — an answer that acquits because the victim asked to die misses the rule the question exists to state.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “she consented and was dying — at most this is homicidio a petición, a mitigated figure, and arguably no crime at all given her suffering” → U.S. common law recognizes no mitigated offense of killing on request and no euthanasia defense: the killing is murder. Several civil-law systems, and the doctrinal tradition the Venezuelan lawyer is trained in, treat the victim's request as a distinct and substantially mitigated offense, so the reflex expects a reduction the common law does not offer.","authority":"Consent and mercy killing: consent no defense to homicide; the act/omission distinction; withdrawal of treatment on a competent patient's valid refusal; no common-law euthanasia defense."},{"n":16,"summary":"A firebomb set to destroy a house kills a nephew inside; a gun discharges accidentally during a bank robbery and kills a customer; a shot fired to frighten a clerk causes a fatal heart attack; a hired felon kills the wrong person during an intimidation errand.","trigger":"Death occurring during the commission or attempted commission of an inherently dangerous felony → murder without any intent to kill, and every accomplice to the felony is liable.","rule":"Under the FELONY-MURDER rule, a killing committed during the commission or attempted commission of an inherently dangerous felony is murder, the intent to commit the felony supplying the malice. The traditional predicates are burglary, arson, rape, robbery and kidnapping, and modern statutes add others. No intent to kill is required, the death need not be foreseeable in its particular manner — a fatal fright-induced heart attack qualifies — and the victim may be anyone other than a co-felon. Liability extends to EVERY accomplice to the underlying felony, including a getaway driver, a lookout and a procurer, whether or not present and whether or not he agreed that anyone be harmed; a supplier who knows the criminal purpose and has a stake in the venture is likewise liable. The defendant must be guilty of the underlying felony: if a defense defeats the felony, felony murder falls with it.","sub":"Homicide – Felony Murder: Rule, Predicates and Accomplices (Master)","sensitivity":"The rule converts an unintended death into murder for every participant in the felony, which is why the tested facts always feature an accident and a peripheral participant. Two dials: the felony must be inherently dangerous and provable, and the killing must fall within the felony's duration. The limits that cut the rule back are separated into Unit 17 because they carry their own jurisdictional splits.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the death was accidental, so the driver waiting outside answers for robbery and no one answers for murder” → the felony-murder rule makes every accomplice a murderer on the strength of the felony alone. There is no civil-law counterpart: the Venezuelan schema routes an unintended death through homicidio preterintencional or culposo and allocates it to whoever caused it, so a lookout who never touched a weapon being convicted of murder has no shelf in the trained schema. This is the single largest under-conviction error in the subject.","authority":"Felony murder: the traditional predicates (burglary, arson, rape, robbery, kidnapping); liability of all accomplices to the felony; no requirement that the manner of death be foreseeable; conviction of the predicate required."},{"n":17,"summary":"A customer resisting a robbery accidentally kills the cashier; a burglar too intoxicated to form the intent to steal kills the watchman; a defendant beats a victim who dies a week later and the prosecution pleads felony murder on the battery.","trigger":"Felony murder asserted → test the four limits: the felony must be independent of the killing, the killing must occur within the felony's duration, the fatal act must (in most jurisdictions) be a co-felon's, and the death of a co-felon is generally excluded.","rule":"Four limits confine the rule. (1) MERGER, or the independent-felony requirement: the predicate must be independent of the killing, so an assaultive felony whose elements are integral to the homicide — battery, aggravated assault, manslaughter — cannot serve; burglary, arson and robbery are independent and do serve. (2) DURATION: the felony continues through immediate flight and ends when the felons reach a place of temporary safety; a death after that point is outside the rule. (3) The IDENTITY OF THE KILLER: under the majority AGENCY rule, liability attaches only where the fatal act was committed by a felon, so a killing by a resisting victim, a bystander or the police falls outside it; the minority PROXIMATE-CAUSE rule reaches the opposite result. (4) The death of a CO-FELON is generally excluded. A corollary follows from the rule itself: because conviction of the predicate is required, a defense that negates the felony — voluntary intoxication negating the intent element of burglary — also defeats felony murder, though the killing may remain murder on another route.","sub":"Homicide – Felony Murder: The Four Limits and the Predicate Corollary","sensitivity":"Each limit is a separate question stem, and two of them carry jurisdictional splits that the call of the question must resolve — agency versus proximate cause, and the co-felon exclusion. The corollary is the most elegant chain in the subject: defeat the predicate's mens rea and the murder charge built on it collapses, while a different route to malice may still convict.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “whoever set the events in motion answers for everything that followed — he created the risk” → the majority U.S. rule asks WHO FIRED, not who started it, and acquits of murder where the fatal shot came from a victim or an officer. The civilian instinct tracks the minority proximate-cause theory, so the trained reflex reaches the right answer in the wrong jurisdiction; and because the two theories coexist across states, the candidate must read the call of the question rather than reason from causation alone.","authority":"Felony-murder limits: the independent-felony (merger) rule; duration and the place-of-temporary-safety test; the majority agency rule versus the minority proximate-cause rule (State v. Canola); the co-felon exclusion; the predicate-defeated corollary."},{"n":18,"summary":"A pickpocket takes a play banknote thinking it real and later slips it back; a man retrieves his own television from a neighbour's house at night; a patron takes a wallet from an unconscious drunk and drops it when police approach; a driver takes identification papers from a dead man's wallet.","trigger":"Property taken from another's possession → larceny requires a TRESPASSORY taking and carrying away, with the intent to permanently deprive existing AT THE MOMENT of the taking.","rule":"LARCENY is the trespassory taking and carrying away of the personal property of another, with the intent to permanently deprive the owner of it. Four points decide the cases. (1) The offense is COMPLETE at the moment of the taking with that intent: a later return, a change of heart, or the abandonment of the property is no defense. (2) A mistake as to the property's VALUE is irrelevant, since the intent is to deprive of the thing rather than of a sum. (3) A genuine CLAIM OF RIGHT negates larcenous intent: one who takes property he honestly believes is his own, or to which he has the superior right of possession, commits no larceny — and this matters beyond larceny, because it also defeats a burglary charge premised on an intent to steal. (4) Where the initial taking was already wrongful but the intent to steal forms only later, the doctrine of CONTINUING TRESPASS supplies the concurrence and the offense is larceny; where possession was lawful at the outset, the offense is embezzlement instead.","sub":"Theft – Larceny: Trespassory Taking and Concurrence (Master)","sensitivity":"Larceny is the baseline against which every other theft offense is defined, and the organizing axis of the whole block is HOW the defendant came to hold the property: wrongfully from the start (larceny, with continuing trespass curing a late intent), lawfully and then converted (embezzlement, Unit 19), or by obtaining title through deceit (false pretenses, Unit 20). Concurrence of act and intent is the recurring switch.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is largely RIGHT — hurto is the trespassory taking of another's movable with intent to appropriate, the claim of right defeats the ánimo de apropiación, and returning the thing does not undo the offense. The calibration friction is the CONCURRENCE machinery: U.S. law fixes the intent at the instant of the taking and then rescues late-forming intent through a specific doctrine (continuing trespass) available only where the taking was already wrongful — a technical gate the codal definition does not draw, and the gate that separates hurto from apropiación indebida.","authority":"Larceny: trespassory taking and asportation with intent to permanently deprive; concurrence at the moment of taking; claim of right; continuing trespass; mistake as to value immaterial."},{"n":19,"summary":"A friend lends her earrings for a dance and the borrower decides during the evening never to return them; an employee entrusted with a $20,000 deposit gambles it, wins, restores the principal and keeps the winnings.","trigger":"Property came into the defendant's hands LAWFULLY and was afterwards converted → embezzlement, not larceny; the amount is fixed at the moment of the misappropriation.","rule":"EMBEZZLEMENT is the fraudulent conversion of the property of another by one who is already in LAWFUL POSSESSION of it. The distinguishing feature is the origin of the possession: lawful at the outset, with the intent to defraud arising afterwards. That is the mirror image of larceny, where the taking was trespassory from the start and a late-forming intent is cured only by continuing trespass. Two consequences follow. The AMOUNT is measured by the value of the property at the time of the conversion, so restoring the principal while keeping the profits does not reduce the sum embezzled, and restitution is never a defense but only a matter in mitigation. And CUSTODY must be distinguished from possession: an employee handed goods to hold in the employer's presence has mere custody, so his misappropriation is larceny, while one entrusted with funds to deposit has possession and commits embezzlement.","sub":"Theft – Embezzlement: Conversion by One in Lawful Possession","sensitivity":"One question decides the label: how did the defendant come to hold the thing? Lawfully means embezzlement whatever happens next. The custody/possession refinement is the second dial, and it is what separates the shop assistant from the trusted bookkeeper on identical conversions. Restoration and profit are red herrings that the amount rule disposes of.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT and the categories align closely — apropiación indebida is precisely conversion by one who received the thing lawfully with the obligation to return it, and it is distinguished from hurto on the same axis. The calibration friction is narrow: U.S. law inserts a third rung, CUSTODY, below possession, so an employee holding goods under the employer's eye commits larceny rather than embezzlement — a distinction the two-category codal scheme does not draw.","authority":"Embezzlement: fraudulent conversion by one in lawful possession; value measured at the time of conversion; restitution no defense; the custody/possession distinction separating larceny from embezzlement."},{"n":20,"summary":"A man takes $10,000 to kill a rival intending only to steal the money; a shopper swaps price tags and pays the lower price; a repairman gains entry by falsely claiming he must inspect the furnace and then steals.","trigger":"Property obtained by a knowing misrepresentation → false pretenses if TITLE passed; larceny by trick if only POSSESSION passed.","rule":"FALSE PRETENSES is obtaining TITLE to the property of another by a knowing misrepresentation of a material past or present fact, made with intent to defraud, on which the victim relies. The decisive question is what the victim parted with: title, in which case the offense is false pretenses; or mere possession, in which case it is LARCENY BY TRICK. Money handed over on a fraudulent promise ordinarily passes title. A false promise of future conduct is generally insufficient at common law, which requires a misrepresentation of past or present fact, though many modern codes reach it. Payment obtained by altering a price tag is commonly analysed as false pretenses, since title passes on the fraudulent representation of price, and modern consolidated statutes fold all of these into a single offense of theft by deception.","sub":"Theft – False Pretenses and Larceny by Trick: Title vs. Possession","sensitivity":"Title or possession is the whole distinction, and the exam signals it by what the victim handed over and on what terms. A second dial is the content of the lie: at common law a misrepresentation of present fact qualifies while a promise of future performance does not. Where a jurisdiction has consolidated its theft offenses, the labels collapse — read the call of the question.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “there was engaño and patrimonial harm — that is estafa, one offense” → U.S. law splits the same conduct into two crimes according to whether title or only possession passed, a distinction estafa does not draw. The civilian schema asks whether deceit produced an act of disposition causing harm, and stops there; the common law then asks a proprietary question about WHAT was transferred, and the answer changes the offense charged — which is why questions in this block turn on the terms of the handover rather than on the gravity of the fraud.","authority":"False pretenses and larceny by trick: title versus possession; misrepresentation of past or present fact; false promises at common law; consolidated theft-by-deception statutes."},{"n":21,"summary":"A thief snatches a purse on the subway and immediately pushes the owner aside to flee; another grabs a watch and later draws a gun to keep it; a bank note threatens a non-existent bomb; a wallet is taken from an unconscious drunk.","trigger":"Larceny committed from the victim's person or presence → robbery only if force or intimidation was used TO OBTAIN the property; force used afterwards to retain it leaves the offense as larceny.","rule":"ROBBERY is larceny from the person or presence of the victim, accomplished by force or by threat of imminent harm. The concurrence requirement is the whole of it: the force or intimidation must be the means of OBTAINING the property. Force applied only after the taking is complete, to retain the property or to escape, does not convert larceny into robbery at common law — though a grab and an immediate push to get away are treated as a single transaction and do constitute robbery, and modern statutes and the Model Penal Code extend robbery to force used in immediate flight. Intimidation suffices without any touching, and a threat of harm from a weapon or accomplice that does not exist still qualifies where the victim reasonably fears; the victim must be aware of the threat, so taking from an unconscious or dead person is larceny. Any assault merges into the robbery.","sub":"Theft – Robbery: Force, Intimidation and Concurrence","sensitivity":"The tested variable is WHEN the force occurred relative to the taking, and the fact patterns are built in pairs to isolate it: grab-then-push is one transaction and robbery, take-then-threaten is larceny plus a separate offense. The second dial is the victim's awareness, which is why the unconscious and the dead produce larceny however violent the handling.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is broadly RIGHT — robo is hurto committed with violence or intimidation against persons, and the aggravation turns on the same two means. The calibration friction is the CONCURRENCE rule and the awareness requirement: Venezuelan doctrine readily treats violence used in flight as part of the robo, whereas the common law demands that the force be the means of obtaining the thing, and it downgrades to larceny where the victim never perceived the threat at all.","authority":"Robbery: larceny from the person or presence by force or intimidation; concurrence of the force with the taking; the single-transaction rule for immediate flight; victim awareness required; merger of the assault."},{"n":22,"summary":"A man arranges to buy what he believes is stolen marijuana from an undercover officer and directs delivery to an accomplice; he never physically touches the goods.","trigger":"Defendant acquires goods believed to be stolen → receiving stolen property requires that the goods actually BE stolen and that the defendant know it; control suffices for receipt, and police-supplied goods make the completed offense legally impossible.","rule":"RECEIVING STOLEN PROPERTY requires that the property in fact be stolen, that the defendant receive it knowing it to be stolen, and that he intend to permanently deprive the owner. Actual physical possession is unnecessary: asserting CONTROL over the goods, including directing their delivery to a third person, is constructive receipt. The offense fails, however, where the goods are not in truth stolen — property recovered by or supplied by law enforcement has lost its stolen character — because an element cannot be satisfied by the defendant's belief. That is legal impossibility as to the completed crime, but the ATTEMPT remains chargeable under the majority approach that judges the attempt on the facts as the defendant believed them to be (Unit 5).","sub":"Theft – Receiving Stolen Property and Constructive Receipt","sensitivity":"Two independent gates: the CHARACTER of the goods, which the defendant's belief cannot supply, and the MODE of receipt, which his lack of physical contact cannot defeat. The sting scenario is built to satisfy the second and fail the first, so the correct answer is attempt rather than the completed offense.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “he bought what he thought was stolen and organised its delivery — that is aprovechamiento de cosas provenientes del delito” → the completed U.S. offense collapses because the goods were not stolen, and the conviction survives only as an attempt. The civilian receptación schema likewise requires that the thing come from a crime, but it does not pair that requirement with a robust attempt doctrine built on the actor's belief, so the trained reflex either convicts of the full offense or acquits entirely — and both are wrong.","authority":"Receiving stolen property: the goods must in fact be stolen; knowledge and intent to deprive; constructive receipt by assertion of control; police-supplied goods and the resulting attempt liability."},{"n":23,"summary":"A student throws a battery at a professor who never notices until he sees a video; a defendant intends only to frighten and is charged with assault; a shove at a soccer match causes a fatal fall.","trigger":"Offensive or harmful contact, or conduct threatening it → battery is a general-intent crime completed by the contact; criminal assault has TWO theories, and only one of them requires the victim's awareness.","rule":"BATTERY is the unlawful application of force to another resulting in bodily injury or an offensive touching. It is a GENERAL-INTENT crime: no intent to injure is required, recklessness or criminal negligence suffices, and the contact may be indirect. Aggravated battery adds a deadly weapon, serious bodily harm or a protected victim. Criminal ASSAULT exists on two independent theories. Under the ATTEMPTED-BATTERY theory it is an attempt, so it requires the specific intent to commit a battery and the victim need not be aware of it at all. Under the THREAT theory it is the intentional creation of a reasonable apprehension of imminent bodily harm, and the victim's awareness is essential. The two theories diverge sharply from tort assault, which always requires apprehension, and a completed battery absorbs the assault that preceded it.","sub":"Crimes Against the Person – Battery and the Two Theories of Assault","sensitivity":"Awareness is the switch, and it points in opposite directions on the two theories: irrelevant for attempted battery, indispensable for the threat theory. The second dial is mens rea — battery is general intent and reachable by recklessness, while attempted-battery assault demands specific intent, so a defendant who meant only to frighten commits neither a battery nor that form of assault.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “lesiones requires injury, and amenaza requires that the victim hear the threat — an unnoticed throw is nothing” → U.S. law convicts of assault on the attempted-battery theory although the victim never perceived a thing, because the offense is an inchoate battery rather than a fright crime. The civilian pairing of lesiones with amenazas maps onto neither theory cleanly, and the shared word 'assault' misleads twice, since criminal assault and tort assault are themselves different offenses in the same legal system.","authority":"Battery and assault: battery as a general-intent offense reachable by recklessness; the attempted-battery and threat theories of criminal assault; divergence from tort assault; merger of assault into battery."},{"n":24,"summary":"A repairman lies his way inside and steals; a man enters at night with a copied key to retrieve his own painting; an employer enters an employee's unlocked house to recover equipment he believes is his; a planner directs another to break in.","trigger":"Entry into a structure → burglary requires a breaking and an entry with the intent to commit a felony inside, and that intent must exist AT THE MOMENT of entry.","rule":"Common-law BURGLARY is the breaking and entering of the dwelling of another at night with the intent to commit a felony therein; modern statutes commonly drop the night and dwelling requirements and reach any structure. A BREAKING may be actual, including opening an unlocked door or window, or CONSTRUCTIVE, where entry is obtained by fraud, threat or an unauthorised key — but walking through a wide-open door is no breaking at common law. ENTRY occurs when any part of the body or of an instrument used to commit the felony crosses the plane. The intent to commit a felony must exist AT THE MOMENT OF ENTRY: forming it afterwards defeats the charge, and a genuine claim of right to the property inside negates the intent to steal and therefore the burglary as well. One who procures an innocent or unwitting agent to make the entry, himself holding the felonious intent, is liable as though he entered.","sub":"Property Offenses – Burglary: Breaking, Entry and Intent at Entry","sensitivity":"Timing is the element the questions attack: intent at entry, not intent formed inside. The second recurring switch is the claim of right, which defeats the felony element and so defeats the burglary — which is why the man retrieving his own property walks free of a charge that looks unanswerable on the facts. Constructive breaking by deceit is the third.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “violación de domicilio plus hurto — two offenses, and the trespass is complete on entering without permission” → U.S. burglary is a single compound offense whose gravity comes from the INTENT carried across the threshold, so an unlawful entry without felonious purpose is not burglary at all, while an entry through an unlocked door with that purpose is. The civilian pairing of a domicile offense with a separate theft supplies no reason to interrogate the defendant's state of mind at the instant of entry — the very question that decides every burglary problem.","authority":"Burglary: breaking (actual and constructive) and entry; the common-law dwelling and night requirements and their modern abandonment; intent to commit a felony at the moment of entry; claim of right; innocent-agent entry."},{"n":25,"summary":"A homeless man lights a fire in an attached garage to keep warm and the house burns; a woman throws a cigarette during a burglary and the ceiling chars; a father demands a ride at toy-gun point and his son drives him across town.","trigger":"Burning of a structure, or restraint and movement of a person → arson requires malice and a burning; kidnapping requires unlawful restraint accomplished by force or threat, in most jurisdictions with movement or concealment.","rule":"ARSON at common law is the malicious burning of the dwelling of another; modern statutes extend it to any structure and often to one's own property. MALICE here means the intent to burn OR reckless disregard of an obvious risk of burning — no ill will and no desire to destroy is needed, so setting a warming fire in an attached garage suffices. There must be a BURNING: charring of the structure qualifies, while smoke damage, blackening or scorching does not, and damage confined to personal property inside is not arson. Where a death results, arson is a classic felony-murder predicate. KIDNAPPING is the unlawful restraint of another accomplished by force or threat, requiring in most jurisdictions some movement or concealment in a secret place; an apparent weapon satisfies the threat element where the victim reasonably fears imminent serious harm, the victim's theoretical chance to escape does not defeat it, and a family relationship is no defense.","sub":"Arson and Kidnapping – Malice, Burning, Restraint and Asportation","sensitivity":"Arson turns on two words the questions attack separately: MALICE, which recklessness satisfies, and BURNING, which requires charring rather than smoke. Kidnapping turns on whether the threat was reasonably feared — not on whether the weapon was real — and on whether the movement was more than incidental to another offense.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is broadly RIGHT — incendio is a crime of danger reachable by dolo eventual, and privación ilícita de libertad covers the restraint. The calibration friction is in the thresholds rather than the concepts: the common law demands an actual charring of the STRUCTURE, excluding smoke and contents damage, and most kidnapping statutes add an asportation element that the civilian deprivation-of-liberty offense does not require.","authority":"Arson and kidnapping: malice satisfied by reckless disregard; charring required, scorching insufficient; arson as a felony-murder predicate; kidnapping's restraint and asportation elements; apparent weapons satisfying the threat element."},{"n":26,"summary":"A homeowner shoots a fleeing car burglar 150 feet away; a woman who started a confrontation withdraws and then shoots her pursuer; a man repels a raised-fists threat with pepper spray in a retreat jurisdiction; a wealthy man stabs a would-be thief to teach him a lesson.","trigger":"Force used against another and justification claimed → test the interest defended, the proportionality of the force, and, for deadly force only, any duty to retreat.","rule":"SELF-DEFENSE permits force reasonably necessary to repel an imminent unlawful threat, and DEADLY force only against an imminent threat of death or serious bodily harm, judged by the defendant's reasonable belief; an honest but unreasonable belief supports imperfect self-defense, which mitigates murder to manslaughter in many jurisdictions. An INITIAL AGGRESSOR may regain the privilege by clearly communicating withdrawal, or where the victim escalates non-deadly aggression into deadly force. Any duty to RETREAT applies only before using DEADLY force; a majority of jurisdictions impose none, and none applies in one's own dwelling. DEFENSE OF PROPERTY permits reasonable non-deadly force only: deadly force may never be used solely to protect personal property, nor may mechanical devices such as spring guns be set to do what the owner could not do in person. Defense of habitation is broader, and defense of others is measured by the reasonable-belief standard in most modern jurisdictions.","sub":"Defenses – Justification: Self-Defense, Defense of Property, Retreat (Master)","sensitivity":"Three dials, and the questions turn one at a time: WHAT is being defended (a person permits deadly force, chattels never do), WHO started it and whether withdrawal was communicated, and WHETHER the force used was deadly at all — which is the only question the retreat rule ever reaches. The eggshell victim is a distractor: a disproportionate death does not retroactively justify the force.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT in structure — legítima defensa requires an unlawful aggression, a proportionate means and no sufficient provocation by the defender (C.P. art. 65(3)), and Venezuelan doctrine likewise refuses deadly force for mere property. The calibration friction is the RETREAT question, which the codal proportionality analysis absorbs rather than isolating, and the initial-aggressor rule, where the common law offers a precise mechanism for regaining the privilege that the requirement of no prior provocation states only as a bar.","authority":"Justification defenses: self-defense and the reasonable-belief standard; imperfect self-defense; the initial-aggressor rule and regaining the privilege by communicated withdrawal; retreat only before deadly force and the castle exception; no deadly force to protect chattels; the spring-gun rule (People v. Ceballos); defense of habitation and of others."},{"n":27,"summary":"A defendant with no prior diagnosis suddenly attacks a neighbour at a card game and raises insanity; a murder defendant pleads insanity in a jurisdiction treating it as an affirmative defense.","trigger":"Insanity raised → identify which TEST the jurisdiction applies and, separately, who bears which burden; the two questions are independent.","rule":"Four tests coexist across U.S. jurisdictions. M'NAGHTEN: at the time of the act, a mental disease or defect left the defendant unable to know the nature and quality of his act, or unable to know that it was wrong. IRRESISTIBLE IMPULSE: he was unable to control his conduct or conform it to the law. The MODEL PENAL CODE substantial-capacity test: he lacked substantial capacity either to appreciate the criminality of his conduct or to conform it to the law. DURHAM: the act was the product of a mental disease or defect. BURDENS are a separate question with no single answer: the defendant always bears the burden of production, while the burden of persuasion may rest on the prosecution to disprove insanity beyond a reasonable doubt, or on the defendant by a preponderance, and under federal law by clear and convincing evidence. A formal prior diagnosis is not required, and insanity is distinct from competency to stand trial, which concerns the defendant's present ability to understand the proceedings and assist counsel.","sub":"Defenses – Insanity: The Four Tests and the Burden Question","sensitivity":"Two independent variables the question stem always supplies: which test, and which burden allocation. Reading past either one produces a confident wrong answer, because the same facts acquit under the substantial-capacity test and convict under M'Naghten. Note the temporal frame — insanity is assessed at the time of the ACT, competency at the time of trial.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “inimputabilidad by mental illness is a single legal question the expert informs and the court decides” → U.S. law runs four competing tests across jurisdictions, with three different burden allocations layered on top, so the governing standard must be read from the question rather than known. The civilian schema (C.P. art. 62: exemption for one who at the time of the act was in a state of mental disturbance sufficient to deprive him of consciousness or freedom of his acts) supplies one criterion applied uniformly — leaving no place for a doctrine whose content changes with the forum.","authority":"Insanity: M'Naghten; irresistible impulse; the Model Penal Code substantial-capacity test (§ 4.01); Durham; the burden-of-production and burden-of-persuasion split, including 18 U.S.C. § 17 (clear and convincing evidence in federal prosecutions) and Leland v. Oregon; insanity distinguished from competency to stand trial (Dusky v. United States)."},{"n":28,"summary":"A very drunk man has sex after consent is revoked; another commits several offenses while intoxicated and asks which conviction fails; a burglar too drunk to form the intent to steal kills the watchman; an executive signs false filings his lawyer drafted; a felon keeps a found handgun meaning to hand it in.","trigger":"Defendant asserts intoxication, mistake of fact, or reliance on legal advice → each operates only by negating a mental element, so availability depends on which mens rea category the offense occupies.","rule":"These defenses are not excuses but denials of an element, and each is bounded by the mens rea of the offense charged. VOLUNTARY INTOXICATION negates only SPECIFIC intent, and only where it actually prevented formation of that intent; it is no defense to general-intent, malice or strict-liability crimes, and never to depraved-heart murder. Because it can negate the intent element of a predicate felony, it may defeat a felony-murder charge built on that felony while leaving murder available on another route. INVOLUNTARY intoxication — by fraud, duress, or unanticipated medical reaction — is treated as insanity and is far broader. MISTAKE OF FACT negates specific intent if honest, however unreasonable; negates general intent only if reasonable; and never excuses a strict-liability element such as the victim's age. MISTAKE OF LAW is ordinarily no defense, with narrow exceptions: reliance on an official statement of the law later held erroneous, a statute not reasonably made available, and — most importantly — where the mistake negates a specific mens rea such as 'willfully'. Advice of counsel is not a freestanding defense and bears on willfulness only where the defendant fully disclosed the material facts and relied in good faith.","sub":"Defenses – Intoxication, Mistake of Fact, and Mistake of Law (Master)","sensitivity":"All three defenses run through the same gate built in Unit 1: identify the mens rea category first, and the availability of the defense follows mechanically. Two high-yield refinements: voluntary intoxication can collapse a felony-murder charge by destroying its predicate, and mistake of law — normally worthless — becomes powerful when the statute uses the word 'willfully'.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the drunkenness reduces culpability across the board, and error de prohibición invencible excuses” → U.S. law makes voluntary intoxication irrelevant to most crimes and treats ignorance of the law as no defense at all outside three narrow exceptions. Venezuelan doctrine grades intoxication as a mitigating or exempting circumstance according to its degree and origin rather than by the offense's mens rea category, and it recognises invincible error of prohibition as excluding culpability — so the trained reflex claims relief in exactly the cases where the common law refuses it, and misses the one place it is available: when the statute requires willfulness.","authority":"Intoxication and mistake: voluntary intoxication limited to specific-intent offenses; involuntary intoxication treated as insanity; mistake of fact graded by the offense's mens rea category; mistake of law and its three exceptions (official reliance, unavailable statute, negation of a specific mens rea such as willfulness); advice of counsel as bearing on willfulness only."}],"cases":[{"n":1,"summary":"One evening, a woman was driving above the speed limit on a country road. As she rounded a sharp curve, she lost control of the car and crossed over to the shoulder on the other side of the road. Her car hit a truck that was parked on the shoulder with its hood up while its driver waited for a tow truck.","trigger":"Traffic misdemeanor causes death","rule":"Unintentional killing during a misdemeanor constitutes involuntary manslaughter.","sub":"Involuntary manslaughter","family":"Homicide","unit":13},{"n":2,"summary":"A defendant was arrested and charged with burglary. Under state law, police are required to obtain DNA samples from all persons arrested for felonies and to store those samples in a state registry. The police informed the defendant of the state law and said that they would be using a cheek swab to obtain a DNA sample from him.","trigger":"DNA taken from felony arrestee","rule":"DNA collection from felony arrestees does not violate the Fourth Amendment.","sub":"DNA / 4A","family":"Fourth Amendment","unit":null},{"n":3,"summary":"Assigned counsel for a capital defendant had heard that his client had an unsavory background and a highly dysfunctional childhood. He was reluctant to delve into the details for fear that they might reflect poorly on his client, and he decided not to investigate. After his client was convicted, counsel presented a penalty phase defense that repeated the denials of the liability phase.","trigger":"Capital counsel fails to investigate mitigation","rule":"Failure to investigate mitigation in capital cases is ineffective assistance.","sub":"Innefective Assistance of Counsel","family":"Sixth Amendment","unit":null},{"n":4,"summary":"Friend unknowingly drives man to cousin’s house; only learns of robbery as they flee.","trigger":"No accomplice liability without intent to help the crime.","rule":"Before a person can be found guilty as an accomplice, they must have the mens rea of helping the criminal complete a crime.","sub":"Accomplice Liability","family":"Parties to Crime","unit":8},{"n":5,"summary":"Woman pickpocketed wallet during hug; date watched and approved; she charged with robbery/larceny","trigger":"Liability for another’s theft turns on accomplice/conspiracy intent and act in furtherance","rule":"For both accomplice liability and conspiracy liability, the defendant must have the specific intent to commit the crime (mens rea) and perform some type of act in furtherance of the crime.","sub":"Accomplice/Conspiracy","family":"Parties to Crime","unit":8},{"n":6,"summary":"Homeless person breaks into attached garage, starts fire to keep warm; fire spreads and burns house.","trigger":"Reckless fire-setting in another’s dwelling = arson.","rule":"Malice, for purposes of arson, means that there was either an intent to burn or reckless disregard of a high risk of burning, the dwelling house of another.","sub":"Arson","family":"Arson and Kidnapping","unit":25},{"n":7,"summary":"Gun accidentally discharged during foiled bank robbery; attempted murder charged to man, driver, and gun seller","trigger":"Attempt liability asserted without intent to kill","rule":"Attempt requires specific intent; accidental injury cannot support attempted murder","sub":"Attempt","family":"Inchoate Offenses","unit":4},{"n":8,"summary":"Man had sex with 15-year-old who used a perfect fake ID; charged with attempted statutory rape","trigger":"Attempt requires specific intent regardless of completed offense mens rea. Strict liability crimes like rape don’t have MENS REA","rule":"The mens rea required for any attempt crime is the specific intent that the crime be committed.","sub":"Attempt","family":"Mens Rea","unit":1},{"n":9,"summary":"Defendant tries to pry open neighbor’s window to steal coins, then ‘gives up’; unknown to him, entry was impossible due to theft-proof windows and cameras recorded it.","trigger":"Attempt completed by substantial step; abandonment defense fails if not voluntary and/or after attempt is complete; factual impossibility not a defense.","rule":"In a majority of states, voluntary abandonment is not a defense if a crime has already been committed.","sub":"Attempt","family":"Inchoate Offenses","unit":4},{"n":10,"summary":"Shoots at husband’s hand to scare; ricochet kills wife; husband minor injury.","trigger":"Attempted murder requires specific intent to kill (not merely scare).","rule":"Attempted murder requires the specific intent to kill.","sub":"Attempt – Murder (Intent)","family":"Mens Rea","unit":9},{"n":11,"summary":"Partygoer uses handheld BAC device showing .07 to decide he is safe to drive; police breathalyzer reads .08 and he is charged with attempting to drive drunk.","trigger":"Attempt liability requires specific intent to commit the target offense; defendant thought he was below legal limit.","rule":"Any attempt crime requires the specific intent to commit the crime.","sub":"Attempt – Specific Intent","family":"Inchoate Offenses","unit":4},{"n":12,"summary":"Uses BAC device reads .07 but actual .08; charged with attempted DUI.","trigger":"Attempt crimes require specific intent to commit target offense.","rule":"Any attempt crime requires the specific intent to commit the crime.","sub":"Attempt – Specific Intent","family":"Mens Rea","unit":1},{"n":13,"summary":"Diner fires at husband’s hand from two feet away intending only to scare him; bullet hits hand, then ricochets and kills wife; husband suffers minor injury.","trigger":"Attempted murder requires specific intent to kill, which is absent when defendant only intends to scare.","rule":"Attempted murder requires the specific intent to kill.","sub":"Attempt – Specific Intent to Kill","family":"Inchoate Offenses","unit":4},{"n":14,"summary":"Man breaks into neighbor’s house at night to retrieve his own painting; neighbor sees him, has a heart attack, but survives.","trigger":"Attempted murder requires specific intent to kill; felony-murder logic is not enough.","rule":"Although murder can be found through the felony murder rule, attempted murder requires the specific intent to kill.","sub":"Attempt / Attempted Murder","family":"Inchoate Offenses","unit":4},{"n":15,"summary":"Burglary conspiracy; cigarette thrown; fire charred ceiling; woman charged with attempted arson","trigger":"Attempted arson charge requires intent to burn","rule":"Attempt requires specific intent to commit the crime; lack of intent to burn defeats attempted arson","sub":"Attempt/Conspiracy","family":"Inchoate Offenses","unit":4},{"n":16,"summary":"Brother, cousin, and nephew plot to kill governor; brother and cousin then plot to kill bragging nephew; cousin sabotages harness causing fall, brother later pushes unconscious nephew into river where he drowns.","trigger":"Co-conspirator liability for reasonably foreseeable acts in furtherance of the conspiracy, including killings.","rule":"One co-conspirator will be liable for the action of others if the actions are in furtherance of the conspiracy and are reasonably foreseeable.","sub":"Conspiracy – Co-conspirator Liability (Pinkerton)","family":"Conspiracy","unit":7},{"n":17,"summary":"Pair agree to robbery scheme; woman feigns injury, man threatens elderly victim with gun but they flee empty‑handed.","trigger":"No conspiracy to armed robbery if she never agreed to use gun, but she’s liable for foreseeable attempt in furtherance.","rule":"A person cannot be guilty of forming a conspiracy for a crime they never intended to commit, although they can be found guilty of any crime committed by a co-conspirator that is in furtherance and the foreseeable scope of the conspiracy.","sub":"Conspiracy & Attempt / Robbery","family":"Conspiracy","unit":7},{"n":18,"summary":"Homeowner shoots fleeing car burglar from window; car 150 ft away; charged aggravated assault.","trigger":"Deadly force not permitted solely to protect personal property (common law).","rule":"At common law, deadly force cannot be used to protect personal property.","sub":"Defense of Property – Deadly Force","family":"Defenses","unit":26},{"n":19,"summary":"Homeowner sees woman trying to break into car parked on street 150 feet away; after warning shot, he shoots her in leg as she runs away.","trigger":"Deadly force used solely to protect personal property (car) at common law is not justified.","rule":"At common law, deadly force cannot be used to protect personal property.","sub":"Defenses – Defense of Property / Deadly Force","family":"Defenses","unit":26},{"n":20,"summary":"Impaired driver going 65 mph in 35 mph residential zone hits and kills a poorly visible jogger; charged with murder and seeks lesser-offense instruction.","trigger":"Unintentional killing caused by gross negligence creating high risk of death supports involuntary manslaughter.","rule":"Involuntary manslaughter is a lesser offense of murder, requiring gross negligence that leads to an unreasonably high risk of death or great bodily harm.","sub":"Homicide – Involuntary Manslaughter","family":"Homicide","unit":13},{"n":21,"summary":"Impaired driver speeding 65 in 35 kills jogger; mitigating factors; charged with murder; request lesser-included.","trigger":"Lesser included of murder: involuntary manslaughter (gross negligence).","rule":"Involuntary manslaughter is a lesser offense of murder, requiring gross negligence that leads to an unreasonably high risk of death or great bodily harm.","sub":"Homicide – Involuntary Manslaughter","family":"Homicide","unit":13},{"n":22,"summary":"Shoots husband at close range intending to kill; bullet kills wife via ricochet.","trigger":"Malice for murder can be shown by intent to kill/GBH, extreme recklessness, or felony murder.","rule":"At common law, the malice required for murder can be found either through the intent to kill, the intent to cause great bodily harm, the reckless disregard of a high chance that death could occur, or through the felony murder rule.","sub":"Homicide – Malice Murder","family":"Homicide","unit":10},{"n":23,"summary":"Terminally ill wife asks husband to disconnect respirator; husband complies and she dies peacefully. Husband is prosecuted for homicide.","trigger":"Victim consent is not a defense to homicide; mercy killing can still support murder charges.","rule":"A crime, even with the victim’s consent, is a crime against society that can still be prosecuted.","sub":"Homicide – Murder (Degrees)","family":"Homicide","unit":15},{"n":24,"summary":"Restaurant diner intentionally shoots husband in head at close range; bullet ricochets and kills wife; husband survives, wife dies.","trigger":"Intent to kill one victim transfers to unintended victim while attempt remains for original target.","rule":"At common law, the malice required for murder can be found either through the intent to kill, the intent to cause great bodily harm, the reckless disregard of a high chance that death could occur, or through the felony murder rule.","sub":"Homicide – Transferred Intent & Attempt","family":"Homicide","unit":10},{"n":25,"summary":"Man firebombs brother’s house intending to burn it down; nephew, unknown to him, dies inside.","trigger":"Felony murder for death caused during arson even without intent to kill victim.","rule":"62. A. Even if the D did not intend to commit murder, he can be found guilty of murder under the felony murder rule, as long as the elements of the underlying felony can be proven.","sub":"Homicide / Felony Murder & Arson","family":"Homicide","unit":16},{"n":26,"summary":"Woman starts confrontation and pokes jogger; jogger follows her into house and advances; woman shoots in fear.","trigger":"Initial aggressor who clearly withdraws can regain full self-defense.","rule":"70. D. Although an initial aggressor cannot usually use self-defense as a complete defense, they can if they clearly communicated their withdrawal to the other person involved.","sub":"Homicide / Self-Defense & Initial Aggressor","family":"Defenses","unit":26},{"n":27,"summary":"Man intends to kill business rival with premeditation; startled by squirrel, fires and misses; bullet kills ex-wife (domestic partner) instead; statute makes intentional murder of domestic partner capital, D&P murder is first degree.","trigger":"Transferred intent: intent to kill one transfers to actual victim for homicide grading.","rule":"If a person has the mens rea to commit a crime but unintentionally commits it on the wrong victim, they will still be guilty of the crime under a transferred intent theory.","sub":"Homicide / Transferred Intent","family":"Mens Rea","unit":9},{"n":28,"summary":"Wealthy man stabbed would-be thief to 'teach him a lesson'; victim unexpectedly died due to hemophilia","trigger":"Use of deadly force to protect personal property resulting in death","rule":"A person cannot use deadly force to protect personal property.","sub":"Homicide/Defense of Property","family":"Defenses","unit":26},{"n":29,"summary":"Caterer asked butler for safe combo to steal; butler feigned agreement; caterer went to room but was stopped before touching safe","trigger":"Solicitation plus steps toward committing larceny (attempt) under common law","rule":"Solicitation will merge into a completed attempt charge if asked what the D can be convicted of.","sub":"Inchoate (Solicitation/Attempt)","family":"Inchoate Offenses","unit":6},{"n":30,"summary":"Murder defendant pleads insanity in a jurisdiction treating insanity as an affirmative defense and must meet the related burdens.","trigger":"Insanity as an affirmative defense: defendant produces some evidence; prosecution must then disprove insanity.","rule":"With the affirmative defense of insanity, the defendant has the burden of producing evidence that the defendant is insane but the prosecution has the burden to persuade the jury that the defendant is not insane.","sub":"Insanity – Burden of Proof","family":"Defenses","unit":27},{"n":31,"summary":"Murder trial; insanity as affirmative defense jurisdiction.","trigger":"Burden allocation for affirmative defense of insanity (production vs persuasion).","rule":"With the affirmative defense of insanity, the defendant has the burden of producing evidence that the defendant is insane but the prosecution has the burden to persuade the jury that the defendant is not insane.","sub":"Insanity – Burdens","family":"Defenses","unit":27},{"n":32,"summary":"Intoxicated defendant committed multiple crimes; question asks which cannot be convicted","trigger":"Voluntary intoxication offered to negate specific intent","rule":"Voluntary intoxication will only be a defense to a specific intent crime if it prevents the defendant from being able to form the mens rea required for the crime.","sub":"Intoxication","family":"Mens Rea","unit":1},{"n":33,"summary":"Pickpocket takes play $20 bill thinking it’s real, then sneaks it back into purse after realizing.","trigger":"Larceny complete upon taking with intent to permanently deprive, regardless of value or later return.","rule":"Mistake of fact about the value of the item stolen does not negate the crime of larceny. A person will still be guilty of larceny even if they return the stolen item.","sub":"Larceny / Mistake of Value","family":"Theft","unit":18},{"n":34,"summary":"Very drunk man has sex with woman after she revokes consent and screams for him to stop.","trigger":"Voluntary intoxication no defense to rape (general intent).","rule":"Although voluntary intoxication can be a defense to a specific intent crime, it cannot be a defense to a general intent crime, like rape.","sub":"Rape / Intoxication","family":"Mens Rea","unit":1},{"n":35,"summary":"Man arranges to buy what he believes is stolen marijuana from undercover officer and directs delivery to accomplice.","trigger":"Asserting control over property = constructive receipt for receiving stolen property.","rule":"Asserting control over property will be deemed constructive receipt or possession for the purposes of receiving stolen property.","sub":"Receiving Stolen Property / Attempt","family":"Inchoate Offenses","unit":5},{"n":36,"summary":"Thief grabbed watch then later used a gun to prevent owner from reclaiming it","trigger":"Force used to retain property rather than to take it","rule":"For a theft to be robbery, the force must be used to acquire possession, not to keep possession once the thief already has possession.","sub":"Robbery/Larceny","family":"Theft","unit":21},{"n":37,"summary":"Employee entrusted with $20k deposit; gambles and wins; returns $20k; keeps $17.5k; buys gift.","trigger":"Value/amount for theft offenses measured at time of taking.","rule":"With theft crimes, the amount stolen is determined by the value of the item at the time of the crime.","sub":"Theft – Embezzlement (Amount)","family":"Theft","unit":19},{"n":38,"summary":"Hitman-for-hire offer to undercover; takes $10k then flees; claims only intended to steal money.","trigger":"Obtaining title by misrepresentation constitutes false pretenses.","rule":"False pretenses involves obtaining title to the victim’s property through the use of fraud.","sub":"Theft – False Pretenses","family":"Theft","unit":20},{"n":39,"summary":"Snatches purse from cart; victim falls; employee grabs strap; thief yanks, drops purse, runs.","trigger":"Larceny vs robbery: robbery requires force/threat used to take property (not to retain after taking).","rule":"Larceny is the trespassory taking and carrying away the personal property of another with the intent to permanently deprive them of it. The crime will be robbery if the crime occurs in the victim’s presence with force or threat of force. The force must be used to take the property, not to maintain it once it has already been taken.","sub":"Theft – Larceny vs Robbery","family":"Theft","unit":21},{"n":40,"summary":"Man swaps price tags to pay less for an item and buys it at the lower price.","trigger":"Title obtained by fraud → larceny by false pretenses.","rule":"When a person fraudulently obtains title to personal property of another, as opposed to just possession, it will be larceny by false pretenses.","sub":"Theft / Larceny by False Pretenses","family":"Theft","unit":20},{"n":41,"summary":"Employee given $20,000 to deposit; instead gambles with it, wins $38,000, then replaces $20,000, buys boss a $500 gift, and keeps $17,500.","trigger":"Embezzlement amount is based on value of property at time of initial misappropriation.","rule":"With theft crimes, the amount stolen is determined by the value of the item at the time of the crime.","sub":"Theft Crimes – Embezzlement & Amount","family":"Theft","unit":19},{"n":42,"summary":"Man offers to kill business rival for $10,000 but secretly only intends to steal the money; undercover officer pays him and he tries to flee with cash.","trigger":"Obtaining title to victim’s money through fraudulent representation constitutes false pretenses.","rule":"False pretenses involves obtaining title to the victim’s property through the use of fraud.","sub":"Theft Crimes – False Pretenses","family":"Theft","unit":20},{"n":43,"summary":"Shopper’s unattended purse is taken from cart; woman falls and breaks ankle; employee grabs strap, falls as thief pulls, wallet falls out, thief drops purse and runs.","trigger":"Force used only to retain property after taking, not to take it from victim’s person or presence, is larceny not robbery.","rule":"Larceny is the trespassory taking and carrying away the personal property of another with the intent to permanently deprive them of it. The crime will be robbery if the crime occurs in the victim’s presence with force or threat of force. The force must be used to take the property, not to maintain it once it has already been taken.","sub":"Theft Crimes – Larceny vs Robbery / Force Timing","family":"Theft","unit":21},{"n":44,"summary":"During a casual card game at home, the defendant suddenly “snapped” and tried to rip his neighbor’s nose off. The defendant had no formal diagnosis, but people had noticed escalating violence recently. A court-appointed psychiatrist evaluated him for trial, and defense counsel wants to raise insanity.","trigger":"Whether, at the time of the battery, the defendant had a mental disease/defect that prevented him from understanding what he was doing or that it was wrong.","rule":"To successfully prove not guilty by reason of insanity under M’Naghten, the defense must prove that the defendant suffered from a mental disease or defect at the time of the crime and did not know the nature and quality of his act, or that the act was wrong.","sub":"Insanity – M’Naghten","family":"Defenses","unit":27},{"n":45,"summary":"Two neighbors swapped a plasma TV for a projector for one week so one could ‘try before buying,’ with the right to change his mind. After deciding he disliked the projector, the man couldn’t reach the neighbor, got angry, sold the neighbor’s projector to a pawn shop, then later used a key to enter the neighbor’s home at night and quietly took back his own TV.","trigger":"Whether the man’s self‑help retrieval of his own property constitutes larceny (and what other crimes may apply).","rule":"Taking back your own property, if you have the better rights to it, will not be larceny. But, if he rented the TV and the man tried to obtain back the neigbore in this example would have a better right over the TV.","sub":"Theft – Larceny/Embezzlement","family":"Theft","unit":18},{"n":46,"summary":"After drinking at a bar, Defendant became angry when a town drunk bumped into him and spilled his drink. After time passed, Defendant deliberately broke a beer bottle, kept the jagged edge, waited until the victim left the bar, and then slashed the victim’s throat.","trigger":"Killing following a period of reflection and preparation.","rule":"Deliberation and premeditation—shown by time to cool off and conscious preparation—elevate murder to first degree.","sub":"Homicide – First Degree Murder","family":"Homicide","unit":11},{"n":47,"summary":"Roommate secretly put a knockout drug into Law Student’s coffee intending only to make Law Student sleep. Roommate miscalculated the dosage, causing Law Student’s death.","trigger":"Unintentional killing resulting from reckless or dangerous conduct.","rule":"An unintentional killing without malice but resulting from dangerous conduct constitutes involuntary manslaughter.","sub":"Homicide – Involuntary Manslaughter","family":"Homicide","unit":13},{"n":48,"summary":"Bank Robber entered a bank with a loaded gun to commit robbery. During a struggle with a resisting customer, the gun accidentally discharged, killing the customer.","trigger":"Death caused during the commission of an inherently dangerous felony.","rule":"A killing that occurs during the commission or attempted commission of a dangerous felony constitutes felony murder.","sub":"Homicide – Felony Murder","family":"Homicide","unit":16},{"n":49,"summary":"Husband unplugged his terminally ill wife’s respirator at her request after doctors confirmed she would die within days. Wife died peacefully shortly thereafter.","trigger":"Intentional act causing death without legal justification or excuse.","rule":"Assisting in ending a person’s life constitutes homicide despite consent or terminal illness.","sub":"Homicide – Murder","family":"Homicide","unit":15},{"n":50,"summary":"Owner hired Felon to rob and intimidate a rival video store owner. Felon mistakenly confronted an employee and, during a struggle, shot and killed him.","trigger":"Death resulting from a foreseeable crime committed by a hired accomplice.","rule":"An accomplice is liable for any foreseeable crimes committed in furtherance of the agreed-upon felony.","sub":"Accomplice Liability – Felony Murder","family":"Parties to Crime","unit":8},{"n":51,"summary":"After being verbally taunted at work about her performance, Employee became enraged, grabbed a letter opener, and stabbed Taunter in the heart, killing him instantly.","trigger":"Intentional killing without adequate provocation to reduce malice.","rule":"Words alone are insufficient provocation; intentional killing with malice constitutes murder.","sub":"Homicide – Murder","family":"Homicide","unit":12},{"n":52,"summary":"Father failed to take his critically ill child to the hospital due to fear of medical bills, instead using only a wet towel as treatment. The child died overnight.","trigger":"Death caused by criminal negligence or failure to act where a duty exists.","rule":"A killing resulting from criminal negligence or breach of a legal duty constitutes involuntary manslaughter.","sub":"Homicide – Involuntary Manslaughter","family":"Actus Reus","unit":2},{"n":53,"summary":"Waiter, angered by Playboy’s behavior with his ex‑fiancé, deliberately poisoned Playboy’s food intending severe suffering but not death under a modern statute.","trigger":"Intent to cause serious bodily injury demonstrating malice.","rule":"Intent to cause serious bodily injury constitutes malice sufficient for murder under modern statutes.","sub":"Homicide – Malice Murder","family":"Homicide","unit":10},{"n":54,"summary":"Under common law analysis, Waiter poisoned Playboy’s food intending serious bodily harm but not death.","trigger":"Intent to cause grievous bodily harm.","rule":"At common law, intent to cause serious bodily harm satisfies malice aforethought for murder.","sub":"Homicide – Common Law Murder","family":"Homicide","unit":10},{"n":55,"summary":"Petty Thief burglarized a neighbor’s home with a gun. When surprised by Neighbor, the gun accidentally discharged, killing Neighbor during the burglary.","trigger":"Killing occurring during the commission of burglary.","rule":"A killing committed during an enumerated felony supports felony murder in addition to the underlying felony. For felony murder you need the elements of the underlying felony to be committed, if so the burglary merges into the felony murder rule (killing happened during the felony). If he entered into the neighbor’s house and shot him in the head and kill him, then you can get him for murder and burglary. You don’t need the felony murder rule if you killed him on purpose. Felony murder rule • Felony is still in progress, until temporary safe space • Does not apply if co-felon is killed • Must be convicted of underlying felony. If there is a defense for the underlying felony defendant cannot be convicted of felony murder. • The felony committed must be independent of the act that produced the death. For example: I beat somebody really bad with a bat. This is a battery, but that person goes to the hospital and dies 1 week latter. This is not felony murder it’s just murder. This would be intent to commit GBH, and not battery and murder, only murder","sub":"Felony Murder – Burglary","family":"Homicide","unit":17},{"n":56,"summary":"Showoff drove at extreme speeds to evade police, including through a school zone, and crashed while swerving to avoid a child, killing his passenger.","trigger":"Reckless conduct showing extreme disregard for human life.","rule":"A killing caused by reckless indifference to human life constitutes involuntary manslaughter.","sub":"Homicide – Reckless Manslaughter","family":"Homicide","unit":14},{"n":57,"summary":"After heavy drinking at a bar, a man stole a truck and drove 75 mph in a 35-mph zone. He struck and killed a pedestrian. The prosecutor charged him with murder, and the issue is what homicide offense fits these facts.","trigger":"Unintentional killing caused by extremely dangerous driving while intoxicated—focus on gross/criminal negligence supporting involuntary manslaughter (lesser included of murder).","rule":"Involuntary manslaughter is a type of homicide that is a lesser offense to a murder charge. It requires that a party acted grossly or criminally negligent, resulting in a death.","sub":"Homicide – Involuntary Manslaughter","family":"Homicide","unit":13},{"n":58,"summary":"On a winter night, a homeowner’s furnace failed. A man called a heating repair company for emergency service. The company owner arrived, falsely claimed he needed to enter to inspect and fix the furnace, and once inside stole items from the home. The issue is whether entry by fraud satisfies burglary’s ‘breaking’ element.","trigger":"Entry obtained by deception/fraud can count as a constructive breaking for burglary, even without forced entry.","rule":"Using fraud to gain entry will be considered a constructive breaking, satisfying the breaking element of a burglary.","sub":"Burglary – Constructive Breaking","family":"Theft","unit":20},{"n":59,"summary":"In criminal law class, a student throws a battery intending to hit the professor; he misses and the professor is unaware until later from a video; prosecutor charges criminal assault.","trigger":"Attempted battery theory of criminal assault (intent to commit battery + substantial step) can apply even if the victim is unaware.","rule":"In criminal law, an unsuccessful battery attempt is one way of finding an assault, regardless of if the victim was aware of the attempt.","sub":"Crimes Against the Person – Assault (attempted battery; victim awareness not required)","family":"Crimes Against the Person","unit":23},{"n":60,"summary":"Driver speeds past stopped traffic and a school bus with flashing lights, hits a child boarding the bus; child survives after hospitalization; state prosecutes and asks what is the most serious possible conviction under these facts.","trigger":"Attempt charge requires specific intent; many reckless/negligent crimes cannot be “attempted.”","rule":"An attempted crime requires the mens rea of specific intent to commit that crime.","sub":"Inchoate Offenses – Attempt (specific intent requirement)","family":"Inchoate Offenses","unit":4},{"n":61,"summary":"Brother, sister, and cousin agree to rob a bank (no weapons, cousin as lookout/getaway); cousin drives away at the start; inside, security guard tackles brother and gun discharges killing a teller; question asks what crimes cousin can be convicted of (felony murder/conspiracy/robbery).","trigger":"Conspiracy is a separate inchoate offense that remains punishable even if the target offense is completed.","rule":"Conspiracy does not merge with a completed crime at common law.","sub":"Conspiracy – No merger with completed offense (common law)","family":"Inchoate Offenses","unit":6},{"n":62,"summary":"Brother, sister, and cousin agree to rob a bank with no weapons; cousin is lookout/getaway driver but has a change of heart and drives away as soon as the others enter. Inside, a security guard’s gun discharges during a struggle and kills a teller. All are charged with felony murder; issue is cousin’s liability.","trigger":"Co-conspirator liability applies to foreseeable crimes in furtherance of the conspiracy unless the conspirator effectively withdraws by notifying co-conspirators.","rule":"A co-conspirator is liable for any crimes committed by other co-conspirators that occur in furtherance of the conspiracy and within the foreseeable scope of the conspiracy unless they have withdrawn from the conspiracy by giving notice to other co-conspirators when withdrawing and before","sub":"Conspiracy – Co-conspirator liability; withdrawal by notice","family":"Conspiracy","unit":7},{"n":63,"summary":"After a gang leader announces a party to celebrate a member’s release, he decides the released member is an informant and stabs him to death in front of the other eight gang members. The others watch and do nothing; all eight are convicted as accomplices and appeal.","trigger":"Accomplice liability requires intent to assist and actual aid/encouragement; mere presence and failure to act is insufficient.","rule":"Mere presence (and failure to act) is insufficient for accomplice liability; the accomplice must intend the crime and aid/encourage it.","sub":"Parties to Crime – Accomplice liability (mere presence insufficient)","family":"Parties to Crime","unit":8},{"n":64,"summary":"Gravely ill patient needs a heart transplant and is scheduled to be flown to another hospital. Nephew poisons him, causing a reaction that delays travel; the plane later crashes killing all aboard. By the next day, the poison’s effects weaken the patient’s heart and he dies of a heart attack. Nephew is charged with homicide.","trigger":"Homicide causation: defendant liable if his act is a but-for/substantial factor contributing to death; preexisting illness or other possible causes do not relieve liability.","rule":"A defendant is guilty of homicide if his act is a but-for/substantial factor contributing to death; preexisting illness or other possible causes do not relieve liability.","sub":"Homicide – Causation (but-for/substantial factor)","family":"Causation","unit":3},{"n":65,"summary":"During a heated argument, a defendant intentionally shoots and kills another person. The facts show anger/annoyance, but nothing like sudden discovery of adultery or serious assault that would qualify as legally adequate provocation. Issue is whether the killing is murder or voluntary manslaughter.","trigger":"Intentional killing with 'malice' where claimed provocation is only anger/annoyance and does not meet the legal standard for adequate provocation.","rule":"Common law murder requires malice (intent to kill, intent to cause great bodily harm, depraved-heart reckless indifference, or felony murder); mere anger/annoyance is not adequate provocation, so an intentional killing is murder rather than voluntary manslaughter.","sub":"Homicide – Murder vs voluntary manslaughter (malice; inadequate provocation)","family":"Homicide","unit":12},{"n":66,"summary":"A person plans a burglary and directs someone else to do the breaking and entering. The person who actually enters may not share the same felonious intent, but the planner intends that a felony be committed inside.","trigger":"Burglary liability attaches to one who causes/uses another to do the breaking and entering with intent to commit a felony inside, even if the actor lacks that intent.","rule":"Burglary requires breaking + entering + structure + intent to commit a felony therein; the person who directs/uses another to do the breaking/entering with felonious intent can be liable, even if the actor lacks intent.","sub":"Property Crimes – Burglary (breaking/entering by agent; intent element)","family":"Property Offenses","unit":24},{"n":67,"summary":"Defendant attempts to commit a crime (often framed as 'assault' via attempted battery) but lacks the specific intent to cause the prohibited result. The question tests whether an attempt-based offense can exist without intent.","trigger":"Attempt crimes require specific intent; attempted-battery assault requires intent to batter, so no intent means no assault.","rule":"Attempt crimes require specific intent—so “assault” defined as attempted battery requires intent to batter; no intent → no assault, even if a battery would have been strict-liability/low mens rea.","sub":"Inchoate – Attempt (specific intent) / Assault as attempted battery","family":"Crimes Against the Person","unit":23},{"n":68,"summary":"A very drunk man leaves a bar and passes out asleep in a gutter near an alley. Another patron follows him, reaches into the drunk man’s pocket, takes his wallet, and starts walking away; when he hears police officers approaching he drops the wallet and runs off. The question asks for the most serious crime the patron can be convicted of (crimes listed in descending order).","trigger":"Taking property from a passed-out/unaware person occurs without force or threats; robbery requires force/threat.","rule":"Robbery = larceny + force/threat; taking property from an unaware/passed-out victim without force is larceny, not robbery.","sub":"Property Crimes – Robbery vs larceny (force/threat)","family":"Theft","unit":18},{"n":69,"summary":"A defendant becomes intoxicated, stops his car in the street, breaks a liquor store window, and enters intending to take liquor; when a night watchman arrives, the defendant strikes him with a bottle, killing him, then drives off at high speed. The jurisdiction’s statutes define burglary to include breaking and entering a building with intent to commit a crime inside, murder includes killing during burglary, and intoxication is not a defense unless it negates an element. At the defendant’s murder trial, the issue is whether intoxication negates the burglary intent and therefore defeats felony murder based on burglary.","trigger":"Voluntary intoxication negates the specific intent required for the underlying felony (burglary intent-to-commit-crime-inside), defeating felony murder based on that felony.","rule":"Voluntary intoxication is a defense only if it negates an element; if it negates intent for the underlying felony (e.g., burglary intent-to-commit-crime-inside), it also defeats felony murder based on that felony.","sub":"Defenses – Voluntary intoxication; felony murder predicate","family":"Homicide","unit":17},{"n":70,"summary":"In a jurisdiction that follows a “retreat” doctrine, a defendant charged with assault and battery pleads self-defense. At a tavern, a husband approaches the defendant and threatens him (“Get out of here, or I’ll break your nose”) and raises his fists menacingly; the defendant warns him not to come closer and then uses pepper spray on the husband, causing the husband to fall in pain. The issue is whether the defendant had to retreat before using this non-deadly force.","trigger":"Self-defense uses non-deadly force; retreat is not required before using non-deadly force (retreat doctrine applies to deadly force).","rule":"The retreat doctrine applies only before using deadly force; there is no duty to retreat before using non-deadly force (e.g., pepper spray).","sub":"Self-Defense – Retreat (deadly vs non-deadly force)","family":"Defenses","unit":26},{"n":71,"summary":"Driving down a dark road, a defendant accidentally runs over a man and finds the victim is dead. Fearing he may be held responsible, the defendant takes the victim’s wallet containing a substantial amount of money, removes the identification papers, and then puts the wallet and money back into the victim’s pocket. The charge turns on whether the taking from a dead/unaware victim without force constitutes robbery or larceny.","trigger":"Taking from a dead/unaware victim without force/threat is larceny, not robbery.","rule":"Robbery requires force or threat of force; taking property from a dead/unaware victim is larceny, not robbery, because there’s no force/putting in fear.","sub":"Property Crimes – Robbery vs larceny (unaware/dead victim)","family":"Theft","unit":18},{"n":72,"summary":"Attempt liability question: defendant believed state law made it a crime to buy codeine without a prescription and tried to do so by purchasing cough syrup containing codeine. Unknown to him, the statute had been repealed and buying codeine without a prescription was no longer illegal.","trigger":"Attempt charge where completed conduct would not be criminal because the statute was repealed (legal impossibility).","rule":"Legal impossibility is a defense to attempt—if the completed act would not be a crime (because the statute was repealed / conduct is legal), the defendant is not guilty of attempt even if he believed it was illegal.","sub":"Inchoate – Attempt; legal impossibility defense","family":"Inchoate Offenses","unit":5},{"n":73,"summary":"Defendant decides to kill his neighbor, goes to neighbor’s house, and sees the neighbor’s brother who resembles the neighbor. Thinking the brother is the neighbor, defendant shoots at him; the shot misses and wounds a bystander some distance away whom defendant did not see. Issue is who is the intended victim for attempt‑murder purposes.","trigger":"Attempted murder requires specific intent to kill; mistaken identity/transfer does not change intent analysis.","rule":"Attempted murder requires intent to kill the person the defendant meant to kill; mistake of identity doesn’t change the intended victim, and transferred intent doesn’t apply to attempt.","sub":"Inchoate – Attempted murder; intent","family":"Mens Rea","unit":9},{"n":74,"summary":"Woman and friend plan a bank robbery: woman offers to pay the friend one‑third of the stolen proceeds if he will drive the getaway car; friend agrees after woman promises not to hurt anyone. Woman buys a handgun from a sporting‑goods store owner after telling him she needs it for a bank robbery; owner sells it at four times the normal price. During the robbery, the gun accidentally discharges and kills a customer; issue is who besides the woman can be convicted of murder at common law.","trigger":"Felony murder + accomplice liability: who is liable for a killing during a robbery (getaway driver, supplier/merchant).","rule":"Under felony murder, all accomplices to the underlying felony are liable for killings during its commission; a supplier can be an accomplice where the facts show intent to facilitate (e.g., “stake in the venture”).","sub":"Homicide – Felony murder; accomplice liability","family":"Parties to Crime","unit":8},{"n":75,"summary":"After a breakup, a man is moving out and tries to take a painting; his ex says it was a Valentine’s Day gift and threatens to call police. He leaves it behind, but later—knowing she is out of town—he sneaks into her house at night using a copied key, takes the painting, leaves the key inside, and locks up. He is charged with burglary for entering to commit a felony.","trigger":"Burglary requires intent to commit a felony at entry; a good‑faith claim of right/disputed ownership can negate larceny intent and thus the felony element.","rule":"Taking something that you dispute ownership in may not be considered larceny, therefore it may not satisfy the felony requirement of burglary. Remember key with no permission is constructive entry just as forcing the entering of the dwelling.","sub":"Burglary – Intent to commit felony; claim of right / disputed ownership","family":"Property Offenses","unit":24},{"n":76,"summary":"A man walked into a bank and gave the bank teller a note that said, “Do not cause a fuss. I have a partner who has a bomb and will detonate it inside this bank if you do not give me $10,000 in unmarked bills right now.” The teller, afraid to look around, did as the man instructed. The man then left the bank. The man had lied about having a partner there and was completely unarmed. The man was caught and criminally tried. Which of the following crimes can the man be convicted of?","trigger":"Multiple offenses charged; determine convictions after merger of lesser‑included offenses.","rule":"When the question asks for which crimes a person can be convicted, make sure to merge lesser offense for Double Jeopardy purposes.","sub":"Double Jeopardy – Merger","family":"Inchoate Offenses","unit":6},{"n":77,"summary":"A defendant was charged with attempted theft from an automated teller machine (ATM). The prosecution alleges that the defendant was in possession of someone else’s ATM card and was attempting to withdraw money. The defendant was not successful because the personal identification number (PIN) he entered was incorrect each time. The ATM machine has a security feature that locks a card and does not dispense it back to a customer if three unsuccessful attempts are made at entering the PIN number. The defendant was located by using the bank’s cameras and records.","trigger":"Attempt charge; requires specific intent to commit the target offense.","rule":"Attempted crimes require the specific intent to commit the crime.","sub":"Attempt – Specific intent","family":"Inchoate Offenses","unit":4},{"n":78,"summary":"A father conditions permission to attend a rock concert on his son painting the house; the son refuses. An hour before the concert, the son points a toy gun at his father in the father’s car and demands a ride to the concert. The father could have jumped out but, believing his life was in danger, agrees and drives; he is later stopped by police for erratic driving. The son is charged with kidnapping.","trigger":"Kidnapping by restraint/asportation through threat of deadly force; whether an apparent weapon (toy gun) can supply the required threat if the victim reasonably fears harm.","rule":"Kidnapping requires unlawful restraint (often plus asportation) by force or threat; a toy gun can satisfy the threat element if it causes the victim to reasonably fear imminent serious harm and the victim’s liberty is restrained.","sub":"Kidnapping – Restraint/asportation; threat","family":"Arson and Kidnapping","unit":25},{"n":79,"summary":"Before a homecoming dance, a girl lets her friend borrow the girl’s earrings (and a dress) for the dance, with the friend intending to return them afterward. While at the dance, after receiving compliments, the friend decides she will never return the earrings and keeps them.","trigger":"Property obtained lawfully by borrowing/entrustment and later fraudulently converted; distinguish larceny (trespassory taking) from embezzlement (conversion by one in lawful possession).","rule":"Embezzlement occurs when someone in lawful possession of another’s property later fraudulently converts it; forming the intent to steal after lawful possession attaches supports embezzlement, not larceny. Continuing trespass applies ONLY when: Initial taking was wrongful / trespassory, AND Intent to steal forms later. Example: You grab someone’s backpack “just to move it,” then later decide to keep it. Difference between both:Larceny by Continuing trespass: ❌ wrongful possession at start → ✔ intent later Embezzlement: ✔ lawful possession at start → ❌ intent later","sub":"Theft Offenses – Embezzlement vs larceny","family":"Theft","unit":18},{"n":80,"summary":"A woman drinking at a bar (two rum‑and‑cokes over two hours) hears an elderly man—quite drunk on whiskey—yell to the crowded barroom: “In my day, any woman who drank hard liquor and talked to men in a bar was considered a slut!” She replies: “Times have changed, old man, and anyone who thinks differently will die.” He responds: “Thanks for proving my point, slut.” The woman then pulls a .32‑caliber revolver from her purse, aims at his nose, and shoots him between the eyes; he dies instantly.","trigger":"Intentional killing with a firearm after a verbal exchange; analyze deliberation/premeditation versus heat of passion.","rule":"Premeditation and deliberation can be formed in an instant; intentionally killing with a deadly weapon after reflection—however brief—supports premeditated murder rather than voluntary manslaughter.","sub":"Homicide – Premeditated murder","family":"Homicide","unit":11},{"n":81,"summary":"After a girls’ soccer championship, a parent insults a referee. The referee shoves the parent, who falls, hits his head, and begins bleeding. The parent is a hemophiliac, continues bleeding, and dies from blood loss shortly thereafter. The referee is charged with involuntary manslaughter.","trigger":"Defendant commits an unlawful battery that causes death; victim has an unusual frailty that makes death more likely.","rule":"A defendant takes the victim as found; if the defendant’s unlawful act causes the victim’s death, the defendant is liable even if an unusual condition makes the harm greater than foreseeable. Involuntary manslaughter (IM) is the “unintentional killing + criminal negligence or an unlawful act” bucket—and here the death results from the referee’s unlawful battery (shoving the parent). IM (unlawful act): “I meant to hit/shove him (minor harm/offense), but he died.”","sub":"Homicide – Involuntary manslaughter; causation/eggshell victim","family":"Causation","unit":3},{"n":82,"summary":"Two robbers commit a store robbery: one robs inside while the second waits outside in a getaway car. A customer tries to shoot the inside robber but misses and accidentally kills the cashier standing next to the robber. Police arrest the second robber outside and charge him with the cashier’s murder. The jurisdiction follows the majority view.","trigger":"Felony-murder where the killing is committed by a non-felon (customer/police) rather than a co-felon; apply majority agency rule.","rule":"Under the majority (agency) rule, felony-murder liability generally does not attach when the killing is committed by a non-felon rather than by a co-felon. The other theory is the proximate cause, so if you started this you are also guilty. If a cofelon kill’s someone during the commission of the felony the other cofelon would also be responsible","sub":"Felony Murder – Agency rule","family":"Homicide","unit":17},{"n":83,"summary":"A girl and her friend go to a nightclub planning to start a fist fight with the first person who gives them attitude. When a man tells the girl “stop pushing me,” the girl smiles and gives her friend a thumbs-up, prompting the friend to punch and kick the man. The question asks whether the girl can be liable as an accomplice.","trigger":"Encouragement/signaling that prompts the principal’s attack plus shared intent to engage in the crime.","rule":"Accomplice liability requires intent to aid/encourage the crime and an act of assistance or encouragement; a signal that prompts the assault can satisfy the act requirement.","sub":"Parties – Accomplice liability","family":"Parties to Crime","unit":8},{"n":84,"summary":"Executive hires a securities lawyer to prepare SEC filings. Lawyer drafts reports containing material misstatements and knows they are false but doesn’t tell executive. Executive signs and submits the reports and is prosecuted for willfully making false statements to the SEC.","trigger":"Advice-of-counsel defense to “willful” false-statement crime.","rule":"Advice of counsel may negate willfulness only when the defendant fully discloses material facts and reasonably relies in good faith; signing and submitting false filings can still be willful even if counsel drafted them.","sub":"Defenses – Advice of counsel / willfulness","family":"Defenses","unit":28},{"n":85,"summary":"Clerk asks to borrow a friend’s car (friend agrees based on past safe borrowing). Next day clerk robs a bank at gunpoint and kills the security guard. Question asks liability of clerk and friend.","trigger":"Accomplice liability and felony murder when friend merely lends car without knowledge of plan.","rule":"A person is an accomplice only if they intend to aid the crime; mere lending of property without knowledge/intent is not enough. The robber is guilty of felony murder for a killing during an inherently dangerous felony.","sub":"Parties & Felony Murder – Accomplice intent","family":"Parties to Crime","unit":8},{"n":86,"summary":"A business owner suspects an employee has been stealing computer equipment. One night, when the employee and her family are away, the owner goes to the employee’s house to look for and retrieve the equipment. He brings a picklock, but the back door is unlocked so he enters, searches, hears noises outside, leaves, and is arrested by police on patrol. He is charged with burglary.","trigger":"Burglary charge where defendant entered a dwelling at night but claims he lacked intent to commit a crime inside because he was retrieving his own property.","rule":"Burglary requires intent to commit a felony at entry; entering only to reclaim own property lacks that intent.","sub":"Burglary – Intent at entry","family":"Property Offenses","unit":24},{"n":87,"summary":"A man comes home early and finds his wife engaged in sexual intercourse with a neighbor. He grabs a gun from a dresser drawer and shoots and kills the neighbor. He is charged. The jurisdiction follows common-law homicide offenses and the question is what homicide offenses the jury should be instructed on.","trigger":"Intentional killing immediately after discovering spouse’s adultery; analyze adequate provocation and heat of passion instructions.","rule":"Adequate provocation supports instructing on murder and voluntary manslaughter.","sub":"Homicide – Murder vs voluntary manslaughter","family":"Homicide","unit":12},{"n":88,"summary":"Man attempts armed robbery; gun accidentally fires killing manager; later fires shot to scare clerk who dies of fright-induced heart attack.","trigger":"Felony murder during robbery/attempt; deaths of non-cofelons during commission; foreseeability not required.","rule":"A death caused during the commission or immediate flight of an inherently dangerous felony (e.g., robbery) supports felony murder for any non-cofelon victim.","sub":"Homicide – Felony Murder","family":"Homicide","unit":16},{"n":89,"summary":"Man points loaded handgun with finger on trigger at friend to demand return of debit card; bystander throws bottle, striking man’s hand and causing gun to discharge and kill friend; man convicted of murder under common law.","trigger":"Pointing a loaded gun at someone is reckless disregard of high risk of death; common law malice (depraved heart).","rule":"Recklessly creating a high risk of death with conscious disregard supports common-law murder as depraved-heart (malice) murder, even without intent to kill.","sub":"Homicide – Depraved Heart Murder (Common Law Malice)","family":"Homicide","unit":14},{"n":90,"summary":"On subway, man grabs woman’s purse from seat next to her and immediately pushes her aside to flee; issue: larceny vs robbery (common law).","trigger":"Force used during taking/escape as part of the same transaction.","rule":"Robbery is larceny from the person/presence by force or threat of force; force used to accomplish the taking or immediate escape satisfies robbery (timing/concurrence). GRAB+PUSH+RUN: ROBBERY. IF I TAKE IT AND RUN AND DONT USE FORCE ITS LARCENY. FOR ROBBERY YOU HAVE TO USE FORCE AT THE TIME YOU TAKE THE PROPERTY. Robbery is Larceny + 2: Trespassery taking carrying away of the personal property of another with the intent to permanently deprive from the victim's person or their presence and you do it with force or threat of force (CONCURRENCE+MENS REA+ACTUS REA)","sub":"Robbery — Force/Timing (Concurrence)","family":"Theft","unit":21},{"n":91,"summary":"A man was paroled after serving five years in prison for forgery. Three weeks later, he found a handgun in a high school parking lot. Fearing that students might find it and get into trouble, he put it in the trunk of his car. He was lawfully stopped for speeding and consented to a search of his car and trunk, where the officer discovered the gun. The man was charged under a federal statute prohibiting the knowing possession of a firearm by a convicted felon.","trigger":"Felon knowingly possesses firearm briefly to dispose of it; looking for the best defense fact.","rule":"For a knowing-possession offense, intent to use the firearm unlawfully is irrelevant; a helpful defense fact is lawful/innocent possession aimed at promptly turning the firearm over to authorities (e.g., en route to police).","sub":"Possession Offenses / Mens Rea (Knowing Possession) / Innocent Possession","family":"Mens Rea","unit":1},{"n":92,"summary":"A man and a woman were partners in an automobile leasing agency. The man had been embezzling money, and when the woman became suspicious and wanted an audit, the man panicked and decided to have her killed. He contacted a professional killer and hired him to kill the woman for $10,000. A short time later, the woman died in a car crash arranged by the killer. The question asks, in addition to embezzlement, what crime or crimes the man may be properly convicted of.","trigger":"Hiring a killer: agreement to kill + killing occurs; solicitation vs conspiracy vs murder; merger rules.","rule":"Solicitation merges into conspiracy when the solicited party agrees; conspiracy does not merge into the completed crime at common law, so a conspirator may be convicted of both conspiracy to commit murder and murder when the killing occurs. Here, the man solicited (encoraged anothe to commit a crime) the professional killer to kill the woman; when he accepted it's no longer solicitation, now it's conspiracy to commit murder. If the professional killer would have said NO, the man would only have commited solicitation. SOLICITATION MERGES INTO CONSPIRACY.","sub":"Inchoate Crimes / Solicitation & Conspiracy / Merger / Pinkerton Liability","family":"Inchoate Offenses","unit":6},{"n":93,"summary":"A man and a woman agreed that the woman would rob a bank and the man would steal a car beforehand for the woman to use as a getaway vehicle. The man stole a car and parked it two blocks from the bank. He left the car key under the floor mat with a note saying he wanted nothing more to do with the scheme. The next day, the woman robbed the bank, ran to the spot where the man had left the car, escaped, and disappeared; she never shared money with the man. The jurisdiction requires bilateral conspiracy. The question asks whether the man can be convicted of conspiring with the woman to rob the bank.","trigger":"Agreement + overt act (stealing/placing getaway car) + attempted 'withdrawal' not communicated.","rule":"Conspiracy requires an agreement (and often an overt act); withdrawal requires communicating withdrawal to co-conspirators—mere abandonment without notice does not withdraw (though withdrawal may limit liability for later substantive crimes).","sub":"Conspiracy / Withdrawal / Overt Act / Bilateral Conspiracy","family":"Conspiracy","unit":7},{"n":94,"summary":"Multiple people are involved in a planned crime. The question tests when a defendant can be liable both as an accomplice and as a conspirator, focusing on whether the defendant had the required specific intent to commit the crime and whether the defendant did some act in furtherance of the criminal plan.","trigger":"Accomplice/conspiracy liability question testing specific intent + act in furtherance.","rule":"For both accomplice liability and conspiracy liability, the defendant must have the specific intent to commit the crime (mens rea) and perform some type of act in furtherance of the crime.","sub":"Criminal Liability — Accomplice & Conspiracy (Mens Rea + Act)","family":"Parties to Crime","unit":8},{"n":95,"summary":"A defendant commits an offense after voluntarily becoming intoxicated. The question tests whether voluntary intoxication can negate the mens rea element and when it is available as a defense, depending on whether the charged offense requires specific intent.","trigger":"Voluntary intoxication offered to negate mens rea; specific-intent limitation.","rule":"Voluntary intoxication will only be a defense to a specific intent crime if it prevents the defendant from being able to form the mens rea required for the crime.","sub":"Defenses — Voluntary Intoxication","family":"Mens Rea","unit":1}]}],"mee":{"slug":"mee","name":"Business Associations","layer":"essay","bigPicture":"BIG PICTURE — STRUCTURAL MISALIGNMENT IN BUSINESS ASSOCIATIONS\n\nWHERE YOU START. You were trained to see a company as a contract. The sociedad is constituted by agreement, acquires personality and its limitation of liability through registration, and is governed by administradores whose powers are defined in an instrument and opposable to third parties once published. A partner who acts is exercising a mandato, and the question about anything he did is whether he stayed inside the powers conferred. Duties of fidelity exist, their breach produces an obligation to repair damage, and the action against administrators is resolved by the assembly. In a sociedad en nombre colectivo the partners answer personally, unlimitedly and solidariamente, and solidarity means the creditor chooses whom to sue. Nobody may be compelled to remain a partner, and a withdrawal from a company of indefinite duration dissolves it.\n\nWHAT THE U.S. SYSTEM IS. Entity forms are DEFAULTS, not elections: carrying on a business as co-owners for profit creates a general partnership whether or not anyone intended one, and the protective forms exist only on filing. Every partner is a general AGENT of the firm, with power to bind it for anything within the ordinary course — measured by what the firm habitually does, not by the gravity of the act. Fiduciary duties are enforced by EQUITY, which reaches the asset rather than its price. The claim for a wrong to the entity belongs to the entity and is pursued DERIVATIVELY by an owner against those in control. A partner's departure is DISSOCIATION, an event that operates on the PARTNER and not automatically on the firm: whether the business ends depends on the form of the partnership and on whether the departure was rightful.\n\nWHAT DIVERGES. Three trained habits produce the wrong answer. First, trusting the instrument: you look for the limit written in the poder or the charter, when U.S. law asks what the principal manifested to the third party and disregards an internal limit nobody was told about. Second, fusing departure with dissolution — treating every withdrawal as the end of the firm — when U.S. law separates the two events and lets the outcome turn on the partnership's form and on the regularity of the exit; the habit makes the correct answer unthinkable rather than merely unknown. Third, asking for damages: you can prove every breach and still lose the building, because the remedy that recovers it is a constructive trust and your system has no such thing.\n\nWHAT IS THE SAME. Your instincts are sound in more places than the diagnosis suggests. The partner who deals with the firm on both sides is in conflict of interest, and the duty of loyalty is familiar ground. An act of disposal unconnected to the company's object exceeds ordinary administration and needs everyone's agreement. Culpa in eligendo is real: engaging someone you knew to be unfit is your own fault. Unregistered ventures leave their participants exposed. And the retiring partner is not released from obligations contracted while he was a partner. Where these converge, the failure is usually one of essay FORM — knowing the rule and not writing it — rather than of law.","families":[{"name":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","count":39},{"name":"Entity Formation & Defaults","count":15},{"name":"Partner Liability & Collection","count":14},{"name":"Dissociation & Winding Up","count":13},{"name":"Fiduciary Duties","count":12},{"name":"Agency – Vicarious Liability","count":9},{"name":"Partnership Property & Interests","count":9},{"name":"Shareholder & Derivative Litigation","count":7},{"name":"Mergers & Appraisal","count":6},{"name":"Directors' Conflicting Interests","count":5},{"name":"Choice of Law & Entity Purpose","count":4},{"name":"Outcome Matrix","count":3},{"name":"Veil Piercing","count":3},{"name":"Equitable Remedies","count":2}],"scenarios":[{"id":"MEE-026","issueCount":3,"chain":"Three INDEPENDENT theories, not a chain: (1) that parent and subsidiary are partners, so the parent is liable as a partner; (2) that the parent is bound as principal because its own manager signed; (3) that the subsidiary's separate existence should be disregarded. Each fails for a different reason and each must be answered on its own terms; resolving one says nothing about the others."},{"id":"MEE-027","issueCount":5,"chain":"One factual configuration tested three times over, under three different relationships, each with its own liability consequence. (1) Were they PARTNERS — does profit-sharing as compensation establish co-ownership and shared control? → (2) if partners, joint and several liability for a partner's tort in the ordinary course. (3) Was he an EMPLOYEE — does the principal control the manner and means? → (4) if an employee, respondeat superior within the scope of employment. (5) If neither — a non-employee agent — no vicarious liability absent special circumstances. Items 2, 4 and 5 are alternatives, not a sequence: each is reached only on its own characterisation, and the answer must work all three rather than choose one."},{"id":"MEE-028","issueCount":5,"chain":"Three independent questions sharing one set of facts. FORMATION: (1) is the person who shares profits, shares losses and manages a PARTNER → (2) is the person who receives the identical profit share as loan repayment a partner? The two are deliberately paired: the same economic indicator, opposite results. TRANSFER: (3) what can a partner assign — the transferable interest in distributions, or partner status itself → (4) does the transferee acquire management and inspection rights? Item 4 is decided entirely by item 3 and is where the point is won or lost. PROPERTY: (5) may a partner use property titled in the partnership for personal purposes? Independent of the other four."},{"id":"MEE-029","issueCount":4,"chain":"A single timeline, with liability turning on which side of each date the obligation falls. (1) Are partners personally liable, jointly and severally, for an obligation arising from a partner's wrongful act in the ordinary course? → (2) is the partner admitted AFTER that obligation arose personally liable for it? → (3) what does converting to an LLP require, and does the filing reach obligations that already existed? → (4) what is the liability position for a claim arising AFTER the election? Each item is the same question asked at a different moment, and the answer to each is determined by sequence rather than by conduct. Items 3 and 4 are a matched pair: the filing is prospective only, so the earlier claim survives it and the later one does not."},{"id":"MEE-043","issueCount":3,"chain":"A chain of escalating remedies: (1) the right to inspect books and records, which turns on proper purpose → (2) if suit follows, whether the board's refusal of the demand insulates it from a derivative action → (3) whether conscious disregard of illegality is protected by the business judgment rule at all. Item 2 presupposes that the shareholder has the information item 1 provides; item 3 is what defeats the board's defence in item 2."},{"id":"MEE-053","issueCount":7,"chain":"Five steps, each conditioned on the last: (1) do controlling members owe fiduciary duties at all → (2) does blocking the company's claim breach loyalty → (3) is a derivative action available after refusal → (4) is the minority's DIRECT action available instead → (5) is dissolution available for oppression. Two further items address whether the loss can be shifted to the controller personally: limited liability, and the absence of grounds to disregard it."},{"id":"MEE-066","issueCount":7,"chain":"One chain, strictly ordered, then a change of subject. THE TRANSACTION: (1) corporate powers are exercised by the board and approved by majority vote at a meeting with quorum → (2) whether the vote is a director's conflicting-interest transaction → (3) whether the duty of loyalty is breached absent disinterested approval → (4) who bears the burden once no qualified approval exists → (5) whether the transaction can still be upheld as substantively fair. THE REMEDY: (6) whether the harm is the corporation's, making the action derivative → (7) whether the plaintiff owned shares at the time of the conduct challenged. Item 4 is unreachable without resolving item 3, and item 5 exists only because item 4 shifted the burden; items 6 and 7 are independent of the merits and dispose of part of the claim regardless of how the transaction is judged."},{"id":"MEE-076","issueCount":10,"chain":"Two parallel chains. CHAIN A — the transaction: (1) merger approval requirements and which state's law supplies them → (2) power of the majority to approve → (3) whether the minority can block → (4) once blocking fails, entitlement to appraisal → (5) availability of appraisal and the market-out exception → (6) the procedural prerequisites to preserve it. CHAIN B — the duties: (7) internal affairs doctrine → (8) which state's law governs after the merger → (9) what the surviving state's benefit-corporation law permits → (10) the statutory liability shield. Item 4 is unlocked only by resolving item 3 against the minority; items 9 and 10 are unreachable without first resolving item 8."},{"id":"MEE-081","issueCount":12,"chain":"Every one of the twelve items is the same two questions asked again: WAS THERE AUTHORITY, and WAS THE PRINCIPAL DISCLOSED. (1–2) establish the framework and the three disclosure states. CHIPS: (3) actual authority exceeded → (4) whether apparent authority can fill the gap → (5) if neither, who answers to the third party. LENSES: (6) an undisclosed principal is bound by an authorised contract → (7) the principal's later regret is irrelevant → (8) the agent who signed in her own name is also a party. SWITCHES: (9) no authority, but the principal accepts the benefit knowing the facts → (10) ratification relates back → (11) an unidentified principal leaves the agent liable → (12) ratification does not release her. The three transactions are independent of each other and must each be worked separately; within each, the items are strictly sequential."},{"id":"MEE-090","issueCount":10,"chain":"One timeline, split into two branches by a single formal fact, then rejoined. WRONGFUL BRANCH: (1) may he leave at all, and is leaving in breach of the agreement wrongful? → (2) what does wrongfulness cost him? → (3) does his departure force a winding up? → (4) what may the others do instead? → (5) may he take part in a winding up if one occurs? RIGHTFUL BRANCH, the counterfactual: (6) with proper notice, is the dissociation still wrongful? → (7) what does it now trigger? → (8) what is his role in it? BOTH BRANCHES REJOIN: (9) what is he liable for after he leaves, and (10) on what basis. Items 3 and 7 are the same question answered oppositely, and the whole scenario exists to make that contrast visible."},{"id":"MEE-092","issueCount":10,"chain":"Three questions in strict order, because each is unanswerable until the one before it is settled. WHAT IS THIS BUSINESS: (1) does conduct alone form a general partnership? → (2) does the unsigned, unfiled LLC paperwork change that? AUTHORITY: (3) may one partner bind the firm alone? → (4) was this purchase within the ordinary course? → (5) does another partner's unspoken objection defeat the authority? DEPARTURE: (6) what makes a dissociation effective? → (7) is it wrongful in a partnership with no fixed term? → (8) does it dissolve the firm? → (9) what is she owed? → (10) what must she do to start the clock on payment. Item 1 governs everything: if the entity is misidentified, the remaining nine items are answered under the wrong statute."},{"id":"MEE-096","issueCount":11,"chain":"Two contracts of opposite character, with the firm's internal relations sitting between them. THE TRUCK: (1) is a partner an agent of the firm? → (2) is this purchase in the ordinary course? → (3) does the dealership's belief rest on the firm's own conduct? → (4) do the partners' internal objections reach the dealership? INTERNAL RELATIONS, independent of both contracts: (5) must the firm repay his deposit? → (6) who answers for the unpaid balance? → (7) is he owed anything for the extra hours? THE LAND: (8) is this act in the ordinary course at all? → (9) what did it therefore require? → (10) can apparent authority save it given what the developer knew? → (11) is the firm bound? Items 1–4 and 8–11 are the same four questions asked of two transactions, and they come out opposite ways."},{"id":"MEE-102","issueCount":10,"chain":"Three independent questions, the first of them a matched pair. FORMATION: (1) does the arrangement form a partnership? → (2) is the contributor who shares losses and manages a partner? → (3) is the one repaid out of profits a partner? → (5) do regular distributions change the answer? TRANSFER: (6) what may a partner assign? → (7) does the assignment remove her as partner or dissolve the firm? → (8) what is the transferee owed? → (9) may he manage or inspect? PROPERTY: (4) whose is the van? → (10) may the owner use it personally? The three questions share one set of facts and are otherwise unconnected."},{"id":"MEE-110","issueCount":11,"chain":"One relationship, two separate harms, and three routes to liability. THE CONTRACT: (2) what created any appearance of authority? → (3) is the principal bound although the driver exceeded his instructions? → (4) was the sign shop's reliance reasonable? → (5) is she liable on the contract? → (6) is the driver personally liable too? THE INJURY: (7) can her statement to the customer make her answerable for his driving? → (8) does apparent authority reach TORTS as well as contracts? → (9) does his status as an independent contractor defeat that? INDEPENDENT OF BOTH: (1) and (10) her own negligence in choosing him, which stands whether or not either of the other routes works. Item 11 asks for the resulting matrix, which is the real question: four parties, and who owes what to whom."},{"id":"MEE-116","issueCount":11,"chain":"One chain, and the defendant's whole case rests on where one event falls in it. BASELINE: (1) what duties govern absent an agreement? → (2) may he leave at will? THE OPPORTUNITY: (3) did the landlord's call impose a duty to disclose? → (5) could he take it for himself? → (10) did he breach? THE TIMING DEFENCE: (4) what did the email accomplish? → (6) what state is the firm in afterwards? → (7) may he compete during it? → (8) does dissociation end the duty of loyalty? REMEDY: (9) and (11) what follows from the breach. Item 8 is the hinge of the scenario: if his defence succeeds, items 5, 7, 9 and 10 all fall with it."},{"id":"MEE-121","issueCount":11,"chain":"The same two questions asked of three purchases, each landing on a different combination. CHAIRS: (1) the framework → (2) did she have actual authority? → (3–4) could apparent authority arise where the principal never contacted the store? → (5) who is liable, given she signed in her own name. BIKE: (6) actual authority absent → (7) did keeping it with knowledge affirm it? → (8) with what effect. OVEN: (9) did his telephone call create apparent authority? → (10) is he bound although she had no actual authority? Item 11 asks for the matrix. The three transactions are independent; within each, strictly sequential."},{"id":"MEE-127","issueCount":11,"chain":"One defect at the start, and everything follows from it. WHAT IS THIS: (1) can a limited partnership exist with no general partner who assented? → (2) what did their conduct form instead? → (3) does the later certificate cure it? → (4) so what is the entity. THE CONTRACT CLAIM: (5) are the three personally liable? → (6) what must the creditor do to reach them? → (7) when may it reach them. THE TORT CLAIM: (8) are they liable for the death? → (9) does the certificate, filed before the death, protect them? → (10) does the creditor's procedure differ for a tort? Item 11 asks for the matrix. Items 1–4 govern: misidentify the entity and the remaining seven items are answered under the wrong statute."}],"issues":[{"n":1,"scenario":"MEE-026","item":"1 of 3","summary":"Parent LLC was the sole member of Sub LLC. Parent selected Sub’s manager, shared personnel, coordinated business activities, and received all of Sub’s profits. A newspaper described them as partners. VanCo argued Parent was liable as a partner of Sub for Sub’s unpaid delivery contract.","trigger":"Alleged sharing of profits, personnel, and coordinated business operations.","rule":"A partnership exists only if parties carry on as co-owners of a business for profit; profit distributions alone in a parent-subsidiary relationship do not create a partnership. The burden to show it is a partnership is on the party alleging that it is a partnership. Intent to be a partnership is irrelevant. The intent to act as a partnership is what is relevant. Look at factors such as: i. Title to property and whose name it is in ii. How the parties name the entity by the parties iii. How much activity goes on in this entity? iv. Sharing gross returns and losses","sub":"Partnership – Existence","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":5},{"n":2,"scenario":"MEE-026","item":"2 of 3","summary":"VanCo’s delivery agreement with Sub was signed by Greta, Parent’s manager, who was visiting Sub’s office and signed the contract as “agent of Sub.” VanCo sought to bind Parent to the contract.","trigger":"Contract signed by parent’s manager while acting for subsidiary.","rule":"A principal is bound by a contract only if the agent acts with actual or apparent authority on the principal’s behalf; signing solely as agent of another entity does not bind the principal. Greta as agent of principal just signing the K in the name of another (Sub) does not bind the principal Parent","sub":"Agency – Authority to Bind Principal","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":6},{"n":3,"scenario":"MEE-026","item":"3 of 3","summary":"Sub became insolvent and unable to pay VanCo. Parent and Sub shared personnel and coordinated operations, but maintained separate finances, observed LLC formalities, and discontinued distributions when Sub became financially distressed.","trigger":"Creditor seeks to disregard LLC separateness due to control and coordination.","rule":"Courts will pierce the LLC veil only upon a showing of domination plus fraud or injustice; mere control, coordination, or profit distributions are insufficient.","sub":"LLC Veil Piercing","family":"Veil Piercing","unit":7},{"n":4,"scenario":"MEE-027","item":"1 of 5","summary":"A woman owns and operates a food-truck business and hires a man to purchase and deliver produce from a farmers’ market. She pays him $20 per delivery plus 10% of profits, but he has no role in management or control of the business.","trigger":"Sharing profits without shared control or co-ownership.","rule":"A partnership requires co-ownership and shared control; profit-sharing alone, especially as compensation, does not establish a partnership. Receipt by a person of a share of business profits is prima facia evidence that they are a partner (creates a presumption of partnership) UNLESS: i. paid as a result of a debt by installments or otherwise ii. wages to an employee or independent contractor iii. rent to a landlord iv. annuity or health benefit to representative of a dead partner v. paid as interest on a loan vi. for sale of the goodwill of a business or other property","sub":"Partnership – Existence","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":17},{"n":5,"scenario":"MEE-027","item":"2 of 5","summary":"Assuming arguendo the woman and man were partners, the man negligently drove into a farmer’s stall while purchasing produce for the food truck.","trigger":"Partner commits a tort while acting in ordinary course of partnership business.","rule":"Partners are jointly and severally liable for torts committed by a partner acting in the ordinary course of partnership business.","sub":"Partnership – Tort Liability","family":"Agency – Vicarious Liability","unit":18},{"n":6,"scenario":"MEE-027","item":"3 of 5","summary":"The man buys produce independently, selects vendors, uses his own car and credit card, and is paid per delivery, while the woman lacks control over the manner and means of his work.","trigger":"Lack of principal’s control over manner and means of work.","rule":"An employee exists only when the employer controls the manner and means of the work; absence of such control indicates no employment relationship.","sub":"Agency – Employee vs Independent Contractor","family":"Agency – Vicarious Liability","unit":19},{"n":7,"scenario":"MEE-027","item":"4 of 5","summary":"If the man were deemed an employee, his negligent driving into the farmer’s stall occurred while he was buying produce for the food truck.","trigger":"Employee commits tort within scope of employment.","rule":"An employer is vicariously liable for torts committed by an employee acting within the scope of employment.","sub":"Agency – Respondeat Superior","family":"Agency – Vicarious Liability","unit":19},{"n":8,"scenario":"MEE-027","item":"5 of 5","summary":"The man acted as a non-employee agent purchasing produce for the woman but was not subject to her control over how he performed the task.","trigger":"Agent acts without principal’s control over manner and means.","rule":"A principal is generally not vicariously liable for the negligence of an independent contractor absent special circumstances or authorization.","sub":"Agency – Independent Contractor Liability","family":"Agency – Vicarious Liability","unit":19},{"n":9,"scenario":"MEE-028","item":"1 of 5","summary":"Wendy opened Kibble as a sole proprietorship and later accepted money from Mary, who was promised 15% of profits and losses and began working in the business and participating in planning, while Angelo contributed money only as a loan to be repaid from profits.","trigger":"Sharing of profits and losses plus participation in management.","rule":"A partnership is an association of two of more people as co-owners on a business for profit. Te intent of the partners to be a partnership is not enough, the intent to act as one is. There is no need to to have a partnership agreement, because the Uniform Partnership Act applies. The existence of a partnership is determined by the totality of the circumstances, but there is a presumption that sharing losses and profits can be considered a partnership.","sub":"Partnership – Formation","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":20},{"n":10,"scenario":"MEE-028","item":"2 of 5","summary":"Angelo received 15% of monthly profits solely as repayment of a loan, did not share losses, and had no role in day-to-day operations or management of Kibble.","trigger":"Profit payments made in repayment of a debt.","rule":"Receipt of profits as repayment of a loan does not create a partnership and does not make the recipient a partner. Here, Angelo received profits from Kibble not because he was a partner, but because he loaned Kibble mondey and this was for a repyament of a debt.","sub":"Partnership – Profit Exception (Creditor)","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":20},{"n":11,"scenario":"MEE-028","item":"3 of 5","summary":"Mary assigned her entire partnership interest in Kibble to her son Bob as a gift, and Wendy received notice of the transfer but objected to Bob’s involvement in the business.","trigger":"Partner transfers right to receive distributions to a third party.","rule":"In a partnership a partner cannot assign his rights as a partner to a third party, he can only assign his profits, because the decision to be included as a partner requieres unanimity of all partners. Here, Mary assigned all her rights to Bob, and only profits can be asigned. Therefore, Bob was entitled to Kibble monthy profits, but he is not a partner.","sub":"Partnership – Transferable Interest","family":"Partnership Property & Interests","unit":21},{"n":12,"scenario":"MEE-028","item":"4 of 5","summary":"Bob demanded access to Kibble’s books and records after receiving Mary’s assigned interest, but Wendy refused to allow inspection.","trigger":"Transferee of partnership interest seeks management rights.","rule":"In a partnership only partners are entitled to inspect books and records. Here, Bob was not a partner, he was only assigned Mary'ss profits thus he cannot inspect book and records of Kibble.","sub":"Partnership – Books and Records","family":"Partnership Property & Interests","unit":21},{"n":13,"scenario":"MEE-028","item":"5 of 5","summary":"Wendy used Kibble’s delivery van, purchased in the partnership’s name with partnership funds, on Sundays to transport her nieces to softball games without Mary’s consent.","trigger":"Use of partnership property for personal purposes.","rule":"Property acquired in the name of the partnership is partnership property and may not be used for personal purposes without consent of the partners.","sub":"Partnership – Partnership Property","family":"Partnership Property & Interests","unit":22},{"n":14,"scenario":"MEE-029","item":"1 of 4","summary":"Adam and Ben formed Empire, a general partnership buying and selling antique cars. Ben falsely represented a car as a rare movie car, inducing a collector to overpay. The misrepresentation occurred in the ordinary course of Empire’s business.","trigger":"Partner commits wrongful act in ordinary course of partnership business.","rule":"Partners in a general partnership are personally, jointly and severally liable for partnership obligations arising from a partner’s wrongful acts committed in the ordinary course of business. As a general rule a person suing a partnership must exhaust first remedies against the P and later if there is no money go against the partners personally","sub":"Partnership – Partner Liability","family":"Partner Liability & Collection","unit":23},{"n":15,"scenario":"MEE-029","item":"2 of 4","summary":"After the collector’s claim arose, Diane was admitted as a new partner in Empire. The partnership agreement was amended, and Diane learned of the pending claim.","trigger":"Partner admitted after partnership obligation arose.","rule":"A person admitted as a partner is not personally liable for partnership obligations incurred before admission. NO retroactive liability for new partners BUT the new partner the new partner can loose their contribution in the partnership. Here, Diane was admitted after the collector's suit, she would not be personally, jointly and severally liable because she was included in the partnership after the suit was filed, but she can loose her cotribution in the partership.","sub":"Partnership – Incoming Partner","family":"Partner Liability & Collection","unit":23},{"n":16,"scenario":"MEE-029","item":"3 of 4","summary":"Adam, Ben, and Diane filed a statement of qualification converting Empire into an LLP after the collector’s claim arose but before judgment.","trigger":"LLP election after obligation already exists.","rule":"To turn a General Partnership into and LLP there must be a filing of a statement of qualification. Partners remain personally liable for partnership obligations incurred before an LLP election. Here, Adan and Ben remain personally liable for the obligations before the LLP election but not Diane.","sub":"LLP – Pre‑Election Liability","family":"Partner Liability & Collection","unit":24},{"n":17,"scenario":"MEE-029","item":"4 of 4","summary":"After Empire became an LLP, a driver relied on representations that a car was roadworthy, suffered a fatal crash, and the driver’s estate sued the partners.","trigger":"Tort claim arises after LLP election.","rule":"In an LLP, partners are not personally liable for partnership obligations arising after the LLP election absent personal misconduct. The LLP creates a force field against liability. Therefore, drivers estate can only go against Empire LLP. If one of the partners screwed up they can go against him.","sub":"LLP – Post‑Election Liability","family":"Partner Liability & Collection","unit":24},{"n":18,"scenario":"MEE-043","item":"1 of 3","summary":"A shareholder of MEGA Inc. read a newspaper report alleging MEGA paid bribes to foreign officials to open factories abroad. She requested inspection of board minutes and accounting records to decide whether to sue directors for permitting illegal conduct.","trigger":"Shareholder seeks inspection to investigate alleged illegal corporate conduct.","rule":"A shareholder has a proper purpose to inspect corporate books and records when seeking information to investigate possible director misconduct or illegal corporate activity and the scoop of the request is reasonable.","sub":"Shareholder Inspection Rights – Proper Purpose","family":"Shareholder & Derivative Litigation","unit":8},{"n":19,"scenario":"MEE-043","item":"2 of 3","summary":"MEGA’s board refused inspection and declined to investigate the bribery allegations, asserting that doing so would not be in the corporation’s best interests, without conducting any inquiry.","trigger":"Board refusal to investigate allegations of illegality without reasonable inquiry.","rule":"A board may not obtain dismissal of a derivative suit when its refusal to act was not made in good faith after a reasonable investigation.","sub":"Derivative Suits – Demand Refused","family":"Shareholder & Derivative Litigation","unit":9},{"n":20,"scenario":"MEE-043","item":"3 of 3","summary":"The MEGA directors chose not to investigate or remediate alleged illegal foreign bribes, believing the conduct benefited the corporation financially.","trigger":"Directors knowingly disregard credible evidence of illegal conduct.","rule":"Directors breach their duty of good faith by consciously disregarding illegal conduct, and such decisions are not protected by the business judgment rule.","sub":"Duty of Good Faith – Business Judgment Rule","family":"Fiduciary Duties","unit":10},{"n":21,"scenario":"MEE-053","item":"1 of 7","summary":"Acme and Brown formed a member-managed LLC in which Acme held a 55% voting interest and management control. Acme supplied concrete to the LLC. After Acme’s defective concrete caused the homes to collapse, Acme refused to have the LLC sue Acme.","trigger":"Controlling member refuses to cause LLC to bring a meritorious claim.","rule":"Members of a member-managed LLC owe fiduciary duties of loyalty and care to the LLC and its members.","sub":"LLC Fiduciary Duties – Loyalty and Care","family":"Fiduciary Duties","unit":11},{"n":22,"scenario":"MEE-053","item":"2 of 7","summary":"Acme blocked the LLC from bringing a sure-fire claim against Acme for defective concrete.","trigger":"Self-interested refusal to pursue LLC claim.","rule":"A controlling member breaches the duty of loyalty by preventing the LLC from pursuing a valuable claim to obtain an improper personal benefit.","sub":"LLC Duty of Loyalty – Self-Interest","family":"Fiduciary Duties","unit":11},{"n":23,"scenario":"MEE-053","item":"3 of 7","summary":"Brown demanded that the LLC sue Acme, but Acme refused.","trigger":"Demand made and refused.","rule":"A member may bring a derivative action on behalf of an LLC after demand is refused or would be futile.","sub":"Derivative Action – LLC","family":"Shareholder & Derivative Litigation","unit":9},{"n":24,"scenario":"MEE-053","item":"4 of 7","summary":"Brown attempted to sue Acme directly for losses suffered by the LLC.","trigger":"Member seeks recovery for LLC injury.","rule":"A member may not bring a direct action for an injury suffered by the LLC unless the member suffers a distinct personal injury.","sub":"Direct vs Derivative Claims","family":"Shareholder & Derivative Litigation","unit":12},{"n":25,"scenario":"MEE-053","item":"5 of 7","summary":"Brown sought judicial dissolution based on Acme’s conduct.","trigger":"Oppressive conduct by controlling member.","rule":"A court may order judicial dissolution of an LLC when those in control act oppressively or in a manner directly harmful to a member.","sub":"Judicial Dissolution – Oppression","family":"Shareholder & Derivative Litigation","unit":13},{"n":26,"scenario":"MEE-053","item":"6 of 7","summary":"The LLC lacked assets to satisfy a $15 million judgment, and Brown argued losses should be allocated to Acme.","trigger":"Attempt to impose personal liability for LLC debts.","rule":"Under the rule of limited liability, LLC members and managers are not personally liable for LLC obligations absent veil piercing or direct liability.","sub":"Limited Liability – LLC Members","family":"Veil Piercing","unit":7},{"n":27,"scenario":"MEE-053","item":"7 of 7","summary":"No evidence showed fraud, undercapitalization, or misuse of the LLC form.","trigger":"Request to pierce LLC veil.","rule":"Absent grounds for veil piercing, LLC losses are not allocated to members even if profits are shared.","sub":"Veil Piercing – Not Established","family":"Veil Piercing","unit":7},{"n":28,"scenario":"MEE-066","item":"1 of 7","summary":"Amy and Bill formed Beer Corporation (BC), served as sole directors, and routinely used BC funds to attend brewing trade shows in Germany. After Sharon invested and joined the board, Amy and Bill proposed a Belgium trip at corporate expense, Sharon objected, but Amy and Bill voted as a majority to approve BC paying for their Belgium travel.","trigger":"Board vote approving directors’ own travel expenses.","rule":"Corporate powers are exercised by the board, and actions are approved by majority vote at a meeting with quorum.","sub":"Board Authority / Majority Vote","family":"Directors' Conflicting Interests","unit":14},{"n":29,"scenario":"MEE-066","item":"2 of 7","summary":"Amy and Bill voted to have BC pay for their Belgium trip even though they personally benefited from the travel.","trigger":"Directors approve transaction in which they have personal financial interest.","rule":"A transaction in which a director has a material financial interest is a director’s conflicting interest transaction (DCIT) subject to duty of loyalty scrutiny.","sub":"Director Conflicting Interest Transaction","family":"Directors' Conflicting Interests","unit":14},{"n":30,"scenario":"MEE-066","item":"3 of 7","summary":"Amy and Bill’s Belgium travel included brewery visits and incidental sightseeing; BC competitors sent employees to Europe for similar purposes.","trigger":"Directors personally benefit from corporate-paid travel.","rule":"Directors violate the duty of loyalty unless the conflicted transaction is approved by disinterested directors/shareholders or is substantively fair to the corporation.","sub":"Duty of Loyalty / Fairness","family":"Directors' Conflicting Interests","unit":15},{"n":31,"scenario":"MEE-066","item":"4 of 7","summary":"Sharon did not approve the Belgium trip and threatened to sue; Amy and Bill proceeded anyway and charged the trip to BC’s credit card.","trigger":"No disinterested approval of conflicted transaction.","rule":"Absent approval by qualified directors or shareholders, the burden shifts to the interested directors to prove the transaction was fair to the corporation.","sub":"Safe Harbor / Burden of Proof","family":"Directors' Conflicting Interests","unit":15},{"n":32,"scenario":"MEE-066","item":"5 of 7","summary":"Amy and Bill argue the Belgium trip was primarily for business purposes consistent with competitors’ practices and beneficial to BC.","trigger":"Directors attempt to justify conflicted transaction.","rule":"A conflicted transaction may be upheld if directors prove it was fair to the corporation, considering purpose, comparables, and overall benefit.","sub":"Substantive Fairness","family":"Directors' Conflicting Interests","unit":15},{"n":33,"scenario":"MEE-066","item":"6 of 7","summary":"Sharon seeks to personally recover the Belgium expenses from Amy and Bill.","trigger":"Shareholder seeks direct recovery for corporate injury.","rule":"Claims for breach of fiduciary duty causing harm to the corporation must be brought derivatively, and any recovery belongs to the corporation.","sub":"Derivative Recovery Rule","family":"Shareholder & Derivative Litigation","unit":16},{"n":34,"scenario":"MEE-066","item":"7 of 7","summary":"Sharon also seeks recovery for Amy and Bill’s earlier Germany trips that occurred before Sharon became a shareholder.","trigger":"Shareholder challenges transactions predating ownership.","rule":"A plaintiff must be a shareholder at the time of the challenged conduct to have standing to bring a derivative action (contemporaneous ownership rule).","sub":"Contemporaneous Ownership","family":"Shareholder & Derivative Litigation","unit":16},{"n":35,"scenario":"MEE-076","item":"1 of 10","summary":"Carlos, Diana, and Ethan own all shares of Winery Inc. in State A and are its only directors. Carlos and Diana want Winery Inc. to merge into a newly formed benefit corporation in State B (Organic Wines Corp.) to pursue sustainable organic practices. Ethan objects.","trigger":"Board + shareholder approval of merger. FOR PURPOSE OF CONFLICT OF LAW BETWEEN DIFFERENT STATES REMEMBER THAT THE LAW OF THE STATE OF INCORPORATION APPLIES.","rule":"In a merger of two corporations incorporated in different states, each corporation is generally subject to the merger requirements of the state in which it is incorporated. A corporate merger is when two corporations are combined into one surviving corporation, and the shares of the merged corporation are merged into the surviving corporation. Under the MBCA, a merger requires approval by a majority of the board of directors and a majority of outstanding shares entitled to vote.","sub":"Merger Approval","family":"Mergers & Appraisal","unit":1},{"n":36,"scenario":"MEE-076","item":"2 of 10","summary":"Carlos and Diana together control a majority of the board and two‑thirds of the shares.","trigger":"Majority ownership.","rule":"Shareholders holding a majority of voting power can approve a merger over dissenting minority shareholders.","sub":"Shareholder Voting","family":"Mergers & Appraisal","unit":1},{"n":37,"scenario":"MEE-076","item":"3 of 10","summary":"Ethan votes against the merger and seeks to block it.","trigger":"Minority opposition.","rule":"A dissenting minority shareholder cannot block a merger approved by the required statutory majorities.","sub":"Minority Rights","family":"Mergers & Appraisal","unit":1},{"n":38,"scenario":"MEE-076","item":"4 of 10","summary":"After merger approval, Ethan demands cash instead of receiving shares in Organic Wines.","trigger":"Dissent from merger.","rule":"A shareholder who dissents from a merger is entitled to appraisal rights and payment of the fair value of shares.","sub":"Appraisal Rights","family":"Mergers & Appraisal","unit":2},{"n":39,"scenario":"MEE-076","item":"5 of 10","summary":"Winery Inc. is closely held and merger required shareholder vote.","trigger":"Availability of appraisal.","rule":"Appraisal rights apply in mergers requiring shareholder approval unless market‑out exception applies.","sub":"Cash-Out Appraisal","family":"Mergers & Appraisal","unit":2},{"n":40,"scenario":"MEE-076","item":"6 of 10","summary":"Ethan gives notice and demands fair value payment.","trigger":"Procedural steps.","rule":"To preserve appraisal rights, shareholder must give notice, not vote for merger, and timely demand payment.","sub":"Appraisal Procedure","family":"Mergers & Appraisal","unit":2},{"n":41,"scenario":"MEE-076","item":"7 of 10","summary":"Organic Wines Corp. is formed as a benefit corporation in State B and survives the merger.","trigger":"Different states involved.","rule":"Under the internal affairs doctrine, matters of director duties are governed by the law of the state of incorporation.","sub":"Internal Affairs Doctrine","family":"Choice of Law & Entity Purpose","unit":3},{"n":42,"scenario":"MEE-076","item":"8 of 10","summary":"State A law requires directors to maximize shareholder profits; State B benefit‑corp law allows pursuit of social/environmental goals.","trigger":"Conflict of laws.","rule":"The internal affairs of the foreign corporation-that is, the legal relationships between and among its shareholders, directors, and officers-are governed by the state of incorporation even when the corporation's business and assets are located primarily in other states or the corporation operates in another state. After merger, the surviving corporation’s state law governs director duties.","sub":"Choice of Law","family":"Choice of Law & Entity Purpose","unit":3},{"n":43,"scenario":"MEE-076","item":"9 of 10","summary":"Ethan sues directors in State A for prioritizing sustainability over profits.","trigger":"Challenge to director conduct.","rule":"Directors of a benefit corporation may lawfully prioritize stated public benefits even at expense of shareholder profits.","sub":"Benefit Corporation Duties","family":"Choice of Law & Entity Purpose","unit":4},{"n":44,"scenario":"MEE-076","item":"10 of 10","summary":"Organic Wines directors act consistently with stated environmental purpose.","trigger":"Statutory protection.","rule":"Benefit‑corp statutes shield directors from liability when decisions further the corporation’s declared public benefit.","sub":"Director Liability – Benefit Corps","family":"Choice of Law & Entity Purpose","unit":4},{"n":45,"scenario":"MEE-081","item":"1 of 12","summary":"An inventor retained a woman to act as his agent to purchase: (i) 25 computer chips, (ii) 25 blue lenses, and (iii) 25 lawn-mower shutoff switches. The inventor expressly instructed her to purchase only (a) Series A computer chips, (b) blue lenses that cost no more than $300 each, and (c) shutoff switches that could shut down a mower in less than one second after hitting a foreign object. All elements of contract formation and enforceability are satisfied for each contract—so liability turns on agency (authority, disclosure of principal, and ratification).","trigger":"Agent enters contracts for principal; liability depends on authority and disclosure status of principal.","rule":"A principal is bound by a contract entered by an agent only if the agent had actual authority (express or implied), apparent authority (principal’s manifestations to the third party), or the principal later ratifies the contract with knowledge of material facts.","sub":"Authority / Ratification (Overview)","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":25},{"n":46,"scenario":"MEE-081","item":"2 of 12","summary":"The woman made three separate purchase contracts with three manufacturers, but she signed each contract in a different way: (1) chips—she signed “as agent” and identified the inventor; (2) lenses—she signed only in her own name and never mentioned she was an agent; (3) switches—she signed in her own name but told the manufacturer she was acting as an agent without identifying her principal.","trigger":"Different disclosure status for each transaction.","rule":"If the principal is disclosed, the agent is generally not personally liable on the contract; if the principal is undisclosed or unidentified (partially disclosed), the agent is personally liable on the contract, and the principal is also bound if the agent acted with actual authority (or ratification).","sub":"Disclosed vs. Undisclosed vs. Unidentified Principal","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":25},{"n":47,"scenario":"MEE-081","item":"3 of 12","summary":"CHIPS: The woman contacted a chip manufacturer and stated she was the inventor’s agent and wanted to purchase 25 Series A chips on his behalf. The manufacturer said Series A chips cost $800 each but offered Series B chips with similar functionality for $90 each. Without discussing the substitution with the inventor, the woman agreed to buy 25 Series B chips and signed the purchase contract “as agent” of the inventor. The Series B chips were shipped to the woman. When she delivered them and explained the “great deal,” the inventor refused to accept them and also refused to pay the manufacturer.","trigger":"Agent deviates from principal’s explicit instructions (Series B instead of Series A).","rule":"An agent has actual authority only to the extent the principal manifests consent for the agent to act; when an agent exceeds actual authority, the principal is not bound unless apparent authority exists or the principal ratifies.","sub":"Actual Authority – Exceeded","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":26},{"n":48,"scenario":"MEE-081","item":"4 of 12","summary":"CHIPS: Here, the inventor expressly limited authority to Series A chips. The woman knowingly purchased Series B chips instead. The chip manufacturer knew it was selling Series B (a different product than the Series A the agent initially requested) and had no manifestations from the inventor expanding authority.","trigger":"No actual authority; no apparent authority from principal’s manifestations.","rule":"Apparent authority arises only from the principal’s manifestations to the third party; an agent’s statements or unilateral conduct cannot create apparent authority.","sub":"Apparent Authority – Source of Manifestations","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":26},{"n":49,"scenario":"MEE-081","item":"5 of 12","summary":"CHIPS: Because the inventor is a disclosed principal (the woman identified him and signed “as agent”), the manufacturer normally would sue only the inventor if the agent had authority. But the inventor is not bound because the woman lacked authority to substitute Series B for Series A and the inventor promptly refused acceptance/payment (no ratification).","trigger":"Disclosed principal + unauthorized contract.","rule":"When an agent purports to act for a disclosed principal but lacks authority, the principal is not liable; instead, the agent is liable to the third party for breach of the implied warranty of authority (unless the third party knew the agent lacked authority).","sub":"Implied Warranty of Authority","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":26},{"n":50,"scenario":"MEE-081","item":"6 of 12","summary":"LENSES: The woman contacted a lens manufacturer and signed a contract in her name alone for 25 blue lenses at $295 per lens. She did not tell the lens manufacturer she was acting as anyone’s agent. The lenses were shipped to her, but when she brought them to the inventor, he refused to accept them because he had decided it would be better to use red lenses. The inventor refused to pay for the blue lenses.","trigger":"Agent acts within purchase limits but does not disclose principal.","rule":"An undisclosed principal is bound by a contract made by an agent acting within actual authority; the third party may hold the principal liable once discovered.","sub":"Undisclosed Principal – Principal Liability","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":27},{"n":51,"scenario":"MEE-081","item":"7 of 12","summary":"LENSES: The inventor authorized blue lenses costing no more than $300 each. The woman bought blue lenses for $295 each—so she acted within actual authority, even though the inventor later changed his mind and preferred red lenses after the contract was made.","trigger":"Within actual authority; principal later regrets purchase.","rule":"A principal cannot avoid liability on an authorized contract merely because the principal later changes preferences; if the agent acted within actual authority at the time of contracting, the principal is bound.","sub":"Actual Authority – Time of Contract","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":27},{"n":52,"scenario":"MEE-081","item":"8 of 12","summary":"LENSES: Because the woman did not disclose any principal and signed in her own name, she is a party to the contract with the lens manufacturer.","trigger":"Agent contracts in own name (principal undisclosed).","rule":"When a principal is undisclosed, the agent is personally liable on the contract to the third party; the third party may sue the agent, the undisclosed principal (once discovered), or both (subject to one satisfaction).","sub":"Agent Liability – Undisclosed Principal","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":27},{"n":53,"scenario":"MEE-081","item":"9 of 12","summary":"SWITCHES: The woman contacted a switch manufacturer and signed a contract in her name alone for shutoff switches that would shut down a lawn mower in less than five seconds (substantially slower than the inventor’s instruction of under one second). When she signed, she told the manufacturer she was acting as someone’s agent but did not disclose her principal’s identity. The switches were shipped to her. The inventor recognized the switches were not what she had been told to buy, but he nonetheless used them to build lawn mowers and later refused to pay the manufacturer.","trigger":"Unidentified (partially disclosed) principal + agent exceeds authority + principal knowingly uses goods.","rule":"A principal is not bound by an unauthorized contract unless the principal ratifies; ratification occurs when the principal, with knowledge of material facts, affirms the contract or accepts its benefits (e.g., uses the goods).","sub":"Ratification – Knowledge + Acceptance of Benefits","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":28},{"n":54,"scenario":"MEE-081","item":"10 of 12","summary":"SWITCHES: The inventor knew the switches did not meet the under-one-second specification but still used them to build mowers. By knowingly accepting and using the benefit of the contract, the inventor ratified the woman’s contract with the switch manufacturer.","trigger":"Principal uses nonconforming goods with knowledge.","rule":"A principal who ratifies is bound as if the agent had authority from the outset; ratification relates back to the time of contracting.","sub":"Effect of Ratification (Relation Back)","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":28},{"n":55,"scenario":"MEE-081","item":"11 of 12","summary":"SWITCHES: Because the woman disclosed she was an agent but did not disclose the principal’s identity, the principal is “unidentified/partially disclosed.”","trigger":"Third party knows there is a principal but not who it is.","rule":"When the principal is unidentified (partially disclosed), the agent is personally liable on the contract unless the contract provides otherwise, and the principal is also liable if the agent had authority or the principal ratifies.","sub":"Unidentified Principal – Agent & Principal Liability","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":28},{"n":56,"scenario":"MEE-081","item":"12 of 12","summary":"SWITCHES: Bottom line—after the inventor’s ratification, the switch manufacturer may hold the inventor liable. The woman also remains liable on the contract (unidentified principal) unless the manufacturer agrees to look solely to the inventor (novation/release).","trigger":"Post-ratification allocation of liability.","rule":"Even after ratification, an agent who contracted for an unidentified principal remains liable on the contract unless the third party releases the agent or agrees to substitute the principal alone (novation).","sub":"Agent Liability After Ratification","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":28},{"n":57,"scenario":"MEE-090","item":"1 of 10","summary":"Adam immediately withdraws without giving required six‑month notice.","trigger":"Immediate dissociation.","rule":"Under UPA (1997), a partner may dissociate at any time, but dissociation in violation of partnership agreement is wrongful.","sub":"Wrongful Dissociation","family":"Dissociation & Winding Up","unit":29},{"n":58,"scenario":"MEE-090","item":"2 of 10","summary":"Partnership agreement requires six months written notice.","trigger":"Breach of partnership agreement.","rule":"A wrongfully dissociating partner is liable to partnership and partners for damages caused by dissociation.","sub":"Damages for Wrongful Dissociation","family":"Dissociation & Winding Up","unit":29},{"n":59,"scenario":"MEE-090","item":"3 of 10","summary":"Adam seeks to force immediate shutdown and sale of assets.","trigger":"Effect of wrongful dissociation.","rule":"Wrongful dissociation does not automatically require winding up; remaining partners may continue business and buy out dissociating partner.","sub":"Continuation vs Winding Up","family":"Dissociation & Winding Up","unit":29},{"n":60,"scenario":"MEE-090","item":"4 of 10","summary":"Beth and Chris want to continue operating PSS.","trigger":"Election to continue.","rule":"Remaining partners may elect to continue partnership and purchase dissociating partner’s interest at statutory buyout price.","sub":"Buyout of Partner","family":"Dissociation & Winding Up","unit":29},{"n":61,"scenario":"MEE-090","item":"5 of 10","summary":"Because Adam dissociated wrongfully.","trigger":"Participation rights.","rule":"A wrongfully dissociating partner has no right to participate in winding up.","sub":"No Winding‑Up Rights","family":"Dissociation & Winding Up","unit":29},{"n":62,"scenario":"MEE-090","item":"6 of 10","summary":"If Adam gives proper six‑month notice.","trigger":"Proper dissociation.","rule":"If dissociation complies with agreement, it is not wrongful and partner may force dissolution and winding up.","sub":"Rightful Dissociation","family":"Dissociation & Winding Up","unit":30},{"n":63,"scenario":"MEE-090","item":"7 of 10","summary":"Adam gives six months notice and withdraws.","trigger":"Dissolution.","rule":"Proper dissociation causes dissolution and winding up unless remaining partners agree to continue.","sub":"Dissolution","family":"Dissociation & Winding Up","unit":30},{"n":64,"scenario":"MEE-090","item":"8 of 10","summary":"Adam after proper notice wants sale of assets.","trigger":"Winding up.","rule":"Upon rightful dissociation, partner may participate in winding up but cannot control process.","sub":"Winding Up","family":"Dissociation & Winding Up","unit":30},{"n":65,"scenario":"MEE-090","item":"9 of 10","summary":"Partnership incurs debts during winding up.","trigger":"Post‑withdrawal liability.","rule":"A dissociating partner remains liable for partnership obligations incurred during winding up.","sub":"Liability After Dissociation","family":"Partner Liability & Collection","unit":31},{"n":66,"scenario":"MEE-090","item":"10 of 10","summary":"Creditors seek payment after winding up.","trigger":"Partner liability.","rule":"Partners are jointly and severally liable for partnership obligations incurred before and during winding up.","sub":"Joint & Several Liability","family":"Partner Liability & Collection","unit":31},{"n":67,"scenario":"MEE-092","item":"1 of 10","summary":"Carol, Jean, and Pat agreed to share profits and run radiology practice but never filed LLC papers.","trigger":"Determine business entity.","rule":"A general partnership is formed when two or more persons associate to carry on a business for profit, even without filing formal documents.","sub":"General Partnership Formation","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":32},{"n":68,"scenario":"MEE-092","item":"2 of 10","summary":"Attorney prepared LLC documents but none were signed or filed.","trigger":"Intent to form LLC.","rule":"An LLC is formed only upon filing articles of organization; intent alone does not create an LLC.","sub":"Failure to Form LLC","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":32},{"n":69,"scenario":"MEE-092","item":"3 of 10","summary":"Carol purchased $400,000 imaging machine without consent.","trigger":"Partner authority.","rule":"Each partner has actual authority to bind the partnership in transactions within the ordinary course of partnership business.","sub":"Partner Authority","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":33},{"n":70,"scenario":"MEE-092","item":"4 of 10","summary":"Practice previously bought similar imaging equipment.","trigger":"Ordinary course of business.","rule":"Acts consistent with prior business practices are within ordinary course and bind partnership.","sub":"Ordinary Course","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":33},{"n":71,"scenario":"MEE-092","item":"5 of 10","summary":"Jean had concerns but never told Carol before purchase.","trigger":"Knowledge of disagreement.","rule":"A partner lacks authority only if she knows other partners object or action is outside ordinary course.","sub":"Limits on Authority","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":33},{"n":72,"scenario":"MEE-092","item":"6 of 10","summary":"Jean told Carol: 'I’m out of here and never coming back.'","trigger":"Withdrawal from partnership.","rule":"A partner is dissociated when partnership has notice of partner’s express will to withdraw.","sub":"Dissociation","family":"Dissociation & Winding Up","unit":34},{"n":73,"scenario":"MEE-092","item":"7 of 10","summary":"No fixed term agreement existed.","trigger":"At‑will partnership.","rule":"In an at‑will partnership, a partner may dissociate at any time without wrongful dissociation.","sub":"At‑Will Partnership","family":"Dissociation & Winding Up","unit":34},{"n":74,"scenario":"MEE-092","item":"8 of 10","summary":"Carol and Pat agreed to continue business without Jean.","trigger":"Effect of dissociation.","rule":"If remaining partners continue business, dissociation does not cause dissolution.","sub":"Continuation of Partnership","family":"Dissociation & Winding Up","unit":34},{"n":75,"scenario":"MEE-092","item":"9 of 10","summary":"Jean seeks payment for her one‑third interest.","trigger":"Buyout right.","rule":"A dissociated partner is entitled to a buyout of her partnership interest when partnership continues.","sub":"Partner Buyout","family":"Dissociation & Winding Up","unit":34},{"n":76,"scenario":"MEE-092","item":"10 of 10","summary":"Jean made only oral demand for payment.","trigger":"Written demand requirement.","rule":"Buyout payment timetable begins only after dissociating partner makes written demand.","sub":"Written Demand / 120 Days","family":"Dissociation & Winding Up","unit":34},{"n":77,"scenario":"MEE-096","item":"1 of 11","summary":"Aldo, Belinda, and Carlos were equal partners in a general partnership operating a trash collection business in State A with no written partnership agreement. Aldo managed daily operations; Belinda kept books; Carlos owned the landfill used by the company.","trigger":"Determine agency status of partner.","rule":"Each partner is an agent of the partnership for acts apparently carrying on the ordinary course of partnership business.","sub":"Partner as Agent","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":35},{"n":78,"scenario":"MEE-096","item":"2 of 11","summary":"Aldo contracted in the partnership’s name to buy a $100,000 all‑electric garbage truck from a dealership that had previously sold trucks to the partnership.","trigger":"Purchase of business equipment.","rule":"An act of a partner for apparently carrying on the ordinary course binds the partnership unless the third party knew the partner lacked authority.","sub":"Ordinary Course Authority","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":35},{"n":79,"scenario":"MEE-096","item":"3 of 11","summary":"Electric garbage trucks had become common in the trash industry and were more fuel‑efficient; dealership had sold trucks to Aldo for partnership before.","trigger":"Third‑party reasonable belief.","rule":"Apparent authority exists when partnership conduct would lead a reasonable third party to believe the partner was authorized.","sub":"Apparent Authority","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":35},{"n":80,"scenario":"MEE-096","item":"4 of 11","summary":"Belinda and Carlos never authorized the purchase and Carlos objected afterward, but dealership had no notice Aldo lacked authority.","trigger":"Lack of partner consent.","rule":"Internal restrictions do not defeat apparent authority absent notice to the third party.","sub":"Limits on Authority","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":35},{"n":81,"scenario":"MEE-096","item":"5 of 11","summary":"Aldo paid $30,000 from his own funds as down payment on the truck.","trigger":"Partner advances funds.","rule":"A partnership must reimburse a partner for payments made on behalf of the partnership in the ordinary course of business.","sub":"Partner Reimbursement","family":"Partner Liability & Collection","unit":37},{"n":82,"scenario":"MEE-096","item":"6 of 11","summary":"Carlos worried about cost but partnership lacked sufficient funds for remaining $70,000 balance.","trigger":"Personal liability of partners.","rule":"Partners are jointly and severally liable for partnership obligations.","sub":"Partner Liability","family":"Partner Liability & Collection","unit":37},{"n":83,"scenario":"MEE-096","item":"7 of 11","summary":"Aldo spent about twice as much time running the business as Belinda and Carlos and demanded compensation for his services.","trigger":"Claim for compensation.","rule":"Absent agreement, a partner is not entitled to remuneration for services rendered to the partnership except in winding up.","sub":"No Partner Salary","family":"Partner Liability & Collection","unit":37},{"n":84,"scenario":"MEE-096","item":"8 of 11","summary":"Five years earlier partnership bought 500 acres of land in State B zoned residential only as speculative investment unrelated to trash business.","trigger":"Nature of land transaction.","rule":"Acts outside the ordinary course require unanimous partner consent.","sub":"Extraordinary Acts","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":36},{"n":85,"scenario":"MEE-096","item":"9 of 11","summary":"Aldo alone contracted to sell the land to a developer; developer knew partnership operated trash business only in State A.","trigger":"Unauthorized land sale.","rule":"A partner lacks actual authority for extraordinary transactions without unanimous approval.","sub":"No Actual Authority","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":36},{"n":86,"scenario":"MEE-096","item":"10 of 11","summary":"Developer was aware land was unrelated to trash business and that Aldo was acting alone.","trigger":"Third‑party knowledge.","rule":"No apparent authority exists when the third party knows the partner lacks authority.","sub":"No Apparent Authority","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":36},{"n":87,"scenario":"MEE-096","item":"11 of 11","summary":"Carlos immediately told Aldo the land sale was not binding; Aldo nevertheless executed contract.","trigger":"Binding effect of sale.","rule":"Partnership is not bound by a partner’s act outside ordinary course when there is no actual or apparent authority.","sub":"Partnership Not Bound","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":36},{"n":88,"scenario":"MEE-102","item":"1 of 10","summary":"On January 2, Fran opened Petals flower shop as a sole proprietorship. The shop soon experienced financial difficulty and Fran could not pay bills.","trigger":"Initial sole proprietorship.","rule":"A partnership forms when two or more persons associate as co-owners of a business for profit, regardless of intent.","sub":"Partnership Formation","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":20},{"n":89,"scenario":"MEE-102","item":"2 of 10","summary":"On March 1 Fran asked Gina and Hank for financial help. On April 1 Gina delivered a check payable to Petals. Fran agreed Gina would receive 25% of monthly net profits and share losses. Gina began working in the shop and helped with business planning.","trigger":"Profit sharing + participation.","rule":"Sharing profits and losses plus participation in management creates presumption of partnership.","sub":"Profit Sharing Presumption","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":20},{"n":90,"scenario":"MEE-102","item":"3 of 10","summary":"On April 2 Hank delivered a check payable to Petals marked 'loan to Petals.' Hank agreed to receive 25% of net profits only until his loan plus interest was repaid in full.","trigger":"Hank loan characterization.","rule":"Receipt of profits in repayment of a debt does not make the recipient a partner.","sub":"Creditor Exception","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":20},{"n":91,"scenario":"MEE-102","item":"4 of 10","summary":"Fran used Gina and Hank’s funds to buy equipment, supplies, and a delivery truck titled in Petals’ name.","trigger":"Use of contributed funds.","rule":"Property purchased in partnership name is partnership property.","sub":"Partnership Property","family":"Partnership Property & Interests","unit":22},{"n":92,"scenario":"MEE-102","item":"5 of 10","summary":"Beginning in April, Fran distributed 25% of monthly profits to Gina and Hank.","trigger":"Distribution of profits.","rule":"Regular profit distributions support inference of partnership unless falling within creditor exception.","sub":"Profit Distribution","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":20},{"n":93,"scenario":"MEE-102","item":"6 of 10","summary":"On September 1, Gina assigned all her interest in Petals to her son Ivan as a gift and notified Fran, who stated she wanted nothing to do with Ivan.","trigger":"Assignment of partnership interest.","rule":"A partner may transfer the right to receive distributions, but the transferee does not become a partner absent consent.","sub":"Transferable Interest","family":"Partnership Property & Interests","unit":21},{"n":94,"scenario":"MEE-102","item":"7 of 10","summary":"Gina continued working in Petals operations after the assignment.","trigger":"Status after assignment.","rule":"Transfer of economic interest does not dissolve partnership nor remove assignor as partner.","sub":"Effect of Assignment","family":"Partnership Property & Interests","unit":21},{"n":95,"scenario":"MEE-102","item":"8 of 10","summary":"On September 30 Fran distributed profits to Gina and Hank but paid nothing to Ivan.","trigger":"Ivan seeks Gina’s share.","rule":"A transferee of a partner’s interest is entitled to receive the distributions the transferor would have received.","sub":"Rights of Transferee","family":"Partnership Property & Interests","unit":21},{"n":96,"scenario":"MEE-102","item":"9 of 10","summary":"On October 10 Ivan demanded Gina’s profit share and inspection of Petals’ books and records.","trigger":"Demand for records.","rule":"A transferee is not entitled to participate in management or inspect partnership books.","sub":"No Management Rights","family":"Partnership Property & Interests","unit":21},{"n":97,"scenario":"MEE-102","item":"10 of 10","summary":"On October 15 Gina learned Fran was using Petals delivery truck on Sundays to transport her children to soccer games. Fran refused to stop, noting truck unused Sundays.","trigger":"Personal use of partnership property.","rule":"Partnership property belongs to the partnership; a partner may not use it for personal purposes absent agreement.","sub":"Use of Partnership Property","family":"Partnership Property & Interests","unit":22},{"n":98,"scenario":"MEE-110","item":"1 of 11","summary":"Linda owned and operated a clothing store as a sole proprietorship. To increase sales, she decided to offer same‑day delivery using a driver hired as an independent contractor found through a website listing local drivers, hourly rates, reviews, and driving records. The site showed the driver had poor reviews, misbehavior, unsafe driving, and three negligent‑driving lawsuits, yet Linda hired him because he was inexpensive and had his own van.","trigger":"Principal hires risky agent.","rule":"A principal is directly liable for harm to third parties caused by negligent selection of an agent when the principal knew or should have known the agent was unfit. Restatement (Third) of Agency §7.05.","sub":"Negligent Selection","family":"Agency – Vicarious Liability","unit":40},{"n":99,"scenario":"MEE-110","item":"2 of 11","summary":"Linda instructed the driver that while delivering he must place removable signs advertising the store on both sides of his van and told him not to spend more than $300 to buy the signs. She gave him a business card identifying him as acting for the store and wrote on the back, 'This is my agent to purchase signs for my store.'","trigger":"Manifestations creating apparent authority.","rule":"Apparent authority exists when a third party reasonably believes the agent has authority and that belief is traceable to the principal’s manifestations. Restatement.","sub":"Apparent Authority – Creation","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":38},{"n":100,"scenario":"MEE-110","item":"3 of 11","summary":"The driver went to a sign shop, showed Linda’s business card, and purchased custom signs for $450 on credit. The signs were within normal market range. Linda later refused to pay because the driver exceeded the $300 limit.","trigger":"Agent exceeds actual authority.","rule":"A principal is bound by contracts entered by an agent acting with apparent authority even if the agent exceeded actual authority. In this case the principal (Linda) can later go against driver for contribution for the excess of $150, but not for the $300 because driver acted with actual and apparent authority.","sub":"Principal Bound by Apparent Authority","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":38},{"n":101,"scenario":"MEE-110","item":"4 of 11","summary":"The sign shop reasonably relied on Linda’s business card and had no notice of any price limitation.","trigger":"Third‑party reliance.","rule":"An agent has apparent authority to transact when the principal’s manifestations would cause a reasonable third party to believe the agent is authorized unless the third party has notice of limitations.","sub":"Reasonable Reliance","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":38},{"n":102,"scenario":"MEE-110","item":"5 of 11","summary":"Linda is therefore liable to the sign shop for the purchase price even though the driver breached instructions.","trigger":"Contract liability.","rule":"When an agent contracts on behalf of a disclosed principal with apparent authority, the contract binds the principal.","sub":"Disclosed Principal Liability","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":38},{"n":103,"scenario":"MEE-110","item":"6 of 11","summary":"The driver argues he should not be liable for the signs because Linda was disclosed.","trigger":"Agent contract liability.","rule":"When an agent with actual or apparent authority contracts for a disclosed principal, the agent is not personally liable on the contract.","sub":"Agent Not Liable – Disclosed Principal","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":38},{"n":104,"scenario":"MEE-110","item":"7 of 11","summary":"Three weeks later Linda told a customer, 'My driver is on his way to make a delivery to you in a van with the store’s name on its side.' The customer watched the van arrive and walked toward it.","trigger":"Representation to customer.","rule":"A principal may be vicariously liable for torts of a non‑employee agent when the agent acts with apparent authority and the third party reasonably relies on that appearance.","sub":"Vicarious Liability – Apparent Authority","family":"Agency – Vicarious Liability","unit":39},{"n":105,"scenario":"MEE-110","item":"8 of 11","summary":"As the customer approached, the driver negligently hit the accelerator, striking the customer and causing serious injuries while making the delivery.","trigger":"Tort during apparent authority.","rule":"A principal is subject to vicarious liability for torts committed by an agent while dealing with a third party on the principal’s behalf when the agent acts with apparent authority. Restatement (Third) §7.08.","sub":"Apparent Authority Tort Liability","family":"Agency – Vicarious Liability","unit":39},{"n":106,"scenario":"MEE-110","item":"9 of 11","summary":"Although the driver was an independent contractor, the customer reasonably believed he was Linda’s delivery driver based on Linda’s statements and store signage.","trigger":"Independent contractor.","rule":"A principal may be vicariously liable for torts of an independent‑contractor agent when apparent authority induced reliance.","sub":"Independent Contractor Exception","family":"Agency – Vicarious Liability","unit":39},{"n":107,"scenario":"MEE-110","item":"10 of 11","summary":"Linda ignored the website warnings about the driver’s unsafe history.","trigger":"Direct negligence.","rule":"A principal who negligently selects an agent is directly liable for resulting injuries regardless of vicarious liability.","sub":"Direct Liability – Negligent Hiring","family":"Agency – Vicarious Liability","unit":40},{"n":108,"scenario":"MEE-110","item":"11 of 11","summary":"Final outcomes: Linda is liable to the sign shop on the contract; the driver is not. Linda is vicariously liable to the customer under apparent authority and directly liable for negligent selection.","trigger":"Outcome.","rule":"Principal bound on contracts via apparent authority; agent not liable for disclosed principal; principal vicariously liable for torts under apparent authority and directly liable for negligent hiring.","sub":"Outcome Matrix","family":"Outcome Matrix","unit":null},{"n":109,"scenario":"MEE-116","item":"1 of 11","summary":"For over ten years, Man and Woman operated Natural Foods Store as equal partners with no written partnership agreement. They leased their storefront from Landlord and had previously discussed purchasing the building but never reached agreement.","trigger":"Formation + background.","rule":"In absence of agreement, a partnership is governed by default RUPA rules; partners owe fiduciary duties of loyalty and care and obligation of good faith and fair dealing.","sub":"Partnership Formation / Fiduciary Baseline","family":"Fiduciary Duties","unit":41},{"n":110,"scenario":"MEE-116","item":"2 of 11","summary":"The partnership was at will. Either partner could withdraw at any time by express notice.","trigger":"At-will status.","rule":"In an at-will partnership, a partner has the power to dissociate at any time by express will.","sub":"At-Will Dissociation","family":"Fiduciary Duties","unit":42},{"n":111,"scenario":"MEE-116","item":"3 of 11","summary":"Landlord contacted Man stating he was willing to sell the building. Man did not inform Woman of this opportunity.","trigger":"Landlord call.","rule":"A partner owes a duty of loyalty to disclose partnership opportunities and may not secretly appropriate them.","sub":"Duty of Loyalty – Disclosure","family":"Fiduciary Duties","unit":41},{"n":112,"scenario":"MEE-116","item":"4 of 11","summary":"Instead, Man emailed Woman stating he was leaving the partnership and enclosed a check for one-half the value of inventory.","trigger":"Email + check.","rule":"A partner’s notice of express will causes dissociation, but dissociation does not terminate fiduciary duties with respect to partnership opportunities arising before dissociation.","sub":"Effect of Dissociation","family":"Fiduciary Duties","unit":42},{"n":113,"scenario":"MEE-116","item":"5 of 11","summary":"Immediately after sending the email, Man negotiated privately with Landlord and entered into a binding agreement to purchase the building for himself.","trigger":"Private purchase.","rule":"A partner may not usurp a partnership opportunity for personal benefit before winding up is complete.","sub":"Partnership Opportunity Doctrine","family":"Fiduciary Duties","unit":41},{"n":114,"scenario":"MEE-116","item":"6 of 11","summary":"Woman responded rejecting Man’s attempted dissolution and stating partnership business had not been wound up.","trigger":"Woman response.","rule":"Upon dissociation in an at-will partnership, dissolution occurs and partnership enters winding up unless remaining partner continues business.","sub":"Dissolution / Winding Up","family":"Fiduciary Duties","unit":42},{"n":115,"scenario":"MEE-116","item":"7 of 11","summary":"Man opened a competing natural foods store in the same neighborhood using supplier relationships developed during partnership.","trigger":"Competing business.","rule":"During winding up, a dissociated partner remains bound by duty of loyalty and may not compete or exploit confidential information.","sub":"Duty During Winding Up","family":"Fiduciary Duties","unit":42},{"n":116,"scenario":"MEE-116","item":"8 of 11","summary":"Man argues dissociation ended his fiduciary obligations before purchase.","trigger":"Defense.","rule":"Under RUPA, duty of loyalty continues for transactions begun before dissociation and during winding up.","sub":"Continuing Fiduciary Duties","family":"Fiduciary Duties","unit":42},{"n":117,"scenario":"MEE-116","item":"9 of 11","summary":"Woman sued seeking constructive trust over the building and accounting of profits.","trigger":"Remedies sought.","rule":"Remedies for breach of fiduciary duty include constructive trust, accounting, and damages.","sub":"Equitable Remedies","family":"Equitable Remedies","unit":43},{"n":118,"scenario":"MEE-116","item":"10 of 11","summary":"Because opportunity to purchase arose while partnership existed and Man concealed it, Man breached duty of loyalty.","trigger":"Breach finding.","rule":"A partner who secretly acquires partnership opportunity must hold it in trust for partnership.","sub":"Breach – Opportunity","family":"Fiduciary Duties","unit":41},{"n":119,"scenario":"MEE-116","item":"11 of 11","summary":"Final outcome: Man wrongfully appropriated partnership opportunity; court should impose constructive trust on building for partnership benefit and order accounting.","trigger":"Outcome.","rule":"Partner dissociation does not permit appropriation of partnership opportunity arising pre-dissociation; fiduciary breach warrants constructive trust and accounting.","sub":"Outcome Matrix","family":"Equitable Remedies","unit":43},{"n":120,"scenario":"MEE-121","item":"1 of 11","summary":"Peter planned to open a 50-seat pizza parlor with home delivery. He instructed Angela to: buy 50 red chairs not exceeding $10,000; buy a new electric bicycle not exceeding $5,000; and buy a pizza oven not exceeding $12,000. Angela replied, 'I fully understand. Agreed.'","trigger":"Principal instructions.","rule":"An agent binds a principal only when acting with actual authority, apparent authority, or the principal later ratifies the transaction.","sub":"Authority – Baseline","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":25},{"n":121,"scenario":"MEE-121","item":"2 of 11","summary":"Angela told the furniture store she wanted 50 red chairs for under $10,000. Store said red chairs cost $20,000 but yellow chairs could be bought for $10,000.","trigger":"Chair pricing.","rule":"Actual authority is created by a principal’s manifestations to the agent reasonably understood as authorizing action on the principal’s behalf.","sub":"Actual Authority","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":44},{"n":122,"scenario":"MEE-121","item":"3 of 11","summary":"Angela signed a contract in her own name for yellow chairs for $10,000 without mentioning Peter.","trigger":"Yellow chair contract.","rule":"Apparent authority requires manifestations from the principal to the third party; absent such manifestations, apparent authority does not exist.","sub":"No Apparent Authority","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":44},{"n":123,"scenario":"MEE-121","item":"4 of 11","summary":"Peter never communicated with the furniture store.","trigger":"No manifestation.","rule":"Without principal manifestations, third party cannot reasonably believe agent is authorized.","sub":"Apparent Authority Fails","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":44},{"n":124,"scenario":"MEE-121","item":"5 of 11","summary":"Angela acted for an undisclosed principal.","trigger":"Undisclosed principal.","rule":"An agent who contracts on behalf of an undisclosed principal is personally liable on the contract.","sub":"Undisclosed Principal","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":44},{"n":125,"scenario":"MEE-121","item":"6 of 11","summary":"Angela bought a used non‑electric cargo bike for $8,000 using her personal funds.","trigger":"Bike purchase.","rule":"Angela exceeded express limits and lacked actual authority to buy used non‑electric bike.","sub":"No Actual Authority – Bike","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":28},{"n":126,"scenario":"MEE-121","item":"7 of 11","summary":"Angela delivered bike to Peter. After two days, Peter decided to keep it and promised to reimburse Angela.","trigger":"Peter keeps bike.","rule":"Ratification is affirmance of a prior unauthorized act, giving it effect as if authorized.","sub":"Ratification","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":28},{"n":127,"scenario":"MEE-121","item":"8 of 11","summary":"By retaining bike with knowledge of facts, Peter ratified Angela’s purchase.","trigger":"Manifest assent.","rule":"Ratification retroactively creates actual authority and obligates principal.","sub":"Effect of Ratification","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":28},{"n":128,"scenario":"MEE-121","item":"9 of 11","summary":"Peter called supplier stating Angela would purchase oven on his behalf.","trigger":"Supplier call.","rule":"Principal manifestations to third party create apparent authority.","sub":"Creation of Apparent Authority","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":38},{"n":129,"scenario":"MEE-121","item":"10 of 11","summary":"Angela signed oven contract 'on behalf of Peter' for $15,000. Market price fair. Peter refused delivery.","trigger":"Pizza oven.","rule":"Principal bound where third party reasonably relies on apparent authority traceable to principal. Here, Angela did not have actual authority because the contract price paid for the oven exceeded $3.000 so Peter can go against her for the difference.","sub":"Apparent Authority – Oven","family":"Agency – Authority & Disclosure","unit":38},{"n":130,"scenario":"MEE-121","item":"11 of 11","summary":"Final outcomes: Peter not bound for chairs; Angela personally liable. Peter must reimburse $8,000 bike. Peter bound to oven contract.","trigger":"Outcome.","rule":"Undisclosed‑principal contracts bind agent; ratification binds principal; apparent authority binds principal.","sub":"Outcome Matrix","family":"Outcome Matrix","unit":null},{"n":131,"scenario":"MEE-127","item":"1 of 11","summary":"Six months ago Andy, Ben, and Carol executed a document titled 'Metropolitan Limited Partnership Agreement.' Each agreed to contribute $500,000. Agreement named Warren as general partner, but Warren never signed.","trigger":"Formation documents.","rule":"A limited partnership requires at least one general partner who actually assents; without a signed general partner agreement, no limited partnership is formed.","sub":"Limited Partnership Formation","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":32},{"n":132,"scenario":"MEE-127","item":"2 of 11","summary":"Agreement provided venture would buy undeveloped land, hold it for development, and resell. Deed named Metropolitan Limited Partnership as grantee. Andy, Ben, and Carol also hired Marketing to promote resale as 'Metropolitan Estates.'","trigger":"Business conduct.","rule":"An association of two or more persons carrying on as co‑owners a business for profit forms a general partnership regardless of intent.","sub":"General Partnership by Conduct","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":32},{"n":133,"scenario":"MEE-127","item":"3 of 11","summary":"Although later a Certificate of Limited Partnership was filed, it occurred only after Marketing sued.","trigger":"Late certificate filing.","rule":"Filing a certificate does not create a limited partnership absent an actual general partner at formation.","sub":"Ineffective Certificate","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":32},{"n":134,"scenario":"MEE-127","item":"4 of 11","summary":"Because Warren never signed and Andy, Ben, and Carol acted as co‑owners, Metropolitan is treated as a general partnership.","trigger":"Entity classification.","rule":"Where statutory LP requirements are unmet, venture defaults to general partnership.","sub":"Entity Status","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":32},{"n":135,"scenario":"MEE-127","item":"5 of 11","summary":"Marketing provided services to MLP. MLP failed to pay. Marketing sued MLP and Andy, Ben, and Carol individually.","trigger":"Marketing debt.","rule":"General partners are jointly and severally liable for partnership obligations.","sub":"Partner Liability – Debts","family":"Partner Liability & Collection","unit":46},{"n":136,"scenario":"MEE-127","item":"6 of 11","summary":"To collect personally, Marketing must first obtain judgment against partnership and partners and attempt execution against partnership assets.","trigger":"Collection procedure.","rule":"A creditor must exhaust partnership assets before enforcing judgment against individual partners unless statute provides otherwise.","sub":"Procedural Steps to Collect","family":"Partner Liability & Collection","unit":46},{"n":137,"scenario":"MEE-127","item":"7 of 11","summary":"Land owned by partnership substantially depreciated due to downturn.","trigger":"Asset insufficiency.","rule":"If partnership assets fail to satisfy judgment, creditor may proceed against partners personally.","sub":"Exhaustion Rule","family":"Partner Liability & Collection","unit":46},{"n":138,"scenario":"MEE-127","item":"8 of 11","summary":"Two weeks after certificate filing, Zack entered property owned by Metropolitan, fell into uncovered well, and died. Estate sued Andy, Ben, and Carol individually for wrongful death.","trigger":"Wrongful death event.","rule":"General partners are jointly and severally liable for torts committed in course of partnership business.","sub":"Partner Tort Liability","family":"Partner Liability & Collection","unit":46},{"n":139,"scenario":"MEE-127","item":"9 of 11","summary":"Zack’s death occurred after certificate filing.","trigger":"Timing issue.","rule":"Later filing does not retroactively create limited partnership or shield partners from liability.","sub":"No Retroactive Protection","family":"Entity Formation & Defaults","unit":45},{"n":140,"scenario":"MEE-127","item":"10 of 11","summary":"Estate seeks recovery from individual partners.","trigger":"Estate claim.","rule":"Tort claimant must follow same procedure: obtain judgment against partnership and partners, attempt collection from partnership first.","sub":"Tort Collection Procedure","family":"Partner Liability & Collection","unit":46},{"n":141,"scenario":"MEE-127","item":"11 of 11","summary":"Final outcomes: Metropolitan is a general partnership; Andy, Ben, and Carol personally liable for Marketing debt and wrongful death; creditors must first pursue partnership assets.","trigger":"Outcome matrix.","rule":"Failure to form LP results in general partnership with joint and several liability; certificate filing too late to protect partners.","sub":"Outcome Matrix","family":"Outcome Matrix","unit":null}],"units":[{"n":1,"scenarios":["MEE-076"],"sequence":"Chain A, items 1–3. Opens the transaction analysis; nothing else in Chain A can be reached until the minority's attempt to block is resolved.","summary":"Two of three shareholders, who together control the board and two-thirds of the voting shares, resolve to merge their close corporation into a new corporation formed in another state. The third shareholder, also a director, votes against and seeks to prevent the merger.","trigger":"Merger of corporations incorporated in different states + approval sought over a dissenting shareholder-director → identify the governing law, then apply the two-tier approval requirement, then determine whether the dissenter can block.","rule":"Each constituent corporation in a merger is subject to the merger requirements of ITS OWN state of incorporation. Under the Model Business Corporation Act a merger requires two approvals: adoption of the plan of merger by a majority of the board of directors, and approval by the shareholders — traditionally a majority of the shares entitled to vote, though the current MBCA default is a majority of the votes cast where a quorum is present, and articles or the board may require a greater vote. A shareholder holding a majority of the voting power may therefore approve a merger over the opposition of a minority holder, and a dissenting minority shareholder has NO power to block a merger that satisfies the statutory majorities. The dissenter's remedy is monetary, not injunctive. Short-form mergers of a 90-percent-owned subsidiary into its parent are an exception requiring no shareholder vote at all.","sub":"Mergers – Approval Requirements and the Power of the Majority","required":"The grader credits three separate statements, not one conclusion: (a) that the law of each corporation's state of incorporation supplies its own merger requirements; (b) the two-tier approval requirement stated as board AND shareholder approval with the vote threshold named; (c) the express conclusion that the dissenter cannot block. Merging these into a single sentence of conclusion forfeits two of the three items.","sensitivity":"Validity turns on a counting exercise, not on fairness: two-thirds of the shares and a board majority satisfy the statute, and the analysis of the minority's position ends there. The sequence is also scored — the governing-law statement must precede the approval calculus, because it is what authorises the use of MBCA thresholds at all. Source: MEE-076 sub-issues 1–3.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the majority cannot impose on the minority a merger that changes the company's purpose — this is abuso de mayoría and the resolution is challengeable” → U.S. corporate law resolves the question by arithmetic and gives the minority a valuation remedy instead of a veto. Venezuelan fusión under the Code of Commerce is built around a shareholders' meeting decision plus publication and a creditor OPPOSITION period, so the trained instinct looks for a right of opposition and for judicial review of the majority's motive — neither of which exists here. The protective mechanism has moved: from opposing the transaction to being paid for exiting it.","authority":"Mergers – Approval Requirements and the Power of the Majority. Model Business Corporation Act §§ 11.02, 11.04, § 11.04(e). Scenario MEE-076."},{"n":2,"scenarios":["MEE-076"],"sequence":"Chain A, items 4–6. Unlocked only by resolving item 3 against the minority: the remedy question arises precisely because the transaction cannot be stopped.","summary":"Having failed to block the merger, the dissenting shareholder of a closely held corporation gives notice, refrains from voting in favour, and demands cash payment of the fair value of his shares instead of shares in the surviving corporation.","trigger":"Shareholder dissents from a merger requiring shareholder approval → appraisal entitlement, subject to the market-out exception, and conditioned on strict procedural compliance.","rule":"A shareholder who dissents from a merger is entitled to APPRAISAL: a judicial determination of the fair value of his shares and payment in cash. The right attaches to transactions requiring shareholder approval, including mergers, and is subject to the MARKET-OUT exception, under which appraisal is unavailable where the shares are publicly traded or held by a large number of holders — so the right is available in a closely held corporation. Preservation of the right depends on three procedural steps, each indispensable: written notice of intent to demand payment BEFORE the vote, refraining from voting in favour of the merger, and a timely written demand for payment after approval. Failure at any step forfeits the remedy, whatever the merits. Fair value is determined without discount for lack of marketability or minority status in most jurisdictions, and appraisal is ordinarily the dissenter's EXCLUSIVE remedy absent fraud or illegality.","sub":"Mergers – Appraisal Rights: Entitlement, Market-Out, and Procedure","required":"Four creditable statements: entitlement to appraisal on dissent; availability because the market-out exception does not reach a close corporation; the three procedural prerequisites enumerated separately; and the exclusivity of the remedy. Candidates who state only 'he is entitled to fair value' collect one item of four — the procedural enumeration is where the points are.","sensitivity":"The right is procedural before it is substantive: notice, abstention and timely demand are conditions precedent, and missing one destroys a claim that is otherwise unanswerable on the merits. The market-out exception is the switch that decides availability, and it turns on the character of the shares rather than on the fairness of the price. Source: MEE-076 sub-issues 4–6.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “he objected on the record and he is being forced out — his right to be paid follows from the objection itself” → in U.S. law the objection is worthless unless it was made in the prescribed form, before the vote, and followed by a timely written demand. The Venezuelan derecho de receso in the cases where the Code of Commerce grants it is exercised by the dissenting shareholder without a comparable schedule of forfeiting steps, so the reflex treats the substantive entitlement as self-executing and omits the procedure — which is simultaneously the item the grader weights most heavily and the only advice that would actually protect the client.","authority":"Mergers – Appraisal Rights: Entitlement, Market-Out, and Procedure. Model Business Corporation Act § 13.02, § 13.02(b), §§ 13.21–13.24. Scenario MEE-076."},{"n":3,"scenarios":["MEE-076"],"sequence":"Chain B, items 7–8. Opens the duties analysis and must be resolved before anything can be said about what the directors were permitted to do.","summary":"The surviving corporation is formed in a second state whose law permits directors to pursue social and environmental purposes, while the state of the merged corporation required directors to maximise shareholder profit. The dissenting shareholder sues the surviving corporation's directors in the first state.","trigger":"Suit over director conduct where the corporation's state of incorporation differs from the forum, or has changed by merger → the internal affairs doctrine selects the governing law mechanically.","rule":"Under the INTERNAL AFFAIRS DOCTRINE, the internal affairs of a corporation — the legal relationships among the corporation, its shareholders, directors and officers, including the existence and content of fiduciary duties — are governed by the law of the STATE OF INCORPORATION, even where the corporation's business, assets and shareholders are located primarily elsewhere and even where suit is brought in another state's courts. The forum applies that law rather than its own. Where a merger changes the state of incorporation, the surviving corporation's state law governs director duties from the effective date forward, so a merger is a lawful means of changing the applicable duty regime. Matters that are NOT internal affairs — torts, contracts with outsiders, securities and employment obligations — remain governed by ordinary choice-of-law rules.","sub":"Choice of Law – The Internal Affairs Doctrine and Reincorporation by Merger","required":"The grader credits the doctrine by name, the identification of the surviving corporation's state as the governing law, and the express recognition that the forum court applies foreign corporate law rather than its own. A candidate who analyses director duties under the forum's law loses not only this item but every item that depends on it — the two benefit-corporation items become unreachable.","sensitivity":"One formula answers the entire question: state of incorporation. No weighing of contacts, no most-significant-relationship analysis, no public-policy override of the forum. The temporal dimension is the second switch — the merger moves the corporation into a new duty regime prospectively, which is the mechanism the fact pattern is built to test. Source: MEE-076 sub-issues 7–8.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the company operates here, its shareholders are here, and the forum's mandatory rules on the duties of administrators cannot be displaced by relocating the seat” → U.S. law hands the question to the state of incorporation by rule, and treats reincorporation by merger as a legitimate way to change the duty regime. A single national commercial code makes the concept unthinkable: in Venezuela there is no menu of internal-affairs regimes to choose from, and the lex societatis principle never has to arbitrate between fifty competing bodies of fiduciary law. This is the deepest misalignment in the essay — not a rule the reader lacks, but a market in corporate law that his system does not have.","authority":"Choice of Law – The Internal Affairs Doctrine and Reincorporation by Merger. Restatement (Second) of Conflict of Laws § 302; Model Business Corporation Act § 11.07. Scenario MEE-076."},{"n":4,"scenarios":["MEE-076"],"sequence":"Chain B, items 9–10. Reachable only after item 8 fixes the surviving state's law as governing.","summary":"The surviving corporation is a benefit corporation whose charter declares environmental purposes. Its directors act consistently with that declared purpose at the expense of shareholder profit, and are sued for it.","trigger":"Directors of a benefit corporation prioritise a declared public benefit over shareholder profit → statutory authorisation and the accompanying liability protection.","rule":"A BENEFIT CORPORATION is a for-profit entity whose charter commits it to one or more declared public benefits. Benefit-corporation statutes alter the default duty: directors are required or permitted to consider the declared public benefit and the interests of stakeholders alongside shareholder value, and they do NOT breach their fiduciary duty by pursuing the declared purpose even at the expense of profit. The statutes ordinarily provide that directors have no duty to any beneficiary of the public purpose, and shield them from monetary liability for decisions that further the declared benefit. Enforcement is normally limited to a benefit enforcement proceeding brought by the corporation or by shareholders meeting an ownership threshold, and the business judgment rule continues to apply to the decisions themselves.","sub":"Benefit Corporations – Permissible Purposes and the Director Liability Shield","required":"Two distinct items: that pursuing the declared benefit is lawful and not a breach, and that the statute shields the directors from liability for it. The second is not a restatement of the first and must be written separately. Naming the business judgment rule as the residual standard adds a creditable line.","sensitivity":"The entity form, chosen at incorporation and declared in the charter, is what makes lawful conduct that would otherwise be a breach. Nothing turns on the wisdom of the decision or on the size of the loss to shareholders — only on whether the conduct furthers the DECLARED purpose. Source: MEE-076 sub-issues 9–10.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “administrators owe their duty to the company and its interés social — sacrificing profit for an environmental agenda is a breach, and the good motive does not excuse it” → the benefit-corporation statute converts that same conduct into compliance. Venezuelan company law offers no vehicle by which a for-profit entity can bind itself in its charter to non-shareholder purposes and thereby immunise its directors; the administrator's duty runs to the company, and stakeholder interests enter only through separate regulatory obligations. There is nothing to translate here — the reader must learn that the form of the entity can rewrite the duty.","authority":"Benefit Corporations – Permissible Purposes and the Director Liability Shield. Model Benefit Corporation Legislation §§ 201, 301, § 305. Scenario MEE-076."},{"n":5,"scenarios":["MEE-026"],"sequence":"Independent theory 1 of 3. Not conditioned on the others and does not condition them.","summary":"A parent entity selects its subsidiary's manager, shares personnel, coordinates operations and receives all of the subsidiary's profits; the press calls them partners. A creditor of the subsidiary argues the two are partners so that the parent is liable.","trigger":"Liability asserted on the ground that two entities are partners → partnership exists only if they carry on as CO-OWNERS of a business for profit; the party alleging it bears the burden.","rule":"A partnership is an association of two or more persons carrying on as CO-OWNERS a business for profit. No writing or intent to form a partnership is required, and the label the parties or third parties use is irrelevant. Sharing of PROFITS raises a presumption of partnership, but the presumption does not apply where the payments are explicable on another basis — and distributions from a subsidiary to its sole member are payments on an ownership interest, not a division of profits between co-owners. Co-ownership is shown by shared control and the sharing of losses, which are indicia and not elements, rather than by mere coordination or common management. The burden of proving that a partnership exists rests on the party asserting it, and neither a press description nor an integrated corporate group satisfies it.","sub":"Partnership Formation – Co-ownership and What Does Not Create It","required":"Three creditable statements: the co-ownership definition; that profit sharing raises a presumption which parent-subsidiary distributions do not trigger; and that the burden lies on the party alleging the partnership. Concluding 'they are not partners' without stating the definition and the burden collects almost nothing.","sensitivity":"The switch is the CHARACTER of the money flow, not its existence: distributions on an ownership interest are not profit sharing between co-owners. Everything else in the fact pattern — shared staff, coordinated activity, the newspaper's language — is deliberately included to be found insufficient. Source: MEE-026 sub-issue 1.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “common management, shared personnel and a single flow of profits reveal a unidad económica — they operate as one enterprise and answer as one” → U.S. partnership law asks only whether they are co-owners carrying on a business for profit, and answers no. The reflex is imported from ANOTHER BRANCH of the reader's own law: in Venezuela the unidad económica and grupo de empresas doctrines are developed principally in LABOUR law, where they attribute liability across affiliated companies on precisely these indicia and are applied vigorously. They are not a general remedy available to a commercial creditor. So the reader holds a doctrine that fits these facts perfectly, is correct in its own field, and is being applied outside it — the most dangerous configuration in the framework, because the error is invisible from inside the reasoning.","authority":"Partnership Formation – Co-ownership and What Does Not Create It. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 202(a), § 202(c). Scenario MEE-026."},{"n":6,"scenarios":["MEE-026"],"sequence":"Independent theory 2 of 3. Turns on one formal fact and is unaffected by the outcome of theories 1 and 3.","summary":"The subsidiary's supply contract was signed by the parent's own manager, who was visiting the subsidiary's office and signed expressly as agent of the subsidiary. The supplier argues that the parent is bound because its manager signed.","trigger":"Contract signed by a person who is an agent of one entity while purporting to act for another → the principal is bound only where the agent acted with actual or apparent authority ON THAT PRINCIPAL'S BEHALF.","rule":"A principal is bound by a contract only where the agent had ACTUAL authority — express or implied from the principal's manifestations to the agent — or APPARENT authority, arising from the principal's manifestations to the third party such that the third party reasonably believes the agent acts for that principal. The third party's belief must be traceable to the principal and cannot rest on the agent's own assertion of authority, though the principal's manifestation may be indirect — as where he places the agent in a position that ordinarily carries such authority. Where an individual signs expressly as agent OF ANOTHER ENTITY, he manifests that he acts on that entity's behalf, and the contract binds that entity alone; the fact that he also holds a position with a different entity does not make the latter a party. An agent who acts without authority binds himself, not the purported principal, and a principal not bound may nonetheless become liable by RATIFICATION.","sub":"Agency – Actual and Apparent Authority to Bind a Principal","required":"The grader needs both branches named and disposed of — actual authority and apparent authority — plus the point that apparent authority requires a manifestation by the PRINCIPAL rather than by the agent. Answering only that 'he signed for the subsidiary' states the conclusion and skips the analysis that earns the credit.","sensitivity":"One formal fact controls: the capacity stated in the signature. It defeats both branches at once, because it negates actual authority to bind the parent and simultaneously prevents any reasonable belief that the parent was the contracting party. Source: MEE-026 sub-issue 2.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the person who signed is the parent's own administrator, so the parent is bound — the representative's acts reach the represented” → U.S. agency law asks on WHOSE behalf the agent purported to act, and the answer is settled by the stated capacity. Civilian representation is organised around the powers a representative holds (mandato, poder, representación orgánica), so the trained question is 'what could he do' rather than 'for whom was he acting' — and apparent authority, which requires a manifestation traceable to the principal, has no direct codal counterpart.","authority":"Agency – Actual and Apparent Authority to Bind a Principal. Restatement (Third) of Agency §§ 2.01, 2.03, § 3.03, § 6.01. Scenario MEE-026."},{"n":7,"scenarios":["MEE-026","MEE-053"],"sequence":"MEE-026: independent theory 3 of 3. MEE-053: items 6–7, reached only after the derivative and dissolution questions are resolved.","summary":"A creditor of an insolvent subsidiary asks the court to disregard its separate existence given the parent's control and coordination, though finances were separate and formalities observed. Elsewhere a minority member asks that a fifteen-million-dollar shortfall be allocated to the controlling member, with no evidence of fraud or undercapitalisation.","trigger":"Claimant seeks to reach a member or parent for the entity's obligations → limited liability is the rule; disregarding it requires domination PLUS fraud or injustice.","rule":"Members and managers of a limited liability company are not personally liable for the company's obligations; that is the defining consequence of the form, and it is not displaced merely because losses exceed the entity's assets or because profits were shared. A court will disregard the entity only on a showing of BOTH domination or complete control AND that the form was used to perpetrate fraud or work an injustice. Control, common management, shared personnel, coordinated operations and the receipt of distributions are insufficient standing alone. The recognised grounds are commingling of funds, failure to observe formalities, undercapitalisation at formation, and use of the entity to evade an obligation or defraud creditors — and the absence of those facts is dispositive however large the unsatisfied judgment.","sub":"Limited Liability and Veil Piercing – The Domination-Plus Requirement","required":"The grader credits limited liability as the stated rule, the TWO-part test named as two parts, and the express finding that the second part is unmet on these facts. A candidate who lists the indicia of control and concludes that the veil should be pierced has argued the wrong half of the test.","sensitivity":"The second element does the work, and the fact patterns are engineered so that the first is abundantly satisfied and the second is absent. Separate finances, observed formalities and the cessation of distributions when the entity weakened are affirmative evidence AGAINST injustice, not neutral background. Source: MEE-026 sub-issue 3; MEE-053 sub-issues 6–7.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the controller dominated the entity and the creditor is left unpaid — that is abuse of the corporate form, and the veil falls” → U.S. law treats domination as necessary but never sufficient, and requires an independent finding of fraud or injustice. Two things feed the reflex. The civilian levantamiento del velo does operate substantially on control and economic reality. And alongside it sits the unidad económica doctrine of LABOUR law, which reaches affiliated companies on indicia of common control and is applied far more readily than any commercial piercing remedy — so the reader's most vivid experience of disregarding an entity comes from a field where the threshold is genuinely lower. An unpaid creditor of an insolvent affiliate therefore reads as the paradigm case for piercing, which is why this doctrine generates over-conviction of the parent in both scenarios where it appears.","authority":"Limited Liability and Veil Piercing – The Domination-Plus Requirement. Uniform Limited Liability Company Act (2006, last amended 2013) § 304; common-law veil-piercing doctrine. Scenario MEE-026, MEE-053."},{"n":8,"scenarios":["MEE-043"],"sequence":"Item 1 of 3. Opens the chain: the information it yields is what makes the later derivative analysis possible.","summary":"A shareholder reads a press report alleging that the corporation bribed foreign officials. She asks to inspect board minutes and accounting records in order to decide whether to sue the directors. The board refuses.","trigger":"Shareholder demands inspection of corporate books and records → the right turns on a PROPER PURPOSE reasonably related to her interest as a shareholder, and on the scope of the request.","rule":"A shareholder has a statutory right to inspect corporate books and records on written demand stating a PROPER PURPOSE — one reasonably related to her interest as a shareholder. Investigating suspected mismanagement, waste, self-dealing or illegal corporate conduct is a proper purpose, and a credible basis such as a substantiated press report suffices; the shareholder need not prove wrongdoing in advance, and deciding whether to bring suit is itself a recognised purpose. The demand must be reasonable in SCOPE, confined to records essential to the stated purpose, and the corporation may impose confidentiality conditions. Basic records such as the articles, bylaws and minutes of shareholder meetings are available on demand without more; accounting records and board minutes require the proper-purpose showing. A purpose that is purely personal, or aimed at harassment or at aiding a competitor, is improper, and the corporation bears the burden of proving impropriety once a proper purpose is stated.","sub":"Shareholder Inspection Rights – Proper Purpose and Scope","required":"Three creditable statements: that inspection requires a proper purpose reasonably related to shareholder status; that investigating possible director misconduct or illegality IS such a purpose on a credible basis; and that the request must be reasonable in scope. Concluding that 'she may inspect' without naming the proper-purpose standard forfeits most of the item.","sensitivity":"The switch is the PURPOSE, not the strength of the underlying allegation: a credible press report is enough to make the purpose proper, and the shareholder is not required to establish the wrongdoing she seeks to investigate. Scope is the second, independent limit — a proper purpose does not open the whole corporate archive. Source: MEE-043 sub-issue 1.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the derecho de información lets a shareholder examine the books in the fifteen days before the general meeting — outside that window, and for an investigation of her own, she has no entitlement” → U.S. law grants an inspection right exercisable at any time, whose breadth expands with the purpose asserted. The Venezuelan right is narrow and tied to the meeting calendar, so the trained reflex denies a request the U.S. system grants, and has no concept of a purpose-indexed entitlement that widens precisely because misconduct is suspected.","authority":"Shareholder Inspection Rights – Proper Purpose and Scope. Model Business Corporation Act §§ 16.01–16.04, § 16.02(c). Scenario MEE-043."},{"n":9,"scenarios":["MEE-043","MEE-053"],"sequence":"MEE-043: item 2 of 3, presupposing the information obtained at item 1 and defeated only by item 3. MEE-053: item 3 of 7, unlocked by the breach found at item 2 and standing against the direct action at item 4.","summary":"A board refuses a shareholder's demand to investigate bribery allegations without making any inquiry. Elsewhere a controlling member of an LLC refuses the minority's demand that the company sue him for supplying defective material.","trigger":"Claim belonging to the entity that those in control will not pursue → derivative action, subject to the demand requirement and its futility exception, and to the effect of a refusal.","rule":"A DERIVATIVE action is brought by a shareholder or member to enforce a claim belonging to the ENTITY, with any recovery running to the entity. The plaintiff must have been an owner at the time of the wrong and remain one throughout, must make a written DEMAND on those in control, and must ordinarily wait ninety days unless irreparable injury would result. Demand is EXCUSED as futile where those who would decide are themselves the wrongdoers or are not disinterested — as where the controlling member is the proposed defendant. Where demand is refused, dismissal follows only if the refusal was made in GOOD FAITH after a REASONABLE INVESTIGATION by disinterested decision-makers; a refusal reached without any inquiry earns no deference and does not bar the suit. Court approval is required for settlement or dismissal, and a successful plaintiff may recover expenses from the entity.","sub":"Derivative Actions – Demand, Futility, and the Effect of Refusal","required":"Four items: the derivative character of the claim and that recovery runs to the entity; the demand requirement; futility where the decision-makers are the wrongdoers; and — the one most often missed — that a refusal without investigation is not entitled to deference. Each must be written as its own proposition.","sensitivity":"Two independent routes past the board: futility before demand, or an unreasoned refusal after it. The fact patterns supply one of each, and the second is the subtler — the board's stated business justification is irrelevant BECAUSE no inquiry preceded it. Source: MEE-043 sub-issue 2; MEE-053 sub-issue 3.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the action against the administrators belongs to the company and only the shareholders' meeting can resolve to bring it — a single shareholder cannot litigate the company's claim over the board's objection” → the derivative action does exactly that. The reflex is a correct statement of Venezuelan law: the Code of Commerce channels the acción social de responsabilidad through the assembly (art. 310), and the minority's protection lies not in standing to sue on the company's behalf but in a denunciation of grave irregularities to the commercial judge (art. 291), available to shareholders representing a fifth of the capital. So the reader is not ignorant of a remedy — he holds a different one, judicial and supervisory rather than representative. He therefore reaches for the wrong instrument, and misses the doctrine that strips the board of deference precisely because it failed to investigate.","authority":"Derivative Actions – Demand, Futility, and the Effect of Refusal. Model Business Corporation Act §§ 7.42, 7.44. Scenario MEE-043, MEE-053."},{"n":10,"scenarios":["MEE-043"],"sequence":"Item 3 of 3. Answers the board's defence to item 2; without it the refusal analysis is incomplete.","summary":"Directors decline to investigate or remediate credible allegations that the corporation bribed foreign officials, believing the conduct profitable for the corporation.","trigger":"Directors consciously disregard credible evidence of illegal conduct → the business judgment rule does not protect the decision, because good faith is a precondition of the rule rather than a matter it excuses.","rule":"The BUSINESS JUDGMENT RULE presumes that in making a decision directors acted on an informed basis, in good faith, and in the honest belief that the action was in the corporation's best interests; a court will not review the merits or second-guess the wisdom of a protected decision. The presumption is REBUTTED where any of its conditions fails — where the decision was uninformed, tainted by self-interest, or not taken in good faith. Directors act in bad faith, and outside the rule, when they consciously disregard known illegal conduct or fail to act in the face of a known duty, and no belief that illegality serves the corporation financially can supply good faith: a decision to violate the law is not a protected exercise of business judgment. Sustained inattention to a compliance system — an utter failure to implement or monitor reporting controls — is a related bad-faith failure of oversight.","sub":"Duty of Good Faith – Conscious Disregard of Illegality and the Limits of the Business Judgment Rule","required":"Two propositions must both appear: the rule and its three conditions, and the specific holding that conscious disregard of illegality defeats good faith and therefore the rule. Writing only that the directors 'breached their duty' skips the framework the item is testing.","sensitivity":"Good faith is a CONDITION of the protection, not a defence within it — which is why profitability is irrelevant. The structural consequence is that the same facts defeat the board's litigation defence in Unit 9: a refusal grounded in a decision that the rule never protected cannot itself claim deference. Source: MEE-043 sub-issue 3.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “administrators answer for damage caused by conduct contrary to law — illegality is never excused by profit” → that conclusion is right, and it is reached without the framework the examination is testing. The Venezuelan administrator is judged against a standard of diligence with courts free to review the merits, so there is no deference rule to rebut: the reader neither invokes the business judgment rule where it protects a client nor recognises that the scored point here is the narrow bad-faith exception to it. The error is invisible because the outcome is correct.","authority":"Duty of Good Faith – Conscious Disregard of Illegality and the Limits of the Business Judgment Rule. Model Business Corporation Act § 8.30(a), § 8.31(a)(2). Scenario MEE-043."},{"n":11,"scenarios":["MEE-053"],"sequence":"Items 1–2 of 7. Opens the chain: the duty must exist before the blocking can be a breach, and the breach is what unlocks every remedy that follows.","summary":"A member holding 55 per cent of the voting interest and management control of a member-managed LLC also supplies its raw material. His defective supply destroys the company's product, and he then uses his control to prevent the company from suing him.","trigger":"Controlling member of a member-managed LLC acts on both sides of a matter → fiduciary duties of loyalty and care are owed to the company and to the other members, and blocking the company's claim against himself breaches loyalty.","rule":"In a MEMBER-MANAGED limited liability company each member owes the company and the other members the fiduciary duty of LOYALTY — to account for benefits derived from the company's activities, to refrain from dealing with the company as or on behalf of an adverse party, and to refrain from competing — together with a duty of CARE, ordinarily framed as refraining from grossly negligent or reckless conduct, intentional misconduct or knowing violation of law. Members also owe a contractual obligation of good faith and fair dealing. A controlling member who uses his control to prevent the company from pursuing a valuable claim AGAINST HIMSELF breaches the duty of loyalty, because the decision confers an improper personal benefit at the company's expense; the business judgment rule does not shelter a self-interested decision. The operating agreement may limit or alter fiduciary duties within statutory bounds, but may not eliminate the obligation of good faith and fair dealing.","sub":"LLC Fiduciary Duties – Loyalty, Care, and the Self-Interested Controller","required":"Two items, stated separately: that members of a member-managed LLC owe loyalty and care to the company and to each other, and that blocking the company's claim against oneself is a breach of loyalty because it secures a personal benefit. A conclusion that the controller 'acted improperly' without naming the duty and the benefit collects one at most.","sensitivity":"Management structure is the threshold fact: in a member-managed company the duty attaches to the MEMBER, whereas in a manager-managed company it attaches to the manager and non-managing members owe far less. The breach then turns on the personal benefit rather than on the size of the company's loss. Source: MEE-053 sub-issues 1–2.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT — a controller who deals with the company on both sides and then shields himself is in conflict of interest, and the duty of loyalty is familiar territory. The calibration friction is where the duty comes from: in U.S. law it attaches to the MEMBER by reason of the management structure and can be reshaped by the operating agreement, whereas the civilian locates the duty in the office of administrator and treats it as a matter of mandatory law. The reader will find the breach and may then misidentify who owed the duty in a manager-managed company.","authority":"LLC Fiduciary Duties – Loyalty, Care, and the Self-Interested Controller. Uniform Limited Liability Company Act (2006, last amended 2013) § 409. Scenario MEE-053."},{"n":12,"scenarios":["MEE-053"],"sequence":"Item 4 of 7. Stands directly against Unit 9: the direct action is the wrong vehicle for the same wrong, and both must be addressed.","summary":"The minority member, having been refused, sues the controlling member directly to recover the fifteen-million-dollar loss suffered by the company.","trigger":"Member sues in his own name for a loss sustained by the entity → the direct action is barred unless the member suffered an injury DISTINCT from the entity's.","rule":"A member may not bring a DIRECT action to recover for an injury suffered by the entity; such a claim is derivative, and any recovery belongs to the entity. A direct action lies only where the member suffered a harm SEPARATE AND DISTINCT from the harm to the entity and to the other owners — for example denial of inspection or voting rights, breach of an obligation owed to him personally, or wrongful exclusion. Diminution in the value of an ownership interest caused by injury to the entity is the paradigm derivative harm and does not become direct because the member is the only one economically affected. Some jurisdictions relax the rule for closely held entities and permit a direct action where there is no risk of prejudice to creditors or other members, but that is an exception to be identified as such rather than the rule.","sub":"Direct versus Derivative Claims – Whose Injury","required":"The grader credits the rule that entity injury must be pursued derivatively, the distinct-injury exception stated as the test, and the express application concluding that a diminished interest is not a distinct injury. Answering the merits of the controller's conduct without addressing the form of the action loses the item entirely.","sensitivity":"The switch is WHOSE injury, not who is out of pocket: the minority member's economic loss is real and still derivative, because it flows through the entity. Recognising the closely-held-entity exception adds a creditable line but does not displace the rule. Source: MEE-053 sub-issue 4.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “he suffered the loss, so he sues for it — whoever bears the damage has the action” → U.S. law channels the claim through the entity and denies the owner standing to recover his own economic loss. The civilian distinction between daño a la sociedad and daño al socio exists, but without a derivative mechanism the practical consequence is unfamiliar: the correct advice is to sue on behalf of the company he does not control, rather than in his own name for the money he actually lost.","authority":"Direct versus Derivative Claims – Whose Injury. Uniform Limited Liability Company Act (2006, last amended 2013) §§ 901–902. Scenario MEE-053."},{"n":13,"scenarios":["MEE-053"],"sequence":"Item 5 of 7. Independent of the litigation remedies: available even where the derivative action succeeds, and the remedy most often omitted.","summary":"The minority member seeks judicial dissolution of the LLC on the basis of the controller's conduct in supplying defective material and then shielding himself from the company's claim.","trigger":"Member petitions to dissolve the entity → judicial dissolution is available where those in control act oppressively, illegally or fraudulently, or where it is not reasonably practicable to carry on the business.","rule":"A court may order the JUDICIAL DISSOLUTION of a limited liability company on a member's application where the managers or controlling members have acted, are acting, or will act in a manner that is illegal, fraudulent or OPPRESSIVE and directly harmful to the applicant, or where it is not reasonably practicable to carry on the company's activities in conformity with the operating agreement. Oppression is conduct that defeats the reasonable expectations of the minority — exclusion from management or information, diversion of value, or the use of control to entrench a personal advantage. Dissolution is discretionary and equitable: courts may award a lesser remedy where one suffices, including a buy-out of the applicant's interest, an accounting, or the appointment of a custodian or receiver, and deadlock alone may satisfy the not-reasonably-practicable ground without any misconduct.","sub":"Judicial Dissolution – Oppression and the Not-Reasonably-Practicable Ground","required":"Two items: the statutory grounds named, and the application concluding that using control to block the company's claim while profiting from the defective supply is oppressive and directly harmful. Noting that the court may grant a lesser remedy such as a buy-out earns an additional line.","sensitivity":"Two independent grounds, and the second requires no wrongdoing at all. The discretionary character is the second dial: the question is not only whether the ground exists but whether dissolution is the proportionate response, which is why the buy-out alternative belongs in the answer. Source: MEE-053 sub-issue 5.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “dissolution is for the causes listed in the statute and the articles — expiry of the term, impossibility of the object, loss of capital; a court does not dissolve a solvent, functioning company because the majority behaved badly” → U.S. law makes oppression of the minority itself a ground, and puts a buy-out in the court's hands as a lesser remedy. Venezuelan minority protection runs through the comisario and a judicial complaint of grave irregularities, which can produce inspection or the convening of an assembly but not the unwinding of the company or a forced purchase of the minority's stake. This is consequently the remedy the reader is most likely to omit, on facts that support it plainly.","authority":"Judicial Dissolution – Oppression and the Not-Reasonably-Practicable Ground. Uniform Limited Liability Company Act (2006, last amended 2013) § 701(a)(4). Scenario MEE-053."},{"n":14,"scenarios":["MEE-066"],"sequence":"Items 1–2 of 7. Opens the chain: the vote must qualify as valid board action before its conflicted character can be assessed, and the category must be named before any cleansing procedure becomes relevant.","summary":"Two of the three directors of a close corporation, who are also its shareholders, vote at a board meeting to have the corporation pay for a trip abroad from which they personally benefit. No disinterested director participates in the decision.","trigger":"Board approves a transaction in which the voting directors themselves have a material financial interest.","rule":"All corporate powers are exercised by or under the authority of the BOARD, which acts by majority vote of the directors present at a meeting at which a quorum exists (MBCA §§ 8.01, 8.20, 8.24). Board action is therefore procedurally valid even where its substance is objectionable, and the two questions are separate. A DIRECTOR'S CONFLICTING-INTEREST TRANSACTION is one to which the director, or a person related to him, is a party or in which he has a material financial interest of which he knows (MBCA § 8.60). The category is defined by the interest itself, not by unfairness, secrecy or loss: a conflicting-interest transaction is not unlawful and may be entirely beneficial to the corporation. What the interest triggers is a duty of DISCLOSURE to the other directors and the availability of a validating procedure; it does not disqualify the transaction, and the presence of an interest is the beginning of the analysis rather than its conclusion.","sub":"Board Action and the Director's Conflicting-Interest Transaction","required":"Two propositions must appear separately. First, that corporate powers are exercised by the board acting by majority vote with a quorum — stating that the vote was validly taken as board action. Second, that the transaction is a director's conflicting-interest transaction BECAUSE the voting directors had a material financial interest in it, with the interest identified. Writing only that the directors 'improperly used corporate funds' collapses both items into a conclusion and earns neither: the grader is looking for the category to be named before its consequences are argued.","sensitivity":"The switch is the presence of a material financial interest known to the director, not the fairness or the amount of the expenditure. A transaction of identical substance approved by directors with no personal stake is ordinary board action requiring no further justification; the same transaction approved by the beneficiaries is a conflicting-interest transaction from the moment it is voted. Naming the category is what makes the next four items reachable — without it there is no procedure to have skipped and no burden to have shifted. Source: MEE-066 sub-issues 1–2.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the administrator with an interest opposed to the company's must declare it and abstain from intervening — they voted on their own benefit, so the vote is tainted and the decision void” → U.S. law treats the interest as triggering a procedure, not as invalidating the vote: the board acted, the action stands, and the conflict opens a separate inquiry into whether it was cleansed or is fair. Venezuelan law states the duty in the form of an abstention obligation, so the trained reader reaches for nullity of the resolution and stops there. The term 'conflict of interest' translates perfectly and the operative consequence does not — which is why this unit is a false cognate rather than a void: the reader recognises the situation and misidentifies what follows from it.","authority":"Board Action and the Director's Conflicting-Interest Transaction. Model Business Corporation Act §§ 8.01, § 8.60. Scenario MEE-066."},{"n":15,"scenarios":["MEE-066"],"sequence":"Items 3–5 of 7. Reachable only once Unit 14 has named the conflicting interest. Item 4 exists because no qualified approval was obtained, and item 5 exists only because item 4 shifted the burden.","summary":"No disinterested director or shareholder approved the expenditure; the objecting shareholder withheld approval and threatened suit, and the two interested directors proceeded regardless. They later argue that the trip served a legitimate competitive purpose and was therefore fair to the corporation.","trigger":"A conflicting-interest transaction proceeds without approval by disinterested directors or shareholders.","rule":"A director's conflicting-interest transaction is INSULATED from challenge on that ground if it is approved, after required disclosure, by a majority of QUALIFIED DIRECTORS — those with no interest in the transaction — or by holders of a majority of the qualified shares (MBCA §§ 8.61–8.63). That approval is a safe harbour: it makes the transaction unassailable for conflict, and the analysis ends. Where no such approval exists, the transaction is not thereby void; instead the BURDEN shifts to the interested directors to prove that the transaction, judged according to the circumstances at the relevant time, was FAIR to the corporation (MBCA § 8.61(b)(3)). Fairness is assessed substantively — the purpose of the transaction, the reasonableness of the terms and amount, and whether the corporation received value commensurate with what it paid — and a mixed personal and corporate benefit does not defeat fairness where the corporate purpose is genuine and the cost proportionate to it. The defence is available but is theirs to establish.","sub":"Safe Harbour, Burden Shift, and the Substantive Fairness Defence","required":"Three creditable propositions, each stated as its own step. That approval by qualified (disinterested) directors or shareholders after disclosure would have insulated the transaction — stated as the rule even though it did not happen here, because its absence is what produces the next step. That, no such approval having been obtained, the BURDEN OF PROOF rests on the interested directors rather than on the challenger. And that the transaction may still be upheld if they prove substantive fairness, with the fairness factors named. An answer concluding that the directors breached the duty of loyalty, without stating the safe harbour they failed to use or identifying who must prove what, forfeits the two items that carry the analysis.","sensitivity":"The switch is the ABSENCE of qualified approval, and its effect is evidentiary rather than substantive: it does not condemn the transaction, it relocates the burden. Had one disinterested director approved after disclosure, the same expenditure would be beyond challenge and items 4 and 5 would not exist. The second switch is that fairness survives the shift — the interested directors are not without a defence, and identifying the defence is part of the credited answer even where it is likely to fail on these facts. Source: MEE-066 sub-issues 3–5.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the administrators acted in their own interest against the company's, so they must indemnify it; whether the trip had a business side is a question of evidence to be weighed by the judge” → U.S. law provides a procedure that would have made the identical transaction lawful in advance, and its omission is what determines who must prove fairness. Venezuelan law has no cleansing mechanism of this kind: liability is assessed against a standard of diligence with the burden on the claimant and the court free to weigh the merits, so there is nothing in the domestic apparatus that corresponds to a safe harbour or to a burden that shifts because a procedure was skipped. The reader consequently argues fairness — the correct battleground — while omitting the two rules that explain why he is standing on it, and would also fail to advise a client to obtain the disinterested approval that would have avoided the dispute entirely.","authority":"Safe Harbour, Burden Shift, and the Substantive Fairness Defence. Model Business Corporation Act §§ 8.61–8.63, § 8.61(b)(3). Scenario MEE-066."},{"n":16,"scenarios":["MEE-066"],"sequence":"Items 6–7 of 7. Independent of the merits and of Units 14–15: these two items dispose of the claim's form and of part of its subject matter however the transaction is ultimately judged.","summary":"The objecting shareholder seeks to recover the travel expenses personally from the two directors, and also seeks recovery for earlier trips of the same kind taken before she acquired her shares.","trigger":"Shareholder sues in her own name for a corporate loss, and challenges conduct predating her ownership.","rule":"A claim for breach of fiduciary duty causing harm to the CORPORATION belongs to the corporation and must be brought DERIVATIVELY; any recovery runs to the corporation and not to the shareholder who sued (MBCA §§ 7.40–7.47). A shareholder may not recover personally for a diminution in the value of her shares caused by injury to the corporation, and the rule holds even in a close corporation where she is the only shareholder economically affected. Separately, standing requires CONTEMPORANEOUS OWNERSHIP (MBCA § 7.41): the plaintiff must have been a shareholder at the time of the act or omission complained of, and must remain one throughout the litigation. A shareholder therefore has no standing to challenge conduct that occurred before she acquired her shares, however similar that conduct is to conduct she may properly challenge, and that portion of the claim fails on standing without any examination of its merits.","sub":"Derivative Recovery and Contemporaneous Ownership Standing","required":"Two items, and the second is the one most often lost. That the harm is the corporation's, so the action must be derivative and any recovery belongs to the corporation — stating expressly that she cannot recover the expenses personally. And that she lacks standing as to the earlier trips because she did not own shares when they occurred, naming the contemporaneous-ownership requirement. An answer that argues the directors' liability for all the trips together, however well, loses both items: the grader is crediting the recognition that part of this claim fails on form and part on timing, neither being a question about the directors' conduct.","sensitivity":"Two switches, both formal and neither about the merits. WHOSE injury: the shareholder's economic loss is genuine and still derivative because it flows through the corporation, so the remedy she wants — payment to herself — is unavailable on any view of the facts. WHEN she acquired the shares: standing is fixed at the date of the conduct, so the earlier trips are outside her reach even though they are indistinguishable in substance from the trip she may challenge. Source: MEE-066 sub-issues 6–7.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “she is a shareholder now, the same wrong was repeated over several years, and the harm to her holding is continuing — so she may claim for all of it” → U.S. law fixes standing at the moment of the conduct, and the earlier trips are lost for that reason alone. This is a timing bar rather than a doctrinal one, which is precisely why the trained reader does not look for it: the civilian minority shareholder's protection is exercised through the assembly and through a judicial complaint of grave irregularities, remedies that address the state of affairs he finds on acquiring his position rather than only the acts committed after it. The reader therefore writes a competent argument on the merits of every trip and is scored as having missed the item that disposes of half the claim.","authority":"Derivative Recovery and Contemporaneous Ownership Standing. Model Business Corporation Act §§ 7.40–7.47, § 7.41. Scenario MEE-066."},{"n":17,"scenarios":["MEE-027"],"sequence":"Item 1 of 5. The first of three alternative characterisations. It must be resolved before item 2, which exists only if this one succeeds.","summary":"A woman owns and operates a food-truck business and engages a man to buy produce and deliver it to her. He is paid a share of the profits as his compensation, but she exercises no control over how he works and the business is not held out as jointly owned.","trigger":"Profit-sharing without co-ownership or shared control of the business.","rule":"A partnership is the association of two or more persons to carry on as CO-OWNERS a business for profit, whether or not they intend to form a partnership (UPA § 202(a)). The controlling indicia are co-ownership and the sharing of CONTROL over the business — a right to participate in management — together with the sharing of profits AND losses. Profit-sharing raises a presumption of partnership, but the statute expressly excludes payments received as COMPENSATION for services or as rent, interest, or repayment of a debt (UPA § 202(c)(3)). A person paid out of profits for his labour is therefore not a partner, and subjective intent neither creates a partnership where the elements are absent nor prevents one where they are present. The absence of shared control is ordinarily decisive.","sub":"Partnership Existence – Profit-Sharing as Compensation","required":"The definition must be stated with CO-OWNERSHIP and shared control as its elements, and the statutory exception for profits received as compensation for services must be named expressly and applied to these facts. A conclusion that 'they were not partners because there was no intention to form a partnership' misstates the law, since intent is not the test, and earns nothing. The credited answer says: profit-sharing would ordinarily suggest a partnership, this profit-sharing was compensation, and there was no shared control — therefore no partnership.","sensitivity":"The switch is the CHARACTER of the profit payment, not its existence or size. The same percentage of the same profits paid to a person who also shares losses and participates in management produces a partnership; paid as the price of his services it produces an employment or agency relationship and nothing more. Control is the confirming element: where the payer directs nothing, co-ownership is absent whatever the parties call themselves. Source: MEE-027 sub-issue 1.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “they agreed to share the profits of a common business, each contributed — she capital, he industry — and that is a sociedad; the socio industrial who contributes labour is a partner in every civil-law system” → U.S. law asks whether he CO-OWNS the business and shares control, and expressly excludes profits paid for services. The trap is that the civilian category of socio industrial makes the very fact pattern that defeats partnership here — labour paid out of profits — the paradigm of partnership there. The reader therefore finds a partnership on the strongest possible domestic reasoning, and in doing so answers item 1 wrongly while making items 3 to 5 unreachable, because he has no remaining question about the relationship.","authority":"Partnership Existence – Profit-Sharing as Compensation. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 202(a), § 202(c)(3). Scenario MEE-027."},{"n":18,"scenarios":["MEE-027"],"sequence":"Item 2 of 5. Reached only on the assumption that item 1 succeeded; it must be written in the alternative even after concluding that no partnership existed.","summary":"Assuming the two were partners, one of them negligently damages a third party's property while driving to make a delivery of produce for the business.","trigger":"A partner commits a tort while acting in the ordinary course of the partnership's business.","rule":"A partnership is liable for loss or injury caused by the wrongful act or omission of a partner acting in the ORDINARY COURSE of the partnership's business or with the authority of the partnership (UPA § 305(a)). All partners are liable JOINTLY AND SEVERALLY for the obligations of the partnership, including those arising in tort (UPA § 306(a)), so a claimant may proceed against any partner for the whole, subject to the requirement that partnership assets be exhausted before a judgment creditor may levy on a partner's individual assets (UPA § 307(d)). Purchasing and transporting supplies for the business is ordinary-course activity, and the partner's own negligence makes him liable directly as well as through the partnership.","sub":"Partner Tort Liability in the Ordinary Course","required":"Two elements must be stated: that the tort was committed in the ORDINARY COURSE of the partnership's business, applied to the delivery, and that partners are liable JOINTLY AND SEVERALLY for it — the phrase itself being what the grader credits. The item is also lost by omission rather than by error: a candidate who concludes at item 1 that there was no partnership and therefore says nothing here loses the whole item, because the examination requires the alternative to be written out.","sensitivity":"The switch is whether the act falls within the ordinary course of the business, which is what connects the individual tort to the other partner's personal assets. An identical accident while running a private errand would leave the partnership untouched. The second and more procedural switch is that the alternative must be ARGUED even where the primary characterisation was rejected — on an essay the conditional analysis is itself the scored unit. Source: MEE-027 sub-issue 2.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT — partners in a sociedad en nombre colectivo answer personally, unlimitedly and solidarily for the obligations of the partnership, including those arising from a partner's fault in the conduct of the business, and the reader will state the rule and reach the correct result without assistance. The calibration friction is procedural rather than substantive: the domestic rule is stated as unlimited solidary liability, while the U.S. formulation adds the exhaustion requirement — partnership assets first, then individual assets — which the reader may omit as a technicality. The greater risk here is not misunderstanding the rule but declining to write it, having concluded at item 1 that no partnership existed; the doctrine is familiar and the loss comes from essay form.","authority":"Partner Tort Liability in the Ordinary Course. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 305(a), § 306(a), § 307(d). Scenario MEE-027."},{"n":19,"scenarios":["MEE-027"],"sequence":"Items 3–5 of 5. The second and third alternative characterisations. Item 4 follows only if he is an employee; item 5 is the residual answer if he is neither partner nor employee, and closes the question.","summary":"The man selects his own vendors, uses his own vehicle and his own credit card, is paid per delivery, and is subject to no direction as to how the work is done. He causes the accident while engaged on the errand for which he was engaged.","trigger":"An agent causes harm while the principal exercises no control over the manner and means of the work.","rule":"An EMPLOYEE is one whose principal has the right to control the MANNER AND MEANS of performance; where that right is absent the person is an independent contractor, and the factors are the supplying of tools and workplace, the method of payment, the distinct nature of the occupation, the degree of skill, and the right to direct how the task is done rather than what is to be achieved (Restatement (Third) of Agency § 7.07(3)(a)). Where the person IS an employee, the principal is vicariously liable under RESPONDEAT SUPERIOR for torts committed within the SCOPE of employment (Restatement (Third) of Agency §§ 2.04, 7.07(1)–(2)). Where the person is a NON-EMPLOYEE AGENT — an independent contractor — the principal is NOT vicariously liable for his negligence, subject to recognised exceptions for non-delegable duties, inherently dangerous activity, negligent selection of the contractor, and torts the principal authorised or ratified. The characterisation therefore selects the regime, and only one of the two applies.","sub":"Employee versus Independent Contractor – Control over Manner and Means","required":"Three propositions, written as three. The CONTROL test over the manner and means of the work, applied to the facts that he chose vendors, used his own car and card and was paid per delivery. That an employee's tort within the scope of employment would bind the principal under respondeat superior — stated in the alternative. And that a principal is NOT vicariously liable for the negligence of an independent contractor, with at least one recognised exception named. Concluding that 'she is liable because he was working for her' answers none of the three: the grader is crediting the test, then each regime in turn.","sensitivity":"The switch is the right to control HOW the work is done, not whether the work was done for the principal's benefit or on her instructions. The same errand, the same accident and the same commercial purpose produce liability if she could direct his method and none if she could only specify the result. This is the pivot of the whole scenario: it is the third time the same facts are characterised, and it is the characterisation that determines whether the injured third party recovers from her at all. Source: MEE-027 sub-issues 3–5.","archetype":"Dual-system architecture","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the owner or principal answers for the damage caused by his servants and dependants in the exercise of the functions entrusted to them — he was carrying out the task she entrusted, so she answers” → U.S. law first sorts the relationship into one of two regimes and then applies the rule belonging to that regime, and one of the two regimes confers immunity. Venezuelan law reaches liability through a single channel of responsibility for dependants, with the independent contractor a weakly developed exclusion rather than a structural alternative. The reader therefore does not perceive that a threshold test is selecting between two mutually exclusive systems, and writes the liability rule without the gate that decides whether it applies — which is the reverse of the credited order.","authority":"Employee versus Independent Contractor – Control over Manner and Means. Restatement (Third) of Agency § 7.07(3)(a), §§ 2.04. Scenario MEE-027."},{"n":20,"scenarios":["MEE-028","MEE-102"],"sequence":"MEE-028: items 1–2 of 5. MEE-102: items 1–3 and 5 of 10. In both scenarios a deliberately paired contrast — the same economic indicator applied to two contributors, with opposite results. In MEE-102 the pair is reinforced by months of actual profit distributions to both, which change nothing.","summary":"A sole proprietor later takes money from two people. The first is promised a share of monthly profits, shares losses and takes part in running the business. The second receives an identical profit share purely as repayment of a loan, shares no losses and has no role in management.","trigger":"Two contributors receive the same profit share; one shares losses and manages, the other is repaid a debt.","rule":"A partnership is the association of two or more persons to carry on as co-owners a business for profit, WHETHER OR NOT the persons intend to form a partnership (UPA § 202(a)): the relationship arises from the arrangement's substance and cannot be avoided by disclaiming it or created by asserting it. The sharing of profits creates a PRESUMPTION of partnership, which is rebutted where the payments are received in repayment of a DEBT, as compensation for services, as rent, or as interest (UPA § 202(c)). The elements that confirm co-ownership are the sharing of LOSSES and the right to participate in MANAGEMENT. A contributor who shares profits and losses and manages is a partner; a lender who receives the same percentage, bears no losses and manages nothing is a creditor, and his priority and remedies are those of a creditor rather than a partner.","sub":"Partnership Formation – The Profit Presumption and the Creditor Exception","required":"Four creditable statements. That intent is NOT the test, the relationship arising from the arrangement itself. That profit-sharing raises a presumption of partnership. That the first contributor is a partner because she shares LOSSES and participates in MANAGEMENT, both named. And that the second is not a partner because his profits are repayment of a DEBT, the statutory exception named as an exception. Answering both contributors alike — in either direction — loses one of the two items no matter how well the reasoning is expressed.","sensitivity":"The switch is not the profit share, which is identical for both, but the pair of confirming elements: loss-sharing and management. Their presence makes a partner of the first; the characterisation of the payment as debt repayment removes the second from the presumption altogether. The structural point is that the examination supplies a matched pair precisely to catch reasoning that is applied symmetrically to facts that differ in one element — and it does so twice, in two separate questions, which is itself the evidence that this is a scored pattern rather than an accident of one fact pattern. Regular distributions actually made are corroboration, not a test: they support the inference of partnership for the one who shares losses and manages, and they remain debt repayment for the one who does not. Source: MEE-028 sub-issues 1–2; MEE-102 sub-issues 1–3, 5.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “a sociedad requires the affectio societatis — the will to associate as partners; she never intended to take in a partner, so no partnership arose, and the second contributor is plainly a lender under a mutuo” → U.S. law forms a partnership WHETHER OR NOT the parties intended one, and the intent on which the civilian reasoning turns is expressly irrelevant. The reader reaches the right answer on item 2 and the wrong one on item 1, by the same route: the very element that is constitutive in the civilian sociedad is the one the statute discards. Because the domestic doctrine explains both results at once, the error is invisible — he will believe he has answered both items and will have argued the wrong test twice.","authority":"Partnership Formation – The Profit Presumption and the Creditor Exception. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 202(a), § 202(c). Scenario MEE-028, MEE-102."},{"n":21,"scenarios":["MEE-028","MEE-102"],"sequence":"MEE-028: items 3–4 of 5, where item 4 is decided entirely by item 3. MEE-102: items 6–9 of 10, which add a question MEE-028 does not ask — what the transfer does to the ASSIGNOR and to the firm, answered at item 7 before the transferee's rights are reached.","summary":"A partner assigns her entire partnership interest to her son as a gift, with notice to the other partner. The son then demands access to the partnership's books and records, and is refused.","trigger":"Partner transfers her interest to a third party, who then asserts the rights of a partner.","rule":"A partner's TRANSFERABLE INTEREST is limited to the right to receive DISTRIBUTIONS; it is personal property and may be assigned in whole or in part without the consent of the other partners (UPA §§ 502, 503). The transfer does NOT make the transferee a partner, does not entitle him to participate in management or in the conduct of the business, and does not give him access to books and records or to information concerning partnership transactions. Admission of a new partner requires the CONSENT of all the partners (UPA § 402). Equally, the transfer does not remove the ASSIGNOR: she remains a partner, with all her management rights and fiduciary duties intact, and the transfer is not itself a dissociation and does not dissolve the partnership — she has parted with the money, not with the position. The transferee is entitled to the distributions the transferor would have received, and on dissolution to an account (UPA § 408). The assignment is therefore fully effective as to money and wholly ineffective as to governance, and a refusal of inspection is the correct response rather than a breach.","sub":"The Transferable Interest – Distributions Without Governance","required":"Four propositions. That what a partner may assign is the TRANSFERABLE INTEREST, meaning the right to receive distributions, and that the assignment does not confer partner status, admission requiring the consent of all partners. That the assignor REMAINS a partner and the firm is not dissolved by the transfer — stated expressly where, as in MEE-102, she carries on working after it. That the transferee is entitled to the distributions the assignor would have received, so withholding them is a breach. And that he has NO right to inspect the books, the refusal being lawful. An answer stating that the transferee 'became a partner' inverts the rule; one stating that 'the assignment was invalid without consent' also loses the item, because the assignment was valid as to distributions.","sensitivity":"The switch is WHICH bundle of rights the assignment carries: the economic interest passes freely and without consent, the governance interest does not pass at all, and — the corollary MEE-102 adds — the governance interest does not leave the assignor either. It is not transferred and it is not extinguished. Notice to the other partners is irrelevant in both directions: it neither perfects a transfer of status nor is required for the transfer of distributions. The consequence to state is counter-intuitive at both ends: the gift worked, the son still gets nothing he asked for, and the mother is still a partner. Source: MEE-028 sub-issues 3–4; MEE-102 sub-issues 6–9.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “in a sociedad de personas a partner cannot substitute a third party without the consent of the others — so either the assignment is ineffective, or, if the others acquiesced, the son has stepped into his mother's position with all her rights” → U.S. law splits the interest in two and gives the assignee one half automatically and the other half never. There is no domestic construct corresponding to a freely transferable economic interest that yields distributions to a non-partner while leaving governance untouched: the civilian analysis is binary — either the cession of the status is valid or it is not — so the reader has no category in which a transfer is simultaneously effective and irrelevant. He will therefore answer item 4 by reference to whether consent was given, which is not the question, and will characterise a lawful refusal as an obstruction.","authority":"The Transferable Interest – Distributions Without Governance. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) §§ 502, § 402, § 408. Scenario MEE-028, MEE-102."},{"n":22,"scenarios":["MEE-028","MEE-102"],"sequence":"MEE-028: item 5 of 5. MEE-102: items 4 and 10 of 10, split — item 4 establishes that the van is partnership property when it is bought, and item 10 returns to it much later to ask about its personal use. Independent of the other questions in both scenarios.","summary":"The managing partner uses a delivery van, bought in the partnership's name with partnership funds, for her personal weekend errands.","trigger":"A partner uses property titled in the partnership's name for personal purposes.","rule":"Property is PARTNERSHIP PROPERTY, and not the property of any partner individually, if it is acquired in the NAME of the partnership or with partnership FUNDS — either being sufficient, and both together conclusive (UPA §§ 203, 204). A partner is NOT a co-owner of partnership property and has no interest in it that can be transferred (UPA § 501), and may use or possess partnership property only on behalf of the partnership (UPA § 401); the property belongs to the entity, and use for personal purposes requires the CONSENT of the other partners. A partner who appropriates the use of partnership property breaches the duty of LOYALTY, which requires him to account to the partnership for any benefit derived from its property or from the conduct of its business (UPA § 409(b)), and he is accountable for the value of the use whether or not the partnership suffered a loss.","sub":"Partnership Property and the Duty to Account for Its Use","required":"Two statements. That the van is PARTNERSHIP PROPERTY because it was acquired in the partnership's name and with partnership funds — the titling test named, not merely the fact recited. And that personal use requires the other partners' consent, the partner being accountable for the benefit derived. Writing that she 'misused company assets' without stating why the van is partnership property omits the element the item is testing, since the whole point is that the test is formal and is satisfied twice over here.","sensitivity":"The switch is the NAME in which the property was acquired, or the source of the funds — formal indicators, not the use to which the asset is generally put. A van bought by the partner personally and lent to the business would remain hers to use at weekends however constantly the business relied on it. Liability then attaches to the benefit taken rather than to any loss caused, which is why the absence of harm to the business is no answer. MEE-102 sharpens the point by having the money come from the other contributors while title is taken in the firm's name: the source of the funds and the name on the title point the same way, and the partner who bought it with someone else's money has the least claim of all to use it privately. Source: MEE-028 sub-issue 5; MEE-102 sub-issues 4, 10.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT — assets contributed to or acquired by the sociedad form the patrimonio social, distinct from the partners' own estates, and a partner who applies company property to personal ends must answer for it; the reader reaches the correct conclusion and for substantially the correct reason. Two points of calibration remain. The U.S. test is FORMAL — title or funds — rather than an inquiry into the asset's economic destination, so it must be stated as a test and not assumed. And the remedy is an accounting for the BENEFIT derived rather than compensation for damage caused, so an answer framed as 'no harm, no liability' would be wrong on a point the domestic doctrine does not force the reader to confront.","authority":"Partnership Property and the Duty to Account for Its Use. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) §§ 203, § 501, § 401, § 409(b). Scenario MEE-028, MEE-102."},{"n":23,"scenarios":["MEE-029"],"sequence":"Items 1–2 of 4. Item 1 establishes the obligation and item 2 asks who is behind it; item 2 is unreachable until the obligation is fixed in time.","summary":"One partner of a general partnership makes a false representation to a buyer in the course of the business, generating a claim against the partnership. A third person is admitted as a partner only afterwards, under a partnership agreement silent as to prior obligations.","trigger":"A partnership obligation arises from a partner's wrongful act, and a new partner is admitted afterwards.","rule":"A partnership is liable for the wrongful act of a partner acting in the ORDINARY COURSE of its business, and all partners are liable JOINTLY AND SEVERALLY for partnership obligations, whether arising in contract or in tort (UPA §§ 305, 306(a)). A person admitted as a partner, however, is NOT personally liable for any partnership obligation incurred BEFORE his admission (UPA § 306(b)). His contribution and his interest in the partnership are exposed to the claim, because the obligation remains the partnership's and is satisfied from partnership assets; what is protected is his INDIVIDUAL property. The rule is not a matter of notice or of what he knew, and it is not altered by the partnership agreement's silence: liability turns solely on whether the obligation predated admission.","sub":"Partner Liability and the Incoming Partner's Protection","required":"Three statements. That the misrepresentation was made in the ORDINARY COURSE, making it a partnership obligation. That the existing partners are liable JOINTLY AND SEVERALLY and personally. And that the incoming partner is NOT personally liable for it because it arose before her admission, with the distinction drawn between her individual assets, which are protected, and her interest in the partnership, which is not. Concluding that 'all three partners are liable as partners' loses the item that the question was built to test.","sensitivity":"The switch is the DATE the obligation arose relative to the date of admission, and nothing else — not knowledge of the claim, not the terms of the partnership agreement, not whether the new partner benefited from the transaction. The refinement that earns the remaining credit is that the protection is partial: her contribution is still reachable through the partnership, so the correct answer is neither that she is liable nor that she is untouched. Source: MEE-029 sub-issues 1–2.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “she became a partner in a sociedad en nombre colectivo, and partners answer personally, unlimitedly and solidarily for the obligations of the partnership — she joined the partnership as it stood, debts included” → U.S. law protects the individual assets of a partner admitted after the obligation arose, while leaving her contribution exposed. The category 'incoming partner' translates and its consequences do not: the civilian conception of joining an existing sociedad is accession to a going concern with its liabilities attached, so the reader states the harshest form of the liability rule and applies it to the one partner the statute shields. The error is doubly costly because it is reached by correctly stating the rule that governs the other two partners.","authority":"Partner Liability and the Incoming Partner's Protection. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) §§ 305, § 306(b). Scenario MEE-029."},{"n":24,"scenarios":["MEE-029"],"sequence":"Items 3–4 of 4. A matched pair closing the timeline: the same election is tested against an obligation that predates it and one that follows it, and the two answers are opposite.","summary":"The three partners file a statement of qualification converting the partnership into a limited liability partnership, after the first claim has already arisen. A later buyer then relies on a representation that goods are fit for use and suffers serious injury.","trigger":"A partnership elects limited liability status after one obligation has arisen and before another does.","rule":"A general partnership becomes a LIMITED LIABILITY PARTNERSHIP only by FILING a statement of qualification with the State, approved by the vote required to amend the partnership agreement (UPA § 901(b)); the status exists from the filing and not from the partners' agreement or intention. The entity remains the same partnership, and the election is PROSPECTIVE: an obligation incurred BEFORE the filing remains one for which the partners are personally, jointly and severally liable, and the conversion cannot be used to escape existing creditors. For obligations arising AFTER the filing, no partner is personally liable solely by reason of being a partner (UPA § 306(c)) — the shield covering obligations in contract and in tort alike. The shield does not protect a partner from liability for his OWN wrongful conduct: a partner who himself made the misrepresentation remains personally liable for it, as a tortfeasor rather than as a partner.","sub":"The LLP Election – Filing, Prospective Effect, and the Personal-Conduct Exception","required":"Four creditable statements. That LLP status requires the FILING of a statement of qualification and exists only from that filing. That the election is PROSPECTIVE, so the earlier claim remains one of personal joint and several liability. That for the later claim no partner is personally liable merely by being a partner. And that the partner who personally made the representation remains liable for his OWN conduct notwithstanding the shield. An answer that treats the conversion as protecting the partners generally, or as ineffective because it came late, loses at least two of the four.","sensitivity":"The switch is the FILING DATE, which divides the obligations into two sets governed by opposite rules, and the filing itself is a condition of the status rather than evidence of it. The second switch operates within the shield: it protects a partner from vicarious liability for the partnership's obligations, not from his own tort, so the identity of the person who made the representation determines whether the shield answers the claim at all. Source: MEE-029 sub-issues 3–4.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “either they are a sociedad en nombre colectivo, in which the partners answer personally without limit, or they have transformed into a company with limited liability, which requires a new corporate form, amended articles and registration with effects that the reader instinctively treats as reorganising the entity” → U.S. law leaves the same partnership in existence and switches off personal liability from the date of a filing, prospectively, without changing the form of the entity or its identity. Venezuelan law offers no partnership that limits its partners' liability by registration while remaining a partnership: limitation of liability is a property of the corporate type, obtained by adopting it. The reader therefore has no framework in which to place a single entity whose partners are exposed for last month's claim and protected for next month's, and will resolve the ambiguity by choosing one regime for the whole timeline — losing the pair of items the question exists to score.","authority":"The LLP Election – Filing, Prospective Effect, and the Personal-Conduct Exception. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 901(b), § 306(c). Scenario MEE-029."},{"n":25,"scenarios":["MEE-081","MEE-121"],"sequence":"MEE-081: items 1–2 of 12. MEE-121: item 1 of 11. In both scenarios the framework the remaining items apply, and in both the same agent transacts three times with a different combination of the two axes each time — which is the evidence that this is a scored structure and not a feature of one fact pattern.","summary":"An agent makes three contracts for the same principal, signing one as his named agent, one in her own name with no mention of him, and one stating she acts for a principal she does not name.","trigger":"An agent contracts with a third party, and the manner of signing differs from contract to contract.","rule":"Two independent questions govern every agency contract. AUTHORITY decides whether the PRINCIPAL is bound: he is bound where the agent had ACTUAL authority (express or implied from the principal's manifestations to the agent), or APPARENT authority (from the principal's manifestations to the THIRD PARTY), or where he later ratifies. DISCLOSURE decides whether the AGENT is bound, and there are three states. Where the principal is DISCLOSED — the third party knows both that there is a principal and who he is — the agent is not personally liable. Where he is UNIDENTIFIED or partially disclosed — the third party knows there is a principal but not his identity — the agent IS personally liable. Where he is UNDISCLOSED — the third party believes the agent is acting for herself — the agent is personally liable and the principal may be reached once discovered. The two axes are independent: authority and disclosure can each be present or absent in any combination, and the combination decides who the third party may sue.","sub":"The Two Axes of Every Agency Contract – Authority and Disclosure","required":"Both axes must be named as axes before any transaction is analysed: that authority determines whether the principal is bound, and that the principal's disclosure status determines whether the agent is bound. The three disclosure states must be distinguished by name — disclosed, unidentified or partially disclosed, and undisclosed — with the liability consequence attached to each. An answer that works the three purchases one by one without first stating this framework can still reach correct outcomes and will lose the two items that exist to test whether the candidate knows why.","sensitivity":"The switch is that these are TWO questions, not one. Authority answered alone tells you only whether the principal is bound and says nothing about the agent; disclosure answered alone tells you only whether the agent is bound and says nothing about the principal. Two separate examinations build the scenario as a matrix precisely to catch a reader who collapses them, which is why the same agent, acting for the same principal on the same day, produces a different set of defendants in each transaction. Source: MEE-081 sub-issues 1–2; MEE-121 sub-issue 1.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the question is whether she acted within the powers conferred by the mandate — if she did, the principal is bound and the agent drops out; if she did not, the principal is not bound” → U.S. law asks a second question that the civilian frame does not contain, and the second question decides half the outcomes. Venezuelan law knows the distinction between acting in the principal's name and acting in one's own name, but it resolves it through the contract of mandate between principal and agent rather than as a standing rule of third-party liability, and there is no graduated three-state taxonomy in which merely withholding the principal's NAME — while admitting he exists — keeps the agent permanently on the contract. The reader therefore has one axis where the examination has two, and cannot reach the items that turn on the second.","authority":"The Two Axes of Every Agency Contract – Authority and Disclosure. Restatement (Third) of Agency § 1.04(2), §§ 2.01, 2.03, §§ 6.01–6.03. Scenario MEE-081, MEE-121."},{"n":26,"scenarios":["MEE-081"],"sequence":"Items 3–5 of 12, the CHIPS transaction. Strictly sequential: item 4 exists only because item 3 failed, and item 5 only because item 4 also failed.","summary":"The principal authorised the purchase of one specified series of chips. The agent identified her principal to the manufacturer, signed as his agent, and knowingly bought a different series. The manufacturer knew she said she was his agent and knew nothing else about the arrangement.","trigger":"An agent identified as such exceeds an express instruction, and the third party has heard nothing from the principal.","rule":"ACTUAL authority extends only as far as the principal has manifested consent; an agent who knowingly departs from an express limitation acts outside it, and the principal is not bound on that ground. APPARENT authority can still bind him, but only where the belief of the third party is traceable to the PRINCIPAL'S OWN manifestations — what he said or did, or allowed to appear. An agent's assertion of her own authority creates none: the third party who relies on the agent's word alone relies on nothing the principal ever said. Where neither actual nor apparent authority exists and the principal does not ratify, the principal is not liable at all. The third party is not left without a remedy: an agent who purports to act for a principal impliedly WARRANTS that she has the authority she claims, and is personally liable for breach of that warranty even though the principal was disclosed and she would otherwise have dropped out of the contract.","sub":"Authority Exceeded – Apparent Authority's Source and the Agent's Implied Warranty","required":"Three separate propositions. That the agent exceeded her actual authority, applied to the specified series. That apparent authority does not arise, BECAUSE apparent authority flows only from the principal's manifestations to the third party and this principal made none — the rule must be stated as the reason, not assumed. And that the agent is personally liable for breach of the implied warranty of authority, notwithstanding that her principal was disclosed. An answer concluding that 'the inventor is not bound and the manufacturer has no claim' gets the first proposition right and loses the other two, which are the ones the transaction was built to test.","sensitivity":"The switch on apparent authority is the SOURCE of the appearance, not its reasonableness. The manufacturer's belief was entirely reasonable, and it still fails, because everything he relied on came from the agent. Had the principal sent so much as a letter of introduction, the same facts would bind him. The second switch is that disclosure, which normally protects the agent, does not save her here: the warranty of authority is a separate promise she made by presenting herself as authorised. Source: MEE-081 sub-issues 3–5.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “she acted beyond the mandate, so the act does not bind the principal and the manufacturer must bear its own loss for having failed to verify her powers — the burden of checking the poder lies with whoever deals with a representative” → U.S. law shifts that loss onto the AGENT through a warranty implied in her own conduct. The civilian system places the risk of an unverified power on the third party, which is why the notarised poder exists and why its production is expected; there is no doctrine making the representative personally answerable merely for having claimed a power she lacked. The reader therefore closes the analysis one step early, with a conclusion that feels complete and disposes of only one of three scored items.","authority":"Authority Exceeded – Apparent Authority's Source and the Agent's Implied Warranty. Restatement (Third) of Agency § 2.03, § 6.10. Scenario MEE-081."},{"n":27,"scenarios":["MEE-081"],"sequence":"Items 6–8 of 12, the LENSES transaction. Independent of the other two. The mirror image of the chips: authority present, disclosure absent.","summary":"The principal authorised blue lenses at up to a stated price. The agent bought them below that price, but contracted in her own name alone and never mentioned that she was acting for anyone. The principal afterwards decided he did not want them.","trigger":"An agent acts within her authority but contracts in her own name, concealing that a principal exists.","rule":"An UNDISCLOSED principal is bound by a contract his agent makes within her actual authority, and the third party may enforce it against him once his existence and identity are discovered. Concealment does not protect him: he receives the benefit of the bargain and takes its burden. Nor does regret release him — authority is measured at the MOMENT OF CONTRACTING, and a principal who authorised a purchase cannot escape it by changing his mind afterwards, since nothing that happens later withdraws an authority that existed when the agent acted. The agent, having signed in her own name, is a party to the contract in her own right and remains personally liable on it. The third party may therefore pursue the agent, or the principal once discovered, though he is entitled to only one satisfaction.","sub":"The Undisclosed Principal – Both Are Bound","required":"Three propositions. That an undisclosed principal is bound by a contract made within actual authority, and may be sued once discovered. That the agent acted within her authority — measured at the time of contracting — so the principal's subsequent change of mind is irrelevant, stated as a rule rather than as a fairness point. And that the agent, having contracted in her own name, is personally liable as well. The item most often lost is the third: a candidate who has just concluded that the principal is bound tends to treat the agent as having dropped out, which is precisely the inference the undisclosed-principal rule does not permit.","sensitivity":"The switch is that concealment cuts in both directions and releases nobody. The principal is liable although he was hidden; the agent is liable although she was authorised. Contrast the chips transaction, where the agent was named and therefore protected and the principal was disclosed and therefore not bound — the two transactions invert each other, and the examination sets them side by side for that reason. Source: MEE-081 sub-issues 6–8.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “she contracted in her own name, so she contracted for herself — the inventor is a stranger to that contract and the manufacturer has no action against him; between them there is only the internal mandate” → U.S. law reaches the hidden principal directly. This is the representación indirecta of the civilian mandate, and the term maps almost perfectly onto 'undisclosed principal' — which is exactly why it misleads. In the civilian construction the agent who acts in his own name acquires the rights and obligations personally, and the principal must be brought in through the mandate rather than sued by the third party. The reader recognises the situation instantly, names it correctly, and draws the opposite conclusion about who may be sued.","authority":"The Undisclosed Principal – Both Are Bound. Restatement (Third) of Agency § 2.06, § 6.03. Scenario MEE-081."},{"n":28,"scenarios":["MEE-081","MEE-121"],"sequence":"MEE-081: items 9–12 of 12, closing that scenario. MEE-121: items 6–8 of 11, the cargo bike. In MEE-081 the principal accepted non-conforming goods; in MEE-121 he kept an item of the wrong kind altogether and promised to reimburse the agent — the same affirmation by conduct, one step more explicit.","summary":"The agent contracted for switches meeting a stated performance specification, stating that she acted for a principal but not naming him, and bought switches that did not meet it. The principal learned they did not conform and used them anyway to build his mowers.","trigger":"A principal knowingly accepts the benefit of a contract his agent had no authority to make.","rule":"A principal is not bound by an unauthorised contract unless he RATIFIES it. Ratification requires KNOWLEDGE of the material facts and an affirmation — which may be express, or implied from conduct, and accepting and using the benefit of the contract with knowledge of the defect is the classic implied affirmation. Its effect RELATES BACK: the principal is treated as though the agent had been authorised from the outset, and he cannot ratify the parts he likes and reject the rest. Separately, on the disclosure axis, a principal who is UNIDENTIFIED — the third party knowing that a principal exists but not who he is — does not protect his agent: she is personally liable on the contract unless it provides otherwise. Ratification binds the principal but does not release her; she is discharged only if the third party releases her or agrees to substitute the principal.","sub":"Ratification by Accepting the Benefit, and Who Remains Liable Afterwards","required":"Four propositions. That the agent lacked authority, the switches not meeting the specification. That the principal RATIFIED by using them with knowledge of the defect — the two elements, knowledge and affirmation by conduct, both named. That ratification relates back, so he is bound as though authorised from the start. And that the agent remains personally liable notwithstanding the ratification, because the principal was unidentified. Concluding that 'by using the switches he accepted them' states the conclusion without the doctrine and collects little; the four items are scored as four.","sensitivity":"The switch is KNOWLEDGE at the moment of accepting the benefit. Had the principal used the goods without knowing what they were, there would be no ratification and no liability. His knowledge converts a commercial act into a legal one, and a promise to reimburse the agent removes any doubt about the affirmation. The second switch is that ratification operates on the authority axis only — it brings the principal in, and does nothing to take the agent out where the principal was unidentified. Source: MEE-081 sub-issues 9–12; MEE-121 sub-issues 6–8.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the switches were defective, so he may reject them and refuse payment; if he used them he may claim the reduction in value, but the contract was never his because she exceeded her powers” → in U.S. law the moment he used them knowing of the defect he adopted the contract entire, retroactively, and lost the objection he was about to make. The trap is temporal rather than doctrinal, and the civilian apparatus is closer than it looks: confirmación of a voidable act can be TACIT and inferred from voluntary execution, so the outcome is reachable from the reader's own system. What is missing is the RETROACTIVE reach of ratification to an act the principal never authorised at all — not the curing of a defect in his own consent, but the adoption of someone else's unauthorised contract as though it had been his from the first moment. The reader analyses the merits of the defect while the item scores the instant at which the right to complain disappeared, and it disappeared through his own conduct rather than the agent's.","authority":"Ratification by Accepting the Benefit, and Who Remains Liable Afterwards. Restatement (Third) of Agency §§ 4.01–4.02, § 4.06, § 6.02. Scenario MEE-081, MEE-121."},{"n":29,"scenarios":["MEE-090"],"sequence":"Items 1–5 of 10. The wrongful branch. Item 3 is the pivot: everything after it depends on the firm NOT dissolving.","summary":"A partner withdraws immediately in breach of an agreement requiring six months' written notice, and demands that the business be shut down and its assets sold. The remaining partners want to continue.","trigger":"A partner leaves in breach of the partnership agreement and demands liquidation.","rule":"A partner always has the POWER to dissociate, at any time and by express will, even where he has no RIGHT to do so; the act is effective and cannot be prevented. Where it breaches the partnership agreement the dissociation is WRONGFUL (UPA § 602), and the partner is liable to the partnership and to the other partners for the damages it causes, which are set off against anything owed to him. Wrongful dissociation does NOT dissolve the partnership. Where the business continues, the partnership must purchase the dissociated partner's interest at the BUYOUT PRICE — what he would have received had the business been wound up and its assets sold at going-concern or liquidation value, whichever is greater — less the damages he caused, and payment to a wrongfully dissociating partner may be deferred until the term expires. He has no right to participate in winding up if one nonetheless occurs (UPA § 802; § 803 in the pre-2013 numbering).","sub":"Wrongful Dissociation – Power Without Right, and the Buyout","required":"Five propositions, and the third carries the others. That he had the POWER to dissociate though not the right. That the dissociation was WRONGFUL because it breached the agreement. That wrongful dissociation does NOT force a winding up — stated expressly, because it answers what he asked for. That the remaining partners may continue and must buy out his interest at the statutory buyout price, less damages. And that he may not take part in a winding up. An answer holding that the partnership dissolved and must be liquidated loses everything from the third item onward, however well the first two are argued.","sensitivity":"The switch is the separation of POWER from RIGHT. He can leave — no one can hold him — and that is the only thing his leaving achieves. Everything he wanted from leaving, the shutdown and the sale, is denied to him precisely because the departure was wrongful. Note the asymmetry the examination is testing: wrongfulness does not reduce his entitlement to be paid, it defers it and offsets it, while stripping him of any voice in what happens to the business. Source: MEE-090 sub-issues 1–5.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “nobody may be compelled to remain in a partnership; in a company of indefinite duration a partner's withdrawal dissolves it, and what follows is liquidation — his failure to give notice sounds in damages, nothing more” → U.S. law splits the departure of the PARTNER from the ending of the FIRM, and the wrongful departure produces the first without the second. The civilian frame has no equivalent of dissociation as a self-standing legal event: the renuncia of a partner in a sociedad of indefinite duration operates on the SOCIEDAD, as a cause of dissolution, and although the partners may agree to continue the business, that continuation rests on their agreement rather than on a statutory default that keeps the firm alive and prices the leaver out. Because departure and dissolution are so closely tied in his system, the correct answer is not merely unknown to the reader — it is counter-intuitive at the level of structure, and he will argue confidently for the liquidation the statute exists to prevent.","authority":"Wrongful Dissociation – Power Without Right, and the Buyout. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 602, § 802. Scenario MEE-090."},{"n":30,"scenarios":["MEE-090"],"sequence":"Items 6–8 of 10. The counterfactual branch. Item 7 answers the same question as item 3 and answers it the other way, which is the point of the pair.","summary":"The same partner, on the assumption that he gave the six months' written notice the agreement required, and then asked for the same thing: that the business be wound up and its assets sold.","trigger":"A partner leaves in compliance with the partnership agreement and seeks a winding up.","rule":"Where the dissociation complies with the partnership agreement it is RIGHTFUL, and no damages arise. In a partnership AT WILL, a rightful dissociation by express will causes DISSOLUTION and the winding up of the business (UPA § 801(1)); in a partnership for a definite term or undertaking, more is required. Dissolution is not instantaneous liquidation: the partnership continues for the limited purpose of winding up, and the remaining partners may still agree unanimously to continue the business, in which case the dissociated partner is bought out instead. A partner who has not wrongfully dissociated MAY participate in winding up (UPA § 802; § 803 in the pre-2013 numbering) — he may take part, but he does not control it, and cannot dictate that assets be sold in a particular way or at a particular time.","sub":"Rightful Dissociation – What the Notice Buys","required":"Three propositions. That with proper notice the dissociation is rightful, so no damages follow. That in a partnership at will a rightful dissociation by express will causes dissolution and winding up — the opposite result to the wrongful branch, and it must be stated as the contrast. And that he may participate in the winding up but does not control it. The examination asks the same question twice on purpose; an answer that treats the two branches alike, in either direction, forfeits the branch it did not distinguish.","sensitivity":"The switch is the notice, and nothing else. Same partner, same intention, same business: with the notice he can force the firm into winding up and take part in it; without it he cannot force anything and is shown the door with a cheque. No fact about the merits of his departure, his reasons, or his contribution changes either answer. The refinement worth stating is that even rightful dissociation does not guarantee liquidation, since the remaining partners may unanimously agree to continue. Source: MEE-090 sub-issues 6–8.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the notice period is a term of the contract between partners; failing to observe it makes him liable for the harm caused, but it cannot change the legal nature of his withdrawal or what the law does with it” → in U.S. law the notice is the condition that selects between two statutory regimes with opposite outcomes. This is the classic sequence trap and it is hard to see from the civilian side, because there the withdrawal takes effect on the sociedad whether or not the agreed formalities were observed, and non-compliance is answered in damages rather than by a change of regime. The reader treats a switch as a penalty clause. He is not wrong about the damages; he simply does not see that the same clause is also deciding whether the business survives.","authority":"Rightful Dissociation – What the Notice Buys. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 801(1), § 802. Scenario MEE-090."},{"n":31,"scenarios":["MEE-090"],"sequence":"Items 9–10 of 10. Both branches rejoin here: the exposure is the same whether he left rightfully or wrongfully.","summary":"After the partner has withdrawn, the business incurs further debts while it is being wound up, and creditors then look for payment.","trigger":"Creditors pursue a partner for obligations arising around and after his departure.","rule":"Dissociation does not end a partner's liability for obligations the partnership incurred BEFORE he left (UPA § 703(a)); he remains liable on them, and is discharged only by agreement with the creditor or by a material alteration of the obligation without his consent. He may also be liable for obligations incurred AFTER dissociation, within two years, where the other party reasonably believed him still a partner and had no notice of the dissociation — exposure the partnership can cut off by filing a statement of dissociation, which operates as notice ninety days after filing. Where the partnership is winding up, obligations properly incurred in completing existing business bind the partners as before. Throughout, partners are liable JOINTLY AND SEVERALLY for partnership obligations (UPA § 306(a)), so a creditor may pursue any one of them for the whole, subject to exhausting partnership assets first (UPA § 307(d)).","sub":"Liability After Departure and Through Winding Up","required":"Two propositions, plainly stated. That he remains liable for obligations incurred before his dissociation and for those properly incurred during the winding up of existing business. And that partner liability is JOINT AND SEVERAL, so any one partner may be pursued for the whole. The phrase itself is what the grader credits; 'the partners are all responsible' does not collect it. A candidate who adds that the exposure to new creditors can be cut off by notice, or by a filed statement of dissociation, earns the refinement.","sensitivity":"The switch is WHEN the obligation arose relative to the departure, and — for later obligations — whether the creditor knew of it. Leaving does not clean the slate, and it does not matter here whether the leaving was rightful: this item is the one place in the scenario where the two branches converge on the same answer, which is worth saying expressly in an answer that has spent the previous eight items distinguishing them. Source: MEE-090 sub-issues 9–10.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT — in a sociedad en nombre colectivo the partners answer personally, unlimitedly and solidariamente, and a retiring partner is not released from obligations contracted while he was a partner. The reader reaches the correct conclusion and states the correct reason, and this unit is recorded to make that visible. Two calibrations only. The exposure to obligations arising AFTER departure, resting on what the creditor reasonably believed and extinguishable by a filed notice, is sharper and more procedural than the civilian treatment, which turns on publicity of the withdrawal in general terms. And the U.S. formulation adds the exhaustion requirement before a partner's personal assets can be reached, which the reader may omit as a technicality when it is in fact his client's protection.","authority":"Liability After Departure and Through Winding Up. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 703(a), § 306(a), § 307(d). Scenario MEE-090."},{"n":32,"scenarios":["MEE-092","MEE-127"],"sequence":"MEE-092: items 1–2 of 10. MEE-127: items 1–4 of 11. In both, the entity the parties believed they had formed does not exist and the default supplies a general partnership; MEE-127 adds the specific reason an LP fails and the effect of a late certificate, taken up in Unit 45.","summary":"Three people agree to carry on a practice together and share its profits. Documents for a limited liability company are prepared but never signed and never filed. They operate as though the company existed.","trigger":"Parties intend a limited liability entity, never file, and carry on the business anyway.","rule":"A GENERAL PARTNERSHIP is formed when two or more persons associate to carry on as co-owners a business for profit, WHETHER OR NOT they intend to form a partnership and without any filing, agreement in writing or other formality (UPA § 202(a)). It is the default form: it arises by operation of law from the conduct itself. A limited liability company, by contrast, comes into existence ONLY on the filing of articles of organisation with the State; preparing documents, instructing a lawyer, or intending to form one creates nothing. The consequence is not a defective LLC but a fully constituted general partnership governed by the partnership statute in every respect — equal management rights, each partner an agent of the firm, and personal, joint and several liability of every partner for its obligations, which is precisely the exposure the parties believed they had avoided.","sub":"The Default Entity – When the Intended Form Fails, a General Partnership Remains","required":"Two propositions, and the second is not merely the negative of the first. That their conduct formed a GENERAL PARTNERSHIP, with the elements named and intent expressly irrelevant. And that no LLC exists because an LLC requires FILING, so the limited liability they believed they had does not exist and each of them is personally liable. An answer that says 'the LLC was never formed' without naming what they do have leaves the remaining eight items without a governing statute, and an answer that treats the situation as a defective LLC to be cured analyses a problem the facts do not present.","sensitivity":"The switch is the FILING in MEE-092 and the ASSENT OF A GENERAL PARTNER in MEE-127, and in neither case the parties' intentions, their diligence or their lawyer's competence. Note what does NOT turn on it: the business is not invalid, the contracts are not void, and nothing needs curing. The only thing the defect changes is who pays if the firm cannot — and that is the whole point of the item. Source: MEE-092 sub-issues 1–2; MEE-127 sub-issues 1–4.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT — a company acquires personality and the shield that goes with it through registration, so an unregistered venture leaves its participants personally exposed. The reader will reach the correct outcome, and by sound reasoning from the constitutive effect of registration; the Code of Commerce reaches the same place expressly, treating a company whose formalities were not completed as not legally constituted and holding the partners personally and solidarily liable to third parties (art. 219). Two calibrations decide whether he can then answer the rest of the question. First, U.S. law gives him not a defective or irregular company but a COMPLETE alternative entity with its own statute and default rules, whereas the sociedad irregular of the civilian tradition is a penalised state pending regularisation — so the reader tends to write about the defect instead of switching statutes and carrying on. Second, formation here requires no agreement, no writing and no intent at all, which is broader than the civilian contract-based sociedad and is what makes the default arise silently.","authority":"The Default Entity – When the Intended Form Fails, a General Partnership Remains. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 202(a). Scenario MEE-092, MEE-127."},{"n":33,"scenarios":["MEE-092"],"sequence":"Items 3–5 of 10. Reachable only once the entity is identified. Item 5 is the one that decides the outcome, and it turns on something one partner did NOT do.","summary":"One partner buys a four-hundred-thousand-dollar piece of imaging equipment without consulting the others. The practice had bought similar equipment before. Another partner had misgivings about the purchase but never said so to anyone before it was made.","trigger":"One partner commits the firm to a large purchase alone, and a co-partner's objection was never voiced.","rule":"Each partner is an AGENT of the partnership, and an act of a partner for apparently carrying on the partnership business IN THE ORDINARY COURSE binds the partnership — including as to third parties who do not know of any limitation (UPA § 301(1)). Whether an act is in the ordinary course is judged against the business the firm actually conducts, and the firm's OWN PRIOR PRACTICE is the strongest evidence: an acquisition of the same kind the practice has made before is ordinary course whatever its size. Differences arising in the ordinary course are decided by a MAJORITY of the partners, and acts outside the ordinary course require the consent of ALL (UPA § 401). A partner's authority is defeated only where the act is outside the ordinary course, or where he KNOWS the other partners object; an objection that is felt but never communicated restricts nothing, because authority is measured by what the acting partner knew.","sub":"Partner Authority in the Ordinary Course, and What Actually Defeats It","required":"Three propositions. That each partner is an agent with authority to bind the firm for acts in the ordinary course of business. That this purchase WAS in the ordinary course, and the reason must be the practice's prior acquisitions of similar equipment — not the amount, and not its usefulness. And that the silent partner's objection does not defeat the authority BECAUSE it was never communicated, so the acting partner did not know of it. Concluding that 'a purchase of this size required all three to agree' inverts the rule and loses all three items.","sensitivity":"The switch is COMMUNICATION, not disagreement. Had she said one sentence to him before he signed, his authority would have been defeated and the firm would not be bound. She thought it and did not say it, and that silence is the whole difference. The second, quieter switch is that magnitude is not the test: four hundred thousand dollars sounds extraordinary and is ordinary here, because the firm had done it before. Source: MEE-092 sub-issues 3–5.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “a disposition of this magnitude exceeds ordinary administration and requires the agreement of all the partners; one of them cannot commit the firm to four hundred thousand dollars alone, and the fact that another objected confirms there was no valid decision” → U.S. law treats every partner as a general agent whose ordinary-course acts bind the firm, measures 'ordinary course' by the firm's own history rather than by amount, and disregards an objection that was never spoken. The trap is the phrase itself: 'ordinary course of business' reads as the civilian actos de simple administración opposed to actos de disposición, a distinction drawn by the NATURE and gravity of the act. Here it is drawn by what this firm habitually does. The reader applies a familiar-sounding test with a different content, and reaches the opposite result on all three items.","authority":"Partner Authority in the Ordinary Course, and What Actually Defeats It. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 301(1), § 401. Scenario MEE-092."},{"n":34,"scenarios":["MEE-092"],"sequence":"Items 6–10 of 10. Closes the scenario. Compare with MEE-090, which asks the same departure questions where the agreement DID impose a notice period.","summary":"One partner tells another that she is leaving and never coming back. There is no fixed term for the business. The remaining two agree to carry on, and she asks orally to be paid for her one-third interest.","trigger":"A partner withdraws from a partnership with no fixed term and seeks payment for her interest.","rule":"A partner is DISSOCIATED when the partnership has NOTICE of that partner's express will to withdraw (UPA § 601(1)); no formality, writing or acceptance is required, and telling one partner gives the partnership notice. In a partnership AT WILL — no definite term and no particular undertaking — a dissociation by express will is RIGHTFUL, so no damages arise. Where the remaining partners CONTINUE the business, the dissociation does not dissolve the partnership; the firm persists as the same entity and must BUY OUT the dissociated partner's interest at the statutory buyout price (UPA § 701). The timetable for payment, however, begins only on a WRITTEN DEMAND: the partnership must pay, or tender an estimate with an explanation, within 120 days after a written demand for payment, and an oral request does not start that period running.","sub":"At-Will Dissociation, Continuation, and the Written Demand","required":"Five propositions, each briefly stated. That she is dissociated because the partnership had notice of her express will to withdraw. That in an at-will partnership the dissociation is rightful, so she owes no damages. That because the others continue, there is no dissolution. That she is entitled to a buyout of her interest at the statutory price. And that the 120-day payment timetable begins only on a WRITTEN demand, so her oral request has not started it. The last item is the one most often missed, and it is the only one that turns on a formality.","sensitivity":"Two switches, both formal. The ABSENCE of a fixed term makes the departure rightful — the identical words spoken in a term partnership, or in breach of a notice clause, would be wrongful and would cost her, which is exactly the contrast MEE-090 draws. And the FORM of her demand controls the clock: her entitlement to be paid exists from the moment she leaves, but her right to be paid within a period does not begin until she writes. Nothing about the amount owed changes; only when it becomes due. Source: MEE-092 sub-issues 6–10.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “she announced her withdrawal, so she is out; her right to be paid her share follows from her participation, and how she asked for it is immaterial — a demand is a demand” → U.S. law makes the written demand the event that starts the statutory period, so an oral request leaves her with a claim and no timetable to enforce it. The civilian tradition conditions a partner's economic claim on the liquidation of his account rather than on a formality of demand, and treats the form of an extrajudicial request as a matter of proof only. The reader therefore answers the substance correctly and omits the one procedural step the item scores — the same shape of error as the notice clause in MEE-090, and worth noticing as a pattern rather than as two separate oversights.","authority":"At-Will Dissociation, Continuation, and the Written Demand. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 601(1), § 701. Scenario MEE-092."},{"n":35,"scenarios":["MEE-096"],"sequence":"Items 1–4 of 11, the TRUCK. Sequential: item 4 is the one the outcome turns on, and it asks what the third party was told.","summary":"One of three partners contracts alone, in the firm's name, to buy a hundred-thousand-dollar electric refuse truck from a dealership that had sold the firm trucks before. Such trucks had become common in the trade. The other partners never authorised it and one objected afterwards; the dealership was never told anything about any limitation.","trigger":"A partner contracts alone in the firm's name, and the other partners' objection was never communicated to the third party.","rule":"Each partner is an AGENT of the partnership, and an act of a partner apparently carrying on the partnership business in the ORDINARY COURSE binds the partnership (UPA § 301(1)). Ordinary course is measured against the business the firm actually carries on, and the firm's own trading history is the best evidence of it. APPARENT AUTHORITY here rests not on the partner's assertion but on the PARTNERSHIP'S conduct: the status of partner is itself a manifestation to the world, reinforced where the firm has dealt with this very supplier before. Internal restrictions — a decision among the partners, an objection, even a unanimous prohibition — do NOT defeat that authority unless the third party had NOTICE of them. The partnership is bound unless the third party knew or had received notification that the partner lacked authority, and a limitation the third party was never told about is legally invisible to him however clearly the partners expressed it among themselves.","sub":"Ordinary-Course Authority and Why Internal Limits Need Notice","required":"Four propositions. That each partner is an agent of the firm. That the purchase is in the ORDINARY COURSE, and the reason must be the trade and the firm's prior dealings, not the utility of the truck. That apparent authority arises from the partnership's own conduct, including the prior dealings with this dealership. And — the item that decides the case — that the partners' internal objection does not defeat authority BECAUSE the dealership had no notice of it. An answer resting on the size of the purchase, or on the absence of consent among the partners, loses all four.","sensitivity":"The switch is NOTICE TO THE THIRD PARTY, not the existence or the clarity of the internal limit. Two partners who genuinely never agreed, and one who objected outright, count for nothing against a supplier who was told nothing. Had the firm sent the dealership a single line withdrawing his authority, the same contract would not bind them. Note also what the amount does not do: a hundred thousand dollars is ordinary here because the firm buys trucks. Source: MEE-096 sub-issues 1–4.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “limits on a manager's powers are opposable to third parties once they appear in the company's constitutive documents — whoever contracts with a representative is expected to check them, and here two of three partners never consented, so the firm is not bound” → U.S. partnership law disregards an internal limitation the third party was never actually told about. The false cognate is NOTICE. In the civilian system publicity does the work of notice: what is registered is deemed known to everyone, which is why an unconsulted limit still binds the outsider. Here notice means what this particular third party actually knew or was told, and registration of a partnership agreement plays no equivalent role. The reader applies a familiar word with a different content and reaches the opposite result on the item that decides the transaction.","authority":"Ordinary-Course Authority and Why Internal Limits Need Notice. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 301(1). Scenario MEE-096."},{"n":36,"scenarios":["MEE-096"],"sequence":"Items 8–11 of 11, the LAND. The same four questions as Unit 35, answered the other way. Read the two units together: the contrast is the lesson.","summary":"The firm had bought five hundred acres in another State years earlier as a speculation unrelated to refuse collection. One partner alone contracts to sell that land to a developer who knows the firm operates a refuse business in a different State and knows the partner is acting alone. Another partner tells him at once that the sale is not binding; he signs anyway.","trigger":"A partner alone disposes of an asset unrelated to the firm's business, dealing with a third party who knows the circumstances.","rule":"An act OUTSIDE the ordinary course of the partnership's business requires the consent of ALL the partners (UPA § 401); a single partner has no actual authority to undertake it, and a majority would not suffice. Whether an act is outside the ordinary course is judged by the business the firm actually carries on — not by the nature of the asset in the abstract, so the disposal of land held purely as an unrelated speculation falls outside a refuse-collection business. APPARENT authority cannot supply what actual authority lacks where the third party KNOWS the partner lacks it: apparent authority protects a reasonable belief, and a developer who knows the firm's line of business, knows the land is unconnected to it, and knows the partner is acting alone has no such belief to protect. With neither actual nor apparent authority and no ratification by the other partners, the partnership is NOT bound, and the partner alone answers to the developer.","sub":"Acts Outside the Ordinary Course – Unanimity and the Knowing Third Party","required":"Four propositions mirroring Unit 35. That the sale is OUTSIDE the ordinary course, with the reason being the firm's actual business rather than the character of land as such. That it therefore required UNANIMOUS consent, which it did not have. That apparent authority fails BECAUSE the developer knew the relevant facts. And that the partnership is not bound. The trap for a strong candidate is brevity: having concluded that the act was extraordinary, he stops, and loses the apparent-authority item that the developer's knowledge was placed in the facts to test.","sensitivity":"The switch is the same one as in Unit 35 and it points the other way: what the THIRD PARTY knew. Change one fact — make the developer ignorant of the firm's business — and apparent authority becomes arguable even though actual authority is still absent. The examination pairs the two transactions so that the same four questions produce opposite answers, and the difference lies wholly in the third party's knowledge and the firm's trading scope, not in the partner's conduct, which was identical in both. Source: MEE-096 sub-issues 8–11.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT — the disposal of an immovable unconnected to the company's object exceeds ordinary administration, requires the agreement of all the partners, and a purchaser who knew the circumstances cannot invoke good faith. The reader reaches every one of the four correct conclusions. The warning belongs here rather than a correction: he reaches them by sorting the two transactions on the NATURE OF THE ASSET — a truck is business equipment, distant farmland is not — which happens to give the right pair of answers in this scenario and is not the test. The same instinct produced the wrong answer on the truck in Unit 35. A candidate who notices only that he got the land right will conclude his method works, when what worked was a coincidence between his categories and these facts.","authority":"Acts Outside the Ordinary Course – Unanimity and the Knowing Third Party. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 401. Scenario MEE-096."},{"n":37,"scenarios":["MEE-096"],"sequence":"Items 5–7 of 11. Independent of both contracts: these three are about the partners' relations among themselves, and are answered the same way whichever transaction binds the firm.","summary":"The partner paid thirty thousand dollars of his own money as the deposit on the truck. The firm cannot fund the seventy-thousand-dollar balance. He also works about twice as many hours as the other two and wants to be paid for the extra time.","trigger":"A partner advances his own funds for the firm, the firm cannot pay a debt, and one partner contributes more labour than the others.","rule":"A partnership must REIMBURSE a partner for payments made on its behalf (UPA § 401(b)) and INDEMNIFY him for liabilities incurred by reason of his capacity as a partner (UPA § 401(c)); the advance is treated as a loan to the firm and bears interest. Partners are liable JOINTLY AND SEVERALLY for the obligations of the partnership (UPA § 306(a)), so where the firm cannot pay, the creditor may pursue any partner for the whole, subject to exhausting partnership assets first (UPA § 307(d)); a partner who pays more than his share may seek contribution from the others. But a partner is NOT entitled to REMUNERATION for services rendered to the partnership, except reasonable compensation for services in winding up (UPA § 401). Absent agreement, working twice as many hours earns nothing extra: the partner's return is his share of profits, and effort does not adjust it.","sub":"Reimbursement, Joint and Several Liability, and No Pay for Labour","required":"Three propositions, and the third is counter-intuitive enough that it must be stated flatly. That the firm must reimburse his thirty-thousand-dollar advance, made in the ordinary course. That the partners are JOINTLY AND SEVERALLY liable for the unpaid balance, the phrase itself being what is credited. And that he is entitled to NOTHING for the extra hours absent an agreement, the exception for winding up named. An answer that awards him a quantum meruit or an adjusted profit share for the additional work loses the item outright.","sensitivity":"The switch on the third item is the ABSENCE OF AGREEMENT, and the statutory default is unforgiving: it presumes that partners contribute unequal effort and that the profit share is the whole of the bargain. Note the asymmetry the examination is testing — MONEY he advances comes back to him with interest, LABOUR he contributes does not, and the two claims sit in the same fact pattern so that a candidate who treats them alike gets one of them wrong. Source: MEE-096 sub-issues 5–7.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “he worked twice as much for the common enterprise; whatever the agreement says, he is owed something for it — either remuneration for his administration or an adjustment of his share, because nobody is expected to enrich his partners with his own labour for nothing” → U.S. partnership law gives him nothing at all. There is no default entitlement to be paid for partnership services, and the socio industrial of the civilian tradition — whose contribution IS his labour and who is credited for it — has no counterpart in the statutory default, which knows only capital accounts and profit shares. The reimbursement rule makes the gap harder to see, because the reader who correctly recovers the thirty thousand dollars naturally extends the same logic to the hours and finds the opposite rule waiting.","authority":"Reimbursement, Joint and Several Liability, and No Pay for Labour. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 401(b), § 401(c), § 306(a), § 307(d), § 401. Scenario MEE-096."},{"n":38,"scenarios":["MEE-110","MEE-121"],"sequence":"MEE-110: items 2–6 of 11, the sign contract. MEE-121: items 9–10 of 11, the pizza oven. The two scenarios show the two ways a principal manufactures the appearance — by EQUIPPING the agent with his card and signage, and by TELEPHONING the third party directly to announce her.","summary":"The principal gave her driver a business card and told him to display shop signage on his van while delivering. He produced the card at a sign shop and bought custom signs on credit for more than she had authorised. The sign shop knew nothing of any price limit.","trigger":"An agent equipped with the principal's business card and instructed to display her signage exceeds an authorised amount.","rule":"APPARENT AUTHORITY exists where a third party reasonably believes the agent is authorised and that belief is traceable to the PRINCIPAL'S manifestations (Restatement (Third) of Agency § 2.03). The manifestation need not be a statement about authority: equipping an agent with the principal's business card, and instructing him to trade under her name and signage, are manifestations that a reasonable third party may rely on. A principal is BOUND by a contract made with apparent authority even though the agent exceeded his ACTUAL authority, provided the third party neither knew nor had notice of the limitation; her remedy is against the agent for the breach of instructions, not against the third party. Where the principal is DISCLOSED and the agent acted with actual or apparent authority, the AGENT is not personally liable on the contract — he is not a party to it, and the fact that he disobeyed his instructions does not make him one.","sub":"Apparent Authority Created by Equipment and Name, and the Disclosed Agent's Immunity","required":"Five propositions. That apparent authority arises from the PRINCIPAL'S manifestations, with the business card and the instruction to display her signage identified as the manifestations. That she is bound although the driver exceeded his actual authority. That the sign shop's reliance was reasonable and it had no notice of the limit. That she is therefore liable to the sign shop. And that the DRIVER is NOT personally liable, because the principal was disclosed. The last is the item most often left out: it is not a defence anyone raised, it is a scored proposition, and an answer silent on the agent's position forfeits it.","sensitivity":"The switch is that the principal manufactured the appearance himself. In MEE-110 he never spoke to the sign shop but put his name on the agent's van and his card in her hand; in MEE-121 he telephoned the oven supplier and said she would be buying on his behalf. Either is a manifestation, and in both the principal is bound although the agent exceeded or lacked actual authority. Contrast Unit 26 and the chairs in Unit 44, where the agent asserted her own authority and the principal had done nothing — there the identical reliance failed. What the principal DID, not what the third party believed, is where the analysis has to look. Source: MEE-110 sub-issues 2–6; MEE-121 sub-issues 9–10.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “he had a mandate limited to a stated amount and exceeded it; a business card is not a power of attorney, and whoever grants credit to a representative must satisfy himself of the extent of his powers — the shop's claim lies against the driver” → U.S. law binds the principal because she created the appearance, and leaves her to recover from her own agent. The gap is that the civilian system locates authority in the INSTRUMENT that confers it, so the analysis asks what the poder said; there is no developed doctrine under which the trappings of a business — a card, a name on a vehicle — confer power to bind. The teoría de la apariencia exists but operates as an equitable corrective in narrow circumstances rather than as a standing basis of authority, which is why the reader reaches for the document and finds the limit written in it.","authority":"Apparent Authority Created by Equipment and Name, and the Disclosed Agent's Immunity. Restatement (Third) of Agency § 2.03. Scenario MEE-110, MEE-121."},{"n":39,"scenarios":["MEE-110"],"sequence":"Items 7–9 of 11, the INJURY. Read against Unit 19, which states the default this unit excepts — the two must be held together or each will be misapplied.","summary":"The principal telephoned a customer to say her driver was on his way in a van bearing the shop's name. As the customer approached the van the driver negligently accelerated and injured her. The driver was an independent contractor, not an employee.","trigger":"A non-employee agent injures a third party who was told by the principal to expect him.","rule":"The default is that a principal is NOT vicariously liable for the torts of a non-employee agent — an independent contractor. There is an exception: a principal IS subject to vicarious liability for a tort committed by an agent acting with APPARENT AUTHORITY in dealing with a third party on the principal's behalf, where the third party's reliance on that appearance was reasonable (Restatement (Third) of Agency § 7.08). Apparent authority therefore reaches beyond contract into tort. The principal's own statement to the customer — that her driver was coming, in a van bearing her name — is the manifestation, and the customer's reliance on it is what brings the injury within the exception. The agent's classification as an independent contractor does not defeat it: the exception operates precisely where the vicarious route would otherwise be closed, and the agent remains personally liable for his own negligence in any event.","sub":"Apparent Authority Reaching Torts – The Independent-Contractor Exception","required":"Three propositions, and the first must be stated even though it points the other way. That the default rule denies vicarious liability for an independent contractor's torts. That apparent authority nonetheless applies to TORTS, not only to contracts, where the agent acts with it in dealing with a third party. And that her telephone call and her signage were the manifestations on which the customer reasonably relied, so she is vicariously liable notwithstanding his status. An answer that jumps straight to liability without stating the default it is excepting collects the outcome and loses the structure.","sensitivity":"The switch is the principal's own communication to THIS third party. Had she said nothing and the driver simply arrived, the independent-contractor default would hold and this route would close — leaving only her negligent selection. One telephone call moved the case across the line. The structural point worth holding: this unit and Unit 19 state a default and its exception, and a candidate who has learned only one of them will answer the other scenario wrongly. Source: MEE-110 sub-issues 7–9.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT — she put him in her service, dressed his van in her name and announced him to her customer, so she answers for the harm he caused in performing what she entrusted to him. The conclusion is correct and the reasoning is close to the actual rule. The warning is what he will infer from being right. His system reaches liability through a general presumption for the acts of dependants, so he will conclude that the presumption is the rule; U.S. law reaches the same result through a NARROW EXCEPTION to the opposite default. The consequence is visible in Unit 19, where the same instinct produces a confidently wrong answer on a driver nobody was told about. Getting this scenario right teaches him nothing about that one, and may cost him it.","authority":"Apparent Authority Reaching Torts – The Independent-Contractor Exception. Restatement (Third) of Agency § 7.08. Scenario MEE-110."},{"n":40,"scenarios":["MEE-110"],"sequence":"Items 1 and 10 of 11. Independent of everything else: item 1 opens the fact pattern and item 10 returns to it after the injury, and it stands whether or not either other route to liability works.","summary":"Before engaging the driver the principal saw online warnings about his unsafe driving history and engaged him anyway. He later injured a customer by negligent driving.","trigger":"A principal engages an agent she knew or should have known was unfit, and the agent causes the harm his unfitness made likely.","rule":"A principal is DIRECTLY liable in negligence for harm to a third party caused by the negligent SELECTION, retention, training or supervision of an agent — where the principal knew or should have known the agent was unfit for the task, and the resulting harm is of the kind the unfitness made foreseeable (Restatement (Third) of Agency § 7.05). This liability is the principal's own, not borrowed: it rests on HER breach of duty, not on his. It therefore does not depend on the agent being an employee, and survives the independent-contractor characterisation entirely; it is also unaffected by whether apparent authority existed. It is one of the recognised exceptions to the general rule that a principal is not liable for the torts of an independent contractor, and it is available in addition to, not instead of, any vicarious route.","sub":"Negligent Selection – The Principal's Own Liability","required":"Two propositions, and both must be framed as a distinct theory. That the principal is DIRECTLY liable for negligently selecting an agent she knew or should have known was unfit, the online warnings being the knowledge. And — the framing that carries the marks — that this liability is her own and therefore survives his status as an independent contractor and stands independently of the apparent-authority route. An answer that mentions the warnings while discussing her responsibility for his driving has used the fact and lost the theory.","sensitivity":"The switch is that the duty breached is HERS. Nothing about the driver's status, the customer's beliefs, or the appearance of the van bears on it; the only questions are what she knew or should have known and whether the harm was of the foreseeable kind. Note the structural role this unit plays: it is the answer that survives when the others fail, which is exactly why the examination puts the warnings in the first paragraph and returns to them in the last item. Source: MEE-110 sub-issues 1, 10.","archetype":"Convergence","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: the civilian instinct is RIGHT — culpa in eligendo is a familiar idea, and a principal who entrusts a dangerous task to someone he knew to be unfit answers for his own fault in choosing him. The doctrine converges almost exactly. What diverges is its PROCEDURAL STANDING, and that is what the item scores. In the civilian frame the negligent choice tends to be absorbed into the general responsibility for those one employs, appearing as an aggravating consideration rather than as a separate cause of action; here it is an independent theory that must be pleaded and argued as such, and its whole value is that it defeats the defence — he was an independent contractor — which would otherwise end the case. The reader who knows the concept but folds it into his liability argument loses two items while being substantively correct.","authority":"Negligent Selection – The Principal's Own Liability. Restatement (Third) of Agency § 7.05. Scenario MEE-110."},{"n":41,"scenarios":["MEE-116"],"sequence":"Items 1, 3, 5 and 10 of 11. Opens the chain: the call must belong to the firm before anything he did with it can be a wrong.","summary":"Two equal partners have run a shop for ten years in leased premises, with no written agreement. The landlord telephones one of them offering to sell the building. He tells his partner nothing and later buys it himself.","trigger":"A business opportunity is offered personally to one partner in connection with the partnership's affairs.","rule":"Absent an agreement the partnership is governed by the statutory defaults, and each partner owes the partnership and the other partners the duty of LOYALTY, the duty of CARE, and the contractual obligation of good faith and fair dealing (UPA § 409). The duty of loyalty requires a partner to ACCOUNT to the partnership for any property, profit or benefit derived from the conduct of its business or from the USE BY HIM OF PARTNERSHIP PROPERTY, including the appropriation of a partnership OPPORTUNITY. An opportunity presented to a partner because of the partnership's business, or closely related to it — the chance to buy the very premises the firm occupies is the paradigm — belongs to the partnership, not to the partner who happens to receive the call. He must DISCLOSE it, and he may take it for himself only with the informed consent of the other partners. Concealment is itself a breach, independently of what he then does with the opportunity.","sub":"The Partnership Opportunity – Whose Chance Was It","required":"Three propositions. That partners owe the duty of loyalty, the duty of care and the obligation of good faith, arising from the statute in the absence of any agreement. That the landlord's offer was a PARTNERSHIP OPPORTUNITY because it concerned the premises the firm occupied and came to him through the firm's business — this is the item that must be argued rather than assumed. And that he was obliged to disclose it and could take it only with her informed consent, so both the concealment and the purchase were breaches. An answer that condemns his conduct as bad faith, without establishing that the opportunity was the firm's, has skipped the proposition on which every later item depends.","sensitivity":"The switch is WHY the call came to him: because the firm was the tenant. An unconnected chance — a building in another town, offered to him as a private investor — would have been his to take. The doctrine attaches to the opportunity's relationship with the partnership's business, not to who answered the telephone, and not to whether the partnership could have afforded it. Note that the concealment alone completes a breach: he was already liable before he bought anything. Source: MEE-116 sub-issues 1, 3, 5, 10.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “his conduct offends good faith and he may not compete with his own firm, so he answers for the harm; but the landlord chose to call HIM, and an offer made to a person is that person's to accept — the firm had no right to the building” → U.S. law treats the opportunity itself as belonging to the partnership. The civilian tradition supplies the prohibition on competition and the duty of fidelity, but it has no doctrine under which an unaccepted commercial offer becomes an asset of the company in which the partner holds it in trust: an opportunity is not a thing capable of being owned. The reader therefore identifies the disloyalty and misses the property, which is why he can reach the right verdict and still be unable to claim the building — and the building is what the case is about.","authority":"The Partnership Opportunity – Whose Chance Was It. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 409. Scenario MEE-116."},{"n":42,"scenarios":["MEE-116"],"sequence":"Items 2, 4, 6, 7 and 8 of 11. The hinge of the scenario. If the defence at item 8 succeeded, items 5, 7, 9 and 10 would all fall with it.","summary":"He emailed his partner that he was leaving and enclosed a cheque for half the stock, then immediately bought the building and opened a competing shop nearby using suppliers cultivated during the partnership. His defence is that his duties ended when he sent the email.","trigger":"A partner dissociates and then acts on a matter that arose before he left, arguing his duties ended with his departure.","rule":"In a partnership AT WILL a partner has the power to dissociate at any time by express will, and notice of that will is effective on receipt (UPA § 601(1)); his departure is real. But dissociation does NOT extinguish the duty of loyalty. It continues in two respects. As to matters arising BEFORE dissociation — a transaction begun, or an opportunity received, while he was still a partner — the duty binds him afterwards as it did before. And where the partnership is DISSOLVED and being WOUND UP, every partner remains subject to the duty of loyalty throughout the winding up (UPA § 603(b)(3)), which is not complete merely because one partner has sent a cheque and declared it so. What dissociation DOES release is the forward-looking restraint: a dissociated partner may compete with the firm as to matters arising after he leaves. Competing by exploiting relationships and information developed as a partner, in a firm still winding up, is not such a matter.","sub":"Dissociation Does Not End the Duty of Loyalty","required":"Four propositions, and the fourth answers his case. That he had the power to dissociate at will and the email achieved that. That his dissociation caused dissolution, and the partnership entered winding up — which his unilateral cheque did not complete. That the duty of loyalty CONTINUES for matters arising before dissociation and throughout winding up, with the statutory basis named. And that his competing shop, built on partnership suppliers during the winding up, therefore breached it. Conceding that his duties ended when he left — even while condemning the earlier concealment — surrenders four of the eleven items.","sensitivity":"The switch is WHEN THE OPPORTUNITY AROSE, not when he acted on it. He sequenced his conduct with care — conceal, resign, then buy — and the sequence is exactly what does not work, because the duty attaches to the matter and not to the calendar. The mirror-image point is worth stating for the client's benefit: had the landlord called him a month AFTER the winding up was complete, the same purchase would have been unimpeachable. Source: MEE-116 sub-issues 2, 4, 6–8.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “he ceased to be a partner when he gave notice; from that moment the fidelity he owed the company ended, and what he did afterwards with his own money is his own affair — the wrong, if any, was concealing the call while he was still inside” → U.S. law follows the matter rather than the membership, so the duty reaches forward past his departure and through the winding up. This is a sequence trap inverted: normally the trained reader is caught because acting too late destroys a right, whereas here he is caught by supposing that acting later escapes a duty. Note precisely what the civilian frame does and does not carry forward, because the distinction is what he gets wrong. DEBTS survive his exit — he is not released from obligations contracted while he was a partner, as Unit 31 records. What his system treats as ending with the status is the FIDUCIARY duty: there is no notion of a winding-up period during which a former partner remains bound by loyalty as to matters that arose before he left. He therefore concedes the defence in part, which is worse than conceding it entirely: he keeps the verdict and loses the remedy.","authority":"Dissociation Does Not End the Duty of Loyalty. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 601(1), § 603(b)(3). Scenario MEE-116."},{"n":43,"scenarios":["MEE-116"],"sequence":"Items 9 and 11 of 11. Closes the scenario. Reachable only once the breach is established, and it is where the case is actually won or lost for the client.","summary":"The wronged partner sues for a constructive trust over the building and an account of the profits of the competing shop.","trigger":"A fiduciary has acquired identifiable property through his breach, and the beneficiary wants the property rather than money.","rule":"A breach of the duty of loyalty gives rise to EQUITABLE remedies, not merely to damages. Where the partner has acquired identifiable PROPERTY through the breach, the court may impose a CONSTRUCTIVE TRUST: he is treated as holding the asset for the partnership, and must convey it — so the building itself, not its value, is recovered, and the partnership captures any increase in its worth. An ACCOUNTING of profits requires him to disgorge what he gained from the breach, including the profits of the competing business built on partnership relationships, and disgorgement is measured by HIS gain rather than by the partnership's loss, so it is available even where the partnership can prove no loss at all. A partner may also bring a direct action against another partner to enforce these duties (UPA § 410(b)); the remedies are cumulative, and the court may in addition order damages and removal of any benefit obtained.","sub":"Constructive Trust and Accounting – Taking the Asset, Not Its Price","required":"Two propositions, and both must name the remedy. That a CONSTRUCTIVE TRUST may be imposed over the building, so that he holds it for the partnership and must convey it. And that he must ACCOUNT for the profits derived from the breach, including those of the competing shop, disgorgement being measured by his gain and not by her loss. An answer that establishes the breach and then asks for damages loses both items and, in practice, loses the client the building — which is the outcome the whole fact pattern was constructed to reach.","sensitivity":"The switch is that the remedy follows the ASSET. Because the thing acquired is identifiable, equity reaches it rather than its value, and the partnership takes the building with whatever it is now worth. The second switch is the measure: an accounting looks at what HE gained, so his liability does not depend on proving that the firm would have bought the building or could have afforded it — an argument that would defeat a damages claim entirely. Source: MEE-116 sub-issues 9, 11.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “he breached his obligations, so he owes compensation for the damage caused — and the damage is hard to prove, since the firm had not agreed to buy the building and may not have been able to; perhaps the loss of the lease, perhaps the profits diverted” → U.S. equity takes the building. The constructive trust has no counterpart in the civilian system, where the response to breach is reparation of damage and the claimant must show a loss; there is no mechanism converting the wrongdoer into a trustee of an asset he bought with his own money, and no remedy measured by the defendant's gain rather than the plaintiff's loss. This is the void that decides the case in practice: a reader can identify every breach correctly, argue them persuasively, and still ask the court for the one thing that leaves his client without the premises.","authority":"Constructive Trust and Accounting – Taking the Asset, Not Its Price. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 410(b). Scenario MEE-116."},{"n":44,"scenarios":["MEE-121"],"sequence":"Items 2–5 of 11, the CHAIRS. Sequential. This is the cell of the matrix that MEE-081 does not test: no authority of any kind AND a concealed principal.","summary":"The agent was told to buy fifty red chairs for no more than ten thousand dollars. Told that red chairs cost twice that, she bought fifty YELLOW chairs for ten thousand, signed the contract in her own name, and never mentioned her principal. The principal had never communicated with the store.","trigger":"An agent departs from her instructions and contracts in her own name with a third party the principal never contacted.","rule":"ACTUAL authority extends only as far as the principal's manifestations to the agent reasonably permit; an instruction to buy red chairs within a price limit does not authorise yellow chairs, and complying with the price while abandoning the specification is still a departure. APPARENT authority requires a manifestation from the PRINCIPAL to the THIRD PARTY, and where the principal has had no communication with the third party at all there is nothing from which any belief about authority could arise — the third party did not even know a principal existed. Where the principal is UNDISCLOSED and the agent had no authority, neither route reaches him: he is not bound, because nothing he did or said supports the contract, and ratification would be required to bind him. The AGENT, having contracted in her own name, is a party to the contract and is personally liable on it in full.","sub":"No Authority and a Concealed Principal – The Agent Alone Answers","required":"Four propositions. That she exceeded her actual authority, the specification and not merely the price being the limit. That apparent authority cannot arise BECAUSE the principal never communicated with the store — the reason must be stated, since its absence is what the item tests. That the principal is therefore not bound. And that the agent is personally liable, having contracted in her own name. The trap for a confident candidate is compression: 'she acted for herself, so she is liable' states the last proposition and skips the two that carry the marks.","sensitivity":"The switch is that BOTH axes fail against the principal at once, which is what distinguishes this transaction from every other in this scenario and in MEE-081. Compare: with authority and concealment, both are liable (Unit 27); without authority but with disclosure, the principal escapes and the agent answers on her warranty (Unit 26); here, without authority and without disclosure, the principal escapes and the agent answers as a CONTRACTING PARTY, which is a larger liability than the warranty. The four combinations are the examination's real subject. Source: MEE-121 sub-issues 2–5.","archetype":"False cognate","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “she contracted in her own name and outside her instructions, so she contracted for herself — the principal is a stranger to it, and that is the end of the matter” → the conclusion about who is liable is right, and the reasoning omits the two propositions the item scores. The false cognate is the mandate itself: in the civilian frame acting in one's own name and acting beyond one's powers are two independent grounds each sufficient to detach the principal, so once either is found the analysis stops. U.S. law requires both axes to be worked separately even when they point the same way, because each is a scored proposition and because the combination determines the SIZE of the agent's liability — party to the contract rather than warrantor of authority. Being right here for one reason costs the marks awarded for the other.","authority":"No Authority and a Concealed Principal – The Agent Alone Answers. Restatement (Third) of Agency § 2.03, § 6.03, § 6.10. Scenario MEE-121."},{"n":45,"scenarios":["MEE-127"],"sequence":"Items 1, 3 and 9 of 11. The defect and the attempted cure. Read with Unit 32, which supplies the default that fills the gap.","summary":"Three people executed a document headed 'Limited Partnership Agreement' and named a fourth as general partner, but he never signed and never assented. A Certificate of Limited Partnership was filed only after a creditor had sued. Two weeks after that filing, a visitor died on the entity's land.","trigger":"An intended limited partnership has no general partner who assented, and a certificate is filed after claims have arisen.","rule":"A LIMITED PARTNERSHIP requires at least one GENERAL PARTNER, and a person becomes a general partner only by ACTUALLY ASSENTING to that status; naming someone in an agreement he never signed makes him nothing. Without a general partner the limited partnership does not come into existence, whatever the document is called and however clearly the subscribers intended it. Filing a CERTIFICATE does not cure the defect: the certificate presupposes the substantive requirements, and its filing neither supplies the missing assent nor operates RETROACTIVELY. Any protection a valid filing might confer runs FORWARD from the filing only, so a claim that had already arisen is untouched — and where no limited partnership ever existed, a later claim is untouched too. The subscribers, having carried on the business as co-owners for profit, are general partners of the general partnership their conduct formed, with the personal liability that entails.","sub":"Why the Limited Partnership Failed, and Why the Late Certificate Cannot Save It","required":"Three propositions. That no limited partnership exists BECAUSE no general partner assented — the requirement named, and the unsigned nomination identified as insufficient. That the later certificate does not create one and does not operate retroactively. And that the death occurring after the filing is therefore unprotected, since there was never a limited partnership for the filing to perfect. An answer that rests on the title of the document, or that treats the filing as constitutive, misidentifies the entity and takes the remaining seven items off the scoring grid.","sensitivity":"Two switches, both formal, neither about anyone's intentions. ASSENT: one signature by the nominated general partner would have created the entity the parties thought they had. TIMING: even a valid filing looks only forward, so its date relative to each claim decides what it can touch — and here it touches nothing, because the substantive defect was never cured. Note the trap in the second: the fact pattern places the death AFTER the filing precisely to invite the inference that the filing must protect them by then. Source: MEE-127 sub-issues 1, 3, 9.","archetype":"Sequence & waiver traps","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the constitutive act says it is a limited partnership and the certificate is on the register; the type of company is what its charter declares and registration perfects it — at the latest, from the filing the partners are protected, so the death two weeks later falls inside the shield” → U.S. law makes the shield depend on a substantive fact the paper cannot supply, and gives the filing no retroactive reach even when the substance is present. The civilian instinct to trust the register is sound in its own system and is precisely what the item punishes. The trap is temporal and doubly baited: the reader is invited first to accept the document, then — if he resists that — to accept the filing date as a dividing line. Both invitations are declined by the same rule: the entity never existed to be registered.","authority":"Why the Limited Partnership Failed, and Why the Late Certificate Cannot Save It. Uniform Limited Partnership Act (2001, last amended 2013) § 201, § 201(b)–(c). Scenario MEE-127."},{"n":46,"scenarios":["MEE-127"],"sequence":"Items 5–8 and 10 of 11. Reachable only once the entity is identified. The same procedural question is asked twice — once for a contract creditor and once for a tort claimant — and answered the same way.","summary":"A marketing firm supplied services and was not paid; a visitor died falling into an uncovered well on the entity's land. Both pursue the three subscribers personally. The entity's only asset, the land, has lost much of its value.","trigger":"A creditor of a general partnership seeks to reach the partners' personal assets.","rule":"Partners are liable JOINTLY AND SEVERALLY for ALL obligations of the partnership, in contract and in tort alike (UPA § 306(a)); a partnership is liable for loss caused by a partner's wrongful act or omission in the ordinary course of its business (UPA § 305(a)), and premises liability arising from land the firm holds for its business is such an obligation. Liability alone is not collection. A claimant must NAME the partners as defendants — a judgment against the partnership alone is not a judgment against any partner (UPA § 307(c)) — and must first EXHAUST partnership assets: a judgment creditor may levy on a partner's individual property only where the partnership's assets have been exhausted, or execution against them has been returned unsatisfied, or the court grants leave (UPA § 307(d)). The sequence applies identically to tort and contract claimants, and the depreciation of the firm's only asset does not shorten it — it merely means the exhaustion will be quick.","sub":"Joint and Several Liability Is Not Collection – Naming and Exhaustion","required":"Four propositions. That the partners are JOINTLY AND SEVERALLY liable for the debt. That they are equally so for the death, the land being held for the partnership's business. That the claimant must sue the PARTNERS as well as the partnership, a judgment against the firm alone reaching no partner. And that partnership assets must be EXHAUSTED before personal assets can be levied on. The last two are the items most often lost: a candidate who states the liability and stops has answered the question of who owes and not the question the examination asked, which is how the claimant actually gets paid.","sensitivity":"The switch is procedural, not substantive: the liability exists from the first day and is unenforceable against a partner personally until the sequence is completed. Note that the examination asks it twice, for a contract creditor and for a tort claimant, to establish that the answer does not vary with the nature of the claim — and note what the depreciated land does NOT do: it does not excuse the exhaustion step, it only makes it short. Source: MEE-127 sub-issues 5–8, 10.","archetype":"Institutional void","misalignment":"Reflex → answer: “the obligation is solidary, so the creditor may proceed against the company or against any partner at his choice, for the whole — that is what solidarity means, and the emptiness of the company's patrimony is exactly why he would go straight to the partners” → U.S. law imposes an order of attack: name the partners, take judgment against them, exhaust the firm's assets, and only then levy personally. The civilian obligación solidaria confers precisely the freedom of choice this rule withdraws, so there is no domestic construct corresponding to a liability that is joint and several in substance yet subsidiary in execution. The reader therefore answers the merits impeccably and omits both procedural items, which in this scenario are two of the eleven — and in practice are the difference between a judgment and a payment.","authority":"Joint and Several Liability Is Not Collection – Naming and Exhaustion. Uniform Partnership Act (1997, last amended 2013) § 306(a), § 305(a), § 307(c), § 307(d). Scenario MEE-127."}]},"totals":{"cases":948,"units":342,"meeIssues":141,"meeUnits":46,"meeScenarios":17}};
